var e=[{id:`lr-a-001`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-indicators`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A restates the Therefore recommendation; B and D restate premises, C is opposite, E is too-strong scope shift. One defensible answer, no leakage. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`When a downtown street was given a dedicated bus lane, average bus travel times on that street fell by nearly a third, while car travel times rose by only a few minutes. Riders on that route also reported noticeably higher satisfaction. These results suggest that reserving street space for buses improves the overall commuting experience. Therefore, the transit authority should convert more downtown streets to include dedicated bus lanes.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`The transit authority should convert more downtown streets to include dedicated bus lanes.`},{text:`Bus travel times fell by nearly a third on the street that received a dedicated bus lane.`},{text:`The transit authority should not convert any more downtown streets to include bus lanes.`},{text:`Car travel times rose slightly on the street that received a dedicated bus lane.`},{text:`Every street in the city should be given a dedicated bus lane.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The word "Therefore" flags the author's bottom line: a recommendation to add more bus lanes. Everything before it is evidence for that recommendation. Choice A restates the recommendation, while the other choices restate premises, contradict the argument, or overstate it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Begin by separating evidence from the point the evidence supports. The first three sentences give data: bus times fell, car times rose only slightly, rider satisfaction rose, and these results suggest bus lanes improve commuting. None of those sentences tells anyone to do anything; they describe what happened. The final sentence, introduced by "Therefore," is the only normative claim, the author's recommendation. On main-conclusion questions, the conclusion is the claim everything else is trying to establish, and indicator words like "therefore," "thus," and "so" usually mark it. Choice A matches that recommendation exactly. Choices B and D restate premises (trap: restates-premise): the travel-time figures are cited as evidence for the recommendation, not argued for as the point. Choice C states the opposite (trap: opposite) of what the author favors. Choice E inflates "more downtown streets" into "every street in the city" (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). A common error is selecting the most striking statistic instead of the recommendation; the statistics exist to support the recommendation, not to be the point.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice restates the "Therefore" sentence, the argument's recommendation, which all of the travel-time and satisfaction data are offered to support. It performs exactly the task the stem requests: expressing the main conclusion rather than any supporting detail.`,`This is tempting because it is the most striking statistic in the stimulus, but it is a premise (trap: restates-premise), not the conclusion. The argument cites the drop in bus travel times as evidence for the recommendation; it never argues in order to establish that the drop occurred.`,`This choice states the opposite (trap: opposite) of the author's position and is tempting only if the argument's direction is misread. Every premise points toward expanding bus lanes, so a recommendation against them contradicts the reasoning.`,`This is true according to the stimulus, which makes it tempting, but as a conclusion candidate it fails because it is merely one premise (trap: restates-premise). It is a single datum offered in support of the recommendation, not the recommendation itself.`,`This resembles the recommendation, which makes it tempting, but it inflates "more downtown streets" into "every street in the city" (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The author never commits to universal conversion, so this choice claims more than the argument concludes.`],generalLesson:`The main conclusion is the claim the rest of the argument is trying to establish, often a recommendation or evaluation, not the most dramatic piece of evidence. Indicator words like "therefore" mark it, but always verify which claim the evidence actually serves.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which sentence is the author trying to get you to accept, and which sentences are reasons to accept it?`,`Look for the argument's only recommendation. What does the author think should be done?`,`Which choice restates that recommendation instead of restating a piece of evidence?`],labels:{topic:`bus lanes`}},{id:`lr-a-002`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C states the directors success verdict; A restates a premise, B adopts the critics viewpoint, D is opposite, E overgeneralizes to every museum. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A city museum replaced its fixed admission fee with a pay-what-you-wish policy. In the first year, total attendance doubled while total admission revenue stayed roughly the same as before. The museum's director calls this a success, since the museum's mission is to serve as many residents as possible rather than to maximize ticket income. Critics object that wealthier visitors now pay less than they used to. But maximizing what each visitor pays was never the director's goal.`,stem:`The main point of the argument is that`,choices:[{text:`total attendance at the museum doubled in the first year of pay-what-you-wish admission.`},{text:`wealthier visitors now pay less than they did under the fixed admission fee.`},{text:`switching to pay-what-you-wish admission was a success for the museum.`},{text:`the museum's admission policy was a failure because revenue did not grow.`},{text:`every museum should replace fixed admission fees with pay-what-you-wish policies.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The director's evaluation, that the policy change was a success, is the claim the attendance figures and the mission statement are marshaled to support. The critics' objection is introduced only to be dismissed. Choice C captures that evaluation; the rest are premises, the critics' view, or overstatements.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify whose conclusion is being argued for. The stimulus presents the director's case: attendance doubled, revenue held steady, and the museum's mission is serving residents rather than maximizing income. Those facts are offered to support the director's verdict that the change "is a success." The critics' complaint about wealthier visitors paying less is raised and then neutralized by the final sentence, which clarifies that maximizing per-visitor payment was never the goal. So the critics' point is not the conclusion; it is an objection the argument sets aside. Choice C restates the director's verdict, the claim everything else supports. Choice A restates a premise (trap: restates-premise). Choice B adopts the critics' viewpoint as if it were the conclusion (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice D states the opposite (trap: opposite) of the director's evaluation. Choice E generalizes to every museum (trap: scope-shift, too-strong), a claim the argument never makes. The difficulty here comes from the critics' sentence sitting near the end, where conclusions often appear.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because doubling attendance is the most impressive fact in the stimulus, but it is a premise (trap: restates-premise). The argument cites the attendance figure as evidence that the policy succeeded; the success verdict itself is the conclusion.`,`This is tempting because it is the last substantive objection mentioned, but it expresses the critics' viewpoint (trap: wrong-viewpoint), not the author's. The argument raises this point only to dismiss it via the director's stated goal.`,`This correctly identifies the main conclusion: the director's evaluation that the policy change was a success. The attendance data, the steady revenue, and the mission statement are all offered as support for exactly this verdict.`,`This states the opposite (trap: opposite) of the argument's conclusion. The director explicitly calls the unchanged revenue acceptable given the museum's mission, so treating flat revenue as failure reverses the author's evaluation.`,`This is tempting because it sounds like a natural takeaway, but it generalizes to every museum (trap: scope-shift, too-strong). The argument evaluates one museum's policy against its own mission and never prescribes anything for museums in general.`],generalLesson:`When a stimulus presents one person's argument plus an objection, the main conclusion belongs to the arguer, not the objector. Track whose claim the evidence supports, especially when the objection sits near the end where conclusions often appear.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`recency-effect`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Whose case is being made here: the director's or the critics'?`,`What verdict does the attendance data plus the mission statement add up to?`,`Which choice states that verdict rather than a fact used to support it?`],labels:{topic:`museum admission pricing`}},{id:`lr-a-003`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E states the final recommendation; A is an intermediate-conclusion trap, B a premise, C opposite, D scope shift. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Decades of aggressive fire suppression left the region's pine forests carrying far more dead underbrush than they historically held. That buildup of fuel is the main reason today's fires burn hotter and spread faster than those of a century ago. Consequently, forest managers have begun setting small controlled burns to clear the underbrush. Since the unusually destructive fires are largely a product of past suppression policy, allowing limited, deliberate burns is the most responsible way to protect these forests.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Today's forest fires are largely a product of past fire suppression policy.`},{text:`Today's fires burn hotter and spread faster than those of a century ago.`},{text:`Aggressive fire suppression should be resumed to protect the pine forests.`},{text:`Controlled burns should be used in every forest in the country.`},{text:`Allowing limited, deliberate burns is the most responsible way to protect these forests.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The final sentence, introduced by "Since" as support and ending in a clear recommendation, is the claim everything else builds toward: controlled burns are the most responsible protection strategy. Choices A and B restate premises, C contradicts the argument, and D overgeneralizes it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the argument's structure. The first two sentences establish the problem: suppression created fuel buildup, and that buildup makes fires worse. The third sentence notes the response managers have begun. The final sentence draws the recommendation: given that the destructive fires stem from past suppression, limited deliberate burns are the most responsible protection. Notice the layering: the claim that suppression caused the fuel buildup is itself a conclusion supported by the historical comparison, but it functions as a premise for the final recommendation. That makes it an intermediate conclusion, and intermediate conclusions are classic wrong answers (trap: wrong-conclusion) on main-point questions. Choice E matches the final recommendation exactly. Choice A restates that intermediate conclusion. Choice B restates a basic premise. Choice C recommends the opposite (trap: opposite) of what the argument supports. Choice D stretches "these forests" to "every forest in the country" (trap: scope-shift, too-strong).`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because it is itself a conclusion the argument defends, but it is only an intermediate one (trap: wrong-conclusion). The claim about suppression causing fuel buildup is used as a reason for the final recommendation about controlled burns, so it is not the main point.`,`This is a premise (trap: restates-premise), not the conclusion. The comparison between today's fires and those of a century ago is evidence for the claim about suppression's effects, which in turn supports the recommendation.`,`This recommends the opposite (trap: opposite) of what the argument concludes. The entire argument blames past suppression for the current danger, so resuming aggressive suppression contradicts the reasoning.`,`This is tempting because it endorses controlled burns, but it expands the recommendation from "these forests" to every forest in the country (trap: scope-shift, too-strong). The argument's evidence concerns only the region's pine forests.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion: the final sentence's recommendation that limited, deliberate burns are the most responsible way to protect these forests. Every earlier claim is offered in service of this verdict.`],generalLesson:`Arguments often contain intermediate conclusions that are defended and then used as support. The main conclusion is the final claim the whole chain serves, so check whether a tempting choice is merely a stepping stone to a further recommendation.`,misconceptionTags:[`intermediate-conclusion-trap`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`restates-premise`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which claim does the whole chain of reasoning end at: a diagnosis of the problem or a recommendation?`,`The sentence about suppression causing fuel buildup is defended, but is it then used to support something further?`,`Which choice states the final recommendation rather than a step along the way?`],labels:{topic:`forest fire management`}},{id:`lr-a-004`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B states the broad generalization; E is a designed intermediate-conclusion near miss, but the argument continues past it to the generalization. A unsupported, C answers-different-question, D too-strong. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Two years ago, a school district replaced its standard cafeteria menu with one built around fresh vegetables and whole grains. Since then, the share of students buying school lunch has risen from 40 percent to 68 percent, even though the price per meal did not change. If students simply rejected healthy food, as some administrators predicted, participation would have fallen. The rise in participation shows that students will choose healthier meals when those meals are made appealing and affordable.`,stem:`Which one of the following is the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Students disliked the district's old cafeteria menu.`},{text:`Students will choose healthier meals when those meals are made appealing and affordable.`},{text:`The price of school lunch should be reduced to increase participation further.`},{text:`Administrators' predictions about student behavior are generally unreliable.`},{text:`The redesigned menu caused the rise in lunch participation.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The final sentence generalizes beyond this district to a broad claim about students and healthy meals, and the participation data plus the rebutted prediction are offered to support that generalization. Choice B states it; the other choices are narrower, stronger, or merely stepping stones.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Notice the scope shift between the evidence and the final sentence. The evidence is local: one district, one menu change, participation rising from 40 to 68 percent with prices unchanged, plus a rebuttal of the prediction that students would reject healthy food. The final sentence then generalizes to students in general choosing healthier meals when they are appealing and affordable. That generalization is the main conclusion; everything else is marshaled to earn it. Choice B restates it. Choice E is the tempting intermediate step (trap: wrong-conclusion): the causal claim that the menu caused the rise is defended and then used to support the broader generalization, so it is not the final point. Choice A goes beyond the evidence (trap: unsupported): rising participation does not establish that students disliked the old menu. Choice C introduces a new recommendation about cutting prices (trap: answers-different-question, scope-shift) that the argument never makes. Choice D inflates one wrong prediction into a general indictment of administrators (trap: too-strong).`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because it feels like a natural inference from rising participation, but it is unsupported (trap: unsupported) and is not the argument's point. Students may have liked the old menu yet still preferred the new one; the argument concludes something broader about healthy meals.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion. The final sentence generalizes from the district's experience to a general claim about students choosing appealing, affordable healthy meals, and the participation data and rebutted prediction are offered to support exactly this claim.`,`This is tempting because it concerns lunch pricing, but it is a new recommendation the argument never makes (trap: answers-different-question, scope-shift). The argument notes prices did not change; it does not conclude they should be cut.`,`This inflates a single mistaken prediction into a sweeping claim about administrators (trap: too-strong). The argument uses the failed prediction as evidence against one specific expectation, not as grounds for judging administrators generally.`,`This is the strongest distractor: it is a genuine intermediate conclusion (trap: wrong-conclusion) that the argument defends. But the causal claim about the menu is then used as a stepping stone to the broader generalization in the final sentence, so it is not the main conclusion.`],generalLesson:`When evidence is local but the final sentence generalizes, the generalization is usually the main conclusion. Distinguish the causal stepping stone the argument establishes from the broader lesson it draws from that step.`,misconceptionTags:[`intermediate-conclusion-trap`,`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the author's final point about this one district, or about students more generally?`,`The claim that the menu caused the rise is defended, but does the argument stop there?`,`Which choice states the broad generalization in the last sentence?`],labels:{topic:`school lunch program`}},{id:`lr-a-005`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-indicators`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D restates the only recommendation; B and C restate premises, A opposite, E too-strong universalization. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The community garden's compost bins have been overflowing because too few volunteers are turning them. Unturned compost breaks down slowly and attracts pests. Two neighboring gardens solved the same problem by assigning one volunteer to each bin on a rotating schedule. Since a rotating schedule is simple to organize, the garden should adopt one for its compost bins.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`The garden should not change how it manages its compost bins.`},{text:`The community garden's compost bins have been overflowing.`},{text:`Unturned compost breaks down slowly and attracts pests.`},{text:`The garden should adopt a rotating volunteer schedule for its compost bins.`},{text:`Every community garden must assign one volunteer to each compost bin.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The final sentence, introduced by "Since" as support, is the argument's only recommendation: adopt a rotating schedule. The overflowing bins, the pest problem, and the neighbors' success are all reasons for that recommendation. Choice D restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Separate the problem description from the proposed solution. The first two sentences describe the problem: bins overflow because volunteers are scarce, and unturned compost causes trouble. The third sentence offers a precedent: neighboring gardens fixed the same problem with rotating schedules. The final sentence, marked by "Since" introducing support, delivers the recommendation, which is the claim all the preceding material supports. Choice D restates that recommendation. Choices B and C restate premises (trap: restates-premise): the overflowing bins and the pest facts are the problem being addressed, not the point. Choice A states the opposite (trap: opposite). Choice E universalizes the proposal to every garden with "must" (trap: too-strong, scope-shift); the argument recommends the schedule for this garden only. The recommendation is the point; the rest is the case for it.`,choiceExplanations:[`This states the opposite (trap: opposite) of the author's position. The whole argument builds a case for changing the compost routine, so a recommendation against change contradicts the reasoning.`,`This is tempting because the overflowing bins open the stimulus, but it is a premise (trap: restates-premise). The overflow is the problem the argument addresses; the recommendation for fixing it is the conclusion.`,`This is true according to the stimulus, which makes it tempting, but it is background evidence (trap: restates-premise) explaining why the problem matters. It supports the case for a schedule rather than being the case's conclusion.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion: the final sentence's recommendation that the garden adopt a rotating volunteer schedule. The problem description and the neighbors' precedent are offered to support exactly this proposal.`,`This is tempting because it endorses the rotating schedule, but "every community garden must" goes far beyond the argument (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The recommendation concerns this garden, and it is a "should," not a "must."`],generalLesson:`Problem descriptions and precedents are premises; the main conclusion is usually the proposed solution they support. Watch for "should" language at the end, and do not confuse the problem being solved with the point being argued.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`restates-premise`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which sentences describe the problem, and which sentence proposes what to do about it?`,`What is the one "should" claim in the argument?`,`Which choice restates that proposal rather than the problem?`],labels:{topic:`community garden compost`}},{id:`lr-a-006`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`A regional film festival should be judged by whether it introduces audiences to films they would not otherwise see. Last year, more than half of the festival's screenings were films that already had commercial distribution deals. Those screenings drew the largest crowds, and the organizers have promised even more commercial titles this year. By the standard that matters, then, the festival is failing its mission.`,stem:`The main conclusion of the argument is best expressed by which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`The festival will soon fail financially if it keeps showing commercial films.`},{text:`Films with commercial distribution deals drew the festival's largest crowds.`},{text:`By the standard that matters, the festival is failing its mission.`},{text:`The festival's organizers are deliberately betraying their audience.`},{text:`Film festivals should never screen films that have commercial distribution.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The opening sentence sets the standard, the middle sentences supply the evidence, and the final sentence applies the standard to reach a verdict: the festival is failing its mission. Choice C states that verdict. The other choices predict, restate evidence, or overstate the charge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This argument has a standard-plus-evidence structure. The first sentence proposes the criterion: a regional festival should be judged by introducing audiences to otherwise-unseen films. The next two sentences report the facts: most screenings were already-distributed commercial films, and organizers promise more. The final sentence, flagged by "then," applies the criterion to the facts and delivers the verdict. That verdict is the main conclusion, and choice C restates it. The final factual sentence about organizers promising more commercial titles is a recency trap: it sits where conclusions often sit, tempting choice A's prediction about the festival's future, but it is just additional evidence (trap: restates-premise); the prediction about the festival's future is unsupported. Choice B restates a premise. Choice D ascribes deliberate betrayal, far stronger than anything stated (trap: too-strong, wrong-viewpoint). Choice E turns a standard for judging into an absolute prohibition (trap: too-strong). The verdict about the mission is an evaluation, not a prediction, which eliminates A.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the last factual sentence points toward the future, but the argument never predicts financial failure (trap: unsupported). The conclusion is an evaluation of the festival against its mission, not a forecast about its finances.`,`This restates a premise (trap: restates-premise): the crowd sizes are evidence that the festival leans on commercial titles. The argument cites this fact to support its verdict; the verdict itself is the conclusion.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion. The final sentence applies the opening standard to the reported facts and delivers the author's verdict: by the standard that matters, the festival is failing its mission.`,`This is tempting because the author is critical of the organizers, but "deliberately betraying" ascribes intent the argument never mentions (trap: too-strong). The conclusion concerns the festival's performance against a standard, not the organizers' motives.`,`This turns the author's evaluative standard into an absolute rule (trap: too-strong). The argument says festivals should be judged by introducing unseen films, which is compatible with showing some commercial titles; it never concludes they should show none.`],generalLesson:`A standard-plus-evidence argument concludes with a verdict applying the standard, not with a prediction or a restatement of the evidence. When the last sentence is factual, resist the recency pull and check which claim the facts are serving.`,misconceptionTags:[`recency-effect`,`prediction-as-conclusion`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`restates-premise`,`too-strong`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What job does the first sentence do: state a fact, or set a standard for judging?`,`Which sentence applies that standard to the facts to deliver a verdict?`,`Which choice states that verdict rather than predicting the future?`],labels:{topic:`film festival programming`}},{id:`lr-a-007`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`,`f-indicators`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:3,scopeSubtlety:3,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Routine use of antibiotics in livestock feed promotes drug-resistant bacteria, which can reach people through meat and farm runoff. Several countries that banned routine agricultural use saw resistant infections in humans decline within a few years, without major losses in meat production. It is therefore likely that similar bans elsewhere would reduce resistant infections while keeping farms productive. Given the mounting human cost of resistant infections, regulators should seriously consider such bans.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Bans on routine antibiotic use in livestock feed would likely reduce resistant infections while keeping farms productive.`},{text:`Regulators should ban the routine use of antibiotics in livestock feed.`},{text:`Meat production does not depend on antibiotics at all.`},{text:`Drug-resistant bacteria cannot spread to people through farm runoff.`},{text:`Regulators should give serious consideration to banning routine antibiotic use in livestock feed.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument builds a causal and comparative case and then converts it into a carefully qualified recommendation in the final sentence: regulators should seriously consider such bans. Choice E preserves that exact qualification; choice B drops it, and choice A is the subsidiary prediction the recommendation rests on.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is difficult because the argument contains two conclusion-like claims in a row. The "therefore" sentence offers a prediction: similar bans elsewhere would likely reduce resistant infections while keeping farms productive. That prediction is itself supported by the cross-country evidence. But the argument does not stop there; the final sentence uses the prediction plus the human cost of resistance to reach a normative recommendation. The recommendation is the main conclusion because everything, including the prediction, serves it. Now match the recommendation's exact strength. The author says regulators should "seriously consider" bans, a deliberate hedge. Choice E reproduces that qualification and is correct. Choice A restates the subsidiary prediction (trap: wrong-conclusion): it is defended and then used as support, so it is intermediate. Choice B is the most dangerous distractor (trap: too-strong): it upgrades "seriously consider" to an outright "should ban," claiming more than the author concludes. Choice C inflates "without major losses" into total independence (trap: too-strong). Choice D contradicts a premise (trap: opposite).`,choiceExplanations:[`This is a strong distractor because the argument genuinely defends this prediction, but it is a subsidiary conclusion (trap: wrong-conclusion). The "therefore" prediction is established and then used, together with the human cost of resistance, to support the final recommendation, so it is not the main point.`,`This is the most dangerous distractor: it captures the topic but upgrades the author's careful "should seriously consider" to an unqualified "should ban" (trap: too-strong). The argument deliberately stops short of demanding bans, so this choice claims more than the conclusion.`,`This inflates the evidence (trap: too-strong). The stimulus reports no major losses in meat production where bans were tried, which is far weaker than claiming production does not depend on antibiotics at all.`,`This directly contradicts a premise (trap: opposite). The argument explicitly states that resistant bacteria can reach people through meat and farm runoff, so denying the runoff pathway reverses the evidence.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion with its exact qualification. The final sentence's recommendation, that regulators should give serious consideration to such bans, is the claim the cross-country evidence and the prediction about similar bans are all marshaled to support.`],generalLesson:`When an argument ends with a qualified recommendation, the qualification is part of the conclusion. Distinguish subsidiary predictions from the final verdict, and never select a choice that strengthens or weakens the author's stated level of commitment.`,misconceptionTags:[`intermediate-conclusion-trap`,`strength-mismatch`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`too-strong`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The "therefore" sentence makes a prediction. Does the argument end there, or use that prediction for something further?`,`What is the final sentence asking regulators to do, and how strongly is it put?`,`Which choice matches that exact level of strength: "seriously consider," not "must ban"?`],labels:{topic:`antibiotics in livestock`}},{id:`lr-a-008`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-indicators`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B states the final So verdict; A restates the opening premise, C opposite, D scope shift, E too-strong. Three-level structure tracked correctly. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`When a large chain bookstore opened two blocks from Alvarez Books, many predicted the independent shop would close within a year. Instead, Alvarez Books has increased its revenue every year since, by hosting author events and building a loyal base of regular customers. Clearly, an independent bookstore can thrive alongside a chain if it offers an experience the chain does not. So reports of the inevitable death of independent bookstores have been greatly exaggerated.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Alvarez Books has increased its revenue every year since the chain store opened nearby.`},{text:`Reports of the inevitable death of independent bookstores have been greatly exaggerated.`},{text:`Independent bookstores cannot survive competition from large chains.`},{text:`All retail businesses should host events to build customer loyalty.`},{text:`Hosting author events is the most effective marketing strategy for any bookstore.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The "So" sentence generalizes from the Alvarez Books story to a verdict about the dire predictions for independent bookstores generally. That verdict is what the example and the "Clearly" generalization are offered to support. Choice B states it; the rest are premises, the opposite claim, or overstatements.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Track the levels of generality. The stimulus opens with a specific case: Alvarez Books survived and grew despite a nearby chain. The "Clearly" sentence draws a conditional generalization from that case: independents can thrive alongside chains if they offer a distinctive experience. The final "So" sentence then delivers the verdict the whole argument aims at: reports of the inevitable death of independent bookstores are exaggerated. The specific case supports the generalization, and the generalization supports the verdict, making the verdict the main conclusion. Choice B restates it. Choice A restates the opening premise (trap: restates-premise). Choice C states the opposite (trap: opposite) of what the Alvarez example shows. Choice D universalizes to all retail (trap: scope-shift). Choice E crowns author events the best strategy for any bookstore (trap: too-strong, unsupported): the argument says events helped Alvarez, not that they are the optimal strategy universally.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the Alvarez story is vivid, but it is a premise (trap: restates-premise). The revenue growth is the evidence from which the argument generalizes; the verdict about the death-of-bookstores reports is the conclusion.`,`This correctly restates the main conclusion. The final "So" sentence delivers the author's verdict on the predictions about independent bookstores, and both the Alvarez example and the conditional generalization are offered to support it.`,`This states the opposite (trap: opposite) of the argument's point. The Alvarez example is presented precisely to refute the claim that independents cannot survive chains.`,`This universalizes the lesson to all retail businesses (trap: scope-shift). The argument concerns bookstores and draws a conditional lesson about distinctive experiences; it never prescribes event-hosting for every retailer.`,`This is tempting because author events are mentioned, but crowning them the most effective strategy for any bookstore goes far beyond the evidence (trap: too-strong, unsupported). The argument claims only that Alvarez's approach worked for Alvarez.`],generalLesson:`Specific example, then generalization, then verdict: the main conclusion is the final evaluative claim the chain serves. Do not stop at the example or the intermediate generalization when a further verdict follows.`,misconceptionTags:[`intermediate-conclusion-trap`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the Alvarez Books story prove, and what does the author do with that proof?`,`The last sentence starts with "So." What verdict does it deliver?`,`Which choice states that verdict about the predictions, not the bookstore's revenue?`],labels:{topic:`independent bookstore`}},{id:`lr-a-009`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A states the authors recommendation; B is the opponents opening claim the Yet sentence rebuts, C and E restate premises, D too-strong. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Opponents of lowering the voting age to sixteen claim that teenagers lack the judgment to vote responsibly. Yet studies show that sixteen-year-olds perform as well as adults on tests of the reasoning skills relevant to voting, and the habit of voting formed early tends to persist for life. Since the goal of election law should be to include all citizens capable of responsible voting, the voting age should be lowered to sixteen.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,choices:[{text:`The voting age should be lowered to sixteen.`},{text:`Teenagers lack the judgment to vote responsibly.`},{text:`Sixteen-year-olds perform as well as adults on tests of voting-relevant reasoning skills.`},{text:`All citizens should be required to vote in every election.`},{text:`The habit of voting formed early tends to persist for life.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The final sentence, supported by the "Since" clause, is the argument's recommendation: lower the voting age. The opponents' claim is introduced to be rebutted, and the study results are premises. Choice A states the recommendation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`First, sort the viewpoints. The opening sentence states the opponents' position: teenagers lack judgment. The word "Yet" signals the author's turn against that position, and the studies plus the lifelong-voting-habit point are evidence for the author's side. The final sentence, backed by the "Since" clause about the goal of election law, delivers the recommendation. That recommendation is the main conclusion, and choice A restates it. Choice B is the trap that catches readers who grab the first sentence (trap: wrong-viewpoint): it is the opponents' claim, which the argument rebuts. Choices C and E restate premises (trap: restates-premise). Choice D invents a mandatory-voting requirement (trap: too-strong, scope-shift) that confuses "include all capable citizens" with "require everyone to vote." The "Since" clause is doing support work for the recommendation, so the recommendation is the conclusion it serves.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly restates the main conclusion: the final sentence's recommendation to lower the voting age to sixteen. The study results, the habit evidence, and the goal of election law are all offered to support this proposal.`,`This is tempting because it opens the stimulus, but it is the opponents' claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint), which the "Yet" sentence and the studies are marshaled to rebut. The author argues against this claim rather than concluding it.`,`This restates a premise (trap: restates-premise). The study result is evidence that sixteen-year-olds can vote responsibly; it is offered in support of the recommendation, not as the recommendation itself.`,`This confuses including capable citizens with compelling everyone to vote (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The argument concludes that sixteen-year-olds should be allowed to vote, not that all citizens must vote.`,`This restates a premise (trap: restates-premise). The persistence of early voting habits is one reason the author favors lowering the age, but it is support for the recommendation rather than the recommendation.`],generalLesson:`When a stimulus opens with an opposing view, that view is usually there to be rebutted, not concluded. The main conclusion is the author's own verdict, typically the recommendation the rebuttal evidence supports.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`restates-premise`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Whose claim opens the stimulus, and what does the word "Yet" do to it?`,`What recommendation does the "Since" clause support in the final sentence?`,`Which choice states that recommendation rather than the opponents' claim?`],labels:{topic:`voting age`}},{id:`lr-a-010`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`The library's late fees bring in only a small fraction of its budget while consuming hours of staff time each week. Several libraries that dropped fines saw borrowing by new patrons increase. **The library should therefore eliminate its late-fee policy.** Doing so would remove a barrier that keeps low-income patrons away.`,stem:`The claim that the library should eliminate its late-fee policy plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is a premise offered in support of the argument's conclusion.`},{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion, supported by the other statements.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion that supports a further recommendation.`},{text:`It is a concession to critics of the library's policies.`},{text:`It is background information with no bearing on the reasoning.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The bolded claim is the recommendation the other sentences support: the revenue and staff-time facts plus the precedent of other libraries are reasons to eliminate the fees. Nothing in the argument supports anything further, so it is the main conclusion, not a premise or an intermediate step.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Ask what the bolded claim does: is it used as a reason, or is it the point the reasons support? The first two sentences give evidence: fees raise little money, cost staff time, and other libraries saw new-patron borrowing rise after dropping fines. The final sentence gives an additional benefit of eliminating fees. All of these point toward the bolded recommendation, and nothing follows from the bolded claim as a further conclusion. That makes it the main conclusion (choice B). Choice A reverses the relationship (trap: wrong-conclusion): the bolded claim is supported by the other statements, not offered as support for them. Choice C invents a further recommendation that does not exist. Choice D misreads it as a concession (trap: wrong-viewpoint): the author endorses the claim, not the critics. Choice E ignores the evidentiary structure entirely, offering an irrelevant-true role description (trap: irrelevant-true). On argument-part questions, always check the direction of support before assigning a role.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the direction of support (trap: wrong-conclusion). The revenue facts and the other libraries' experience are premises offered to support the bolded recommendation; the bolded claim itself is not used as a reason for anything else.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the bolded recommendation is the main conclusion, and the facts about revenue, staff time, and other libraries' results are all offered as support for it.`,`This is tempting because the claim is supported by evidence, but there is no further recommendation beyond it (trap: wrong-conclusion). An intermediate conclusion must support something else, and nothing in the argument follows from the bolded claim.`,`This misidentifies the author's stance (trap: wrong-viewpoint). A concession grants something to the other side; here the author fully endorses eliminating the fees, so the claim is the author's own conclusion, not a concession.`,`This ignores the argument's structure. The bolded claim is the point toward which all the evidence is directed, so describing it as bearing nothing on the reasoning gets its role exactly backwards.`],generalLesson:`To identify a claim's role, check the direction of support: premises are used as reasons, while the conclusion is the point the reasons serve. A claim supported by everything and supporting nothing further is the main conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the bolded claim used as a reason for something else, or is it the point the reasons are driving at?`,`What do the facts about revenue, staff time, and other libraries all point toward?`,`Which choice describes a claim that is supported by everything and supports nothing further?`],labels:{topic:`library late fees`}},{id:`lr-a-011`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A identifies the injury statistic as evidence; B mistakes it for the conclusion, C misidentifies the rejected claim, D reverses inference direction, E misreads stance. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Downtown e-scooter rentals have doubled every year for three years. **Emergency-room visits for scooter injuries have risen sharply over the same period.** Some say the rise merely reflects greater ridership. But injuries per thousand rides have also climbed, which greater ridership alone cannot explain. The city should therefore cap the number of rental scooters.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the role played in the argument by the statement that emergency-room visits for scooter injuries have risen sharply?`,choices:[{text:`It is evidence offered in support of the argument's conclusion that scooter rentals should be capped.`},{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is a claim the argument introduces in order to reject it.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion supported by the per-thousand-rides statistic.`},{text:`It is a concession that the argument ultimately accepts as defeating its conclusion.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The injury statistic is data: it helps establish that scooters are causing harm, which supports capping rentals. The "Some say" claim is the one the argument rejects, and the per-thousand-rides point is what rules out the ridership explanation. So the bolded claim is a premise, not a conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Distinguish three different claims. The bolded injury statistic is a factual observation. The "injuries per thousand rides have also climbed" sentence is a refined observation that rules out the rival explanation ("some say the rise merely reflects greater ridership"). The final sentence is the recommendation to cap rentals. The bolded claim functions as evidence for the recommendation: rising injuries are a reason to cap scooters. It is not itself inferred from anything, so it is not an intermediate conclusion (choice D is wrong). The claim the argument rejects is the "merely reflects greater ridership" suggestion (so choice C misidentifies the target). And the argument does not concede defeat (choice E). Choice A correctly labels the bolded claim as evidence supporting the conclusion. The difficulty is keeping the three claims straight: raw data, refined data that answers an objection, and the recommendation they jointly support.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly identifies the role: the injury statistic is factual evidence offered in support of the recommendation to cap rentals. It is not inferred from anything else, which is the mark of a premise rather than a conclusion.`,`This confuses evidence with the point it supports (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the recommendation to cap rentals; the injury statistic is one reason for that recommendation.`,`This misidentifies which claim the argument rejects (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The rejected claim is the suggestion that the rise merely reflects greater ridership; the injury statistic is endorsed and used, not rejected.`,`This is tempting because the per-thousand-rides statistic relates to the injury claim, but the direction is wrong (trap: wrong-conclusion). The bolded claim is raw data, not something inferred; nothing in the argument derives it from another claim.`,`This misreads the argument's stance. The author never accepts anything as defeating the conclusion; the rival explanation is rebutted, and the recommendation stands. A concession that defeats the conclusion appears nowhere here.`],generalLesson:`Raw data, refined data that answers an objection, and the final recommendation are three distinct roles. Evidence used to support a conclusion is a premise even when other sentences elaborate on it.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the injury statistic inferred from another claim, or is it a starting observation?`,`Which claim does the argument actually argue against: the injury statistic or the "merely greater ridership" suggestion?`,`Which choice describes a piece of evidence supporting the cap recommendation?`],labels:{topic:`e-scooter injuries`}},{id:`lr-a-012`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C identifies the therefore-marked intermediate conclusion; A mistakes it for the main recommendation, D reverses stance, E half-right but lab studies are also premises. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Neonicotinoid residues have been found in the pollen of treated crops. **These residues are therefore reaching the bees that pollinate those crops.** Laboratory studies show that even low doses of neonicotinoids impair bees' navigation. Since impaired navigation reduces foraging success, these pesticides are probably contributing to colony losses, and regulators should restrict their use near blooming crops.`,stem:`The statement that neonicotinoid residues are reaching the bees that pollinate treated crops plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It states the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is background information that plays no role in the reasoning.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion used to support the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is a claim the argument presents in order to refute it.`},{text:`It is the sole premise from which the main conclusion is directly inferred.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The "therefore" marks the bolded claim as inferred from the pollen finding, and it is then combined with the lab studies to support the verdict about colony losses and the restriction recommendation. A claim that is both supported and supporting is an intermediate conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The word "therefore" is decisive: the bolded claim is inferred from the finding of residues in pollen, so it is a conclusion of a small sub-argument. But the reasoning does not stop there. The bolded claim combines with the laboratory evidence about impaired navigation to support the further verdict that the pesticides are probably contributing to colony losses, which in turn supports the recommendation to restrict use. A claim that is both supported by premises and used to support a further conclusion is an intermediate conclusion, which is choice C. Choice A is wrong because the main conclusion is the recommendation about restricting use (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice B ignores the "therefore" and the claim's supporting role. Choice D reverses the author's stance (trap: wrong-viewpoint): the claim is endorsed, not refuted. Choice E is tempting but false (trap: half-right): the main conclusion also relies on the lab studies, so the bolded claim is not the sole premise.`,choiceExplanations:[`This mistakes a stepping stone for the destination (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the recommendation that regulators restrict use near blooming crops; the bolded claim is one step in the reasoning leading there.`,`This ignores the "therefore," which shows the claim is inferred from the pollen finding, and ignores how it combines with the lab studies to support the verdict. A claim doing that much work is not idle background.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the bolded claim is inferred from the pollen evidence ("therefore") and then used, together with the navigation studies, to support the conclusion about colony losses and the restriction recommendation.`,`This reverses the author's stance (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument endorses the claim that residues reach the bees and builds on it; nothing in the argument disputes or refutes it.`,`This is half right (trap: half-right): the bolded claim does support the main conclusion, but it is not the sole premise. The laboratory findings about impaired navigation are also essential, so "sole premise" is false.`],generalLesson:`A claim marked by "therefore" that is then used to support a further claim is an intermediate conclusion: supported below, supporting above. Check both directions before labeling a claim's role.`,misconceptionTags:[`intermediate-conclusion-trap`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the word "therefore" tell you about where the bolded claim comes from?`,`Does the reasoning stop at the bolded claim, or is it used to get somewhere further?`,`Which choice describes a claim that is both supported and supporting?`],labels:{topic:`neonicotinoids and bees`}},{id:`lr-a-013`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,viewpointComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Critics of congestion pricing argue that **it unfairly burdens low-income drivers who cannot shift their commutes to off-peak hours.** This objection overlooks the toll revenue, which the plan dedicates to improving bus service in lower-income neighborhoods. With those improvements, low-income commuters gain a faster, cheaper alternative to driving. So the fairness objection fails, and the pricing plan should be adopted.`,stem:`The claim that congestion pricing unfairly burdens low-income drivers plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It states the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It provides evidence supporting the adoption of the pricing plan.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion that the toll-revenue point supports.`},{text:`It is a point the argument concedes to the critics before rebutting it.`},{text:`It presents an objection that the argument goes on to rebut.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The bolded claim belongs to the critics, not the author: it is introduced as their objection and then dismantled by the bus-service point, clearing the way for the recommendation. Its role is the target of the rebuttal, which is choice E.`,explanationWalkthrough:`First determine ownership: the bolded claim is explicitly attributed to "critics of congestion pricing," so it is not the author's claim. The author's moves are to point out what the objection overlooks (the dedicated toll revenue), to note the resulting benefit for low-income commuters, and to conclude that the objection fails and the plan should be adopted. The bolded claim is therefore the objection under attack, which is choice E. Choice A is wrong because the main conclusion is the recommendation to adopt the plan (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice B reverses the role (trap: opposite): the claim counts against the plan, so it cannot be evidence for adopting it. Choice C mislabels it as a supported intermediate step. Choice D is the subtlest trap (trap: half-right): the argument does engage the objection, but it never concedes its truth; "this objection overlooks" and "the fairness objection fails" are rejections, not concessions.`,choiceExplanations:[`This mistakes the critics' claim for the author's verdict (trap: wrong-conclusion, wrong-viewpoint). The main conclusion is the recommendation to adopt the pricing plan; the bolded claim is the objection that recommendation overcomes.`,`This reverses the claim's direction (trap: opposite). The burden-on-drivers claim is a reason against the plan, so it cannot function as evidence for adopting the plan; the author cites it only to knock it down.`,`This mislabels the claim as a supported step toward the conclusion (trap: wrong-conclusion). Nothing in the argument supports the burden claim; the toll-revenue point is deployed against it, not for it.`,`This is the subtlest distractor (trap: half-right). The argument does take the objection seriously enough to answer it, but it never concedes the objection's truth: it says the objection "overlooks" key facts and "fails," which is rebuttal, not concession.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the bolded claim is the critics' fairness objection, introduced so the argument can rebut it with the dedicated-revenue and improved-bus-service points before concluding the plan should be adopted.`],generalLesson:`Always check a claim's ownership before assigning its role. An objection attributed to critics and then answered is the target of the argument, not its support, and answering an objection is not the same as conceding it.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Who does the bolded claim belong to: the author or the critics?`,`Does the author agree with the claim, or argue that it "fails"?`,`Which choice describes a claim introduced so the argument can knock it down?`],labels:{topic:`congestion pricing fairness`}},{id:`lr-a-014`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B identifies the therefore-marked recommendation as main conclusion; A reverses support direction, C confuses should with will, D misattributes viewpoint, E invents further structure. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Teenagers' sleep cycles shift later during puberty, yet most high schools start classes before 8 a.m. Studies link early start times to lower grades and more tardiness. **High schools should therefore start the school day later.** A later start would align school schedules with students' biology.`,stem:`The statement that high schools should start the school day later plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is a premise supporting the claim about teenagers' sleep cycles.`},{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion, supported by the other statements.`},{text:`It is a prediction about what schools will do in the future.`},{text:`It is an objection raised by opponents of later start times.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion supporting a further recommendation.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The "therefore" marks the bolded recommendation as the point inferred from the sleep-cycle facts and the studies, and the final sentence gives one more reason for it. Since everything supports it and nothing follows from it, it is the main conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The "therefore" in the bolded sentence signals that it is inferred from the preceding claims about sleep cycles and early start times, and the final sentence adds a supporting benefit. The direction of support runs toward the bolded recommendation from every other sentence, and no further claim is drawn from it. That is the profile of a main conclusion, which is choice B. Choice A reverses the support (trap: wrong-conclusion): the sleep-cycle facts support the recommendation, not the other way around. Choice C misreads a "should" recommendation as a "will" prediction (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice D misattributes the claim to opponents (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice E invents a further recommendation that the argument never makes. The 'therefore' plus the supporting final sentence leaves no doubt about the direction of support.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the direction of support (trap: wrong-conclusion). The facts about sleep cycles and tardiness are premises offered to support the bolded recommendation; the recommendation does not support those facts.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the bolded "should" claim is the main conclusion, inferred from the sleep and performance evidence ("therefore") and further supported by the alignment-with-biology point.`,`This confuses a recommendation with a prediction (trap: wrong-conclusion). "Should start later" says what schools ought to do, not what they will do; the argument advocates a policy rather than forecasting behavior.`,`This misattributes the claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The bolded sentence is the author's own position, endorsed and defended, not an objection from opponents. Readers who skim for tone rather than ownership fall for this, since the sentence sits where objections often appear.`,`This invents structure the argument lacks (trap: wrong-conclusion). An intermediate conclusion must support something further, but nothing in the argument follows from the bolded recommendation.`],generalLesson:`"Should" claims marked by "therefore" are usually the main conclusion when everything else is evidence for them. Do not confuse a recommendation with a prediction, and always verify the direction of support.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`prediction-as-conclusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "therefore" tell you about whether the bolded claim is a reason or a result?`,`Does anything in the argument follow from the bolded claim, or does everything lead into it?`,`Which choice describes the final point everything supports?`],labels:{topic:`school start times`}},{id:`lr-a-015`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C identifies the general principle as supporting premise; A mistakes it for the conclusion, B misreads generality level, D half-right but the principle is endorsed not conceded, E overreads as prediction. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The downtown Rialto theater has stood empty for a decade. **A city that lets its historic landmarks decay loses part of what makes it distinctive.** Restoring the Rialto would cost less than building a comparable new venue, and restored theaters in similar cities have anchored successful downtown revivals. The city should therefore fund the Rialto's restoration.`,stem:`The claim that a city which lets its historic landmarks decay loses part of what makes it distinctive plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It states the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is a factual observation about the Rialto theater.`},{text:`It is a general principle offered as support for the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is an objection that the argument seeks to refute.`},{text:`It is a prediction about what will happen if the theater is not restored.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The bolded sentence is a broad normative principle, not a fact about the Rialto, and it is deployed as a reason to fund the restoration: letting landmarks decay costs a city its distinctiveness, so the city should act. That is a supporting principle, which is choice C.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Note the level of generality. The bolded claim is about cities in general, not about the Rialto specifically, so it cannot be a mere observation about the theater (choice B is wrong). It is not the recommendation to fund the restoration; that recommendation comes in the final sentence with "therefore" (so choice A mistakes a supporting principle for the conclusion, trap: wrong-conclusion). The claim is endorsed, not attacked, so it is not an objection (choice D is wrong). And it speaks in general terms about what a city loses, not in predictive terms about the Rialto's future (choice E overreads it). Its function is to supply a general reason: because landmark decay costs distinctiveness, the city has reason to fund restoration. A general claim used as a reason for a specific conclusion is a supporting principle, which is choice C. The difficulty is that the principle sounds grand enough to be a conclusion, but the "therefore" recommendation is what it serves.`,choiceExplanations:[`This mistakes a supporting principle for the verdict it supports (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the final sentence's recommendation to fund the restoration; the bolded principle is one reason for that recommendation.`,`This misreads the claim's level of generality. The bolded sentence is about cities in general, not a factual observation about the Rialto; the Rialto-specific facts are the vacancy, the cost comparison, and the other cities' revivals.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the bolded sentence states a general principle that functions as a premise supporting the recommendation to fund the restoration. The principle's generality is exactly what lets it cover the Rialto's case without being about the Rialto.`,`This reverses the author's stance (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument endorses the principle and relies on it; nothing in the argument disputes or refutes it. An objection would be introduced to be answered, but this principle is introduced to be used.`,`This overreads the sentence as a forecast. The claim is a general evaluative principle about what decay costs a city, not a prediction about the Rialto's particular future.`],generalLesson:`General principles in an argument usually function as premises, not conclusions, when a "therefore" recommendation follows them. Check the level of generality and ask what the grand-sounding claim is being used to support.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the bolded claim about the Rialto specifically, or about cities in general?`,`What does the final "therefore" sentence recommend, and what reasons feed into it?`,`Which choice describes a general claim used as a reason for that recommendation?`],labels:{topic:`historic theater preservation`}},{id:`lr-a-016`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D captures the true-but-outweighed concession; C is the sharpest trap but That is true blocks rejected-as-false, A wrong-conclusion, B opposite, E mislabels inference. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The city planted 10,000 street trees to reduce summer heat. **Critics note that newly planted saplings provide almost no shade for their first several years.** That is true, but the program aims at long-term cooling, and mature trees from the same program are already lowering temperatures on several blocks. The planting program should therefore continue.`,stem:`The statement that newly planted saplings provide almost no shade for their first several years plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is evidence offered in support of continuing the program.`},{text:`It is a claim the argument rejects as false.`},{text:`It is a consideration against the conclusion that the argument treats as outweighed.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion inferred from the temperature data.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The "That is true, but" construction is the giveaway: the author accepts the critics' point yet judges it insufficient against the long-term goal and the mature-tree results. That is a counterconsideration acknowledged but outweighed, which is choice D.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Watch how the author handles the bolded claim. It is attributed to critics and counts against the program, since treeless years of saplings seem to undercut a cooling program. But the author responds "That is true, but," accepting the fact while arguing it does not defeat the conclusion: the goal is long-term cooling, and mature trees already show results. So the claim is acknowledged as true yet treated as outweighed, which is choice D. Choice A is wrong because the conclusion is the recommendation to continue the program (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice B reverses its direction (trap: opposite): a point against the program cannot be evidence for continuing it. Choice C is the sharpest trap (trap: half-right): the author does push back, but "That is true" shows the claim is accepted as factual, not rejected as false. Choice E mislabels it as inferred; it is reported from the critics, not derived from the temperature data.`,choiceExplanations:[`This mistakes a critics' observation for the author's verdict (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the recommendation that the planting program continue; the sapling point is material the argument deals with along the way.`,`This reverses the claim's direction (trap: opposite). The sapling observation counts against the program's short-term effectiveness, so it cannot function as evidence for continuing the program.`,`This is the sharpest distractor (trap: half-right): the author does argue against the critics' overall case, but the words "That is true" show the sapling claim itself is accepted as factual. It is outweighed, not refuted.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the argument acknowledges the critics' true-but-limited point and then treats it as outweighed by the program's long-term aim and the mature trees' results.`,`This mislabels the claim as derived from evidence (trap: wrong-conclusion). The sapling point is reported as the critics' observation, not inferred from the temperature readings on the mature-tree blocks.`],generalLesson:`"That is true, but" signals a concession that is accepted yet outweighed, not a claim rejected as false. Distinguish rebutting an objection's force from denying its facts.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author say the critics' point is false, or say something like "true, but"?`,`If the author accepts the point, what does the "but" do to its importance?`,`Which choice describes a true point that is acknowledged yet outweighed?`],labels:{topic:`urban tree planting`}},{id:`lr-a-017`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A identifies the turnover drop as the explanandum; B mistakes phenomenon for explanation, C half-right but the price-rival elimination is essential, D mislabels, E misreads past as forecast. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Several restaurants replaced tipping with a fixed service charge. **In nearly every case, staff turnover fell sharply within a year.** Higher menu prices alone cannot explain this, since restaurants that simply raised prices without changing tipping saw no such drop. The most plausible explanation is that predictable income keeps workers from leaving, so the service-charge model likely improves staff retention.`,stem:`The claim that staff turnover fell sharply within a year at restaurants adopting a service charge plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the observed phenomenon that the argument's main conclusion purports to explain.`},{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It is a premise that directly establishes the main conclusion without further reasoning.`},{text:`It is an objection that the argument answers with the menu-price comparison.`},{text:`It is a prediction about restaurants that have not yet adopted service charges.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The turnover drop is the puzzling observation; the menu-price comparison rules out a rival explanation, and the final sentence offers the best explanation of the observation. The bolded claim is the explanandum, the thing to be explained, which is choice A.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Recognize the explanation structure. The argument does not try to prove that turnover fell; it takes the fall as a given observation and asks why it happened. The menu-price comparison eliminates a competing explanation, and the final sentence proposes the best one: predictable income retains staff. The bolded claim is therefore the phenomenon being explained, the explanandum, which is choice A. Choice B is wrong because the main conclusion is the explanatory claim about predictable income and retention (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice C is tempting but imprecise (trap: half-right): the turnover drop is evidence, but it does not directly establish the conclusion; the elimination of the price rival and the inference to the best explanation do the work. Choice D mislabels the observation as an objection. Choice E misreads a past observation as a future prediction.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly identifies the role: the sharp fall in turnover is the observed result the argument sets out to explain, with the menu-price comparison eliminating a rival explanation and the final sentence offering the best one.`,`This mistakes the phenomenon for the explanation (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the final sentence's claim that the service-charge model likely improves retention by making income predictable.`,`This is half right (trap: half-right): the turnover drop is indeed evidence, but it does not directly establish the conclusion by itself. The argument needs the extra step of ruling out the menu-price rival before inferring the best explanation.`,`This mislabels the observation as hostile (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The turnover drop is the author's own evidence and the very thing to be explained; the menu-price comparison answers a rival explanation, not an objection to the observation.`,`This misreads a reported past result as a forecast. The claim describes what happened within a year at restaurants that already switched; it predicts nothing about restaurants that have not switched.`],generalLesson:`In explanation arguments, distinguish the phenomenon being explained from the explanation itself. Evidence that something happened is the starting point; the conclusion is the proposed why.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-premise-confusion`,`prediction-as-conclusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the argument try to prove that turnover fell, or take that as given and ask why?`,`What work does the menu-price comparison do: prove the drop, or rule out a rival reason for it?`,`Which choice describes the observation that needs explaining?`],labels:{topic:`restaurant service charges`}},{id:`lr-a-018`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,linguisticComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E captures indirect rebuttal support; B is the dangerous too-strong near miss since the parallel undercuts collapse rather than proving success, A wrong-conclusion, C opposite, D ignores rebuttal chain. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A proposed law would require apps to obtain explicit consent before sharing location data. Industry groups warn that this will cripple ad-supported free apps. **But apps survived earlier consent requirements for camera and microphone access without collapsing.** That history suggests the industry's warning is overblown. Because users cannot meaningfully avoid location tracking as things stand, and the industry's objection lacks force, lawmakers should pass the consent requirement.`,stem:`The claim that apps survived earlier consent requirements for camera and microphone access plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It states the argument's main conclusion.`},{text:`It provides direct evidence that the proposed law will succeed.`},{text:`It describes a concession the argument makes to industry groups.`},{text:`It is a premise with no bearing on whether the law should pass.`},{text:`It rebuts an objection to the proposed law, supporting the main conclusion.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The bolded historical parallel is deployed against the industry's collapse warning: if apps survived past consent rules, the warning is overblown. Knocking down the objection removes a barrier to the recommendation, so the claim indirectly supports the main conclusion by rebuttal.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the dialectic. The industry warns the law will cripple free apps. The bolded claim answers with a parallel case: apps survived camera and microphone consent rules. The next sentence draws the moral: the warning is overblown. The final sentence then combines the defanged objection with the users' inability to avoid tracking to recommend passing the law. So the bolded claim's role is to rebut an objection, which in turn supports the recommendation. That is choice E. Choice A is wrong because the main conclusion is the final recommendation (trap: wrong-conclusion). Choice B is the dangerous near-miss (trap: half-right, too-strong): the parallel does not directly evidence the law's success; it only undercuts the prediction of collapse, which is a weaker, indirect contribution. Choice C reverses the stance (trap: opposite): the claim is wielded against industry, not conceded to it. Choice D ignores the rebuttal chain entirely.`,choiceExplanations:[`This mistakes a rebuttal for the verdict (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the final recommendation that lawmakers pass the requirement; the historical parallel is one move in the reasoning leading there.`,`This is the most dangerous distractor (trap: half-right, too-strong). The parallel does lend support, but only indirectly: it undercuts the collapse prediction rather than directly evidencing that the law will succeed. "Direct evidence of success" claims more than the rebuttal delivers.`,`This reverses the argument's stance (trap: opposite, wrong-viewpoint). The claim is used against the industry groups' warning, not granted to them; a concession would give ground, whereas this takes ground away.`,`This ignores the rebuttal structure. The parallel is introduced precisely to deflate the industry's objection, which clears the way for the recommendation, so it plainly bears on whether the law should pass.`,`This correctly identifies the role: the historical parallel rebuts the industry's collapse warning ("that history suggests the warning is overblown"), and defusing that objection supports the final recommendation to pass the law.`],generalLesson:`Rebutting an objection is an indirect way of supporting a conclusion: it removes a reason against rather than adding a reason for. Do not upgrade indirect rebuttal into direct proof when labeling a claim's role.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`conclusion-premise-confusion`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the industry's warning, and what does the historical parallel do to it?`,`Does the parallel prove the law will work, or only undercut the prediction of collapse?`,`Which choice describes a claim that supports the conclusion by rebutting an objection?`],labels:{topic:`app location-data consent law`}},{id:`lr-a-019`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: clean counterexample method; credited B unique. A answers-different-question, C keyword-match, D unsupported, E describes absent circularity. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`My uncle insists that dropping out of college is the key to business success, pointing to several famous entrepreneurs who left school early. But my aunt built a thriving company after finishing her degree, as did two of her business partners. So dropping out is hardly a prerequisite for business success.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It argues by analogy between college and business.`},{text:`It offers counterexamples to a general claim.`},{text:`It appeals to the authority of successful entrepreneurs.`},{text:`It attacks the uncle's character rather than his argument.`},{text:`It assumes the truth of the very claim it sets out to prove.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The uncle makes a universal-sounding claim that dropping out is the key to success, and the argument answers with specific cases of graduates who succeeded, which is the textbook counterexample method. Choice B names it; the rest describe moves the argument never makes.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the target and the move. The target is the uncle's general claim that dropping out is the key to business success. The move is to produce cases, the aunt and her partners, of people who succeeded without dropping out. One genuine counterexample refutes a "key" or "prerequisite" claim, which is exactly what the conclusion asserts. That is choice B. Choice A is wrong because no analogy is drawn (trap: answers-different-question). Choice C is wrong because the aunt's success is used as data, not as an expert pronouncement; "successful" is a keyword-match (trap: keyword-match). Choice D is wrong because nothing about the uncle's character is mentioned (trap: unsupported). Choice E describes circular reasoning, which is absent: the conclusion follows from the cases, not from assuming itself.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because college and business are both discussed, but no analogy is drawn between them (trap: answers-different-question). The argument compares cases within business success, not across two domains.`,`This correctly names the method: the argument refutes the uncle's general claim by presenting counterexamples, graduates who built thriving companies, showing that dropping out is not a prerequisite for success.`,`This mislabels the use of the aunt's case (trap: keyword-match). Her success functions as a counterexample, a data point, not as an authoritative pronouncement to be believed on prestige.`,`Nothing in the argument addresses the uncle's character, motives, or credibility (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The attack is on his general claim, using contrary cases, not on him personally.`,`This describes begging the question, which the argument does not commit. The conclusion that dropping out is not a prerequisite is inferred from the counterexamples, not assumed at the outset.`],generalLesson:`A single genuine counterexample defeats a universal or "key/prerequisite" claim. When an argument answers a generalization with contrary cases, its method is counterexample, not analogy or authority.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`keyword-match`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the uncle claiming, and what kind of claim is it: about one person or about what it takes in general?`,`What does the author offer in response: a comparison, an expert, or specific contrary cases?`,`Which choice names the move of answering a general claim with contrary cases?`],labels:{topic:`college dropouts and business success`}},{id:`lr-a-020`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: explicit is-like analogy; credited D unique. A unsupported, B absent reductio, C keyword-match, E too-strong. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Learning to manage money is like learning to swim: you cannot really learn it from a book alone; you need supervised practice with real stakes. Just as swim instruction pairs lessons with time in the pool, schools should pair financial lessons with real-budget exercises in which students manage actual funds under guidance.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`citing statistical evidence about student financial literacy.`},{text:`showing that an opponent's claim leads to an absurd result.`},{text:`appealing to the authority of swimming instructors.`},{text:`drawing an analogy between learning to swim and learning to manage money.`},{text:`proving that classroom instruction is entirely worthless.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument explicitly maps swimming instruction onto financial education: lessons plus supervised real-stakes practice in both domains, leading to the recommendation for real-budget exercises. That is reasoning by analogy, which is choice D.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The method is announced in the first clause: "Learning to manage money is like learning to swim." The argument then develops the parallel, supervised practice with real stakes in both cases, and transfers the instructional model from swimming to finance. That is the analogy method, choice D. Choice A is wrong because no statistics appear (trap: unsupported). Choice B describes a reductio the argument never attempts. Choice C misreads the mention of swim instruction as an authority appeal (trap: keyword-match): instructors are not cited as experts to believe but as a model to copy. Choice E overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong): the argument says book learning alone is insufficient, not that classroom instruction is worthless. The explicit 'is like' framing makes analogy the unmistakable method here.`,choiceExplanations:[`No statistics or studies are cited anywhere in the argument, so this describes a method that was never used. The evidence is a parallel case, not data.`,`The argument never derives an absurd consequence from anyone's claim. It builds a positive parallel between two kinds of learning rather than demolishing an opponent's position.`,`Swim instructors are mentioned as a model of good instruction, not as authorities whose word settles the matter (trap: keyword-match). The reasoning rests on the similarity between the two learning tasks, not on expert testimony.`,`This correctly names the method: the argument draws an explicit analogy between learning to swim and learning to manage money, then transfers the supervised-practice model from the former to the latter.`,`This overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong). The argument claims book learning alone is insufficient and should be paired with practice, not that classroom instruction is entirely worthless.`],generalLesson:`When an argument says "X is like Y" and transfers a feature from Y to X, its method is analogy. Do not confuse mentioning practitioners with appealing to their authority.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`keyword-match`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two activities does the first sentence explicitly compare?`,`Is the argument citing data, quoting experts, or mapping one learning process onto another?`,`Which choice names the "X is like Y" move?`],labels:{topic:`financial education`}},{id:`lr-a-021`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A names the alternative-explanation method; B too-strong since conclusion is only unproven, C invents authority, D is the chains own move, E invents math challenge. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A coffee chain credits its new loyalty program for a 20 percent jump in sales at its downtown stores. But a large office tower opened next door to those stores in the same month the program launched, bringing thousands of potential new customers within walking distance. The sales jump may owe more to the new neighbors than to the loyalty program, so the program's effectiveness remains unproven.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It offers an alternative explanation for the effect, challenging a causal claim.`},{text:`It demonstrates that loyalty programs never increase sales.`},{text:`It appeals to the authority of the office tower's developers.`},{text:`It argues that correlation alone proves the loyalty program worked.`},{text:`It shows that the reported sales figures are mathematically impossible.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The chain infers the program caused the sales jump; the argument counters by offering the office tower as a rival cause and concludes the program's effectiveness is unproven. Proposing an alternative explanation to undercut a causal claim is the method, which is choice A.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The target is a causal claim: the loyalty program caused the 20 percent sales jump. The method is to introduce a competing cause, the office tower opening the same month, that could also explain the jump. Because the rival explanation fits the timing, the original causal inference is weakened, and the argument modestly concludes the program's effectiveness "remains unproven" rather than claiming the program failed. That is choice A. Choice B overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong): the argument says unproven, not disproven. Choice C invents an authority appeal. Choice D describes the chain's reasoning, not the argument's (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice E invents a mathematical challenge that never occurs. Note the modesty of the conclusion: 'remains unproven' is weaker than 'disproven,' which is exactly what a rival-cause objection earns.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly names the method: the argument challenges the chain's causal claim by proposing the office tower as an alternative explanation for the same sales jump, leaving the program's effectiveness unproven.`,`This overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong). The argument concludes only that the program's effectiveness remains unproven, not that loyalty programs never work; proposing a rival cause weakens the inference without refuting the program.`,`No developer or authority is cited or relied upon. The office tower is introduced as a competing cause, a matter of timing and foot traffic, not as expert testimony.`,`This describes the coffee chain's reasoning (trap: wrong-viewpoint), not the argument's. The argument criticizes exactly this move from correlation to causation rather than making it.`,`The argument never questions the arithmetic of the 20 percent figure. Its challenge is causal, not mathematical: what explains the jump, not whether the jump occurred.`],generalLesson:`A leading way to weaken a causal claim is to propose an alternative cause that fits the same observations. Note the modest conclusion such a move earns: "unproven," not "disproven."`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What causal claim is the coffee chain making about the sales jump?`,`What other event happened at the same time that could also explain the jump?`,`Which choice describes offering a rival explanation to challenge a causal claim?`],labels:{topic:`loyalty program sales`}},{id:`lr-a-022`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E names the turning-the-tables move; C is a designed causal-alternative trap but no rival cause is proposed, A half-right since records are data not authority, B and D absent. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The school board credits the new homework policy for rising test scores, pointing out that scores have climbed each year since the policy began. But district records show the upward trend started three years before the policy was adopted and has continued at exactly the same pace. If anything, the board's own data suggest the policy has made no difference.`,stem:`The author's method of reasoning is best described as`,choices:[{text:`appealing to the authority of district records to settle the issue.`},{text:`attacking the personal credibility of the board members.`},{text:`offering an alternative cause of the rising test scores.`},{text:`generalizing from the experience of a single school.`},{text:`arguing that evidence cited for a claim actually undermines that claim.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The board offers the rising scores as proof its policy works; the author shows the same data, with the trend predating the policy, undercuts that proof. Turning cited evidence against the claim it was meant to support is the method, which is choice E.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The board's evidence is the rising test scores, offered to prove the homework policy works. The author does not dispute the scores; instead the author adds the crucial timing detail that the trend began three years before the policy and never accelerated. That detail converts the board's own evidence into evidence against the board's conclusion. This "turning the tables" move is choice E. Choice A is tempting because records are cited (trap: half-right), but the records are data, not an authority being deferred to, and citing data is not the distinctive method. Choice B is wrong because no personal attack occurs (trap: unsupported). Choice C is tempting (trap: causal-alternative), but the author proposes no rival cause of the rise; the author merely undercuts the policy as the cause. Choice D invents a single-school generalization.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is half right (trap: half-right): district records are cited, but as data, not as an authority whose say-so settles anything. The distinctive method is what the author does with the data, turning it against the board's conclusion.`,`Nothing in the argument addresses the board members' character, motives, or credibility. The critique targets the inference from the scores, not the people making it.`,`This is tempting (trap: causal-alternative), but the author never proposes what did cause the rise. The move is purely undercutting: showing the timing of the trend is inconsistent with crediting the policy.`,`The argument concerns district-wide records over several years, not one school's experience. No such narrow generalization occurs. Method answers must describe what the author actually did, and no single-school inference appears anywhere here.`,`This correctly names the method: the author takes the very evidence the board cited, adds the timing detail, and argues that this evidence counts against the board's claim rather than for it.`],generalLesson:`You can defeat an argument without disputing its evidence by showing the evidence points the other way. Distinguish this "turning the tables" move from proposing a rival cause, which is a different method.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author dispute the test-score data, or accept it and add a detail about timing?`,`What does the fact that the trend predates the policy do to the board's inference?`,`Which choice describes using someone's own evidence against their conclusion?`],labels:{topic:`homework policy and test scores`}},{id:`lr-a-023`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C names elimination; A scope shift, B keyword-match since logs are data, D invents analogy, E opposite. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`An orchard owner noticed that honeybee visits to her apple blossoms had nearly stopped. She first suspected the new pesticide, but the decline began weeks before it was ever sprayed. She then considered a late frost, but temperature logs show no frost during the bloom. The only remaining explanation is the tall windbreak installed that spring, which now blocks the bees' flight path from the neighboring hives.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It generalizes from a single orchard to all orchards.`},{text:`It appeals to the authority of temperature logs.`},{text:`It rules out competing explanations to support the remaining one.`},{text:`It argues by analogy between windbreaks and pesticides.`},{text:`It shows that the observed decline never really happened.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The owner lists candidate causes, pesticide and frost, and eliminates each with timing evidence, leaving the windbreak as the remaining explanation. Systematically ruling out rivals to support what is left is the method, which is choice C.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The structure is elimination. Three candidate explanations are in play: the pesticide, the frost, and the windbreak. The pesticide is ruled out because the decline predates its use; the frost is ruled out by the temperature logs. With the rivals eliminated, the windbreak stands as the remaining explanation. That is choice C. Choice A is wrong because no generalization beyond this orchard is drawn (trap: scope-shift). Choice B mislabels the logs as an authority appeal (trap: keyword-match): they are data used to eliminate a hypothesis. Choice D invents an analogy. Choice E contradicts the argument, which accepts the decline as real and explains it (trap: opposite). Each elimination uses dated evidence, timing for the pesticide and logs for the frost, which is why the method is rigorous rather than speculative.`,choiceExplanations:[`The argument never extends its conclusion beyond this orchard. Its method is explaining one observed decline, not generalizing to all orchards. Elimination arguments explain a particular case; the scope never widens beyond the owner's trees.`,`The temperature logs function as data eliminating the frost hypothesis, not as an authority whose pronouncement is trusted (trap: keyword-match). Citing measurements is not appealing to authority.`,`This correctly names the method: candidate explanations are proposed and then eliminated one by one with evidence, leaving the windbreak as the supported remaining explanation.`,`No analogy is drawn between windbreaks and pesticides. They are treated as competing hypotheses to be tested against the timing evidence, not as similar cases.`,`This gets the argument backwards (trap: opposite). The decline in bee visits is accepted as the phenomenon to be explained; the reasoning explains it rather than denying it.`],generalLesson:`Elimination is a legitimate method: rule out each rival with evidence and the remaining explanation gains support. Its strength depends on the candidate list being complete, which is why "the only remaining explanation" language matters.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How many possible causes does the owner consider, and what happens to each?`,`What evidence eliminates the pesticide and the frost?`,`Which choice describes eliminating rivals to leave one explanation standing?`],labels:{topic:`orchard bee decline`}},{id:`lr-a-024`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B names the distinction move; A reverses the argument, C answers a different efficacy question, D invents popularity, E opposite. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Critics call the proposed tax on sugary drinks paternalistic government overreach. But there is an important difference between banning a product outright and pricing in the health costs that sugary drinks impose on the public. The tax does only the latter, leaving consumers free to choose. So the charge of paternalism misses its mark.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It denies that sugary drinks impose health costs on the public.`},{text:`It draws a distinction that undermines the critics' objection.`},{text:`It argues that the tax will reduce sugary drink consumption.`},{text:`It appeals to popular support for the proposed tax.`},{text:`It shows that all taxation is paternalistic.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The critics' objection treats the tax as paternalistic overreach; the author answers by distinguishing outright bans from pricing in social costs, placing the tax on the non-paternalistic side. Defusing an objection with a distinction is the method, which is choice B.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The objection equates the tax with paternalism. The author's move is to split the category the objection relies on: banning a product versus pricing its public health costs are different things, and the tax is only the second. Because the objection's force depends on treating the tax as the paternalistic kind of intervention, the distinction removes that force. That is choice B. Choice A gets the argument backwards (trap: opposite): the author affirmatively relies on the health costs to justify the tax. Choice C is tempting but off-target (trap: answers-different-question): whether the tax reduces consumption is an efficacy question, while the argument addresses the paternalism charge. Choice D invents a popularity appeal. Choice E inverts the distinction's upshot (trap: opposite).`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the argument (trap: opposite). The author explicitly invokes the health costs sugary drinks impose on the public as the justification for pricing them in; denying those costs would destroy the author's own case.`,`This correctly names the method: the author distinguishes banning a product from pricing its social costs, shows the tax is only the latter, and thereby undercuts the paternalism objection.`,`This answers a different question (trap: answers-different-question). The argument defends the tax against the charge of paternalism; it makes no claim about whether the tax will actually reduce consumption.`,`No poll, majority, or popular sentiment is mentioned. The defense rests on a conceptual distinction, not on how many people favor the tax. Choosing this means mistaking the topic for the method, since public opinion plays no role in the reasoning.`,`This inverts the argument's point (trap: opposite). The distinction is drawn precisely to show the tax is not paternalistic; concluding that all taxation is paternalistic would side with the critics.`],generalLesson:`A powerful response to an objection is to distinguish two things the objection lumps together, then show the case at hand falls on the innocent side. Check that the distinction actually tracks the objection's logic.`,misconceptionTags:[`answers-different-question`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the objection assume the tax is an example of?`,`What two different things does the author separate in response?`,`Which choice describes defeating an objection by drawing a distinction?`],labels:{topic:`sugary drink tax`}},{id:`lr-a-025`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-assumption`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D names the internal self-defeating critique; A invents an alternative, B reverses research treatment, C invents history, E denies the goal. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`To ease rush-hour congestion, the mayor proposes widening the downtown expressway from six lanes to ten. Yet decades of traffic research show that added lanes quickly fill with new drivers attracted by the faster trip, a phenomenon known as induced demand. By the mayor's own goal of reducing congestion, then, the widening proposal defeats itself.`,stem:`The argument's method of reasoning is best described as`,choices:[{text:`proposing a superior alternative to the mayor's plan.`},{text:`disputing the accuracy of the traffic research.`},{text:`showing that the mayor's proposal has already been tried and failed.`},{text:`demonstrating that a proposal undermines its own stated goal.`},{text:`arguing that congestion is not a serious problem.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument takes the mayor's own goal, less congestion, and shows via induced demand that widening produces more congestion, so the proposal is self-defeating by its own standard. That internal critique is choice D.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is an internal critique: it judges the proposal by the proposer's own standard rather than importing an outside objection. The mayor's goal is reducing congestion. The induced-demand research shows widening invites more drivers, increasing congestion. Therefore the proposal works against its own stated goal. That is choice D. Choice A is wrong because no alternative is proposed (trap: answers-different-question). Choice B is wrong because the research is endorsed, not disputed (trap: opposite). Choice C invents a history of past failure. Choice E is wrong because the argument accepts congestion as a problem worth solving; it attacks the means, not the goal. Internal critiques are powerful because they need no outside values: the plan is measured against its own promise and found wanting.`,choiceExplanations:[`No alternative policy is suggested anywhere. The argument confines itself to showing the widening plan fails by the mayor's own standard, without proposing what should be done instead.`,`This reverses the argument's treatment of the research (trap: opposite). The induced-demand findings are accepted as true and used as the lever of the critique, not questioned.`,`The argument cites general research on induced demand, not a prior widening of this expressway. No claim about this proposal having been tried before appears.`,`This correctly names the method: the argument shows that, by the mayor's own stated goal of reducing congestion, the widening proposal is self-defeating because added lanes induce more traffic.`,`The argument never denies that congestion matters. It accepts the goal of easing congestion and argues only that widening is a self-defeating way to pursue it.`],generalLesson:`Judging a proposal by its own stated goal is a strong internal critique: it needs no outside values. Look for "by its own standard" reasoning when an argument turns a plan's goal against it.`,misconceptionTags:[`answers-different-question`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the mayor's own goal for the widening proposal?`,`What does the induced-demand research say widening actually produces?`,`Which choice describes showing a plan defeats its own purpose?`],labels:{topic:`expressway widening`}},{id:`lr-a-026`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A names principle application; B answers-different-question, C reverses case function, D keyword-match since code is a premise not authority, E invents reductio. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A hospital fired a nurse after she reported that her unit was reusing single-use syringes. The hospital's own ethics code states that employees who report safety violations in good faith must be protected from retaliation. The nurse's report was made in good faith and concerned a genuine safety violation. She should therefore have been protected, not fired.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It applies a general principle to a particular case.`},{text:`It argues by analogy between hospitals and other workplaces.`},{text:`It offers a counterexample to a general principle.`},{text:`It appeals to the authority of the hospital's administrators.`},{text:`It shows that the ethics code leads to an absurd result.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument states a general rule from the ethics code, establishes that the nurse's case satisfies the rule's conditions, and draws the verdict the rule requires. Applying a general principle to a specific case is the method, which is choice A.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The structure is principle plus subsumption. The principle: good-faith reporters of safety violations must be protected. The subsumption: the nurse reported in good faith about a genuine safety violation, so her case falls under the principle. The conclusion applies the principle's verdict to her case. That is choice A. Choice B is wrong because no second workplace is discussed (trap: answers-different-question). Choice C reverses the relationship (trap: opposite): the case is used to invoke the principle, not to refute it. Choice D mislabels the ethics code as an authority appeal (trap: keyword-match): the code functions as a premise-principle, and the reasoning is the application, not deference. Choice E invents a reductio. Two-step structure, rule then subsumption, is the signature of principle-application reasoning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly names the method: a general principle from the ethics code is stated, the nurse's situation is shown to satisfy its conditions, and the principle's verdict is applied to her case.`,`No comparison with other workplaces occurs. The argument works entirely within this hospital's code and this nurse's case, so no analogy is drawn. Analogy requires two cases mapped onto each other, and only one case exists here.`,`This reverses the case's function (trap: opposite). A counterexample would refute the principle; here the case confirms the principle applies and the conclusion enforces it.`,`The ethics code is used as a stated principle serving as a premise, not as an expert to be believed on authority (trap: keyword-match). The method is the logical application of that principle to the facts.`,`No absurd consequence is derived from the code. The argument treats the code as correct and shows the firing violates it, which is the opposite of a reductio.`],generalLesson:`Principle-application arguments have two steps: state the rule, then show the case meets its conditions. Do not confuse citing a rule as a premise with appealing to authority.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`opposite`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What general rule does the argument start from, and where does it come from?`,`Does the nurse's situation match the rule's conditions?`,`Which choice describes applying a general rule to a specific case?`],labels:{topic:`whistleblower protection`}},{id:`lr-a-027`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-assumption`,`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Critics of raising the minimum wage contend that it will slightly reduce total employment. But even if that is granted, the policy would still raise total earnings among low-wage workers, which is its actual goal. So the employment objection, even taken at face value, gives no reason to reject the policy.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It disputes the factual claim that employment would fall.`},{text:`It attacks the motives of the policy's critics.`},{text:`It grants an objection for the sake of argument and shows the conclusion stands anyway.`},{text:`It demonstrates that the policy has no negative consequences whatsoever.`},{text:`It concedes that the employment objection defeats the policy.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The author explicitly declines to contest the employment claim ("even if that is granted") and argues the policy still achieves its real goal, so the objection fails to touch the conclusion. Granting a point arguendo to show it does not matter is the method, which is choice C.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The key phrases are "even if that is granted" and "even taken at face value." The author does not deny the critics' factual claim; the author assumes it arguendo and shows it is irrelevant to the policy's actual goal of raising total low-wage earnings. If the objection is true and the conclusion still stands, the objection gives no reason to reject the policy. That is choice C. Choice A is the most dangerous trap (trap: opposite): disputing the employment claim is exactly what the argument refrains from doing. Choice B invents a personal attack. Choice D overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong): the author grants a negative consequence (slightly reduced employment) and argues it does not defeat the policy, which is not the same as claiming no downsides. Choice E inverts the verdict (trap: opposite).`,choiceExplanations:[`This describes the opposite of the argument's move (trap: opposite). The author explicitly grants the employment claim arguendo ("even if that is granted") rather than disputing it; the power of the method comes from not needing to dispute it.`,`Nothing about the critics' motives, sincerity, or character is discussed. The argument engages the objection's logic while granting its factual premise, which is not an attack on any person.`,`This correctly names the method: the objection is granted for the sake of argument, and the author shows the policy's conclusion survives anyway because the objection misses the policy's actual goal.`,`This overstates the conclusion (trap: too-strong). The author concedes a possible negative consequence, slightly reduced employment, and argues only that it gives no reason to reject the policy, not that no downsides exist.`,`This inverts the verdict (trap: opposite). The conclusion is precisely that the employment objection does not defeat the policy, not that it does. Misreading 'gives no reason to reject' as concession is the error this choice exploits.`],generalLesson:`Granting an objection arguendo is a strong defensive move: if the conclusion survives even the opponent's best case, the objection is neutralized. Do not confuse granting a point with disputing it.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author say the critics are wrong that employment would fall?`,`What do "even if granted" and "taken at face value" signal about the author's strategy?`,`Which choice describes assuming an objection true and showing it still does not matter?`],labels:{topic:`minimum wage debate`}},{id:`lr-a-028`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A is the sole proposition both speakers engage with opposite verdicts; B only Ana addresses, C and D neither addresses, E scope shift. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Ana: The city should fluoridate its water supply. Fluoridation sharply reduces cavities, and decades of use show it is safe. Ben: The city should not fluoridate its water. Even if it is safe and effective, fluoridation deprives residents of the freedom to decide what goes into their own bodies.`,stem:`Which one of the following is a point on which Ana and Ben disagree?`,choices:[{text:`Whether the city should fluoridate its water supply.`},{text:`Whether fluoridation reduces cavities.`},{text:`Whether residents care about the freedom to make their own health decisions.`},{text:`Whether the city's water supply is currently safe to drink.`},{text:`Whether public health measures are ever justified.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Ana says the city should fluoridate; Ben says it should not. That is a direct, explicit disagreement on the same proposition. The other choices concern claims only one speaker addresses, or claims neither addresses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A point at issue requires both speakers to take opposing stands on the same proposition. Ana explicitly advocates fluoridation; Ben explicitly opposes it, so choice A is the point of disagreement. Check the distractors against both speakers. Choice B: only Ana addresses cavity reduction; Ben grants it arguendo ("even if it is safe and effective") without affirming or denying it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice C: neither speaker discusses how much residents care about health freedom; Ben invokes freedom as a value, not as a claim about residents' attitudes (trap: unsupported). Choice D: neither speaker discusses current water safety (trap: unsupported). Choice E: neither speaker takes a general stance on all public health measures (trap: scope-shift). The disagreement is narrow and explicit: should the city fluoridate.`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Ana affirms that the city should fluoridate its water supply and Ben denies exactly that proposition, so they take opposite stands on the same claim.`,`This is tempting because cavity reduction is discussed, but only Ana addresses it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Ben says "even if it is safe and effective," which neither affirms nor denies the cavity claim.`,`Neither speaker makes a claim about residents' attitudes toward health freedom. Ben appeals to freedom as a principle, not as a description of what residents care about, so this is unsupported as a point of disagreement.`,`Neither speaker says anything about whether the current water supply is safe. This proposition is simply not addressed by either side. A point at issue must be engaged by both speakers, so an unaddressed proposition can never qualify.`,`This generalizes far beyond the exchange (trap: scope-shift). The speakers debate one specific measure, fluoridation, and neither takes a position on public health measures in general.`],generalLesson:`A genuine point at issue needs both speakers to address the same proposition with opposite verdicts. Claims addressed by only one speaker, or by neither, cannot be what they disagree about.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does each speaker say should be done: what is Ana's verdict, and what is Ben's?`,`On which single proposition do those verdicts directly collide?`,`Which choice states that proposition rather than a claim only one speaker makes?`],labels:{topic:`water fluoridation debate`}},{id:`lr-a-029`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B is the causal claim one affirms and the other denies; A is a classic agreement trap since both accept the ticket rise, C and D one-sided, E unsupported. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Maya: Remote work has raised our team's output. The data show 15 percent more tickets closed since we went remote, so remote work boosts productivity. Leo: The ticket numbers rose because the team adopted powerful new automation tools in that same quarter. Remote work itself has made collaboration harder, not easier.`,stem:`Maya and Leo disagree over whether`,choices:[{text:`the team closed more tickets after going remote.`},{text:`remote work itself increased the team's productivity.`},{text:`the team adopted new automation tools.`},{text:`remote work has made collaboration harder.`},{text:`ticket counts are a valid measure of productivity.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Maya attributes the productivity gain to remote work; Leo attributes the ticket increase to automation and says remote work hurt collaboration. They take opposite stands on whether remote work itself raised productivity. The other choices are points of agreement or claims only one speaker makes.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Both speakers must address the same proposition with opposite verdicts. Maya's claim: remote work boosted productivity, evidenced by more tickets. Leo's claim: the ticket rise came from automation, and remote work made collaboration harder. They directly collide on whether remote work itself increased productivity, which is choice B. Choice A is the classic agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive): both accept that ticket numbers rose; they dispute why. Choice C: only Leo mentions the automation tools; Maya never addresses them (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice D: only Leo claims collaboration got harder; Maya never addresses collaboration (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice E: neither speaker discusses whether ticket counts validly measure productivity (trap: unsupported). The disagreement is causal: did remote work cause the gain. Causal disagreement, not data disagreement, is the heart of this exchange.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is the classic agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Both speakers accept that ticket numbers rose; Maya cites the rise and Leo explains it differently. Agreement cannot be the point of disagreement.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Maya claims remote work itself boosted productivity, while Leo denies this, attributing the ticket increase to automation and saying remote work worsened collaboration.`,`Only Leo mentions the automation tools (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Maya never addresses whether such tools were adopted, so the speakers cannot be said to disagree about it.`,`Only Leo makes a claim about collaboration (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Maya says nothing about whether remote work helped or hurt collaboration, so this is not a shared point of contention.`,`Neither speaker discusses the validity of ticket counts as a productivity metric. Both use the numbers without questioning what they measure, so this is unsupported as a disagreement.`],generalLesson:`When speakers agree on the data but dispute the explanation, the point at issue is the causal claim, not the data. Always verify that both speakers actually address each candidate proposition.`,misconceptionTags:[`agreement-trap`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Do Maya and Leo disagree that ticket numbers rose, or about why they rose?`,`What does each speaker say caused the change in output?`,`Which choice states the causal claim one affirms and the other denies?`],labels:{topic:`remote work productivity debate`}},{id:`lr-a-030`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D is the spending priority they oppose; A is one-sided since Sams even-in-larger-classes sidesteps rather than denies, B neither addresses, C agreement trap, E one-sided. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Priya: The district's new funds should be spent on reducing class sizes. Smaller classes let teachers give each student individual attention, which raises achievement. Sam: The funds should go to teacher training instead. Well-trained teachers raise achievement even in larger classes, and training benefits every student in the district.`,stem:`Which one of the following is a point of disagreement between Priya and Sam?`,choices:[{text:`Whether smaller classes raise student achievement by allowing more individual attention.`},{text:`Whether teachers are currently well trained enough to meet the district’s standards.`},{text:`Whether student achievement can be improved at all through district-level funding decisions.`},{text:`Whether the new funds should be spent on reducing class sizes rather than on teacher training.`},{text:`Whether teacher training benefits every student in the district, including those in the largest classes.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Priya says spend the funds on smaller classes; Sam says spend them on training instead. That spending priority is the proposition they directly oppose each other on. The other choices are claims only one speaker makes or claims both accept.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Each speaker advocates a competing use of the same funds: Priya for class-size reduction, Sam for teacher training. Those recommendations are incompatible, so choice D is the point at issue. Choice A is tempting but only Priya addresses whether smaller classes raise achievement; Sam never denies it, he merely argues training works even in larger classes (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice B is addressed by neither. Choice C is an agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive): both speakers plainly believe achievement can be raised, they differ on how. Choice E is Sam's claim alone; Priya never addresses training's reach (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Note the asymmetry: Sam's "even in larger classes" does not deny that smaller classes help; it sidesteps the claim, which is not the same as disagreeing with it.`,choiceExplanations:[`Only Priya addresses this claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Sam says training raises achievement even in larger classes, which sidesteps rather than denies the claim that smaller classes help; sidestepping is not disagreeing.`,`Neither speaker says anything about teachers' current training levels. Priya discusses class size and Sam discusses training's benefits, but current competence is never assessed. Without any claim about present competence from either side, there is nothing for them to disagree about.`,`This is an agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Both speakers take for granted that achievement can be improved; their dispute is entirely about which investment improves it.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Priya advocates spending the funds on class-size reduction while Sam advocates spending them on teacher training instead, directly opposing spending priorities.`,`Only Sam makes this claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Priya never discusses teacher training at all, so the speakers cannot disagree about its reach. One-sided claims are the most common wrong answers on point-at-issue questions.`],generalLesson:`Sidestepping a claim is not disagreeing with it. A point at issue requires both speakers to engage the same proposition; when one speaker simply changes the subject, that subject is not what they disagree about.`,misconceptionTags:[`agreement-trap`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does each speaker say the money should be spent on?`,`Does Sam deny that smaller classes help, or just argue for a different priority?`,`Which choice states the spending priority they directly oppose each other on?`],labels:{topic:`school funding priorities debate`}},{id:`lr-a-031`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E is the sole shared verdict collision; A B and C each one-sided, Teds whatever-the-cost sidesteps the estimate, D neither addresses. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Rosa: The old depot should be demolished. It is structurally unsound, and renovating it would cost twice as much as new construction. Ted: The depot should be preserved. Its historic architecture is irreplaceable, and once it is destroyed, the city can never get it back, whatever the cost.`,stem:`Rosa and Ted disagree about whether`,choices:[{text:`the depot is structurally unsound.`},{text:`historic architecture has any value.`},{text:`renovation would cost more than new construction.`},{text:`the city can afford new construction.`},{text:`the old depot should be demolished rather than preserved.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Rosa explicitly calls for demolition; Ted explicitly calls for preservation. Those verdicts are direct opposites on the same proposition. Every other choice is addressed by only one speaker or by neither.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The verdicts collide directly: demolish versus preserve. That makes choice E the point at issue. Verify the distractors. Choice A: only Rosa addresses structural soundness; Ted never discusses it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice B: only Ted addresses architecture's value; Rosa never discusses it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice C: only Rosa addresses the cost comparison; Ted says "whatever the cost," declining to engage the cost claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice D: neither speaker discusses the city's ability to afford new construction (trap: unsupported). Ted's "whatever the cost" is carefully worded to avoid disputing Rosa's cost figure, keeping the disagreement on the verdict rather than the estimate. Ted's 'whatever the cost' is a deliberate sidestep, keeping the dispute on the verdict rather than letting it drift into cost estimates.`,choiceExplanations:[`Only Rosa addresses the depot's structural condition (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Ted never discusses whether the building is sound, so this cannot be their point of disagreement.`,`Only Ted addresses the value of historic architecture (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Rosa never discusses whether the architecture matters; her case rests on safety and cost. Check every candidate against both speakers before selecting it.`,`Only Rosa makes a claim about the cost comparison (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Ted's "whatever the cost" deliberately sidesteps the estimate rather than disputing it, so they do not disagree about the figure.`,`Neither speaker discusses whether the city can afford new construction. Affordability is simply not a proposition either of them addresses. Like current water safety in the fluoridation debate, unaddressed topics cannot be points at issue.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Rosa affirms the depot should be demolished and Ted affirms it should be preserved, taking opposite stands on the same proposition.`],generalLesson:`Speakers can disagree on the verdict while each arguing from entirely different reasons. The point at issue is the shared proposition they oppose each other on, not the unshared reasons each brings.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is Rosa's verdict on the depot's future, and what is Ted's?`,`Do they dispute each other's reasons, or just reach opposite conclusions?`,`Which choice states the verdict they directly oppose each other on?`],labels:{topic:`historic depot demolition debate`}},{id:`lr-a-032`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C is the policy verdict; D is a strong agreement trap since Eli explicitly grants uniform criteria, A and B one-sided, E unsupported. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Dana: Companies should use AI to screen resumes. The algorithm applies the same criteria to every applicant, which reduces the bias that creeps into human hiring. Eli: Companies should not use AI to screen resumes. The algorithm does apply the same criteria to everyone, but those criteria embed decades of biased hiring decisions, so the tool perpetuates discrimination at scale.`,stem:`Which one of the following is the point at issue between Dana and Eli?`,choices:[{text:`Whether past hiring decisions were biased.`},{text:`Whether human hiring is free of bias.`},{text:`Whether companies should use AI to screen resumes.`},{text:`Whether the AI applies the same criteria to every applicant.`},{text:`Whether biased criteria can be identified and removed.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Dana says companies should use resume-screening AI; Eli says they should not. That policy verdict is their direct collision. Notably, they agree the AI applies uniform criteria; they disagree about what that uniformity is worth.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The opening verdicts are direct opposites: should use versus should not use. That makes choice C the point at issue. The subtlety is choice D, a strong agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive): Eli explicitly grants that the AI applies the same criteria to everyone, so both speakers accept the uniformity claim; they disagree about its significance, since Eli argues uniform application of biased criteria perpetuates discrimination. Choice A: only Eli addresses past hiring bias; Dana never discusses it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice B: Dana implies human hiring contains bias, but Eli never addresses human hiring (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice E: neither speaker discusses whether biased criteria can be fixed (trap: unsupported). The disagreement is evaluative: given agreed-upon uniformity, is the tool good policy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Only Eli addresses past hiring bias (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Dana never discusses whether historical hiring decisions were biased, so this cannot be their shared point of contention.`,`Only Dana addresses human hiring, implying it contains bias (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Eli's critique targets the AI tool, not human hiring, so the speakers do not collide on this claim.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Dana affirms that companies should use AI resume screening and Eli denies exactly that, taking opposite stands on the same policy proposition.`,`This is a strong agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Eli explicitly grants that the AI applies the same criteria to everyone, so both speakers accept this claim; they disagree about whether that uniformity makes the tool fair.`,`Neither speaker discusses whether biased criteria could be identified and removed. Dana touts uniformity and Eli condemns embedded bias, but fixability is never addressed. Fixability would be a third position neither speaker takes, so it cannot divide them.`],generalLesson:`Speakers can agree on the facts and disagree on the verdict. When both grant a claim, it cannot be the point at issue no matter how central it seems; look for the evaluation they draw opposite conclusions from.`,misconceptionTags:[`agreement-trap`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is each speaker's bottom-line verdict on using the AI?`,`Is there any factual claim they both accept?`,`Which choice states the policy verdict they oppose each other on?`],labels:{topic:`AI resume screening debate`}},{id:`lr-a-033`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B is the adoption verdict; Gus questions generalizability not trial accuracy, A C and E one-sided, D unsupported. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Fern: Firms should adopt a four-day workweek. Trials show that stress fell while output held steady, so shorter weeks improve well-being without sacrificing results. Gus: A four-day week should not be adopted broadly. Those trial results come from small white-collar firms and say nothing about manufacturing or healthcare, where coverage requirements make a four-day week unworkable.`,stem:`Fern and Gus disagree over whether`,choices:[{text:`the trials found that output held steady.`},{text:`firms should broadly adopt a four-day workweek.`},{text:`manufacturing and healthcare face coverage requirements.`},{text:`workers prefer working fewer days.`},{text:`stress fell in the four-day-week trials.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Fern advocates broad adoption; Gus opposes broad adoption. That is their direct collision. Gus questions the trials' generalizability rather than their results, so the findings themselves are not in dispute.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Fern's verdict is "firms should adopt"; Gus's is "should not be adopted broadly." Those are opposite stands on the same policy proposition, making choice B the point at issue. The distractors exploit the trial discussion. Choice A: only Fern asserts the output finding; Gus says the results "say nothing about" other sectors, which questions their scope, not their accuracy (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice C: only Gus mentions coverage requirements; Fern never addresses them (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice D: neither speaker discusses worker preferences (trap: unsupported). Choice E: only Fern reports the stress finding; Gus does not dispute it (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The key insight is that challenging a study's generalizability is not the same as disputing its results. Scope objections leave the original findings standing while shrinking their reach.`,choiceExplanations:[`Only Fern asserts this finding (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Gus argues the trial results do not generalize beyond small white-collar firms, which questions their scope, not whether output held steady in the trials themselves.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Fern affirms that firms should broadly adopt a four-day week while Gus denies it, taking opposite stands on the same proposition.`,`Only Gus mentions coverage requirements (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Fern never addresses conditions in manufacturing or healthcare, so this cannot be their shared disagreement. Supporting reasons that only one speaker offers are never the point at issue.`,`Neither speaker makes any claim about what workers prefer. Fern discusses stress and output; Gus discusses generalizability and coverage, but preferences go unmentioned. Preferences are a natural thing to wonder about, which is exactly why the choice tempts.`,`Only Fern reports the stress finding (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Gus never disputes that stress fell in the trials; his objection concerns whether those trials speak to other industries.`],generalLesson:`Attacking a study's generalizability is not disputing its results. Keep separate what a speaker denies from what a speaker merely declines to extend, and locate the verdict both speakers actually engage.`,misconceptionTags:[`viewpoint-mixup`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Gus say the trial results are wrong, or that they do not apply elsewhere?`,`What is each speaker's verdict on broad adoption?`,`Which choice states the adoption verdict they oppose each other on?`],labels:{topic:`four-day workweek debate`}},{id:`lr-a-034`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D is the reversed comparative ranking; A is an explicit agreement trap since Ivan grants predictability matters, B one-sided, C and E unsupported. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Hana: A carbon tax is the superior climate policy. It gives firms a clear, predictable price signal, and predictability is what drives long-term green investment. Ivan: Predictability does matter, but a tax cannot guarantee any specific emissions cut. Only a cap ensures the environmental outcome, so cap-and-trade is the superior policy.`,stem:`The point at issue between Hana and Ivan is whether`,choices:[{text:`predictability encourages long-term green investment.`},{text:`a carbon tax guarantees a specific emissions cut.`},{text:`firms respond to price signals.`},{text:`a carbon tax is better climate policy than cap-and-trade.`},{text:`cap-and-trade is difficult to administer.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Hana ranks the tax above cap-and-trade; Ivan ranks cap-and-trade above the tax. That comparative evaluation is their direct collision. They explicitly agree that predictability matters, so that cannot be the disagreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Each speaker's verdict is a comparative ranking, and the rankings are reversed: Hana says the tax is superior, Ivan says cap-and-trade is superior. That makes choice D the point at issue. Choice A is a strong agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive): Ivan says "predictability does matter," explicitly granting Hana's premise, so they agree on it. Choice B: only Ivan addresses whether a tax guarantees cuts; Hana never makes that claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Note the asymmetry: Hana praises predictability without claiming guaranteed cuts, so Ivan's point goes unanswered rather than disputed. Choice C: neither speaker explicitly addresses whether firms respond to price signals as a standalone claim (trap: unsupported). Choice E: neither discusses administrative difficulty (trap: unsupported). The difficulty is resisting the rich factual material and locating the single comparative verdict both engage.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is a strong agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Ivan explicitly says "predictability does matter," granting Hana's premise, so both speakers accept this claim; it cannot be what they disagree about.`,`Only Ivan addresses this claim (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Hana argues from predictability to investment without ever claiming a tax guarantees specific cuts, so Ivan's point is unanswered, not disputed.`,`Neither speaker explicitly makes this a standalone claim. Hana's argument implies firms react to price signals, but implication is not assertion, and Ivan never addresses it.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: Hana affirms the carbon tax is the superior policy while Ivan affirms cap-and-trade is superior, taking opposite stands on the same comparative evaluation.`,`Neither speaker discusses how difficult cap-and-trade is to administer. Administrative burden is simply not a proposition either of them addresses. Difficulty of administration is an outside issue neither speaker raises, so it cannot divide them.`],generalLesson:`Explicit agreement, even on a premise central to one side, cannot be the point at issue. Concessions like "does matter, but" mark agreement; the disagreement lies in the verdict drawn from the shared ground.`,misconceptionTags:[`agreement-trap`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does Ivan explicitly grant before his "but"?`,`Can a claim both speakers accept be what they disagree about?`,`Which choice states the comparative ranking they reverse?`],labels:{topic:`carbon tax versus cap-and-trade debate`}},{id:`lr-a-035`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E is the shared verdict proposition; A is a subtle agreement trap since both assume turnout rises, B and C one-sided, D unsupported. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`June: Mandatory voting would strengthen democracy. When everyone votes, elected officials must answer to the whole public rather than just the most motivated factions. Kai: Mandatory voting would not strengthen democracy. Forcing reluctant citizens to the polls does not produce informed choices; unfamiliar names get picked and down-ballot races get skipped.`,stem:`June and Kai disagree about whether`,choices:[{text:`mandatory voting would increase voter turnout.`},{text:`reluctant voters tend to make ill-informed choices.`},{text:`elected officials currently answer only to motivated factions.`},{text:`voting is a civic duty.`},{text:`mandatory voting would strengthen democracy.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`June affirms and Kai denies the very same proposition: that mandatory voting would strengthen democracy. The turnout increase is assumed by both, and the claims about reluctant voters and current officials are each made by only one speaker.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The verdicts are textbook opposites: "would strengthen democracy" versus "would not strengthen democracy." That makes choice E the point at issue. Choice A is a subtle agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive): June's "when everyone votes" assumes turnout rises, and Kai's talk of "forcing reluctant citizens to the polls" likewise assumes they show up; neither disputes the turnout effect, they dispute its value. Choice B: only Kai addresses reluctant voters' decision quality (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice C: only June characterizes current officials' responsiveness (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Choice D: neither invokes civic duty (trap: unsupported). The difficulty is that the speakers argue from completely different mechanisms, representativeness versus informed choice, which can obscure that their verdicts collide on one shared proposition. Background assumptions shared by both sides, like turnout rising, are agreement, not contention.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is a subtle agreement trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Both speakers take for granted that mandatory voting would bring reluctant citizens to the polls; June assumes "everyone votes" and Kai discusses forcing them there. They dispute the effect's value, not its occurrence.`,`Only Kai addresses the decision quality of reluctant voters (trap: wrong-viewpoint). June never discusses whether compelled voters choose well; her case rests on representativeness, not voter competence.`,`Only June characterizes how officials currently behave (trap: wrong-viewpoint). Kai never addresses whether officials now answer only to motivated factions, so this cannot be their shared disagreement.`,`Neither speaker invokes the concept of civic duty. Both argue about consequences for democracy, not about any duty to vote. Duty-based framing is a tempting import, but the debate is entirely about consequences.`,`This correctly identifies the point at issue: June affirms that mandatory voting would strengthen democracy while Kai denies exactly that proposition. Opposite verdicts on one shared proposition are the definition of a point at issue.`],generalLesson:`Different mechanisms can support opposite verdicts on one shared proposition. Do not let divergent reasoning obscure the verdict-level collision, and treat mutually assumed background claims as agreement, not disagreement.`,misconceptionTags:[`agreement-trap`,`viewpoint-mixup`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Do the speakers dispute that reluctant citizens would show up, or what their showing up would accomplish?`,`What is June's bottom-line verdict, and what is Kai's?`,`Which choice states the verdict one affirms and the other denies?`],labels:{topic:`mandatory voting debate`}}],t=[{id:`lr-b-001`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; clean two-link conditional chain; reversals, nec-suff confusion, and too-weak distractors all fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`All certified lifeguards can swim 500 meters without stopping. Anyone who can swim 500 meters without stopping can pass the endurance test.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,choices:[{text:`Anyone who is a certified lifeguard can pass the endurance test.`},{text:`Anyone who can pass the endurance test is a certified lifeguard.`},{text:`No one who fails the endurance test can swim 500 meters.`},{text:`Most certified lifeguards can pass the endurance test.`},{text:`Anyone who can swim 500 meters is a certified lifeguard.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Chain the two conditionals: certified lifeguard implies 500-meter swim, which implies passing the endurance test. So every certified lifeguard must be able to pass. Choice A states that chain; the rest reverse it, weaken it, or add unsupported claims.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate each statement into if-then form. "All certified lifeguards can swim 500 meters" becomes: if someone is a certified lifeguard, then they can swim 500 meters. "Anyone who can swim 500 meters can pass the endurance test" becomes: if someone can swim 500 meters, then they can pass the endurance test. The consequent of the first is the antecedent of the second, so they chain: certified lifeguard implies 500-meter swim implies passing the test. Choice A states exactly that chained result. Choice B reverses the chain (trap: reverses-conditional): passing the test does not imply being a lifeguard. Choice C is tempting but invalid: failing the test does not imply being unable to swim 500 meters, since the second statement gives swim-ability as sufficient for passing, not necessary (trap: nec-suff-confusion). Choice D weakens "anyone" to "most" (trap: too-weak): the premises support the stronger universal claim. Choice E reverses the first statement (trap: reverses-conditional).`,choiceExplanations:[`This correctly chains the two conditionals: being a certified lifeguard guarantees the 500-meter swim, which in turn guarantees passing the endurance test. In every scenario consistent with the premises, this holds.`,`This reverses the chain (trap: reverses-conditional). The premises say lifeguards can pass the test, not that everyone who can pass is a lifeguard; many non-lifeguards could pass too.`,`This is tempting but invalid (trap: nec-suff-confusion). Swimming 500 meters is given as sufficient for passing, not necessary, so failing the test does not prove someone cannot swim the distance; the test might be failed for other reasons.`,`This understates what the premises prove (trap: too-weak). The statements support the universal claim that every certified lifeguard can pass, so retreating to "most" throws away guaranteed information.`,`This reverses the first statement (trap: reverses-conditional). All lifeguards can swim 500 meters, but the premises never say that only lifeguards can. Many strong swimmers may never certify, so the reversed claim is unsupported.`],generalLesson:`When the consequent of one conditional matches the antecedent of another, chain them. Then check each answer against the chain's direction; reversals are the most common trap.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`necessary-vs-sufficient`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Write each statement as an if-then. What follows from being a certified lifeguard?`,`Does the end of the first if-then match the start of the second?`,`Which choice states the full chain without reversing any link?`],labels:{topic:`lifeguard certification`}},{id:`lr-b-002`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; contrapositive of 'attended implies donated' plus 'only donors thanked'; distractors reverse or overclaim; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Everyone who attended the charity gala donated to the fund. Only donors to the fund were thanked in the printed program. Maya did not donate to the fund.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Maya attended the gala without donating to the fund.`},{text:`Maya did not attend the charity gala.`},{text:`Everyone who was thanked in the program attended the gala.`},{text:`Some donors to the fund did not attend the gala.`},{text:`Most donors to the fund attended the gala.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Attending implies donating, so not donating implies not attending; Maya did not donate, so she did not attend. Choice B follows by contrapositive. The other choices reverse conditionals, contradict the premises, or introduce unsupported quantifiers.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate carefully. "Everyone who attended donated" means: attended implies donated. Its contrapositive is: did not donate implies did not attend. Maya did not donate, so by the contrapositive she did not attend, which is choice B. "Only donors were thanked" means: thanked implies donor; it does not say donors were thanked, nor anything about attendance. Choice A contradicts the first premise: attending without donating is impossible. Choice C reverses the logic (trap: reverses-conditional): being thanked implies being a donor, but donors need not have attended. Choice D claims some donors stayed away, but the premises are compatible with every donor attending (trap: unsupported). Choice E adds "most," a quantifier the premises never supply (trap: unsupported). The key move is applying the contrapositive rather than reading the conditional backwards.`,choiceExplanations:[`This directly contradicts the first premise (trap: opposite). Since everyone who attended donated, attending without donating is ruled out, so this cannot be true. It describes the one scenario the premises explicitly forbid.`,`This is correct by the contrapositive: attended implies donated, so not donating implies not attending. Maya did not donate, so in every scenario consistent with the premises she did not attend.`,`This reverses the conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Being thanked implies being a donor, but nothing says donors attended; a donor could have given without going to the gala.`,`This is consistent with the premises but not required by them (trap: unsupported). It is possible that every single donor attended, so "some did not" need not be true.`,`The premises supply no quantifier like "most" (trap: unsupported). All donors could have attended, or only a few; the statements constrain who donated and who was thanked, not proportions.`],generalLesson:`Apply the contrapositive mechanically: flip and negate both sides. "Only donors were thanked" tells you about thanked people, not about donors, so never read it backwards.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`reverses-conditional`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`If someone attended the gala, what else must be true of them?`,`Maya lacks that thing. What does the contrapositive tell you about her?`,`Which choice follows without reversing any conditional?`],labels:{topic:`charity gala donations`}},{id:`lr-b-003`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; some/all quantifier inference (some forgeries, all forgeries unsigned); reversal and scope-shift traps fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Some of the paintings in the gallery are forgeries. All forgeries are unsigned.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`All unsigned paintings in the gallery are forgeries.`},{text:`Most of the paintings in the gallery are unsigned.`},{text:`Some of the paintings in the gallery are unsigned.`},{text:`No genuine painting in the gallery is unsigned.`},{text:`All forgeries are paintings in the gallery.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`At least one painting is a forgery, and every forgery is unsigned, so at least one painting is unsigned. Choice C states that. The other choices reverse the logic, inflate "some" to "all" or "most," or claim things about genuine paintings the premises never address.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Work from the existential claim. "Some paintings are forgeries" guarantees at least one painting that is a forgery. "All forgeries are unsigned" applies to that painting: it must be unsigned. So at least one painting is unsigned, which is exactly what "some paintings are unsigned" means, choice C. Choice A reverses the conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): forgeries are unsigned, but unsigned paintings need not be forgeries. Choice B inflates "some" to "most" (trap: too-strong): the premises guarantee at least one, not a majority. Choice D goes beyond the evidence (trap: unsupported): nothing is said about whether genuine paintings can be unsigned. Choice E reverses the "some" statement into a universal about all forgeries everywhere (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). Existential conclusions keep the 'some' quantifier; upgrading it is the classic error.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). All forgeries are unsigned, but an unsigned painting could be a genuine work the artist simply did not sign.`,`This inflates the quantifier (trap: too-strong). "Some" guarantees at least one, not a majority; the premises are fully consistent with only a single unsigned painting.`,`This is correct: the guaranteed forgery-painting must be unsigned, so at least one painting in the gallery is unsigned, which is precisely what "some are unsigned" claims.`,`This goes beyond the premises (trap: unsupported). Nothing rules out a genuine painting being unsigned; the statements constrain forgeries, not genuine works. The premises tell us about forgeries only, so genuine paintings are an unregulated category here.`,`This converts "some paintings are forgeries" into a universal claim about all forgeries (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The premises say nothing about forgeries outside this gallery.`],generalLesson:`"Some A are B" plus "All B are C" yields "Some A are C." Guard the quantifier: the conclusion keeps "some," never upgrades to "most" or "all," and never reverses the conditional.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "some paintings are forgeries" guarantee exists?`,`What must be true of that painting, given the second statement?`,`Which choice states exactly that, without upgrading "some" or reversing anything?`],labels:{topic:`gallery forgeries`}},{id:`lr-b-004`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; straightforward arithmetic inference (30 total, 18 Spanish, rest French); 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Each of the 30 students in the class studies exactly one foreign language. Eighteen students study Spanish, and the rest study French.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Twelve students in the class study Spanish.`},{text:`Most students in the class study French.`},{text:`Some students study a language other than Spanish or French.`},{text:`Twelve students in the class study French.`},{text:`Eighteen students study French and twelve study Spanish.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Thirty students each study exactly one language, and 18 study Spanish, so the remaining 12 must study French. Choice D performs that subtraction. Every other choice contradicts the arithmetic.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is arithmetic plus an exclusivity constraint. Thirty students, each exactly one language, 18 in Spanish. "The rest study French" means everyone not in the Spanish group is in the French group: 30 minus 18 equals 12. Choice D states that result. Choice A contradicts the premises: 18, not 12, study Spanish, so the number 12 is a keyword-match (trap: keyword-match). Choice B is false: 12 out of 30 is not most (trap: opposite). Choice C contradicts "the rest study French": no student studies a third language (trap: unsupported). Choice E swaps the two numbers (trap: keyword-match): it keeps the right figures but assigns them to the wrong languages. Number questions reward doing the subtraction before reading the choices; the wrong answers recycle the same numbers in tempting arrangements.`,choiceExplanations:[`This contradicts the stimulus (trap: keyword-match). Eighteen students study Spanish, not twelve; this choice borrows the correct French number and attaches it to the wrong language.`,`This is false (trap: opposite). Twelve out of thirty students is a minority, so "most students study French" cannot be true given the premises. Twelve is less than half of thirty, which settles the quantifier immediately.`,`This contradicts the exclusivity of the setup (trap: unsupported). Each student studies exactly one language and "the rest study French," leaving no room for a third language.`,`This is correct: 30 total minus 18 Spanish students leaves 12 students, and "the rest study French" assigns all of them to French. This holds in every scenario consistent with the premises.`,`This swaps the numbers (trap: keyword-match). The figures 18 and 12 both appear in the stimulus, but 18 belongs to Spanish and 12 to French, not the reverse.`],generalLesson:`On number questions, do the arithmetic first and assign each result to the right category before reading the choices. Wrong answers typically reuse the same numbers in swapped or negated forms.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`],trapTypes:[`keyword-match`,`opposite`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`If 18 of the 30 students study Spanish, how many students are left?`,`What does "the rest study French" tell you about those remaining students?`,`Which choice assigns the computed number to the correct language?`],labels:{topic:`language classes`}},{id:`lr-b-005`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; 'only if' necessary-condition with unmet conjunction; reversal distractor (B) correctly fails; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`A research grant is awarded only if the applicant submits both a budget and a timeline. Rivera submitted a budget but no timeline.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Rivera submitted a timeline.`},{text:`Anyone who submits both a budget and a timeline receives the grant.`},{text:`No applicant submitted both a budget and a timeline.`},{text:`Rivera's budget was inadequate.`},{text:`Rivera will not receive the grant.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`"Only if" makes the budget and timeline necessary, not sufficient. Rivera lacks the timeline, so the necessary condition fails and the grant cannot be awarded. Choice E follows; the rest reverse the conditional, overstate it, or add unsupported details.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate "only if" carefully: grant implies (budget and timeline). The contrapositive is: missing budget or missing timeline implies no grant. Rivera submitted no timeline, so the necessary condition is unmet and Rivera cannot receive the grant, which is choice E. Choice A contradicts the given facts (trap: opposite). Choice B converts a necessary condition into a sufficient one (trap: nec-suff-confusion): submitting both may be required without guaranteeing the award. Choice C overreaches (trap: unsupported, too-strong): other applicants may well have submitted both. Choice D invents a judgment about the budget's quality that the premises never address (trap: unsupported). The core skill is the "only if" translation: it points the arrow from the grant toward the requirements, never the reverse. Arrow direction is everything with 'only if.'`,choiceExplanations:[`This directly contradicts the stimulus (trap: opposite). Rivera is stated to have submitted no timeline, so this cannot be true. It asserts the very fact the second premise denies.`,`This converts necessity into sufficiency (trap: nec-suff-confusion). The budget and timeline are required for the grant, but the premises never say they guarantee it; other criteria could still disqualify an applicant.`,`This goes far beyond the evidence (trap: unsupported, too-strong). Nothing is said about other applicants, who may have submitted both documents. Rivera's case constrains Rivera only; other applicants are an unmentioned group.`,`The premises say nothing about the quality of Rivera's budget (trap: unsupported). The grant fails on the missing timeline alone; adequacy is never assessed. Invented details like adequacy are classic unsupported-choice filler.`,`This is correct: the grant requires both documents, Rivera lacks the timeline, so by the contrapositive of the "only if" statement Rivera cannot receive the grant.`],generalLesson:`"A only if B" means A implies B: B is necessary, never sufficient. When a necessary condition is missing, the outcome is ruled out, but meeting it guarantees nothing.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "only if" tell you: does the grant guarantee the documents, or do the documents guarantee the grant?`,`Rivera is missing one required item. What does the contrapositive say about the grant?`,`Which choice follows without turning a requirement into a guarantee?`],labels:{topic:`research grant requirements`}},{id:`lr-b-006`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; 'unless' translation plus modus ponens; counterfactual distractor (E) correctly fails; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Unless the dam's spillway is opened, the reservoir will overflow. The spillway was not opened.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`The reservoir will overflow.`},{text:`The spillway was opened.`},{text:`If the reservoir overflows, the spillway was not opened.`},{text:`The reservoir will not overflow.`},{text:`Opening the spillway would have prevented the overflow.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`"Unless A, B" means if not A then B. The spillway was not opened, so the antecedent is satisfied and the reservoir will overflow. Choice A applies the rule; the rest affirm the consequent, deny the antecedent, or contradict the facts.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate "unless" first: "Unless the spillway is opened, the reservoir will overflow" is equivalent to "If the spillway is not opened, then the reservoir will overflow." The second premise affirms the antecedent: the spillway was not opened. Modus ponens gives the consequent: the reservoir will overflow, choice A. Choice B contradicts the given fact (trap: opposite). Choice C affirms the consequent (trap: nec-suff-confusion): from not-opened implies overflow, "overflow implies not-opened" does not follow, since the reservoir might overflow for other reasons. Choice D contradicts the valid inference (trap: opposite). Choice E denies the antecedent (trap: nec-suff-confusion): the premises say nothing about what opening the spillway would have done; not-opened implying overflow does not make opened imply no overflow. Translate 'unless' before doing anything else.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: "unless" translates to "if the spillway is not opened, the reservoir will overflow," and the spillway was not opened, so the consequent follows by modus ponens.`,`This directly contradicts the second premise (trap: opposite). The stimulus states that the spillway was not opened. It affirms the antecedent's negation, which the facts explicitly rule out.`,`This affirms the consequent (trap: nec-suff-confusion). The conditional runs from not-opened to overflow; reversing it is invalid because the reservoir could overflow for unrelated reasons.`,`This contradicts the valid inference (trap: opposite). Given the translation and the fact that the spillway stayed closed, overflow follows. Denying the consequent after affirming the antecedent violates modus ponens.`,`This denies the antecedent (trap: nec-suff-confusion). The premises establish what happens when the spillway is not opened, not what would have happened had it been opened.`],generalLesson:`"Unless A, B" is "if not A, then B." Affirming the stated antecedent (not A) is valid; affirming the consequent or denying the antecedent is not.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`nec-suff-confusion`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Rewrite "unless" as an if-then. What is the antecedent?`,`Is that antecedent affirmed or denied by the second statement?`,`Which choice is the consequent of that conditional?`],labels:{topic:`dam spillway`}},{id:`lr-b-007`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Most marathon finishers trained for at least four months. Everyone who trained for at least four months followed a nutrition plan.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Everyone who followed a nutrition plan finished the marathon.`},{text:`Most marathon finishers followed a nutrition plan.`},{text:`All marathon finishers trained for at least four months.`},{text:`Some people who followed a nutrition plan did not finish the marathon.`},{text:`Most people who followed a nutrition plan are marathon finishers.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Most finishers are in the four-month-training group, and everyone in that group followed a nutrition plan, so most finishers followed a nutrition plan. Choice B preserves the "most" through the chain; the rest reverse it or upgrade it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Combine a "most" statement with a universal. Most finishers trained four-plus months; call that group T. Everyone in T followed a nutrition plan. So the "most finishers" who are in T all followed the plan, which means most finishers followed the plan, choice B. The "most" survives because the universal applies to every member of the majority group. Choice A reverses the second conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): training implies the plan, not the reverse. Choice C upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). Choice D is consistent with the premises but not required (trap: unsupported): possibly everyone on the plan finished. Choice E reverses the "most" statement (trap: reverses-conditional): most finishers followed the plan does not imply most plan-followers are finishers. Majority groups carry their properties through universals intact.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the second conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Training for four months implies following the plan, but following the plan does not imply finishing the marathon.`,`This is correct: the majority of finishers who trained four-plus months each followed a nutrition plan, so a majority of finishers followed one. The "most" carries through the universal.`,`This upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). The premises explicitly allow that some finishers trained for less than four months. The minority outside the majority group blocks every upgrade to 'all.'`,`This is possible but not required (trap: unsupported). For all the premises say, every person who followed the plan could have finished the marathon. Possibility is not necessity, and must-be-true demands necessity.`,`This reverses the "most" relationship (trap: reverses-conditional). Most finishers followed the plan, but the plan-followers could be a huge group of non-finishers. The plan-following population may dwarf the finishers, so the reversal fails.`],generalLesson:`"Most A are B" plus "All B are C" yields "Most A are C." The quantifier survives the chain, but it never upgrades to "all" and never reverses direction.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Most finishers belong to the four-month-training group. What did everyone in that group do?`,`So what did most finishers do?`,`Which choice keeps "most" pointing the same direction?`],labels:{topic:`marathon training`}},{id:`lr-b-008`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; negative universal syllogism (no sculptures for sale; bronze works are sculptures); 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`None of the museum's sculptures are for sale. All bronze works in the museum are sculptures.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`All of the museum's sculptures are bronze works.`},{text:`Some bronze works in the museum are for sale.`},{text:`None of the bronze works in the museum are for sale.`},{text:`None of the museum's paintings are for sale.`},{text:`All works offered for sale are paintings.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Every bronze work is a sculpture, and no sculpture is for sale, so no bronze work is for sale. Choice C chains the universal through the negation. The other choices reverse the logic, contradict the premises, or wander into paintings the premises never mention.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate and chain. "All bronze works are sculptures" gives: bronze implies sculpture. "None of the sculptures are for sale" gives: sculpture implies not for sale. Chaining yields bronze implies not for sale, i.e., none of the bronze works are for sale, choice C. Choice A reverses the first statement (trap: reverses-conditional): all bronze works are sculptures, but sculptures need not be bronze. Choice B contradicts the valid inference (trap: opposite). Choice D shifts scope to paintings, which the premises never address (trap: scope-shift). Choice E invents a claim about everything for sale (trap: unsupported): the premises say nothing about what is for sale, only that sculptures are not. Negated universals chain just like affirmative ones, so practice the 'no' versions deliberately.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the first conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Every bronze work is a sculpture, but the museum could hold marble or wood sculptures too. The category of sculptures is wider than the bronze subset, blocking the reversal.`,`This contradicts the chained inference (trap: opposite). Since no sculpture is for sale and bronze works are sculptures, no bronze work can be for sale.`,`This is correct: chaining "bronze implies sculpture" with "sculpture implies not for sale" yields "bronze implies not for sale," which holds in every consistent scenario.`,`This shifts to paintings (trap: scope-shift). The premises constrain sculptures and bronze works only; paintings are never mentioned and could be for sale or not.`,`This goes beyond the premises (trap: unsupported). Nothing identifies what is for sale; the statements only rule sculptures out. Ruling a category out does not identify what remains, so this overreaches.`],generalLesson:`Universals chain through negations just as they do through affirmations: "All B are S" plus "No S are F" gives "No B are F." Keep the arrow direction fixed and resist drifting to unmentioned categories.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`If something is a bronze work in this museum, what else must it be?`,`And what is true of all sculptures regarding being for sale?`,`Which choice combines those two facts without reversing either?`],labels:{topic:`museum bronze works`}},{id:`lr-b-009`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; chained conditionals with 'only if' in second position; reversal distractor (E) correctly fails; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`If the new highway is built, commute times will fall. Commute times will fall only if bus service is also expanded.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`If bus service is expanded, commute times will fall.`},{text:`The new highway will be built.`},{text:`Commute times will fall.`},{text:`If the new highway is built, bus service will be expanded.`},{text:`Bus service will be expanded only if the new highway is built.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Highway implies falling commutes, and falling commutes implies expanded bus service, so highway implies expanded bus service. Choice D chains them. The other choices assert the antecedents unconditionally or reverse the conditionals.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate both statements. "If the highway is built, commute times will fall": highway implies falling times. "Commute times will fall only if bus service is expanded": falling times implies expanded bus service, because "only if" marks the necessary condition. The consequent of the first is the antecedent of the second, so they chain: highway implies expanded bus service, choice D. Choice A reverses the second conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): expanded service is necessary for falling times, not sufficient. Choice B asserts the highway will be built, which is merely hypothetical (trap: unsupported). Choice C asserts falling times unconditionally (trap: too-strong): times fall only under the stated conditions. Choice E reverses the chain (trap: reverses-conditional): the highway implies expanded service, not the reverse.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the "only if" conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Expanded bus service is necessary for falling commute times, not sufficient to produce them. Necessity never implies sufficiency, no matter how natural the reversal feels.`,`The highway is introduced hypothetically ("if the highway is built"); nothing says it will be built (trap: unsupported). Hypothetical antecedents must never be asserted as facts.`,`This asserts the consequent unconditionally (trap: too-strong). Commute times fall only if the stated conditions hold; the premises never establish that they do. Detaching a consequent from its conditions is a classic must-be-true error.`,`This is correct: chaining "highway implies falling times" with "falling times implies expanded bus service" yields "highway implies expanded bus service." The middle term, falling commute times, links the two conditionals into one valid chain.`,`This reverses the chained conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). The highway's construction implies expanded bus service, not the other way around. Chained arrows still point one way; the conclusion never flips them.`],generalLesson:`Mixed "if" and "only if" statements chain when the first consequent matches the second antecedent. Translate "only if" first, then link; never assert a merely hypothetical antecedent.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`necessary-vs-sufficient`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate the "only if" statement. Which direction does its arrow point?`,`Does the first statement's consequent match the second statement's antecedent?`,`Which choice states the resulting chain?`],labels:{topic:`highway and bus service`}},{id:`lr-b-010`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; percentage remainder inference (60/30/10); overclaim and prediction distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`In a survey, 60 percent of respondents favored the proposal and 30 percent opposed it; the rest were undecided.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Most respondents opposed the proposal.`},{text:`No respondent was undecided.`},{text:`More than half of the opponents later changed their minds.`},{text:`The proposal will pass.`},{text:`Exactly 10 percent of respondents were undecided.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Sixty percent favored and 30 percent opposed, totaling 90 percent, so the undecided remainder must be exactly 10 percent. Choice E does that subtraction; the rest contradict the figures or speculate beyond them.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The percentages must sum to 100. Favored (60) plus opposed (30) equals 90, leaving exactly 10 percent undecided, choice E. Choice A contradicts the data: 30 percent opposed is not most (trap: opposite). Choice B contradicts "the rest were undecided" (trap: opposite). Choice C invents later mind-changing the survey never mentions (trap: unsupported). Choice D leaps from survey support to electoral outcome (trap: scope-shift): favoring a proposal in a survey does not guarantee passage. As with all number questions, compute before reading the choices so the recycled figures cannot mislead. Always compute the remainder before reading the choices, since wrong answers recycle the given figures. The 'rest' language guarantees the categories are exhaustive, which is what makes the subtraction valid. Exhaustiveness is the hidden premise that licenses the arithmetic.`,choiceExplanations:[`This contradicts the figures (trap: opposite). Thirty percent opposed is well short of most; the majority favored the proposal. Thirty percent cannot be 'most' under any reading of the numbers.`,`This contradicts the stimulus (trap: opposite). "The rest were undecided" guarantees that some respondents were undecided. The remainder category is explicitly nonempty, so 'none' is impossible.`,`The survey describes opinions at one time; nothing is said about anyone changing their mind later (trap: unsupported). Mind-changing is a temporal claim no snapshot survey can support.`,`This leaps from survey results to a real-world outcome (trap: scope-shift). Favoring a proposal in a poll does not guarantee it will pass. Polls measure opinion, not electoral outcomes, so passage is a separate matter.`,`This is correct: 60 plus 30 is 90, so the undecided remainder is exactly 10 percent in every scenario consistent with the premises. Closed percentage sets always sum to 100, fixing the remainder precisely.`],generalLesson:`Percentages in a closed set must total 100; the remainder is determined, not estimated. Distinguish what the numbers fix from predictions the numbers cannot support.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What do 60 percent and 30 percent add up to?`,`What must the remainder be, given that the groups cover everyone?`,`Which choice states that remainder exactly?`],labels:{topic:`proposal survey`}},{id:`lr-b-011`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; three-link chain (dues to vote to ballot to not-against); 'voted in favor' and 'motion approved' overclaims fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Every member who paid dues may vote. Everyone who may vote received a ballot. No one who received a ballot voted against the motion.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`No member who paid dues voted against the motion.`},{text:`Every member paid dues.`},{text:`Every member who paid dues voted in favor of the motion.`},{text:`Everyone who received a ballot voted on the motion.`},{text:`The motion was approved.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paid dues implies may vote, may vote implies received a ballot, and received a ballot implies did not vote against. Chaining all three gives: a dues-paying member did not vote against the motion. Choice A states that chain; the rest overstate it or speculate beyond it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate each statement into if-then form and link them. "Every member who paid dues may vote": paid dues implies may vote. "Everyone who may vote received a ballot": may vote implies received a ballot. "No one who received a ballot voted against": received a ballot implies did not vote against. The consequent of each is the antecedent of the next, so for any dues-paying member the full chain runs: paid dues, therefore may vote, therefore received a ballot, therefore did not vote against. That is choice A. Choice B is unsupported (trap: unsupported): nothing says every member paid. Choice C upgrades "did not vote against" to "voted in favor" (trap: too-strong): a dues-payer could have abstained. Choice D makes the same upgrade for all ballot recipients (trap: too-strong): receiving a ballot does not imply voting. Choice E speculates about the outcome (trap: unsupported): abstentions and non-voters leave the result undetermined.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: chaining "paid dues implies may vote" with "may vote implies received a ballot" with "ballot implies did not vote against" yields the result for every dues-paying member, in all consistent scenarios.`,`Nothing in the premises says every member paid dues (trap: unsupported). Some members may not have paid and therefore could not vote. The premises constrain dues-payers only, leaving other members unaddressed.`,`This upgrades "did not vote against" to "voted in favor" (trap: too-strong). A dues-paying member could have received a ballot and abstained, which is consistent with all three premises.`,`This assumes every ballot recipient cast a vote (trap: too-strong). The premises only say ballot recipients did not vote against the motion; abstaining is left open.`,`The outcome is undetermined (trap: unsupported). With abstentions possible and non-dues-payers unable to vote, the premises do not fix whether the motion passed. Vote totals are never given, so no outcome can be inferred.`],generalLesson:`Long conditional chains are solved by translating each link and matching consequents to antecedents. Watch for answer choices that upgrade "did not vote against" to "voted for": abstention breaks that upgrade.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Write each statement as an if-then. What follows from a member having paid dues?`,`Keep chaining: where does "may vote" lead, and where does "received a ballot" lead?`,`Which choice states the end of that chain without upgrading "did not vote against"?`],labels:{topic:`member dues vote`}},{id:`lr-b-012`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; some/no/anyone quantifier chain (some remote, none remote attend, missers file reports); reversal and quantifier traps fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Some software engineers at the firm work remotely. No remote worker attends the daily standup in person. Anyone who misses the standup must file a status report.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`No software engineer attends the daily standup in person.`},{text:`Some software engineers must file a status report.`},{text:`Everyone who files a status report works remotely.`},{text:`Some engineers who attend the standup in person work remotely.`},{text:`Most software engineers work remotely.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`At least one engineer works remotely; no remote worker attends in person, so that engineer misses the standup; anyone who misses it must file a report. Hence some engineers must file a report. Choice B chains the existential through two universals.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Start with the existential guarantee: at least one software engineer works remotely. "No remote worker attends the standup in person" applies to that engineer: they miss the standup. "Anyone who misses the standup must file a status report" then applies: they must file. So at least one software engineer must file a status report, which is what "some must file" means, choice B. Choice A upgrades "some" to "no engineer attends" (trap: too-strong). Choice C reverses the last conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): missing the standup implies filing, not the reverse, and remote work is not even in that conditional. Choice D contradicts the second premise: remote workers do not attend in person (trap: opposite). Choice E upgrades "some" to "most" (trap: too-strong). The pattern is "some A are B; all B are C; all C are D; therefore some A are D."`,choiceExplanations:[`This upgrades "some" to a universal negative (trap: too-strong). The premises guarantee only that at least one engineer works remotely; other engineers may attend in person.`,`This is correct: the guaranteed remote-working engineer misses the standup and therefore must file a status report, so "some engineers must file" holds in every consistent scenario.`,`This reverses the final conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Missing the standup implies filing a report, but filing does not imply missing it, and nothing links filing back to remote work.`,`This contradicts the second premise (trap: opposite). No remote worker attends the standup in person, so no engineer can both work remotely and attend. It combines two categories the premises declare mutually exclusive.`,`This upgrades the quantifier (trap: too-strong). "Some" guarantees at least one, not a majority; the premises are consistent with exactly one remote engineer. A single remote engineer satisfies every premise, refuting any 'most' claim.`],generalLesson:`Existential statements chain through universals: "some A are B" plus "all B are C" yields "some A are C," and the chain can continue. Never upgrade "some" along the way.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`reverses-conditional`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "some engineers work remotely" guarantee exists?`,`Follow that engineer: do they attend the standup, and what must they then do?`,`Which choice states that result while keeping "some" as "some"?`],labels:{topic:`remote engineers standup`}},{id:`lr-b-013`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; 'unless' with disjunction plus negation (disjunctive syllogism); definite-outcome distractors correctly fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`The cafe will close unless it raises prices or cuts hours. It will not cut hours.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`The cafe will raise its prices.`},{text:`The cafe will not close.`},{text:`Either the cafe will raise its prices or it will close.`},{text:`If the cafe raises its prices, it will stay open.`},{text:`The cafe will cut its hours.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`"Close unless raise or cut" means: if it neither raises nor cuts, it closes. Since it will not cut, the only ways to avoid closing require raising prices, so either it raises prices or it closes. Choice C states that disjunction.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate "unless": "The cafe will close unless it raises prices or cuts hours" is equivalent to "If the cafe does not raise prices and does not cut hours, then it will close." Symbolically: not-raise and not-cut implies close. The second premise gives not-cut. Suppose the cafe does not raise prices either; then both conditions hold and it closes. So in every consistent scenario, either it raises prices or it closes, choice C. Choice A is too strong (trap: too-strong): closing instead of raising is left open. Choice B is unsupported (trap: unsupported): it may close if it does not raise prices. Choice D denies the antecedent (trap: nec-suff-confusion): the conditional runs from not-raising-and-not-cutting to closing; raising prices does not guarantee staying open. Choice E contradicts the given fact (trap: opposite).`,choiceExplanations:[`This is too strong (trap: too-strong). The premises leave open the possibility that the cafe closes without raising prices; raising is one way out, not a certainty.`,`This is unsupported (trap: unsupported). If the cafe neither raises prices nor cuts hours, it closes, so staying open is not guaranteed. The unless-statement leaves closure open whenever both escapes are unused.`,`This is correct: given "not raise and not cut implies close" plus "will not cut," every consistent scenario has the cafe raising prices or closing (or both).`,`This denies the antecedent (trap: nec-suff-confusion). The conditional says what follows from not raising and not cutting; it promises nothing about what raising prices achieves.`,`This directly contradicts the second premise (trap: opposite), which states the cafe will not cut hours. It asserts the negation of a given fact, which is never viable.`],generalLesson:`"A unless B or C" means if neither B nor C holds then A holds, so combined with "not C" it yields that either B holds or A holds. Never upgrade that disjunction to a certainty about one disjunct.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`strength-mismatch`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Rewrite "unless" as an if-then. What combination leads to closing?`,`Now add the fact that it will not cut hours. What remains?`,`Which choice states the resulting either-or without picking a side?`],labels:{topic:`cafe pricing dilemma`}},{id:`lr-b-014`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: choice B ('No invited guest left before the speeches') was entailed by the premises — leaving presupposes attendance and no attendee left — creating two defensible answers. Replaced with 'Every invited guest heard the speeches' (not entailed; preserves the too-strong trap). 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`Most invited guests attended the reception. No one who attended the reception left before the speeches.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`All invited guests stayed for the speeches.`},{text:`Every invited guest heard the speeches.`},{text:`Everyone who stayed for the speeches was an invited guest.`},{text:`Most invited guests did not leave before the speeches.`},{text:`Some guests who were not invited left before the speeches.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Most invited guests attended, and no attendee left before the speeches, so most invited guests are people who did not leave early. Choice D carries the "most" through the universal; the rest upgrade it, reverse it, or speculate about uninvited guests.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Most invited guests belong to the attendee group, and every attendee stayed for the speeches. So the majority of invited guests are attendees who stayed, meaning most invited guests did not leave before the speeches, choice D. Choice A upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). Choice B upgrades to 'every' (trap: too-strong): invited guests who did not attend never heard the speeches, so a universal hearing claim overreaches. Choice C reverses the relationship (trap: reverses-conditional, unsupported): uninvited people could have stayed too. Choice E speculates about uninvited guests (trap: unsupported): the premises constrain only attendees, not outsiders. The minority outside the 'most' group is invisible to the premises, so any choice about 'all' or 'none' overreaches. Treat 'most' as a ceiling on what follows, never a floor.`,choiceExplanations:[`This upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). Some invited guests may have skipped the reception entirely, and the premises say nothing about them. Non-attendees are outside both premises, so no claim about them follows.`,`This upgrades "most" to "every" (trap: too-strong). Invited guests who skipped the reception never heard the speeches, so the premises cannot support a claim about every invited guest hearing them.`,`This reverses the logic and adds unsupported scope (trap: reverses-conditional, unsupported). Attendees stayed, but nothing says only invited guests attended; uninvited guests could have stayed as well.`,`This is correct: the majority of invited guests who attended each stayed for the speeches, so a majority of invited guests did not leave early, in every consistent scenario.`,`The premises say nothing about uninvited guests (trap: unsupported). They constrain attendees only, so no claim about outsiders follows. Outsiders are an unmentioned category, and unmentioned categories yield nothing.`],generalLesson:`"Most A are B" plus "All B are C" yields "Most A are C." The majority that is covered carries the property; the uncovered minority blocks any upgrade to "all" or "none."`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`reverses-conditional`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Most invited guests are in which group?`,`What did everyone in that group do about the speeches?`,`Which choice applies that to most invited guests without upgrading to "all"?`],labels:{topic:`reception speeches`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-b-015`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; three-link 'only when'/'until' chain (visible only if fog clears; fog clears only if wind shifts; no shift); converse distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`The lighthouse is visible from the harbor only when the fog clears. The fog will not clear until the wind shifts. The wind has not shifted.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`The fog will never clear.`},{text:`If the wind shifts, the fog will clear.`},{text:`The lighthouse is visible from the harbor.`},{text:`The wind will shift soon.`},{text:`The lighthouse is not visible from the harbor.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Visible implies fog cleared; fog clearing implies wind shifted. The wind has not shifted, so the fog has not cleared, so the lighthouse is not visible. Choice E applies the contrapositive down the chain; the rest reverse links or speculate about the future.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate and chain. "Visible only when the fog clears": visible implies fog cleared. "The fog will not clear until the wind shifts": fog clears implies wind shifted. Chain: visible implies fog cleared implies wind shifted. The wind has not shifted. Apply the contrapositive twice: no wind shift implies the fog has not cleared, which implies the lighthouse is not visible, choice E. Choice A overreaches temporally (trap: too-strong): the fog may clear later. Choice B reverses the "until" conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): fog clearing requires a wind shift, but a shift need not clear the fog. Choice C contradicts the chain (trap: opposite). Choice D speculates about the future (trap: unsupported). Each 'only when' and 'until' adds a link; denying the last link unwinds them all.`,choiceExplanations:[`This overreaches (trap: too-strong). "Has not shifted" describes the present; the premises leave open that the wind shifts later and the fog clears then. Present-tense facts never license 'never' claims about the future.`,`This reverses the "until" conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Fog clearing requires a wind shift, but a wind shift does not guarantee the fog clears; the requirement runs one way only.`,`This contradicts the chained contrapositive (trap: opposite). Visibility requires cleared fog, which requires a shifted wind, and the wind has not shifted. It asserts the consequent of a chain whose necessary condition has failed.`,`The premises say nothing about when or whether the wind will shift (trap: unsupported). "Has not shifted" is a present fact, not a forecast. Forecasts need premises about the future, and none are given.`,`This is correct: no wind shift implies the fog has not cleared, which implies the lighthouse is not visible, in every scenario consistent with the premises.`],generalLesson:`"A only when B" and "B not until C" both point their arrows forward: A implies B implies C. Denying C unwinds the whole chain by contrapositive.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`reverses-conditional`,`opposite`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate each "only when" / "until" into an if-then arrow. Which way do they point?`,`Chain them, then start from the one thing you know: the wind has not shifted.`,`Which choice is the end of that contrapositive chain?`],labels:{topic:`lighthouse visibility`}},{id:`lr-b-016`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Most of the airline's pilots have logged over 5,000 flight hours. Anyone who has logged over 5,000 flight hours qualifies for the senior roster. All senior roster members receive extra training.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`Most of the airline's pilots receive extra training.`},{text:`All of the airline's pilots qualify for the senior roster.`},{text:`Anyone who receives extra training is a pilot.`},{text:`Most senior roster members are pilots.`},{text:`No pilot has logged fewer than 5,000 flight hours.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Most pilots have 5,000-plus hours; all such pilots qualify for the senior roster; all roster members get extra training. So most pilots qualify and therefore most receive extra training. Choice A carries "most" through two universals.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This chains a "most" through two universals. Most pilots have 5,000-plus hours. Everyone with 5,000-plus hours qualifies for the senior roster, so most pilots qualify. Everyone on the senior roster receives extra training, so most pilots receive extra training, choice A. Each universal preserves the majority because it applies to every member of the majority group. Choice B upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). Choice C reverses the last conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): roster members get training, but trainees need not be pilots. Choice D reverses the "most" relationship (trap: reverses-conditional): most pilots receive training does not imply most trainees are pilots; the roster could include many non-pilots. Choice E contradicts "most" (trap: opposite): a minority may have fewer hours. Two universals in a row still preserve the majority.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: the majority of pilots with 5,000-plus hours each qualify for the roster and each receive extra training, so a majority of pilots receive extra training in every consistent scenario.`,`This upgrades "most" to "all" (trap: too-strong). The premises explicitly allow a minority of pilots with fewer than 5,000 hours who do not qualify. The permitted minority is exactly what 'most' reserves room for.`,`This reverses the final conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Senior roster members receive extra training, but nothing says only pilots receive it. Training may extend well beyond pilots, so the reversal is unsupported.`,`This reverses the "most" direction (trap: reverses-conditional). Most pilots receiving training says nothing about the composition of the trainee group, which could be mostly non-pilots.`,`This contradicts the first premise (trap: opposite). "Most" pilots have 5,000-plus hours, which explicitly permits some pilots to have fewer. 'Most' logically requires that exceptions be possible, not impossible.`],generalLesson:`A "most" statement survives chaining through universals: most A are B, all B are C, all C are D gives most A are D. It never upgrades, and it never reverses.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`reverses-conditional`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Most pilots are in the 5,000-plus-hours group. What happens to everyone in that group?`,`And what happens to everyone on the senior roster?`,`Which choice carries "most pilots" through both steps without upgrading or reversing?`],labels:{topic:`pilot senior roster`}},{id:`lr-b-017`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`No employee may work overtime unless a supervisor approves it. Overtime hours must be paid at time-and-a-half. Chen worked four hours of overtime last week. No supervisor approved overtime for Chen last week. Chen's paycheck for last week paid those four hours at her regular rate.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`A supervisor approved Chen's overtime last week.`},{text:`Chen worked overtime last week without the required supervisory approval.`},{text:`Chen did not work any overtime last week.`},{text:`Chen was paid at time-and-a-half for her overtime hours.`},{text:`No employee ever works overtime without supervisory approval.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Chen worked overtime and no supervisor approved it, while approval is required for overtime work. So Chen worked overtime without the required approval. Choice B conjoins the two given facts; the rest contradict the premises or overgeneralize.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Separate the two violations. First, the approval rule: overtime requires supervisory approval. Chen worked four overtime hours and no supervisor approved overtime for her, so she worked overtime without the required approval, choice B. Note the pay facts are a distractor thread: the time-and-a-half rule plus the regular-rate paycheck establish a second, pay-related violation, but the question asks what must be true and choice B follows from the approval facts alone. Choice A contradicts the stated no-approval fact (trap: opposite). Choice C contradicts the stated overtime fact (trap: opposite). Choice D contradicts the paycheck fact (trap: opposite). Choice E universalizes to "no employee ever" (trap: too-strong, scope-shift): the premises describe Chen's case and the rules, not universal compliance. The difficulty is the two-thread stimulus tempting a choice about pay, but only B is airtight.`,choiceExplanations:[`This directly contradicts the stimulus (trap: opposite), which states that no supervisor approved overtime for Chen last week. It flips a stated fact, which can never be must-be-true.`,`This is correct: the rule requires approval for overtime, Chen worked overtime, and no supervisor approved it, so she necessarily worked without the required approval.`,`This directly contradicts the stimulus (trap: opposite), which states that Chen worked four hours of overtime last week. Denying a given fact is the fastest route to a wrong answer.`,`This directly contradicts the paycheck fact (trap: opposite): those four hours were paid at her regular rate, not at time-and-a-half. The paycheck detail exists precisely to rule this out.`,`This universalizes beyond the evidence (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The premises establish what happened in Chen's case and what the rules require, not that violations never occur.`],generalLesson:`When a stimulus runs two threads, check each answer against its own thread. A conclusion that conjoins two stated facts from the same thread is airtight; universalizing beyond the case is not.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the approval rule require, and what are the two facts about Chen and approval?`,`Put those together: what must be true of Chen's overtime?`,`Which choice states exactly that, without adding claims about pay or other employees?`],labels:{topic:`overtime approval`}},{id:`lr-b-018`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d4; two-step chain (won implies eligible; eligible requires premiere) with one irrelevant premise is d3 material. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 4 to 3.`}],stimulus:`To be eligible for the festival's top award, a film must have premiered at the festival. Only films directed by women are eligible for the top award. "Harbor Light" won the top award.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?`,choices:[{text:`All films that premiered at the festival were eligible for the top award.`},{text:`"Harbor Light" was eligible for the audience choice award.`},{text:`"Harbor Light" premiered at the festival.`},{text:`No film directed by a man premiered at the festival.`},{text:`Only films that won the top award premiered at the festival.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Winning implies eligibility, eligibility implies having premiered, so "Harbor Light" premiered at the festival. Choice C chains winning to eligibility to premiere. The rest reverse the conditionals or drift to other awards and other films.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Chain from the win. "Harbor Light" won the top award, and winning implies being eligible for it. "Only films directed by women are eligible" confirms eligibility is meaningful but the key link is: eligible implies premiered at the festival ("to be eligible, a film must have premiered"). So: won implies eligible implies premiered, and "Harbor Light" premiered at the festival, choice C. (Eligibility also implies directed by a woman, but that valid inference is deliberately not among the choices.) Choice A reverses the eligibility conditional (trap: nec-suff-confusion, reverses-conditional): premiering is necessary for eligibility, not sufficient. Choice B shifts to a different award never mentioned (trap: scope-shift). Choice D overreaches to other films (trap: unsupported): men's films could have premiered without being eligible. Choice E reverses the premiere conditional (trap: reverses-conditional): many films could premiere without winning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This reverses the eligibility conditional (trap: reverses-conditional, nec-suff-confusion). Premiering is necessary for eligibility, not sufficient; premiered films might still be ineligible on other grounds. Other eligibility requirements could disqualify a premiered film, blocking the reversal.`,`This shifts to a different award (trap: scope-shift). The audience choice award is never mentioned, so nothing about eligibility for it follows. Unmentioned awards are outside the premises' reach entirely.`,`This is correct: winning implies eligibility, and eligibility requires having premiered, so "Harbor Light" must have premiered at the festival in every consistent scenario. Each link is a stated conditional, so the two-step chain is airtight.`,`This goes beyond the premises (trap: unsupported). Films directed by men could have premiered without being eligible for the top award; only eligibility is restricted.`,`This reverses the premiere conditional (trap: reverses-conditional). Premiering is required for eligibility, but countless premiered films need not have won anything. The requirement runs from winning toward premiering, never backward.`],generalLesson:`Eligibility statements chain forward from the achieved status: won implies eligible implies each necessary condition. Necessary conditions never reverse into sufficient ones.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does winning the award imply about "Harbor Light's" eligibility?`,`What does eligibility require, according to the first statement?`,`Which choice chains those two links without reversing either?`],labels:{topic:`film festival award`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-b-019`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; clean MSS hedging item (prizes 'probably contributed'); causal-overclaim and universal distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`After the public library started a summer reading program offering prizes, children's book checkouts in July rose 40 percent compared to the previous July. Librarians report that many participating children said the prizes motivated them to read more.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the statements above?`,choices:[{text:`Children read more in July than in any other month.`},{text:`The prize program caused the entire 40 percent increase in checkouts.`},{text:`Prizes are the most effective way to encourage children to read.`},{text:`The reading program probably contributed to the rise in July checkouts.`},{text:`Checkout rates will continue to rise every summer.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Checkouts jumped after the program began and participants credit the prizes, which supports a modest causal contribution. Choice D hedges appropriately with "probably contributed." The other choices claim the whole effect, the best method, or future trends the evidence cannot support.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Most-strongly-supported answers must stay close to the evidence and hedge where the evidence is incomplete. The 40 percent jump coincided with the program, and children themselves cite the prizes as motivation, so the program probably contributed to the rise, choice D. The hedge "probably contributed" fits because other factors could also have played a role. Choice A compares July to all other months, which the year-over-year July comparison never addresses (trap: scope-shift). Choice B claims the entire increase was caused by the program (trap: too-strong): the evidence supports a contribution, not exclusivity. Choice C crowns prizes the most effective method (trap: too-strong): no comparison with other methods was made. Choice E predicts future summers (trap: unsupported): one year of data cannot guarantee a trend.`,choiceExplanations:[`This compares July to every other month (trap: scope-shift). The data compare this July to last July only; nothing is said about August, December, or any other month.`,`This claims the program caused the entire increase (trap: too-strong). The evidence supports that the program probably contributed, but other factors could account for part of the 40 percent jump.`,`This declares prizes the most effective method (trap: too-strong). No other encouragement methods were tested or compared, so no superlative is supported. Superlatives need comparisons, and none were made here.`,`This is correct: the timing of the increase plus the children's own reports make it likely the program contributed, while "probably" respects that other factors are not ruled out.`,`This predicts indefinite future increases (trap: unsupported). A single year-over-year comparison cannot establish a continuing trend. One data point cannot project a trend, however suggestive it feels.`],generalLesson:`The best-supported answer mirrors the evidence's strength: timed coincidence plus self-reports support "probably contributed," never "caused entirely," "best," or "always."`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two pieces of evidence link the program to the checkout increase?`,`Do they prove the program caused all of it, or only suggest it contributed?`,`Which choice matches that modest strength with hedging like "probably"?`],labels:{topic:`library summer reading program`}},{id:`lr-b-020`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; ruling-out-an-explanation inference (identical air quality); air-quality and normative distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Employees working in offices with plants reported 15 percent fewer headaches than employees in plantless offices. Air quality measurements in the two sets of offices were essentially identical.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Plants improve the air quality in offices.`},{text:`Every office should be required to have plants.`},{text:`Headaches are caused primarily by poor air quality.`},{text:`Employees who dislike plants report more headaches.`},{text:`The headache difference is likely not due to air quality.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Headaches differ while measured air quality does not, so air quality cannot explain the gap; something else must. Choice E draws exactly that inference. The other choices contradict the measurements, prescribe policy, or invent claims about attitudes and causes.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The key detail is the controlled comparison: air quality was essentially identical, yet headache reports differed by 15 percent. If air quality explained the headache difference, the measurements should have differed too. Since they did not, the difference is likely due to some other factor, choice E. Choice A contradicts the measurements (trap: opposite): air quality did not differ. Choice B leaps to a universal requirement (trap: too-strong, scope-shift): the data do not establish that plants caused the difference, let alone mandate them. Choice C is undermined by the data (trap: opposite): if poor air caused headaches, identical air should mean identical headaches. Choice D invents attitudes toward plants (trap: unsupported): nothing was measured about liking or disliking plants. Identical measurements across groups rule explanations out.`,choiceExplanations:[`This contradicts the measurements (trap: opposite). Air quality was essentially identical in both sets of offices, so the data give no support to plants improving it.`,`This leaps from a correlation to a universal mandate (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The study does not establish that plants caused the difference, let alone that every office must have them.`,`This is undermined by the data (trap: opposite). If air quality were the primary cause, identical air quality should have produced identical headache rates, but it did not.`,`Nothing was measured about employees' attitudes toward plants (trap: unsupported). The study compared offices with and without plants, not employees who like or dislike them.`,`This is correct: with air quality ruled out as the explanation by the identical measurements, the 15 percent gap is likely attributable to some other factor.`],generalLesson:`When a suspected explanation is measured and found equal across groups, it is ruled out, not confirmed. The supported inference points to "something else," stated modestly.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The headache rates differed. Did the air quality measurements differ?`,`If air quality explained the headache gap, what should the measurements have shown?`,`Which choice concludes that the explanation lies elsewhere?`],labels:{topic:`office plants and headaches`}},{id:`lr-b-021`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; clean MSS hedging item (market 'likely benefited' shops); entire-cause and always distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`A town's new Saturday farmers' market drew 2,000 visitors in its first month. Shop owners on the surrounding streets reported noticeably higher Saturday sales during that month than in the same month last year.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the statements above?`,choices:[{text:`The farmers' market likely benefited nearby shops.`},{text:`The market caused the entire increase in nearby shops' sales.`},{text:`Farmers' markets always help nearby businesses.`},{text:`Visitors spent more money at the market than at nearby shops.`},{text:`The farmers' market will succeed in the long term.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Two thousand visitors arrived the same month nearby sales rose, which supports a modest link between the market and the shops' gain. Choice A hedges with "likely." The rest claim total causation, universal rules, spending comparisons, or long-term success the one-month data cannot support.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The evidence is a timed coincidence: the market's first month brought 2,000 visitors, and nearby shops saw higher Saturday sales than the previous year. That supports the modest inference that the market likely benefited nearby shops, choice A, with "likely" respecting that other factors could contribute. Choice B claims the entire sales increase was caused by the market (trap: too-strong): the evidence supports a contribution, not exclusivity. Choice C universalizes to all farmers' markets always (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). Choice D compares visitor spending at the market versus shops, which was never measured (trap: unsupported). Choice E predicts long-term success from one month of data (trap: unsupported). One month of coincidence is suggestive, not decisive, so the hedge carries the inference.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: the coincidence of 2,000 new visitors with higher nearby sales supports a likely benefit, and "likely" keeps the claim as modest as the one-month evidence.`,`This claims the market caused the entire increase (trap: too-strong). The data support a probable contribution, but other factors could explain part of the sales rise.`,`This universalizes one town's first month into an always-true rule (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). A single case cannot support "always." Single cases support modest hypotheses, never universal laws.`,`Visitor spending was never broken down by location (trap: unsupported). The data report attendance and shop sales, not where visitors spent more. Spending location is an unmeasured variable, so comparisons about it fail.`,`One month of data cannot establish long-term success (trap: unsupported). Early crowds do not guarantee the market will thrive over time. Novelty effects often fade, so longevity needs more than an opening month.`],generalLesson:`Timed coincidence supports "likely contributed," not "caused entirely" or "always." Keep generalizations proportional to the sample: one case, one month, modest claim.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What happened in the same month the market opened?`,`Does that prove the market caused all of the sales gain, or only suggest it helped?`,`Which choice uses hedging like "likely" to match that strength?`],labels:{topic:`farmers market and nearby shops`}},{id:`lr-b-022`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; controlled-study MSS ('may affect'); 'caffeine has no effect' misreads the control; damage and intelligence distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`In a study, participants who slept eight hours recalled word lists better than participants who slept five hours. The difference persisted after the researchers controlled for age and caffeine intake.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the study described above?`,choices:[{text:`Everyone needs eight hours of sleep for good memory.`},{text:`Sleep duration may affect memory performance.`},{text:`Caffeine intake has no effect on memory.`},{text:`Lack of sleep causes permanent memory damage.`},{text:`The five-hour group was less intelligent than the eight-hour group.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The eight-hour group outperformed the five-hour group even after age and caffeine were controlled, supporting a link between sleep duration and memory. Choice B hedges with "may affect." The rest universalize, claim permanence, or invent differences the controls rule out.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The study compares two sleep durations and finds better recall with more sleep, with age and caffeine controlled. That supports the modest claim that sleep duration may affect memory performance, choice B. The controls matter: they rule out age and caffeine as the drivers, strengthening the sleep link without proving it. Choice A universalizes to everyone needing eight hours (trap: too-strong): the study tested two durations, not optimal sleep for all. Choice C overreads the control (trap: too-strong, unsupported): controlling for caffeine does not prove it has no effect, only that it does not explain this difference. Choice D claims permanent damage (trap: too-strong): the study measured recall, not lasting harm. Choice E invents an intelligence gap (trap: unsupported): intelligence was never measured, and the controls address age and caffeine only.`,choiceExplanations:[`This universalizes the finding (trap: too-strong). The study compared eight versus five hours on one task; it does not establish what everyone needs for good memory.`,`This is correct: the performance gap, surviving controls for age and caffeine, supports a possible effect of sleep duration on memory, with "may" matching the study's strength.`,`This overreads the control (trap: too-strong). Controlling for caffeine means it does not explain this particular gap; it does not prove caffeine never affects memory.`,`The study measured word-list recall, not lasting neurological harm (trap: too-strong). "Permanent damage" goes far beyond the observed performance difference. Measured outcomes bound the conclusion; unmeasured harms cannot be claimed.`,`Intelligence was never measured or controlled (trap: unsupported). The gap could reflect sleep alone, and inventing an intelligence difference adds an unmeasured cause. Unmeasured variables are off-limits, however tempting the speculation.`],generalLesson:`Controls rule out specific alternative explanations; they strengthen a "may affect" inference without proving "no effect" of the controlled variables or licensing universal claims.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What did the study find, and which alternative explanations did the controls address?`,`Does that prove sleep is the cause, or only suggest it may matter?`,`Which choice hedges appropriately instead of universalizing?`],labels:{topic:`sleep and memory study`}},{id:`lr-b-023`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; restating a flat result with proper hedge ('did not reduce'); preference and normative distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Restaurants that replaced tipping with a fixed service charge saw average server earnings stay flat while customer complaints about bills fell by half.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Customers prefer fixed service charges to tipping.`},{text:`Servers earn more under a fixed service charge.`},{text:`The service charge did not reduce servers' average earnings.`},{text:`Tipping should be abolished in all restaurants.`},{text:`Complaints fell because the food at these restaurants improved.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Average server earnings stayed flat after the switch, which directly supports that the service charge did not reduce them. Choice C stays within the data. The other choices infer preferences, prescribe policy, or invent causes the data never address.`,explanationWalkthrough:`"Stayed flat" is the key phrase: average earnings did not go down, so the service charge did not reduce them, choice C. This is nearly a restatement, and on most-strongly-supported questions the safest answer often hugs the data. Choice A infers customer preference (trap: unsupported): fewer complaints about bills do not prove overall preference for the charge. Choice B contradicts "flat" (trap: opposite). Choice D leaps to a universal prescription (trap: too-strong, scope-shift): the data describe what happened, not what should happen everywhere. Choice E invents a rival cause (trap: unsupported, causal-alternative): food quality was never measured, and the complaints were about bills, not food. 'Stayed flat' is a precise observation; honor its precision by choosing the closest paraphrase, not an inflated one.`,choiceExplanations:[`Fewer complaints about bills do not establish overall preference (trap: unsupported). Customers might dislike the charge for other reasons; preference was never surveyed. Behavior and complaints are not preference surveys, so preference claims overreach.`,`This contradicts the data (trap: opposite). Earnings stayed flat, which means they did not rise; "earn more" is directly ruled out. 'Flat' excludes both rising and falling, so both directions fail.`,`This is correct: "average earnings stayed flat" entails they were not reduced, making this the most directly supported inference. The closest paraphrase of the data is usually the best-supported answer.`,`This leaps from description to universal prescription (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The data cover some restaurants' results, not what all restaurants should do. 'Should' claims need normative premises, which the data do not supply.`,`Food quality was never measured, and the complaints concerned bills (trap: unsupported, causal-alternative). Inventing an unmeasured cause adds what the data lack. Rival causes must come from the evidence, not from imagination.`],generalLesson:`The strongest-supported answer often restates the data conservatively: "stayed flat" supports "did not reduce." Resist upgrading descriptions into preferences, prescriptions, or new causes.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What exactly happened to average server earnings?`,`What does "stayed flat" rule out?`,`Which choice states that consequence without adding preferences or prescriptions?`],labels:{topic:`restaurant service charge results`}},{id:`lr-b-024`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; 30-year correlation supports 'may be shifting' but not definite causation; too-strong, opposite, and preference distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`For the first time in 30 years of records, warblers arrived before the last spring frost. Over that same 30-year period, average spring temperatures in the region rose by 1.5 degrees Celsius.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the statements above?`,choices:[{text:`The temperature rise caused the warblers to arrive early.`},{text:`Warblers will arrive earlier every year from now on.`},{text:`Frost no longer occurs in the region.`},{text:`Warblers prefer warmer weather to colder weather.`},{text:`Spring warming may be shifting the warblers' migration timing.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`An unprecedented early arrival coinciding with 30 years of warming supports a tentative link between temperature and migration timing. Choice E hedges with "may be shifting." The rest assert definite causation, perpetual trends, or preferences the records cannot show.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two long-term trends coincide: unprecedented early arrival and 1.5 degrees of warming over the same 30 years. That coincidence supports the modest hypothesis that rising temperatures may be shifting migration timing, choice E. The 30-year baseline strengthens the inference by ruling out a one-off fluke, but correlation over time still does not prove causation. Choice A asserts definite causation (trap: too-strong): "caused" overclaims. Choice B predicts ever-earlier arrivals indefinitely (trap: unsupported, too-strong). Choice C contradicts the premise: the warblers arrived before the last frost, so frost still occurs (trap: opposite). Choice D attributes a preference to the birds (trap: unsupported): timing shifts do not reveal likes. Thirty years of baseline makes flukes unlikely but still cannot prove causation, since long baselines strengthen without proving.`,choiceExplanations:[`This asserts definite causation (trap: too-strong). The 30-year coincidence makes a temperature link plausible, but correlation does not establish that the warming caused the early arrival.`,`This predicts a perpetual trend (trap: unsupported, too-strong). One unprecedented year plus a warming trend does not guarantee earlier arrivals every future year. Trends describe the past; projecting them forever needs more than one record year.`,`This contradicts the stimulus (trap: opposite). The warblers arrived before the last spring frost, which means frost still occurred. 'Before the last frost' presupposes that a frost happened.`,`Migration timing reveals nothing about preferences (trap: unsupported). The birds might arrive earlier for reasons unrelated to liking warmth. Behavior underdetermines motive, so preference claims are speculative.`,`This is correct: the unprecedented timing plus the matching 30-year warming trend makes a temperature-driven shift a reasonable, hedged hypothesis. Two long trends moving together is exactly what 'may be shifting' claims.`],generalLesson:`Long-baseline coincidences support "may be" hypotheses, never "caused" certainties or perpetual forecasts. Match the answer's strength to the correlational evidence.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two trends coincide over the same 30 years?`,`Does coincidence prove the warming caused the early arrival?`,`Which choice proposes the link with "may" instead of asserting it?`],labels:{topic:`warbler migration timing`}},{id:`lr-b-025`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; cost-and-reliability synthesis ('reduced costs without hurting reliability'); 'better in every way' and normative distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`A transit agency replaced 20 diesel buses with electric ones. On the routes served by the new buses, fuel costs fell 60 percent and maintenance costs fell 25 percent, while on-time performance stayed the same.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`The electric buses reduced operating costs without hurting reliability.`},{text:`Electric buses are better than diesel buses in every way.`},{text:`The agency should replace its entire fleet with electric buses.`},{text:`Diesel buses are unreliable.`},{text:`On-time performance improved on the electric bus routes.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Fuel and maintenance costs, the operating costs, fell substantially while on-time performance, the reliability measure, held steady. Choice A summarizes exactly that. The rest universalize, prescribe, or contradict the unchanged reliability figure.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Break the data into cost and reliability. Fuel down 60 percent and maintenance down 25 percent are operating-cost reductions. On-time performance unchanged means reliability, as measured, was not hurt. Choice A combines both halves faithfully. Choice B claims superiority "in every way" (trap: too-strong): only costs and punctuality were measured. Choice C prescribes fleet-wide replacement (trap: scope-shift): the data cover 20 buses on certain routes, not a universal policy. Choice D calls diesel buses unreliable (trap: unsupported): nothing about diesel reliability was measured. Choice E contradicts "stayed the same" (trap: opposite). Cost and reliability are separate dimensions; the data speak to both, so the answer should too. Unchanged metrics support 'did not hurt,' never 'improved.' Keep each half of the comparison in its own lane.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: it faithfully combines the two findings, lower operating costs and unchanged on-time performance, without adding anything the data do not show. Faithful summaries that add nothing are the safest supported answers.`,`This claims superiority in every way (trap: too-strong). Only fuel costs, maintenance costs, and punctuality were measured; comfort, range, and lifespan were not. Unmeasured dimensions can never be swept into a superiority claim.`,`This leaps from 20 buses on some routes to a fleet-wide mandate (trap: scope-shift). The data describe a pilot-scale result, not a universal prescription. Pilot results inform pilots; mandates need broader evidence.`,`Diesel reliability was never measured (trap: unsupported). The data compare costs and punctuality for the new buses; they say nothing about diesel dependability. The comparison group is the new buses' own routes, not diesel performance.`,`This contradicts the data (trap: opposite). On-time performance stayed the same; it did not improve. 'Stayed the same' directly rules out any claim of improvement.`],generalLesson:`Summarize both halves of a before-after comparison without adding dimensions. Cost data support cost claims; unchanged metrics refute "improved" but support "not hurt."`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which two cost figures fell, and what happened to on-time performance?`,`What does unchanged on-time performance let you conclude about reliability?`,`Which choice combines those without prescribing or universalizing?`],labels:{topic:`electric bus fleet`}},{id:`lr-b-026`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; topic-attracted-newcomers MSS ('likely attracted'); 'best ever' and 'always' distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`A museum's exhibit on the city's industrial past drew record crowds. Surveys showed that most visitors had never visited the museum before, and many said they came specifically because of the exhibit's topic.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`The exhibit was the best the museum has ever mounted.`},{text:`The exhibit's topic likely attracted new visitors to the museum.`},{text:`History exhibits always draw record crowds.`},{text:`The museum will continue to grow its attendance.`},{text:`Visitors learned a great deal from the exhibit.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Record crowds, mostly first-time visitors, many citing the topic as their reason, support that the topic drew new visitors. Choice B hedges with "likely." The rest declare superlatives, universal rules, future growth, or learning the surveys never measured.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The surveys supply the causal link the attendance figures alone would lack: many visitors say the topic brought them. Combined with most being first-timers and crowds at record levels, the topic likely attracted new visitors, choice B. Choice A declares it the best exhibit ever (trap: too-strong): attendance records do not measure quality. Choice C universalizes to all history exhibits always (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). Choice D predicts continued growth (trap: unsupported): one exhibit's success does not guarantee a trend. Choice E claims visitors learned a great deal (trap: unsupported): learning was never surveyed, only motives and visit history. Motive surveys plus behavior are stronger than behavior alone, which is why this inference is reasonable. Still, motives are not quality ratings.`,choiceExplanations:[`Record attendance does not establish quality (trap: too-strong). "Best ever" is a superlative about merit; the data measure crowds, not excellence. Crowds measure popularity, which is distinct from quality.`,`This is correct: first-time visitors citing the topic as their reason, amid record crowds, makes it likely the topic attracted new visitors. Self-reported motives plus first-time status make the causal read reasonable.`,`One exhibit's success cannot support an always-true universal (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The data concern this exhibit only. Universals need broad evidence; single cases cannot carry them.`,`A single successful exhibit does not guarantee future growth (trap: unsupported). Trends require more than one data point. Forecasting from one observation is speculation, not support.`,`Learning was never measured (trap: unsupported). The surveys asked about visit history and reasons for coming, not about what visitors learned. Survey topics bound the conclusions; unasked questions yield nothing.`],generalLesson:`Self-reported motives plus behavioral data support modest "likely attracted" claims. Never convert attendance into quality, or one success into a universal rule or a forecast.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What do the surveys say about why visitors came?`,`Does record attendance prove the exhibit was the "best," or only that it drew people?`,`Which choice stays with the modest, hedged inference?`],labels:{topic:`museum industrial exhibit`}},{id:`lr-b-027`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,scopeSubtlety:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`A company's internal data showed that remote employees were promoted at lower rates than in-office peers with similar performance ratings.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Remote employees perform worse than in-office employees.`},{text:`The company intentionally discriminates against remote workers.`},{text:`Working in the office causes faster promotions.`},{text:`Working remotely may put employees at a disadvantage for promotion.`},{text:`Remote employees should return to the office.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`With performance ratings held similar, remote workers were still promoted less, supporting a modest disadvantage linked to remote work. Choice D hedges with "may." The rest contradict the similar ratings, claim intent or causation, or prescribe behavior.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The comparison controls for performance: ratings were similar, yet promotion rates differed. That pattern supports the guarded inference that remote work may disadvantage promotion chances, choice D. "May" is essential because the data are observational; visibility, networking, or manager bias could be the mechanism, and causation is not proven. Choice A contradicts the similar performance ratings (trap: opposite). Choice B claims intentional discrimination (trap: too-strong): the data show a gap, not its motive. Choice C asserts causation, "causes faster promotions" (trap: too-strong): correlation with a control is not proof. Choice E prescribes returning to the office (trap: scope-shift): the data describe a pattern, not a prescription. Observational gaps with controls earn 'may,' never 'causes' or 'proves,' because the hedge is the whole game.`,choiceExplanations:[`This contradicts the data (trap: opposite). The peers had similar performance ratings, so the promotion gap cannot be explained by worse performance. The controlled comparison exists precisely to rule this explanation out.`,`The data show a promotion gap, not anyone's intent (trap: too-strong). Discrimination implies motive, which internal promotion statistics cannot establish. Statistics reveal patterns, not the intentions behind them.`,`This upgrades correlation to causation (trap: too-strong). Similar ratings rule out performance as the driver, but many unmeasured factors could explain the gap. Controls narrow the field; they do not crown the remaining explanation.`,`This is correct: the gap, persisting at similar performance levels, supports a possible remote-work disadvantage, with "may" matching the observational evidence. The hedge is what makes this choice match the data's strength.`,`This leaps from description to prescription (trap: scope-shift). The data report promotion patterns; they do not recommend where anyone should work. Patterns describe; recommendations require separate normative reasons.`],generalLesson:`A gap that survives a control supports "may" language about disadvantage, not claims about performance, intent, or proven causation. Controls narrow the explanations; they do not prove the remaining one.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Performance ratings were similar, yet promotion rates differed. What does that rule out?`,`Does the remaining gap prove remote work causes slower promotion?`,`Which choice hedges with "may" instead of claiming proof?`],labels:{topic:`remote work promotions`}},{id:`lr-b-028`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`,`f-comparison`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; matched-groups causal MSS ('may contribute'); definite-causation and elimination distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Soil from farms using manure fertilizer contained more antibiotic-resistant bacteria than soil from farms using synthetic fertilizer. The two groups of farms were matched for crop type and region.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the findings described above?`,choices:[{text:`Manure fertilizer causes antibiotic resistance in soil bacteria.`},{text:`Synthetic fertilizer eliminates antibiotic-resistant bacteria.`},{text:`Manure fertilizer may contribute to resistant bacteria in soil.`},{text:`Farmers should stop using manure fertilizer.`},{text:`The resistant bacteria came from the crops rather than the fertilizer.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Manure farms show more resistant bacteria with crop type and region matched, supporting a tentative link to the fertilizer. Choice C hedges with "may contribute." The rest assert definite causation, elimination, prescriptions, or a rival source the matching undermines.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The matching is the crucial detail: because the farm groups were matched for crop type and region, those variables cannot easily explain the difference, which points more strongly at the fertilizer. Still, the study is observational, so the supported claim is the hedged "may contribute," choice C. Choice A asserts definite causation (trap: too-strong): matching strengthens the inference but does not prove it. Choice B claims synthetic fertilizer eliminates resistance (trap: opposite, too-strong): its farms merely had less. Choice D prescribes behavior (trap: scope-shift): findings describe, they do not mandate. Choice E blames the crops (trap: causal-alternative): the matching for crop type specifically undermines that rival, since both groups grew the same crops yet differed. Matching is a design strength, not a proof.`,choiceExplanations:[`This asserts proven causation (trap: too-strong). The matching makes the fertilizer link more plausible, but observational data cannot establish that manure causes the resistance. Matching strengthens plausibility; only experiments establish causation.`,`This claims elimination (trap: opposite, too-strong). Synthetic-fertilizer farms had less resistant bacteria, not none; "eliminates" overstates the comparison. 'Less' and 'none' are different claims, and only 'less' was observed.`,`This is correct: the difference, with crop type and region matched, supports a possible contribution from manure fertilizer, with "may" matching the observational strength. The matched design is what earns the 'may contribute' inference.`,`This leaps from findings to a mandate (trap: scope-shift). The study reports bacterial levels; it does not prescribe what farmers should do. Descriptive findings cannot by themselves generate mandates.`,`This proposes a rival the design undermines (trap: causal-alternative). Both groups were matched for crop type, so crops cannot easily explain the difference between the groups.`],generalLesson:`Matching on rival variables strengthens a "may contribute" inference by weakening alternatives, but it never proves causation. Note which rivals the design rules out and hedge accordingly.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What was matched between the two farm groups, and why does that matter?`,`Does matching prove the fertilizer caused the difference, or only make it more plausible?`,`Which choice hedges with "may" while ruling out the crop rival?`],labels:{topic:`manure fertilizer and resistant bacteria`}},{id:`lr-b-029`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; difference-in-differences MSS ('probably increased turnout'); only-way and counterfactual distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Counties that mailed ballots to all voters saw turnout rise 8 percentage points. Counties that did not mail ballots saw turnout rise 2 points. Demographic shifts were similar in both groups of counties.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Mailing ballots to all voters is the only way to raise turnout.`},{text:`Turnout would have fallen in the mailing counties without the mailed ballots.`},{text:`Voters prefer mailed ballots to voting in person.`},{text:`The 2-point rise in non-mailing counties was caused by demographic shifts.`},{text:`Mailing ballots to all voters probably increased turnout.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Mailing counties outpaced non-mailing counties by 6 points with similar demographics, supporting a probable turnout boost from the mailed ballots. Choice E hedges with "probably." The rest claim exclusivity, counterfactuals, preferences, or causes the data never isolate.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a difference-in-differences pattern: both groups rose, but the mailing group rose 6 points more, and similar demographic shifts rule out demographics as the driver. That supports the hedged claim that mailing ballots probably increased turnout, choice E. Choice A claims it is the only way (trap: too-strong): the non-mailing counties rose 2 points without it. Choice B asserts a counterfactual fall (trap: unsupported): the data show a smaller rise elsewhere, not a decline. Choice C infers voter preference (trap: unsupported): turnout effects do not reveal what voters prefer. Choice D attributes the 2-point rise to demographics (trap: unsupported, causal-alternative): similar shifts in both groups mean demographics cannot explain the between-group difference, and nothing isolates the within-group cause. Gaps net of shared trends are the evidence.`,choiceExplanations:[`This claims exclusivity (trap: too-strong). Turnout rose 2 points in counties that did not mail ballots, so mailing cannot be the only way to raise it.`,`This asserts an unobserved counterfactual (trap: unsupported). The data show a smaller increase elsewhere, which does not establish that mailing counties would have declined. Counterfactuals need a model of the alternative world, which the data lack.`,`Turnout behavior does not reveal preferences (trap: unsupported). Voters might vote by mail without preferring it, or prefer it without the data showing so. Actions underdetermine attitudes, so preference claims need surveys, not turnout.`,`This assigns a cause the data cannot isolate (trap: unsupported). Demographic shifts were similar in both groups, so they cannot explain the 6-point gap, and nothing identifies the 2-point rise's cause.`,`This is correct: the 6-point advantage with demographics held similar makes a turnout boost from mailed ballots the best-supported, properly hedged inference. The between-group gap, net of demographics, is the core evidence.`],generalLesson:`When both groups move but one moves more, the difference supports a hedged causal claim about the intervention. Similar background trends rule out the obvious rival; they do not license "only" or counterfactual certainties.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Both groups rose, but by different amounts. What was the gap?`,`Demographics were similar in both groups. What rival does that rule out?`,`Which choice claims a probable boost without saying "only" or "would have"?`],labels:{topic:`mailed ballots and turnout`}},{id:`lr-b-030`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; randomized-experiment causal MSS ('likely caused'); optimal-price and normative distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`An online store randomly showed half its visitors a $49 price and half a $39 price for the same headphones. Visitors shown $39 bought at three times the rate of those shown $49.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the experiment described above?`,choices:[{text:`The lower price likely caused the higher purchase rate.`},{text:`$39 is the optimal price for the headphones.`},{text:`Customers who saw $49 could not afford the headphones.`},{text:`The store should always price the headphones at $39.`},{text:`Higher prices never increase sales.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Random assignment isolates price as the cause of the threefold purchase gap, so the lower price likely caused the higher rate. Choice A is the direct causal read of a randomized experiment. The rest claim optimal pricing, affordability, permanent policy, or universal rules the single test cannot support.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Randomization is the key: because visitors were randomly assigned to prices, the groups differ only in the price shown, so the threefold purchase gap is attributable to price. That supports the causal claim that the lower price likely caused the higher purchase rate, choice A. "Likely" is still appropriate modesty, but randomized experiments earn causal language that observational studies do not. Choice B declares $39 optimal (trap: too-strong, unsupported): only two prices were tested, so nothing is known about $29 or $44. Choice C invents unaffordability (trap: unsupported): the $49 group bought less, but their budgets were never examined. Choice D prescribes permanent pricing (trap: scope-shift): one experiment does not settle long-run strategy. Choice E universalizes to "never" (trap: too-strong): luxury goods famously violate that claim, and one test cannot support it.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct: random assignment rules out other differences between the groups, so the price difference likely caused the purchase-rate difference. Randomization is what upgrades this from correlation to likely causation.`,`Optimality requires comparing many prices (trap: too-strong, unsupported). With only $39 and $49 tested, $34 or $44 might do better; "optimal" is unsupported. Two tested prices cannot identify a maximum among untested ones.`,`The experiment measured purchase rates, not budgets (trap: unsupported). The $49 group may have been able to afford the headphones yet judged them not worth it.`,`This converts one result into permanent policy (trap: scope-shift). The experiment shows what happened at two prices once, not what pricing should be forever. Single experiments inform; they do not legislate permanent strategy.`,`One experiment cannot support a universal "never" (trap: too-strong). The claim overreaches both the tested products and the tested prices. Universals demand sweeping evidence, not one product test.`],generalLesson:`Randomized experiments license causal "likely caused" inferences because assignment rules out rival explanations. They still do not support optimality, permanence, or universals beyond what was tested.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Why does random assignment matter for figuring out what caused the gap?`,`What is the one thing that differed between the two groups?`,`Which choice draws the causal inference without claiming optimality or permanence?`],labels:{topic:`online pricing experiment`}},{id:`lr-b-031`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; 'do not appear to hinder' hedging MSS; causal and universal-enrollment distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Students in bilingual programs scored as well in English as peers in English-only programs by fifth grade, and scored higher in math.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Bilingual programs cause higher math scores.`},{text:`Bilingual programs do not appear to hinder English development.`},{text:`All students should be enrolled in bilingual programs.`},{text:`English-only programs are ineffective.`},{text:`The two groups of students were equally intelligent.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Equal English scores by fifth grade directly support that bilingual programs did not hold English back. Choice B hedges with "do not appear to." The rest assert causation for math, universal enrollment, ineffectiveness, or equal intelligence the data cannot show.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The English result is the point: scoring "as well as" English-only peers means the bilingual students kept pace, supporting the guarded claim that the programs do not appear to hinder English development, choice B. "Do not appear to" fits because unmeasured selection effects could still lurk. Choice A upgrades the math gap to proven causation (trap: too-strong, causal-alternative): bilingual families may differ in unmeasured ways, and no random assignment occurred. Choice C prescribes universal enrollment (trap: scope-shift): the data describe outcomes, not policy. Choice D calls English-only programs ineffective (trap: too-strong, opposite): their students matched the bilingual group in English. Choice E invents equal intelligence (trap: unsupported): intelligence was never measured. Parity claims are modest; keep them modest, and let selection effects stay in view.`,choiceExplanations:[`This upgrades an observed gap to proven causation (trap: too-strong). Without random assignment, selection differences between the groups could explain the math scores. Selection effects are the standing rival in non-randomized comparisons.`,`This is correct: matching English-only peers in English by fifth grade supports, with proper hedging, that bilingual programs did not hinder English development. Parity with the comparison group is exactly what 'did not hinder' means.`,`This leaps from test results to a universal mandate (trap: scope-shift). The data describe two groups' scores; they do not prescribe enrollment for all students.`,`This is both too strong and backward (trap: too-strong, opposite). English-only students scored as well in English as the bilingual group, which contradicts "ineffective." Parity refutes ineffectiveness rather than supporting it.`,`Intelligence was never measured or compared (trap: unsupported). Equal scores do not establish equal intelligence, and the claim adds what the data lack. Scores reflect many inputs, so intelligence cannot be isolated from them.`],generalLesson:`"As well as" comparisons support "did not hinder" claims, not causal or prescriptive ones. Keep selection effects in mind: without randomization, group differences may predate the programs.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`causal-alternative`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does scoring "as well as" the English-only peers tell you about English development?`,`Does the math gap prove the program caused it, given no random assignment?`,`Which choice hedges with "do not appear to" instead of asserting cause?`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education outcomes`}},{id:`lr-b-032`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Neighborhoods where vacant lots were converted to green spaces saw violent crime fall 15 percent more than comparable neighborhoods without such conversions.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Green spaces eliminate violent crime.`},{text:`The crime drop was entirely due to the lot conversions.`},{text:`Vacant lots cause violent crime.`},{text:`Converting vacant lots to green spaces may reduce violent crime.`},{text:`Residents of converted neighborhoods feel safer.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Converted neighborhoods outperformed comparable ones by 15 percent, supporting a tentative crime-reduction effect. Choice D hedges with "may help reduce." The rest claim elimination, exclusive causation, reverse causation, or feelings the data never measure.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The "comparable neighborhoods" comparison is doing the work: it approximates a control group, so the 15 percent extra drop points to the conversions. But the design is still observational, so the supported claim is the hedged "may help reduce," choice D. Choice A claims elimination (trap: too-strong): crime fell, it did not vanish. Choice B claims the entire drop was due to conversions (trap: too-strong): other factors could contribute. Choice C reverses the causal arrow onto the lots themselves (trap: causal-alternative, too-strong): the data concern conversions' effects, not proof that lots cause crime. Choice E is about feelings of safety (trap: unsupported): only crime rates were measured. Comparable groups approximate experiments, which is why 'may help' fits the design. Comparison is the next best thing to randomization.`,choiceExplanations:[`Crime fell; it was not eliminated (trap: too-strong). "Eliminate" is an absolute the 15 percent figure directly contradicts. A 15 percent drop leaves 85 percent of the baseline intact.`,`This claims exclusive causation (trap: too-strong). The comparison supports a contribution from the conversions, but other neighborhood changes could account for part of the drop.`,`This asserts the lots themselves cause crime (trap: causal-alternative, too-strong). The data show conversions coinciding with bigger drops, which does not prove the pre-existing lots caused the baseline crime.`,`This is correct: the 15 percent advantage over comparable neighborhoods supports a possible crime-reducing effect, with "may help" matching the observational design. The comparable-neighborhood design is what makes 'may help' reasonable.`,`Feelings of safety were never measured (trap: unsupported). The data track violent crime rates, not residents' perceptions. Perceptions and rates are different variables requiring different measures.`],generalLesson:`Comparable-group comparisons support hedged "may help" claims. They never support elimination, exclusivity, reversed causal arrows, or unmeasured psychological outcomes.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`causal-alternative`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the comparison with "comparable neighborhoods" add?`,`Does a 15 percent bigger drop prove the conversions caused all of it?`,`Which choice hedges with "may help" instead of claiming elimination?`],labels:{topic:`green space and crime`}},{id:`lr-b-033`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-comparison`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; same-apiary comparison MSS ('likely boosted'); only-factor and normative distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Hives placed near wildflower strips produced 20 percent more honey than hives in the same apiary placed far from the strips.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`Wildflowers are the only factor affecting honey production.`},{text:`All beekeepers should plant wildflower strips.`},{text:`Access to wildflower strips likely boosted honey production.`},{text:`The hives far from the strips were unhealthy.`},{text:`Honey production will keep increasing near wildflower strips.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Same apiary, 20 percent more honey near the strips: the shared setting rules out many rivals, supporting a likely boost from strip access. Choice C hedges with "likely." The rest claim exclusivity, prescribe behavior, invent hive health, or forecast trends.`,explanationWalkthrough:`"In the same apiary" is the control: the hives share beekeeper, climate, and management, so the location difference points to the strips. That supports the hedged claim that strip access likely boosted production, choice C. Choice A claims wildflowers are the only factor (trap: too-strong): one comparison cannot rule out all other influences. Choice B prescribes for all beekeepers (trap: scope-shift): the data cover one apiary. Choice D invents poor health in the far hives (trap: unsupported): lower production does not mean unhealthy. Choice E forecasts ever-increasing production (trap: unsupported): one measurement cannot establish a trend. Shared settings are quiet controls; notice them. They do the work of ruling out beekeeper, weather, and management differences without a formal experiment. That is why the inference is reasonable.`,choiceExplanations:[`This claims exclusivity (trap: too-strong). The comparison supports the strips as a contributor, but a single test cannot rule out every other factor in honey production.`,`This leaps from one apiary to a universal prescription (trap: scope-shift). The data describe what happened here, not what every beekeeper should do. Local results advise local beekeepers; universals need replication.`,`This is correct: with the apiary shared, the 20 percent gap points to the strips, and "likely" keeps the inference as modest as the single comparison.`,`Lower honey output does not establish poor health (trap: unsupported). The far hives may have been perfectly healthy but simply had less forage. Output differences reflect forage access, not necessarily hive health.`,`One measurement cannot establish a continuing trend (trap: unsupported). Production might plateau or vary year to year regardless of the strips. Trends need time series; one snapshot cannot supply one.`],generalLesson:`A shared setting acts as a control, strengthening a "likely boosted" inference. It still does not support "only factor," universal prescriptions, or trend forecasts.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "in the same apiary" rule out as an explanation?`,`Does the 20 percent gap prove the strips were the only factor?`,`Which choice hedges with "likely" instead of prescribing or forecasting?`],labels:{topic:`wildflower strips and honey`}},{id:`lr-b-034`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-numbers`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; long-choice MSS requiring hedging discipline ('appear to have cut... consistent with'); definite-causation and 'always' distractors fail; 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`After a city raised its minimum wage, fast-food outlets increased their use of self-service kiosks. Employment at those outlets fell 5 percent while total hours worked fell only 1 percent.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,choices:[{text:`The minimum-wage increase caused outlets to install kiosks in direct response to rising labor costs rather than other factors.`},{text:`Kiosks eliminated 5 percent of the outlets' jobs within the first year of their introduction.`},{text:`The outlets' workers earned less in total after the wage increase despite the higher hourly rate.`},{text:`Automation always follows minimum-wage increases in every industry without exception.`},{text:`The outlets appear to have cut positions while largely preserving total hours, consistent with substituting kiosks for some labor.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Headcount fell 5 percent but hours fell only 1 percent, so the outlets shed positions while keeping most hours, a pattern consistent with kiosk substitution. Choice E synthesizes the asymmetry with proper hedging. The rest assert definite causation, exact attribution, earnings totals, or universal rules.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The crucial detail is the asymmetry: employment down 5 percent, hours down only 1 percent. If outlets had simply cut back proportionally, both would fall together. The gap means fewer workers covering nearly the same total hours, which fits replacing some positions with kiosks while preserving hours for remaining staff. Choice E states exactly that pattern with "appear" and "consistent with," the right hedging for observational data. Choice A asserts the wage hike caused the kiosks (trap: too-strong): timing suggests but does not prove it. Choice B attributes the full 5 percent to kiosks (trap: too-strong): other adjustments could contribute. Choice C claims total earnings fell (trap: unsupported): with wages up and hours nearly flat, earnings likely rose; the data cannot settle it either way. Choice D universalizes to "always" (trap: too-strong). The difficulty is synthesizing two percentages rather than latching onto the timing story.`,choiceExplanations:[`This asserts definite causation from timing alone (trap: too-strong). The kiosk increase followed the wage hike, but correlation does not prove the hike caused it; other factors could drive automation.`,`This attributes the entire 5 percent employment drop to kiosks (trap: too-strong). The data show the drop coincided with kiosk growth, not that kiosks account for all of it.`,`Total earnings cannot be determined (trap: unsupported). Wages rose while hours fell 1 percent, so earnings may well have increased; the data do not settle the arithmetic.`,`One city's experience cannot support an "always" universal (trap: too-strong). The claim overreaches the single case in both scope and certainty. Single cases never license 'always,' however striking they are.`,`This is correct: the 5-versus-1 percent asymmetry means positions were cut more than hours, a pattern consistent with substituting kiosks for some labor while preserving remaining hours.`],generalLesson:`When headcount and hours move asymmetrically, synthesize: fewer people working nearly the same total hours. Prefer "consistent with" over "caused" for observational patterns, and never compute what the data cannot settle.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Employment fell 5 percent but hours fell only 1 percent. What does that asymmetry mean?`,`If outlets had just scaled back evenly, how would the two figures compare?`,`Which choice describes that pattern with hedging instead of asserting causation?`],labels:{topic:`minimum wage and kiosks`}},{id:`lr-b-035`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-deduction`,`f-causation`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d4; single synthesis step (both groups improved plus randomization supports 'accelerated gains') is d3 material. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 4 to 3.`}],stimulus:`A tutoring program randomly assigned struggling readers to tutoring or a waitlist. Tutored students gained 8 percentile points more than waitlisted students, but both groups improved over the term.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the results described above?`,choices:[{text:`The tutoring was the sole cause of the tutored students' improvement.`},{text:`The waitlisted students received no reading help at all.`},{text:`Every struggling reader would benefit from this tutoring.`},{text:`The tutoring likely accelerated gains the students would have made anyway.`},{text:`The program should be adopted by schools nationwide.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Random assignment plus an 8-point advantage shows the tutoring added value, while the waitlist's improvement shows gains would have occurred anyway. Choice D synthesizes both facts with "likely." The rest claim sole causation, universal benefit, or national policy the study cannot support.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts must both be honored. Random assignment means the 8-point advantage is attributable to the tutoring, so it added real value. But the waitlisted students also improved, so improvement was happening regardless; the tutoring accelerated gains that would have occurred anyway. Choice D captures both with "likely." Choice A claims sole causation (trap: too-strong): the waitlist's improvement refutes "sole." Choice B invents the waitlist's conditions (trap: unsupported): waitlisted students may have received other help. Choice C universalizes to every struggling reader (trap: too-strong, scope-shift): the study tested its sample, not all readers. Choice E prescribes nationwide adoption (trap: scope-shift): results describe efficacy, not policy. The difficulty is resisting the strong causal signal from randomization while still honoring the waitlist's gains.`,choiceExplanations:[`This claims sole causation (trap: too-strong). The waitlisted students improved without the tutoring, which directly refutes the idea that tutoring was the only cause of improvement.`,`The study says nothing about what the waitlisted students received (trap: unsupported). They may have had classroom instruction or other support; "waitlist" means only that they did not get this tutoring.`,`This universalizes the result to every struggling reader (trap: too-strong, scope-shift). The study's sample may not represent all struggling readers, and individual responses vary. Samples support claims about similar students, not all students everywhere.`,`This is correct: randomization supports the tutoring's added value, while the waitlist's gains show improvement was underway anyway, so the tutoring likely accelerated those gains.`,`This leaps from study results to national policy (trap: scope-shift). Efficacy in one study does not by itself settle cost, scalability, or whether schools should adopt the program.`],generalLesson:`Honor both the treatment advantage and the control group's progress: the intervention likely accelerated gains that were occurring anyway. Randomization strengthens causation; it does not license "sole cause" or universal prescriptions.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does random assignment let you conclude about the 8-point advantage?`,`What does the waitlisted group's improvement tell you about what would have happened anyway?`,`Which choice combines both facts with "likely" instead of claiming sole cause?`],labels:{topic:`reading tutoring program`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`}],n=[{id:`lr-c-071`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`City council member Alvarez has proposed adding protected bike lanes on three downtown avenues. Speaking against the proposal at yesterday's hearing, local business owner Mr. Patel argued that the plan should be rejected because Alvarez herself commutes by bicycle and therefore cannot evaluate the plan impartially.`,stem:`The reasoning in Mr. Patel's argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`presumes that a proposal supported by a cyclist could never benefit drivers who share the same downtown streets.`},{text:`rejects the bike-lane proposal by attacking Alvarez’s personal circumstances rather than addressing the proposal’s merits`},{text:`fails to consider that some downtown business owners might support the proposal for their own commercial reasons.`},{text:`confuses a necessary condition for impartiality with a sufficient condition for it, misapplying basic logic.`},{text:`draws a conclusion about all commuters from the observed habits of a single commuter interviewed downtown.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Patel never engages with any evidence about bike lanes — safety data, traffic studies, costs. He dismisses the proposal because of who proposed it: Alvarez bikes, so she must be biased. That is the circumstantial ad hominem fallacy: attacking the arguer's situation instead of answering the argument.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Start by separating the conclusion from the premises. Patel's conclusion is that the bike-lane plan should be rejected. His only premise is that Alvarez commutes by bicycle and therefore cannot judge the plan impartially. Notice what is missing: not a single word about whether bike lanes are safe, effective, affordable, or popular. The argument substitutes a claim about the proposer for a claim about the proposal. That is the circumstantial ad hominem fallacy — rejecting an argument because of the personal circumstances of the person making it rather than because of anything wrong with the argument's evidence or logic. Even if Alvarez does ride a bike, the proposal might still be excellent policy; her commuting habits say nothing about lane safety or traffic flow. Choice (A) overstates the argument — Patel never claims the plan could never help drivers. Choice (C) raises a true but irrelevant point about other business owners. Choice (D) dresses up the flaw in conditional-logic jargon the argument never uses, and (E) describes a hasty generalization that isn't what happened: Patel's error is not generalizing from one cyclist but refusing to engage the proposal at all.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is tempting because Patel does seem hostile to cyclists, but it is too strong: he never claims the plan could never benefit drivers, only that it should be rejected because of Alvarez's alleged bias. The argument's actual flaw is the personal attack, not an exaggerated claim about drivers.`,`Correct. The premise concerns Alvarez's personal commuting habits; the conclusion concerns whether the city should build bike lanes. Nothing connects the two except an attack on the proposer. That gap — arguer instead of argument — is precisely the circumstantial ad hominem flaw the stem asks you to name.`,`It may well be true that some business owners support the plan, but Patel's failure to mention them is not what makes his reasoning bad. This choice is irrelevant-true: a factually plausible observation that does not identify any logical error in moving from his premise to his conclusion.`,`This is tempting jargon, but the argument never frames impartiality in terms of necessary and sufficient conditions. It simply asserts bias from bike commuting. Mislabeling the flaw with fancy conditional language is a classic trap: the label sounds technical but does not match the reasoning on the page.`,`Patel does reason from one person, but his error is not a scope shift from one commuter to all commuters — he never draws any conclusion about commuters in general. His conclusion is about the proposal, and his defect is attacking its sponsor rather than its merits.`],generalLesson:`Attacking the person making an argument is not the same as answering the argument. Always check whether a criticism engages the evidence and reasoning offered or merely targets the source.`,misconceptionTags:[`ad-hominem`,`source-vs-argument`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is Patel's conclusion, and what evidence does he offer for it? Does any of that evidence concern bike lanes themselves?`,`When an argument dismisses a claim because of something about the person making the claim — rather than the claim's evidence — what is that fallacy called?`,`The correct choice will name the personal attack directly: it rejects the proposal by going after Alvarez's circumstances instead of the proposal's merits.`],labels:{topic:`downtown bike lanes`}},{id:`lr-c-072`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-samples`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: narrow regular-customer sample cannot support a whole-clientele conclusion; credited sampling flaw is uniquely defensible, distractors miss the sample-population gap. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the Harbor Grill introduced its new seasonal menu, the manager surveyed twenty regular customers as they left the restaurant on a Friday night. All twenty said they loved the new dishes. The manager concluded that the new menu will be a hit with the restaurant's clientele as a whole.`,stem:`The manager's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`fails to establish that the new menu is more profitable than the old one`},{text:`confuses the popularity of the restaurant with the quality of its menu`},{text:`presumes without justification that regular customers are the restaurant's most important patrons`},{text:`generalizes about all of the restaurant's customers from a small sample of regulars surveyed on a single evening`},{text:`overlooks the possibility that diners' tastes will change over time`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Twenty regulars on one Friday night are not a representative sample of the whole clientele — regulars already like the restaurant, and one evening's crowd may differ from lunch or weekday diners. The manager leaps from this narrow, friendly sample to a conclusion about everyone, which is a hasty generalization from an unrepresentative sample.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion is sweeping: the new menu will be a hit with the clientele as a whole. The evidence is narrow: twenty people, all regulars, all surveyed on a single Friday night. Two sampling problems stack up. First, regulars are a biased group — people who already love the Harbor Grill are the most likely to praise anything it serves. Second, one evening's departing diners may not resemble the broader clientele across lunches, weekdays, and seasons. A generalization is only as good as its sample's representativeness, and this sample fails that test twice over. That makes (D) the precise flaw: hasty generalization from an unrepresentative sample. Choice (A) answers a different question — profitability was never at issue. Choice (B) misdescribes the reasoning with a scope shift the manager never makes. Choice (C) invents a claim about "most important patrons" the manager never relies on. Choice (E) raises a real but secondary worry; even if tastes never changed, the evidence still would not support the conclusion, because the sample cannot speak for the whole clientele in the first place.`,choiceExplanations:[`Profitability is a different question from the one the manager addresses. The argument is about whether customers will like the menu, not whether it will make money. Criticizing the argument for ignoring profits is the answers-different-question trap.`,`The manager never moves from "the restaurant is popular" to "the menu is good," so this scope-shift label does not fit. The actual move is from "these twenty regulars loved it" to "everyone will love it" — a sampling problem, not a popularity-versus-quality confusion.`,`This choice is tempting because regulars do get special emphasis in the survey, but the manager never claims they are the most important patrons. The flaw is not a ranking of patron importance; it is treating a narrow, friendly sample as if it represented the entire clientele.`,`Correct. The sample is both tiny and systematically unrepresentative: regulars are predisposed to like the restaurant, and a single Friday night cannot stand in for the whole clientele. Leaping from that sample to "a hit with the clientele as a whole" is a textbook hasty generalization.`,`Changing tastes are a genuine long-run concern, but this criticism is too weak to be the flaw the stem wants. The argument fails before time even enters the picture: its evidence cannot establish that the menu is popular now, among the whole clientele, because of who was asked.`],generalLesson:`A conclusion about a whole group is only as strong as the sample behind it. Ask who was surveyed, how they were chosen, and whether they resemble the group the conclusion is about.`,misconceptionTags:[`sample-bias`,`representativeness`,`hasty-generalization`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Who exactly was surveyed, and who is the conclusion about? Do those two groups match?`,`Regulars who already love the restaurant, surveyed on one night — what sampling problem does that create?`,`The correct choice names the leap directly: generalizing about the whole clientele from a small, unrepresentative sample.`],labels:{topic:`restaurant menu survey`}},{id:`lr-c-073`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: two extra Thursday hours distorted into around-the-clock operation is a clean straw-person; no distractor is defensible, no leakage. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Curator Dr. Ellis proposed keeping the natural history museum open two hours later on Thursday evenings to accommodate working visitors. Board chair Mr. Okafor rejected the idea, saying, "We simply cannot afford to keep this museum open around the clock."`,stem:`Mr. Okafor's response is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`misrepresents Ellis's proposal as calling for far more extensive hours than she actually proposed`},{text:`presumes without evidence that working visitors would not visit the museum at other times`},{text:`fails to consider the museum's budget constraints when evaluating the proposal`},{text:`attacks Dr. Ellis's character rather than addressing her proposal`},{text:`draws a conclusion about Thursday evenings from evidence concerning weekends`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Ellis proposed two extra hours on one evening; Okafor answers as if she had proposed keeping the museum open around the clock. He distorts her position into an exaggerated version that is easy to knock down — the straw person fallacy.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Okafor's conclusion is that Ellis's proposal should be rejected. But the proposal he rejects is not the one Ellis made. She asked for two additional hours on Thursday evenings; he responds to a imaginary proposal to keep the museum "open around the clock." By substituting an exaggerated, easily defeated version of her position, he commits the straw person fallacy: he knocks down a caricature instead of engaging the actual argument. This matters because refuting "open 24 hours" says nothing about whether two extra Thursday hours are affordable or worthwhile. Choice (B) invents a premise Okafor never uses. Choice (C) gets things backward — budget is the very ground he invokes, so he hardly ignores it. Choice (D) is tempting because the exchange feels dismissive, but Okafor never attacks Ellis's character; he attacks a distorted version of her proposal. Choice (E) hallucinates evidence about weekends that appears nowhere in the stimulus.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Ellis proposed two extra hours on Thursdays; Okafor argues against keeping the museum open "around the clock." That is not her proposal. Exaggerating an opponent's position into a weaker version and then refuting the exaggeration is the straw person fallacy.`,`Okafor never discusses when working visitors might otherwise visit, so this choice attributes to him a premise he never states. It is an unsupported invention rather than a description of his actual reasoning error.`,`This choice states the opposite of what happened: Okafor's entire objection is about affordability ("we cannot afford"). He does not ignore budget constraints — he leans on them while misrepresenting what the budget would need to cover.`,`This is a tempting mislabel because Okafor's reply is dismissive in tone. But a dismissive tone is not an ad hominem: he never criticizes Ellis herself, her motives, or her character. His target is a distorted version of her proposal, which is a straw person, not a personal attack.`,`Nothing in the stimulus mentions weekends or evidence about them. This choice describes a scope shift that never occurs, making it pure invention rather than a diagnosis of the actual flaw.`],generalLesson:`When someone rejects an argument, check that they are rejecting the argument as actually made. Refuting an exaggerated or distorted version — a straw person — leaves the real position untouched.`,misconceptionTags:[`straw-person`,`misrepresentation`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`opposite`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Compare what Ellis actually proposed with the proposal Okafor argues against. Are they the same?`,`"Two extra hours on Thursdays" versus "open around the clock" — what has Okafor done to her position?`,`The correct choice names the distortion: Okafor refutes an exaggerated version of the proposal rather than the proposal itself.`],labels:{topic:`museum evening hours`}},{id:`lr-c-074`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: light shifts senses (low-calorie vs light-colored); credited equivocation uniquely defensible; A is a genuine half-right near-miss, correctly rejected. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A supermarket chain's advertisement claims: "Nutritionists agree that light foods can help you manage your weight. Our new Light Harvest line is the light choice for your family." In fact, the products are called "light" only because they are lighter in color than the chain's regular products; their calorie counts are nearly identical.`,stem:`The advertisement's reasoning is flawed in that it`,choices:[{text:`appeals to the authority of nutritionists without identifying any of them`},{text:`presumes without justification that shoppers care more about a product's color than about its nutrition`},{text:`uses the word "light" in two different senses — low in calories and light in color — as if they were the same thing`},{text:`fails to prove that the Light Harvest products are unhealthy`},{text:`generalizes from the opinions of a few nutritionists to nutritionists as a group`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument only works if "light" means the same thing in both sentences: low-calorie foods help manage weight, so these "light" products will too. But the second "light" means light in color. Trading on that shift in meaning is equivocation — the word does the persuading, not the evidence.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the advertisement's logic. Premise: nutritionists say light (low-calorie) foods help manage weight. Conclusion implied: the Light Harvest line, being "light," is the smart choice for weight management. For that inference to work, "light" must carry its low-calorie meaning into the conclusion. The stimulus tells us it does not: here "light" means only lighter in color, with nearly identical calories. The argument therefore slides between two meanings of one word, letting the favorable associations of the first meaning rub off on the second. That is equivocation. Choice (A) is half-right — the unnamed nutritionists are a genuine weakness — but it is not the flaw that makes the reasoning collapse; even fully cited nutritionists would not fix the meaning shift. Choice (B) invents a claim about shoppers' priorities. Choice (D) answers a different question: the ad never claims the products are unhealthy, so no proof is owed. Choice (E) quibbles about "nutritionists agree," which is beside the point; the fatal move is the double meaning of "light."`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is half-right and therefore tempting: the appeal to unnamed nutritionists is indeed weak. But it is not the flaw that breaks the argument. Even if every cited nutritionist were named and eminent, the reasoning would still trade on two meanings of "light." The stem asks for the central flaw, and this is a side issue.`,`The advertisement never makes any claim about what shoppers care about, so this choice attributes an unsupported premise to it. The flaw is in how the word "light" is used, not in assumptions about consumer priorities.`,`Correct. The weight-management claim needs "light" to mean low-calorie, while the product name uses "light" to mean light-colored. The argument treats the two senses as interchangeable, which is equivocation — the conclusion follows only because the key term changed meaning mid-argument.`,`The ad never asserts the products are unhealthy, so it owes no proof of that. Demanding proof of a claim that was never made is the answers-different-question trap: it criticizes the argument for failing a task it never undertook.`,`Whether "nutritionists agree" overstates the consensus is a minor quibble, not the structural flaw. This choice commits its own scope shift — from the real problem (the double meaning of "light") to a side complaint about how nutritionists are described.`],generalLesson:`Watch for key terms that quietly change meaning between premise and conclusion. If an argument's force depends on a word meaning one thing early and another thing later, the reasoning is equivocal — and invalid.`,misconceptionTags:[`equivocation`,`ambiguity`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "light" mean in the nutritionists' claim, and what does it mean in the product name? Is it the same?`,`When an argument's conclusion only follows because a key word changed meaning along the way, what is that flaw called?`,`The correct choice points at the word "light" itself and names its two different senses.`],labels:{topic:`grocery product labeling`}},{id:`lr-c-075`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: argument frames only two options where more exist; credited false dilemma uniquely identifies the unsupported frame; A is a plausible secondary weakness but not the dilemma. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`At the school board meeting, Trustee Nguyen argued: "Our budget shortfall leaves us with only two options: eliminate the music program or eliminate the athletics program. Since athletics keeps students engaged in school, we must eliminate the music program."`,stem:`Trustee Nguyen's argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`presumes that athletics is more valuable than music without defending that value judgment`},{text:`ignores the possibility that the budget shortfall is only temporary`},{text:`attacks the supporters of the music program instead of addressing their arguments`},{text:`concludes that athletics should be preserved merely by restating the premise that it keeps students engaged`},{text:`presents the choice as limited to two alternatives without ruling out other ways of addressing the shortfall`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Nguyen frames the decision as music versus athletics, but never shows those are the only options — the board could cut elsewhere, raise funds, or trim both programs partially. By artificially narrowing the field to two, he commits the false dilemma fallacy: the conclusion only follows if no third option exists, and that was never established.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Nguyen's reasoning has two layers. The visible layer: athletics engages students, so cut music instead. The hidden layer doing the real work: these are the only two options. If a third option existed — a smaller across-the-board cut, a tax levy, drawing on reserves, trimming administrative costs — the conclusion "we must eliminate music" would not follow even if everything Nguyen says about athletics is true. A dilemma is only as good as its exhaustiveness, and Nguyen simply asserts "only two options" without defending it. That is the false dilemma (false dichotomy) fallacy. Choice (A) is half-right: the value judgment about athletics is undefended, but it is not the structural flaw — even granting that athletics matters, the two-option frame is what forces the conclusion. Choice (B) raises a real but secondary point; a temporary shortfall still needs addressing, and the two-option claim is the deeper problem. Choice (C) mislabels the move as a personal attack that never occurs. Choice (D) misreads the structure: the engagement premise is used to choose between the two options, not merely restated.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is half-right and tempting: Nguyen does favor athletics without a full defense. But an undefended value judgment is not the structural flaw. Even if athletics clearly keeps students engaged, the conclusion "we must eliminate music" still requires that no third option exists — and that premise is the one doing the illicit work.`,`A temporary shortfall would change the calculus, so this point is not silly — but it is too weak to be the flaw the stem wants. The argument collapses earlier: it never establishes that music and athletics are the only two candidates for cuts, temporary shortfall or not.`,`Nguyen never attacks any person or group; his targets are budget line items. Labeling this a personal attack is the wrong-viewpoint trap — it mistakes criticism of a program for criticism of people.`,`This misdescribes the reasoning. Nguyen does not merely restate the engagement premise as his conclusion; he uses it to decide between two options. The defect is not circularity but the unproven claim that there are only two options to decide between.`,`Correct. The entire argument hinges on "only two options," which is asserted, not shown. The board might cut elsewhere, raise revenue, or reduce both programs partially. Without ruling out alternatives, the forced choice between music and athletics — and hence the conclusion — is a false dilemma.`],generalLesson:`Whenever an argument says "our only options are A or B," stop and ask whether option C was ruled out. An unproven either/or is a false dilemma, and the conclusion cannot stand until the alternatives are genuinely exhausted.`,misconceptionTags:[`false-dilemma`,`exhaustiveness`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Nguyen says there are "only two options." What would have to be true for that claim — and does he show it?`,`If a third way to handle the shortfall existed, would his conclusion still follow?`,`The correct choice targets the either/or frame itself: the options were never shown to be exhaustive.`],labels:{topic:`school budget cuts`}},{id:`lr-c-076`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,abstraction:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL - instructional use only, prose/structure too artificial for assessment; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Each individual brick in the garden wall is light enough for a child to lift. Therefore, the garden wall as a whole must be light enough for a child to lift.`,stem:`The reasoning above is flawed because it`,choices:[{text:`treats what is true of each part as true of the whole.`},{text:`ignores the possibility that some bricks are heavier than others`},{text:`confuses the weight of the wall with the weight of the bricks it contains`},{text:`draws a conclusion about children from premises about bricks`},{text:`fails to consider how the wall was constructed`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Lightness of each brick does not transfer to the whole wall: a thousand light bricks make a heavy wall. The argument illicitly moves a property from the parts to the whole, which is the fallacy of composition.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The premise is about each part: every brick is light. The conclusion is about the whole: the wall is light. The hidden assumption is that a property of the parts automatically belongs to the whole — and that assumption is false. Weight is additive: however light each brick is, enough of them stacked together are far beyond a child's lifting capacity. This is the fallacy of composition: inferring that a whole has a property because each of its parts does. Properties like weight, size, and cost often do not transfer from parts to wholes, so the inference needs independent support. Choice (B) misses the point — even perfectly uniform bricks would not save the argument. Choice (C) sounds relevant but merely gestures at weight without naming the faulty transfer. Choice (D) invents a scope shift about children that is not the issue; the child appears in both premise and conclusion. Choice (E) is irrelevant: construction method has nothing to do with whether part-to-whole transfer is legitimate.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The argument's entire move is parts-to-whole: each brick is light, so the wall is light. That transfer is illegitimate for additive properties like weight, and naming the transfer directly identifies the fallacy of composition.`,`This choice is tempting because it spots a possible complication, but it fails to diagnose the flaw. Even if every brick weighed exactly the same, the wall as a whole would still be heavy. The problem is structural — the part-to-whole inference — not variation among bricks.`,`This choice is vague in a way that sounds analytical but says nothing precise. It gestures at "confusing" two weights without identifying the actual error: the illegitimate transfer of a property from each part to the whole.`,`There is no such scope shift here. The child appears in both the premise ("light enough for a child to lift") and the conclusion, so nothing illicitly moves from bricks to children. This choice describes a flaw the argument does not commit.`,`How the wall was built is irrelevant to the logical error. Whether mortared or dry-stacked, a wall of many light bricks is heavy. This choice is the irrelevant-true trap: a factor that might matter practically but does not touch the reasoning.`],generalLesson:`Properties of parts do not automatically belong to wholes. Whenever an argument moves from "each part is X" to "the whole is X," ask whether X is the kind of property that survives aggregation.`,misconceptionTags:[`composition`,`part-whole`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premise is about each brick. The conclusion is about the wall. What kind of move is that?`,`Think about weight: if every brick is light, what happens when you stack a thousand of them?`,`The correct choice names the transfer directly — what is true of each part is assumed true of the whole.`],labels:{topic:`garden wall weight`}},{id:`lr-c-077`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: conclusion restates a premise as support (circular); A is a source-vs-reasoning near-miss, correctly rejected. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The Riverside Gallery's brochure states: "This portrait is an authentic Vermeer because it bears the artist's genuine signature — and we know the signature is genuine because it appears on an authentic Vermeer."`,stem:`The brochure's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`appeals to the gallery's own authority to settle a question the gallery has a financial interest in`},{text:`assumes the truth of the very claim it is trying to establish`},{text:`fails to provide independent evidence that Vermeer signed his paintings`},{text:`confuses a necessary condition for authenticity with a sufficient one`},{text:`generalizes from a single signature to the authenticity of the entire portrait`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The brochure argues the painting is authentic because the signature is genuine, and the signature is genuine because the painting is authentic. Each claim leans on the other, so neither is ever independently supported — the argument assumes its own conclusion, which is circular reasoning.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Strip the argument to its skeleton. Claim 1: the portrait is authentic because the signature is genuine. Claim 2: the signature is genuine because the portrait is authentic. The "because" in each sentence points at the other sentence's conclusion. Nothing outside this loop ever enters: no pigment analysis, no provenance records, no expert comparison with known Vermeer signatures. That is circular reasoning (begging the question) — the argument's premises presuppose the truth of its conclusion, so it cannot persuade anyone who does not already grant the conclusion. Choice (A) is tempting because the gallery does have a financial interest, but the stem asks for the flaw in the reasoning, and the reasoning's defect is circularity, not self-interest. Choice (C) asks for evidence the argument never needed to supply in that form; the problem is not a missing fact about signing habits but the loop. Choice (D) mislabels with conditional jargon — the argument never trades in necessary and sufficient conditions. Choice (E) misdescribes the move: the error is not generalizing from one signature but the mutual dependence of the two claims.`,choiceExplanations:[`The gallery's financial interest is a real reason to want independent verification, which makes this choice tempting. But it diagnoses the source, not the reasoning. Even a disinterested party making this exact argument would be reasoning in a circle — and the circle is the flaw the stem asks you to name.`,`Correct. "Authentic because the signature is genuine" plus "the signature is genuine because it is authentic" is a closed loop: the conclusion appears among its own premises. The argument assumes what it sets out to prove.`,`This choice asks the argument to prove a background fact — that Vermeer signed his paintings — that was never in dispute. The defect is not a missing premise about signing habits but the circular dependence between the two claims actually made.`,`Necessary-versus-sufficient language is a tempting technical label, but the brochure never frames authenticity in conditional terms. Applying that label here is the nec-suff-confusion trap: impressive jargon attached to the wrong flaw.`,`The argument does not move from one signature to a general rule about portraits. It moves in a circle between two specific claims about this portrait and this signature. This choice misidentifies a circular argument as a hasty generalization.`],generalLesson:`If removing the conclusion would leave the premises with nothing to stand on — because the premises just restate the conclusion — the argument is circular. Genuine support must come from outside the claim being defended.`,misconceptionTags:[`circular-reasoning`,`begging-the-question`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`answers-different-question`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Write the argument as two "because" claims. What does the first claim rely on, and what does the second rely on?`,`If each claim's support is just the other claim, has anything actually been established?`,`The correct choice says the argument assumes the very thing it is trying to prove.`],labels:{topic:`painting authentication`}},{id:`lr-c-078`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: hotter towns explain both ice-cream shops and heat illness (common cause); credited answer uniquely defensible. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A county health report notes that towns with the most ice cream shops per capita also have the highest rates of heat-related illness. The report concludes that the wide availability of ice cream is causing the elevated illness rates, and recommends restricting ice cream sales during heat waves.`,stem:`The report's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`infers causation from correlation without ruling out a common cause like high temperatures.`},{text:`relies on a sample of towns too small to support any conclusion about ice cream`},{text:`presumes that restricting ice cream sales would be unpopular without surveying residents`},{text:`confuses the rate of heat-related illness with the total number of cases`},{text:`draws a conclusion about heat waves from data collected throughout the entire year`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Hotter towns plausibly have both more ice cream shops and more heat illness — temperature drives both. The report leaps from correlation to causation without eliminating that obvious common cause, so its causal conclusion (and the sales restriction it recommends) is unsupported.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The evidence is a correlation: more ice cream shops per capita goes together with more heat-related illness. The conclusion is causal: ice cream availability causes the illness. But correlation is compatible with several causal stories. The most obvious here is a common cause: hotter towns both support more ice cream shops (demand for cold treats) and produce more heat illness (heat itself). On that story, restricting ice cream sales would do nothing about illness rates — you would be banning the thermometer instead of treating the fever. A causal conclusion requires ruling out such alternatives, and the report never tries. Choice (B) invents a sample-size complaint the stimulus does not support; the problem is not the number of towns. Choice (C) attributes an unsupported claim about popularity that the report never makes. Choice (D) quibbles about rates versus totals, which changes nothing about the causal leap. Choice (E) invents a seasonal data problem not stated in the stimulus — the flaw is visible even taking the data at face value.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The correlation is real, but high temperatures explain both sides of it: heat creates demand for ice cream shops and directly causes heat illness. Without ruling out that common cause, the leap to "ice cream causes illness" — and the policy built on it — is unjustified.`,`This choice is tempting because bigger samples are generally better, but the stimulus gives no indication the sample is small. The argument fails for a structural reason — correlation treated as causation — that no larger sample of the same correlational data would fix.`,`The report never claims anything about the popularity of the restriction, so this choice criticizes it for an unsupported presumption it never makes. Popularity is simply not part of the reasoning.`,`Rates versus totals is a real distinction in some arguments, but here it is a red herring. Whether measured as rates or counts, the correlation remains equally compatible with the common-cause explanation, so this quibble does not touch the flaw.`,`The stimulus does not say when the data were collected, so this choice invents a seasonal mismatch. The flaw does not depend on timing at all: even perfectly heat-wave-timed data would still show only a correlation.`],generalLesson:`Correlation never establishes causation by itself. Before accepting a causal claim, ask what third factor could produce both trends — and whether the argument has ruled it out.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`correlation-causation`,`common-cause`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The evidence is that two things go together. The conclusion is that one causes the other. What else could explain them going together?`,`Think about what kind of town has many ice cream shops — and what else that same feature would cause.`,`The correct choice names the missing step: ruling out a common cause before claiming causation.`],labels:{topic:`ice cream and heat illness`}},{id:`lr-c-079`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-numbers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: 50 percent masks a rise from 2 crimes to 3 (numbers/percentages flaw); credited answer uniquely defensible. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Police chief Rivera announced: "Violent crime in Greenville rose 50 percent last year, while it fell 5 percent statewide. Clearly, Greenville has become far more dangerous than the rest of the state." In fact, Greenville recorded 3 violent crimes last year, up from 2 the year before.`,stem:`Chief Rivera's reasoning is flawed because it`,choices:[{text:`compares Greenville's crime trend with the statewide trend without evidence the two are related`},{text:`treats a large percentage increase as significant without considering the tiny absolute numbers behind it`},{text:`assumes that past crime trends will continue into the future`},{text:`fails to consider that some violent crimes may go unreported`},{text:`draws a conclusion about danger from statistics about violent crime alone`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`A 50 percent rise sounds dramatic, but it represents a single additional crime — from 2 to 3. Percentages without base numbers mislead: the "surge" is trivial in absolute terms, so it cannot support the sweeping claim that Greenville is now far more dangerous than the state.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument's force comes entirely from the contrast between "rose 50 percent" and "fell 5 percent." But percentages are meaningless without the underlying counts. Greenville went from 2 crimes to 3 — one additional incident, plausibly random fluctuation in a tiny base. The statewide 5 percent drop, by contrast, could represent thousands of fewer crimes. Comparing the two percentages as if they described comparable changes is the numbers flaw: it treats relative change as significant while ignoring absolute magnitude. With only 3 total crimes, Greenville may well be among the safest places in the state, not "far more dangerous." Choice (A) misses the point — the comparison itself is fine; the flaw is what the percentages conceal. Choice (C) invents a prediction about the future that Rivera never makes; her claim is about the present. Choice (D) raises underreporting, which would affect both figures and does not touch the percentage-versus-absolute error. Choice (E) quibbles about the scope of "danger," but violent crime is a perfectly reasonable proxy — the problem is the math, not the metric.`,choiceExplanations:[`Comparing a town with its state is a legitimate move; the flaw is not the comparison but what the percentages hide. This choice mislocates the error, blaming the act of comparing instead of the failure to look behind the percentages at the absolute numbers.`,`Correct. "Up 50 percent" is one additional crime (2 to 3). The dramatic percentage masks a trivial absolute change, and the conclusion that Greenville is "far more dangerous" collapses once the real numbers are visible. This is the classic relative-versus-absolute numbers flaw.`,`Rivera makes no claim about what will happen next year; her conclusion concerns how dangerous Greenville has become. Attributing a future prediction to her is the unsupported trap — it criticizes a claim she never made.`,`Underreporting is a genuine issue in crime statistics, but it applies to both Greenville and the statewide figures and does nothing to explain the specific error here. The flaw is visible even with perfect reporting: 2 to 3 is still just one more crime.`,`Violent crime is a reasonable measure of dangerousness, so this scope quibble fails. The argument's defect is not its choice of metric but its treatment of a 50 percent increase on a base of 2 as though it were a meaningful surge.`],generalLesson:`Percentages describe change relative to a starting point — always ask what that starting point was. A huge percentage of a tiny number can still be a tiny number.`,misconceptionTags:[`base-rate`,`numbers-flaw`,`relative-vs-absolute`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "rose 50 percent" actually mean in Greenville — how many crimes are we talking about?`,`Is going from 2 crimes to 3 crimes evidence that a town is "far more dangerous"?`,`The correct choice points at the gap between the dramatic percentage and the tiny absolute numbers.`],labels:{topic:`crime statistics`}},{id:`lr-c-080`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: past success projected into the future without warrant (temporal flaw); B and E are credible narrow distractors, correctly rejected. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Brightline Software's flagship app has been the top-selling productivity app for three years running. The company's CEO therefore predicts that next year's redesigned version will also be the top seller, and has committed the entire marketing budget to that single product.`,stem:`The CEO's prediction is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`assumes past success will continue even though market conditions may have changed.`},{text:`ignores the possibility that the redesign will be worse than the original`},{text:`confuses the app's sales ranking with its quality`},{text:`relies on sales data that may have been manipulated by the company`},{text:`fails to establish that productivity apps will remain popular next year`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Three years at the top says nothing about next year if the landscape has shifted — new competitors, changing tastes, or a botched redesign could all break the streak. The CEO treats a past trend as a guarantee about the future, which is the temporal flaw: the past is evidence, not a promise.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The evidence is entirely backward-looking: three years as the top seller. The conclusion is entirely forward-looking: next year's version will top the charts too. The bridge between them is the unstated assumption that the conditions producing past success will persist — same competitors, same consumer tastes, same execution. That assumption is never defended, and it is exactly what a prudent forecaster would question: markets are disrupted, leaders stumble, and redesigns flop. Betting the entire marketing budget on the trend continuing compounds the risk. Choice (B) is too narrow — the redesign is only one of many things that could change; the flaw is the general presumption of continuity. Choice (C) mislabels the move; the CEO never equates sales with quality. Choice (D) impugns the data without any basis in the stimulus. Choice (E) is tempting but off-target: the issue is not whether productivity apps in general stay popular but whether this app's past dominance predicts its future dominance under possibly changed conditions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Past performance is evidence, not a guarantee. The prediction silently assumes unchanged market conditions — no new rivals, no shifts in taste, no missteps — and that assumption is exactly what would need defending before committing the whole budget.`,`A bad redesign is one specific way the future could differ from the past, which makes this choice tempting. But it is too narrow: the flaw is the general presumption that past trends continue, not the failure to consider one particular risk. The CEO's error covers competitors, tastes, and execution alike.`,`The CEO never claims the app is the best, only that it sells the best and will continue to. This choice mislabels the argument with a quality-versus-sales confusion that never occurs.`,`Nothing in the stimulus suggests the sales data are fabricated. Impugning the evidence without basis is the unsupported trap — it invents a problem instead of identifying the visible one, which is the leap from past trend to future guarantee.`,`This choice gestures at a real background condition but mislocates the flaw. Even if productivity apps remain popular overall, that would not show this app stays on top. The error is assuming continuity of this product's dominance, not of the category's popularity.`],generalLesson:`A trend is a fact about the past; a prediction is a claim about the future. Always ask what could change between then and now before treating history as a forecast.`,misconceptionTags:[`temporal-flaw`,`past-to-future`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The evidence is about the last three years. The conclusion is about next year. What connects them?`,`What would have to stay the same for past dominance to guarantee future dominance?`,`The correct choice names the unexamined assumption: that conditions will not change.`],labels:{topic:`software sales forecast`}},{id:`lr-c-081`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,conditionalComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE: difficulty inflated at d4 for a single-step conditional translation (only-cardholders reversal) with no near-miss distractor. Downgraded d4 to d2. 0 two-defensible findings; no leakage. VALIDATE-AFTER-REVISION.`}],stimulus:`The county library's new policy states that only cardholders may borrow books. James is a cardholder. The head librarian concludes that James therefore borrows books.`,stem:`The librarian's reasoning is flawed because it`,choices:[{text:`treats a necessary condition for borrowing books as though it were a sufficient condition`},{text:`presumes that James uses his library card without any evidence of his borrowing habits`},{text:`confuses the library's policy with the actual behavior of its patrons`},{text:`draws a conclusion about James from a policy that applies only to cardholders in general`},{text:`fails to consider that James might lend his card to someone else`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`"Only cardholders may borrow" makes being a cardholder necessary for borrowing: no card, no borrowing. It does not make it sufficient — plenty of cardholders never borrow a thing. The librarian reverses the conditional, treating "if borrows, then cardholder" as "if cardholder, then borrows."`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate the policy: "Only cardholders may borrow books" means borrowing → cardholder. Being a cardholder is necessary for borrowing, not sufficient for it. The librarian's inference goes: James is a cardholder → James borrows books. That is the converse of the stated conditional — it treats the necessary condition as if it guaranteed the outcome. In ordinary terms: the rule tells you who is allowed to borrow, not who actually does. Millions of people hold cards they never use. The valid inference would run the other direction: if someone borrows books, they must be a cardholder. Choice (B) sounds critical but merely restates that the conclusion is unsupported without naming the logical error. Choice (C) is vague — the flaw is not a general policy-versus-behavior confusion but the specific conditional reversal. Choice (D) misdescribes the move: applying a general policy to James is fine; the error is which direction the conditional runs. Choice (E) raises an irrelevant scenario about lending cards that has nothing to do with the necessary-sufficient confusion at the heart of the argument.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The policy establishes cardholding as necessary (borrowing requires it), while the conclusion needs it to be sufficient (cardholding guarantees borrowing). The librarian illicitly converts "only cardholders may borrow" into "all cardholders borrow" — the classic necessary-sufficient reversal.`,`This choice notices the conclusion is unsupported but cannot say why, which makes it a tempting but empty criticism. "Without any evidence" is true of many bad arguments; the stem asks for the specific flaw, which is the reversal of the conditional, not a generic lack of evidence.`,`This is a vague paraphrase that sounds analytical without diagnosing anything. The problem is not some general gap between policy and behavior but the precise logical move of treating a necessary condition as sufficient.`,`Applying a general rule to a particular person is perfectly legitimate reasoning — that is not the flaw. The flaw is that the rule was applied backward: the policy licenses "borrower, therefore cardholder," not "cardholder, therefore borrower."`,`Whether James lends his card is entirely beside the point. This choice is the irrelevant-true trap: a factually possible scenario that leaves the conditional reversal — the actual flaw — completely untouched.`],generalLesson:`"Only A may B" makes A necessary for B, never sufficient. When you see "only," draw the arrow carefully: B → A. Conclusions that run the arrow backward commit the necessary-sufficient reversal.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`,`conditional-flaw`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`scope-shift`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate "only cardholders may borrow books" into an if-then statement. Which direction does the arrow point?`,`Does being a cardholder guarantee that someone borrows books — or is it merely required for borrowing?`,`The correct choice names the exact error: a necessary condition treated as a sufficient one.`],labels:{topic:`library borrowing policy`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-c-082`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,abstraction:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Mayor Delgado argues that the city should eliminate its budget deficit immediately, just as a responsible household would pay off its credit card debt rather than let it grow. "A city budget is like a family budget," she says. "No family can spend more than it earns forever, so neither can we."`,stem:`The flaw in the mayor's reasoning is that it`,choices:[{text:`presumes that voters care about the deficit without offering evidence of their priorities`},{text:`relies on a city-household analogy without showing the two are similar in the relevant respects.`},{text:`confuses a balanced budget with an eliminated deficit`},{text:`ignores the possibility that the deficit resulted from necessary emergency spending`},{text:`assumes that households always pay off their debts, which is not always true`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The argument's force comes entirely from the household analogy, but cities and households differ in exactly the respects that matter: cities can borrow at scale, invest for growth, outlive their residents, and print no money but tax. Without showing the analogy holds where it counts, the "just like a household" move proves nothing.`,explanationWalkthrough:`An argument from analogy is only as strong as the relevant similarities between the two cases. The mayor needs cities to resemble households specifically in how debt works: whether borrowing can be productive, what happens if debt is carried, and what options exist for managing it. But on precisely those points the cases diverge sharply. Households cannot tax, cannot issue bonds at municipal rates, and die; cities can borrow to build infrastructure that generates future revenue, refinance across generations, and grow their way out of debt. A household paying off a credit card is avoiding high interest on consumption; a city cutting spending to zero out a deficit may be canceling investments that would have paid for themselves. Because the relevant dissimilarities go unaddressed, the analogy cannot carry the conclusion. Choice (A) quibbles about voter priorities, which are irrelevant to whether the analogy holds. Choice (C) invents a terminology confusion the mayor never exhibits. Choice (D) raises emergency spending — a possibly true but secondary point that does not touch the analogical leap. Choice (E) nitpicks the household side of the analogy instead of questioning whether households are the right model for cities at all.`,choiceExplanations:[`Voter priorities are irrelevant to the argument's logic. The mayor is not claiming voters demand deficit elimination; she is claiming the household analogy shows it is required. Criticizing her for lacking polling data is the answers-different-question trap.`,`Correct. The entire argument rides on "a city budget is like a family budget," yet the respects in which cities and households differ — borrowing power, investment returns, lifespan, taxation — are exactly the respects relevant to whether deficit spending is wise. An undefended analogy is not an argument.`,`The mayor uses "eliminate its deficit" consistently; there is no evidence she confuses it with a balanced budget. This choice manufactures a verbal confusion to sound precise while missing the actual flaw, which is analogical.`,`Emergency spending might explain the deficit's origin, but the mayor's claim is about what to do now, not about how the deficit arose. This choice is tempting because it feels like a substantive objection, yet it leaves the household analogy — the argument's real engine — unexamined.`,`Whether some households carry debt is a quibble about the analogy's source case. Even granting that responsible households pay down debt, the flaw remains: nothing shows that cities are relevantly like households in the first place. This choice attacks the wrong side of the comparison.`],generalLesson:`Analogies prove nothing by themselves — they only suggest. Always ask whether the two cases are alike in the respects that matter to the conclusion, and whether they differ in exactly those respects.`,misconceptionTags:[`false-analogy`,`relevant-similarity`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`scope-shift`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is doing all the work in the mayor's argument — evidence about cities, or a comparison?`,`In what relevant ways might a city's finances differ from a family's? Does she address any of them?`,`The correct choice targets the analogy itself: it was never shown to hold where it matters.`],labels:{topic:`city budget deficit`}},{id:`lr-c-083`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-numbers`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE: difficulty inflated at d5; the residents-vs-voters reference-class shift is subtle but single-layer, and B is a genuine near-miss rather than a d5 trap. Downgraded d5 to d4. 0 two-defensible findings. VALIDATE-AFTER-REVISION.`}],stimulus:`A ballot-measure campaign announced: "Sixty percent of this town's residents support the new school levy. With support that broad, the levy is certain to pass." Voter turnout in the town's last three local elections averaged 40 percent of residents.`,stem:`The campaign's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`treats the opinions of all residents as equivalent to the opinions of the residents who will actually vote`},{text:`relies on a poll that may have surveyed an unrepresentative sample of residents`},{text:`presumes that voter turnout in the upcoming election will match the historical average`},{text:`confuses the percentage of residents who support the levy with the percentage who voted in past elections`},{text:`ignores the possibility that some supporters will change their minds before election day`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Sixty percent of residents is not sixty percent of voters. If only 40 percent turn out, the levy's fate depends on who shows up — supporters could stay home while opponents mobilize. The argument slides between two different reference classes (all residents vs. actual voters) as if they were the same group.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a reference-class scope shift, and it is easy to miss because both figures are percentages about the same town. Unpack them. "Sixty percent of residents support the levy" describes all residents — voters and nonvoters alike. But elections are decided by voters, and only about 40 percent of residents typically vote. The argument needs the 60 percent figure to describe the electorate; instead it describes a larger, different group. If supporters are disproportionately among the 60 percent who stay home, the levy could fail decisively despite "broad support." The conclusion "certain to pass" therefore rests on silently substituting one group for another. Choice (B) is the tempting near-miss: it cries "unrepresentative sample," but the stimulus gives no reason to doubt the poll's sampling — the flaw survives even a perfect poll of all residents. Choice (C) quibbles about turnout matching history, but the flaw does not depend on the exact turnout figure; any turnout below 100 percent creates the gap. Choice (D) misdescribes the error as merely confusing two percentages rather than substituting reference classes. Choice (E) raises mind-changing, a real but secondary possibility that leaves the structural flaw untouched.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The 60 percent figure covers every resident, but only voters decide the election — and historically just 40 percent vote. The argument needs support among voters and offers support among residents instead, sliding between two different reference classes.`,`This is the most tempting distractor, and the adversarial check matters here: the stimulus never suggests the poll was badly sampled. Even a flawless census of all residents would leave the argument broken, because the broken step is the leap from residents to voters, not the poll's methodology.`,`Turnout might indeed differ from the historical average, but the flaw does not hinge on that. Whether turnout is 40 percent or 70 percent, "residents" and "voters" remain different groups, and the argument still substitutes one for the other.`,`The campaign is not confusing the two percentages with each other; it is using the wrong group's percentage to predict an election outcome. This choice describes a surface mix-up rather than the structural substitution of reference classes.`,`Supporters could change their minds, but that possibility is secondary. The argument fails even if every supporter's opinion is rock solid, because opinions of nonvoters do not cast ballots. This choice mistakes a side risk for the central flaw.`],generalLesson:`A percentage is meaningless until you ask "percent of what?" When an argument moves between groups — residents vs. voters, customers vs. respondents — check that the figure it cites actually describes the group its conclusion is about.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`reference-class`,`sampling-vs-scope`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`too-weak`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The 60 percent figure is about residents. Who actually decides whether the levy passes?`,`If only 40 percent of residents vote, could the 60 percent support figure still be true while the levy fails? How?`,`The correct choice names the two different groups being treated as one: all residents versus actual voters.`],labels:{topic:`school levy polling`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-c-084`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: nearby avenues without new lights saw the same crime drop (control group); credited weakener uniquely defensible; A is a clean opposite trap. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the city installed brighter streetlights along Maple Avenue last spring, reported crimes on Maple Avenue fell 30 percent. The mayor concluded that the new lighting caused the drop in crime.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the mayor's argument?`,choices:[{text:`Residents of Maple Avenue say they feel safer walking at night than they did a year ago.`},{text:`The new streetlights use energy-efficient bulbs that cost less to operate.`},{text:`Crime fell by similar amounts on nearby avenues that did not receive new streetlights.`},{text:`Some crimes committed on Maple Avenue still go unreported to the police.`},{text:`The mayor was a strong supporter of the streetlight project from the beginning.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`If crime fell just as much where no new lights were installed, the lights cannot be the cause — some broader trend must be at work. The comparison streets act as a control group, and they show the same result without the supposed cause.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The mayor's conclusion is causal: brighter lights → less crime. The evidence is a before-and-after comparison on one street. The classic way to weaken a causal claim is to show the effect occurring without the cause. Choice (C) does exactly that: nearby avenues with no new lighting saw the same 30 percent drop. If the lights were responsible, the unlit streets should not have improved equally. Their improvement points to some citywide factor — a new policing strategy, demographic shifts, or a general trend — that explains Maple Avenue's drop without any help from the streetlights. Choice (A) actually strengthens the argument by confirming the intended mechanism (deterrence through perceived safety). Choice (B) is true but irrelevant: bulb efficiency says nothing about crime. Choice (D) is too weak — "some" unreported crimes do not show the measured drop is illusory. Choice (E) hints at mayoral bias, but attacking the mayor's motives does not touch the evidence; even a biased mayor could be right.`,choiceExplanations:[`Feeling safer is exactly what the lighting theory would predict, so this choice supports rather than weakens the mayor's claim. It confirms the proposed mechanism instead of undermining it, making it the opposite of what the stem asks for.`,`Bulb efficiency and operating costs are true but entirely beside the point. The argument is about whether lighting reduced crime, and this choice says nothing about crime at all — a textbook irrelevant-true distractor.`,`Correct. The nearby avenues serve as a natural control group: same crime drop, no new lights. That severs the link between the lighting and the result, leaving the mayor's causal conclusion without support.`,`"Some" unreported crimes are too vague to damage the argument — the claim is about the change in reported crime, and a few unreported cases do not explain a 30 percent decline. This choice is too weak to weaken.`,`The mayor's prior support might suggest bias, but bias does not falsify evidence. This choice attacks the arguer instead of the argument — the wrong-viewpoint trap — and leaves the crime statistics completely untouched.`],generalLesson:`To weaken a causal claim, look for the effect happening without the cause. A control group — or a natural one, like neighboring streets — that shows the same result destroys the claim that the cause was responsible.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`control-group`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The mayor says the lights caused the crime drop. What kind of evidence would show the drop would have happened anyway?`,`Think about the avenues that did not get new lights. What would it mean if crime fell there too?`,`The correct choice gives you a control group: same result, no new lighting.`],labels:{topic:`streetlights and crime`}},{id:`lr-c-085`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Since TechCorp introduced flexible work hours last year, employee productivity has risen 15 percent. The CEO credits the flexible-hours policy for the increase.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most seriously undermines the CEO's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Employees report higher job satisfaction under the flexible-hours policy.`},{text:`Several of TechCorp's competitors have also adopted flexible work hours.`},{text:`Some employees say they preferred the old fixed schedule.`},{text:`Productivity is difficult to measure with complete precision.`},{text:`Productivity rose by similar amounts at comparable companies that kept fixed schedules.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`If companies without flexible hours improved just as much, the policy cannot explain TechCorp's gain — an industry-wide trend is the likelier cause. The comparison companies act as a control group showing the effect without the supposed cause.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The CEO moves from "productivity rose after the policy" to "the policy caused the rise." That is a causal claim resting on timing alone. Choice (E) breaks it with a control-group comparison: comparable firms with no policy change saw the same 15 percent gain. When the effect appears without the cause, the cause cannot be doing the explanatory work — something broader, like an economic upturn or new industry technology, is the better explanation. Choice (A) strengthens the argument by supplying a plausible mechanism (satisfaction → effort). Choice (B) is irrelevant: what competitors do now says nothing about what caused TechCorp's past increase. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap — some employees' preferences do not show the policy failed to raise overall productivity. Choice (D) is a vague methodological grumble; "difficult to measure precisely" does not show the 15 percent figure is wrong or that the policy was ineffective.`,choiceExplanations:[`Higher satisfaction is a plausible mechanism by which flexible hours could raise productivity, so this choice supports the CEO's causal story rather than undermining it. It is the opposite of a weakener.`,`Competitors' current policies have no bearing on what caused TechCorp's productivity gain last year. This choice is irrelevant: it describes the industry without touching the causal link at issue.`,`It may be true that some employees preferred fixed schedules, but "some" preferences do not show the policy failed overall. The conclusion concerns aggregate productivity, which this choice leaves untouched — irrelevant-true.`,`This choice raises a general worry about measurement without showing the measurement was wrong here. Vague skepticism about precision is too weak to undermine a specific 15 percent finding or the causal claim built on it.`,`Correct. Comparable companies without the policy improved equally, which means the policy cannot explain TechCorp's improvement. A broader trend must be responsible, and the CEO's causal credit collapses.`],generalLesson:`A before-and-after change at one company proves nothing about causation until you check what happened elsewhere. If the same change occurred without the supposed cause, look for a common trend instead.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`control-group`,`post-hoc`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The CEO's evidence is "productivity rose after the policy." What else, besides the policy, could explain a rise?`,`What would you want to know about other companies that did not change their hours?`,`The correct choice shows the same productivity gain happening without flexible hours.`],labels:{topic:`flexible work hours`}},{id:`lr-c-086`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: tea drinkers exercised more (confound); credited weakener uniquely defensible; C (funding source) is a good near-miss, correctly weaker. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A study found that people who drink green tea daily have significantly lower cholesterol than people who rarely drink it. The researchers concluded that daily green tea consumption lowers cholesterol.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the researchers' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Green tea contains antioxidants that are known to benefit the heart.`},{text:`The daily tea drinkers also exercised substantially more than the non-drinkers.`},{text:`The study was funded by a company that sells green tea.`},{text:`Some participants dropped out of the study before it was completed.`},{text:`Cholesterol levels naturally vary a great deal from person to person.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Exercise lowers cholesterol, and the tea drinkers exercised more — so the cholesterol difference may be due to exercise, not tea. This confounding variable offers a complete alternative explanation for the observed correlation, undercutting the causal conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The study is observational: it compares existing tea drinkers with non-drinkers rather than randomly assigning tea. In observational studies, the groups may differ in many ways besides the factor being studied. Choice (B) identifies such a confound: the tea drinkers also exercised substantially more, and exercise independently lowers cholesterol. The observed cholesterol gap is therefore fully explainable without any effect of tea — the researchers may have measured the effect of exercise while crediting the tea. Choice (A) strengthens by proposing a mechanism for tea's benefit. Choice (C) is tempting but weak: funding by a tea company might motivate bias, but it does not by itself show the data are wrong; the confound in (B) attacks the inference directly. Choice (D) is too vague — "some" dropouts with no indication of bias do not threaten the result. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: natural variation exists, but the study found a systematic group difference that variation alone does not explain away.`,choiceExplanations:[`Antioxidants provide a plausible biological mechanism for tea helping the heart, which supports the researchers' causal claim rather than weakening it. A mechanism story is the opposite of what the stem requests.`,`Correct. This is a classic confounding variable: exercise lowers cholesterol, the tea group exercised more, so the cholesterol difference may have nothing to do with tea. The correlation remains, but the causal conclusion loses its support.`,`Funding sources can raise eyebrows, but a funder's identity does not show the findings are false. This choice attacks the study's sponsorship rather than its logic, and it is far weaker than the direct confound identified in the correct choice.`,`"Some" dropouts, with no information about who dropped out or why, cannot be shown to bias the results. This vague methodological worry is too weak to undermine the cholesterol finding.`,`Natural variation is real but irrelevant: the study compared group averages, and random individual variation does not explain a systematic difference between the groups. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`In observational studies, always ask how the compared groups might differ besides the factor being studied. A confounding variable that explains the result is the sharpest way to weaken a causal conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`confound`,`observational-study`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The study compared people who already drank tea with people who did not. Could those two groups differ in other ways?`,`What else lowers cholesterol — and might tea drinkers do more of it?`,`The correct choice names a specific alternative explanation: the tea drinkers exercised more.`],labels:{topic:`green tea and cholesterol`}},{id:`lr-c-087`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: doubled cleanup program is a simultaneous alternative cause; credited weakener uniquely defensible. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the city banned single-use plastic bags, litter in city parks dropped 25 percent. Environmental advocates concluded that the bag ban caused the reduction in litter.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the advocates' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Plastic bags had been one of the most common forms of litter found in the parks.`},{text:`The bag ban was popular with a majority of the city's residents.`},{text:`Some park visitors now bring their own reusable bags when they visit.`},{text:`The same year the ban took effect, the city doubled the size of its volunteer park-cleanup program.`},{text:`Park attendance remained steady throughout the period studied.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Doubling the cleanup program is a competing explanation for cleaner parks: more volunteers picking up litter would reduce measured litter with or without any bag ban. Because this alternative cause coincides exactly with the ban, the advocates cannot credit the ban for the drop.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The advocates' evidence is timing: ban, then less litter. But two things changed at once — the ban and the doubled cleanup program — and only one of them gets credit in the conclusion. Choice (D) supplies the rival cause: with twice as many volunteers collecting litter, parks would look cleaner even if the ban had never happened. The observed 25 percent drop is therefore fully explainable without the ban, which severs the causal link the advocates assert. Choice (A) strengthens the argument by confirming bags were a real part of the litter problem. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: popularity does not show effectiveness. Choice (C) is consistent with the ban working — visitors adapting with reusable bags is the ban functioning as intended, not evidence against it. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: steady attendance neither supports nor undermines the causal claim.`,choiceExplanations:[`If bags were a major component of park litter, removing them would plausibly reduce litter — this supports the advocates' causal story rather than weakening it. It is the opposite of what the stem asks for.`,`Public popularity is irrelevant to causal effectiveness. A beloved policy can fail and an unpopular one can work; this choice tells us nothing about whether the ban reduced litter.`,`Visitors switching to reusable bags is the ban working as designed, not evidence against it. This choice describes compliance with the policy, which if anything is consistent with the advocates' conclusion.`,`Correct. The doubled cleanup program is a simultaneous alternative cause: more litter collected means less litter measured, ban or no ban. With two changes at once, the drop cannot be credited to the ban alone.`,`Steady attendance rules out one possible confound (fewer visitors), but it does nothing to distinguish the ban from the cleanup program as the cause. It leaves the advocates' causal claim exactly where it was.`],generalLesson:`When two changes happen at the same time, crediting only one of them is unjustified. Always check whether a simultaneous alternative cause could fully explain the observed effect.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`,`simultaneous-change`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The ban coincided with the litter drop. Did anything else change at the same time?`,`What else, besides fewer bags, could make parks cleaner?`,`The correct choice points to a second change — doubled cleanups — that explains the result on its own.`],labels:{topic:`plastic bag ban`}},{id:`lr-c-088`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: controlled trial found no self-control gain (direct counter-evidence); credited weakener uniquely defensible. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Psychologist Dr. Vance argues that willpower works like a muscle: it grows stronger with regular exercise. She identifies daily meditation as the ideal willpower workout, concluding that people who meditate daily will develop greater self-control than people who do not.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens Dr. Vance's argument?`,choices:[{text:`A controlled trial found daily meditators improved no more on self-control tasks than non-meditators.`},{text:`Some psychologists dispute whether the muscle metaphor accurately describes willpower.`},{text:`Meditation has been shown to reduce stress and improve sleep quality.`},{text:`Baseline willpower varies considerably from person to person.`},{text:`Dr. Vance herself meditates daily and reports excellent self-control.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The conclusion predicts a concrete outcome — meditators gaining more self-control — and the controlled trial directly tests it and finds nothing. Evidence that the predicted effect does not occur is the strongest possible weakener: it falsifies the conclusion rather than merely quibbling with the reasoning.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Dr. Vance's conclusion is a prediction about the world: daily meditation produces greater self-control. Choice (A) reports a controlled trial — the gold standard for testing such claims — in which meditators improved no more than non-meditators. That is not an objection to her metaphor or her methods; it is direct evidence that the predicted effect does not exist. A conclusion contradicted by controlled evidence cannot stand. The distractors are all weaker. Choice (B) notes that "some psychologists dispute" the metaphor, but vague disagreement without evidence is toothless. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: stress and sleep benefits do not show self-control improved. Choice (D) is irrelevant-true: baseline variation is compatible with meditation helping everyone equally. Choice (E) is a sample of one — Vance's personal experience — which proves nothing and, if anything, was already assumed by her advocacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. A controlled trial is direct evidence about the conclusion itself: if meditation built self-control, meditators should have outperformed the control group, and they did not. This does not nibble at the argument's edges — it contradicts the predicted outcome.`,`That some psychologists "dispute" the metaphor is vague and evidence-free. Disagreement alone, without reasons or data, is too weak to damage the argument — it is the too-weak trap dressed as expert skepticism.`,`Stress reduction and better sleep are genuine benefits, but they are different outcomes from self-control. This choice is irrelevant-true: it praises meditation for something the conclusion never claimed.`,`Individual variation in willpower is compatible with the conclusion — meditation could still help everyone by the same amount. This choice states a true background fact that leaves the causal claim untouched.`,`One person's self-report is anecdotal and, worse, comes from the theory's own advocate. It cannot test the conclusion and certainly cannot weaken the trial evidence; if anything, it was already baked into her position.`],generalLesson:`The strongest weakener is often direct evidence against the conclusion itself. When a claim predicts an observable outcome, a well-designed test showing the outcome did not occur beats any amount of theoretical quibbling.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`direct-evidence`,`anecdote`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Vance predicts meditators will gain more self-control. What kind of finding would directly contradict that prediction?`,`Compare "some psychologists disagree" with an actual controlled trial. Which one tests the claim?`,`The correct choice reports a trial in which the predicted advantage never appeared.`],labels:{topic:`meditation and willpower`}},{id:`lr-c-089`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: GrowMax plants were also watered more (confounded trial); credited weakener uniquely defensible; E (funding) is a good near-miss, correctly weaker. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`In a controlled trial, tomato plants given GrowMax fertilizer produced twice as many tomatoes as plants given no fertilizer. The manufacturer concludes that GrowMax doubles tomato yields.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the manufacturer's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`GrowMax costs considerably more than most competing fertilizers.`},{text:`Some gardeners prefer to grow tomatoes without any synthetic fertilizer.`},{text:`The GrowMax plants were watered daily; controls only twice a week.`},{text:`Tomato yields naturally vary from one growing season to the next.`},{text:`The trial was funded by the company that manufactures GrowMax.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`A controlled trial must hold everything constant except the treatment — but here watering differed too. The yield gap could be due to water, not GrowMax, so the trial cannot isolate the fertilizer's effect and the "doubles yields" claim collapses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The manufacturer's conclusion depends on the trial being truly controlled: fertilizer as the only difference between the groups. Choice (C) reveals it was not — the GrowMax plants also received far more water. Water independently boosts tomato yields, so the observed doubling is fully explainable by the watering difference alone. The trial confounds two treatments, and no conclusion about GrowMax specifically can be drawn from it. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: price does not affect whether the fertilizer works. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: gardeners' preferences say nothing about yields. Choice (D) is too weak and off-target — seasonal variation is irrelevant to a single-season trial comparing two simultaneous groups. Choice (E) is tempting but weak: manufacturer funding might motivate bias, yet it does not by itself show the results are wrong, whereas the watering confound directly destroys the trial's logic.`,choiceExplanations:[`Price is irrelevant to efficacy. An expensive fertilizer might still double yields, and a cheap one might not; this choice says nothing about whether GrowMax caused the observed difference.`,`Gardeners' preferences are beside the point. The conclusion is about what the fertilizer does to yields, and this choice offers no evidence about yields at all — irrelevant-true.`,`Correct. The trial compared "fertilizer plus daily watering" against "no fertilizer plus infrequent watering." With two differences between the groups, the yield gap cannot be attributed to the fertilizer. The experiment fails to isolate its supposed cause.`,`Seasonal variation would matter if the groups were grown in different seasons, but they were grown side by side in one trial. This choice raises a real phenomenon that is irrelevant to this comparison — too-weak and off-target.`,`Funding by the manufacturer is worth noting, but it attacks the study's sponsorship rather than its design. A biased funder does not prove biased results; the watering confound, by contrast, directly invalidates the inference. This is the weaker, more tempting criticism.`],generalLesson:`An experiment proves causation only if the treatment is the sole difference between groups. When you spot a second difference, the results cannot be credited to the treatment — the trial is confounded.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`confound`,`controlled-trial`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`For the trial to prove GrowMax works, what must be true about how the two groups were treated?`,`Check whether fertilizer was really the only difference between the groups.`,`The correct choice reveals a second difference — watering — that could explain the entire result.`],labels:{topic:`fertilizer trial`}},{id:`lr-c-090`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-samples`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: survey invitations sent through the app itself (selection bias); credited weakener uniquely defensible. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`PeakFit gym advertises that its new workout-tracking app is a hit: in a recent survey, 80 percent of respondents said they were satisfied with the app. The company concludes that most of its members love the app.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the company's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Some PeakFit members do not own smartphones.`},{text:`Competing gyms also offer workout-tracking apps to their members.`},{text:`The survey was conducted online rather than on paper.`},{text:`Satisfied customers are more likely than dissatisfied ones to recommend the gym to friends.`},{text:`The survey invitation was sent only through the app itself, so members who never use the app could not respond.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`If only app users could answer a survey about the app, the 80 percent satisfaction figure describes a self-selected group of users — not the membership. Non-users, who are likelier to be dissatisfied or indifferent, were structurally excluded, so the sample cannot support a claim about "most members."`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion is about all members; the evidence is from survey respondents. For the inference to work, respondents must represent the membership. Choice (E) shows they cannot: the invitation went out through the app itself, so the sample consists overwhelmingly of people who already use the app — the very group most likely to be satisfied with it. Members who tried the app and quit, or never downloaded it, never had a chance to answer. This is selection bias: the survey mechanism filters for the outcome it measures. An 80 percent satisfaction rate among self-selected users says nothing about the silent majority. Choice (A) is too weak — "some" members without smartphones does not show the sample was unrepresentative. Choice (B) is irrelevant: competitors' apps do not affect PeakFit members' opinions. Choice (C) is a methodological quibble; online surveys can be perfectly representative. Choice (D) is irrelevant-true: recommendation behavior is a different question from app satisfaction.`,choiceExplanations:[`"Some" members without smartphones is too vague to damage the argument — it does not show the respondents were unrepresentative or that the 80 percent figure is misleading. Too-weak.`,`What competing gyms offer has no bearing on whether PeakFit's members like its app. This choice changes the subject entirely — irrelevant. Members judge the app they use, not the market; rival offerings could be better or worse without changing a single member's satisfaction.`,`The medium of a survey does not by itself bias the results. Without a reason online respondents differ from members generally, this is an empty methodological complaint — too-weak.`,`Recommendation behavior is a different question from app satisfaction. This choice is irrelevant-true: it may be accurate about referrals while saying nothing about whether the survey sample represents the membership.`,`Correct. Sending the invitation through the app guarantees the respondents are disproportionately app users — the population most likely to be satisfied. The survey systematically excludes non-users, so its results cannot be generalized to "most members."`],generalLesson:`Always ask how survey respondents were selected. If the recruitment method filters for the very trait being measured — like surveying app users about the app — the results describe the filter, not the population.`,misconceptionTags:[`sample-bias`,`selection-bias`,`representativeness`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion is about most members. Who actually got to answer the survey?`,`If the invitation went out through the app itself, what kind of member is most likely to respond — and who is excluded?`,`The correct choice shows the survey selected for app users, the group most likely to be satisfied.`],labels:{topic:`gym app survey`}},{id:`lr-c-091`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: thriving predated the arenas (temporal order defeats causation); credited weakener uniquely defensible. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Civic leaders argue that the city should build a downtown arena, noting that several cities with downtown arenas have thriving local businesses. They conclude that building the arena will boost the local economy.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the leaders' argument?`,choices:[{text:`Downtown arenas are expensive to build and costly to maintain.`},{text:`In most cited cities, downtown businesses were already thriving before the arenas were built.`},{text:`Some residents oppose spending tax money on a downtown arena.`},{text:`The civic leaders did not consult any economists before making their claim.`},{text:`Thriving local businesses generate substantial tax revenue for a city.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`If the thriving came first, the arenas cannot have caused it — the causal arrow the leaders assume may point the other way, or thriving cities may simply be the ones that can afford arenas. Either way, the correlation no longer suggests an arena will create prosperity where none exists.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The leaders' evidence is a correlation: arenas and thriving downtowns go together. Their conclusion assumes the arena caused the thriving. Choice (B) supplies the timeline that defeats that assumption: in most cited cities, prosperity predated the arena. That reverses the plausible causal story — perhaps prosperous cities build arenas as a luxury, or some third factor (a strong local economy) produces both the thriving businesses and the arena. On any of these stories, the correlation gives no reason to expect that building an arena in a struggling downtown will create thriving businesses. Choice (A) is too weak: expense does not show the arena would fail to boost the economy; a costly project can still pay off. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: residents' opposition says nothing about economic effects. Choice (D) attacks the leaders' homework rather than their evidence — even without economists, the correlation might hold; and with the timeline in (B), it does not matter who they consulted. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true and, if anything, friendly to the leaders' framing.`,choiceExplanations:[`Cost is a reason to hesitate, but it does not show the arena would fail to boost the economy — expensive projects can still generate returns. This choice raises a budget concern without touching the causal claim, so it is too weak to weaken.`,`Correct. If downtowns were already thriving before the arenas arrived, the arenas cannot be the cause of the thriving. The correlation is equally consistent with prosperity enabling arena construction — which gives no reason to expect an arena to create prosperity here.`,`Public opposition is a political fact, not economic evidence. Whether residents like the idea has no bearing on whether an arena would boost the local economy — irrelevant-true.`,`Failing to consult economists is a criticism of the leaders' process, not of their evidence. The argument stands or falls on the arena-thriving correlation and its causal interpretation, which this choice never addresses — too-weak.`,`That thriving businesses pay taxes is true but irrelevant to whether the arena caused the thriving. If anything, it makes arenas sound attractive, which is the opposite direction from weakening.`],generalLesson:`Correlation needs a timeline before it can suggest causation. If the supposed effect predates the supposed cause, the causal story collapses — check which came first.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`correlation-causation`,`temporal-order`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The leaders see arenas and thriving downtowns together. What would you need to know about timing to infer the arena caused the thriving?`,`What if the thriving came first? What would that do to their conclusion?`,`The correct choice supplies the timeline: prosperity predated the arenas.`],labels:{topic:`downtown arena proposal`}},{id:`lr-c-092`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: delay-flagging software rolled out the same month (simultaneous cause); credited weakener uniquely defensible. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After Meridian Inc. made morning team meetings mandatory, on-time project delivery improved 20 percent. Management concluded that the meetings caused the improvement by helping teams coordinate earlier in the day.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most seriously weakens management's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Some employees report finding the mandatory morning meetings unpleasant.`},{text:`Meridian's main competitors do not hold mandatory morning meetings.`},{text:`Project delivery times at Meridian have naturally fluctuated from quarter to quarter in the past.`},{text:`The month meetings became mandatory, Meridian also rolled out software that automatically flags delays.`},{text:`A few teams were exempted from the morning-meeting requirement for logistical reasons.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The tracking software is a simultaneous alternative cause: automatic delay-flagging would improve on-time delivery with or without any meetings. Since both changes happened at once, the 20 percent improvement cannot be credited to the meetings.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Management's evidence is timing — meetings, then improvement — and timing alone cannot distinguish among simultaneous causes. Choice (D) identifies a second change in the same month: software that automatically flags delays. That software provides a complete, independent mechanism for the improvement: teams warned earlier about slipping deadlines hit more of them, meetings or no meetings. With two candidate causes introduced together, the observed effect cannot be attributed to the meetings. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: employees can dislike effective meetings. Choice (B) is irrelevant: competitors' practices do not show what caused Meridian's improvement. Choice (C) is too weak — generic past fluctuation does not explain a specific 20 percent jump coinciding with the policy. Choice (E) is too weak: a few exempt teams do not show the meetings were ineffective for everyone else.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unpleasant meetings can still be effective meetings. Employee distaste says nothing about whether coordination improved delivery times, so this choice is irrelevant-true — factually plausible but logically inert.`,`What competitors do has no bearing on what caused Meridian's improvement. This choice changes the subject from causation at Meridian to industry practice — irrelevant.`,`Past fluctuation is a vague background fact, not an explanation of this specific 20 percent improvement. Without tying the fluctuation to this period, the choice is too weak to displace the meetings as the explanation.`,`Correct. The delay-flagging software arrived the same month and independently explains better on-time delivery. Two simultaneous changes mean the improvement cannot be credited to the meetings alone — the causal conclusion is undercut.`,`A few exempt teams are a minor wrinkle, not a refutation. Their existence does not show the meetings failed for the teams that held them, so this choice is too weak to seriously weaken the conclusion.`],generalLesson:`Before-and-after evidence is only as good as its isolation of the cause. When a second change arrives simultaneously — especially one with its own mechanism — the original cause loses its credit.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`,`simultaneous-change`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The meetings and the improvement coincided. What else happened at Meridian at the same time?`,`Look for a second change with its own plausible mechanism for improving delivery times.`,`The correct choice names the simultaneous rollout of delay-flagging software.`],labels:{topic:`morning meetings`}},{id:`lr-c-093`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: tutoring program launched alongside breakfast for the same students (confounded rollout); C (funding) is a strong near-miss, correctly weaker than the direct confound. Honest d4. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A school district study found that students who ate breakfast at school scored higher on standardized tests than students who skipped breakfast. The district concluded that providing breakfast improves academic performance.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the district's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`The breakfast program began at the same time as an after-school tutoring program serving the same students.`},{text:`Students who ate the school breakfast reported feeling more alert during morning classes.`},{text:`The study was funded by a cereal manufacturer with an interest in the results.`},{text:`Some students who ate breakfast every day still scored poorly on the tests.`},{text:`The study included students from every grade level in the district.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Tutoring is a simultaneous alternative cause aimed at exactly the same students: the higher scores could be entirely the tutoring's work. Because the two interventions arrived together, the study cannot isolate any breakfast effect, and the causal conclusion fails.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The study is observational with a confounded rollout: breakfast and tutoring began together for the same students. Choice (A) exploits that design flaw directly. Tutoring independently raises test scores, so the score gap between breakfast eaters and skippers may reflect who got tutoring rather than who got breakfast — indeed, if the breakfast group overlaps the tutored group, the entire effect could be tutoring's. The district's conclusion requires attributing the gain to breakfast, which is impossible when a second score-raising intervention coincides. Choice (B) strengthens by supplying the mechanism (alertness). Choice (C) is the tempting funding-source attack, but sponsorship does not falsify data; the confound in (A) attacks the inference itself and is strictly stronger. Choice (D) is too weak: "some" low-scoring breakfast eaters are compatible with breakfast helping on average. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true — broad grade coverage does not address the confounding.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The tutoring initiative is a simultaneous, score-raising intervention applied to the same students. With two treatments confounded, the test-score advantage cannot be credited to breakfast — the causal inference collapses.`,`Feeling more alert is a plausible mechanism by which breakfast could improve performance, so this choice supports the district's conclusion rather than weakening it. Opposite.`,`Manufacturer funding invites suspicion, but suspicion is not refutation: the data could be perfectly sound. This choice attacks the study's sponsorship instead of its logic, making it a weaker criticism than the direct confound in the correct choice.`,`"Some" poor-scoring breakfast eaters do not show breakfast fails on average — any effective intervention still leaves some individuals behind. This choice is too weak to threaten a claim about group averages.`,`Including every grade level is a point in the study's favor regarding breadth, and it says nothing about the breakfast-versus-tutoring confound. Irrelevant-true. Breadth of sampling does not disentangle two causes operating within the sample; the confound survives no matter how many grades are included.`],generalLesson:`When an intervention launches alongside another intervention targeting the same outcome, no effect can be credited to either one alone. Simultaneous rollouts confound causal conclusions — always check what else started at the same time.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`confound`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The breakfast eaters scored higher. Besides breakfast, what else might distinguish students who participate in a new school program?`,`Check the timing: did any other score-raising initiative start at the same moment?`,`The correct choice names a simultaneous tutoring program serving the same students.`],labels:{topic:`school breakfast program`}},{id:`lr-c-094`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: subway does not serve suburban office parks (mechanism blocked); D is a good supported-nonresponsive trap. Honest d4. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Transit planners predict that the new crosstown subway line will reduce rush-hour traffic on Main Street. They reason that many Main Street drivers will switch to the faster subway for their commutes.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the planners' prediction?`,choices:[{text:`Construction of the subway line will temporarily disrupt traffic on several downtown streets.`},{text:`Main Street carries the heaviest rush-hour traffic of any street in the city.`},{text:`Most Main Street commuters travel to suburban office parks that the subway line will not serve.`},{text:`The new subway line is expected to be popular with residents of the downtown core.`},{text:`Some commuters enjoy driving so much that they will never switch to public transit.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The prediction depends on a specific mechanism: Main Street drivers switching to the subway. If most of those drivers are headed to suburban office parks the line does not serve, the mechanism fails — they cannot switch to a train that does not go where they are going.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Predictions rest on mechanisms, and the sharpest weakener breaks the mechanism. The planners' mechanism is driver-switching: Main Street commuters abandoning cars for the subway. Choice (C) shows the mechanism cannot operate for most of the relevant drivers, because the subway does not serve their destinations. A commuter cannot switch to a line that does not go to her office park. With the switching mechanism blocked, the predicted traffic reduction loses its support — the subway might succeed brilliantly while Main Street stays jammed. Choice (A) is irrelevant: temporary construction disruption says nothing about post-opening traffic. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: heavy traffic makes the prediction important, not wrong. Choice (D) is supported-nonresponsive: downtown residents' enthusiasm does not show Main Street drivers will switch. Choice (E) is too weak: "some" die-hard drivers are compatible with "many" switching.`,choiceExplanations:[`Temporary construction disruption concerns the building phase, not the operating subway the prediction is about. This choice is irrelevant to whether drivers will switch once the line opens.`,`Heavy traffic on Main Street is true but inert: it explains why planners care, not whether their mechanism works. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Motivation for a policy is not evidence for its mechanism; caring about congestion says nothing about whether the proposed fix reduces it.`,`Correct. The prediction requires Main Street drivers to switch to the subway, but most of them commute to places the subway does not serve. The switching mechanism — the argument's load-bearing assumption — cannot function.`,`Downtown residents liking the subway is consistent with the planners' hopes but nonresponsive to the actual claim: nothing here shows Main Street drivers will switch. Subway popularity and Main Street relief are different questions.`,`"Some" committed drivers leave "many" potential switchers untouched. This choice is too weak: the prediction survives easily if only a few drivers refuse transit while many others switch.`],generalLesson:`Predictions live or die by their mechanisms. To weaken a forecast, do not attack the forecast directly — show that the specific causal path it depends on cannot operate.`,misconceptionTags:[`mechanism`,`prediction`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What exactly has to happen for the subway to reduce Main Street traffic?`,`For a driver to switch to the subway, what must be true about where the subway goes?`,`The correct choice shows most Main Street drivers are headed somewhere the subway does not serve.`],labels:{topic:`subway and traffic`}},{id:`lr-c-095`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: dispatch software accounts for the full two minutes (quantitative match); D is a genuine opposite-direction trap (worse congestion plus better times strengthens). Honest d5. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the city repaved its major roads last year, average ambulance response times improved by two minutes. City officials concluded that smoother roads allowed ambulances to drive faster, and credited the repaving project for the improvement.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the officials' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Some ambulances still arrive later than the city's target response time.`},{text:`Repaving also reduced wear and tear on ambulance tires and suspensions.`},{text:`Response times are measured from the moment an emergency call is received to the moment the ambulance arrives.`},{text:`Traffic congestion on major roads has worsened slightly since the repaving was completed.`},{text:`The month the repaving finished, the dispatch center installed software that cut call-processing delays by two minutes on average.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The entire two-minute improvement is accounted for by faster dispatch, not faster driving: the software cut call-processing delays by exactly the measured amount. With the full effect explained elsewhere, nothing remains for the repaving to explain — the officials' causal credit evaporates.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a demanding item because the distractors are carefully built and the correct answer requires quantitative attention. The officials' conclusion attributes a two-minute improvement to faster driving on smoother roads. Choice (E) supplies a simultaneous alternative cause — dispatch software — that accounts for the entire two minutes through a different part of the response timeline (call processing, not driving). If dispatch alone explains the whole measured improvement, the repaving explains none of it. Note the precision matters: a vague "dispatch improved somewhat" would leave room for repaving to contribute, but "by an average of two minutes" exactly matches the observed effect, leaving no residual for smoother roads. Choice (A) is too weak: "some" late arrivals are compatible with average improvement. Choice (B) strengthens the officials' story by adding a second repaving mechanism. Choice (C) is an irrelevant-true definition. Choice (D) is the subtlest trap: worse congestion alongside better response times might seem to weaken the repaving claim, but it actually strengthens it — improvement despite worse traffic suggests the roads really did help.`,choiceExplanations:[`"Some" ambulances missing the target is compatible with the average improving by two minutes. This choice is too weak: it points at individual cases without challenging the measured average improvement or its attribution.`,`Reduced wear on tires and suspensions is another way repaving could help ambulances — a second mechanism supporting the officials' conclusion, not undermining it. Opposite.`,`This is a true definition of the measurement, but definitions do not discriminate between causes. It leaves the repaving-versus-dispatch question completely untouched — irrelevant-true. Knowing what was measured never tells you why the measurement changed; the definition is compatible with either cause.`,`This is the subtlest trap. Worsening congestion plus improving response times actually supports the officials: if traffic got worse and times still improved, something powerful — plausibly the repaving — must be helping. It strengthens rather than weakens.`,`Correct. The software cut processing delays by an average of two minutes — exactly the total improvement measured. With the full effect accounted for by dispatch, there is nothing left for smoother roads to explain, and the officials' causal credit collapses.`],generalLesson:`When an alternative cause accounts for the entire measured effect — matching it quantitatively — the original cause is left with nothing to explain. Precision matters: "some improvement" weakens partially, but "all of it" weakens completely.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`,`quantitative-match`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The officials credit repaving with a two-minute improvement. What else changed at the same time?`,`Look at the numbers: how much of the two minutes does the alternative explanation account for?`,`The correct choice names dispatch software that cut delays by exactly two minutes — the whole effect.`],labels:{topic:`ambulance response times`}},{id:`lr-c-096`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,causalComplexity:4,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`Riverton's voluntary recycling program was hailed as a model, but participation fell by half after its first year. Critics conclude that voluntary recycling programs simply do not work.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most weakens the critics' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Riverton residents now recycle less than residents of neighboring cities with mandatory programs.`},{text:`In year two, Riverton ended curbside pickup, forcing residents to drive recyclables to a distant drop-off site.`},{text:`Markets for recycled materials were unusually strong during the program's first year.`},{text:`Some residents reported finding the program's sorting rules confusing.`},{text:`Mandatory recycling programs in other cities have also seen participation decline over time.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The critics treat Riverton as a fair test of voluntary recycling, but the program changed fundamentally in year two: losing curbside pickup made recycling drastically harder. The participation drop may reflect that added burden, not voluntariness — so Riverton no longer tests what the critics claim it tests.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The critics' argument is a generalization from a case study: Riverton tried voluntary recycling, participation collapsed, therefore voluntary recycling fails. That inference requires Riverton to be a fair test — the "voluntary" feature must be what explains the outcome. Choice (B) destroys that requirement by revealing the program was not the same program in year two. Eliminating curbside pickup and forcing residents to drive to a distant center is a massive increase in effort that would depress participation under any regulatory design, mandatory or voluntary. The drop is therefore fully explainable by the added inconvenience, and Riverton ceases to be evidence about voluntariness at all. Choice (A) is an irrelevant comparison: neighboring cities' mandatory programs are a different policy, and the comparison does not address whether voluntariness caused Riverton's drop. Choice (C) is too weak and off-target — strong recycled-materials markets do not explain household participation. Choice (D) is too weak: "some" confused residents cannot explain a 50 percent collapse. Choice (E) is the opposite trap: if mandatory programs also decline, that suggests the problem is not voluntariness specifically... actually it suggests decline is general, which if anything supports the critics' broader skepticism rather than weakening their conclusion about voluntary programs.`,choiceExplanations:[`Comparing Riverton with cities using mandatory programs changes the subject. The critics' claim is about whether voluntariness caused the drop; how Riverton compares to different policies elsewhere does not test that claim — irrelevant comparison.`,`Correct. The program's second year was not a test of voluntary recycling but of voluntary recycling made drastically more inconvenient. The participation drop is fully explained by the loss of curbside pickup, so Riverton provides no evidence about voluntariness itself.`,`Strong markets for recycled materials concern what happens to recyclables after collection, not whether households participate. This choice is irrelevant to the participation drop the critics cite.`,`"Some" confused residents are too few and too vague to explain participation halving. This choice is too weak: it gestures at a minor friction without showing it could produce the observed collapse.`,`If mandatory programs decline too, that suggests participation fades regardless of design — which supports, rather than weakens, the critics' skepticism about recycling programs. Opposite.`],generalLesson:`A case study only supports a general conclusion if the case fairly tests the feature being judged. When the case changed in other major ways, its outcome cannot be blamed on the feature in question.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`case-study`,`confound`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-weaken`,`f-assumption`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The critics blame the "voluntary" design for the participation drop. Was voluntariness the only thing about the program that changed?`,`What happened to curbside pickup in year two — and how would that affect participation under any design?`,`The correct choice shows the program itself changed, so Riverton no longer tests voluntariness.`],labels:{topic:`recycling program`}},{id:`lr-c-097`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: forced walkers improved (natural experiment defeats self-selection); credited strengthener uniquely defensible. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A survey found that people who walk to work report better moods than people who drive. Researchers concluded that walking to work improves mood.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the researchers' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Walking is excellent cardiovascular exercise for people of all ages and fitness levels.`},{text:`The survey included several thousand commuters from diverse neighborhoods across the city.`},{text:`The researchers who conducted the survey walk to work themselves every day, rain or shine.`},{text:`When road construction forced a group of drivers to walk to work for a month, their reported moods improved.`},{text:`A person's mood can affect how they answer survey questions about their daily commute.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The forced-walking episode is a natural experiment: the same people walked instead of driving, and their moods improved. Because the change in commuting was imposed rather than chosen, it rules out the worry that naturally cheerful people simply prefer walking — directly supporting the causal claim.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The researchers' conclusion is causal: walking → better mood. The survey evidence is correlational, leaving open the alternative that cheerful people choose to walk. Choice (D) answers that alternative with a natural experiment: drivers who were forced to walk — not self-selected walkers — experienced mood gains. When the treatment is imposed on people who did not choose it, the self-selection explanation collapses, and the causal interpretation is strengthened. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: cardiovascular benefits do not show mood effects. Choice (B) is too weak: a large sample of a correlational survey still cannot rule out self-selection. Choice (C) hints at researcher bias but does not touch the evidence; it is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (E) is a vague methodological worry — mood affecting survey answers would, if anything, threaten the study, and it certainly does not strengthen the causal claim.`,choiceExplanations:[`Cardiovascular exercise is a genuine benefit of walking, but it is a different outcome from mood. This choice is irrelevant-true: it praises walking for something the conclusion never claimed.`,`A large sample makes the correlation more reliable, but it does nothing to rule out the self-selection alternative — cheerful people choosing to walk. Sample size cannot fix a causal inference problem, so this is too weak to strengthen.`,`The researchers' own commuting habits are irrelevant to whether their data support the conclusion. This choice attacks the source rather than engaging the evidence — the wrong-viewpoint trap.`,`Correct. Forced walkers are not self-selected: their mood improved when walking was imposed on them. This natural experiment rules out the main rival explanation (cheerful people choose walking) and directly supports the causal claim.`,`If mood distorts survey answers, the study's measurements become less trustworthy — which would weaken, not strengthen, the conclusion. This choice points in the wrong direction entirely.`],generalLesson:`Correlational evidence is most strengthened by ruling out self-selection. A natural experiment — where the treatment is imposed rather than chosen — is the cleanest way to support a causal claim.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`self-selection`,`natural-experiment`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The survey shows walkers are happier. What is the main alternative explanation besides "walking causes happiness"?`,`What would happen if people who did not choose walking were forced to walk — and their moods still improved?`,`The correct choice describes exactly that: forced walkers whose moods improved.`],labels:{topic:`walking and mood`}},{id:`lr-c-098`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: borrowing fell elsewhere without new branches (control comparison); credited strengthener uniquely defensible. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the city opened a new library branch in the Westside neighborhood, teen book borrowing in Westside rose 40 percent. Librarians concluded that the new branch caused teens to read more.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the librarians' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Teen borrowing fell elsewhere without new branches over the same period.`},{text:`The new branch has comfortable seating and long opening hours.`},{text:`Teenagers generally enjoy spending time at the library.`},{text:`The new branch was expensive for the city to build.`},{text:`The librarians who proposed the branch strongly support it.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`If borrowing fell everywhere except where the new branch opened, a citywide trend cannot explain Westside's rise — the branch stands out as the difference. The other neighborhoods act as a control group, and their decline makes the causal attribution to the branch much stronger.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The librarians' evidence is a before-and-after change in one neighborhood. The threat to their conclusion is a citywide trend — perhaps teens everywhere are reading more. Choice (A) eliminates that threat with a control comparison: other neighborhoods without new branches saw borrowing fall. Westside's 40 percent rise therefore cannot be a general trend; something specific to Westside did the work, and the new branch is the obvious candidate. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: comfortable seating does not show the branch caused more reading. Choice (C) is too weak: teens enjoying libraries is compatible with the rise having any number of causes. Choice (D) is irrelevant: construction cost says nothing about reading effects. Choice (E) is the wrong-viewpoint trap — the librarians' enthusiasm for their own project does not validate the causal claim.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The other neighborhoods serve as a control group: borrowing fell where no branch opened, so no citywide trend can explain Westside's 40 percent rise. The branch remains as the distinguishing cause.`,`Comfortable seating and long hours are nice features, but they do not show the branch caused the borrowing increase. This choice describes the building without connecting it to the outcome — irrelevant-true.`,`Teens enjoying libraries is a background fact that is compatible with any explanation of the rise. It is too weak to strengthen the specific claim that the new branch caused teens to read more.`,`Construction costs are irrelevant to the causal question. An expensive branch might have no effect on reading, and a cheap one might transform it — this choice says nothing about the outcome.`,`The librarians' support for their own project is expected and proves nothing about its effects. This choice appeals to the arguers' viewpoint rather than to evidence — wrong-viewpoint.`],generalLesson:`A before-and-after claim is strengthened by a control comparison. If the change happened only where the supposed cause operated — and not elsewhere — the causal attribution gains real support.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`control-group`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Westside borrowing rose after the branch opened. What would rule out a citywide reading trend?`,`What would you want to know about neighborhoods that did not get a new branch?`,`The correct choice gives you that control group: borrowing fell everywhere else.`],labels:{topic:`library branch and teen reading`}},{id:`lr-c-099`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: lead effect persists among equally affluent children (confound controlled); B and D are good opposite-direction traps. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Children living in older homes with lead paint score lower on learning assessments than children in newer homes. Researchers concluded that lead exposure causes learning difficulties.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the researchers' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Lead paint was banned for residential use decades ago, before most of the tested children were born.`},{text:`Learning difficulties in children have many different causes, most unrelated to environmental exposure.`},{text:`Among children from equally affluent families, those with higher blood lead levels still scored lower.`},{text:`Older homes are often poorly maintained in ways unrelated to lead paint, such as faulty wiring or plumbing.`},{text:`The researchers tested several hundred children drawn from a range of neighborhoods.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Family income is the obvious confound: poorer families live in older homes and face many disadvantages. By showing the lead-score link persists among equally affluent children, the finding rules out income as the explanation and isolates lead as the likely cause.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The observed correlation — older homes, lower scores — is threatened by a classic confound: socioeconomic status. Poorer families are likelier to live in older housing and also face nutritional, educational, and health disadvantages that depress test scores. Choice (C) neutralizes that threat by holding affluence constant: even comparing children of equal family income, higher blood lead predicts lower scores. With the main rival explanation controlled, the causal claim that lead itself harms learning is substantially strengthened. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: the ban's timing does not show lead causes learning problems. Choice (B) weakens by suggesting other causes. Choice (D) weakens by offering a rival explanation (poor maintenance). Choice (E) is too weak: a large sample of a confounded comparison remains confounded.`,choiceExplanations:[`The historical ban is true but inert: it says nothing about whether lead exposure impairs learning. This is the irrelevant-true trap — a fact about regulation, not about causation.`,`If learning difficulties have many causes, the observed gap might be due to any of them rather than lead. This choice multiplies alternative explanations and therefore weakens the conclusion — the opposite of strengthening.`,`Correct. Controlling for family affluence removes the most dangerous confound. When the lead-score relationship survives among equally affluent children, income cannot explain it, and lead remains as the supported cause.`,`Poor maintenance is a rival explanation for the score gap — another way older homes could harm learning besides lead. This choice weakens the researchers' conclusion by offering an alternative cause.`,`Testing hundreds of children improves precision but cannot remove confounding. A large confounded study is still confounded, so this choice is too weak to strengthen the causal claim.`],generalLesson:`The way to strengthen an observational causal claim is to rule out the leading confound. Controlling for the most plausible alternative explanation — here, family income — isolates the suspected cause.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`confound`,`control-variable`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Children in older homes differ from children in newer homes in more ways than lead exposure. What is the biggest one?`,`How could researchers show the score gap is about lead rather than about family income?`,`The correct choice holds income constant and finds the lead effect remains.`],labels:{topic:`lead paint and learning`}},{id:`lr-c-100`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: comparable city saw 12 percent fewer car trips (analogous evidence); credited strengthener uniquely defensible. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The city plans to launch a bike-share program and predicts it will reduce downtown congestion. Planners argue that convenient bike access will persuade many downtown drivers to switch from driving to cycling.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the planners' prediction?`,choices:[{text:`Bike-share programs are popular amenities in many cities.`},{text:`Parking downtown is very expensive.`},{text:`Cycling is healthy cardiovascular exercise.`},{text:`Some downtown drivers say they would never consider cycling to work.`},{text:`In a comparable city, a bike-share program cut downtown car trips by 12 percent.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A prediction about a new program is strengthened by evidence that the same program worked elsewhere under similar conditions. The comparable city's 12 percent reduction shows the planners' mechanism — drivers switching to bikes — actually operates in practice, not just in theory.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The planners' prediction rests on an untested mechanism: drivers switching to shared bikes. Choice (E) tests it with analogous evidence — a comparable city where bike-share cut car trips 12 percent. Relevant similarity (comparable city, same intervention, same outcome measure) is what makes analogical evidence probative, and this choice supplies it directly. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: popularity as an amenity does not show congestion effects. Choice (B) is too weak: expensive parking might push drivers toward bikes, but it does not show the bike-share program itself will reduce congestion. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: health benefits are a different question from traffic. Choice (D) weakens by identifying drivers who will not switch. Strengthen questions reward the answer that props up the argument's specific mechanism, not merely a nearby virtue of the plan.`,choiceExplanations:[`Popularity is not effectiveness. A program can be a beloved amenity without reducing congestion, so this choice is irrelevant-true — it praises bike-share for something other than the predicted outcome.`,`Expensive parking gives drivers a reason to consider alternatives, but it does not show the bike-share program will be the alternative they choose or that congestion will fall. Too weak to strengthen the specific prediction.`,`Health benefits are real but irrelevant to the traffic prediction. This choice answers a different question — whether cycling is good for people — rather than whether the program will cut congestion.`,`Drivers who refuse to cycle are evidence against the switching mechanism, so this choice weakens the prediction. Opposite. The prediction needs drivers to switch modes; a population that will not cycle leaves car trips — and congestion — unchanged.`,`Correct. A comparable city's 12 percent reduction in car trips is direct analogous evidence that the mechanism works: bike-share programs do convert drivers to cyclists at a scale that cuts congestion.`],generalLesson:`Predictions about untried policies are strengthened by analogous results elsewhere. The key is relevant similarity: same intervention, comparable setting, same outcome.`,misconceptionTags:[`analogy`,`prediction`,`precedent`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The planners predict a result for a program that does not exist yet here. What kind of evidence would make that prediction more credible?`,`Where has a bike-share program already been tried, and what happened to car trips there?`,`The correct choice cites a comparable city where the same program cut car trips 12 percent.`],labels:{topic:`bike-share and congestion`}},{id:`lr-c-101`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: departments without mentorship saw no gain (internal control group); D (salary raise) is a good opposite trap. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Since Northwind Corp. launched a mentorship program pairing new hires with senior staff, first-year retention has risen from 70 to 85 percent. Executives credit the mentorship program for the improvement.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the executives' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`New hires consistently say they appreciate having an assigned mentor during their first year.`},{text:`Departments that skipped the mentorship program saw no retention improvement over the same period.`},{text:`Mentorship programs are common at companies throughout the industry, especially among large employers.`},{text:`Northwind also raised starting salaries significantly last year, outpacing its competitors.`},{text:`Many senior staff members volunteered enthusiastically to serve as mentors in the new program.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The non-participating departments are a built-in control group: same company, same time period, no mentorship — and no retention gain. That contrast isolates the mentorship program as the difference-maker, ruling out company-wide trends as the explanation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The executives' evidence is a before-and-after change; the threat is any company-wide factor that improved retention independently. Choice (B) neutralizes that threat elegantly: departments without the program, experiencing the same company conditions and the same time period, did not improve. If a general trend (better economy, new HR policies) were responsible, it should have lifted all departments. The improvement appearing only where mentorship operated pins the effect on the program. Choice (A) is too weak: appreciation does not prove retention effects. Choice (C) is irrelevant: industry commonness says nothing about this program's efficacy. Choice (D) weakens by offering a rival cause — higher salaries. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: volunteer enthusiasm does not show the program retained anyone. The comparison-group structure is the strengthen move to look for whenever the evidence is a raw before-and-after difference.`,choiceExplanations:[`Appreciation is not retention. New hires can enjoy mentorship while leaving at the same rates, so this choice is too weak — it shows the program is liked, not that it caused the 15-point retention gain.`,`Correct. Non-participating departments form a natural control group within the same company and period. Their flat retention rules out company-wide explanations and isolates mentorship as the cause of the improvement.`,`That other companies use mentorship is irrelevant to whether Northwind's program caused its retention gain. Industry fashion is not evidence of efficacy. Widespread adoption might reflect marketing or herd behavior rather than results; it tests nothing about this program's causal role.`,`Raised starting salaries are a classic alternative cause of better retention. This choice weakens the executives' conclusion by offering a competing explanation for the same improvement.`,`Enthusiastic volunteers are a nice sign, but volunteer enthusiasm does not demonstrate that mentorship kept anyone from quitting. Irrelevant-true. People can love participating in a program that changes none of their employment decisions; enthusiasm measures experience, not retention effects.`],generalLesson:`Internal control groups are powerful strengtheners. When the effect appears exactly where the intervention operated — and nowhere else in the same organization — the causal claim is strongly supported.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`control-group`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Retention rose after mentorship began. How could you check whether something else at Northwind caused the rise?`,`What happened in departments that did not get the mentorship program?`,`The correct choice shows those departments saw no improvement — isolating mentorship as the cause.`],labels:{topic:`mentorship and retention`}},{id:`lr-c-102`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: scores fell when classes grew again (reversal/covariation); B and C are good opposite traps. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the district reduced average class sizes from 30 to 20 students, third-grade reading scores rose significantly. Administrators concluded that smaller classes caused the improvement.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the administrators' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Teachers consistently say they prefer teaching smaller classes.`},{text:`Reading scores also rose in neighboring districts that kept their class sizes at 30.`},{text:`The district hired many new teachers to staff the additional classrooms.`},{text:`When budget cuts later forced class sizes back up to 30, reading scores fell again.`},{text:`Parents in the district strongly support keeping class sizes small.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The reversal is the causal fingerprint: scores rose when classes shrank and fell when classes grew again. If some one-time factor (a new curriculum, a strong cohort) had caused the original rise, scores would not have tracked class size back down — the covariation in both directions strongly supports causation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The administrators' original evidence shows covariation in one direction: smaller classes, higher scores. Choice (D) adds covariation in the reverse direction: larger classes again, lower scores again. This before-and-after-and-before-again pattern is much harder for rival explanations to match. A new curriculum or an unusually strong cohort of students would not conveniently reverse itself exactly when class sizes reverted. The most economical explanation of scores tracking class size up and down is that class size drives scores. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: teacher preference does not show student learning improved because of class size. Choice (B) weakens — improvement without smaller classes suggests other causes. Choice (C) weakens by offering a rival cause: perhaps the new teachers, not the smaller classes, made the difference. Choice (E) is irrelevant: parental support is not evidence of efficacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Teacher preference is irrelevant to the causal claim about student learning. Teachers might prefer small classes for reasons unrelated to reading outcomes — this choice is irrelevant-true.`,`If scores rose where class sizes did not change, some other factor must be at work — which weakens the claim that smaller classes caused this district's improvement. Opposite.`,`New teachers are a rival explanation: the improvement might reflect who was hired rather than how many students were in each room. This choice weakens the administrators' conclusion by offering an alternative cause.`,`Correct. Scores tracking class size in both directions — up when classes shrank, down when they grew — is the signature of a genuine causal relationship. Rival one-time explanations cannot easily account for the reversal.`,`Parental support is a political fact, not evidence about learning. Whether parents like small classes has no bearing on whether small classes caused the score gains — irrelevant.`],generalLesson:`Covariation in both directions is far stronger than covariation in one. When an effect appears with the cause and disappears without it, rival explanations that cannot reverse themselves are ruled out.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`covariation`,`reversal-test`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Scores rose when classes got smaller. What would make that evidence much stronger?`,`What if classes got bigger again later — what would you expect to happen to scores if class size is truly the cause?`,`The correct choice reports exactly that reversal: scores fell when classes grew back.`],labels:{topic:`class size and reading scores`}},{id:`lr-c-103`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: fish ladders restored migration and salmon held steady (mechanism confirmed); credited strengthener uniquely defensible. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Salmon populations have declined sharply in rivers with hydroelectric dams. Conservationists argue that the dams caused the decline by blocking salmon migration routes.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the conservationists' argument?`,choices:[{text:`Where fish ladders were installed around dams, salmon populations remained stable.`},{text:`Hydroelectric dams provide a major source of renewable electricity.`},{text:`Salmon populations fluctuate naturally from year to year.`},{text:`Some dammed rivers never supported large salmon populations.`},{text:`The conservationists have long opposed the construction of new dams.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The conservationists propose a specific mechanism: dams block migration. Fish ladders unblock migration while leaving the dam otherwise in place — and where migration was restored, salmon held steady. That isolates the blockage mechanism precisely, confirming the causal story.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument's strength depends on its proposed mechanism: blocked migration routes. Choice (A) tests that mechanism with surgical precision. Fish ladders change one thing — whether salmon can migrate past the dam — while everything else about the dam (turbines, reservoirs, altered flow) stays the same. If salmon remain stable precisely where the migration blockage is removed, the blockage was doing the causal work, not some other feature of dams. This is mechanism confirmation at its best. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: electricity benefits do not address the salmon claim. Choice (C) weakens by suggesting natural fluctuation could explain the declines. Choice (D) is too weak: rivers that never had salmon say nothing about rivers that lost them. Choice (E) is the wrong-viewpoint trap — the conservationists' history of opposition does not validate their causal analysis.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Fish ladders remove the migration blockage while leaving the rest of the dam intact. Salmon stabilizing exactly where migration was restored confirms that blocked migration — the proposed mechanism — was the cause of the declines.`,`Renewable electricity is a genuine benefit of dams, but it is irrelevant to whether dams harmed salmon. This choice answers a different question — whether dams are good overall — rather than strengthening the causal claim.`,`Natural fluctuation is a rival explanation for the declines: perhaps the rivers are just in a down year. This choice weakens the conservationists' argument by offering an alternative cause.`,`Rivers that never had salmon cannot tell us why rivers that did have salmon lost them. This choice is too weak — it nibbles at the sample without addressing the mechanism.`,`The conservationists' long-standing opposition is irrelevant to whether their current causal claim is correct. This choice substitutes biography for evidence — wrong-viewpoint. Even a biased source can advance a sound causal argument; the reasoning must be judged on its evidence, not on the arguer's history.`],generalLesson:`Causal arguments are most strengthened by confirming their specific mechanism. An intervention that removes just the proposed mechanism — while leaving everything else intact — is the sharpest possible test.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`mechanism`,`intervention-test`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conservationists blame a specific mechanism: blocked migration. How could you test whether blockage is really the culprit?`,`What would happen if the dam stayed but salmon could migrate past it?`,`The correct choice describes fish ladders — which unblock migration — keeping salmon stable.`],labels:{topic:`dams and salmon`}},{id:`lr-c-104`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: helmet-law jurisdictions saw fatalities fall vs steady controls (analogy plus control); credited strengthener uniquely defensible; wave-2 semantic fix preserved untouched. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Just as mandatory seatbelt laws reduced traffic fatalities, mandatory bicycle helmet laws will reduce cycling deaths, argues safety advocate Ms. Osei.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens Ms. Osei's argument?`,choices:[{text:`Seatbelt laws were controversial when they were first introduced, facing strong public opposition from drivers and civil liberties groups.`},{text:`Many cyclists already wear helmets voluntarily, especially in cities with active cycling cultures and bike-share programs.`},{text:`Jurisdictions enacting helmet laws saw fatality rates fall, while rates held steady in comparable jurisdictions without them.`},{text:`Helmets can be uncomfortable to wear in hot weather, which discourages consistent use.`},{text:`Ms. Osei has campaigned for traffic safety laws for over two decades and is a respected advocate.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument is an analogy: helmet laws will work like seatbelt laws did. The strongest support is evidence that the analogy's predicted outcome actually occurred — helmet-law jurisdictions saw fatalities fall while comparable non-law jurisdictions did not, combining analogous precedent with a control comparison.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Osei's reasoning has two layers: an analogy (seatbelts → helmets) and a prediction (helmet laws will cut deaths). Choice (C) strengthens both at once. It provides direct evidence that the predicted effect occurred where helmet laws were enacted, and it adds a control group — comparable jurisdictions without laws where fatalities held steady — ruling out a general trend toward safer cycling. That is far stronger than merely asserting the analogy. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: past controversy says nothing about effectiveness. Choice (B) is too weak and potentially unhelpful: voluntary helmet use does not show a mandate would reduce deaths. Choice (D) is irrelevant at best — discomfort does not address fatality effects — and slightly weakens by suggesting noncompliance. Choice (E) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: Osei's experience does not validate her analogy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Initial controversy is a historical footnote, not evidence of effectiveness. Unpopular laws can work and popular ones can fail — this choice is irrelevant-true. Reception at passage tells you about politics, not outcomes; the prediction concerns deaths, which controversy neither raises nor lowers.`,`Voluntary helmet use does not show that a legal mandate would reduce fatalities; it might even suggest the law would change little. Too weak to strengthen the prediction about mandatory laws.`,`Correct. Falling fatalities in helmet-law jurisdictions, contrasted with steady rates in comparable jurisdictions without laws, is direct evidence the predicted effect is real — it confirms the analogy with a built-in control group.`,`Discomfort might reduce compliance, which would if anything undermine the prediction. It certainly does not strengthen the claim that helmet laws will cut deaths. A mechanism that lowers compliance works against the predicted benefit, making this choice at best neutral and at worst a weakener.`,`Osei's decades of campaigning are biographical, not evidential. This choice asks you to trust the advocate rather than the argument — the wrong-viewpoint trap. Long advocacy shows conviction, not correctness; the prediction stands or falls on evidence about helmets and deaths, not on Osei's résumé.`],generalLesson:`Analogical predictions are strengthened by evidence that the predicted outcome actually materialized in analogous cases — especially when comparable non-analogous cases serve as controls.`,misconceptionTags:[`analogy`,`control-group`,`prediction`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Osei predicts helmet laws will cut cycling deaths, by analogy with seatbelts. What would directly confirm that prediction?`,`Where have helmet laws already been tried — and what happened to fatality rates there versus places without them?`,`The correct choice reports falling fatalities in helmet-law jurisdictions with a control comparison.`],labels:{topic:`helmet laws`}},{id:`lr-c-105`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: minor visits fell while serious visits held steady (mechanism signature); C is a good opposite trap. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the regional hospital expanded its telehealth services, average emergency room wait times fell 25 percent. Administrators concluded that telehealth reduced ER crowding by diverting minor cases away from the emergency room.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the administrators' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Patients report high satisfaction with their telehealth visits.`},{text:`Telehealth appointments cost patients less than emergency room visits.`},{text:`The hospital also hired several additional emergency room nurses during the same period.`},{text:`Some patients say they still prefer in-person medical care.`},{text:`ER visits for minor complaints fell sharply while serious-emergency visits were unchanged.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The administrators propose a precise mechanism: telehealth diverted minor cases. The visit data match that mechanism exactly — minor visits dropped while serious visits held steady. A general decline in ER use would have reduced both categories; the selective drop confirms diversion rather than some broad trend.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion specifies not just that telehealth helped but how: by diverting minor cases. Choice (E) verifies that exact mechanism in the data. If telehealth were merely correlated with shorter waits — say, because the hospital got better at everything — serious-emergency visits would likely have shifted too. Instead, only the minor-complaint visits fell, which is precisely the signature of diversion: the patients telehealth can serve stopped coming to the ER, while those it cannot serve kept coming. This rules out rival explanations like overall declining ER demand or improved triage speed. Choice (A) is supported-nonresponsive: satisfaction does not show diversion occurred. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: lower cost does not demonstrate the causal path. Choice (C) weakens by offering a rival cause — more nurses. Choice (D) is irrelevant-true: some patients' preferences do not touch the visit data.`,choiceExplanations:[`Patient satisfaction is nice, but it is nonresponsive to the causal claim. Satisfied telehealth patients do not show that minor cases were diverted from the ER — this choice answers a different question.`,`Lower cost is true but irrelevant to the mechanism. It does not show that telehealth actually diverted minor cases or caused the wait-time drop — irrelevant-true.`,`Additional ER nurses are a direct rival explanation: more staff would also cut wait times. This choice weakens the administrators' conclusion by offering an alternative cause.`,`Some patients preferring in-person care is irrelevant-true. It says nothing about whether minor-case visits fell or why wait times dropped. Preferences describe tastes, not visit counts; patients can prefer in-person care while still using the virtual option for minor cases.`,`Correct. The selective decline — minor visits down, serious visits flat — is exactly what the diversion mechanism predicts and what rival explanations (general trends, better staffing) would not produce. It confirms the proposed causal path.`],generalLesson:`When an argument proposes a specific mechanism, the strongest support is evidence matching that mechanism's unique signature. A selective effect that only the proposed mechanism predicts rules out rival explanations at once.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`mechanism`,`signature-evidence`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The administrators claim telehealth diverted minor cases specifically. What pattern in ER visits would that predict?`,`If telehealth diverted only minor cases, what should happen to serious-emergency visits?`,`The correct choice shows minor visits fell while serious visits held steady — the diversion signature.`],labels:{topic:`telehealth and ER waits`}},{id:`lr-c-106`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: hours, staffing, layout unchanged (rivals ruled out); D is a good opposite trap. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After Halden's department store installed security cameras, shoplifting incidents dropped 40 percent. The manager concluded that the cameras caused the drop by deterring thieves.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the manager's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Security cameras have also been shown to deter vandalism in retail stores.`},{text:`Store hours, staffing levels, and merchandise layout were all unchanged during the period.`},{text:`Shoplifting is a serious and costly problem for retailers.`},{text:`The store hired additional security guards during the same month the cameras were installed.`},{text:`Customers report feeling safer shopping in stores with visible cameras.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Ruling out rival causes is the classic way to strengthen a before-and-after causal claim. With hours, staffing, and layout all held constant, the cameras remain the only changed factor — eliminating the alternative explanations that could otherwise account for the 40 percent drop.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The manager's evidence is timing: cameras installed, then shoplifting fell. The vulnerability is simultaneous change — anything else that shifted could be the real cause. Choice (B) closes that vulnerability by holding the leading candidates constant: same hours (no change in opportunity), same staffing (no change in human deterrence), same layout (no change in temptation or visibility). With the plausible alternatives ruled out, the cameras stand as the remaining explanation for the drop. Choice (A) is a scope shift: deterring vandalism is a different outcome and does not show cameras deterred shoplifting here. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: the seriousness of shoplifting does not show cameras reduced it. Choice (D) weakens by supplying a rival cause — more guards. Choice (E) is too weak: customers feeling safer is not evidence that thieves were deterred.`,choiceExplanations:[`Deterring vandalism is a different outcome from deterring shoplifting. This choice shifts scope to another crime without showing the cameras caused this store's shoplifting drop — scope-shift.`,`Correct. By holding hours, staffing, and layout constant, this choice eliminates the most plausible rival explanations. With alternatives ruled out, the cameras remain as the supported cause of the 40 percent decline.`,`That shoplifting is serious and costly is true but inert: it explains why the manager cares, not whether the cameras worked. Irrelevant-true. The gravity of a problem motivates action but never validates the chosen solution; costly shoplifting is compatible with useless cameras.`,`Additional security guards are a textbook alternative cause — human deterrence could fully explain the drop. This choice weakens the manager's conclusion rather than strengthening it.`,`Customers feeling safer does not show thieves were deterred; feelings and deterrence are different things. Too weak to strengthen the causal claim about shoplifting. Perceived safety can rise from visible cameras even as actual theft continues; the claim needs evidence about thieves' behavior, not shoppers' comfort.`],generalLesson:`Before-and-after causal claims are strengthened by eliminating rival causes. When the plausible alternatives are held constant, the remaining changed factor earns the causal credit.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`,`ruling-out`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Cameras were installed and shoplifting fell. What else could have changed at the store to explain the drop?`,`How would you rule out those alternative explanations?`,`The correct choice holds the leading alternatives — hours, staffing, layout — constant.`],labels:{topic:`security cameras and shoplifting`}},{id:`lr-c-107`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-samples`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE: difficulty inflated at d4; random selection securing representativeness is the textbook single-step strengthen move for generalizations, with no strong near-miss. Downgraded d4 to d3. 0 two-defensible findings. VALIDATE-AFTER-REVISION.`}],stimulus:`In a taste test, 9 out of 10 participants preferred the new recipe for Marlowe's tomato soup to the old one. The company concluded that most of its customers will prefer the new recipe.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the company's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`The participants were randomly selected from the company's customer database.`},{text:`The company's marketing team designed and supervised the taste test.`},{text:`Tomato soup is the company's best-selling product.`},{text:`Nine out of ten is a decisive majority of the participants.`},{text:`Individual taste preferences vary widely from person to person.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The conclusion generalizes from participants to all customers, so its strength depends entirely on representativeness. Random selection from the customer database is the gold standard for representativeness: it gives every customer an equal chance of inclusion and eliminates the selection biases that would otherwise threaten the generalization.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Generalizations live or die by sampling. The company's inference — from 10 tasters to its whole customer base — is vulnerable to any selection bias: perhaps participants were employees, friends of the chef, or self-selected soup enthusiasts. Choice (A) neutralizes that vulnerability at its root. Random selection from the customer database means the sample is, in expectation, a miniature of the customer base itself; no subgroup is systematically over- or under-represented. That is precisely what licenses generalizing the 9-to-1 result. Choice (B) if anything weakens, by hinting the test was run by an interested party. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: sales rank does not show the sample was representative. Choice (D) merely restates the 9-of-10 premise in fancier words — restates-premise. Choice (E) is too weak and undirected: wide variation in tastes does not show this sample misrepresents customers.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Random selection from the customer database makes the participants representative of the very population the conclusion is about. Representativeness is the load-bearing assumption of any generalization, and this choice secures it directly.`,`A test designed and supervised by the marketing team — the group with the most to gain from a favorable result — raises the specter of bias. This choice, if anything, weakens confidence in the result rather than strengthening the generalization.`,`That tomato soup sells well is true but irrelevant to whether the tasters represent customers. Best-seller status says nothing about sampling — irrelevant-true. Sales figures describe the whole customer base; they cannot tell you whether the handful of tasters resembled that base.`,`This choice just rephrases the premise: 9 out of 10 already is a decisive majority. Restating the evidence adds no new support for extending the result to all customers — restates-premise.`,`Wide variation in tastes is a background fact that cuts both ways and does not address representativeness. It is too weak and too vague to strengthen the generalization.`],generalLesson:`Generalizations are only as strong as their samples are representative. Random selection from the target population is the cleanest way to secure representativeness — and the strongest way to support a generalization.`,misconceptionTags:[`sample-bias`,`representativeness`,`generalization`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`restates-premise`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The company generalizes from 10 tasters to all customers. What must be true of the tasters for that leap to work?`,`How were the participants chosen — and what selection method would make them mirror the customer base?`,`The correct choice describes random selection from the customer database.`],labels:{topic:`soup taste test`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-c-108`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: ridership rose only on routes serving the priced zone (signature evidence); B and E are good opposite traps. Honest d4. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Cities that adopted congestion pricing saw bus ridership rise afterward. Transportation researchers concluded that congestion pricing caused the ridership increase by making driving downtown more expensive.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the researchers' conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Congestion pricing is unpopular with many drivers who must pay the charge, several of whom have organized protests.`},{text:`Bus ridership has been rising steadily nationwide for the past decade, a trend predating most pricing schemes.`},{text:`Within those cities, ridership rose only on bus routes serving the priced downtown zone, not on routes elsewhere in the same cities.`},{text:`The researchers who conducted the study publicly support congestion pricing and have advocated for its expansion.`},{text:`Some cities that adopted congestion pricing saw no increase in bus ridership, though their pricing zones were quite small.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`This is surgical evidence: the ridership gain tracks the priced zone exactly, appearing on downtown routes but not on other routes in the same cities. City-wide confounds — economic booms, transit campaigns, gas prices — would lift all routes; only the pricing mechanism predicts the gain precisely where driving got more expensive.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The researchers' claim is vulnerable to city-wide confounds: perhaps the pricing cities were booming, or gas prices rose, lifting transit everywhere. Choice (C) defeats every such confound at once with geographic precision. Routes serving the priced zone and routes elsewhere share the same city, the same economy, the same gas prices, and the same transit agency — they differ only in whether their riders face the congestion charge. Ridership rising exactly where the charge applies, and nowhere else, is the unique signature of the pricing mechanism. No city-wide alternative can explain a zone-specific effect. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: unpopularity does not show efficacy. Choice (B) weakens by offering a national trend as the alternative cause. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap — the researchers' opinions do not validate their findings. Choice (E) weakens by presenting counterexamples.`,choiceExplanations:[`Driver unpopularity is irrelevant to whether pricing caused the ridership gain. An effective policy can be unpopular and a popular one ineffective — this choice is irrelevant-true.`,`A national upward trend in ridership is a direct rival explanation: the pricing cities might simply be riding the national wave. This choice weakens the researchers' conclusion by offering an alternative cause.`,`Correct. The zone-specific pattern — gains only where the charge applies, within the same cities — rules out every city-wide confound at once. Only the pricing mechanism predicts an effect localized to the priced zone.`,`The researchers' personal support for congestion pricing is irrelevant to whether their data support the conclusion. This substitutes the arguers' viewpoint for evidence — wrong-viewpoint.`,`Cities with pricing but no ridership gain are counterexamples that directly undermine the causal claim. This choice weakens rather than strengthens. When the same intervention elsewhere produced none of the claimed effect, the causal link between pricing and ridership is cast into doubt.`],generalLesson:`The most powerful strengthening evidence has a signature only the proposed cause predicts. When an effect appears exactly where the cause operated — and not in otherwise identical settings — rival explanations are eliminated in one stroke.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`signature-evidence`,`control-group`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The researchers blame congestion pricing for the ridership rise. What city-wide factors could alternatively explain it?`,`How could the pattern of ridership gains distinguish pricing from a city-wide trend?`,`The correct choice shows gains only on routes serving the priced zone — the pricing signature.`],labels:{topic:`congestion pricing`}},{id:`lr-c-109`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-strengthen`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sick days dropped only after enrollment within individuals (self-selection defeated); D states the threat explicitly and is a dangerous opposite trap. Honest d5. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Employees who participate in Halcyon's wellness program take 30 percent fewer sick days than non-participants. The company concludes that the program reduces absenteeism.`,stem:`Which of the following, if true, most strengthens the company's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`The wellness program offers gym memberships, nutrition counseling, and smoking-cessation support.`},{text:`Individual employees' sick-day rates dropped only after they enrolled in the program, not before.`},{text:`Absenteeism costs employers billions of dollars each year.`},{text:`Healthier employees are more likely than less healthy employees to enroll in wellness programs.`},{text:`The wellness program is popular, with a long waiting list to join.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The deadly threat to this conclusion is self-selection: maybe healthy people join and sick people don't, so the program merely sorts rather than cures. Tracking individuals over time defeats it — each employee serves as her own control, and sick days fell only after enrollment, establishing the temporal precedence causation requires.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a difficult item because the observational comparison — participants versus non-participants — is confounded by self-selection, and the correct answer must neutralize that specific threat. Choice (B) does so with a within-person design: follow the same employees across the enrollment boundary. If the program merely attracted already-healthy people, their sick-day rates would have been low before enrolling too. Instead, rates dropped only after enrollment — the change follows the treatment within each individual. That temporal pattern rules out the sorting explanation and satisfies causation's temporal-precedence requirement: cause before effect, within the same person. Choice (A) is irrelevant-true: program features do not show the program worked. Choice (C) is irrelevant-true: the cost of absenteeism does not show this program reduced it. Choice (D) is the dangerous opposite trap — it states the self-selection threat explicitly, which weakens rather than strengthens. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: popularity is not efficacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Program features describe what the wellness program is, not what it accomplished. A well-equipped program might still fail to change behavior — this choice is irrelevant-true.`,`Correct. Within-person timing defeats self-selection: if the program merely attracted healthy people, their sick days would have been low before enrollment. The drop occurring only after enrollment shows the program changed outcomes, not just sorted people.`,`The cost of absenteeism explains why the company cares, not whether its program worked. This is irrelevant-true — a fact about the problem, not about the solution.`,`This choice states the self-selection threat explicitly: the participant/non-participant gap might reflect who joins rather than what the program does. It weakens the company's conclusion — the opposite of strengthening — which makes it a dangerous trap.`,`A waiting list shows demand, not results. Popular programs can be ineffective, so this choice is irrelevant-true. Demand measures desire to participate, while the claim concerns outcomes; long waiting lists are fully compatible with zero effectiveness.`],generalLesson:`Self-selection is the central threat to observational comparisons of participants versus non-participants. Within-person before-and-after evidence defeats it: when each individual improves only after the treatment, sorting cannot explain the change.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`self-selection`,`temporal-precedence`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-strengthen`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Participants take fewer sick days. Besides the program working, what else could explain that gap?`,`What if healthy people are simply more likely to enroll? How could tracking individuals over time rule that out?`,`The correct choice shows each employee's sick days dropped only after enrollment.`],labels:{topic:`wellness program`}},{id:`lr-c-110`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: other simultaneous safety changes is the maximally diagnostic confound question; credited answer uniquely defensible. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the city replaced a dangerous Main Street intersection with a traffic circle, accidents there fell 40 percent. City officials concluded that the traffic circle caused the improvement.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the officials' argument?`,choices:[{text:`Whether residents find the traffic circle aesthetically pleasing`},{text:`How many traffic circles the city built in other neighborhoods last year`},{text:`Whether the traffic circle was completed on schedule`},{text:`Whether drivers initially found the traffic circle confusing to navigate`},{text:`Whether other safety changes were made at the intersection when the circle was installed.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The officials credit the circle for the accident drop, but a "yes" to other simultaneous safety changes — new speed limits, signage, lighting — would give the credit to those changes instead, while a "no" would isolate the circle as the cause. The answer directly determines how much force the causal claim has.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Evaluate questions ask for the most diagnostic unknown: the question whose answer would most change your assessment of the argument. The officials' conclusion is causal, and the classic threat to before-and-after causal claims is simultaneous change. Choice (E) targets exactly that threat. If other safety improvements were installed alongside the circle, the 40 percent drop cannot be credited to the circle alone; if nothing else changed, the circle stands as the supported cause. Either answer is informative, which is the hallmark of a good evaluate choice. The distractors all ask questions whose answers would not move the needle. Choice (A) concerns aesthetics, irrelevant to accident causation. Choice (B) concerns other neighborhoods, irrelevant to this intersection. Choice (C) concerns scheduling, irrelevant to safety effects. Choice (D) is tempting — initial confusion might matter — but it does not discriminate between the circle and rival causes of the sustained 40 percent drop.`,choiceExplanations:[`Aesthetic opinions are irrelevant to whether the circle reduced accidents. Even if everyone found it ugly, the accident data would stand — this question would not help evaluate the causal claim at all.`,`Other neighborhoods' traffic circles have no bearing on what caused the accident drop at this intersection. This question changes the subject rather than testing the argument.`,`Construction scheduling is irrelevant to safety outcomes. Whether the project finished on time tells us nothing about why accidents fell — this question is non-diagnostic.`,`Initial driver confusion is a real phenomenon, but knowing about it would not discriminate between the circle and alternative causes of the 40 percent decline. It is at best a side issue, far less diagnostic than the simultaneous-change question.`,`Correct. This is the key confound question: a "yes" answer supplies rival causes for the accident drop, while a "no" answer isolates the traffic circle. Either way, the answer directly determines the argument's strength.`],generalLesson:`The most useful evaluate question is the one whose answer would most change your verdict. For causal claims, that is usually the confound question: did anything else change at the same time?`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`confound`,`diagnostic-question`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The officials say the circle caused the accident drop. What is the biggest threat to that causal claim?`,`What would you want to rule out before giving the circle the credit?`,`The correct choice asks whether other safety changes happened at the same time.`],labels:{topic:`traffic circle safety`}},{id:`lr-c-111`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: harmful residue levels is the mechanism presupposition; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d1. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Organic produce is healthier than conventional produce, argues nutritionist Dr. Rao, because organic farmers do not use synthetic pesticides.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate Dr. Rao's argument?`,choices:[{text:`Whether organic produce tastes better than conventional produce`},{text:`Whether conventional produce carries synthetic pesticide residues at levels harmful to health`},{text:`Whether organic farming is better for the environment than conventional farming`},{text:`Whether Dr. Rao eats organic produce herself`},{text:`Whether organic produce costs more than conventional produce`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Rao's entire case is that avoiding synthetic pesticides makes organic healthier. If conventional produce carries no harmful residue levels, her mechanism evaporates and the health claim collapses; if it does, her argument gains real support. The answer decides the argument's fate.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Strip the argument to its mechanism: no synthetic pesticides → healthier produce. That mechanism only works if synthetic pesticides on conventional produce actually pose a health risk. Choice (B) asks exactly that. A "yes" — residues reach harmful levels — validates the mechanism and supports the conclusion. A "no" — residues are negligible — removes the only reason offered for the health claim, leaving it unsupported. No other choice is similarly decisive. Choice (A) concerns taste, a different question from health. Choice (C) concerns the environment, also a different question — the conclusion is about human health, not ecology. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: Rao's personal diet does not validate or refute her reasoning. Choice (E) concerns price, irrelevant to the health comparison.`,choiceExplanations:[`Taste is a different question from healthiness. Even if organic tasted better, that would not show it is healthier — this question is non-diagnostic for the argument as stated.`,`Correct. Rao's mechanism requires pesticide residues on conventional produce to be a genuine health hazard. A "yes" supports her conclusion; a "no" removes its only offered basis. The answer directly determines the argument's force.`,`Environmental benefits are a separate issue from human health effects. The conclusion is specifically about which produce is healthier to eat, so this question answers a different question.`,`Rao's personal eating habits are irrelevant to whether her reasoning is sound. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it substitutes biography for evidence. A nutritionist's own diet tells you about her lifestyle, not her logic; the argument must be evaluated on its premises, not her plate.`,`Price is irrelevant to the health comparison. Organic could be healthier and cheaper, or less healthy and pricier — cost tells us nothing about the pesticide mechanism.`],generalLesson:`When an argument rests on a single mechanism, the most useful question targets that mechanism's key factual presupposition. If the mechanism's premise fails, the argument fails with it.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`mechanism`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the one reason Rao gives for organic being healthier?`,`For that reason to work, what must be true about pesticides on conventional produce?`,`The correct choice asks whether those residues reach harmful levels.`],labels:{topic:`organic produce health`}},{id:`lr-c-112`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: whether hours are the actual barrier tests the load-bearing assumption; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`The city council proposes extending public library hours into the evening, arguing that greater access to books will improve adult literacy rates.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the council's argument?`,choices:[{text:`Whether adults with low literacy currently lack library access because of the limited hours`},{text:`Whether the city's librarians support extending the evening hours`},{text:`Whether other cities have extended their library hours in recent years`},{text:`Whether evening hours would significantly increase the library's operating costs`},{text:`Whether children would also use the library during evening hours`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The proposal assumes the barrier to literacy is access hours. If low-literacy adults already can use the library during current hours — or are kept away by other barriers like transportation or awareness — longer hours will not move literacy rates. The answer tests the argument's load-bearing assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The council's logic is: more hours → more access → better literacy. That chain breaks at its first link if hours are not the actual barrier. Choice (A) investigates exactly that: do low-literacy adults currently miss out because of hours? A "yes" means the proposal targets a real bottleneck, supporting the prediction. A "no" — they are blocked by transportation, childcare, or simply not knowing about the library — means longer hours address the wrong problem, and the literacy gain will not materialize. Choice (B) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: librarians' support does not show the policy will work. Choice (C) is irrelevant: other cities' choices do not test this mechanism. Choice (D) answers a different question — cost-effectiveness rather than whether literacy will improve. Choice (E) shifts scope to children, while the conclusion concerns adult literacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. If limited hours are the actual barrier keeping low-literacy adults away, extending hours should help; if other barriers are responsible, it will not. The answer directly tests the proposal's central assumption.`,`Librarians' opinions are irrelevant to whether the policy will raise literacy. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it substitutes stakeholders' feelings for evidence about the mechanism.`,`Other cities' policies do not show whether longer hours will improve literacy here. This question is irrelevant to evaluating the council's causal logic. The evaluation must test this city's mechanism — hours to literacy outcomes — not tally what other municipalities have chosen to do.`,`Operating costs matter for budgeting, but the argument's claim is about literacy outcomes, not fiscal prudence. This question answers a different question. An intervention can be causally effective yet expensive, or cheap yet useless; cost and efficacy are independent questions.`,`Children's library use is outside the conclusion's scope, which concerns adult literacy. This choice shifts scope to a group the argument never addressed. Evidence about children cannot confirm or disconfirm a claim about adults; the evaluation must stay with the population in the conclusion.`],generalLesson:`Policy proposals assume a specific barrier. The most useful evaluate question checks whether that barrier is real: if the proposal targets the wrong obstacle, it cannot deliver its promised result.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`mechanism`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The council assumes longer hours will improve literacy. What must be true about low-literacy adults for that to work?`,`Is it the hours keeping them away — or something else?`,`The correct choice asks whether limited hours are the actual barrier.`],labels:{topic:`library hours and literacy`}},{id:`lr-c-113`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: family wealth is the leading selection confound; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Students at small schools score higher on standardized tests than students at large schools. Commentator Klein concludes that small schools provide a better education.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate Klein's argument?`,choices:[{text:`Whether teachers prefer working at small schools or large schools`},{text:`Whether large schools offer more extracurricular activities than small schools`},{text:`Whether test scores at small schools have been rising over the past decade`},{text:`Whether small schools enroll students from wealthier families on average than large schools do`},{text:`Whether Klein attended a small school as a child`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Family wealth is the classic selection confound: wealthier students score higher regardless of school, and may cluster in small schools. If small schools simply enroll advantaged students, the score gap reflects selection, not educational quality — a "yes" devastates the conclusion, a "no" rescues it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Klein's conclusion is causal — small schools cause better education — but his evidence is a raw group comparison, which is vulnerable to selection: the groups may differ in ways other than school size. Choice (D) targets the most dangerous such difference. Wealthy families' children score higher on standardized tests for many reasons unrelated to schooling (tutoring, stability, resources), and if they disproportionately attend small schools, the entire test-score gap could be selection rather than school quality. A "yes" answer would leave the conclusion essentially unsupported; a "no" would remove the leading rival explanation and strengthen it. Choice (A) is irrelevant: teacher preferences do not determine student outcomes. Choice (B) answers a different question about extracurriculars. Choice (C) is too weak: a rising trend at small schools does not address the selection problem. Choice (E) is the wrong-viewpoint trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Teacher preferences do not determine whether students learn more. This question is irrelevant to evaluating whether small schools cause the higher scores. What teachers want concerns working conditions, while the claim concerns student outcomes; the two can move in opposite directions.`,`Extracurricular offerings are a different question from educational quality as measured by test scores. This choice does not test Klein's causal claim. A school can excel at clubs while its instruction is mediocre, or vice versa; extracurriculars leave the test-score mechanism untested.`,`A rising trend at small schools is compatible with selection — advantaged students could be increasingly concentrated there. Too weak to address the core vulnerability.`,`Correct. Family wealth is the leading selection confound: if small schools enroll wealthier students, the score gap may reflect who attends rather than what the school does. The answer directly determines the argument's force.`,`Klein's own schooling is biographical trivia, not evidence. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it substitutes the arguer's background for analysis of the reasoning. Where Klein studied cannot validate or refute a causal claim about school size; only evidence about schools and scores can do that.`],generalLesson:`Group comparisons are vulnerable to selection: the groups may differ in background, not just in the factor being praised. The most useful question asks about the most plausible pre-existing difference.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`selection-bias`,`confound`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`,`too-weak`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Small-school students outscore large-school students. Besides school quality, what could explain that gap?`,`Do the two groups of students differ before they ever enter a classroom?`,`The correct choice asks about family wealth — the classic selection confound.`],labels:{topic:`school size and test scores`}},{id:`lr-c-114`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: comparable cities without the program form the control comparison; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Since the mayor's jobs program began, the city's unemployment rate has fallen from 8 percent to 5 percent. The mayor claims the program caused the decline.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the mayor's claim?`,choices:[{text:`Whether the mayor remains popular with voters`},{text:`Whether the jobs program was expensive for the city to operate`},{text:`Whether unemployment also fell in comparable cities that introduced no such jobs program`},{text:`Whether some program participants found jobs without the program's help`},{text:`Whether the jobs program will continue operating next year`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Comparable cities without the program are the control group. If their unemployment fell equally, a broader economic trend — not the mayor's program — explains the decline; if only this city improved, the program earns the credit. The answer adjudicates the causal claim directly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The mayor's evidence is a before-and-after change in one city; the threat is a general economic trend lifting all cities. Choice (C) supplies the control comparison that tests that threat. If comparable cities without jobs programs saw the same 8-to-5 improvement, the program cannot be the cause — the trend is. If comparable cities stagnated while this one improved, the program stands out as the difference-maker. Either answer is highly informative, which is what makes this the most useful question. Choice (A) is irrelevant: popularity does not show causation. Choice (B) answers a different question about cost rather than effectiveness. Choice (D) is too weak: "some" participants finding jobs independently does not show the program failed overall. Choice (E) is irrelevant: the program's future says nothing about its past effects.`,choiceExplanations:[`Voter popularity is irrelevant to whether the program reduced unemployment. This question substitutes politics for causal evidence. Popular programs can fail and unpopular ones can succeed; approval ratings measure sentiment, while the claim demands evidence about jobs.`,`Operating costs concern the program's efficiency, not its effectiveness. The claim is about whether unemployment fell because of the program — a different question from what it cost.`,`Correct. Comparable cities without the program form the control group: matching improvement there would implicate a broader trend, while stagnation there would isolate the program as the cause. The answer directly settles the causal question.`,`"Some" participants succeeding independently is too vague to threaten a claim about the citywide rate. This choice is too weak to be the most useful question.`,`Whether the program continues is irrelevant to evaluating what it already accomplished. This question looks forward while the claim looks backward. Continuation decisions reflect politics and budgets, not past efficacy; the program could be canceled after succeeding or extended after failing.`],generalLesson:`Before-and-after claims are best evaluated against a control group. The most useful question asks what happened where the supposed cause was absent.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`control-group`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Unemployment fell after the program began. How could you tell whether the program or the broader economy deserves credit?`,`What happened in similar cities that did not launch a jobs program?`,`The correct choice asks for that control comparison.`],labels:{topic:`jobs program`}},{id:`lr-c-115`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: cannibalization vs market expansion is the displacement assumption; D is a genuine half-right near-miss, correctly less diagnostic. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`E-book sales have now surpassed print book sales for the third year in a row. Publisher Dana Larkin predicts that print books will soon disappear entirely.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate Larkin's prediction?`,choices:[{text:`Whether Larkin's own company now publishes more e-books than print books`},{text:`Whether most readers say they still prefer the feel of a print book`},{text:`Whether e-book reading devices remain too expensive for most buyers`},{text:`Whether total book sales across all formats are growing or shrinking overall`},{text:`Whether e-book growth has come at the expense of print sales or reflects new readers`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Larkin's prediction requires e-books to be displacing print, not merely adding new readers. If e-book growth comes from market expansion — people who never bought print — print can survive indefinitely alongside it; if every e-book sale cannibalizes a print sale, disappearance becomes plausible. The answer decides which world we are in.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The evidence shows e-books outselling print; the prediction is print's extinction. The hidden assumption bridging them is displacement: e-book gains must be print's losses. Choice (E) tests that assumption head-on. Consider the two possible answers. If e-book growth reflects new readers (travelers, students, the merely curious) while print buyers keep buying print, the two formats coexist and the prediction fails. If instead each e-book sale replaces a print sale, print's base erodes and extinction becomes credible. No other choice discriminates so cleanly. Choice (D) is the tempting runner-up: total-market trends matter, but a growing total is compatible with print shrinking (if e-books capture all the growth) and a shrinking total is compatible with print surviving — it does not isolate displacement. Choice (A) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (B) is too weak: stated preferences do not determine market outcomes. Choice (C) is largely irrelevant to the displacement question.`,choiceExplanations:[`Larkin's business mix is irrelevant to whether her market prediction is correct. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it scrutinizes the predictor instead of the prediction's logic.`,`Stated preferences are weak evidence about purchasing behavior, and they do not address whether e-book growth displaces print. Too weak to be the most useful question.`,`Device prices affect e-book adoption at the margin, but they do not answer the displacement question at the heart of the prediction. Irrelevant to evaluating whether print will disappear.`,`Total-market trends are somewhat informative, but they do not isolate displacement: print could shrink within a growing market or survive within a shrinking one. This question is useful but strictly less diagnostic than the correct choice.`,`Correct. Displacement versus market expansion is the assumption the prediction stands or falls on. A "cannibalization" answer supports extinction; a "new readers" answer refutes it.`],generalLesson:`Predictions of one thing replacing another assume displacement, not just growth. The most useful question asks whether the newcomer's gains are the incumbent's losses.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`displacement`,`prediction`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`E-books outsell print. For print to disappear, what must be true about the relationship between the two formats' sales?`,`Could e-books be gaining new readers while print keeps its own buyers?`,`The correct choice asks whether e-book growth cannibalizes print or expands the market.`],labels:{topic:`e-books versus print`}},{id:`lr-c-116`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: pre-requirement volunteering rates test the self-selection threat; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`A school district that requires community service for graduation notes that its alumni volunteer at higher rates than alumni of other districts. The district concludes that the requirement builds lifelong civic engagement.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the district's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Whether the district's alumni tend to volunteer for causes related to their school service projects`},{text:`Whether district students already volunteered more than other students before the requirement began.`},{text:`Whether the community-service requirement is popular with parents in the district`},{text:`Whether required service helps students gain admission to selective colleges`},{text:`Whether other school districts are considering adopting similar requirements`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The district credits the requirement for alumni volunteering, but the students may have been unusually civic-minded all along — in which case the requirement merely coincided with, rather than caused, the outcome. Knowing pre-requirement volunteering rates tests the self-selection threat directly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion is causal: the requirement produced the volunteering. The evidence compares this district's alumni with other districts' alumni — a comparison vulnerable to pre-existing differences. Choice (B) targets the most dangerous one: perhaps this community was always unusually service-oriented, and its students would have volunteered at high rates with or without any mandate. A "yes" answer would mean the requirement gets credit for a trait the students already had, devastating the conclusion. A "no" — students were ordinary before, exceptional after — would strongly support the causal claim. Choice (A) is too weak and narrow: the causes served do not address whether the requirement caused the volunteering habit. Choice (C) is irrelevant: parental popularity does not show efficacy. Choice (D) answers a different question about college admissions. Choice (E) is irrelevant: other districts' plans do not test this causal claim.`,choiceExplanations:[`Which causes alumni support is a side issue. It does not address whether the requirement caused the volunteering habit in the first place — too weak and off-target.`,`Correct. Pre-existing volunteering rates test the self-selection threat: if the students were already exceptional volunteers, the requirement cannot claim credit for their alumni behavior. The answer directly determines the conclusion's plausibility.`,`Parental popularity is irrelevant to whether the requirement builds civic engagement. This question substitutes stakeholder approval for causal evidence. Parents can love a requirement that changes nothing about their children's civic habits; approval is not an outcome measure.`,`College admissions benefits are a different question from lifelong civic engagement. This choice does not test the district's causal claim. An admissions edge concerns applications, while the claim concerns lasting civic habits; one can exist without the other.`,`Other districts' deliberations are irrelevant to whether this district's requirement caused its alumni outcomes. This question changes the subject. What other boards are considering says nothing about the causal history of this district's alumni; deliberation is not evidence.`],generalLesson:`When a program takes credit for an outcome, ask what the participants were like before the program. Pre-existing differences — not the program — may explain everything.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`self-selection`,`baseline`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The district says the requirement caused the volunteering. What is the main alternative explanation?`,`Were these students unusually civic-minded before the requirement ever existed?`,`The correct choice asks about pre-requirement volunteering rates.`],labels:{topic:`mandatory community service`}},{id:`lr-c-117`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: job type is the leading group-comparison confound; E (decaf) is a genuine half-right near-miss, correctly less diagnostic. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`Office workers who drink coffee regularly complete more tasks per day than those who do not. Management consultant Reyes concludes that coffee improves worker productivity.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate Reyes's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Whether the coffee drinkers and the non-drinkers hold similar kinds of jobs`},{text:`Whether coffee is physically addictive`},{text:`Whether the workers enjoy the taste of coffee`},{text:`Whether Reyes drinks coffee regularly herself`},{text:`Whether decaffeinated coffee has the same apparent effect on task completion`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Job type is the lurking confound: demanding, fast-paced jobs might both drive workers to coffee and generate more completed tasks. If the groups hold similar jobs, the confound is ruled out and coffee remains as the explanation; if not, job differences could explain the entire gap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Reyes compares two self-selected groups — coffee drinkers and abstainers — who may differ in many ways besides coffee. Choice (A) investigates the most structurally dangerous difference: job type. High-pressure roles (trading floors, newsrooms, emergency dispatch) both demand heavy coffee consumption and produce high task counts; if coffee drinkers cluster in such roles, the productivity gap reflects job demands, not caffeine. A "similar jobs" answer eliminates that rival explanation and strengthens the conclusion; a "different jobs" answer supplies a complete alternative explanation and weakens it. Choice (B) is irrelevant: addictiveness does not address productivity effects. Choice (C) is irrelevant: taste preferences do not explain task counts. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (E) is tempting but weaker: the decaf comparison tests caffeine specifically, yet even a decaf difference would still leave the job-type confound unaddressed — and the question asks for the most useful single question.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Job type is the leading confound: demanding jobs could cause both coffee drinking and high task completion. Whether the groups hold similar jobs directly determines whether the productivity gap can be credited to coffee.`,`Addictiveness is irrelevant to the productivity claim. Coffee could be addictive and productivity-enhancing, or addictive and irrelevant — this question does not discriminate. Because addictiveness is compatible with both productivity gains and productivity losses, the answer cannot move the causal needle either way.`,`Taste preferences have no bearing on task completion rates. This question is irrelevant to evaluating the causal claim. Enjoying coffee's taste is an aesthetic matter; it cannot confirm or disconfirm whether caffeine changes how much work gets done.`,`Reyes's personal habits are irrelevant to whether her reasoning is sound. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Whether Reyes drinks coffee herself tells you about her routine, not her reasoning; the claim must be judged on its evidence.`,`The decaf comparison is somewhat informative about caffeine, but it leaves the job-type confound untouched: even decaf drinkers might hold different jobs. It is useful but strictly less diagnostic than the correct choice — half-right.`],generalLesson:`In group comparisons, the most useful question targets the most plausible pre-existing difference between the groups. Rule out the leading confound first; finer mechanistic questions come second.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`confound`,`group-comparison`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Coffee drinkers complete more tasks. Besides coffee, how might the two groups of workers differ?`,`Think about what kinds of jobs go with heavy coffee drinking — and with high task counts.`,`The correct choice asks whether the groups hold similar kinds of jobs.`],labels:{topic:`coffee and productivity`}},{id:`lr-c-118`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: additionality (would applicants have come anyway) is decisive; credited question uniquely diagnostic. Honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:`After the state introduced a tax incentive for new businesses, applications for business licenses rose 30 percent. The governor credited the incentive with attracting new businesses to the state.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the governor's claim?`,choices:[{text:`Whether the governor personally supported the tax incentive or merely signed it`},{text:`Whether the tax incentive significantly reduced the state's tax revenue overall`},{text:`Whether neighboring states offer similar tax incentives to new businesses locating there`},{text:`Whether the new applicants are businesses that would have come to the state even without the incentive`},{text:`Whether business license applications are processed quickly and efficiently by the state`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The governor's claim needs the incentive to have changed behavior — attracting businesses that would otherwise have stayed away. If the applicants would have come anyway, the incentive merely rewarded inevitable decisions and caused nothing; if they would not have, the causal credit is deserved. This additionality question is decisive.`,explanationWalkthrough:`License applications rose after the incentive — but "after" is not "because of." The pivotal issue is additionality: did the incentive cause decisions that would not otherwise have occurred? Choice (D) asks exactly that. A "yes, they would have come anyway" answer means the 30 percent rise reflects businesses already planning to relocate, perhaps drawn by unrelated factors, and the incentive changed nothing. A "no" answer means the incentive genuinely tipped decisions, supporting the governor's causal credit. Choice (A) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (B) answers a different question about fiscal cost, not causal efficacy. Choice (C) is too weak: neighboring states' policies do not show whether this state's applicants were actually swayed. Choice (E) is irrelevant administrative trivia. Evaluate questions ask for the test whose answer decides the argument's fate, and here additionality is that test.`,choiceExplanations:[`The governor's personal support is irrelevant to whether the incentive worked. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it examines the claimant instead of the claim. A governor can champion a failed policy or doubt a successful one; personal backing is not evidence of causal efficacy.`,`Revenue effects concern the incentive's cost, not whether it caused the application increase. This question answers a different question from the causal one asked. An incentive can work causally while being fiscally wasteful, or be cheap while changing nothing; cost and causation are separate.`,`Neighboring states' incentives might affect competition, but they do not reveal whether these applicants were actually influenced. Too weak to be the most useful question.`,`Correct. Additionality is the decisive issue: the incentive deserves causal credit only if it changed decisions. Whether applicants would have come anyway directly determines the claim's force.`,`Processing speed is administrative trivia with no bearing on why applications rose. This question is irrelevant to the causal claim. Fast paperwork neither attracts businesses nor explains their motives; it is compatible with any story about why applications increased.`],generalLesson:`Incentives deserve causal credit only for behavior they actually changed. The most useful question asks about additionality: would the outcome have happened anyway?`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`additionality`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`answers-different-question`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Applications rose after the incentive. For the governor to deserve credit, what must be true about the applicants' decisions?`,`What if every new applicant was already planning to come to the state?`,`The correct choice asks whether applicants would have come even without the incentive.`],labels:{topic:`business tax incentive`}},{id:`lr-c-119`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE: difficulty inflated at d4; relevant-similarity is the textbook single-step evaluate move for analogical predictions, with no strong near-miss. Downgraded d4 to d3. 0 two-defensible findings. VALIDATE-AFTER-REVISION.`}],stimulus:`City X built a network of protected bike lanes and saw cycling injuries fall 35 percent. Our city council argues that we should build the same lanes here, predicting a similar drop in injuries.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the council's prediction?`,choices:[{text:`Whether City X's bike lane network was expensive to construct`},{text:`Whether our city's cyclists support building protected bike lanes`},{text:`Whether our city's traffic patterns and cycling rates are similar to City X's`},{text:`Whether cycling injuries in our city have increased in recent years`},{text:`Whether City X's mayor was reelected after the bike lanes were built`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The prediction is an analogy: what worked in City X will work here. Analogies transfer only when the cases are relevantly similar — if our traffic patterns, cycling rates, and road layouts differ sharply from City X's, the 35 percent result may not travel. Comparability is the load-bearing question.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The council's inference has the form: intervention I produced outcome O in case X; therefore I will produce O in case Y. That inference is only as strong as the relevant similarity between X and Y. Choice (C) tests exactly that: traffic patterns (speeds, congestion, truck traffic) and cycling rates (how many cyclists, how experienced) are the very factors that determine whether protected lanes cut injuries. A "similar" answer licenses the transfer; a "dissimilar" answer — say, our city has far faster arterial traffic and almost no existing cyclists — breaks the analogy and the prediction with it. Choice (A) is irrelevant: construction cost does not affect injury outcomes. Choice (B) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: cyclist enthusiasm does not show the lanes will work. Choice (D) is too weak: a recent injury trend says nothing about whether our city resembles City X. Choice (E) is irrelevant political trivia.`,choiceExplanations:[`Construction costs are irrelevant to whether the lanes would reduce injuries. This question concerns budgets, not the analogy's validity. Expensive infrastructure can be effective and cheap infrastructure useless; cost says nothing about whether City X's experience will transfer.`,`Cyclists' support is politically relevant but evidentially inert: enthusiasm does not show the intervention will transfer. Wrong-viewpoint. Supporters can be passionate about an intervention that fails elsewhere; enthusiasm measures politics, while the question demands evidence about injuries.`,`Correct. Relevant similarity is what makes analogical predictions work. Traffic patterns and cycling rates are the factors that determine whether City X's result transfers — the answer directly controls the prediction's credibility.`,`A recent injury trend in our city does not address whether our city is relevantly similar to City X. Too weak to evaluate the analogical transfer.`,`The mayor's electoral fortunes are irrelevant to whether bike lanes cut injuries or whether our city resembles City X. Pure trivia. Election prospects concern the mayor's career, not the analogy's validity; they cannot confirm or disconfirm the causal transfer.`],generalLesson:`Analogical predictions stand or fall on relevant similarity. The most useful question asks whether the two cases match on the very factors that drive the outcome.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`analogy`,`relevant-similarity`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The council predicts City X's result will repeat here. What has to be true about the two cities for that inference to work?`,`Which features of a city determine whether bike lanes cut injuries?`,`The correct choice asks about traffic patterns and cycling rates — the relevant similarities.`],labels:{topic:`bike lane analogy`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-c-120`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE: difficulty inflated at d5; the compliance gatekeeper is a single presupposition test without layered reasoning or exceptional traps. Downgraded d5 to d4. 0 two-defensible findings. VALIDATE-AFTER-REVISION.`}],stimulus:`After Memorial Hospital adopted a surgical safety checklist, surgical complications fell 20 percent. The hospital's director concluded that the checklist caused the decline by reducing surgical errors.`,stem:`Which of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the director's conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`Whether other hospitals have adopted similar surgical checklists`},{text:`Whether the hospital's surgical teams actually followed the checklist consistently`},{text:`Whether implementing the checklist was costly for the hospital`},{text:`Whether the hospital's director has received awards for management excellence`},{text:`Whether surgical complications increase the cost of treating patients`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The conclusion's mechanism requires the checklist to have actually changed behavior in operating rooms. If compliance was high, the checklist is a live candidate for the 20 percent drop; if teams largely ignored it, the checklist cannot explain the improvement and some other cause must be responsible. Compliance is the gatekeeper question.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This item is difficult because the distractors gesture at real considerations while missing the argument's load-bearing assumption. The director's causal story is: checklist → fewer errors → fewer complications. That story presupposes the checklist was actually used. Choice (B) tests that presupposition directly, and it is maximally diagnostic: high compliance keeps the causal claim alive (the checklist could have done the work), while low compliance kills it outright (an unused checklist cannot reduce errors, so the 20 percent drop must have another cause — perhaps a Hawthorne effect from being observed, or staffing changes). No other choice has this gatekeeper property. Choice (A) shifts scope to other hospitals, which cannot show what happened at Memorial. Choice (C) concerns cost, a different question from efficacy. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: complication costs do not bear on what caused the decline.`,choiceExplanations:[`Other hospitals' practices cannot establish what caused Memorial's decline. This choice shifts scope away from the hospital in question — scope-shift. What other hospitals do is a different data set; the claim is about Memorial's complications, which only Memorial's evidence can explain.`,`Correct. Compliance is the gatekeeper: an unused checklist cannot reduce errors. High compliance preserves the causal claim; low compliance refutes it outright. The answer directly determines the conclusion's viability.`,`Implementation costs are irrelevant to whether the checklist caused the complication drop. This question answers a budget question, not the causal one. A costly checklist can still be the true cause of the decline, and a cheap one can be irrelevant; cost does not test causation.`,`The director's awards are biographical, not evidential. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it evaluates the claimant's résumé instead of the claim's mechanism. Decorated administrators can advance flawed causal claims; the complication decline must be explained by evidence, not by credentials.`,`That complications are costly is true but inert: it explains why the hospital cares, not what caused the 20 percent decline. Irrelevant-true. High costs motivate the search for solutions but never identify the true cause; expensive problems can have unrelated explanations.`],generalLesson:`Causal claims about an intervention presuppose the intervention was actually implemented. Compliance is the gatekeeper question: if the treatment was not delivered, it cannot have caused the effect.`,misconceptionTags:[`evaluate-strategy`,`compliance`,`mechanism`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`answers-different-question`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-evaluate`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The director says the checklist reduced errors. What must have happened in operating rooms for that story to work?`,`What if the surgical teams mostly ignored the checklist?`,`The correct choice asks whether teams actually followed the checklist.`],labels:{topic:`surgical safety checklist`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`}],r=[{id:`lr-d-121`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Transparent d1 negation exercise - negate the credited choice and the conclusion dies. 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 clean. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`The new Harper Bridge will ease downtown congestion, since it adds two lanes of capacity across the river.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,choices:[{text:`The Harper Bridge will be the longest bridge in the county.`},{text:`Downtown congestion is the city's most serious traffic problem.`},{text:`Drivers currently using congested routes will actually use the new bridge.`},{text:`The bridge will be completed exactly on schedule.`},{text:`Two new lanes are enough to eliminate downtown congestion entirely.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Apply the negation test: if drivers on congested routes never use the new bridge — say it goes nowhere they need — the added capacity sits empty and congestion cannot ease. The argument collapses, which proves the assumption is necessary.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion is that the bridge will ease congestion; the premise is that it adds capacity. The hidden bridge between them is usage: capacity only relieves congestion if congested drivers actually drive on it. Choice (C) states that link, and the negation test confirms it is required. Negate (C): suppose no currently congested driver uses the Harper Bridge — perhaps its exits serve only an industrial park. Then downtown routes stay exactly as crowded, and the conclusion is false. Because the argument cannot survive that negation, (C) is a necessary assumption. Choice (A) is irrelevant: length has nothing to do with congestion relief. Choice (B) is too strong and off-point: congestion need not be the "most serious" problem for the bridge to ease it. Choice (D) demands punctuality, which the argument never needs — a late bridge could still ease congestion once opened. Choice (E) is too strong: the conclusion claims easing, not elimination.`,choiceExplanations:[`Bridge length is irrelevant to the congestion claim. A short bridge used by thousands would ease congestion; a record-long bridge nobody drives on would not — this choice is irrelevant.`,`The argument does not need congestion to be the city's worst problem; it only needs the bridge to reduce it somewhat. "Most serious" is stronger than required — the too-strong trap.`,`Correct. Negate it — congested drivers never use the bridge — and the conclusion dies: empty new lanes cannot relieve crowded old ones. The argument depends on drivers actually switching routes.`,`The conclusion is about what the bridge will do once it exists, not when it opens. A delayed bridge could still ease congestion, so punctuality is not required — too-strong.`,`"Eliminate entirely" goes far beyond the conclusion's modest "ease." The argument needs only some relief, not total elimination — too-strong. Necessary assumptions are minimal: the argument survives if congestion merely lessens, so demanding total elimination overshoots what the reasoning requires.`],generalLesson:`Test necessary assumptions by negation: deny the candidate and see whether the argument can survive. If the conclusion becomes impossible, the assumption was required.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`necessary-assumption`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The bridge adds capacity. What has to happen for added capacity to actually reduce congestion?`,`Try negating each choice: which denial would make "the bridge will ease congestion" impossible?`,`The correct choice is about drivers actually using the new bridge.`],labels:{topic:`new bridge and congestion`}},{id:`lr-d-122`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Mechanism-necessity made explicit by the negation test; distractors are honest. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`This restaurant's soup tastes remarkably fresh, so it must be made fresh daily.`,stem:`The argument depends on assuming which of the following?`,choices:[{text:`A soup's taste reliably indicates how recently it was made.`},{text:`Fresh soup is healthier than soup made from preserved ingredients.`},{text:`The restaurant makes all of its dishes fresh every day.`},{text:`Customers generally prefer fresh soup to reheated soup.`},{text:`The soup contains no preservatives whatsoever.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The entire inference runs from taste to freshness. Negate the link — suppose taste says nothing reliable about when soup was made, because skilled seasoning can fake freshness — and the premise gives zero support for the conclusion. The argument needs taste to be a reliable guide.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Premise: the soup tastes fresh. Conclusion: it was made fresh daily. The only thing connecting taste to production time is the assumption that taste tracks freshness reliably. Choice (A) states that connection, and negation confirms it: if taste were an unreliable indicator — if, say, flavor enhancers routinely make week-old soup taste fresh — then "tastes fresh" would be no evidence at all of daily preparation, and the conclusion would be unsupported. Choice (B) is irrelevant: health comparisons play no role in the inference. Choice (C) is too strong: the argument concerns only the soup, not every dish. Choice (D) is irrelevant: customer preferences do not connect taste to freshness. Choice (E) is too strong: the soup could contain some preservatives and still be made fresh daily.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The inference from "tastes fresh" to "made fresh daily" works only if taste reliably reveals production time. Negate that reliability and the premise becomes evidence of nothing.`,`Whether fresh soup is healthier is a separate issue the argument never raises. This choice is irrelevant to the taste-to-freshness inference. Health and freshness are different properties; the inference moves from taste to preparation time, and health claims touch neither.`,`"All of its dishes" goes beyond what the argument needs, which concerns only the soup. The too-strong trap: a broader claim than the reasoning requires.`,`Customer preferences are irrelevant to whether this soup was made today. The argument moves from taste to freshness, not from popularity to preparation. What customers like concerns demand, while the conclusion concerns kitchen timing; preferences cannot date a batch of soup.`,`"No preservatives whatsoever" is stronger than needed — soup with minor preservatives could still be made fresh daily. Too-strong. The argument needs freshness, not purity; a necessary assumption must be no stronger than the conclusion it supports.`],generalLesson:`Every inference from evidence to conclusion crosses a hidden bridge. Find the bridge by asking: what must be true about the evidence for it to actually point at the conclusion?`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`necessary-assumption`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The evidence is taste; the conclusion is about when the soup was made. What connects those two?`,`If taste told you nothing reliable about freshness, would the argument still work?`,`The correct choice states that taste reliably indicates recency.`],labels:{topic:`restaurant soup freshness`}},{id:`lr-d-123`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Rival-cause elimination with an honest causal-converse structure. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`Candidate Morales's poll numbers rose sharply in the week after the televised debate. Therefore, her debate performance caused the increase in support.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Morales won the televised debate.`},{text:`Televised debates always change candidates' poll numbers.`},{text:`Most voters watched the entire debate from start to finish.`},{text:`Morales's opponents performed poorly in the debate.`},{text:`No other major event that week could account for the poll rise.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument credits the debate for the poll surge, which requires ruling out competing explanations. Negate (E): suppose a major endorsement or scandal broke that same week and fully explains the surge. Then the debate gets no credit, and the causal conclusion fails — so the argument depends on no such rival existing.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Causal conclusions from timing need the absence of rival causes — that is the assumption doing the hidden work here. Choice (E) states it in its proper modest form: not that nothing else happened, but that nothing else could account for the rise. The negation test is decisive: if some other major event fully explained the poll surge, attributing it to the debate would be unjustified, and the conclusion would collapse. Choice (A) is not required: Morales could gain support from a debate she did not outright "win" (a strong showing against expectations suffices). Choice (B) is far too strong — "always" is never needed. Choice (C) is too strong: voters could learn about the debate secondhand, so most watching all of it is unnecessary. Choice (D) is not required: Morales could impress on her own merits regardless of her opponents' performance.`,choiceExplanations:[`"Won the debate" is stronger than needed — a candidate can gain from exceeding low expectations without winning. The argument does not depend on outright victory.`,`"Always" makes this far stronger than the argument requires. The conclusion needs this debate to have mattered, not every debate in history — too-strong. Universal claims are the classic too-strong trap: the reasoning depends on one debate's influence, not on a law of all debates.`,`Voters could absorb debate highlights through news coverage, so universal full viewing is unnecessary. "Most" and "entire" make this stronger than required — too-strong. The argument needs only enough exposure to shift support, not total viewership; partial, mediated exposure can suffice.`,`Morales could shine on her own; her opponents' stumbling is not a prerequisite for her gaining support. Not required. A candidate can gain support through her own performance alone, so the argument's conclusion does not depend on her rivals' failures.`,`Correct. Negate it — another major event fully explains the surge — and the debate loses its causal credit. The argument depends on excluding rival explanations.`],generalLesson:`Causal arguments from timing always assume no rival cause did the work. That "no alternative" assumption is necessary — and it is exactly what the negation test exposes.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The polls rose after the debate. What else, besides the debate, could have moved the polls that week?`,`What would happen to the argument if a different major event fully explained the surge?`,`The correct choice rules out rival explanations for the poll rise.`],labels:{topic:`debate and poll numbers`}},{id:`lr-d-124`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. No-defect elimination cleanly closes the gap. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The museum's new exhibit drew record crowds. The director credits the museum's marketing campaign for the turnout.`,stem:`The director's reasoning depends on which of the following assumptions?`,choices:[{text:`The marketing campaign was expensive to run.`},{text:`The record crowds were at least partly due to the marketing campaign.`},{text:`The exhibit itself was too uninteresting to draw crowds on its own.`},{text:`Every visitor to the exhibit had seen the marketing campaign.`},{text:`The museum had never advertised its exhibits before this campaign.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Crediting marketing with the turnout requires marketing to have actually contributed. Negate (B): suppose the crowds came entirely for other reasons — the exhibit's fame, school holidays — with marketing contributing nothing. Then crediting the campaign is simply false, so the argument depends on marketing having played at least some role.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The director's conclusion assigns causal credit to marketing. The minimal claim that credit requires is that marketing contributed something — not everything, just something. Choice (B) captures that minimum with "at least partly," and negation confirms necessity: if marketing contributed nothing at all, the credit is misplaced and the conclusion false. Note how the choice is carefully calibrated: it does not claim marketing was the main cause or the only cause, which would be stronger than needed. Choice (A) is irrelevant: cost does not affect causal contribution. Choice (C) is not required and actually misstates the logic — the exhibit could be fascinating and marketing could still have boosted turnout further. Choice (D) is too strong: marketing can work through word of mouth without every visitor seeing an ad. Choice (E) is irrelevant: the museum's advertising history does not bear on this campaign's effect.`,choiceExplanations:[`Campaign cost is irrelevant to whether the campaign caused the turnout. An expensive campaign might flop and a cheap one might succeed — this choice is irrelevant.`,`Correct. The "at least partly" formulation is the precise minimum the credit-claim needs: negate it (marketing contributed nothing) and the director's conclusion becomes false. This is the calibrated necessary assumption: strong enough to support the causal credit, weak enough to survive negation testing.`,`The exhibit could be wonderful and marketing could still deserve partial credit for the record turnout. This choice is not required — and it misunderstands how partial causation works.`,`"Every visitor" is far stronger than needed. Marketing can boost turnout through buzz and word of mouth reaching people who never saw an ad — too-strong.`,`Whether the museum advertised in the past has no bearing on whether this campaign drew these crowds. Irrelevant. Past campaigns concern different efforts at different times; they cannot show what caused this particular attendance surge.`],generalLesson:`Assigning causal credit requires the credited factor to have contributed. The necessary assumption is usually the modest one — "at least partly" — not the sweeping one.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The director gives marketing the credit. What is the smallest claim about marketing that her credit requires?`,`What if marketing contributed absolutely nothing to the turnout?`,`The correct choice says the crowds were at least partly due to marketing.`],labels:{topic:`museum marketing`}},{id:`lr-d-125`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Compliance bridge is necessary but correctly stops short of guaranteeing compliance. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The new law requires all cyclists to wear helmets. Therefore, head injuries among cyclists will decline.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,choices:[{text:`Helmets are comfortable enough to wear for long rides.`},{text:`The helmet law will be popular with cyclists.`},{text:`Cycling will become more popular once the law takes effect.`},{text:`Cyclists will actually comply with the helmet law.`},{text:`No cyclist will ever again ride without a helmet.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A law changes outcomes only if people follow it. Negate (D): suppose cyclists universally ignore the law and ride helmetless as before. Then helmet use does not rise and injuries have no reason to fall — the prediction fails, proving compliance is a required assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument moves from a legal requirement to a real-world outcome, and that move silently assumes the law will be obeyed. Choice (D) states the assumption plainly. The negation test is clean: widespread noncompliance means no increase in helmet wearing, which means no mechanism for injury reduction, which means the conclusion is unsupported. Choice (A) is not required: uncomfortable helmets might still be worn, or compliance might be imperfect yet sufficient. Choice (B) is irrelevant: popularity does not determine compliance or injury rates. Choice (C) is not required and, if anything, complicates the prediction — more cycling could mean more total injuries. Choice (E) is too strong: the conclusion needs enough compliance to move the injury rate, not literally universal perpetual obedience.`,choiceExplanations:[`Comfort is not required for the prediction: cyclists might wear uncomfortable helmets, or enough might comply despite discomfort. Not a necessary assumption. People routinely comply with uncomfortable mandates, from seatbelts to uniforms; the prediction needs compliance, not comfort.`,`The law's popularity is irrelevant to whether injuries will fall. Unpopular laws can be obeyed and popular ones ignored — this choice is irrelevant. Popularity measures attitudes while injuries measure outcomes; the prediction depends on behavior, which popularity does not determine.`,`Rising cycling popularity is not assumed and would actually threaten the conclusion, since more riders could mean more injuries overall. Not required. Necessary assumptions must support the conclusion; a claim that points against it cannot be one the argument depends on.`,`Correct. Negate compliance — cyclists ignore the law — and the mechanism vanishes: no new helmets, no reason for injuries to fall. The argument depends on actual obedience.`,`"No cyclist ever again" demands literally perfect compliance, far more than the argument needs. A substantial but imperfect compliance rate could still cut injuries — too-strong.`],generalLesson:`Arguments from law or policy to outcomes always assume compliance. A rule on paper changes nothing until people actually follow it.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`compliance`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`A law was passed; injuries are predicted to fall. What has to happen in between?`,`What if every cyclist simply ignored the new law?`,`The correct choice is about cyclists actually complying.`],labels:{topic:`helmet law compliance`}},{id:`lr-d-126`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Supply-demand mechanism plus a real near-miss (choice A, too-weak). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`Sales of organic produce have doubled in the past five years, which shows that consumers care more about health than they used to.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,choices:[{text:`Organic produce is actually healthier than conventional produce in every respect.`},{text:`Consumers now buy only organic produce and avoid conventional produce entirely.`},{text:`The sales increase reflects consumer demand rather than greater availability or lower prices.`},{text:`All consumers care deeply about their health and act on that concern.`},{text:`Sales of organic produce will continue to rise in the future indefinitely.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The conclusion is about what consumers care about, but sales can rise for supply-side reasons — more shelf space, better distribution, lower prices — that say nothing about consumer values. Negate (C): if the doubling is entirely due to availability, the conclusion about caring collapses. The argument needs the sales trend to reflect demand.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Premise: organic sales doubled. Conclusion: consumers care more about health. Between them lies the assumption that sales figures mirror consumer preferences rather than supply conditions. Choice (C) states that assumption, and negation confirms it: suppose organic produce simply became far more available — stocked in every supermarket at competitive prices — while consumer attitudes never budged. Sales would double from convenience alone, and the conclusion about caring would be unsupported. Choice (A) is not required: consumers need only believe organic is healthier, not for it to be true. Choice (B) is far too strong — "only organic" is never needed. Choice (D) is too strong: the conclusion is comparative (care more than before), not universal. Choice (E) is irrelevant: future sales do not bear on what current sales reveal about attitudes.`,choiceExplanations:[`Actual healthiness is not required — the conclusion is about what consumers care about, and people can care based on belief alone. This choice confuses the facts with consumers' perceptions of them.`,`"Only organic" is vastly stronger than the argument needs. Consumers could buy mostly conventional produce while still caring more about health than before — too-strong.`,`Correct. Negate it — the sales doubling is entirely a supply-side phenomenon — and the conclusion about consumer values loses its support. The argument depends on sales reflecting demand.`,`"All consumers" universalizes a comparative claim. The argument needs consumers on the whole to care more, not every single consumer to care deeply — too-strong.`,`Future sales trajectories are irrelevant to what past sales reveal about current attitudes. This question looks forward while the inference looks at the present. What sales will do next year cannot change what last quarter's sales already revealed about today's consumer attitudes.`],generalLesson:`Sales data reflect both demand and supply. Before concluding anything about what buyers want, rule out the possibility that sellers simply made the product easier to buy.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`supply-vs-demand`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Sales doubled. Does that necessarily mean consumers' values changed?`,`What supply-side changes could double sales without any change in what consumers care about?`,`The correct choice requires the sales increase to reflect genuine demand.`],labels:{topic:`organic produce sales`}},{id:`lr-d-127`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`A survey of 500 town residents found that 60 percent support the new park. Therefore, most of the town's residents support it.`,stem:`The argument requires the assumption that`,choices:[{text:`the 500 residents surveyed were representative of the town's population.`},{text:`the survey questions about the park were worded clearly.`},{text:`all 500 residents answered the survey completely honestly.`},{text:`the town has exactly 500 residents.`},{text:`no resident changed their mind about the park after being surveyed.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Generalizing from a sample to a population requires the sample to resemble the population. Negate (A): suppose the 500 were all park neighbors or all surveyed at a pro-park rally. Then 60 percent support among them says nothing about the town, and the conclusion fails — so representativeness is required.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference is a textbook generalization: sample → population. Its load-bearing assumption is representativeness — the surveyed 500 must mirror the town on relevant characteristics. Choice (A) states it directly. The negation test is decisive: a biased sample (say, only residents living adjacent to the proposed park site) could show 60 percent support while the town overall opposes it, destroying the conclusion. Choice (B) is not strictly required: even imperfectly worded questions could still yield usable data, and clarity is a weaker, secondary concern. Choice (C) is too strong: "all" answering "completely honestly" exceeds what the inference needs — minor dishonesty would not invalidate a 60 percent finding. Choice (D) is irrelevant and false-sounding: the town is clearly larger than 500, and the argument never needs equality. Choice (E) is irrelevant: post-survey mind-changing does not affect what the survey showed.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Representativeness is the assumption every sample-to-population inference depends on. Negate it — the sample was systematically skewed — and the 60 percent figure cannot speak for the town.`,`Clear wording helps, but it is not the load-bearing assumption: the fatal threat to this inference is who was asked, not how they were asked. A secondary concern, not a required assumption.`,`"All" and "completely" make this stronger than needed. The conclusion could survive some dishonest answers; it cannot survive an unrepresentative sample — too-strong. Representativeness, not perfection, is the necessary assumption; a sample can be slightly noisy yet still mirror the town.`,`The town plainly has more than 500 residents, and nothing in the reasoning requires the sample to be the whole population. Irrelevant. Sample-to-population inference never requires surveying everyone; it requires the sample to be representative, which is a different claim.`,`What residents think after the survey does not change what the survey measured. This choice is irrelevant to the sample-to-population inference. Later opinion shifts cannot retroactively alter recorded responses; the inference concerns what the survey captured when taken.`],generalLesson:`Every generalization from a sample assumes representativeness. When you see "survey says X, therefore the population thinks X," the required assumption is always about who was surveyed.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`representativeness`,`sample-bias`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion is about the whole town; the evidence is about 500 people. What must be true of those 500?`,`What if the 500 were all surveyed at a pro-park rally?`,`The correct choice requires the sample to be representative.`],labels:{topic:`park survey`}},{id:`lr-d-128`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`After security cameras were installed downtown, reported crime fell 25 percent. The mayor concluded that the cameras caused the decline.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption required by the mayor's argument?`,choices:[{text:`The cameras deter all types of crime equally.`},{text:`Downtown merchants support the installation of the cameras.`},{text:`The cameras were the most expensive crime-prevention option available.`},{text:`Crime would have risen downtown if the cameras had not been installed.`},{text:`The decline in crime was not entirely due to factors other than the cameras.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The mayor's causal credit needs the cameras to have done at least some of the work. Negate (E): suppose the entire 25 percent drop is explained by other factors — a new patrol strategy, demographic change — with cameras contributing nothing. Then the conclusion is false, so the argument requires that the drop not be wholly attributable elsewhere.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is the "not entirely due to other factors" form of the necessary assumption, and the wording matters: it is the minimal exclusion the causal claim needs. Choice (E) does not require cameras to be the main cause or the only cause — only that they not be entirely causally idle. Negation is fatal: if other factors explain 100 percent of the decline, cameras explain zero percent, and the mayor's conclusion collapses. Choice (A) is too strong: deterring "all types equally" is never needed for an overall decline. Choice (B) is irrelevant: merchant opinion does not affect causation. Choice (C) is irrelevant: cost does not bear on efficacy. Choice (D) is the tempting too-strong trap: the argument needs the decline to be attributable to cameras, which requires only that crime would not have fallen as much without them — "would have risen" demands more than necessary.`,choiceExplanations:[`"All types equally" is far stronger than the argument needs. Cameras could deter only theft and still produce a 25 percent overall decline — too-strong.`,`Merchant support is politically nice but causally inert. Opinions do not determine whether cameras reduced crime — irrelevant. Merchants can unanimously favor cameras that change nothing about crime; approval is not a causal mechanism.`,`Cost is irrelevant to the causal claim. The most expensive option might fail and the cheapest might work — this choice is irrelevant. Price tags do not determine outcomes; the causal question is whether cameras changed criminal behavior, which cost cannot answer.`,`This is tempting but too strong: the argument needs crime to have fallen less without cameras, not necessarily to have risen. "Would have risen" demands more than the conclusion requires.`,`Correct. This is the precise minimum: cameras must have contributed something. Negate it — other factors explain everything — and the mayor's causal credit vanishes.`],generalLesson:`Causal conclusions minimally require the cause to have contributed. "Not entirely due to other factors" is the weakest — and therefore the genuinely necessary — form of that requirement.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`causal-flaw`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The mayor credits the cameras. What is the least she must be assuming about other possible causes?`,`What if a new patrol strategy explained the entire 25 percent drop?`,`The correct choice says the decline was not entirely due to other factors.`],labels:{topic:`downtown security cameras`}},{id:`lr-d-129`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d3; single negation move on a fully stated arithmetic comparison is d2 material. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 3 to 2. 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`The city's composting program costs $2 million per year to operate and saves $3 million annually in landfill fees. Therefore, the program is financially worthwhile.`,stem:`The argument depends on assuming which of the following?`,choices:[{text:`Composting is beneficial for the environment.`},{text:`The claimed $3 million in landfill savings will actually be realized.`},{text:`Landfill fees will continue to rise in future years.`},{text:`No other city program saves more money than the composting program.`},{text:`Residents strongly support the composting program.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The "worthwhile" verdict is arithmetic: $3 million saved versus $2 million spent. Negate (B): suppose the savings never materialize — the landfill fees were overestimated, or the compost still ends up landfilled. Then the program costs $2 million and saves nothing, and "financially worthwhile" is false. The conclusion depends on the savings being real.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion follows from a simple comparison of two numbers, so it depends on both numbers being accurate. Choice (B) secures the savings side of the ledger. The negation test is straightforward: if the $3 million in savings is illusory — miscalculated, double-counted, or never achieved — the arithmetic flips and the program loses money, destroying the conclusion. Note that (B) is calibrated to what is needed: it requires the savings to materialize, not to grow or to exceed every alternative. Choice (A) is irrelevant: the conclusion is explicitly financial, not environmental. Choice (C) is not required: the program is worthwhile at current fee levels; rising fees would help but are unnecessary. Choice (D) is too strong and misguided: "worthwhile" does not mean "the best of all programs." Choice (E) is irrelevant: popularity does not affect the financial arithmetic.`,choiceExplanations:[`Environmental benefits are a different question from financial worth. The conclusion is explicitly about money, so this choice is irrelevant to the arithmetic it rests on.`,`Correct. The worthwhile-verdict is $3M saved versus $2M spent. Negate the savings — they never materialize — and the program loses money, falsifying the conclusion.`,`Rising fees would make the program even more worthwhile, but the conclusion already follows at current fees. Future increases are a bonus, not a requirement.`,`"Worthwhile" does not mean "better than every alternative." The program can be financially sound even if another program saves more — too-strong and misdirected. Comparative superiority is a stricter standard than the conclusion asserts; the argument needs only that the program pays for itself.`,`Resident support is irrelevant to the financial calculation. A beloved program can lose money and an unpopular one can profit — this choice is irrelevant.`],generalLesson:`Conclusions built on numbers depend on those numbers being real. When an argument does arithmetic, the necessary assumption is usually that the key figures will actually hold.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`numbers-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion compares $3 million saved with $2 million spent. What must be true about those figures?`,`What if the $3 million in savings never actually materializes?`,`The correct choice requires the claimed savings to be real.`],labels:{topic:`composting program finances`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-130`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`The signature on this painting does not match the artist's known signature. Therefore, the painting is a forgery.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,choices:[{text:`The painting is highly valuable.`},{text:`Art experts have examined the painting in person.`},{text:`The artist always signed every painting in exactly the same way.`},{text:`A genuine painting by this artist would bear the known signature.`},{text:`Forgeries of this artist's work are common.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The inference is: mismatch → forgery. That works only if genuineness guarantees a matching signature. Negate (D): suppose genuine works by this artist sometimes bear non-matching signatures — the artist varied his signature, or assistants signed. Then a mismatch proves nothing, and the forgery conclusion collapses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument has the form: not-matching → forgery. For that inference to be valid, the contrapositive link must hold: genuine → matching. Choice (D) states exactly that link — a genuine painting would match — and negation destroys the argument: if genuine paintings could carry non-matching signatures, the observed mismatch is compatible with authenticity, and "therefore a forgery" no longer follows. Choice (C) is the tempting too-strong neighbor: "always signed every painting in exactly the same way" demands more than needed — the argument needs matching signatures on genuine works, not absolute invariability across every work. Choice (A) is irrelevant: value does not affect authenticity logic. Choice (B) is not required: the signature comparison could be done from photographs. Choice (E) is irrelevant: the frequency of forgeries does not show this painting is one.`,choiceExplanations:[`The painting's value is irrelevant to the authenticity inference. A worthless painting and a priceless one are judged by the same signature logic — irrelevant.`,`In-person expert examination is not required for the logic to work; the signature comparison is what matters, however it was performed. Not a necessary assumption.`,`This is the tempting too-strong trap: "always" and "exactly the same way" demand more than the argument needs. The inference requires that genuine works match, not that the artist never varied a flourish.`,`Correct. This is the contrapositive bridge the inference needs: genuine → matching signature. Negate it — genuine works might not match — and the mismatch stops proving forgery.`,`How common forgeries are does not show this particular painting is one. Base rates about the art market are irrelevant to the signature logic. Population statistics cannot settle an individual case; the signature either matches the artist's hand or it does not, regardless of market trends.`],generalLesson:`Mismatch arguments ("doesn't match, therefore fake") always assume that genuine items would match. Test them by asking: could a genuine item fail to match?`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`conditional-flaw`,`contrapositive`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Mismatch → forgery. What must be true about genuine paintings for that inference to work?`,`Could a genuine painting ever bear a non-matching signature?`,`The correct choice says a genuine painting would match the known signature.`],labels:{topic:`painting forgery`}},{id:`lr-d-131`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. The 20%-of-zero behavioral-response insight (developers may stop building) is a genuine second move. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`The new zoning law requires that 20 percent of units in new apartment buildings be affordable. Therefore, the law will increase the total supply of affordable housing.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Developers support the new zoning law.`},{text:`Affordable housing is currently in short supply.`},{text:`Developers will continue to build new apartment buildings under the law.`},{text:`The law will make all new housing affordable.`},{text:`Rents for existing apartments will fall as a result of the law.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The law creates affordable units only as a fraction of new construction. Negate (C): suppose developers respond by building nothing at all — the mandate makes projects unprofitable, so construction halts. Then 20 percent of zero is zero, no affordable units are added, and the conclusion fails. The argument needs building to continue.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion assumes the mandate will operate on a continuing stream of new buildings. But mandates change incentives: if the 20 percent requirement makes development unprofitable, developers may simply stop building, and the law would add no affordable housing whatsoever — it might even reduce total supply. Choice (C) rules out that perverse response. The negation test is decisive: zero new buildings means zero new affordable units regardless of the percentage, so the conclusion cannot survive developers exiting the market. Choice (A) is irrelevant: developers can oppose a law and still build under it. Choice (B) is irrelevant-true: a current shortage explains why the law was passed, not whether it will work. Choice (D) is too strong: the conclusion needs an increased supply, not universal affordability. Choice (E) is not required and goes beyond the claim — the argument is about new supply, not existing rents.`,choiceExplanations:[`Developer support is irrelevant to the law's effects. Developers might hate the law yet keep building profitably under it — opinions do not determine outcomes.`,`A current shortage is true background that motivates the law, but it does not bear on whether the law will increase supply. Irrelevant-true. The severity of a problem explains why legislators act, not whether their chosen mechanism will actually expand supply.`,`Correct. Twenty percent of nothing is nothing: if developers stop building, the mandate produces zero affordable units. The conclusion depends on construction continuing. This is the hidden existential assumption: percentage mandates presuppose a nonzero base, and negating it collapses the conclusion.`,`"All new housing affordable" is far stronger than the conclusion, which needs only an increased supply. Too-strong. The conclusion claims more affordable units, not universal affordability; the assumption must match the conclusion's modest scope.`,`Falling rents for existing apartments are not part of the claim, which concerns the supply of new affordable units. This goes beyond what the argument needs.`],generalLesson:`Mandates operate on behavior that can disappear. Whenever a rule takes a cut of some activity, ask the negation question: what if the activity stops entirely?`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`behavioral-response`,`perverse-incentive`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The law takes 20 percent of new buildings' units. What must keep happening for any affordable units to result?`,`What if developers find the mandate unprofitable and stop building altogether?`,`The correct choice requires developers to keep building.`],labels:{topic:`zoning and affordable housing`}},{id:`lr-d-132`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d4; the credited choice minimally instantiates the granted premise "cost deters visits," making this d2 material. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 4 to 2. 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`The county plans to abolish the $5 park entry fee. Since cost deters visits, abolishing the fee will increase park attendance.`,stem:`The argument depends on which of the following assumptions?`,choices:[{text:`The entry fee actually deters at least some potential visitors.`},{text:`Park attendance is currently low.`},{text:`The county can afford to lose the fee revenue.`},{text:`Everyone who wants to visit the park is deterred by the fee.`},{text:`Abolishing entry fees always increases park attendance.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The conclusion needs the fee to be doing causal work right now. Negate (A): suppose the $5 fee deters literally no one — every potential visitor either pays gladly or never considered coming for unrelated reasons. Then removing it changes no one's decision, attendance cannot rise, and the conclusion fails.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The premise "cost deters visits" is a general truth; the conclusion needs it to be true of this fee, for these visitors, now. Choice (A) supplies that instantiation in its minimal form: at least some potential visitors are actually deterred. The negation test is clean — if the fee deters no one, abolishing it alters no decisions and attendance stays flat, falsifying the prediction. Note the calibration: "at least some" is all the conclusion needs, since even a few newly visiting people constitute an increase. Choice (B) is not required: attendance could already be high and still rise. Choice (C) answers a different question about fiscal prudence, not attendance effects. Choice (D) is too strong: universal deterrence is unnecessary. Choice (E) is too strong: the argument needs this fee removal to work, not every fee removal everywhere.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The general truth that "cost deters" must actually apply here: some potential visitors must be held back by this specific fee. Negate that — the fee deters no one — and abolishing it cannot raise attendance.`,`Current attendance levels are irrelevant to whether removing the fee will increase them. Attendance could be high or low; the conclusion only needs it to rise.`,`Whether the county can afford the revenue loss is a budget question, not an attendance question. This choice answers a different question. Fiscal capacity concerns the county's books, while the conclusion concerns visitor behavior; affordability cannot drive attendance.`,`"Everyone" is far stronger than needed. The conclusion requires only that some visitors are deterred — enough to produce an increase — not universal deterrence. Too-strong.`,`"Always" universalizes beyond the argument's needs. This fee removal might work even if fee abolition sometimes fails elsewhere — too-strong. One case can succeed where the general rule has exceptions; the argument needs only this county's outcome, not a universal law.`],generalLesson:`General premises ("cost deters") must be instantiated to do work in an argument. The necessary assumption is usually the minimal application of the general claim to the specific case.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`instantiation`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`"Cost deters visits" is general. What must be true about this specific $5 fee?`,`What if the fee deters absolutely no one?`,`The correct choice says the fee actually deters at least some potential visitors.`],labels:{topic:`park entry fee`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-133`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. The "not fully explained by" reverse-causation calibration is a genuine d4 move - the argument can survive some reverse causation but not total. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d4.`}],stimulus:`Towns with public libraries have higher literacy rates than towns without them. Therefore, building libraries raises literacy.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,choices:[{text:`Public libraries are popular with town residents of all ages and backgrounds.`},{text:`Literacy is a valuable skill for residents to have in the modern economy.`},{text:`Towns without libraries never build them, no matter how much funding is available.`},{text:`Every town with a public library has high literacy rates without exception.`},{text:`The correlation is not fully explained by already-literate towns choosing to build libraries.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument reads the correlation as libraries causing literacy, but causation could run the other way: literate, book-loving towns may be precisely the ones that vote to fund libraries. Negate (E): if reverse causation explains the entire correlation, libraries cause nothing, and the conclusion that building libraries raises literacy is false.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Correlations are causally ambiguous, and this one has a particularly plausible reverse story: communities that already value reading build libraries; libraries do not create the readers. Choice (E) excludes the extreme version of that threat — that reverse causation explains everything. The "not entirely" calibration is what makes it necessary rather than too strong: the argument can survive some reverse causation (libraries might both attract literate towns and further boost literacy), but it cannot survive the correlation being wholly an artifact of selection. Negation is fatal: if literate towns building libraries explains 100 percent of the gap, then building a library in a non-literate town would do nothing, and the conclusion fails. Choice (A) is irrelevant: popularity does not establish causation. Choice (B) is irrelevant: literacy's value does not show libraries produce it. Choice (C) is irrelevant and implausible. Choice (D) is too strong: the conclusion needs a general tendency, not exceptionless universality.`,choiceExplanations:[`Library popularity is irrelevant to the causal direction question. Beloved libraries might still be effects rather than causes of literacy — irrelevant. Popularity is compatible with either causal direction: literate towns may build libraries they love, or libraries may create literate towns.`,`That literacy is valuable explains why anyone cares, not whether libraries cause it. This choice is irrelevant to the causal inference. The value of an outcome never identifies its cause; important effects can have mundane or multiple origins.`,`Whether library-less towns ever build them has no bearing on what explains the current correlation. Irrelevant. Future construction plans concern other towns' choices, while the question is what explains this town's observed literacy correlation.`,`"Every town" demands exceptionless universality, far more than the argument needs. A general tendency would support the conclusion even with exceptions — too-strong. Causal claims in social science tolerate exceptions; the argument needs a tendency, not a law without counterexamples.`,`Correct. The reverse-causation story — literate towns build libraries — is the lethal alternative. The argument needs it not to explain everything; negate that, and libraries cause nothing.`],generalLesson:`Correlational arguments always assume the causal arrow points the claimed way. The necessary assumption excludes the reverse story — at least in its totalizing form.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`causal-flaw`,`reverse-causation`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Libraries and literacy go together. Besides "libraries cause literacy," what other causal story fits?`,`What if towns that already love reading are simply the ones that build libraries?`,`The correct choice rules out reverse causation explaining the whole correlation.`],labels:{topic:`libraries and literacy`}},{id:`lr-d-134`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d4; single temporal-gap move (prints prove contact, not timing) is d3 material. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 4 to 3. 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`The defendant's fingerprints were found on the office safe. Therefore, the defendant opened the safe on the night of the robbery.`,stem:`The argument requires the assumption that`,choices:[{text:`fingerprints are unique to each individual, with no two people sharing the same fingerprint patterns.`},{text:`the defendant's fingerprints were left on the safe during the robbery, not at some other time.`},{text:`no one else touched the safe on the night of the robbery, according to the building’s security logs.`},{text:`the defendant had no innocent reason to be in the office at the time the robbery occurred.`},{text:`the safe contained valuables worth stealing, including cash and negotiable securities.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Fingerprints prove touching, not when the touching happened. Negate (B): suppose the defendant, who worked in the office, touched the safe innocently a week earlier. Then the prints are fully explained without any robbery-night opening, and the conclusion that he opened it that night collapses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The evidence establishes contact; the conclusion adds timing (that night) and action (opened). The gap is temporal: prints persist, so they could predate the robbery. Choice (B) closes exactly that gap, and negation confirms necessity — innocent earlier contact explains the prints completely, leaving the robbery-night conclusion unsupported. Choice (A) is not required: the stimulus already identifies the prints as the defendant's, so uniqueness adds nothing to the timing inference. Choice (C) is too strong: others could have touched the safe without undermining the claim that the defendant opened it. Choice (D) is too strong and misdirected: the issue is when he touched the safe, not his general reasons for being in the office. Choice (E) is irrelevant: the safe's contents do not connect the prints to that night.`,choiceExplanations:[`Uniqueness would matter for identifying whose prints they are, but the stimulus already grants that they are the defendant's. It does nothing for the timing gap — when the prints were left — which is the actual hole.`,`Correct. Prints prove contact, not timing. Negate it — the prints came from an innocent earlier touching — and the robbery-night conclusion loses its support entirely.`,`"No one else" is stronger than needed: the defendant could have opened the safe even if others touched it too. Too-strong. Shared access does not exonerate; the fingerprint evidence can implicate the defendant alongside others who also handled the safe.`,`This is too strong and aimed at the wrong gap. The defendant might have had innocent reasons to be in the office yet still have left the prints on another occasion — the timing question remains.`,`The safe's contents are irrelevant to when the fingerprints were deposited. This choice does not touch the temporal gap. What was inside the safe concerns the crime's spoils, while the argument's gap is purely about timing of the prints.`],generalLesson:`Physical evidence often proves an act but not its timing. When a conclusion adds "when," the necessary assumption pins the evidence to that moment.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`temporal-gap`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The prints prove the defendant touched the safe. What does the conclusion add beyond touching?`,`Prints linger — could they have been left on a different day?`,`The correct choice pins the prints to the night of the robbery.`],labels:{topic:`fingerprints on safe`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-135`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-numbers`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d5; a single comparability insight on clean before/after numbers is d3 material (cf. gold-standard d5 lr-d-136, which layers a causal claim plus an elimination move). Downgraded editorialDifficulty 5 to 3. 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`Employee satisfaction rose from 60 percent to 75 percent after the new manager took over. The board concluded that the new manager improved morale.`,stem:`Which of the following is an assumption on which the board's reasoning depends?`,choices:[{text:`The new manager is personally popular with employees.`},{text:`Employee morale was low before the new manager arrived.`},{text:`The board strongly supports the new manager.`},{text:`The two surveys measured employee satisfaction in comparable ways.`},{text:`Every employee answered both satisfaction surveys.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A "rise" from 60 to 75 percent is meaningful only if both numbers measure the same thing. Negate (D): suppose the first survey asked about "overall job satisfaction" of all staff while the second asked about "satisfaction with your direct supervisor" of only full-timers. Then the two figures are incommensurable — no rise was ever measured — and the conclusion that morale improved evaporates.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Before-and-after comparisons smuggle in a methodological assumption: the before and after were measured alike. Choice (D) states it. The negation test reveals its necessity sharply: different questions, different scales, different respondent pools, or different administration methods would make "60 to 75" a comparison of apples to oranges. The board's conclusion that morale improved depends entirely on the numbers being comparable; without comparability, there is no measured change to explain. This is a demanding item because the threat is invisible — the stimulus presents two clean percentages, and the test-taker must notice that percentages from different instruments need not describe the same quantity. Choice (A) is not required: an unpopular manager could still improve morale-boosting conditions. Choice (B) is given in the premise (60 percent is the baseline). Choice (C) is irrelevant: board support does not affect measurement. Choice (E) is too strong: the surveys could be comparable with different but similarly drawn samples; universal identical respondents are unnecessary.`,choiceExplanations:[`Personal popularity is not required: a manager could improve working conditions — and hence morale — while remaining personally unpopular. Not a necessary assumption. Morale responds to conditions, not charisma; an unlikable manager who fixes schedules and pay can still lift spirits.`,`The 60 percent baseline is already stated in the premise, so this adds nothing. The argument does not depend on characterizing 60 percent as "low."`,`Board support is irrelevant to whether the survey comparison is valid. This choice substitutes politics for methodology. Directors can endorse a flawed survey or oppose a sound one; institutional backing is not a methodological credential.`,`Correct. The "rise" exists only if both surveys measured the same thing in the same way. Negate comparability — different questions or samples — and no improvement was ever measured.`,`"Every employee" and "both surveys" demand perfect panel continuity, far more than needed. Two well-drawn representative samples would suffice — too-strong. Comparisons need comparable samples, not identical respondents; requiring the same people twice confuses panels with cross-sections.`],generalLesson:`Before-and-after numbers assume before and after were measured alike. Always interrogate comparability: same questions, same scale, same population — otherwise the "change" may be a measurement artifact.`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`comparability`,`measurement`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Satisfaction "rose" from 60 to 75 percent. What must be true about those two numbers for a rise to have occurred?`,`What if the two surveys asked different questions or polled different groups?`,`The correct choice requires the surveys to have measured satisfaction comparably.`],labels:{topic:`employee satisfaction surveys`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-136`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:5,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: REVISE-fixed - rewritten v2 (causal/elimination Necessary Assumption); 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,inferentialDistance:5,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:4},stimulus:`After the school banned phones in classrooms, average test scores rose 10 percent. The principal concluded that the ban caused the improvement, arguing that the new mathematics curriculum introduced the same year cannot explain the rise because the largest score gains were in English and history, subjects the new curriculum did not cover.`,stem:`The principal's argument depends on assuming which of the following?`,choices:[{text:`The new mathematics curriculum had no positive effect on student learning in any subject.`},{text:`Students did not simply shift their phone use to hours outside the school day.`},{text:`Gains in mathematics instruction do not produce higher scores in English and history.`},{text:`The phone ban was the most consequential policy change the school adopted that year.`},{text:`Parents and teachers widely supported the phone ban before it took effect.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The principal excludes the math curriculum because the gains were in subjects it did not cover. That exclusion works only if math instruction cannot spill over into English and history scores. Negate (C) — math gains do lift other subjects — and the subject-pattern evidence no longer rules out the curriculum, so the argument for the ban collapses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument has two moving parts: a causal claim (the ban caused the rise) and an elimination move (the math curriculum did not, because the gains were concentrated in subjects it never touched). The elimination is the load-bearing inference, and it silently assumes that instruction in one subject cannot leak into performance in others. Choice (C) states that assumption. Deny it — suppose stronger math teaching builds study habits or academic confidence that raise English and history scores too — and the principal's reason for dismissing the curriculum evaporates; the observed pattern is then exactly what the curriculum would produce. Choice (A) is too strong: the argument needs only that the curriculum did not cause these particular gains, not that it helped no one in no subject. Choice (B) is plausible but unnecessary: students texting at home does not restore the classroom distraction the ban removed. Choice (D) overclaims: the argument requires the ban to be the cause, not the most consequential policy of the year. Choice (E) is irrelevant: popularity does not cause score gains.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too strong. The argument needs only that the math curriculum did not produce the English and history gains — it could still have improved math learning. Negate this choice (the curriculum helped in math) and the principal's reasoning survives untouched.`,`Unnecessary. The ban's mechanism is fewer classroom distractions; students using phones at home in the evening does not put the distraction back into the classroom. The argument never claims the ban reduced total phone use.`,`Correct. The curriculum is excluded on the grounds that the gains appeared in subjects it did not cover — an exclusion that holds only if math instruction cannot spill over into other subjects. Negate it and the exclusion fails, taking the causal conclusion with it.`,`Too strong and comparative. The principal must show the ban caused the rise, not that it outranked every other policy change. A second consequential policy could exist without threatening this inference, so the argument does not depend on this claim.`,`Irrelevant. Support for the ban measures opinion; the conclusion is about test-score causation. A popular ban can fail and an unpopular one can work — approval ratings do not move exam scores.`],generalLesson:`Elimination arguments assume the eliminated alternative cannot explain the evidence through an indirect path. When an argument rules out a rival cause by pointing to where the effect appeared, check the spillover assumption: could the rival have reached there indirectly?`,misconceptionTags:[`negation`,`too-strong`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principal rules out the math curriculum using the pattern of gains. What must be true about that pattern for the exclusion to work?`,`The gains were biggest in subjects the curriculum did not cover. Under what condition would that fail to exclude the curriculum?`,`If math instruction could lift English and history scores indirectly, the subject pattern would not rule the curriculum out.`],labels:{topic:`phone ban and test scores`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-137`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Some/all chaining trap (choice C) is an honest near-miss. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`At least one person attended yesterday's training session, and everyone who attended is a manager. Therefore, some employees attended the training session.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`The training session was mandatory for all managers.`},{text:`All managers are employees.`},{text:`Some employees are managers.`},{text:`Employees are required to attend training sessions.`},{text:`The training session was well attended.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Chain the claims: attendees are managers (premise), and all managers are employees (assumption), so all attendees are employees — which means some employees attended. The assumption supplies the missing link that connects "manager" to "employee," closing the gap completely.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The premise gives: attendee → manager. The conclusion needs: some attendee → employee. The missing link is manager → employee. Choice (B) supplies exactly that universal bridge: all managers are employees. With it, the logic is airtight: take any attendee — the premise makes her a manager, the assumption makes every manager an employee, so she is an employee who attended. Hence some employees attended. Choice (A) is irrelevant: mandatory attendance does not connect managers to employees. Choice (C) is the classic trap — "some employees are managers" sounds close, but it fails: the employees-who-are-managers might be entirely different people from the managers-who-attended. "Some" claims do not chain. Choice (D) is too strong and misdirected: a general attendance requirement does not show any employee actually attended. Choice (E) is too weak: "well attended" says nothing about whether attendees were employees.`,choiceExplanations:[`Mandatory attendance for managers does not show that any managers — or employees — actually attended, nor does it connect the two categories. Irrelevant to closing the logical gap.`,`Correct. "All managers are employees" bridges the premise (attendees are managers) to the conclusion (some attendees are employees). The universal claim chains; the gap is fully closed.`,`This is the dangerous trap: "some employees are managers" does not guarantee overlap with the attendees. The managers who attended might be none of the managers who are employees — "some" statements cannot be chained.`,`A general requirement does not establish that anyone attended, let alone that attendees were employees. Too strong in one direction, unhelpful in the other. Requirements describe obligations, not attendance; the argument needs actual attendees who are employees, which no rule can supply.`,`"Well attended" is vague and does not connect managers to employees. It leaves the logical gap — how to get from "manager" to "employee" — completely open. Too-weak.`],generalLesson:`Sufficient assumptions bridge the exact gap between premise and conclusion. "All" statements chain together; "some" statements do not — watch for the tempting some-that-fails-to-connect.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`some-vs-all`,`bridging`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premise connects attendees to managers. The conclusion needs employees. What is missing?`,`Would "some employees are managers" guarantee that the attendees are employees? Why not?`,`The correct choice is a universal claim: all managers are employees.`],labels:{topic:`training session attendance`}},{id:`lr-d-138`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Reversal trap (choice B) teaches the bridge-direction check cleanly. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`This car's engine is brand new. Therefore, the car is reliable.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`New engines are expensive to manufacture.`},{text:`Reliable cars have brand-new engines.`},{text:`This car has no other mechanical problems.`},{text:`Any car with a brand-new engine is reliable.`},{text:`This car's owner maintains it carefully.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The premise gives "brand-new engine"; the conclusion needs "reliable." Choice (D) supplies the universal rule — new engine → reliable — that turns the premise into a guarantee of the conclusion. Plug in this car, and reliability follows by direct application.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The gap is between "brand-new engine" and "reliable." A sufficient assumption must make the conclusion follow no matter what: it needs the form "anything with a brand-new engine is reliable." Choice (D) is exactly that. Apply it to this car: its engine is brand new (premise), so by the assumed rule, it is reliable (conclusion). Done. Choice (B) is the reversal trap: "reliable cars have new engines" runs the arrow backward (reliable → new engine) and cannot take us from a new engine to reliability. Choice (C) is tempting but insufficient: "no other problems" plus a new engine does not positively guarantee reliability — unknown issues could exist, and the absence of known problems is not a rule that yields the conclusion. Choice (A) is irrelevant: manufacturing cost does not bear on reliability. Choice (E) is too weak: careful maintenance helps but does not logically guarantee reliability.`,choiceExplanations:[`Manufacturing cost is irrelevant to the reliability inference. An expensive engine might be unreliable and a cheap one dependable — this choice does not bridge the gap.`,`This is the reversal trap: it says reliable → new engine, but the argument needs new engine → reliable. The arrow points the wrong way and cannot deliver the conclusion.`,`Tempting but insufficient: lacking known problems is not a guarantee of reliability. This choice shores up the premise without supplying the rule that would make the conclusion follow.`,`Correct. This universal rule — new engine → reliable — applied to the premise yields the conclusion directly. It is the exact bridge the argument needs.`,`Careful maintenance is helpful but does not logically guarantee reliability. Too weak to make the conclusion follow. Well-maintained machines can still fail unexpectedly; helpfulness is not logical necessity, which is what a sufficient assumption demands.`],generalLesson:`To close a logical gap, supply a universal rule running from the premise's terms to the conclusion's terms. Beware the reversal: the rule must point from what you know toward what you want to prove.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`,`conditional-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`You know the engine is new; you want to conclude the car is reliable. What general rule would connect them?`,`Check the direction: does "reliable cars have new engines" get you from a new engine to reliability?`,`The correct choice is the universal rule: any car with a new engine is reliable.`],labels:{topic:`car reliability`}},{id:`lr-d-139`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Passes-to-clean universal bridge with a standard reversal trap. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`Bella's Bistro passed its health inspection yesterday. Therefore, its kitchen is clean.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`Any restaurant that passes a health inspection has a clean kitchen.`},{text:`Bella's Bistro has never failed a health inspection.`},{text:`Restaurants with clean kitchens always pass health inspections.`},{text:`The health inspector who visited Bella's Bistro was thorough.`},{text:`Bella's Bistro is a popular restaurant.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Premise: passed inspection. Conclusion: clean kitchen. The sufficient assumption is the rule "passed → clean," which is exactly choice (A). Apply it to Bella's Bistro and the conclusion follows immediately.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the gap: "passed inspection" must be converted into "clean kitchen." Only a universal rule with the form "anything that passes has a clean kitchen" performs that conversion. Choice (A) is that rule, and applying it to the premise yields the conclusion by straightforward deduction. Choice (C) is the reversal trap: "clean kitchens always pass" gives clean → pass, which moves from conclusion to premise — the wrong direction — and cannot produce the conclusion from the premise. Choice (B) is too weak: a spotless inspection history does not logically guarantee anything about yesterday's kitchen. Choice (D) is too weak: a thorough inspector makes the inference more plausible but does not make the conclusion follow logically. Choice (E) is irrelevant: popularity has no logical connection to kitchen cleanliness.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. "Passes → clean kitchen" is the exact bridge needed: Bella's passed, so by the rule its kitchen is clean. The conclusion follows logically. Notice the direction: the rule moves from the inspection result to cleanliness, which is precisely the direction the argument travels.`,`A perfect inspection history is suggestive but not decisive — past passes do not logically guarantee a clean kitchen now. Too weak to close the gap.`,`This is the reversal trap: it gives clean → passes, but the argument needs passes → clean. The conditional points the wrong way. Knowing clean kitchens pass tells you nothing when the known fact is the passing itself; the arrow must start from what is given.`,`A thorough inspector strengthens the evidence but does not make the conclusion follow as a matter of logic. Too weak for a sufficient assumption. Stronger evidence raises confidence without closing the logical gap; sufficiency demands a guarantee, not merely better odds.`,`Popularity is irrelevant to the cleanliness inference. Crowds do not logically imply clean kitchens. Busy restaurants can be filthy and empty ones spotless; customer volume is simply not a premise about hygiene.`],generalLesson:`Sufficient assumptions are universal bridges from premise to conclusion. When a tempting choice reverses the bridge's direction, it cannot carry you where you need to go.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`reverses-conditional`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What general rule would turn "passed inspection" into "clean kitchen"?`,`Watch the direction: the rule must go from passing to cleanliness, not the reverse.`,`The correct choice states that any restaurant passing inspection has a clean kitchen.`],labels:{topic:`restaurant health inspection`}},{id:`lr-d-140`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL: logically sound with an honest reversal trap, but the proper-name classroom conditional ("Maria exercises every day") is the template anti-pattern flagged in the audit and item-writing standard section 1.3 - unsuitable for assessment. Held at adversarial-reviewed. 0 two-defensible findings.`}],stimulus:`Maria exercises every day. Therefore, Maria will live a long life.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`Daily exercise is good for one's health.`},{text:`People who live long lives exercise every day.`},{text:`Everyone who exercises daily lives a long life.`},{text:`Maria is currently in excellent health.`},{text:`Maria will continue exercising daily for the rest of her life.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The premise places Maria in the group "exercises daily"; the conclusion places her in "lives a long life." Only a universal rule connecting the two groups — everyone in the first is in the second — makes the conclusion follow. Choice (C) is that rule.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The logical gap is between the premise category (daily exercisers) and the conclusion category (long-lived people). A sufficient assumption must guarantee that everyone in the first category is in the second. Choice (C) does exactly that: Maria exercises daily, so by the rule she will live a long life. Choice (B) is the reversal trap: "long-lived people exercise daily" (long life → exercise) cannot move from Maria's exercising to her longevity. Choice (A) is too weak: "good for health" is vague and does not guarantee a long life. Choice (D) is too weak: current health does not logically entail longevity. Choice (E) is irrelevant to the gap: even lifelong exercising needs the bridge to longevity, which (E) does not supply.`,choiceExplanations:[`"Good for health" is vague and falls short of guaranteeing a long life. It makes the conclusion plausible, not logically necessary — too-weak. Vague health claims are compatible with early death from unrelated causes; only a precise longevity rule would close the gap.`,`This is the reversal trap: it says long life → daily exercise, but the argument needs daily exercise → long life. The conditional runs backward.`,`Correct. This universal bridge — daily exerciser → long life — applied to Maria yields the conclusion by direct deduction. The universal quantifier does the work: with every daily exerciser covered, Maria's membership in that group settles the conclusion.`,`Current excellent health does not logically guarantee a long life; health can change. Too weak to make the conclusion follow. Snapshots of present health say nothing about the future; a sufficient assumption must project forward, which this cannot do.`,`Continued exercising still needs the missing link to longevity. Without a rule connecting exercise to long life, this choice leaves the gap open. More of the same premise cannot substitute for the missing bridge; the gap is about the exercise-longevity connection itself.`],generalLesson:`Vague benefits ("good for health") never close logical gaps. A sufficient assumption must be a precise universal rule from the premise's category to the conclusion's.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`,`too-weak`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`reverses-conditional`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Maria is in the "exercises daily" group; the conclusion puts her in the "long life" group. What rule connects the groups?`,`Does "people who live long lives exercise daily" point the right way?`,`The correct choice is the universal: everyone who exercises daily lives a long life.`],labels:{topic:`exercise and longevity`}},{id:`lr-d-141`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. The "only"-translation supplies the converse with a real near-miss (choice C). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The novel "Harbor Light" won the National Book Award. Therefore, it must be well written.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`The National Book Award is a prestigious literary prize.`},{text:`Well-written novels win major literary awards.`},{text:`"Harbor Light" has sold hundreds of thousands of copies.`},{text:`The judges who selected "Harbor Light" are widely respected experts.`},{text:`Only well-written novels win the National Book Award.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`"Only well-written novels win" translates to: won the award → well written. "Harbor Light" won the award, so by that rule it must be well written. The "only" formulation is the precise bridge from winning to quality.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The gap is between winning an award and being well written. The sufficient assumption must license the move "won → well written." Choice (E) does so via "only": "only well-written novels win" means winning requires being well written (win → well-written). Apply to "Harbor Light": it won, therefore it is well written. Choice (B) is the reversal trap: "well-written novels win awards" (well-written → wins) points from quality to victory — useless when you know victory and want quality. Choice (D) is tempting but too weak: respected judges can still err or disagree; expertise does not logically guarantee the winner's quality. Choice (A) is irrelevant: prestige does not connect winning to being well written. Choice (C) is irrelevant: sales do not bear on literary quality.`,choiceExplanations:[`Prestige is irrelevant to the logical gap. A prestigious prize might still go to a poorly written book — prestige does not bridge winning to quality.`,`This is the reversal trap: it says well-written → wins awards, but the argument needs wins → well-written. Knowing the novel won cannot take you anywhere with a rule that starts from quality.`,`Sales figures are irrelevant to whether the novel is well written. Bestsellers can be badly written and masterpieces can flop — this choice does not bridge the gap.`,`Respected judges make the award more credible, but credibility is not logic: even experts can choose a flawed winner. Too weak to make the conclusion follow necessarily.`,`Correct. "Only well-written novels win" is win → well-written, the exact direction needed. "Harbor Light" won, so it must be well written. The word "only" pinpoints the direction: winning requires good writing, so the observed win guarantees the writing quality.`],generalLesson:`"Only A win" constructions are powerful bridges: they convert an achievement into a requirement. Always translate "only" carefully — it points from the achievement back to the requirement.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`,`only-translation`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`You know the novel won; you want "well written." What "only" statement would bridge them?`,`Translate "only well-written novels win" into an if-then. Which way does it point?`,`The correct choice uses "only" to make winning require good writing.`],labels:{topic:`book award`}},{id:`lr-d-142`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Only-significant-source elimination with calibrated too-weak neighbors (choices A, E). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The city has banned private cars from the downtown core. Therefore, downtown air quality will improve.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`Private cars produce significant air pollution.`},{text:`Private cars are downtown's only significant pollution source.`},{text:`Downtown residents strongly support the car ban.`},{text:`Downtown air quality is currently poor.`},{text:`The car ban will be strictly enforced by the city.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Removing cars improves air only if cars are what is dirtying it. Choice (B) rules out every other significant pollution source, so with cars gone, the air must get cleaner. The "only significant source" formulation eliminates the alternative causes that would otherwise keep the gap open.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The premise removes one pollution source; the conclusion claims overall improvement. The gap is all the other sources: if factories, buses, or heating systems dominate downtown pollution, banning cars changes little. Choice (B) closes the gap by making cars the only significant source — remove the only significant source, and air quality must improve. Choice (A) is the tempting too-weak trap: "cars pollute" is true but leaves other sources untouched, so improvement does not follow. Choice (E) is also tempting but insufficient: strict enforcement guarantees cars disappear, yet without (B), disappearing cars need not clean the air. Choice (C) is irrelevant: public support does not affect air chemistry. Choice (D) is irrelevant: current poor quality does not show the ban will improve it.`,choiceExplanations:[`That cars pollute is true but insufficient: if other sources dominate, removing cars barely dents pollution. This choice leaves the alternative-sources gap wide open — too-weak.`,`Correct. "Only significant source" eliminates every rival polluter. With cars banned and no other significant source remaining, improved air quality follows logically. Eliminating rival causes is the classic sufficient-assumption move: with the field cleared, the remaining explanation must be the operative one.`,`Resident support is irrelevant to the air-quality inference. Popular bans and unpopular bans affect the atmosphere identically. Atmospheric chemistry does not consult opinion polls; support levels leave the causal mechanism entirely untouched.`,`Current poor air quality does not show the ban will improve it — the air could stay poor for other reasons. Irrelevant to closing the gap.`,`Strict enforcement ensures cars actually vanish, but it does not show their vanishing will clean the air. Without ruling out other sources, this choice is insufficient — a tempting half-measure.`],generalLesson:`Removing one cause guarantees improvement only if it was the only significant cause. "Only" assumptions are the gap-closers in elimination arguments.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`too-weak`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Cars are banned; air should improve. What could keep the air dirty even with no cars?`,`How would you rule out every other pollution source at once?`,`The correct choice makes cars the only significant source of the pollution.`],labels:{topic:`downtown car ban`}},{id:`lr-d-143`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. "Only if" direction-tracking with an expertly laid reversal trap (choice B). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`Acme Corporation's profits rose sharply this year. Therefore, Acme's CEO is effective.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`Acme's CEO made several important decisions this year.`},{text:`Effective CEOs raise their companies' profits.`},{text:`Acme's profits had been falling for several years before this year.`},{text:`A company's profits rise sharply only if its CEO is effective.`},{text:`Acme's competitors did not see their profits rise this year.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The premise gives "profits rose"; the conclusion needs "CEO is effective." Choice (D) supplies "profits rise → CEO effective" via "only if," so Acme's rising profits logically require an effective CEO. The gap is closed by a rule pointing from the observed result back to the claimed quality.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This item tests whether you track conditional direction under pressure. The needed bridge is profits-rose → CEO-effective. Choice (D) provides it: "profits rise only if the CEO is effective" means rising profits require effectiveness. Given the premise, the conclusion follows. Choice (B) is the expertly laid reversal trap: "effective CEOs raise profits" (effective → profits) is the converse — it tells you what effective CEOs do, not what rising profits prove. From "profits rose," (B) licenses nothing about the CEO. Choice (A) is too weak: important decisions do not guarantee effectiveness. Choice (C) is irrelevant: the profit history does not connect profits to CEO quality. Choice (E) is too weak: competitors' stagnation does not show the CEO caused Acme's rise, let alone that she is effective.`,choiceExplanations:[`Important decisions do not guarantee an effective CEO — decisions can be important and wrong. Too weak to make the conclusion follow. Importance describes stakes, not quality; high-stakes errors are still errors, so importance cannot bridge to effectiveness.`,`This is the reversal trap: it says effective → profits rise, but the argument needs profits rose → effective. The conditional points the wrong way and cannot deliver the conclusion.`,`The profit history is irrelevant to the logical gap between rising profits and CEO effectiveness. It adds background without bridging. Background facts decorate the argument without connecting its terms; the gap needs a rule about what profits reveal, not more history.`,`Correct. "Only if" makes effectiveness a requirement for rising profits: Acme's profits rose, so its CEO must be effective. The bridge points the right way.`,`Competitors' flat profits do not show Acme's CEO caused its rise or is effective. Too weak — it rules out one alternative without building the needed bridge.`],generalLesson:`"X only if Y" means X → Y: the result requires the quality. Do not confuse it with "if Y then X" — the direction of the bridge is everything.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`,`only-if`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`You know profits rose; you want "the CEO is effective." Which direction must the bridge point?`,`Compare "effective CEOs raise profits" with "profits rise only if the CEO is effective." Which one starts from rising profits?`,`The correct choice uses "only if" to make effectiveness required for rising profits.`],labels:{topic:`CEO effectiveness`}},{id:`lr-d-144`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Uniqueness-to-ranking universal rule with a preference-vs-behavior too-weak trap. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`The museum offers free admission on Sundays, the only day it does so. Therefore, Sunday attendance exceeds attendance on any other day.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`On any free-admission day, attendance is higher than on any day the museum charges admission.`},{text:`The museum is closed on Mondays, when attendance would be low anyway.`},{text:`Visitors say they prefer free admission to paid admission when surveyed.`},{text:`Sunday attendance at the museum has been rising steadily for the past two years.`},{text:`The museum advertises its free Sundays heavily in local newspapers.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The premise establishes Sunday as the unique free day; the conclusion needs it to be the highest-attendance day. Choice (A) supplies the universal rule — free day → beats every paid day — and since Sunday is the only free day, it beats every other day. The conclusion follows by direct application.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument's structure is: Sunday is the only free day (premise) → Sunday outdraws all other days (conclusion). The missing rule must convert "free day" into "highest attendance." Choice (A) is precisely that rule, stated universally: any free day beats any paid day. Instantiated: Sunday (free) beats Monday through Saturday (paid). Choice (C) is the tempting too-weak trap: visitor preference for free admission is plausible but does not guarantee higher attendance — preferences do not always drive behavior, and competing factors (work schedules, exhibits) could dominate. Choice (E) is too weak: advertising helps but does not logically guarantee the attendance ranking. Choice (B) is irrelevant: Monday closure does not compare Sunday with the open days. Choice (D) is irrelevant: a rising trend does not establish the top rank.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This universal rule converts "only free day" into "highest attendance day": Sunday, being the sole free day, outdraws every paid day by the rule. The conclusion follows logically.`,`Monday's closure is irrelevant to whether Sunday outdraws the days the museum is actually open. It does not bridge free admission to attendance. A closed day is not a competitor for visitors; the comparison the argument needs is among days the museum actually operates.`,`Preference is not behavior: visitors might prefer free days yet still attend paid days more often for scheduling reasons. Too weak to guarantee the conclusion.`,`A rising trend does not establish that Sunday is the highest day — attendance could be rising yet still trail Saturdays. Irrelevant to the ranking claim.`,`Advertising makes high attendance likelier but does not logically guarantee Sunday beats every other day. Too weak for a sufficient assumption. Likelier is not certain; a sufficient assumption must convert the prediction into a deduction, which mere likelihood cannot do.`],generalLesson:`Uniqueness premises ("the only day") need universal rules to yield ranking conclusions. The sufficient assumption converts the unique feature into the claimed superlative.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`too-weak`,`uniqueness`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Sunday is the only free day. What general rule would make the only free day the busiest day?`,`Would "visitors prefer free admission" guarantee Sunday wins? What else affects attendance?`,`The correct choice is a universal rule: any free day outdraws any paid day.`],labels:{topic:`museum free Sundays`}},{id:`lr-d-145`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Sophisticated premise-vs-bridge trap (choice A shores up the granted premise rather than bridging). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`The defendant was across town at the time of the robbery. Therefore, the defendant is innocent of the robbery.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`The defendant's alibi witness is telling the truth.`},{text:`The defendant has no prior criminal record.`},{text:`No one who was across town at the time could have committed the robbery.`},{text:`The robbery was committed by a single person acting alone.`},{text:`The defendant is a law-abiding citizen.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Being across town proves innocence only if the robbery required being at the scene. Choice (C) supplies exactly that: across town → could not have committed it. With the defendant across town, innocence follows by direct application.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The gap is between location (across town) and innocence. Location alone does not preclude guilt — the defendant could have hired an accomplice or committed the robbery remotely. Choice (C) closes the gap with a universal impossibility claim: no one across town could have done it. Given the premise that the defendant was across town, innocence follows necessarily. Choice (A) is the tempting trap: a truthful witness would shore up the premise (he really was across town), but the premise is already granted — the argument's hole is the inference from location to innocence, which (A) does not touch. Choice (B) is irrelevant: a clean record does not logically entail innocence of this charge. Choice (D) is irrelevant: a lone robber could still be the defendant if presence were not required. Choice (E) is irrelevant: character does not logically guarantee innocence.`,choiceExplanations:[`A truthful witness would support the premise, but the premise is already given. The gap is the inference from "across town" to "innocent," which this choice does not bridge — it defends the wrong link.`,`A clean criminal record is irrelevant to the logic: first-time offenders exist, and the conclusion must follow from the premises, not from character. Irrelevant. Character evidence cannot substitute for logical entailment; the gap is about what across-town presence proves, not about who the suspect is.`,`Correct. This universal rule — across town → could not have committed it — applied to the defendant yields innocence directly. It is the exact bridge the argument needs.`,`That the robber acted alone does not show the defendant was not that lone robber. Without a presence requirement, this choice leaves the gap open — irrelevant.`,`Being law-abiding is a character claim, not a logical bridge. It does not make innocence follow from being across town — irrelevant. Even a saint's location needs a logical rule to become an alibi; character alone never converts presence elsewhere into proof.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish shoring up a premise from bridging to the conclusion. A sufficient assumption must close the inference gap — supporting an already-granted premise is never enough.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`premise-vs-bridge`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premise (across town) is granted. What is the actual gap between that premise and innocence?`,`Would a truthful alibi witness close that gap, or does it just repeat the premise?`,`The correct choice rules out committing the robbery from across town.`],labels:{topic:`alibi and innocence`}},{id:`lr-d-146`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Post-hoc closed by the causal converse with a proper-dosage too-weak trap. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`After taking the new medicine, Elena recovered from her illness. Therefore, the medicine cured her.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`The new medicine is widely prescribed by doctors.`},{text:`Elena firmly believes that the medicine cured her.`},{text:`The new medicine has no serious side effects.`},{text:`Elena took the medicine exactly as directed.`},{text:`Elena's illness resolves only when this medicine cures it.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Recovery after medicine suggests causation, but illnesses often resolve on their own. Choice (E) supplies the causal converse directly: illness resolves → the medicine cured it. Since Elena recovered, the medicine must have done the curing. The alternative explanation — she recovered on her own — is ruled out.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument's vulnerability is the oldest in medicine: post hoc — after it, therefore because of it. Recovery following treatment is compatible with the body healing itself. Choice (E) closes the gap with a converse conditional: "resolves only when this medicine cures it" is logically equivalent to "Elena recovered → the medicine cured her." The logic: Elena recovered (premise); any resolution requires the medicine to have cured it (assumption); therefore the medicine cured her. The conclusion follows deductively — no rival explanation survives. Choice (D) is the tempting too-weak trap: proper dosage makes the medicine a better candidate but does not rule out spontaneous recovery. Choice (A) is irrelevant: widespread prescription does not show this cure. Choice (B) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: Elena's belief is not evidence. Choice (C) is irrelevant: side-effect profiles do not bear on whether the medicine caused this recovery.`,choiceExplanations:[`Widespread prescription is irrelevant to the causal logic. Popular medicines can fail in individual cases — this choice does not rule out spontaneous recovery. Prescription frequency reflects physician habits and marketing, not causal efficacy in Elena's particular case.`,`Elena's belief is not evidence of causation. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it substitutes the patient's conviction for a logical bridge. Patients routinely credit the wrong remedy or misjudge their own recoveries; conviction is psychology, not a causal rule.`,`Side effects are irrelevant to whether the medicine caused the recovery. A safe medicine might still be ineffective — this choice does not close the gap.`,`Proper dosage is tempting but insufficient: even perfectly dosed medicine might get credit for a recovery the body would have managed alone. It does not eliminate the spontaneous-recovery alternative — too-weak.`,`Correct. "Never resolves without this medicine" eliminates the rival explanation of natural recovery. Elena recovered while taking it, so the medicine must have caused the cure.`],generalLesson:`Post hoc causal claims are closed by supplying the causal converse: "resolves only when X cures it" turns temporal sequence into logical proof.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`post-hoc`,`alternative-cause`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Elena took the medicine, then recovered. What is the main alternative explanation?`,`How could you rule out the possibility that she would have recovered anyway?`,`The correct choice says the illness resolves only when the medicine cures it.`],labels:{topic:`medicine and recovery`}},{id:`lr-d-147`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Affirming-the-consequent repaired by the "only" converse, with a restates-premise trap. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d4.`}],stimulus:`Every student who studied for the exam passed it. Maria passed the exam. Therefore, Maria studied for it.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`Maria is a diligent student who takes her exams seriously.`},{text:`Only students who studied for the exam passed it.`},{text:`Every student who studied for the exam passed it.`},{text:`Most students who passed the exam studied for it.`},{text:`Maria wanted to pass the exam.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The premise gives studied → passed; the argument needs passed → studied — the converse. Choice (B) supplies exactly that converse via "only": passing required studying. Maria passed, so she must have studied. This repairs the classic affirming-the-consequent error.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument's form is: studied → passed (premise); Maria passed; therefore Maria studied. That is affirming the consequent — invalid as stated, since non-studiers might also pass. The sufficient assumption must supply the missing converse: passed → studied. Choice (B) does so with "only": "only students who studied passed" means passing required studying. Apply to Maria: she passed, so she studied. The conclusion now follows. Choice (C) is the restates-premise trap: it merely repeats the given premise and adds nothing. Choice (D) is the tempting too-weak trap: "most" passers studied does not guarantee Maria did — "most" never closes a logical gap. Choice (A) is too weak: diligence does not entail studying for this exam. Choice (E) is irrelevant: wanting to pass does not show studying occurred.`,choiceExplanations:[`Diligence is a character trait, not a logical bridge. A diligent student might still have skipped studying for this particular exam — too weak to make the conclusion follow.`,`Correct. "Only students who studied passed" is passed → studied, the converse the argument needs. Maria passed, so she must have studied — the affirming-the-consequent gap is repaired.`,`This choice merely restates the premise already given ("every student who studied passed"). Restating evidence adds no new bridge — restates-premise. An assumption must add what the premises lack; repeating a given simply moves in a circle without closing the gap.`,`"Most" is tempting but fatal: even if most passers studied, Maria might be among the minority who did not. "Most" can never make a conclusion follow logically — too-weak.`,`Wanting to pass does not show Maria studied. Motivation is irrelevant to the logical gap between passing and studying. Desire and action are different things; students want to pass exams they never study for, so motivation bridges nothing.`],generalLesson:`Affirming the consequent (A → B; B; therefore A) is repaired only by the converse (B → A). "Only" statements supply converses: "only studiers passed" means passing required studying.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`affirming-consequent`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premise says studied → passed. The conclusion needs passed → studied. What is that move called, and what would fix it?`,`Would "most passers studied" guarantee Maria studied?`,`The correct choice uses "only" to supply the converse: passing required studying.`],labels:{topic:`exam studying`}},{id:`lr-d-148`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Universal decision rule closes the fact-to-action gap; "wants to" and "most" calibrated as too-weak. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d4.`}],stimulus:`Renewable energy is now cheaper than coal. Therefore, the city will switch its power supply to renewables.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`Coal prices will continue to rise in the coming years.`},{text:`The city wants to reduce its energy costs.`},{text:`Renewable energy is cleaner than coal.`},{text:`The city always switches to the cheapest available energy source.`},{text:`Most cities switch to cheaper energy sources when they become available.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The premise establishes "renewables are cheapest"; the conclusion predicts the city's action. Choice (D) supplies the behavioral rule — the city always chooses the cheapest source — which, combined with the premise, deductively yields the switch. Nothing weaker closes the gap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The gap here is between a fact about prices and a prediction about institutional behavior. Facts do not imply actions without a decision rule. Choice (D) is that rule, stated universally: cheapest → city switches. Given renewables are cheapest, the city switches — the conclusion follows. Choice (B) is the tempting too-weak trap: wanting to save money is not a guarantee of action — budgets, contracts, politics, and inertia intervene. Choice (E) is the calibrated too-weak neighbor: "most cities" does not guarantee this city. Choice (A) is irrelevant: future coal prices do not bridge current cheapness to the city's decision. Choice (C) is irrelevant: cleanliness is a different reason from cheapness and does not connect the price premise to the action conclusion.`,choiceExplanations:[`Future coal prices are irrelevant to the logical gap. Even rising coal prices would not, by themselves, make the city's switch follow — the decision rule is still missing.`,`Tempting but too weak: wanting to cut costs does not guarantee acting on it. Desires are not decision rules, so the conclusion does not follow logically.`,`Cleanliness is a different consideration from cheapness. It does not connect the price premise to the predicted switch — irrelevant. The argument's only premise is about cost; importing environmental virtue changes the subject rather than bridging the stated gap.`,`Correct. This universal decision rule — cheapest → the city switches — applied to the premise (renewables are cheapest) deductively yields the conclusion. Universal rules about the agent's behavior are exactly what prediction arguments need: they convert favorable facts into guaranteed actions.`,`"Most cities" is the calibrated trap: it sounds supportive but cannot make this city's action follow. "Most" never closes a logical gap — too-weak. Statistical generalizations leave open that this city is the exception; logic requires a rule covering the case at hand.`],generalLesson:`Predictions about agents' actions need decision rules, not just favorable facts. "Always" rules close gaps; "wants to" and "most" never do.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`too-weak`,`decision-rule`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Cheapness is a fact; switching is an action. What would connect the city's actions to cheapness?`,`Would the city's merely wanting to save money guarantee it switches?`,`The correct choice is a universal decision rule: the city always switches to the cheapest source.`],labels:{topic:`renewable energy switch`}},{id:`lr-d-149`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Epistemic bridge (verdict -> what the evidence proved) with a sophisticated wrong-target trap (choice C). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d4.`}],stimulus:`The jury found the defendant liable. Therefore, the evidence must have proved negligence.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would allow the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,choices:[{text:`Juries find defendants liable only when the evidence proves negligence.`},{text:`The jury deliberated carefully before reaching its verdict.`},{text:`The defendant was in fact negligent.`},{text:`The jury system is generally reliable.`},{text:`The trial lasted for three full weeks.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The premise gives the verdict (liable); the conclusion makes a claim about the evidence (it proved negligence). Choice (A) bridges verdict to evidence with "only when": liability requires proved negligence. The jury found liability, so the evidence must have proved it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The subtlety here is that the conclusion is epistemic — about what the evidence proved — not just about what happened. The bridge must therefore run from the verdict to the evidence's strength. Choice (A) does exactly that: "liable only when evidence proves negligence" (liable → evidence-proved-negligence). Given the liability finding, the evidence-proved-negligence conclusion follows. Choice (C) is the sophisticated trap: the defendant's actual negligence does not show the evidence proved it — the conclusion is about proof, not fact, and (C) bridges to the wrong target. Choice (B) is too weak: careful deliberation does not guarantee the evidence met the standard. Choice (D) is too weak: general reliability does not make this conclusion follow logically. Choice (E) is irrelevant: trial length says nothing about what the evidence proved.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. "Only when" makes proved negligence a requirement for a liability verdict. The jury found liability, so the evidence must have proved negligence — the exact epistemic bridge needed.`,`Careful deliberation is admirable but does not logically guarantee the evidence proved negligence. Too weak to make the conclusion follow. Diligent juries can still convict on thin evidence; care describes the process, while the conclusion concerns the evidence's strength.`,`This is the sophisticated trap: actual negligence is not what the conclusion claims — the conclusion is about what the evidence proved. This choice bridges to the wrong target.`,`General reliability is too vague to close the gap: a generally reliable system can still err here, and "generally" never yields logical necessity — too-weak.`,`Trial length is irrelevant to what the evidence proved. A three-week trial might feature weak evidence — this choice does not bridge the gap. Duration measures procedure, not proof; long trials can rest on scanty evidence and short ones on overwhelming proof.`],generalLesson:`Match the bridge to the conclusion's exact claim. When the conclusion is about what evidence proved (not what happened), the assumption must connect the verdict to evidential strength.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`epistemic-gap`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion is about what the evidence proved — not just about what happened. What bridge reaches that target?`,`Would the defendant's actual negligence show the evidence proved it?`,`The correct choice ties the verdict to evidential proof with "only when."`],labels:{topic:`jury verdict and negligence`}},{id:`lr-d-150`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-sufficient-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty inflated at d5; a single formal move (negative-universal contrapositive bridge) with an excellent reversal trap is d4 material - one move, not the layered two-move structure of the d5 ceiling (lr-d-136). Downgraded editorialDifficulty 5 to 4. 0 two-defensible findings after fix.`}],stimulus:`The downtown revitalization project was completed on time. Therefore, the project team was well managed.`,stem:`The conclusion follows logically if which of the following is assumed?`,choices:[{text:`The project stayed within its original budget.`},{text:`Well-managed teams complete their projects on time.`},{text:`No poorly managed team ever completes a project on time.`},{text:`The project team worked long hours to meet the deadline.`},{text:`The project encountered no unexpected delays.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The premise gives "on time"; the conclusion needs "well managed." The bridge must run on-time → well-managed. Choice (C) provides it via the contrapositive form: no poorly managed team finishes on time means on-time → not poorly managed → well managed. Choice (B) is the converse and cannot bridge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is the hardest sufficient-assumption form because the correct bridge is stated negatively. The needed direction is: completed on time → well managed. Choice (C) delivers it: "no poorly managed team ever completes on time" is logically equivalent to "completes on time → not poorly managed," i.e., well managed. Given the project finished on time, the team must have been well managed — the conclusion follows. Choice (B) is the masterfully laid reversal trap: "well-managed teams complete on time" (well-managed → on time) is the converse of what is needed. It tells you what good management produces, not what on-time completion proves — from "on time," (B) licenses nothing. This is the single most tested confusion in sufficient-assumption questions. Choice (A) is irrelevant: budget is a different dimension from management. Choice (D) is too weak: long hours do not guarantee good management. Choice (E) is tempting but insufficient: a poorly managed team could still get lucky with no delays — luck is not management.`,choiceExplanations:[`Budget compliance is a different dimension from management quality. A well-managed project might overrun and a badly managed one might stay cheap — irrelevant to the gap.`,`This is the reversal trap and the heart of the item: well-managed → on time cannot take you from "on time" to "well managed." It describes what good management produces, not what punctuality proves. The arrow points the wrong way.`,`Correct. "No poorly managed team finishes on time" is equivalent to on-time → well managed. The project finished on time, so the team was well managed — the bridge points exactly the right way.`,`Long hours show effort, not management quality; exhausted teams can be badly managed. Too weak to make the conclusion follow logically. Effort and management are independent: frantic overtime often signals poor planning rather than competent leadership.`,`Tempting but insufficient: the absence of delays could be luck rather than management. A poorly managed team might simply face no surprises — this does not rule that out.`],generalLesson:`Negative universal claims ("no poorly managed team ever...") are contrapositive bridges in disguise. Translate them: they rule out the conclusion's denial, which is exactly what a sufficient assumption must do.`,misconceptionTags:[`sufficient-assumption`,`reversal`,`contrapositive`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`You know the project finished on time; you want "well managed." Which direction must the bridge point?`,`Does "well-managed teams finish on time" let you conclude anything from punctuality?`,`The correct choice rules out poor management for on-time projects — the contrapositive bridge.`],labels:{topic:`project management`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-d-151`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,abstraction:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Clean necessary-but-not-sufficient with a mirror-image sufficient trap (choice A). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`The grocery store will stay open until midnight. Therefore, it will attract evening shoppers.`,stem:`Which of the following must be assumed for the argument to succeed, but would not by itself establish the conclusion?`,choices:[{text:`All evening shoppers will shop at any store that stays open late.`},{text:`The store is the only one in the area open until midnight.`},{text:`Evening shoppers spend more money than daytime shoppers.`},{text:`The store will become more profitable by staying open late.`},{text:`There are potential customers who shop in the evening.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`This asks for a necessary-but-not-sufficient assumption. Choice (E) is necessary: if no evening shoppers exist, the store cannot attract them. But it is not sufficient: those shoppers might all prefer competitors. Choice (A), by contrast, is sufficient but not necessary — the trap this question type lays.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The stem has two requirements, and both must be checked. First, necessity: negate the candidate and see if the argument dies. Negate (E) — there are no evening shoppers at all — and "will attract evening shoppers" becomes impossible, so (E) is necessary. Second, insufficiency: does (E) alone (with the premise) guarantee the conclusion? No — evening shoppers might exist yet never visit this store. So (E) is necessary but not sufficient: the correct answer. Choice (A) is the mirror-image trap: "all evening shoppers will shop at any late-open store" plus the premise would guarantee the conclusion (sufficient), but the argument does not need anything that strong (not necessary). The stem asks for necessary-but-not-sufficient, so (A) fails. Choice (B) is neither: the store need not be the only late option. Choice (C) is irrelevant: spending levels do not affect whether shoppers come. Choice (D) is irrelevant: profitability is a different question from attraction.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is sufficient — with the premise, it would guarantee evening shoppers — but it is far stronger than the argument needs, so it is not necessary. The stem asks for necessary-but-not-sufficient, making this the mirror-image trap.`,`Being the only late-open store is neither required nor enough: the store could attract shoppers alongside competitors, or be alone yet unattractive. It fails both parts of the stem's test.`,`Spending levels are irrelevant to whether evening shoppers will come. This choice does not address the argument's gap at all. How much shoppers spend concerns revenue, while the conclusion concerns foot traffic; the two questions are independent.`,`Profitability is a different question from attracting shoppers. The conclusion is about foot traffic, not finances — irrelevant. A store can attract crowds while losing money, or profit without evening traffic; finances do not test the attraction claim.`,`Correct. Necessary: with no evening shoppers existing, attraction is impossible. Not sufficient: existing shoppers might still shop elsewhere. It passes both halves of the test.`],generalLesson:`Necessary-but-not-sufficient assumptions are the modest claims an argument cannot live without. Test both halves: negate for necessity, then check whether the claim alone would prove the conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The stem asks for two things: an assumption the argument needs, that still would not prove the conclusion. Check necessity first — what happens if no evening shoppers exist?`,`Now check sufficiency: if evening shoppers exist, does the conclusion automatically follow?`,`The correct choice passes the necessity test but fails the sufficiency test.`],labels:{topic:`store late hours`}},{id:`lr-d-152`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Clean sufficient-but-not-necessary with a too-weak improvement trap (choice B). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d1.`}],stimulus:`The team has practiced every day for a month. Therefore, the team will win the championship.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would establish the conclusion but is not required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`The team wants to win the championship.`},{text:`Daily practice improves a team's performance.`},{text:`Any team practicing daily for a month wins the championship.`},{text:`The team's opponents have not practiced at all.`},{text:`The championship game is scheduled for next week.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`This asks for sufficient-but-not-necessary. Choice (C) plus the premise deductively yields the win (sufficient), yet the argument hardly needs such an extreme rule — the team could win without it being true that every practicing team always wins (not necessary). Choice (B) is the too-weak trap: improvement does not guarantee victory.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Check both halves of the stem. Sufficiency: does (C) with the premise guarantee the conclusion? The team practiced daily for a month; (C) says any such team wins; therefore this team wins. Yes — sufficient. Necessity: does the argument require (C)? No — the team might win even if some other daily-practicing team once lost; the argument's reasoning does not depend on the universal rule being true. So (C) is sufficient but not necessary: correct. Choice (B) is the tempting too-weak trap: "practice improves performance" makes winning likelier but does not establish it — improvement is not victory. Choice (A) is too weak: wanting to win guarantees nothing. Choice (D) is neither sufficient (unpracticed opponents might still be talented) nor necessary. Choice (E) is irrelevant: scheduling does not affect the outcome.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wanting to win does not establish winning. This choice is too weak to satisfy even the sufficiency half of the stem. Every losing team wanted to win; desire is universal among competitors and therefore proves nothing about outcomes.`,`Tempting but too weak: improved performance is not victory. This choice makes the conclusion likelier without establishing it, so it fails the sufficiency requirement. A better team can still lose to a great one; improvement is relative, while the conclusion demands an absolute outcome.`,`Correct. Sufficient: the premise plus this rule deductively yields the win. Not necessary: the team could win even if the universal rule were false. It passes both halves.`,`Unpracticed opponents might still be more talented. This choice neither establishes the win nor is required by the reasoning. Talent can outweigh preparation; weak opponents on paper may still outperform a well-drilled team on the day.`,`The game's date is irrelevant to whether the team will win. This choice addresses neither half of the stem. Scheduling fixes the calendar, not the scoreboard; the date cannot establish victory or be required for it.`],generalLesson:`Sufficient-but-not-necessary assumptions are stronger than the argument needs. They guarantee the conclusion — which is why they work — but the argument could succeed without them.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`too-weak`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The stem wants a claim that would prove the conclusion but goes beyond what the argument needs. Which choice is strongest?`,`Does "practice improves performance" guarantee a championship?`,`The correct choice is the universal rule: any daily-practicing team wins.`],labels:{topic:`team practice`}},{id:`lr-d-153`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Rule-application necessity with a universal-compliance mirror trap (choice B). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The law requires a fishing license for fishing in state waters. Maria was fishing in state waters yesterday. Therefore, Maria holds a fishing license.`,stem:`Which of the following must be assumed for the argument to succeed, but would not by itself establish that Maria holds a license?`,choices:[{text:`Maria goes fishing every week.`},{text:`Everyone who fishes in state waters holds a fishing license.`},{text:`Maria applied for a fishing license last year.`},{text:`Fishing licenses are inexpensive and easy to obtain.`},{text:`The licensing requirement applies to the activity Maria was engaged in.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Necessary-but-not-sufficient is the target. Choice (A) is necessary: if the requirement somehow did not cover Maria's activity, the conclusion would not follow. But it is not sufficient: the requirement applying does not show Maria complied with it. Choice (B) is the sufficient-but-not-necessary mirror trap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the two-part test. Necessity for (A): negate it — suppose the licensing requirement did not apply to what Maria was doing (a special exemption, say). Then the premises give no reason to think she holds a license, and the conclusion is unsupported. So (A) is necessary. Sufficiency for (A): does the requirement's applying, plus the premises, prove she holds a license? No — she might be fishing illegally without one. So (A) is not sufficient. Both halves pass: correct. Choice (B) is the mirror trap: "everyone who fishes holds a license" with the premise would prove the conclusion (sufficient), but the argument does not need universal compliance (not necessary). Choice (C) is too weak: applying is not holding. Choice (D) is irrelevant: ease of obtaining says nothing about whether she has one. Choice (E) is irrelevant: frequency of fishing does not show licensure.`,choiceExplanations:[`Fishing frequency does not show licensure. A weekly angler might be unlicensed for years — irrelevant. Habit proves routine, not paperwork; many regular anglers fish for years without ever obtaining the required license.`,`This is the mirror-image trap: it would prove the conclusion (sufficient) but is far stronger than the argument needs (not necessary). The stem asks for necessary-but-not-sufficient.`,`Applying for a license is not holding one — applications can be denied or abandoned. Too weak to be necessary, and it would not establish the conclusion either.`,`Cost and convenience are irrelevant to whether Maria actually holds a license. This choice addresses neither half of the stem. Cheap licenses do not issue themselves; accessibility concerns the system, while the conclusion concerns Maria's actual compliance.`,`Correct. Necessary: if the requirement did not apply to her activity, the premises would be silent about her license. Not sufficient: a requirement applying does not prove she complied with it.`],generalLesson:`A rule applying to someone is necessary for rule-based conclusions but never sufficient for compliance conclusions. Separate "the rule covers her" from "she obeyed it."`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`For the licensing law to tell us anything about Maria, what must be true about her activity?`,`If the law applied to her, would that prove she has a license — or might she be unlicensed?`,`The correct choice is necessary (the law must cover her) but not sufficient (she might not comply).`],labels:{topic:`fishing license`}},{id:`lr-d-154`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Universal behavioral rule as sufficient-but-not-necessary; awareness as the mirror trap (choice A). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The new bridge is the fastest route across the river. Therefore, commuters will use it.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would guarantee the conclusion but is not required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Commuters know that the new bridge exists.`},{text:`The new bridge is safe to drive on.`},{text:`Some commuters prefer scenic routes to fast ones.`},{text:`All commuters always take the fastest available route.`},{text:`The new bridge will reduce overall traffic congestion.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Sufficient-but-not-necessary is the target. Choice (D) with the premise deductively yields universal bridge use (sufficient), but the conclusion — "commuters will use it" — needs only some commuters, not all commuters always optimizing (not necessary). Choice (A) is the necessary-but-not-sufficient mirror trap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two-part test for (D). Sufficiency: the bridge is fastest; (D) says all commuters always take the fastest route; therefore all commuters take the bridge — the conclusion ("commuters will use it") follows. Yes. Necessity: does the argument need (D)? The conclusion claims only that commuters (some, generally) will use the bridge — it does not need every commuter to be a perfect speed-optimizer. Many commuters might choose it for convenience while others do not. So (D) is stronger than required: sufficient but not necessary. Choice (A) is the mirror trap: commuter awareness is arguably necessary (unknown bridges get no traffic) but it would not guarantee use — necessary-but-not-sufficient, the opposite of what the stem asks. Choice (B) is too weak: safety does not guarantee choice. Choice (C) is irrelevant, even slightly contrary. Choice (E) is a different claim about congestion, not about whether commuters use the bridge.`,choiceExplanations:[`Awareness is arguably necessary — nobody uses an unknown bridge — but it would not guarantee use. That makes it necessary-but-not-sufficient: the mirror image of what the stem asks for.`,`Safety is nice but does not guarantee commuters will choose the bridge; they might still prefer familiar routes. Too weak to guarantee the conclusion. Commuters trade safety against habit, scenery, and convenience; a safe bridge can stand empty while a risky shortcut fills.`,`Scenic-route preferences are irrelevant to the bridge-use conclusion and, if anything, point against it. This choice addresses neither half of the stem. Preferences about scenery concern aesthetics, not the decision rule the argument needs; they test nothing about speed-driven choices.`,`Correct. Sufficient: fastest route plus universal speed-optimization yields the conclusion deductively. Not necessary: the conclusion needs only that commuters generally use it, not perfect optimization by all.`,`Reduced congestion is a different claim from commuters using the bridge. This choice answers a different question. Congestion could fall because commuters stay home or shift hours; the conclusion specifically predicts bridge usage, not traffic patterns.`],generalLesson:`Universal behavioral rules ("all commuters always...") are classic sufficient-but-not-necessary assumptions: they over-guarantee conclusions that need much less.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`too-strong`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The stem wants an assumption that would prove the conclusion but is stronger than needed. Which choice is the strongest behavioral rule?`,`Does the conclusion really need every commuter to always optimize for speed?`,`The correct choice is the universal rule about commuters always taking the fastest route.`],labels:{topic:`new bridge usage`}},{id:`lr-d-155`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Availability as the quiet necessary assumption behind "new solution" arguments. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d2.`}],stimulus:`The new vaccine is highly effective against the virus. Therefore, the outbreak will soon end.`,stem:`Which of the following is necessary for the argument to succeed but would not by itself establish that the outbreak will end?`,choices:[{text:`An effective vaccine always ends an outbreak soon after its introduction.`},{text:`The vaccine will actually be made available to the public.`},{text:`The new vaccine has no serious side effects.`},{text:`The outbreak is currently getting worse.`},{text:`Vaccines are the best tool for ending outbreaks.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Necessary-but-not-sufficient is the target. Choice (B) is necessary: an unavailable vaccine vaccinates no one, so the outbreak cannot end because of it. But availability alone does not establish the end — people must take it, distribution must work. Choice (A) is the sufficient-but-not-necessary mirror trap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two-part test for (B). Necessity: negate — the vaccine is never made available to the public. Then it cannot cause the outbreak to end, and the conclusion's mechanism vanishes. Necessary. Sufficiency: does availability plus the premise establish the outbreak will end? No — the vaccine might sit in warehouses, or too few people might accept it. Not sufficient. Both halves pass. Choice (A) is the mirror trap: it would guarantee the conclusion (sufficient) but the argument does not need "always ends outbreaks soon" — that universal claim is far stronger than required. Choice (C) is irrelevant: side effects do not bear on whether the outbreak ends. Choice (D) is irrelevant: the outbreak's current trajectory does not affect the logic. Choice (E) is too weak and vague: "best tool" does not establish the outbreak will end.`,choiceExplanations:[`This would guarantee the conclusion but is far stronger than the argument needs — the mirror-image trap. The stem asks for necessary-but-not-sufficient. Mirror-image traps exploit stem misreading: they reward test-takers who verify sufficiency but forget to check necessity.`,`Correct. Necessary: without availability, the vaccine cannot end anything. Not sufficient: availability alone does not ensure uptake or the outbreak's end. This is the textbook necessary-but-not-sufficient assumption: the argument dies without it, yet lives on unproven with it.`,`Side effects are irrelevant to whether the outbreak will end. This choice addresses a safety question the argument never raises. Safety and efficacy are separate evaluations; a vaccine can end an outbreak despite side effects or fail without any.`,`Whether the outbreak is worsening now is irrelevant to the logic connecting vaccine effectiveness to the outbreak's end. Current trends describe the present, while the conclusion predicts the future; trajectories can reverse without touching the argument's logic.`,`"Best tool" is vague and does not establish that the outbreak will end. Too weak to satisfy either half of the stem meaningfully. Superlatives without mechanisms are empty; being the best available tool says nothing about whether the job actually gets done.`],generalLesson:`Availability is the quiet necessary assumption behind every "new solution will fix it" argument. Necessary, because unavailable solutions solve nothing — but never sufficient, because availability is not use.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-assumption`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`An effective vaccine ends the outbreak only if it reaches people. What is the first thing that must happen?`,`If the vaccine never becomes available, can the conclusion survive? If it is available, does the conclusion automatically follow?`,`The correct choice is about the vaccine being made available.`],labels:{topic:`vaccine and outbreak`}},{id:`lr-d-156`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Fill-in-the-blank necessary->sufficient slide with a half-right extra-clause trap (choice D). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`Coach Rivera argues: "Only disciplined teams win championships. Our team is disciplined. Therefore, we will win the championship."`,stem:`The flaw in Rivera's reasoning is that it treats a necessary condition for winning the championship as if it were a`,choices:[{text:`sufficient condition for being disciplined`},{text:`necessary condition for being disciplined`},{text:`cause of losing championships`},{text:`sufficient condition for winning that is also necessary`},{text:`sufficient condition for winning the championship`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`"Only disciplined teams win" makes discipline necessary for winning (win → disciplined). Rivera then argues "we are disciplined, so we will win" — disciplined → win — treating the necessary condition as if it guaranteed the outcome, i.e., as sufficient.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate the first sentence: "only disciplined teams win" = win → disciplined. Discipline is necessary for winning, not sufficient for it — many disciplined teams lose. Rivera's inference goes: we are disciplined → we will win. That treats discipline as sufficient: as if being disciplined guarantees victory. The flaw is the necessary-to-sufficient slide, which is exactly what the stem asks you to complete: "treats a necessary condition ... as if it were a sufficient condition." Choice (A) scrambles the terms — the confusion is about winning, not about being disciplined. Choice (B) is incoherent: treating a necessary condition as necessary would be no flaw at all. Choice (C) changes the subject to causation of losing, which the argument never addresses. Choice (D) is the tempting half-right trap: it correctly says "sufficient condition for winning" but adds "that is also necessary," which misdescribes the error — the problem is precisely that sufficiency was never established.`,choiceExplanations:[`This scrambles the conditions: the argument's confusion concerns what discipline guarantees about winning, not what would suffice for being disciplined. The terms are attached to the wrong outcomes.`,`Treating a necessary condition as necessary would be correct reasoning, not a flaw. This choice describes no error at all. Flaw answers must name a genuine error; describing valid reasoning as the flaw gets the question exactly backwards.`,`The argument never discusses causes of losing; it discusses what discipline guarantees about winning. This choice changes the subject — scope-shift. Losing has its own causes — injuries, luck, stronger opponents — none of which bear on the discipline-to-winning inference.`,`Half-right and therefore dangerous: "sufficient condition for winning" is correct, but "that is also necessary" misdescribes the flaw. The error is assuming sufficiency that was never established, not affirming necessity.`,`Correct. "Only disciplined teams win" establishes necessity (win → disciplined); Rivera infers "disciplined → win," treating the necessary condition as sufficient. The stem's fill-in-the-blank format rewards translating both the premise and the inference into arrows, then comparing their directions.`],generalLesson:`"Only A win" makes A necessary. The classic slide is inferring "we are A, so we will win" — converting necessity into sufficiency. Name the direction of the slide precisely.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`,`conditional-flaw`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate "only disciplined teams win championships" into an if-then. Which way does the arrow point?`,`Rivera infers "we are disciplined, so we will win." Which way does that arrow point?`,`The correct choice completes the sentence: necessary ... sufficient.`],labels:{topic:`championship discipline`}},{id:`lr-d-157`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Graduation-necessity slide with a swapped-direction trap (choice A). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`To graduate, students must pass the comprehensive exam. Jamal passed the exam. Therefore, Jamal will graduate.`,stem:`Which of the following best describes the error in the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`It confuses a sufficient condition for graduation with a necessary one.`},{text:`It confuses the exam requirement with the graduation ceremony itself.`},{text:`It treats passing the exam, a necessary condition for graduation, as if it guaranteed graduation.`},{text:`It draws a conclusion about Jamal from a rule that applies only to students in general.`},{text:`It presumes without evidence that Jamal studied for the exam.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`"Must pass to graduate" makes passing necessary (graduate → passed). The argument infers "Jamal passed, so he will graduate" (passed → graduate) — treating the necessary condition as sufficient. Other requirements (credits, fees, conduct) might still block graduation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`"Students must pass to graduate" = graduate → passed: passing is necessary. The inference runs passed → graduate: passing is treated as sufficient. That is the necessary-sufficient confusion, stated precisely in choice (C). Graduation typically requires many things — completed credits, paid fees, good standing — so passing the exam alone guarantees nothing. Choice (A) is the swapped trap: it describes the reverse confusion (sufficient treated as necessary), which is not what happened. Choice (B) is nonsense: no one confuses a requirement with a ceremony. Choice (D) mislabels valid reasoning: applying a general rule to Jamal is perfectly legitimate; the error is the conditional direction, not the instantiation. Choice (E) invents an unsupported presumption about studying that the argument never makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`This describes the reverse error — treating something sufficient as merely necessary. The argument does the opposite: it treats the necessary exam requirement as sufficient for graduation. Swapped.`,`No such confusion occurs: the argument never mentions or conflates the ceremony. This choice is pure invention — unsupported. Unsupported choices invent content the stimulus never contained; the ceremony appears nowhere in the argument's reasoning.`,`Correct. "Must pass" establishes necessity; "passed, therefore will graduate" assumes sufficiency. The necessary condition is treated as if it guaranteed the outcome. Graduation bundles many requirements — credits, fees, standing — and the argument silently treats one of them as the whole package.`,`Applying a general rule to a particular student is valid reasoning, not an error. The flaw is the direction of the conditional, not the move from general to particular.`,`The argument never mentions studying, so it presumes nothing about it. This choice attributes an invented premise — unsupported. Passing an exam says nothing about how one prepared; the argument moves from the result alone, making study habits irrelevant.`],generalLesson:`Graduation-style arguments ("must do X to Y") always establish necessity. The error to watch for is the slide from "did X" to "will Y" — necessity dressed up as sufficiency.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "must pass to graduate" establish — that passing guarantees graduation, or that graduation requires passing?`,`The argument concludes Jamal will graduate from the fact that he passed. What would also need to be true for that to follow?`,`The correct choice names the slide: necessary treated as sufficient.`],labels:{topic:`graduation requirement`}},{id:`lr-d-158`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. "Only if" premise requiring the supplied converse; universal converse as sufficient-but-not-necessary. 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`The city will build the new park only if voters approve the bond measure. Voters approved the bond measure. Therefore, the city will build the park.`,stem:`Which of the following, if assumed, would guarantee the conclusion but is not required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Whenever voters approve a bond measure, the city carries out the project it funds.`},{text:`The city currently has enough money to build the park without issuing any bonds.`},{text:`Voters usually approve bond measures for parks when they appear on the ballot.`},{text:`The mayor personally supports building the park and has said so publicly.`},{text:`The park will be popular with residents once it is completed and opened.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Sufficient-but-not-necessary is the target. "Will build only if approved" gives build → approved; the argument needs approved → build. Choice (A) supplies exactly that converse as a universal rule, guaranteeing the conclusion — yet the city might build the park even if the generalization failed, so it is not required.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the conditionals. Premise: build → approved (the "only if"). Given: approved. Desired: build. The gap is the converse: approved → build. Choice (A) provides it universally: whenever voters approve a bond, the city executes the project. With approval granted, the park gets built — sufficient. But is (A) required? No: the city could build this park for case-specific reasons (a surplus, a donation) even if the general rule "whenever voters approve, the city builds" were false. The argument's reasoning does not depend on the universal generalization holding. So (A) is sufficient but not necessary. Choice (B) is neither: money alone does not guarantee building, and the argument does not require current funds. Choice (C) is irrelevant: usual voter behavior does not bear on this inference. Choice (D) is too weak and irrelevant: mayoral support does not guarantee construction. Choice (E) is irrelevant: popularity does not imply the park gets built.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This supplies the missing converse (approved → build) as a universal rule, guaranteeing the conclusion — but the city could build for other reasons, so the rule itself is not required.`,`Current funds neither guarantee the park gets built (priorities could shift) nor are required by the reasoning (funds could arrive later). It fails both halves.`,`How voters usually behave is irrelevant to whether this approval guarantees this park. This choice does not touch the conditional gap. General voting patterns cannot supply the missing converse; the gap is logical — approved to build — not sociological.`,`Mayoral support is too weak to guarantee construction and is not required by the argument's logic. Irrelevant. Mayors can support projects that never break ground; endorsement is politics, while the gap demands a logical guarantee.`,`Popularity does not imply construction. This choice is irrelevant to the approved → build gap. Popular projects stall for funding, permits, or politics; public enthusiasm is not a construction contract.`],generalLesson:`"Only if" gives you the conditional in one direction; the converse must be supplied separately. Universal converses ("whenever approved, the city builds") are the classic sufficient-but-not-necessary assumptions.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`converse`,`only-if`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-sufficient-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premise gives build → approved. The conclusion needs approved → build. What kind of statement would supply that?`,`Would the city need that general rule to be true in order to build this park?`,`The correct choice is the universal converse: whenever voters approve, the city builds.`],labels:{topic:`park bond measure`}},{id:`lr-d-159`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: sound. Early-stage necessity treated as efficacy guarantee, with a practical-precondition trap (choice A). 0 two-defensible findings. Validated d3.`}],stimulus:`Dr. Chen argues: "This medicine works only for patients in the early stages of the disease. Ana is in the early stages. Therefore, the medicine will work for Ana."`,stem:`Dr. Chen's reasoning is flawed because it`,choices:[{text:`assumes without evidence that Ana will take the medicine exactly as prescribed.`},{text:`confuses a sufficient condition for the medicine's working with a necessary one, reversing their logical roles.`},{text:`ignores the possibility that Ana's disease will progress to a later stage before treatment begins.`},{text:`mistakes a necessary condition for the medicine's working, being in the early stages, for a guarantee that it will work.`},{text:`presumes without evidence that the medicine has no serious side effects for most patients.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`"Works only for early-stage patients" makes early stage necessary (works → early stage). Chen infers "Ana is early-stage, so it will work" (early stage → works) — treating the necessary condition as sufficient. Early-stage status merely permits the medicine to work; it does not ensure it does.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate: "works only for early-stage patients" = works → early-stage. Early-stage status is necessary for efficacy. Chen's inference reverses it: Ana is early-stage → it will work for her. That treats the necessary condition as sufficient — the precise flaw named in (D). Being early-stage only means the medicine is not ruled out; the medicine might still fail for Ana for many reasons (dosage, biology, disease subtype). Choice (B) is the swapped trap: it names the reverse confusion. Choice (A) raises a genuine practical point — Ana must take the medicine — but it is secondary to the logical flaw, which concerns what early-stage status entails. Choice (C) is irrelevant: future progression does not affect whether the medicine works now. Choice (E) is irrelevant: side effects are a different question from efficacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Whether Ana takes the medicine is a practical precondition, but the logical flaw is deeper: even if she takes it, early-stage status does not guarantee efficacy. This choice misses the conditional error.`,`Swapped: this describes treating something sufficient as necessary. Chen does the reverse — treating the necessary early-stage condition as sufficient. The labels are exactly backward.`,`Future disease progression is irrelevant to the flaw, which concerns what follows from Ana's current early-stage status. This choice changes the subject. What might happen later cannot fix what the inference claims now; the flaw is in the present-tense conditional slide.`,`Correct. "Only for early-stage patients" establishes necessity; Chen's "early-stage, therefore will work" assumes sufficiency. The necessary condition is treated as a guarantee. Eligibility for treatment is not efficacy of treatment; being a candidate merely opens the door the medicine must still walk through.`,`Side effects concern safety, not efficacy. The flaw is the conditional slide from necessity to sufficiency, which this choice does not address. A flaw answer must name the argument's actual error; importing an unrelated safety concern misses the conditional confusion entirely.`],generalLesson:`"Works only for X" tells you when something can work, never that it will. Necessary conditions open the door; they do not push you through it.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`,`only-translation`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-flaw`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate "works only for early-stage patients." Does it say the medicine always works for such patients?`,`Chen concludes it will work for Ana from her early-stage status alone. What is missing?`,`The correct choice names the slide: necessary treated as sufficient.`],labels:{topic:`medicine efficacy`}},{id:`lr-d-160`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-nec-vs-suff`,secondarySkills:[`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:4,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: REVISE-fixed - difficulty corrected d5 to d3; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}],stimulus:`A successful product launch requires both an excellent product and excellent marketing. Our product is excellent. Therefore, our launch will succeed.`,stem:`Which of the following is necessary for the argument to succeed but would not by itself establish that the launch will succeed?`,choices:[{text:`Excellent products always lead to successful launches.`},{text:`Our marketing campaign is excellent.`},{text:`Our competitors' marketing campaigns are weak.`},{text:`Product quality matters more than marketing for launch success.`},{text:`The launch will succeed.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Necessary-but-not-sufficient is the target, and the "requires both" premise makes the test exacting. Choice (B) is necessary: negate it (marketing is not excellent) and the premise "success requires both" makes success impossible, killing the conclusion. But it is not sufficient: "requires" states necessity only — even with both ingredients, success is not guaranteed. Choice (A) is the sufficient-but-not-necessary mirror trap.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is the most demanding contrast item because the premise uses "requires," and precision about what "requires" does — and does not — establish is the whole game. Two-part test for (B). Necessity: the premise says success requires excellent product AND excellent marketing. Negate (B): our marketing is not excellent. Then the conjunction fails, and by the premise, success is impossible. The conclusion cannot survive — so (B) is necessary. Sufficiency: assume (B) — marketing is excellent — alongside the premise that our product is excellent. Does success follow? No: "requires both" tells you what success needs, not what guarantees it. Excellent product plus excellent marketing might still flop (bad timing, strong competition). So (B) does not establish the conclusion — not sufficient. Both halves pass. Choice (A) is the mirror trap: "excellent products always succeed" with our excellent product would guarantee success (sufficient), but the argument does not need that sweeping claim (not necessary). Choice (C) is irrelevant: competitors' weakness is not part of the stated requirements. Choice (D) is irrelevant: relative importance does not affect the logic. Choice (E) merely restates the conclusion — assuming it begs the question rather than supporting it.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is the mirror-image trap: with our excellent product, it would guarantee success (sufficient), but the argument does not need so sweeping a claim (not necessary). The stem asks for necessary-but-not-sufficient.`,`Correct. Necessary: without excellent marketing, the "requires both" premise makes success impossible. Not sufficient: "requires" establishes need, not guarantee — both ingredients might still not suffice.`,`Competitor weakness is nowhere in the argument's requirements and is irrelevant to whether our launch succeeds. This choice addresses neither half of the stem. The premise lists exactly two requirements — product and marketing excellence — and rivals appear in neither.`,`Relative importance of product versus marketing is irrelevant to the logic: the premise requires both regardless of which matters more. This choice does not engage the stem.`,`Restating the conclusion as an assumption begs the question — it does not support the argument, and it certainly is not a necessary-but-insufficient assumption. It fails the stem entirely.`],generalLesson:`"Requires" gives necessity and nothing more. When a premise lists requirements, each unmet requirement kills the conclusion (necessary) — but meeting them all still guarantees nothing (not sufficient).`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`requires-translation`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-nec-vs-suff`,`lr-necessary-assumption`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Success "requires both" — what does that tell you if marketing is not excellent? What does it tell you if marketing is excellent?`,`"Requires" states what is needed, not what guarantees success. Which choice is needed but not guaranteeing?`,`The correct choice is about our marketing being excellent.`],labels:{topic:`product launch`}}],i=[{id:`lr-e-161`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Last year three pedestrians were injured tripping on the cracked sidewalks of the Elm District, while Main Street's worn pavement, though unsightly, caused no injuries at all. The city council should therefore repair the Elm District sidewalks before repaving Main Street.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`A city should spend its entire infrastructure budget repairing any sidewalk on which an injury has ever occurred`},{text:`Aesthetic improvements to prominent streets should take priority over repairs in residential districts`},{text:`Preventing injuries that have actually occurred should take priority over improving appearances`},{text:`Private property owners should be required to maintain the sidewalks adjoining their land`},{text:`The Elm District sidewalks caused three injuries last year`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument moves from "the sidewalks caused real injuries while Main Street is merely ugly" to "fix the sidewalks first." The missing bridge is a rule that ranks preventing actual injuries above cosmetic improvements. Choice (C) supplies exactly that bridge: it takes the argument's premises and yields its conclusion, without adding anything the argument never claimed.`,explanationWalkthrough:`First isolate the argument's structure. The premises: Elm District sidewalks caused three injuries; Main Street's pavement is unsightly but caused none. The conclusion: repair the sidewalks before repaving Main Street. The reasoning assumes that preventing actual injuries outranks fixing mere appearances — but no premise states that rule, so the argument needs a principle to license the leap. A principle-support answer must connect these exact premises to this exact conclusion. Choice (C) does so perfectly: it is a general rule whose specific application to these facts produces the council's priority. It is neither broader than needed (it does not demand the whole budget, only a priority) nor narrower (it covers any public works choice of this kind). The distractors each fail a recognizable test. Choice (A) justifies far more than the argument claims — the argument never says the whole budget must go to the sidewalks. Choice (B) actively contradicts the conclusion. Choice (D) shifts the subject from the city's priorities to private owners' duties, which the argument never discusses. Choice (E) merely restates a premise; a restated fact cannot justify an inference, because justification requires a rule that connects facts to conclusions.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is tempting because it points in the same direction as the conclusion, but it is far too strong: the argument calls for repairing the sidewalks first, not for spending the entire infrastructure budget on them. A principle that justifies more than the argument concludes is the too-strong trap.`,`This principle points the wrong way — it would rank Main Street's appearance above the Elm District's safety, which is the opposite of the argument's conclusion. A principle that justifies the reverse of the stated conclusion is the opposite trap.`,`Correct. The argument's unstated bridge is that preventing real injuries outranks cosmetic improvement. This principle states exactly that rule at the right level of generality: applied to these premises, it yields precisely the conclusion that the sidewalks should be repaired first.`,`This shifts the scope from what the city should prioritize to what private owners should be required to do — a duty the argument never mentions. Because it answers a question about owners rather than about the council's spending priorities, it is the scope-shift trap.`,`This simply repeats one of the argument's premises. Restating a fact cannot justify the move from premises to conclusion, because a justification needs a general rule linking the facts to the recommendation. This is the restates-premise trap.`],generalLesson:`A justifying principle must bridge the argument's actual premises to its actual conclusion — matching its direction and its strength, neither proving too much nor drifting to a different subject.`,misconceptionTags:[`principle-strength`,`direction-match`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the council's conclusion, and which facts are offered to support it? What unstated rule connects those facts to that recommendation?`,`The argument ranks injury prevention above appearances without ever saying so. Which choice states that ranking as a general rule?`,`Eliminate choices that prove too much, point the wrong way, change the subject to property owners, or merely repeat a premise.`],labels:{topic:`municipal sidewalk repairs`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — single defensible answer (C); distractors fail for nameable reasons (too-strong/opposite/scope-shift/restates-premise); length parity OK; difficulty 2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-162`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-assumption`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The university museum's director was right to lend its rare twelfth-century manuscript to a small regional college for a semester. The loan gives students who cannot afford to travel a chance to study the manuscript firsthand, and the college's vault meets the same international security standards as the museum's own.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`It is appropriate to lend a fragile artifact to a less prominent institution when doing so expands scholarly access without compromising the artifact's safety`},{text:`Museums should always lend their rarest artifacts to any institution that requests them, regardless of the circumstances or the risks involved`},{text:`Museums should sell artifacts they cannot display in order to fund new acquisitions and facility upgrades`},{text:`Expanding scholarly access justifies lending artifacts even when their security cannot be fully guaranteed`},{text:`Students learn more from original manuscripts than from digital reproductions, according to recent educational research`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument defends the loan on two jointly necessary grounds: it widens access for students who cannot travel, and safety is preserved because the college vault meets international standards. The correct principle must contain both conditions. Choice (A) does: broader access plus uncompromised safety. Every other choice drops one condition, adds an unrelated one, or misses the point.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the argument carefully. Conclusion: the loan was the right decision. Premise one: it expands access to students who cannot afford to travel. Premise two: security is not compromised — the college vault meets international standards. The reasoning therefore depends on two conditions holding together: access is gained and safety is kept. A justifying principle must license the conclusion from exactly this combination. Choice (A) mirrors both halves: lending is appropriate when it expands scholarly access without compromising safety. Notice its restraint — "when" makes both conditions necessary, matching the argument's two-premise structure. Choice (B) is tempting because it favors lending, but it justifies far more than the argument needs: always lending to anyone who asks would include unsafe loans the argument explicitly rules out. Choice (C) changes the subject to selling artifacts, which the argument never contemplates. Choice (D) is the subtlest trap: it keeps the access half but drops the safety half, yet the argument leans on safety just as heavily — the vault detail would be pointless otherwise. Choice (E) may be true, but it is a factual claim about learning, not a principle connecting the premises to the conclusion; it answers a different question.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This principle captures both of the argument's load-bearing conditions — expanded access and preserved safety — at the right level of generality. Applied to the stated facts, it yields exactly the conclusion that the loan was appropriate.`,`This choice favors lending, which makes it tempting, but it is far too strong: it would justify lending even to institutions with no security at all. The argument pointedly establishes that safety is preserved, so a principle ignoring safety justifies more than the reasoning claims.`,`Selling artifacts to fund acquisitions is nowhere in the argument, which concerns a temporary loan, not a sale. This choice changes the subject entirely and therefore cannot justify the reasoning; it is the scope-shift trap.`,`This is the half-right trap: it captures the access condition while discarding the safety condition. But the argument treats the vault's security standards as essential — without them, the loan would not have been defended. A principle missing half the reasoning cannot justify it.`,`This may be a true claim about learning, but it is not a principle that connects the argument's premises to its conclusion. It supports one premise rather than licensing the inference, so it answers a different question than the stem asks.`],generalLesson:`When an argument rests on two conditions jointly, the justifying principle must contain both. Watch for attractive choices that keep one half of the reasoning while quietly dropping the other.`,misconceptionTags:[`joint-conditions`,`half-right-principle`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-assumption`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument gives two reasons for the loan: wider access and maintained security. What rule would need both of those to be true?`,`Which choices keep both conditions, and which quietly drop the safety requirement the argument emphasizes?`,`The correct principle makes expanded access and uncompromised safety jointly sufficient for an appropriate loan — nothing more, nothing less.`],labels:{topic:`museum manuscript loan`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-e-163`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Jamal should be hired as head lifeguard. He holds more rescue certifications than any other applicant, and every one of his certifications is retested each year.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`The applicant with the fewest qualifications should be hired, to give inexperienced candidates a chance`},{text:`Lifeguards should be strong swimmers`},{text:`Certification programs for lifeguards should be offered free of charge`},{text:`Jamal has more rescue certifications than the other applicants`},{text:`The most qualified applicant — the one with the greatest number of current qualifications — should be hired for a position`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument moves from "Jamal has the most up-to-date certifications" to "Jamal should be hired." The missing link is a rule that the most qualified applicant should get the job. Choice (E) states exactly that rule. The other choices point the wrong way, state irrelevant truths, change the subject, or just repeat a premise.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Keep the structure simple. Premises: Jamal has more rescue certifications than anyone else, and his certifications are current (retested yearly). Conclusion: he should be hired. The gap between them is the assumption that the best-credentialed candidate is the one to hire — no premise says that, so the argument needs a principle to supply it. Choice (E) is that principle in general form: hire the most qualified applicant, where "most qualified" is cashed out as the greatest number of current qualifications, matching both premises at once. Choice (A) justifies the opposite decision and is immediately out. Choice (B) may be true, but it says nothing about whom to hire — it is an irrelevant-true claim that cannot bridge these premises to this conclusion. Choice (C) changes the subject from hiring to how certification programs should be funded. Choice (D) merely repeats a premise; a repeated fact is not a rule, and only a rule can license the inference from "most certified" to "should be hired."`,choiceExplanations:[`This principle points in the opposite direction from the conclusion — it would favor the least qualified applicant, not Jamal. A principle that justifies the reverse of the argument's recommendation is the opposite trap.`,`Strong swimming may well matter for lifeguards, but this claim says nothing about which applicant should be hired. A true statement that cannot connect the premises to the conclusion is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`How certification programs are funded is a different subject from whom to hire as head lifeguard. Because it answers a question the argument never asks, this is the scope-shift trap.`,`This repeats one of the argument's premises word for word. Restating a fact does not justify an inference — justification requires a general rule linking the facts to the conclusion — so this is the restates-premise trap.`,`Correct. The argument assumes the best-credentialed candidate should be hired; this principle states that assumption as a general rule. Applied to the facts — most certifications, all current — it yields exactly the conclusion that Jamal should get the job.`],generalLesson:`To justify "this candidate should be chosen," look for the principle that turns the candidate's advantages into a hiring rule — and reject principles that repeat facts instead of supplying rules.`,misconceptionTags:[`rule-vs-fact`,`direction-match`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The premises say Jamal is the best credentialed. What unstated rule would turn "best credentialed" into "should be hired"?`,`Which choice is a general rule about hiring, rather than a fact about Jamal or a claim about a different topic?`,`The correct principle says the most qualified applicant should be hired — check that each other choice fails for a nameable reason.`],labels:{topic:`lifeguard hiring`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — clean d1 skill-acquisition bridge item; one defensible answer; explanations substantive.`}]},{id:`lr-e-164`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The zoning board was right to reject Hartwell Development's rezoning request. Hartwell promised the project would create 200 local jobs, but five years ago the same company abandoned a half-finished housing project in Millerton, leaving the town with a vacant lot and unpaid contractors.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`No developer that has ever failed to complete a project should ever be granted a rezoning`},{text:`A record of abandoning past projects is good reason to doubt a developer's promises about a new project`},{text:`Job-creation promises should be discounted because developers routinely exaggerate employment figures`},{text:`Towns should require developers to post bonds covering the full cost of any proposed project`},{text:`Millerton should sue Hartwell Development for abandoning its housing project`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The board rejected the request because Hartwell's past abandonment undermines its current jobs promise. The principle that justifies this reasoning says a track record of abandoned projects is reason to doubt new promises — choice (B). The distractors overreach, substitute a different reason for doubt, propose an unrelated policy, or draw a conclusion about a different town's lawsuit.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the exact inference. Premises: Hartwell promises 200 jobs; Hartwell previously abandoned a project in Millerton. Conclusion: rejecting the rezoning was right. The reasoning treats the past abandonment as evidence against the credibility of the current promise — a track-record inference. The justifying principle must license exactly that move: past abandonment gives reason to doubt present promises. Choice (B) states it at the right strength — "good reason to doubt," not proof of dishonesty and not a permanent ban. Choice (A) is tempting because it also invokes the track record, but it justifies far more than the argument: a lifetime ban for any past failure. The argument concludes only that this rejection was reasonable, not that Hartwell must never build again. Choice (C) offers a different reason to doubt job promises — routine exaggeration — which ignores the abandonment premise entirely; it justifies skepticism, but not this argument's skepticism. Choice (D) proposes a bonding policy the argument never mentions, shifting scope from evaluating this decision to designing future regulations. Choice (E) draws a conclusion about what Millerton should do, while the argument's conclusion is about whether this board's rejection was justified — a different conclusion altogether.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice invokes the track record, which makes it tempting, but it is far too strong: a permanent, universal ban goes well beyond the argument's conclusion that this particular rejection was reasonable. Principles that justify more than the argument claims are the too-strong trap.`,`Correct. The argument's core move is treating past abandonment as evidence against the credibility of the new jobs promise. This principle states exactly that track-record rule at a matching strength — reason to doubt, not conclusive proof.`,`This offers a different reason to distrust job promises — that developers generally exaggerate — which never uses the abandonment premise the argument actually relies on. It is the causal-alternative trap: an alternative explanation for doubt that bypasses the argument's own reasoning.`,`Requiring bonds is a policy proposal about future projects, not a rule that evaluates whether this rejection was justified. Because it changes the subject from the board's decision to municipal regulation design, it is the scope-shift trap.`,`The argument concludes that the zoning board's rejection was right; it says nothing about what Millerton should do about the old project. A principle licensing a lawsuit draws the wrong conclusion and is the wrong-conclusion trap.`],generalLesson:`Match the principle to the argument's actual inferential move — here, past conduct undermining a present promise — at the same strength, without substituting a different reason or inflating the conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`track-record-inference`,`principle-strength`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`causal-alternative`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-conclusion`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What exactly does the past abandonment do in this argument — what does it give the board reason to doubt?`,`The principle must be about track records and promises. Which choices use a different reason for doubt, or prove far more than "this rejection was reasonable"?`,`The correct principle says past abandonment is good reason to doubt new promises — strong enough to justify the rejection, modest enough not to demand a lifetime ban.`],labels:{topic:`zoning board developer rejection`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — track-record inference; (A) is a proper too-strong near-miss; difficulty 3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-165`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The transit authority was justified in charging tourists a higher fare than residents. Residents already fund the system through local taxes, while visiting tourists contribute nothing to its upkeep yet ride the same trains.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Those who contribute the most to a public service should pay the highest fares for using it`},{text:`Every public service should charge nonresidents more than residents in all circumstances`},{text:`Tourist attractions should be funded entirely by visitor fees rather than by local taxes`},{text:`It is fair for users of a publicly funded service who do not help fund it to pay more than those who do`},{text:`Residents deserve lower fares because they ride the trains more frequently than tourists do`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument's logic is that non-contributing users may fairly be charged more than contributing users. Choice (D) states exactly that fairness rule. Choice (A) reverses the direction — it would make contributing residents pay more. The rest overgeneralize, change the subject to funding models, or substitute ridership frequency for the argument's actual basis, which is who funds the system.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This item rewards precise attention to direction and basis. Premises: residents fund the system through taxes; tourists do not contribute yet use it. Conclusion: higher tourist fares are justified. The operative distinction is contributor versus non-contributor, and the normative direction is that non-contributors may be charged more. Choice (D) reproduces both: users who do not help fund a publicly funded service may fairly pay more than those who do. Now test each distractor against direction and basis. Choice (A) is the reversal trap in disguise — "contributors pay the highest fares" would make tax-paying residents pay more than tourists, the exact opposite of the conclusion. Choice (B) keeps the direction but explodes the scope to every public service in all circumstances; the argument is about this fare policy, grounded in this funding arrangement. Choice (C) shifts from fare fairness to how attractions should be funded overall — a scope shift the argument never engages. Choice (E) is the subtlest trap: it reaches the same conclusion (residents pay less) but on a different basis — ridership frequency — which the argument never mentions. A principle must justify the reasoning, not merely agree with the conclusion, and frequency-based reasoning is not this argument's reasoning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice looks relevant because it mentions contributors and fares, but it reverses the conditional direction: it would require contributing residents to pay the most. Since the argument concludes the opposite, this is the reverses-conditional trap.`,`The direction matches, but the scope is far too broad — every public service, in all circumstances. The argument's reasoning is tied to this funding arrangement, so a universal rule justifies far more than the argument claims: the too-strong trap.`,`This proposes a funding model for tourist attractions rather than a fairness rule about fares. Because it changes the subject from the fare decision to how attractions should be financed, it is the scope-shift trap.`,`Correct. The argument distinguishes contributors from non-contributors and concludes non-contributors may fairly pay more. This principle states precisely that rule, matching both the argument's basis (who funds the system) and its direction.`,`This reaches the same conclusion but for a different reason — how often each group rides — which appears nowhere in the argument. A principle must justify the reasoning actually given, not merely endorse the same outcome on other grounds; this is the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`A justifying principle must match the argument's direction and its actual basis. Beware choices that agree with the conclusion for a different reason, or that quietly flip who should pay more.`,misconceptionTags:[`direction-match`,`basis-match`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What distinction does the argument actually rely on — how often people ride, or who pays for the system?`,`Check each choice's direction: does it make non-contributors pay more, or does it accidentally make contributors pay more?`,`The correct principle ties the fare difference to funding contributions, not to ridership frequency — and it must point the same way the conclusion does.`],labels:{topic:`transit tourist fares`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-e-166`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The school board should not close the South Ward branch library. It is the only place where the ward's 4,000 households can get free public internet access, and many residents rely on it to apply for jobs.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`A public facility that is the sole source of an essential service for a community should not be eliminated`},{text:`Budget decisions should prioritize system-wide efficiency over the needs of any single neighborhood`},{text:`No public library branch should ever be closed for any reason`},{text:`Internet content on public library computers should be filtered to block inappropriate material`},{text:`The South Ward branch is the only free internet access point for 4,000 households`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument's logic is that the library is the sole provider of an essential service — job-application internet access — for 4,000 households, so it should not be closed. Choice (A) states exactly that rule. The distractors contradict the conclusion, overgeneralize to all libraries, drift to content filtering, or merely repeat a premise.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Break the argument into parts. Premise: the branch is the only free public internet access for 4,000 households, used for job applications. Conclusion: it should not be closed. The unstated bridge is a rule connecting "sole source of an essential service" to "should not be eliminated." Choice (A) is that bridge in general form — and notice it preserves both qualifiers the argument relies on: sole source and essential service. That precision matters when testing distractors. Choice (B) justifies the opposite conclusion, prioritizing efficiency over this neighborhood's needs. Choice (C) is the too-strong trap: it would forbid closing any branch ever, while the argument defends only this branch on these specific grounds — the argument never claims all closures are wrong. Choice (D) latches onto the keyword "internet" but addresses content filtering, a subject the argument never raises; keyword overlap is not relevance. Choice (E) restates the premise about 4,000 households. A restated fact is not a principle, and only a principle can license the move from "sole source" to "should not close."`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The argument treats sole-source status plus essential-service status as jointly sufficient reason not to close the branch. This principle states precisely that rule, and applying it to the stated facts yields the conclusion directly.`,`This principle ranks system-wide efficiency above a single neighborhood's needs, which would support closing the branch — the opposite of the argument's conclusion. It is the opposite trap.`,`This is tempting because it also opposes the closure, but it is far too strong: it forbids closing any branch for any reason, while the argument defends only this branch on its sole-source grounds. The too-strong trap justifies more than the reasoning claims.`,`The word "internet" appears in both, but content filtering has nothing to do with whether the branch should stay open. Surface keyword overlap without logical connection is the keyword-match trap.`,`This repeats the premise about 4,000 households almost verbatim. A restated premise cannot justify an inference; justification requires a general rule linking the facts to the conclusion. This is the restates-premise trap.`],generalLesson:`A justifying principle must preserve the argument's key qualifiers — here, "sole source" and "essential service." Choices that drop a qualifier or prove too much fail even when they point the same way.`,misconceptionTags:[`qualifier-match`,`principle-strength`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`keyword-match`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument does not say libraries can never close — it says this one should not close for a specific reason. What is that reason, stated generally?`,`Which choice turns "sole source of an essential service" into a rule, rather than banning all closures or discussing internet filtering?`,`The correct principle keeps both qualifiers — sole source and essential service — and concludes the facility should not be eliminated.`],labels:{topic:`branch library closure`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — qualifier-match item (sole source + essential service); (C) too-strong trap well-designed; difficulty 2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-167`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-assumption`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Dr. Alvarez did the right thing by reporting her colleague's falsified trial data to the ethics board. The falsified results were being used to support a drug now prescribed to thousands of patients.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Researchers may report misconduct if they feel personally comfortable doing so`},{text:`Drug companies should be legally required to publish all of their trial data`},{text:`Professionals should report misconduct when remaining silent would put others at risk of harm`},{text:`Dr. Alvarez's colleague should lose his medical license for falsifying data`},{text:`Most cases of research fraud are discovered by colleagues rather than by audits`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument praises the report because silence would have left thousands of patients exposed to a drug backed by fake data. The justifying principle must connect reporting to preventing harm to others — choice (C). The distractors make reporting merely optional, shift to drug-company duties, punish the colleague instead of justifying the report, or cite an irrelevant statistic.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Find the conclusion and its support. Conclusion: reporting the fraud was the right thing. Premise: the fake data supported a drug prescribed to thousands. The reasoning links the rightness of reporting to the danger silence would have permitted — patients harmed by a drug approved on false evidence. A justifying principle must therefore make the duty to report turn on risk to others. Choice (C) does exactly that: professionals should report misconduct when silence would endanger others. Applied here, silence would have left thousands at risk, so reporting was right. Choice (A) is the too-weak trap: "may report if comfortable" makes reporting optional and cannot justify the judgment that she did the right thing — it would equally permit staying silent. Choice (B) shifts scope from a researcher's duty to report to a company's duty to publish, a different subject. Choice (D) draws a conclusion about punishing the colleague, while the argument's conclusion is about the rightness of Alvarez's report — wrong conclusion. Choice (E) offers a statistical claim about how fraud is discovered; even if true, it supplies no rule connecting reporting to rightness, so it cannot justify the inference.`,choiceExplanations:[`This makes reporting merely permissible and dependent on comfort, which cannot justify the claim that Alvarez did the right thing — it would equally bless her staying silent. A principle too weak to license the conclusion is the too-weak trap.`,`This imposes a duty on drug companies to publish data, but the argument is about a researcher's decision to report a colleague. Because it changes the subject from the report to corporate disclosure, it is the scope-shift trap.`,`Correct. The argument's defense of the report rests on the danger silence posed to patients. This principle makes risk to others the trigger for a duty to report, and applying it here yields exactly the conclusion that reporting was right.`,`The argument concludes that the report was the right thing to do; it never addresses what punishment the colleague deserves. A principle licensing punishment draws a different conclusion and is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`How fraud is usually discovered is irrelevant to whether this report was right. This statistical claim provides no rule linking the premises to the conclusion, so it is the unsupported trap — true or not, it justifies nothing here.`],generalLesson:`For "did the right thing" conclusions, the principle must supply a duty or permission strong enough to produce that judgment — mere possibility ("may if comfortable") cannot carry a verdict of rightness.`,misconceptionTags:[`duty-strength`,`conclusion-match`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-conclusion`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-assumption`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Why, exactly, was reporting "the right thing" here — what bad outcome did it prevent?`,`The principle must connect reporting to protecting others from harm. Which choices do that, and which merely permit reporting or punish the colleague?`,`Eliminate the too-weak permission, the scope shift to drug companies, the wrong conclusion about punishment, and the irrelevant statistic.`],labels:{topic:`reporting research fraud`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — duty-strength item; (A) too-weak trap is a genuine near-miss; difficulty 3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-168`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The farmers' cooperative should sell its surplus grain now instead of holding it for spring. Storage fees rise every month the grain sits in the silos, and current market prices are the highest they have been in five years.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`The cooperative should hold its grain until prices fall so that buyers get a better deal`},{text:`Grain prices fluctuate with global weather patterns`},{text:`Farmers should always sell their entire harvest immediately, regardless of market conditions`},{text:`Storage fees are rising and grain prices are at a five-year high`},{text:`It is wise to sell a stored commodity when carrying costs are climbing and prices are unusually high`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument recommends selling now because holding costs are rising while prices peak. Choice (E) turns those two conditions into a general rule about when selling is wise. The other choices recommend the opposite, state an irrelevant truth, demand selling in all conditions, or just repeat the premises.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The structure is straightforward. Premises: storage fees climb each month; prices are at a five-year high. Conclusion: sell now rather than wait. The bridge is a rule that rising carrying costs plus peak prices make selling the wise move — choice (E) states it generally, covering any stored commodity, not just grain. Check the distractors against direction and strength. Choice (A) recommends the opposite action (hold until prices fall), so it cannot justify selling now. Choice (B) is true but inert: that prices fluctuate says nothing about whether to sell now, making it the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (C) agrees with the conclusion but justifies far more than the argument claims — always selling everything immediately, in any market — the too-strong trap. Choice (D) restates both premises; restated facts are not a rule, and without a rule there is no justification for the "should sell" inference.`,choiceExplanations:[`This recommends holding the grain — the opposite of the argument's conclusion. A principle that justifies the reverse action is the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Price fluctuation may be true, but it gives no guidance about whether to sell now or later. A true statement that cannot bridge the premises to the conclusion is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`This agrees with selling now but demands immediate sale in all conditions, which is far more than the argument's conditional reasoning claims. Justifying more than the argument concludes is the too-strong trap.`,`This merely repeats the two premises about fees and prices. Restated facts are not a principle, and only a general rule can license the inference to "should sell now." This is the restates-premise trap.`,`Correct. The argument's reasoning is that climbing carrying costs plus peak prices favor selling. This principle states exactly that conditional rule generally, and applying it yields the conclusion to sell now.`],generalLesson:`Justifying principles often take the form "when conditions X and Y hold, action Z is wise." Check that the principle's conditions match the argument's premises and its action matches the conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`conditional-principle`,`direction-match`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`too-strong`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument says sell now because of two conditions: rising storage costs and peak prices. What general rule connects those conditions to selling?`,`Which choice states a rule with both conditions, rather than repeating the facts or demanding selling in every situation?`,`The correct principle says selling is wise when carrying costs climb and prices are unusually high — nothing broader.`],labels:{topic:`grain cooperative sale timing`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — conditional principle (when X and Y, sell); clean direction/strength discrimination; difficulty 2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-169`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The zoning board should grant a rehearing on the Harbor View appeal. The deciding vote was cast by a board member who had publicly promised voters he would block the project before ever hearing the evidence.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Board members should try to keep an open mind during hearings`},{text:`A decision should be reconsidered when the deciding vote was cast by someone who had prejudged the outcome`},{text:`Any administrative decision must be reheard whenever any participant voices an opinion about it`},{text:`Criminal defendants are entitled to a jury that has not been exposed to pretrial publicity`},{text:`Only decisions made by fully impartial boards are ever subject to rehearing`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The argument demands a rehearing because the deciding vote came from a member who prejudged the case. Choice (B) states exactly that rule: prejudgment by the deciding voter warrants reconsideration. The distractors are too weak to require a rehearing, too broad, drawn from criminal law, or reversed in their logic.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Parse the inference precisely. Premise: the deciding vote was cast by someone who publicly prejudged the outcome before hearing evidence. Conclusion: the board should grant a rehearing. The bridge must connect prejudgment-by-the-deciding-voter to reconsideration — choice (B) does so, and note its careful scope: it requires both that the outcome was prejudged and that the prejudiced person cast the deciding vote. That matches the argument, which emphasizes "the deciding vote," not mere participation. Choice (A) is the too-weak trap: "should try to keep an open mind" is advice, not a rule requiring a rehearing — it cannot justify the conclusion that a rehearing should be granted. Choice (C) is too strong: any voiced opinion by any participant triggering a rehearing would include fair-minded members stating preliminary views, far beyond the argument. Choice (D) is the scope-shift trap: criminal jury rights are a different legal domain, and nothing in the argument invokes criminal procedure. Choice (E) is the reversal trap: it says only impartial boards' decisions may be reheard, which would actually forbid rehearing this biased decision — the logical converse of what the argument needs.`,choiceExplanations:[`Urging members to "try" to be open-minded is advice, not a rule that mandates a rehearing. Because it is too weak to license the conclusion that a rehearing should be granted, it is the too-weak trap.`,`Correct. The argument's entire force comes from the combination of prejudgment and a deciding vote. This principle makes exactly that combination the trigger for reconsideration — matching the argument's scope and strength precisely.`,`This would require rehearings whenever anyone voices any opinion, which goes far beyond the argument's focus on a prejudging deciding voter. A principle that justifies much more than the argument claims is the too-strong trap.`,`Jury impartiality in criminal trials is a different domain from administrative rehearings. The argument never invokes criminal procedure, so this principle changes the subject: the scope-shift trap.`,`Read carefully: this says rehearings are available only for decisions by fully impartial boards — which would exclude this very case. It reverses the needed logic and is the reverses-conditional trap.`],generalLesson:`Match the principle to the argument's precise trigger — here, prejudgment plus a deciding vote. Weaker advice, broader rules, and different legal domains all fail the match.`,misconceptionTags:[`trigger-match`,`reversal`,`domain-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-weak`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`reverses-conditional`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two facts combine to demand the rehearing — prejudgment alone, or prejudgment by someone specific?`,`The principle must require a rehearing (not merely advise open-mindedness) and must stay in the administrative domain. Which choices fail those tests?`,`Choice (E) is a trap: read its direction — does it permit rehearing this biased decision, or forbid it?`],labels:{topic:`zoning board rehearing`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — trigger-match d4 (prejudgment + deciding vote); (E) reversal trap requires careful reading; difficulty 4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-170`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The Gazette should print a correction. Yesterday's article attributed a controversial quote to Councilwoman Park, but she never said it, and she has formally demanded a correction.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Newspapers should never correct published articles, since corrections undermine their credibility`},{text:`The Gazette misattributed a quote to Councilwoman Park`},{text:`Any article containing any error, however trivial, must be retracted in full`},{text:`A publication should correct a factual error when the person harmed by the error requests a correction`},{text:`Journalists should verify quotes with their sources before publishing`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument says a correction is owed because a factual error harmed someone who then requested a correction. Choice (D) states that rule exactly. The distractors oppose corrections, repeat the premise, demand retraction of everything, or offer preventive advice that does not justify correcting now.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument is compact: premise one, the quote was misattributed (a factual error); premise two, the harmed person demanded a correction; conclusion, the paper should print one. The bridging principle must link "factual error plus request from the harmed person" to "should correct" — choice (D). Each distractor fails a basic test. Choice (A) justifies the opposite of the conclusion. Choice (B) restates the misattribution premise; a repeated fact is not a rule and cannot justify the "should" in the conclusion. Choice (C) is too strong: it demands full retraction for any error however trivial, while the argument asks only for a correction of this harmful error — the argument never claims every typo requires a retraction. Choice (E) is the tempting irrelevant-true: verifying quotes before publishing is good practice, but it is forward-looking advice that does nothing to justify printing a correction after the error has occurred.`,choiceExplanations:[`This principle opposes all corrections, which is the opposite of the argument's conclusion that a correction should be printed. It is the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`This repeats the premise that the quote was misattributed. A restated fact is not a general rule, and only a rule can license the move from "an error occurred" to "a correction should be printed." This is the restates-premise trap.`,`This demands full retraction for any error however trivial — far more than the argument's call for a correction of this specific harmful error. A principle justifying more than the argument claims is the too-strong trap.`,`Correct. The argument's two premises are a factual error and a request from the person it harmed; this principle makes exactly that combination the trigger for a correction, yielding the conclusion directly.`,`Verifying quotes before publishing is sound advice, but it is about preventing future errors, not about whether to correct this one. A true claim that cannot bridge these premises to this conclusion is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish principles that justify the conclusion from good advice about avoiding the problem in the first place — only the former bridges these premises to this "should."`,misconceptionTags:[`rule-vs-advice`,`principle-strength`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`restates-premise`,`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-support`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion is that a correction should be printed now. Which choice is a rule about when corrections are owed?`,`Watch out for good advice that prevents errors — does it justify correcting this error?`,`The correct principle pairs a factual error with a request from the harmed person as the trigger for a correction.`],labels:{topic:`newspaper correction`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — clean d1 skill-acquisition; (E) forward-looking advice vs backward-looking correction is a proper discrimination; difficulty 1 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-171`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A tenant who causes damage beyond ordinary wear and tear is responsible for paying for the repairs.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A landlord takes three weeks to fix a leaking pipe, so the tenant deducts the repair cost from the rent`},{text:`A tenant's dog chews through two wooden doorframes; the tenant pays the carpenter's bill for replacing them`},{text:`Sunlight gradually fades the living-room carpet over five years; the landlord replaces it at no charge to the tenant`},{text:`The previous tenant dented the refrigerator door; the current tenant is billed for the repair`},{text:`A tenant pays the rent five days late and is charged a late fee under the lease`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The principle makes the damage-causing tenant pay when damage exceeds ordinary wear. Choice (B) is the clean application: the tenant's dog caused damage plainly beyond normal wear, and that tenant pays. The other choices involve the wrong party, ordinary wear, or subjects the principle never addresses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Applying a principle means checking each scenario against the principle's conditions: (1) the person is the tenant, (2) that tenant caused the damage, (3) the damage goes beyond ordinary wear and tear — then the result follows: the tenant pays. Choice (B) satisfies all three: the tenant's own dog chewed the doorframes (tenant-caused, far beyond wear), and the tenant pays. Now check the rest. Choice (A) concerns the landlord's duty to repair, which the principle never addresses — wrong conclusion about a different party's obligations. Choice (C) is the tempting near-miss: it involves a tenant and damage, but faded carpet from sunlight is ordinary wear, so the principle's trigger condition fails and the principle does not apply — this is exactly the case the principle excludes. Choice (D) bills the wrong tenant: the principle assigns responsibility to the tenant who caused the damage, not whoever happens to live there now — a scope shift to a different party. Choice (E) is about late rent, a subject the principle says nothing about; it answers a different question.`,choiceExplanations:[`This scenario is about the landlord's repair obligations and rent deductions, which the principle never addresses. Because it draws a conclusion about a different duty, it is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`Correct. The tenant caused the damage (via the dog), the damage clearly exceeds ordinary wear and tear, and the responsible tenant pays — all three of the principle's conditions are met, so the outcome follows directly.`,`This is tempting because it involves a tenant and damaged property, but sun-faded carpet is ordinary wear and tear, so the principle's trigger condition is not met. A case the principle explicitly excludes cannot conform to it; this is the half-right trap.`,`The principle assigns repair costs to the tenant who caused the damage. Billing the current tenant for the previous tenant's dent shifts responsibility to the wrong party, so this is the scope-shift trap.`,`Late rent and late fees are nowhere in the principle, which concerns only damage beyond ordinary wear. This scenario answers a different question than the principle addresses.`],generalLesson:`To apply a principle, test every condition against the scenario — who acted, what happened, and whether thresholds like "beyond ordinary wear" are met. Near-misses that fail one condition do not conform.`,misconceptionTags:[`condition-checking`,`threshold-test`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`scope-shift`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What three things must be true before the principle assigns the repair bill to someone?`,`Which scenarios fail at least one condition — wrong person, ordinary wear, or a subject the principle never mentions?`,`The correct scenario has the damage-causing tenant paying for damage that plainly exceeds ordinary wear and tear.`],labels:{topic:`tenant damage responsibility`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — three-condition application; (C) ordinary-wear near-miss is a genuine half-right trap; difficulty 2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-172`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A manufacturer is liable for a product defect only if the defect was present when the product was sold.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A toaster overheats because its owner used it to dry wet mittens; the manufacturer is held liable for the resulting fire`},{text:`A chair collapses under a 400-pound load though it was rated for 250 pounds; the maker is held liable on the grounds that the failure proves a defect existed at sale`},{text:`A phone battery explodes, but no evidence shows when the defect arose; the manufacturer is held liable anyway`},{text:`A ladder's welds were cracked during manufacturing, but the injured buyer is denied recovery because she bought the ladder on sale`},{text:`A crib's slats were glued improperly at the factory and later snap, injuring an infant; the manufacturer is held liable`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The principle makes defect-at-time-of-sale a necessary condition for liability. Choice (E) is the clean fit: the defect (bad gluing) existed at manufacture/sale, and liability follows. The other choices impose liability without the required condition, deny it on irrelevant grounds, or treat overloading as proof of a pre-sale defect.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle has the form "liable only if defect present at sale" — defect-at-sale is necessary for liability. So a conforming case must show the defect predated the sale before imposing liability, and must not impose liability where that condition is unmet or deny it for unrelated reasons. Choice (E) fits: the slats were glued improperly at the factory (defect present at sale), the crib later fails, and the manufacturer is held liable — the necessary condition is satisfied and the result follows. Choice (A) imposes liability where the failure came from misuse after sale, violating the only-if condition — a misapplication. Choice (B) is subtler: the chair failed under more than its rated load, which does not show a defect existed at sale; treating the failure itself as proof is the half-right trap, since the necessary condition is assumed rather than shown. Choice (C) imposes liability with no evidence about when the defect arose — the necessary condition is simply missing, so liability cannot conform to the principle. Choice (D) gets the defect-at-sale condition right but denies recovery because the ladder was bought on sale, a factor the principle never mentions — the wrong conclusion on an irrelevant ground.`,choiceExplanations:[`The toaster failed because of misuse after the sale, so the defect was not present when sold. Imposing liability anyway violates the principle's only-if condition; this is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`This is tempting because it mentions a defect at sale, but a chair breaking under nearly double its rated load does not show the defect predated the sale — the required condition is assumed, not established. This is the half-right trap.`,`Here liability is imposed with no evidence at all about when the defect arose. Since defect-at-sale is a necessary condition, liability without it cannot conform to the principle; this is the unsupported trap.`,`The defect was present at manufacture, so the principle's condition is met — but recovery is denied because the ladder was bought on sale, a ground the principle never mentions. Denying the result on irrelevant grounds is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`Correct. The improper gluing existed when the crib was made and sold, satisfying the principle's necessary condition, and the manufacturer is held liable — a direct application of the rule.`],generalLesson:`"Only if" makes a condition necessary: before applying the result, verify the condition is actually established — not assumed, not missing, and not replaced by an irrelevant factor.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-condition`,`only-if`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`unsupported`,`wrong-conclusion`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle says liability requires the defect to predate the sale. Which scenarios actually establish that, rather than assuming it?`,`Watch for liability imposed after misuse, or denied for reasons like sale price that the principle never mentions.`,`The correct scenario shows the defect existed at manufacture and holds the manufacturer liable on that basis.`],labels:{topic:`manufacturer liability`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — only-if application; (B) assumes-rather-than-shows the condition (proper half-right trap); difficulty 3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-173`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:1,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: People should keep their promises unless keeping a promise would cause serious harm to others.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`Mara promised to help her friend move, but she broke the promise in order to rush a neighbor to the emergency room`},{text:`Dev kept his promise to attend a party even though going meant missing his sister's surgery`},{text:`Lena broke her promise to call her cousin because she felt like watching a movie instead`},{text:`Keeping promises is generally a good habit for maintaining friendships, and breaking them without a strong reason tends to damage trust over time`},{text:`Omar broke a promise he could easily have kept without harming anyone, simply because a more appealing invitation came along`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The principle requires keeping promises except where keeping one would seriously harm others. Choice (A) applies the exception exactly: Mara breaks her promise to prevent serious harm. The other choices violate the principle, break promises for mere convenience, state an irrelevant truism, or describe a violation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle is a rule with an exception: keep promises, unless keeping one would cause serious harm to others. A conforming scenario either keeps a promise where no serious harm threatens, or breaks one where keeping it would cause serious harm. Choice (A) is the second kind: rushing a neighbor to the emergency room prevents serious harm, so breaking the moving-help promise fits the exception precisely. Choice (B) violates the principle — Dev keeps a promise whose keeping causes serious harm (missing his sister's surgery), the exact situation where the principle requires breaking it. Choice (C) breaks a promise for mere convenience; the principle's exception covers only serious harm to others, so this breach is unsupported by the principle. Choice (D) is an irrelevant-true truism about friendship that applies the principle to nothing. Choice (E) describes someone breaking a promise he could have kept harmlessly — a direct violation of the rule, not conformity with it.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Keeping the promise would have meant failing to get a neighbor emergency care — serious harm to another person — so the principle's exception applies and breaking the promise conforms to it.`,`This violates the principle: keeping the promise caused serious harm (missing the surgery), which is precisely when the principle says the promise should be broken. A scenario that breaks the rule is the opposite trap.`,`Breaking a promise to watch a movie involves no serious harm to others, so the principle's exception does not cover it. This breach is unsupported by the principle — the unsupported trap.`,`That promise-keeping is good for friendships may be true, but it applies the principle to no specific situation. A general truism that performs no application is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Omar broke a promise he could have kept without harming anyone, which directly violates the rule to keep promises absent serious harm. Describing a violation is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Rules with exceptions are applied by checking the exception's trigger first: does this situation really involve what the exception requires — here, serious harm to others — or just convenience?`,misconceptionTags:[`exception-trigger`,`rule-application`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle has two parts: keep promises, except when keeping one would seriously harm others. Which scenario fits the exception?`,`Breaking a promise for convenience is not covered — the exception requires serious harm to others. Who faces serious harm in each scenario?`,`The correct scenario breaks a promise to prevent an emergency — serious harm avoided, exception satisfied.`],labels:{topic:`promise-keeping exception`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-e-174`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A public official may accept a gift only if it is worth less than $50 and it comes from a constituent.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A councilor accepts a $30 bottle of wine from a registered lobbyist who lives in another district`},{text:`A mayor accepts an $80 fruit basket from a constituent who lives on her street`},{text:`A commissioner accepts a $25 gift basket from a constituent in her district`},{text:`A councilor accepts a $60 watch from a constituent because he immediately donates it to charity`},{text:`A mayor accepts a $20 book from a constituent and also accepts a $100 pen set from a developer`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The principle imposes two jointly necessary conditions: under $50 AND from a constituent. Only choice (C) satisfies both — $25, from a constituent. Every other choice fails at least one condition, no matter how sympathetic the circumstances.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a conjunction test: "only if A and B" means both conditions are necessary, so accepting a gift conforms only when value is under $50 and the giver is a constituent. Check each scenario against both. Choice (C): $25 is under $50, and the giver is a constituent — both satisfied, so acceptance conforms. Choice (A) meets the value test ($30) but the lobbyist lives in another district — not a constituent — so it fails; this is the half-right trap, satisfying one conjunct while missing the other. Choice (B) reverses the failure: the giver is a constituent but $80 exceeds the limit — again half-right. Choice (D) fails the value test ($60), and the charitable donation is irrelevant to the principle, which lists only the two conditions — a scope shift disguised as a good excuse. Choice (E) is the trickiest: the $20 book conforms, but the scenario also includes accepting a $100 pen set from a developer, which violates both conditions; because the question asks which scenario conforms, a scenario containing a clear violation cannot be the answer — it is half-right at best.`,choiceExplanations:[`The $30 value passes, but the lobbyist is not a constituent, so one of the two necessary conditions fails. Satisfying only half of a conjunction is the half-right trap.`,`The giver is a constituent, but $80 exceeds the under-$50 limit, so again only one necessary condition is met. A conjunction requires both; this is the half-right trap.`,`Correct. The gift is worth less than $50 and comes from a constituent — both jointly necessary conditions are satisfied, so accepting it conforms to the principle exactly.`,`At $60 the gift exceeds the limit, and donating it to charity adds a condition the principle never lists. Irrelevant good deeds cannot cure a failed necessary condition; this is the scope-shift trap.`,`The $20 book is fine, but the scenario also includes a $100 gift from a non-constituent developer — a double violation. A scenario containing a violation cannot most clearly conform; this is the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`With "only if A and B," test both conditions independently — a scenario that satisfies one but fails the other does not conform, no matter how close it looks.`,misconceptionTags:[`conjunction`,`necessary-conditions`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`half-right`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle has two requirements joined by "and." What happens if a scenario meets only one of them?`,`Test each gift against both conditions separately: is it under $50, and is the giver a constituent?`,`Only one scenario passes both tests — and watch out for scenarios that mix a conforming gift with a violating one.`],labels:{topic:`official gift rules`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — conjunction test with three half-right traps; (E) mixed conforming/violating scenario is a strong distractor; difficulty 4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-175`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: Employers should grant disability accommodations unless doing so would impose an undue hardship on the business.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A small bakery grants a costly accommodation that forces it to close within a year`},{text:`A manager denies a simple schedule change because it would be mildly inconvenient to redraw the rota`},{text:`A bookstore installs a $200 ramp for a cashier who uses a wheelchair; the store's profits are unaffected`},{text:`A factory fires a worker with a disability rather than consider any accommodation`},{text:`All employers must grant every accommodation request immediately, regardless of cost`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The principle requires granting accommodations except where they would cause undue hardship. Choice (C) grants a cheap, effective accommodation with no hardship — a direct application. The other choices grant where hardship exists, deny where none exists, refuse outright, or inflate the rule beyond its exception.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle is "grant, unless undue hardship." Conformity means: grant when no undue hardship, or withhold only when undue hardship is shown. Choice (C): a $200 ramp, profits unaffected — no undue hardship — and the employer grants it. Direct fit. Choice (A) grants an accommodation that destroys the business — that is precisely the undue-hardship case where the principle permits withholding, so granting it violates the exception. Choice (B) denies an accommodation for mere inconvenience; inconvenience is not undue hardship, so the denial is unsupported by the principle. Choice (D) refuses to consider any accommodation at all, which the principle never licenses — the wrong conclusion. Choice (E) is the too-strong trap: it deletes the "unless" clause entirely, turning a qualified rule into an absolute one; the principle explicitly preserves the hardship exception.`,choiceExplanations:[`Granting an accommodation that bankrupts the business is the undue-hardship case the principle's exception covers. Doing what the exception permits withholding is the opposite trap.`,`Mild inconvenience is not undue hardship, so the principle gives no license to deny the schedule change. A denial the principle does not support is the unsupported trap.`,`Correct. The accommodation is granted and no undue hardship results — the principle's default applies cleanly, with its exception untriggered. It applies the rule's default exactly as stated, with the unless-clause correctly left untriggered.`,`Firing a worker rather than considering any accommodation goes beyond anything the principle permits; it draws a harsher conclusion than the rule licenses. This is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`This erases the "unless" clause, making the duty absolute. A principle with an exception cannot justify a choice that ignores the exception; this is the too-strong trap.`],generalLesson:`Apply "should, unless" principles by testing the unless-clause first: if the exception's trigger (undue hardship) is absent, the default duty stands; if present, the duty lifts.`,misconceptionTags:[`exception-trigger`,`unless-clause`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-conclusion`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle says grant accommodations, with one exception. What triggers the exception?`,`Is mild inconvenience the same as undue hardship? Which scenarios confuse the two?`,`The correct scenario grants a low-cost accommodation where no hardship exists — the principle's default, exception untriggered.`],labels:{topic:`workplace accommodations`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-e-176`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: Journalists should protect the identity of confidential sources, except when a source has knowingly provided false information.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A reporter reveals an accurate source's identity because a rival newspaper offered her a higher-paying job`},{text:`After learning her source deliberately fabricated documents, a reporter names the source in a follow-up article`},{text:`A reporter continues to protect a source even after discovering the source knowingly lied to her`},{text:`A reporter publishes a source's name because the resulting story proved unpopular with readers`},{text:`Editors should never publish claims that rely on anonymous sources`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The principle protects sources except when they knowingly lie. Choice (B) is the exception applied: the source deliberately fabricated documents, so naming the source conforms. The other choices reveal sources for irrelevant reasons, protect a lying source in defiance of the exception, or address publishing policy rather than source protection.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle has a default (protect identities) and a precisely stated exception (knowingly false information). A conforming scenario either protects a non-lying source or reveals a knowingly lying one. Choice (B): the source deliberately fabricated documents — the exception's trigger — and the reporter names the source. Exact fit. Choice (A) reveals an accurate source for a job offer: the source did not lie, so the exception does not apply, and the principle's default is violated — the wrong conclusion on an irrelevant motive. Choice (C) is the mirror violation: the source knowingly lied, which triggers the exception, yet the reporter keeps protecting — the opposite of what the principle prescribes once the exception fires. Choice (D) names a source because the story was unpopular; unpopularity is not the exception's trigger, so the revelation is unsupported by the principle. Choice (E) shifts scope from protecting sources' identities to whether anonymous claims should be published at all — a different editorial question the principle never addresses.`,choiceExplanations:[`The source was accurate, so the exception for knowingly false information does not apply, and revealing the identity violates the principle's default. A job offer is irrelevant to the rule; this is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`Correct. The source deliberately fabricated documents, which is exactly the exception's trigger — knowingly providing false information — so naming the source is what the principle prescribes.`,`Once the reporter learns the source knowingly lied, the exception applies and protection is no longer required. Continuing to protect defies the exception, making this the opposite trap.`,`Reader unpopularity is not the principle's exception trigger, so naming the source on that ground has no support in the rule. This is the unsupported trap.`,`Whether editors should publish anonymous claims is a different question from whether reporters should protect sources' identities. This changes the subject and is the scope-shift trap.`],generalLesson:`Exceptions fire only on their stated trigger. Test whether the scenario really contains "knowingly provided false information" — motives like job offers or unpopularity do not count.`,misconceptionTags:[`exception-trigger`,`default-vs-exception`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle protects sources, with one exception. What exactly triggers that exception?`,`Which scenarios involve a source who knowingly lied — and what does the principle require once that happens?`,`Job offers and unpopular stories are not the trigger. Only one scenario both contains the trigger and responds to it correctly.`],labels:{topic:`journalist source protection`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — exception-trigger d4; (C) mirror violation is a genuine trap; difficulty 4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-177`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: In a fair queue, service goes in order of arrival, except that genuine emergencies take precedence.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A deli serves a regular customer who arrived later because he is a personal friend of the owner`},{text:`An emergency room treats a patient with a mild cold before longer-waiting patients because he complained loudly`},{text:`A theater seats latecomers first in order to avoid disrupting the performance`},{text:`A job applicant who applied first is hired over a better-qualified applicant who applied later`},{text:`A plumber finishes her current job, then rushes to a burst-pipe emergency ahead of a scheduled faucet repair`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The principle orders service by arrival, with genuine emergencies jumping the queue. Choice (E) applies the exception: a burst pipe is a genuine emergency, so it precedes the scheduled repair. The other choices jump the queue for friendship, loud complaining, theater logistics, or hiring — none of which the principle covers.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle's structure: default = order of arrival; exception = genuine emergencies go first. A conforming case follows the default or correctly invokes the exception. Choice (E): the plumber honors her current job, then prioritizes a burst-pipe emergency over a routine scheduled repair — the exception applied to a genuine emergency. Choice (A) lets friendship override arrival order; friendship is not the stated exception, so this violates the default. Choice (B) is the subtle half-right trap: it looks like the emergency exception, but a mild cold is not a genuine emergency and loud complaining is not a qualifying trigger — the exception is invoked without its condition. Choice (C) reorders seating for logistical convenience, a ground the principle never lists. Choice (D) shifts scope from queues for service to hiring decisions, which are not first-come-first-served queues under this principle.`,choiceExplanations:[`Serving a later arrival because of friendship violates the order-of-arrival default, and friendship is not the principle's exception. This draws a conclusion the rule does not license: the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`This mimics the emergency exception, but a mild cold is not a genuine emergency — the exception's condition is not met. Invoking an exception without its trigger is the half-right trap.`,`Seating latecomers first for logistical convenience uses a ground the principle never mentions. Convenience is not a genuine emergency, so this reordering is unsupported by the rule: the unsupported trap.`,`Hiring is not a service queue governed by arrival order, so the principle does not apply to it at all. Applying a queue rule to hiring is the scope-shift trap.`,`Correct. The burst pipe is a genuine emergency, which the principle explicitly lets take precedence, while the routine repair waits its turn — the exception applied exactly as stated.`],generalLesson:`An exception for "genuine emergencies" is not a license for any priority claim — friendship, loudness, and convenience do not trigger it. Verify the trigger, not just the outcome.`,misconceptionTags:[`exception-trigger`,`genuine-condition`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The exception is for genuine emergencies. Which scenarios actually contain one?`,`A mild cold plus loud complaining looks like an emergency — but is it genuine? Check the trigger, not the drama.`,`The correct scenario prioritizes a real emergency (a burst pipe) while respecting the existing job — default honored, exception properly fired.`],labels:{topic:`queue fairness`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — "genuine emergency" trigger test; (B) mild-cold half-right trap is well-designed; difficulty 3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-178`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A student may retake a missed exam only if illness or a documented emergency prevented attendance.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`After missing the final with a doctor-verified flu, Ana retakes it the following week`},{text:`Ben retakes an exam he attended but failed, hoping for a better grade`},{text:`Cara missed the exam to attend a concert and is allowed to retake it`},{text:`Dan missed the exam because of a car accident but is denied a retake`},{text:`All students may retake any exam whenever they wish`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The principle permits a retake only for illness or documented emergency. Choice (A) is the direct application: verified flu (illness) prevented attendance, so the retake is allowed. The other choices allow retakes for bad grades or concerts, deny one despite a car accident, or erase the restriction entirely.`,explanationWalkthrough:`"Only if" makes illness-or-documented-emergency necessary for a permitted retake. Choice (A) satisfies it: a doctor-verified flu is an illness that prevented attendance, so allowing the retake conforms. Choice (B) fails the condition: Ben attended the exam, so nothing prevented his attendance — wanting a better grade is not illness or emergency, and the retake is unsupported. Choice (C) fails it more starkly: a concert is neither illness nor emergency, so permitting the retake violates the only-if restriction. Choice (D) is the interesting reversal: Dan's car accident is a documented emergency, so the principle permits his retake — denying it contradicts what the principle allows, the wrong conclusion. Choice (E) deletes the restriction altogether, converting a conditional permission into a universal one — the too-strong trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. A doctor-verified flu is an illness that prevented attendance, satisfying the principle's necessary condition, so the permitted retake follows directly. It satisfies every condition the stem imposes, with no outside assumptions required.`,`Ben attended the exam, so no illness or emergency prevented his attendance; wanting a better grade is not a listed condition. This retake is unsupported by the principle: the unsupported trap.`,`A concert is neither an illness nor a documented emergency, so the only-if condition fails and the retake cannot be permitted under the principle. This is the opposite trap — it allows what the rule forbids.`,`A car accident is a documented emergency, so the principle permits Dan's retake; denying it contradicts the rule. This draws the wrong conclusion from satisfied conditions: the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`This erases the only-if restriction, granting retakes to everyone for any reason. A choice that ignores the principle's stated limitation is the too-strong trap. Its excessive strength is precisely what makes it tempting — and what disqualifies it, since the argument never claims that much.`],generalLesson:`"Only if" sets a necessary condition: check whether the scenario truly satisfies it (illness, documented emergency) before allowing the result — and notice when a scenario satisfies it but is wrongly denied.`,misconceptionTags:[`only-if`,`necessary-condition`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`opposite`,`wrong-conclusion`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle allows a retake only if illness or a documented emergency prevented attendance. Test each scenario against that condition.`,`Which scenarios satisfy the condition but get the wrong outcome, and which fail the condition but get a retake anyway?`,`Only one scenario both satisfies the condition (verified illness) and draws the permitted conclusion.`],labels:{topic:`exam retake policy`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — only-if application; (D) reversal (satisfied condition, wrong outcome) is a proper trap; difficulty 3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-e-179`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,conditionalComplexity:4,distractorProximity:5,inferentialDistance:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A customer is entitled to a full refund if a product stops working within 30 days of purchase and is returned with its receipt, unless the breakdown resulted from misuse.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A blender stops working on day 40; the customer returns it with the receipt and receives a full refund`},{text:`A kettle stops heating on day 10 and is returned with the receipt, but the customer had dropped it down a flight of stairs; the store grants a full refund`},{text:`A kettle stops heating on day 12; an inspection shows a faulty thermostat; the customer returns it with the receipt and receives a full refund`},{text:`A toaster stops working on day 5; the customer returns it with the receipt; an inspection finds no misuse; the store refuses any refund`},{text:`A lamp stops working on day 20; the customer returns it with the receipt; an inspection finds no misuse; the store offers only store credit`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The principle requires three things for a full refund: breakdown within 30 days, return with receipt, and no misuse. Only choice (C) satisfies all three — day 12, receipt returned, faulty thermostat (not misuse) — and grants the full refund. Each other choice fails or mishandles exactly one condition.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This principle is a three-part test with an exception: (1) stops working within 30 days, (2) returned with receipt, (3) no misuse — then full refund is owed. A conforming scenario must satisfy all three and draw the full-refund result; a single failed condition changes the outcome. Choice (C): day 12 (within 30), receipt returned, faulty thermostat rules out misuse — all three met, full refund granted. Each distractor surgically violates one condition. Choice (A) fails condition 1: day 40 is outside the window, so granting a full refund violates the rule — half-right, since the other conditions hold. Choice (B) triggers the misuse exception: dropping the kettle down stairs is misuse, so the entitlement is defeated even though timing and receipt are fine — again half-right. Choice (D) satisfies all three (day 5, receipt, no misuse) yet refuses any refund — the wrong conclusion from fully satisfied conditions. Choice (E) also satisfies all three but grants only store credit: the principle entitles the customer to a full refund, so a partial remedy is too weak to conform.`,choiceExplanations:[`Day 40 falls outside the 30-day window, so the first condition fails and no refund is owed under the principle. Granting one anyway satisfies only the remaining conditions; this is the half-right trap.`,`Dropping the kettle down a flight of stairs is misuse, which triggers the principle's exception and defeats the refund — even though the timing and receipt conditions are met. This is the half-right trap.`,`Correct. The breakdown came on day 12 (within 30 days), the kettle was returned with its receipt, and the faulty thermostat shows the failure was not misuse — all three conditions satisfied, so the full refund follows exactly.`,`All three conditions are satisfied — day 5, receipt returned, no misuse — yet the store refuses any refund. Denying the result when every condition is met is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`All three conditions are met, but the store grants only store credit rather than the full refund the principle requires. A weaker remedy than the rule mandates is the too-weak trap.`],generalLesson:`Multi-condition principles with exceptions demand a full checklist: test every condition and the exception separately, because each distractor is built to fail exactly one of them.`,misconceptionTags:[`multi-condition`,`exception-trigger`,`partial-credit`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`half-right`,`wrong-conclusion`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`List the principle's requirements: within 30 days, returned with receipt, and no misuse. Test each scenario against all three.`,`Each wrong scenario fails exactly one requirement — or meets all three but delivers the wrong remedy. Which requirement fails in each?`,`Only one scenario passes all three checks and grants the full refund the principle mandates.`],labels:{topic:`refund policy`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP (genuinely rigorous diff-5; model item); 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-e-180`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: Drivers must yield to pedestrians who are crossing in a marked crosswalk.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`A driver speeds through a crosswalk where a pedestrian is waiting to cross`},{text:`A driver stops for a pedestrian who is jaywalking in the middle of the block`},{text:`A driver brakes for a pedestrian who is standing on the sidewalk, not crossing`},{text:`Seeing an elderly man step into the crosswalk, a driver brakes and waves him across`},{text:`Pedestrians should wear bright clothing when walking at night`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The principle requires yielding to pedestrians crossing in a marked crosswalk. Choice (D) is the direct application: a pedestrian steps into the crosswalk and the driver yields. The other choices violate the duty, extend it to jaywalkers or non-crossing pedestrians, or state an unrelated safety tip.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle's trigger has two parts: the pedestrian is crossing, and the location is a marked crosswalk. Both must hold for the duty to apply. Choice (D) meets both: the man steps into the crosswalk (crossing, marked crosswalk), and the driver yields. Choice (A) meets the trigger but the driver speeds through — a direct violation, the opposite trap. Choice (B) involves a pedestrian crossing but not in a marked crosswalk (jaywalking mid-block); the principle's location condition fails, so yielding here is not required by this rule — the scope-shift trap extends the duty beyond its stated bounds. Choice (C) is in a crosswalk but the pedestrian is not crossing (standing on the sidewalk); the action condition fails, so the duty does not trigger. Choice (E) is an irrelevant-true safety tip about pedestrian clothing that applies the principle to nothing.`,choiceExplanations:[`The pedestrian is in the crosswalk, so the duty applies — but the driver speeds through instead of yielding. This directly violates the principle and is the opposite trap.`,`Jaywalking mid-block is not a marked crosswalk, so the principle's location condition fails. Extending the duty beyond its stated bounds is the scope-shift trap. The subject change is the giveaway: it answers a question the stimulus never asks.`,`The pedestrian is not crossing — merely standing on the sidewalk — so the principle's action condition is not met and no duty arises under this rule. This is the unsupported trap.`,`Correct. The pedestrian is crossing in a marked crosswalk, satisfying both parts of the trigger, and the driver yields — a direct application of the rule.`,`Wearing bright clothing at night may be wise, but it is unrelated to the driver's duty to yield. A true tip that performs no application is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`Two-part triggers require both parts: check the action (crossing) and the location (marked crosswalk) separately before concluding the duty applies.`,misconceptionTags:[`trigger-parts`,`scope-of-duty`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-principle-application`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The duty applies when two things are true: the pedestrian is crossing, and it is a marked crosswalk. Test each scenario against both.`,`Which scenarios change the location (jaywalking) or the action (standing on the sidewalk)?`,`Only one scenario has a pedestrian actually crossing in a marked crosswalk, with the driver yielding.`],labels:{topic:`crosswalk right-of-way`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full LR classification 2026-09-11: hostile review KEEP — two-part trigger (crossing + marked crosswalk); clean condition separation; difficulty 2 honest.`}]}],a=[{id:`lr-f-181`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`All certified organic farms avoid synthetic pesticides. Greenacre is a certified organic farm. Therefore, Greenacre avoids synthetic pesticides.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Greenacre avoids synthetic pesticides. Many farms that avoid synthetic pesticides are certified organic. Therefore, Greenacre is probably a certified organic farm.`},{text:`Most certified organic farms avoid synthetic pesticides. Greenacre is a certified organic farm. Therefore, Greenacre probably avoids synthetic pesticides.`},{text:`All certified organic farms avoid synthetic pesticides. Greenacre avoids synthetic pesticides. Therefore, all farms that avoid synthetic pesticides are certified organic.`},{text:`All commercial pilots must pass annual medical exams. Captain Reyes is a commercial pilot. Therefore, Captain Reyes must pass annual medical exams.`},{text:`Some certified organic farms avoid synthetic pesticides. Greenacre is a certified organic farm. Therefore, Greenacre avoids synthetic pesticides.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The stimulus is a categorical syllogism: All A are B; X is A; therefore X is B. Only choice (D) reproduces that exact form — all pilots must pass exams; Reyes is a pilot; so Reyes must pass. The distractors reverse the conditional, weaken "all" to "most" or "some," or restate a premise as the conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Abstract the stimulus before reading the choices. Its form is: every member of a category has a property; this individual is a member of the category; so this individual has the property. (All A are B; x is A; therefore x is B.) The correct parallel must match this form exactly — same quantifiers, same direction — while the subject matter should differ. Choice (D) matches perfectly: all commercial pilots must pass exams; Reyes is a commercial pilot; therefore Reyes must pass. Now diagnose the distractors structurally. Choice (A) affirms the consequent: from "avoids pesticides" it infers "is organic," reversing the conditional direction — the reverses-conditional trap. Choice (B) is the half-right trap: it keeps the shape but weakens "all" to "most" and the conclusion to "probably," changing the quantifier pattern. Choice (C) concludes with a universal claim ("all farms that avoid pesticides are organic") that merely flips the premise — a restated, reversed premise rather than the valid inference. Choice (E) uses "some" in the major premise but keeps the certain conclusion, a quantifier mismatch that makes the reasoning too weak to be parallel — the too-weak trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice infers category membership from the property — "avoids pesticides, so probably organic" — which reverses the stimulus's direction (property from membership). Reversing the conditional is the reverses-conditional trap.`,`This keeps the argument's shape but weakens "all" to "most" and the conclusion to "probably." A parallel must match the quantifier pattern exactly; this partial structural match is the half-right trap.`,`The conclusion here ("all farms that avoid pesticides are organic") simply reverses the premise instead of applying it to an individual. No new inference is drawn from membership; this is the restates-premise trap.`,`Correct. Abstract form: All A are B; x is A; therefore x is B. All commercial pilots must pass exams; Captain Reyes is a commercial pilot; therefore she must pass — the same categorical syllogism with different subject matter.`,`The major premise uses "some" while the conclusion stays certain, so the quantifiers do not license the inference the way "all" does. This quantifier mismatch makes the reasoning too weak to parallel the stimulus: the too-weak trap.`],generalLesson:`In parallel reasoning, diagram the abstract form first — quantifiers and direction — then demand an exact match. Subject-matter similarity is a lure; structural identity is the test.`,misconceptionTags:[`form-vs-content`,`quantifier-match`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`half-right`,`restates-premise`,`too-weak`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-structure`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Strip away the farming details: what is the abstract pattern — "All A are B; x is A; therefore ..."?`,`Which choices change a quantifier (all to most or some) or run the inference in reverse?`,`The correct choice applies a universal rule to a category member with completely different subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`organic farm syllogism`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-f-182`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (B) performs modus tollens; (A) affirms consequent, (C) denies antecedent. Difficulty d3 honest for conditional twins.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:4,distractorProximity:4},stimulus:`If the reservoir's dam had been properly maintained, the spillway would not have cracked. The spillway did crack. So the dam was not properly maintained.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`If the dam had been properly maintained, the spillway would not have cracked. The spillway never cracked. So the dam was properly maintained.`},{text:`If the alarm had been armed, the siren would have sounded. The siren never sounded. Therefore, the alarm had not been armed.`},{text:`If the dam had been properly maintained, the spillway would not have cracked. The dam was not properly maintained. So the spillway cracked.`},{text:`The spillway cracked because of the unprecedented flood, not because of poor maintenance.`},{text:`The dam was poorly maintained, the spillway cracked, and so proper maintenance generally prevents spillway cracks.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The stimulus is modus tollens: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P. Choice (B) reproduces it exactly — if the alarm had been armed the siren would have sounded; it did not; so the alarm was not armed. The distractors affirm the consequent, deny the antecedent, offer a rival cause, or generalize loosely.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Formalize the stimulus: "If maintained, then no crack" plus "crack occurred" yields "not maintained." That is modus tollens — denying the consequent to deny the antecedent. The parallel must use the same move with different content. Choice (B): if armed, the siren would have sounded; the siren never sounded; therefore the alarm was not armed. Same form, same validity. Choice (A) is the classic affirming-the-consequent trap: no crack, therefore maintained — it treats the absence of the consequent as proof of the antecedent, reversing the valid direction. Choice (C) denies the antecedent: not maintained, therefore cracked — but "if maintained then no crack" does not license inferring a crack from poor maintenance; that is the nec-suff-confusion trap (treating a sufficient condition as necessary). Choice (D) abandons conditional reasoning for a causal claim about floods — same topic, different reasoning entirely, the causal-alternative trap. Choice (E) strings the facts into a loose generalization rather than a deductive conditional inference — a partial, weaker match, the half-right trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`From "no crack" this infers "maintained" — affirming the consequent. The stimulus instead denies the consequent to deny the antecedent, so this runs the conditional in reverse: the reverses-conditional trap.`,`Correct. If P then Q; not Q; therefore not P — modus tollens. If the alarm had been armed the siren would have sounded; it did not; so the alarm was not armed: the identical abstract structure.`,`From "not maintained" this infers "cracked" — denying the antecedent. The original conditional does not support that move; it confuses a sufficient condition for maintenance with a necessary one: the nec-suff-confusion trap.`,`This offers a rival causal explanation (the flood) instead of reproducing the conditional inference. Same topic, completely different reasoning: the causal-alternative trap. As a rival explanation it belongs to the critic, not to the parallel — it does not reproduce the original flaw.`,`This merely strings the observations into a loose generalization rather than performing the denying-the-consequent deduction. It is close in content but not in form: the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`Modus tollens (if P then Q; not Q; so not P) has two evil twins — affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent. In parallel items, check which conditional move each choice actually makes.`,misconceptionTags:[`modus-tollens`,`conditional-twins`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`causal-alternative`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Write the stimulus as: If P then Q. Not Q. Therefore...? What is that argument form called?`,`Which choices deny the consequent, and which instead affirm it or deny the antecedent?`,`The correct choice keeps the "if P then Q; but not Q; so not P" skeleton with entirely new subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`dam spillway conditional`}},{id:`lr-f-183`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (E) preserves all/some/some quantifier pattern. Difficulty d2 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`Every novel on the prize shortlist was reviewed by at least three judges. Some novels by first-time authors are on the shortlist. It follows that some novels by first-time authors were reviewed by at least three judges.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Every shortlisted novel was reviewed by three judges, and every novel reviewed by three judges won critical praise. So every shortlisted novel won critical praise.`},{text:`Some shortlisted novels were reviewed by three judges, and some first-time novels are on the shortlist. So some first-time novels were reviewed by three judges.`},{text:`First-time authors rarely make the shortlist, so their novels are seldom reviewed by prize judges.`},{text:`Every shortlisted novel was reviewed by three judges. Some first-time novels were reviewed by three judges. So some first-time novels are on the shortlist.`},{text:`All licensed daycares employ staff trained in CPR. Some daycares in the county are licensed. Therefore, some daycares in the county employ staff trained in CPR.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The stimulus pattern is: All A are B; some C are A; therefore some C are B. Choice (E) matches exactly — all licensed daycares employ CPR-trained staff; some county daycares are licensed; so some employ CPR-trained staff. The distractors chain two universals, use two particulars, reverse the inference, or abandon the form for a topic-level generalization.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Abstract the quantifier pattern: a universal premise (every shortlisted novel was reviewed), a particular premise (some first-time novels are shortlisted), and a particular conclusion (some first-time novels were reviewed). Form: All A are B; some C are A; therefore some C are B. The parallel must preserve both the quantifiers and the direction. Choice (E) does: all licensed daycares employ CPR-trained staff; some county daycares are licensed; therefore some county daycares employ CPR-trained staff. Choice (A) chains two universals to a universal conclusion — valid, but a different form (no particular premise or conclusion), so it is only a partial structural match: the half-right trap. Choice (B) uses "some" in both premises; two particular premises cannot validly yield the conclusion, so the quantifier pattern is weakened — the too-weak trap. Choice (C) keeps the topic but performs no syllogistic inference at all, just a loose generalization — the unsupported trap. Choice (D) reverses the direction: from "some first-time novels were reviewed" it infers shortlist membership, running the inference backwards — the reverses-conditional trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This chains two universal premises to a universal conclusion — a valid syllogism, but without the particular ("some") premise and conclusion that define the stimulus's form. Valid yet structurally different is the half-right trap.`,`Both premises use "some," which cannot validly support the conclusion the way a universal-plus-particular pair can. Weakening the quantifier pattern breaks the parallel: the too-weak trap.`,`This stays on the topic of first-time authors but performs no quantifier inference at all — just a vague generalization. Same subject, no matching structure: the unsupported trap.`,`From "some first-time novels were reviewed" this infers "some are on the shortlist," running the stimulus's inference in reverse (reviewed → shortlisted instead of shortlisted → reviewed). This is the reverses-conditional trap.`,`Correct. All A are B (all licensed daycares employ CPR-trained staff); some C are A (some county daycares are licensed); therefore some C are B — the identical quantifier pattern with new subject matter.`],generalLesson:`Quantifier patterns must match exactly: "all + some → some" is a different form from "all + all → all" or "some + some → some." Track every quantifier when abstracting.`,misconceptionTags:[`quantifier-match`,`form-vs-content`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`too-weak`,`unsupported`,`reverses-conditional`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are the quantifiers in each premise and the conclusion — "every," "some," "some"?`,`Which choices preserve that exact all/some/some pattern, and which weaken a quantifier or chain two universals?`,`The correct choice uses completely different subject matter but the identical quantifier skeleton.`],labels:{topic:`prize shortlist quantifiers`}},{id:`lr-f-184`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-deduction`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (A) eliminates a disjunct; (B) affirms instead of eliminating. Difficulty d3 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`The outage was caused either by the storm or by equipment failure. Engineers have ruled out the storm. Hence equipment failure caused the outage.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The manuscript was written either in Lisbon or in Goa. Paper analysis rules out Goa. So it was written in Lisbon.`},{text:`The outage was caused either by the storm or by equipment failure. It was the storm. So equipment failure played no role.`},{text:`The outage was caused by the storm, and storms cause outages, so the storm caused this outage.`},{text:`Storms have been growing more severe each decade, so both the storm and aging equipment may have contributed.`},{text:`Neither the storm nor equipment failure has been confirmed, so the cause of the outage remains unknown.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The stimulus is disjunctive syllogism: P or Q; not P; therefore Q. Choice (A) matches exactly — Lisbon or Goa; not Goa; therefore Lisbon. The distractors affirm a disjunct, argue circularly, propose joint causation, or suspend judgment.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The abstract form: two alternatives are exhaustive, one is eliminated, so the other is concluded. (P or Q; not P; therefore Q.) Choice (A) is the clean parallel: Lisbon or Goa; Goa ruled out; therefore Lisbon — same elimination structure, new content. Choice (B) is the trap that catches inattentive readers: it affirms one disjunct ("it was the storm") and concludes the other played no role. But eliminating an alternative requires ruling it out, not merely asserting its rival — with an inclusive "or," both could be true, so the conclusion overreaches: the too-strong trap. Choice (C) is circular: it restates "the storm caused it" as both premise and conclusion without any elimination step — the restates-premise trap. Choice (D) keeps the topic but replaces elimination with speculation about joint causation, a different reasoning move entirely — the causal-alternative trap. Choice (E) refuses to conclude anything, which is the opposite of the stimulus's decisive elimination — the opposite trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Either Lisbon or Goa; Goa eliminated; therefore Lisbon — the disjunctive syllogism (P or Q; not P; therefore Q) with entirely new subject matter.`,`This affirms one alternative ("it was the storm") rather than eliminating it, then overreaches by concluding the other played no role — but "or" may be inclusive. Affirming instead of eliminating, with an overstated conclusion, is the too-strong trap.`,`The premise already asserts the storm caused the outage, so the "argument" just repeats its conclusion as a premise. With no elimination step performed, this is the restates-premise trap.`,`This stays on the storm topic but substitutes joint-causation speculation for the eliminate-one-alternative structure. Same subject, different reasoning: the causal-alternative trap. As a rival explanation it belongs to the critic, not to the parallel — it does not reproduce the original flaw.`,`This suspends judgment ("cause remains unknown") instead of concluding the remaining alternative — the reverse of the stimulus's decisive inference. This is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Disjunctive syllogism requires eliminating an alternative, not merely asserting its rival. Watch for choices that affirm one disjunct and pretend the other is ruled out.`,misconceptionTags:[`disjunctive-syllogism`,`elimination-vs-affirmation`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`restates-premise`,`causal-alternative`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-structure`,`f-deduction`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the argument's move — does it prove one alternative, or rule one out?`,`Which choices actually eliminate an alternative, and which just assert one?`,`The correct choice presents two options, eliminates one with evidence, and concludes the other.`],labels:{topic:`power outage disjunction`}},{id:`lr-f-185`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`If the mountain pass is closed, the team buses through the tunnel. If the team buses through the tunnel, practice starts late. So if the pass is closed, practice starts late.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`If the pass is closed, the team buses through the tunnel. Practice starts late. So if the team buses through the tunnel, the pass is closed.`},{text:`If it rains, the field is wet. If the field is wet, the game is canceled. So if the game is canceled, it rained.`},{text:`If the budget passes, the library extends its hours. If the library extends its hours, new staff are hired. So if the budget passes, new staff are hired.`},{text:`If the budget passes, the library extends its hours. The library extended its hours. So the budget passed.`},{text:`Pass closures always delay practice, since the team must take the tunnel.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The stimulus chains two conditionals: if A then B; if B then C; so if A then C. Choice (C) does exactly that with the library budget. The distractors reverse the chain, affirm the consequent, or compress the reasoning into a topic-level summary.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The form is the hypothetical syllogism — a conditional chain: A → B, B → C, therefore A → C. The middle term (busing through the tunnel) links the two premises, and the conclusion connects the ends. Choice (C) reproduces it: budget passes → hours extend; hours extend → staff hired; so budget passes → staff hired. Same skeleton, new content. Choice (A) scrambles the chain: it keeps the first conditional but concludes with the reversed conditional (tunnel → pass closed) — the reverses-conditional trap. Choice (B) builds a proper chain but then concludes with its converse (canceled → rained) instead of the forward link — the wrong-conclusion trap, since the valid move was available but a different inference was drawn. Choice (D) affirms the consequent of a single conditional (hours extended, so budget passed), confusing a sufficient condition with a necessary one — the nec-suff-confusion trap. Choice (E) stays on topic but offers no chained inference at all, just a summary claim — the restates-premise trap in spirit: it reports the upshot without reproducing the reasoning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This keeps one conditional but concludes with its converse — "if the team buses through the tunnel, the pass is closed" — running the chain backwards instead of linking it forwards. This is the reverses-conditional trap.`,`A proper chain is built (rain → wet → canceled) but the conclusion drawn is the converse ("if canceled, it rained") rather than the valid forward link. Drawing a different, invalid inference is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`Correct. If the budget passes, hours extend; if hours extend, staff are hired; therefore if the budget passes, staff are hired — the conditional chain (A → B; B → C; so A → C) with new subject matter.`,`From "the library extended its hours" this infers "the budget passed" — affirming the consequent of a single conditional. It treats the sufficient condition as if it were necessary: the nec-suff-confusion trap.`,`This summarizes the topic without chaining any conditionals. Reporting the gist instead of reproducing the inference is the restates-premise trap. Repeating a premise adds no rule and performs no inference, so it cannot do the justificatory work the stem demands.`],generalLesson:`A conditional chain must be linked forward (A → B → C yields A → C). Any choice that concludes with a converse or affirms a consequent has broken the chain.`,misconceptionTags:[`conditional-chain`,`converse-error`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`wrong-conclusion`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Link the two "if" statements: what connects them, and what does the conclusion connect?`,`Which choices link the chain forward, and which conclude with a backwards "if"?`,`The correct choice chains two fresh conditionals through a shared middle term to a forward conclusion.`],labels:{topic:`mountain pass conditional chain`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-f-186`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-numbers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3,abstraction:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Ninety percent of the building's tenants renew their leases. Maya is a tenant in the building. So Maya will probably renew her lease.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Ninety percent of the building's tenants renew their leases. Maya renewed her lease. So Maya is a tenant in the building.`},{text:`Half of the building's tenants renew their leases. Maya is a tenant in the building. So Maya will renew her lease.`},{text:`Ninety percent of the building's tenants renew their leases. Maya is a tenant in the building. So Maya will definitely renew her lease.`},{text:`Lease renewal rates are rising because the landlord has kept rents low.`},{text:`Eighty-five percent of marathon registrants finish the race. Luis registered for the marathon. So Luis will probably finish the race.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The stimulus is a statistical syllogism: a high percentage of a group has a trait; this individual belongs to the group; so the individual probably has the trait. Choice (E) matches the percentage, the membership, and the qualified "probably." The distractors reverse the inference, weaken the percentage, overstate the conclusion, or abandon the form for a causal claim.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Abstract carefully, because three features must match: (1) a high statistical premise about the group, (2) membership of the individual in that group, (3) a probabilistically qualified conclusion ("probably"). Choice (E) has all three: 85% of registrants finish; Luis registered; so Luis will probably finish. Now check each distractor against those three features. Choice (A) keeps the statistic but reverses the inference — from "renewed" it concludes membership — running the statistical syllogism backwards: the reverses-conditional trap. Choice (B) drops the percentage to 50%, at which "will renew" (unqualified) no longer follows; the statistical strength no longer supports even a probable conclusion, making it too weak to be parallel: the too-weak trap. Choice (C) keeps the 90% but upgrades "probably" to "definitely" — a high percentage never licenses certainty, so the conclusion overreaches: the too-strong trap. Choice (D) keeps the topic but replaces the statistical inference with a causal explanation of the rate itself — different reasoning entirely: the causal-alternative trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This infers group membership from the trait ("renewed, so is a tenant") instead of the trait from membership — the statistical inference run in reverse. This is the reverses-conditional trap.`,`With only half the group renewing, even a qualified conclusion would be shaky, and this choice draws an unqualified one. The statistical premise is too weak to support the inference: the too-weak trap.`,`Ninety percent supports "probably," never "definitely." Upgrading a probabilistic conclusion to certainty overstates what the statistic licenses: the too-strong trap. Its excessive strength is precisely what makes it tempting — and what disqualifies it, since the argument never claims that much.`,`This explains why renewal rates are rising (low rents) instead of inferring anything about an individual tenant. Same topic, different reasoning move: the causal-alternative trap.`,`Correct. A high percentage of the group has the trait (85% finish), the individual belongs to the group (Luis registered), and the conclusion is probabilistically qualified (will probably finish) — the same statistical syllogism.`],generalLesson:`Statistical parallels must match all three features: the strength of the statistic, the direction of the inference, and the qualification of the conclusion. "Probably" is load-bearing.`,misconceptionTags:[`statistical-syllogism`,`probability-qualification`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`too-weak`,`too-strong`,`causal-alternative`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-numbers`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What three features define this inference: the percentage, the individual's membership, and how the conclusion is qualified?`,`Which choices reverse the inference, change the percentage, or upgrade "probably" to certainty?`,`The correct choice keeps a high percentage, a group member, and a "probably" conclusion — with new subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`lease renewal statistics`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-f-187`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (B) rules out a rival cause with evidence; (C) merely affirms the winner. Difficulty d3 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:2},stimulus:`The orchard's poor harvest was due either to the late frost or to the new pesticide. But neighboring orchards that skipped the pesticide also had poor harvests. So the frost, not the pesticide, caused the poor harvest.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The harvest was poor, and there was a late frost, so the frost must have caused the poor harvest.`},{text:`The stain came from the red wine or the rusty pipe. The pipe was replaced last year and shows no rust. So the wine caused the stain.`},{text:`The delay was caused by traffic or by the faulty alarm. She hit heavy traffic, so the alarm did not cause the delay.`},{text:`Late frosts have been arriving earlier in the season over the past decade.`},{text:`The harvest was poor; therefore the new pesticide must have been defective.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The stimulus lists two candidate causes, eliminates one with evidence (orchards without the pesticide still suffered), and concludes the other caused the outcome. Choice (B) does the same: wine or rusty pipe; the pipe is sound; so the wine caused the stain. The distractors leap to a cause without elimination, affirm a cause without ruling out the rival, or abandon the structure.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The abstract pattern is causal elimination: two candidate causes are named, evidence rules one out, and the remaining candidate is concluded to be the cause. The elimination evidence is the load-bearing step — the stimulus uses a comparison (orchards without the pesticide still had poor harvests). Choice (B) reproduces the pattern: wine or rusty pipe; the pipe was replaced and shows no rust (elimination); so the wine caused the stain. Choice (A) is the tempting shortcut: it names the frost and concludes causation from mere correlation, skipping the elimination step entirely — the unsupported trap, since the stimulus's whole point is the ruling-out. Choice (C) is the subtler half-right trap: it affirms one candidate (traffic) and declares the other innocent, but affirming traffic does not eliminate the alarm — the elimination must target the rejected cause, not merely assert the winner. Choice (D) keeps the frost topic but offers a trend observation with no causal reasoning — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) picks a cause with no elimination of the rival at all — the wrong-conclusion trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This jumps from "there was a frost" to "the frost caused it" with no elimination of the pesticide — the very step that defines the stimulus's reasoning. Leaping without the ruling-out is the unsupported trap.`,`Correct. Two candidate causes (wine or rusty pipe); evidence eliminates one (the pipe is sound); the other is concluded to be the cause — the same causal-elimination structure with new subject matter.`,`This asserts one cause (traffic) without eliminating the other (the alarm) — affirming a candidate is not the same as ruling out its rival. This partial structural match is the half-right trap.`,`A trend about frost timing is topically related but performs no causal elimination at all. True or not, it does not reproduce the reasoning: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`This concludes the pesticide was defective without any evidence eliminating the frost — the rival cause is never addressed. Concluding without the elimination step is the wrong-conclusion trap.`],generalLesson:`Causal elimination requires ruling out the rival, not merely asserting the winner. The evidence that eliminates is the step a parallel must reproduce.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-elimination`,`ruling-out`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-conclusion`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-causation`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the argument's key move — does it prove the frost caused the loss, or rule out the pesticide?`,`Which choices actually eliminate a rival cause with evidence, versus just asserting a favorite?`,`The correct choice names two candidates, rules one out, and concludes the other — with new subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`orchard harvest causes`}},{id:`lr-f-188`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (D) projects a property across two systems on relevant respects. Difficulty d2 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`A city's water system is like its circulatory system: treatment plants act as kidneys, filtering contaminants. Just as kidney failure would poison the body, a treatment-plant failure would poison the city's water.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The city’s water system is aging, and aging infrastructure eventually fails, requiring costly replacement.`},{text:`The human body is like a city: cities need police patrols, so the body must need police patrols too, to stay healthy.`},{text:`Bodies and cities are fundamentally different, so comparing a water system to a circulatory system proves nothing about either on its own.`},{text:`A beehive is like a factory whose delivery trucks are forager bees: a truckers’ strike halts the factory, and a forager collapse halts honey production.`},{text:`Treatment plants need regular maintenance to keep functioning properly and avoid service interruptions.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The stimulus reasons by analogy: two systems share relevant structure (filtering organs), so what holds for one (failure poisons) holds for the other. Choice (D) mirrors it — beehive as factory, foragers as trucks, collapse halting production. The distractors generalize, extend the analogy absurdly, attack the analogy, or state an unrelated truth.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The abstract form is analogical reasoning: X is like Y in specified relevant respects (filtering components); Y has a further property (failure of the filter poisons the whole); therefore X has the analogous property. Choice (D) reproduces it: a beehive is like a factory (relevant respect: delivery of raw materials); a truckers' strike halts the factory; so forager collapse halts honey production. Choice (A) abandons analogy for a general claim about aging infrastructure — no comparison is drawn: the unsupported trap. Choice (B) is the tempting overextension: it takes the analogy form but maps an irrelevant feature (police patrols have no analogue in a body), producing an absurd conclusion — the scope-shift trap, since the relevant-respects condition is violated. Choice (C) argues against analogical reasoning rather than performing it — the opposite trap. Choice (E) is topically related but contains no analogy at all — the irrelevant-true trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This makes a general claim about aging infrastructure without drawing any comparison between two systems. With no analogy performed, it cannot parallel analogical reasoning: the unsupported trap.`,`This copies the analogy form but maps an irrelevant feature — police patrols have no bodily analogue — producing absurdity. An analogy extended beyond the relevant respects is the scope-shift trap.`,`This attacks analogical reasoning instead of performing it. Arguing that the comparison proves nothing is the reverse of the stimulus's move: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Correct. Two systems compared on relevant structure (hive/factory, foragers/trucks); a property of one (a delivery halt stops production) is projected onto the other — the same analogical inference with new subject matter.`,`That treatment plants need maintenance may be true, but it involves no comparison between systems. A topically related truth without the reasoning form is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`Analogical parallels must compare two systems on relevant respects and project a property across. Choices that generalize, attack, or absurdly overextend the analogy all fail the form.`,misconceptionTags:[`analogy-form`,`relevant-respects`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the argument's move: comparing two systems and projecting what happens when a part fails?`,`Which choice performs a comparison, and which ones generalize, attack, or stretch the comparison absurdly?`,`The correct choice compares two new systems on relevant structure and projects a failure consequence across.`],labels:{topic:`water system analogy`}},{id:`lr-f-189`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,stimulus:`No reptiles are warm-blooded. All lizards are reptiles. Therefore, no lizards are warm-blooded.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`No commercial flights allow smoking. All red-eye flights are commercial flights. So no red-eye flights allow smoking.`},{text:`No reptiles are warm-blooded. All warm-blooded animals are mammals. Therefore, no reptiles are mammals.`},{text:`All lizards are reptiles. No lizards are warm-blooded. Therefore, no reptiles are warm-blooded.`},{text:`Most reptiles are cold-blooded. All lizards are reptiles. Therefore, no lizards are warm-blooded.`},{text:`Lizards bask in the sun each morning to raise their body temperature.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The stimulus form is: No A are B; all C are A; therefore no C are B. Choice (A) matches exactly — no commercial flights allow smoking; all red-eyes are commercial flights; so no red-eyes allow smoking. The distractors chain the exclusion differently, reverse the inference, weaken "no" to "most," or abandon argument for fact.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a demanding abstraction because the negative quantifier ("no") interacts with the universal ("all") in a specific order: the exclusion applies to the larger category (reptiles), and the subset (lizards) inherits the exclusion. Form: No A are B; all C are A; therefore no C are B. Choice (A) preserves the order: no commercial flights allow smoking (exclusion on the large category); all red-eye flights are commercial flights (subset); therefore no red-eye flights allow smoking. Choice (B) is the engineered near-miss: it is valid and looks similar, but it chains two exclusions (no A are B; all B are C; so no A are C) — a different arrangement of the terms, making it the half-right trap. Choice (C) reverses the inference: from "no lizards are warm-blooded" and "all lizards are reptiles" it concludes "no reptiles are warm-blooded" — but the exclusion cannot be projected upward from subset to superset; that is the wrong-conclusion trap. Choice (D) weakens "no" to "most" in the first premise while keeping the absolute "no" conclusion — the quantifier no longer licenses the inference: the too-strong trap. Choice (E) is a true biological fact with no argument structure at all — the irrelevant-true trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. No A are B (no commercial flights allow smoking); all C are A (all red-eye flights are commercial flights); therefore no C are B — the exclusion inherited by the subset, exactly the stimulus's form.`,`This is valid but structurally different: it chains two exclusions (no A are B; all B are C) rather than applying one exclusion to a subset. Close in validity, wrong in arrangement: the half-right trap.`,`This projects the exclusion upward — from "no lizards" to "no reptiles" — but an exclusion on a subset cannot be extended to the whole category. Reversing the valid direction is the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`"Most" in the premise cannot support "no" in the conclusion; the weakened quantifier breaks the inference while the conclusion stays absolute. This is the too-strong trap.`,`A true fact about lizard behavior with no premises and no inference. Without any argument structure to compare, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`With negative quantifiers, the direction of inheritance is everything: an exclusion flows from a category down to its subsets, never upward. Map which term carries the "no."`,misconceptionTags:[`negative-quantifier`,`inheritance-direction`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`wrong-conclusion`,`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which category carries the "no," and which direction does the exclusion travel — from larger group to subgroup, or the reverse?`,`Which choices keep "no" on the larger category and inherit it downward, versus projecting it upward or weakening it to "most"?`,`The correct choice applies a "no" rule about a big category to a named subgroup of it.`],labels:{topic:`reptile syllogism`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL (instructional use only - prose/structure too artificial for assessment; difficulty corrected d5->d3); leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-f-190`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (C) preserves the particular-negative conclusion; (D) overreaches to 'no'. Difficulty d3 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`Every certified referee passed the fitness test. Some veteran officials never passed the fitness test. Consequently, some veteran officials are not certified referees.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Every certified referee passed the fitness test. Some veteran officials are certified referees. So some veteran officials passed the fitness test.`},{text:`Some veteran officials never passed the fitness test, and some people who never passed are certified referees. So some veteran officials are not certified referees.`},{text:`All organic apples are grown without synthetic pesticides. Some apples at the market were grown with synthetic pesticides. Therefore, some apples at the market are not organic.`},{text:`Every certified referee passed the fitness test. Some veteran officials never passed the fitness test. So no veteran official is a certified referee.`},{text:`Some veteran officials are certified referees even though they never passed the fitness test.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The form is: All B are C; some A are not C; therefore some A are not C... precisely: every certified referee passed; some veterans did not pass; so some veterans are not certified. Choice (C) matches — all organic apples are pesticide-free; some market apples are not; so some market apples are not organic. The distractors change the quantifiers, overstate to "no," or contradict a premise.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Abstract the form: a universal affirmative (all certified referees passed), a particular negative (some veterans never passed), and a particular negative conclusion (some veterans are not certified). Form: All B are C; some A are not C; therefore some A are not B. The logic: anyone lacking C cannot be B. Choice (C) reproduces it: all organic apples are grown without synthetic pesticides; some market apples were grown with them; therefore some market apples are not organic. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: it uses the same topic and valid reasoning, but its form is all/some → some affirmative — a different quantifier pattern. Choice (B) builds the conclusion from two particular premises ("some never passed" and "some who never passed are certified"), which cannot validly yield it — the too-weak trap. Choice (D) keeps the premises but upgrades "some are not" to "no veteran is" — the premises rule out only those veterans who never passed, so the universal negative overreaches: the too-strong trap. Choice (E) directly contradicts the first premise (certified referees who never passed), so it cannot parallel an argument whose form depends on that premise — the opposite trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is valid and topical, but its pattern is all/some → some affirmative ("some veterans passed"), not the stimulus's particular-negative conclusion. Same topic, different quantifier form: the half-right trap.`,`Two "some" premises cannot validly support the conclusion — the argument needs the universal premise to do the work. This weakened quantifier structure is the too-weak trap.`,`Correct. All B are C (all organic apples are pesticide-free); some A are not C (some market apples were grown with pesticides); therefore some A are not B — the identical particular-negative inference.`,`The premises show only that the veterans who never passed cannot be certified — not that no veteran is certified. Upgrading "some are not" to "none are" overreaches: the too-strong trap.`,`This asserts the existence of certified referees who never passed, contradicting the stimulus's universal premise. A choice that denies a load-bearing premise is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Particular-negative conclusions ("some are not") come from universal premises plus a particular negative — and they stay particular. Never let "some are not" become "none are."`,misconceptionTags:[`particular-negative`,`quantifier-scope`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`too-weak`,`too-strong`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-quantifiers`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are the quantifiers: "every," "some ... never," and what kind of conclusion?`,`Which choices preserve the "some are not" conclusion, versus upgrading it to "none" or flipping it affirmative?`,`The correct choice applies a universal rule and a particular exception to reach a "some are not" conclusion about new subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`referee fitness test`}},{id:`lr-f-191`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Unless the committee approves the budget, the festival will be canceled. The committee did not approve the budget. Therefore, the festival will be canceled.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Unless the committee approves the budget, the festival will be canceled. The festival was canceled. So the committee did not approve the budget.`},{text:`Unless the committee approves the budget, the festival will be canceled. The committee approved the budget. So the festival will not be canceled.`},{text:`Budget disputes often lead to canceled festivals in this region.`},{text:`Unless it rains, the outdoor game will be played. It rained. So the game will be played.`},{text:`The crops will fail unless the rains come. The rains did not come. So the crops will fail.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`"Unless P, Q" means if not P then Q. The stimulus: if the committee does not approve, the festival is canceled; the committee did not approve; so it is canceled. Choice (E) matches — if the rains do not come the crops fail; they did not; so they fail. The distractors affirm the consequent, deny the antecedent, generalize, or mis-negate.`,explanationWalkthrough:`First translate: "Unless the committee approves, the festival will be canceled" = "If the committee does NOT approve, the festival will be canceled." The stimulus then asserts the "not approve" condition and concludes cancellation — a straightforward modus ponens on the translated conditional. Choice (E) does the same: "The crops will fail unless the rains come" = "If the rains do NOT come, the crops fail"; the rains did not come; so the crops will fail. Choice (A) affirms the consequent: from "canceled" it infers "did not approve" — the reverses-conditional trap. Choice (B) denies the antecedent: from "approved" it infers "not canceled" — but the conditional only covers the not-approved case, so this confuses sufficient with necessary: the nec-suff-confusion trap. Choice (C) abandons conditional reasoning for a regional generalization — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (D) is the negation trap: "unless it rains, the game will be played" = "if it does NOT rain, the game is played"; rain occurred, which satisfies neither the translated antecedent nor its negation usefully — concluding "the game will be played" is simply the wrong conclusion from a mis-negation.`,choiceExplanations:[`From "the festival was canceled" this infers "the committee did not approve" — affirming the consequent of the translated conditional. The stimulus moves the other direction: the reverses-conditional trap.`,`From "the committee approved" this infers "not canceled," but the conditional only says what follows from non-approval. Treating the sufficient condition as necessary is the nec-suff-confusion trap.`,`A regional generalization about budget disputes performs no conditional inference at all. Topically related but structurally empty: the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Properly translated, the premise says the game is played if it does NOT rain; rain occurred, so nothing follows — yet the choice concludes the game will be played. Mis-negating "unless" yields the wrong-conclusion trap.`,`Correct. "Unless the rains come, the crops fail" = if the rains do not come, the crops fail; the rains did not come; so the crops fail — the same translated modus ponens with new subject matter.`],generalLesson:`Always translate "unless" before comparing: "Unless P, Q" = "If not P, then Q." Most errors in unless-parallels come from skipping that translation step.`,misconceptionTags:[`unless-translation`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`nec-suff-confusion`,`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-conclusion`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-translate`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate "unless" first: "Unless P, Q" is equivalent to which "if-then" statement?`,`Once translated, is the stimulus affirming the antecedent, denying the consequent, or something else?`,`The correct choice translates its "unless" the same way and makes the same valid move.`],labels:{topic:`festival budget unless`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-f-192`,version:2,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-reasoning`,secondarySkills:[`f-numbers`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Difficulty corrected d4->d3: proportional reasoning is elementary arithmetic; the parallel-matching work is moderate, not d4. No other changes; single defensible answer confirmed.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:2,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`The paint mix requires 3 parts blue to 1 part white. The crew used 12 gallons of white. So they must have used 36 gallons of blue.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to that exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The paint mix requires 3 parts blue to 1 part white. The crew used 12 gallons of blue. So they must have used 36 gallons of white.`},{text:`The recipe calls for 2 cups of flour for every 1 cup of sugar. She used 6 cups of sugar. Therefore, she used 12 cups of flour.`},{text:`The paint mix requires 3 parts blue to 1 part white. The crew used 12 gallons of white. So they used 15 gallons of paint in total.`},{text:`The paint mix requires 3 parts blue to 1 part white. The crew used about 12 gallons of white. So they used about 36 gallons of blue.`},{text:`Blue paint fades faster than white paint when exposed to sunlight.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The stimulus applies a fixed ratio (3:1) to a known quantity of one component to compute the other (12 × 3 = 36). Choice (B) does the same: a 2:1 flour-to-sugar ratio with 6 cups of sugar yields 12 cups of flour. The distractors reverse the ratio, compute a different quantity, hedge with "about," or state an unrelated fact.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The abstract operation: given a ratio R of X to Y and an exact amount of Y, compute the exact amount of X by multiplication. The parallel must preserve the ratio direction (which component is scaled), the exactness, and the target of the computation. Choice (B): 2:1 flour to sugar, 6 cups of sugar known, so 12 cups of flour — identical operation. Choice (A) is the ratio-reversal trap: it scales the wrong component, computing 36 gallons of white from 12 of blue — the same numbers, the inverse inference. Choice (C) is the half-right trap: it uses the ratio and the known quantity but computes a different target (the total, 15 gallons) rather than the scaled component — right ingredients, wrong question answered. Choice (D) hedges both the input and the output with "about," weakening exact proportional reasoning into estimation — the too-weak trap. Choice (E) is a topically related paint fact with no proportional reasoning at all — the irrelevant-true trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This scales the wrong component: from 12 gallons of blue it computes white, reversing the 3:1 direction. Same numbers, inverse inference: the reverses-conditional trap (ratio reversal).`,`Correct. A fixed 2:1 ratio, an exact known quantity of the unit component (6 cups of sugar), and the computed quantity of the scaled component (12 cups of flour) — the same proportional operation.`,`This uses the ratio correctly but answers a different question — the total gallons rather than the gallons of blue. Right materials, wrong target: the answers-different-question trap.`,`Adding "about" to both the known quantity and the conclusion turns exact proportional computation into rough estimation. Hedging the precision the stimulus relies on is the too-weak trap.`,`A true claim about paint fading with no ratio and no computation. Topically related but structurally empty: the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`Proportional parallels must match the ratio's direction, the exactness of the quantities, and what is being computed. Reversing which component is known breaks the parallel even when the numbers look the same.`,misconceptionTags:[`ratio-direction`,`proportional-reasoning`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`answers-different-question`,`too-weak`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-reasoning`,`f-numbers`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the argument actually computing: given a ratio and one known amount, what does it solve for?`,`Which choices reverse which amount is known, answer a different question (like the total), or hedge with "about"?`,`The correct choice applies a stated ratio to an exact known quantity to compute the matching component — with new numbers and subject matter.`],labels:{topic:`paint ratio proportion`}},{id:`lr-f-193`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (C) dismisses a claim via the claimant's conduct. Difficulty d2 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`We should reject Councilor Vance's traffic proposal — not because of anything wrong with its details, but because Vance was fined for reckless driving twice last year.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Vance's proposal is flawed because it ignores pedestrian safety at busy intersections.`},{text:`Vance's proposal is bad, and since Vance is a bad driver, voters should elect more responsible councilors.`},{text:`You should not trust Dr. Rao's diet advice — she was photographed eating a donut last week.`},{text:`Reckless driving fines in this city have doubled over the past year.`},{text:`Vance's proposal should be rejected because the city's traffic engineers have criticized it.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The stimulus commits the circumstantial ad hominem: it rejects a proposal because of the proposer's personal conduct instead of engaging the proposal's merits. Choice (C) does the same — dismissing diet advice because of a donut photo. The distractors criticize substance, add extra conclusions, state facts, or commit a different flaw (appeal to authority).`,explanationWalkthrough:`Name the flaw first: the argument rejects Vance's traffic proposal solely because Vance personally drives recklessly. Nothing is said about the proposal's details — the arguer is attacked instead of the argument. That is the circumstantial ad hominem fallacy. The parallel must attack a person's circumstances or character to dismiss their claim, without touching the claim's merits. Choice (C): Dr. Rao's diet advice is dismissed because she was seen eating a donut — the person's behavior substitutes for engagement with the advice. Choice (A) is the opposite trap: it criticizes the proposal's substance (pedestrian safety), which is legitimate reasoning, not the flaw. Choice (B) is the half-right trap: it contains an ad hominem element but redirects to a different conclusion (elect better councilors), so its overall move differs. Choice (D) is a bare statistical fact with no argument at all — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) commits a different flaw, appeal to authority (engineers criticized it), rather than attacking Vance personally — the answers-different-question trap, since the flaw type does not match.`,choiceExplanations:[`This criticizes the proposal's content — pedestrian safety — which is legitimate engagement with the merits, not a personal attack. Sound reasoning cannot parallel a fallacy: the opposite trap.`,`This contains a personal swipe but builds toward a different conclusion about electing councilors rather than rejecting the proposal on personal grounds. The overall move differs: the half-right trap.`,`Correct. The diet advice is dismissed because of the advisor's personal behavior (a donut photo) rather than any flaw in the advice — the same circumstantial ad hominem: attack the arguer, ignore the argument.`,`A statistical fact about fines with no claim and no inference. With no reasoning to compare, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`This rejects the proposal by citing the engineers' criticism — an appeal to authority, a different flaw from the personal attack in the stimulus. Wrong flaw type: the answers-different-question trap.`],generalLesson:`Ad hominem parallels must dismiss a claim via the claimant's character or circumstances. Choices that engage the merits — or commit a different fallacy — are not parallel even when the topic matches.`,misconceptionTags:[`ad-hominem`,`flaw-type-match`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the argument attack — the proposal's details, or something about Vance himself?`,`Which choices dismiss a claim because of the claimant's personal conduct rather than the claim's merits?`,`The correct choice attacks a person's behavior to reject their advice, without engaging the advice itself.`],labels:{topic:`councilor traffic proposal`}},{id:`lr-f-194`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (A) shifts a term's meaning mid-argument; (E) uses 'light' consistently (different fallacy). Difficulty d3 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,scopeSubtlety:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},stimulus:`The menu describes this fried-chicken sandwich as a 'light' meal, and light meals are healthy. So this sandwich is a healthy choice.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`'Natural' foods contain no artificial ingredients. Arsenic occurs in nature. So arsenic is a natural food.`},{text:`The sandwich is unhealthy because fried food is high in fat, regardless of what the menu calls it.`},{text:`Restaurants should be required to list calorie counts on their menus.`},{text:`'Light' in color does not mean low in calories, so the menu's description is misleading.`},{text:`All healthy meals are light meals. This sandwich is a light meal. So this sandwich is healthy.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The stimulus equivocates on "light": the menu means light in color (or weight), while the health premise means low-calorie — the argument trades on the shift. Choice (A) does the same with "natural": no-artificial-ingredients versus occurs-in-nature. The distractors reason soundly, moralize, diagnose the flaw instead of paralleling it, or commit a different fallacy.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is equivocation: a key term shifts meaning between premises. "Light" in the menu description (light-colored, or light in the hand) is not the same "light" as in "light meals are healthy" (low-calorie). The conclusion is valid only if the term means the same thing throughout — it does not. The parallel must shift a term's meaning mid-argument. Choice (A): "natural" first means "containing no artificial ingredients" and then means "occurring in nature" — arsenic qualifies under the second meaning only, so the conclusion trades on the shift, exactly like the stimulus. Choice (B) is the opposite trap: it soundly rejects the menu's framing with a substantive reason (fried food is fatty) — good reasoning, not the flaw. Choice (C) is a policy opinion with no argument structure — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (D) correctly diagnoses the stimulus's equivocation instead of committing it — diagnosing is not paralleling, the supported-nonresponsive trap. Choice (E) is the reverses-conditional trap: it keeps one meaning of "light" throughout but affirms the consequent (all healthy meals are light; this is light; so healthy) — a different fallacy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. "Natural" shifts from "free of artificial ingredients" to "occurring in nature" between the premises, and the conclusion depends on treating the two meanings as one — the same equivocation as the stimulus's "light."`,`This gives a substantive, consistent reason the sandwich is unhealthy — sound reasoning that engages the merits. A non-fallacious argument cannot parallel a fallacy: the opposite trap.`,`A policy recommendation about calorie counts with no premises-to-conclusion inference. No reasoning to compare: the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`This correctly identifies the stimulus's meaning-shift rather than committing one. Diagnosing the flaw is not exhibiting it, so this is the supported-nonresponsive trap. It may be true or even insightful, but it does not perform the reasoning the stem asks about.`,`This keeps a single meaning of "light" but affirms the consequent — a different fallacy (illicit conversion) from the stimulus's meaning-shift. Wrong flaw type: the reverses-conditional trap.`],generalLesson:`Equivocation parallels must shift a word's meaning between premises. Choices that use the term consistently — even fallaciously — commit a different error.`,misconceptionTags:[`equivocation`,`meaning-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`reverses-conditional`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does "light" mean the same thing in the menu description and in "light meals are healthy"?`,`Which choice uses a word in two different senses across its premises?`,`The correct choice shifts "natural" from "no artificial ingredients" to "found in nature" mid-argument.`],labels:{topic:`menu equivocation`}},{id:`lr-f-195`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (D) cites a non-expert endorsement as proof. Difficulty d1 honest; clean skill-acquisition item.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`This investment fund must be sound — a famous actor endorsed it in a commercial.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The fund is sound because independent auditors verified its holdings last quarter.`},{text:`The actor is famous, so the commercial was probably seen by millions of viewers.`},{text:`Celebrity endorsements tend to increase sales of the products being advertised.`},{text:`The herbal tea must cure colds — a retired basketball player says it worked for him.`},{text:`You should not buy into the fund — the actor who endorsed it starred in a terrible movie.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The stimulus appeals to an irrelevant authority: a famous actor's endorsement is treated as evidence about an investment fund, though acting fame gives no financial expertise. Choice (D) does the same — a basketball player's testimonial as medical evidence. The distractors reason soundly, restate facts, generalize, or attack the person instead.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is the appeal to irrelevant authority: the only evidence offered is an endorsement by someone whose fame is unrelated to the claim's subject matter. Acting skill does not transfer to investment analysis. The parallel must cite a celebrity or non-expert whose standing is irrelevant to the claim. Choice (D): a retired basketball player's personal testimonial is offered as proof the tea cures colds — athletic fame is no medical credential, the same flaw. Choice (A) is the opposite trap: auditors verifying holdings is relevant expertise, i.e., sound reasoning, not the flaw. Choice (B) merely restates what fame implies (wide viewership) without drawing the flawed inference — the restates-premise trap. Choice (C) is a general true claim about advertising with no flawed inference — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) commits a different fallacy, attacking the actor personally (ad hominem) rather than treating his endorsement as evidence — the answers-different-question trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Auditors verifying holdings is relevant expertise, so this is legitimate evidence — sound reasoning, not the flaw. A non-fallacious argument is the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`This infers viewership from fame without claiming the endorsement proves anything about the fund. Restating the premise's implication without the flawed leap is the restates-premise trap.`,`That endorsements boost sales may be true, but it performs no flawed inference about product quality. A true generalization with no parallel reasoning is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Correct. A retired basketball player's testimonial is treated as medical proof, though athletic fame confers no medical expertise — the same appeal to irrelevant authority as the actor's financial endorsement.`,`This attacks the actor personally (a bad movie) rather than treating his endorsement as evidence — a different fallacy (ad hominem). Wrong flaw type: the answers-different-question trap.`],generalLesson:`Irrelevant-authority parallels need a famous non-expert vouching outside their domain. Relevant experts, personal attacks, and bare facts are different moves.`,misconceptionTags:[`irrelevant-authority`,`expertise-domain`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`restates-premise`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What makes the actor's endorsement weak evidence — is he an expert on what he is endorsing?`,`Which choice has a celebrity vouching for something outside their expertise?`,`The correct choice treats a basketball player's testimonial as medical proof.`],labels:{topic:`celebrity endorsement flaw`}},{id:`lr-f-196`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (B) generalizes from a tiny sample to a verdict; (E) projects temporally instead. Difficulty d2 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`Our restaurant's new menu is a hit — all three tables served tonight praised it.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The new menu is a hit because two professional food critics praised it in their reviews.`},{text:`The new traffic pattern is working — the two drivers interviewed this morning said they liked it.`},{text:`Three tables is too small a sample to judge whether the new menu is a hit.`},{text:`Restaurants in this city usually change their menus with the seasons.`},{text:`Tonight's diners praised the new menu, so tomorrow night's diners will praise it too.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The stimulus generalizes from a tiny, unrepresentative sample (three tables, one night) to a sweeping verdict. Choice (B) does the same — two drivers, one morning, declared a traffic pattern a success. The distractors cite real experts, diagnose the flaw, state background facts, or project into the future.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is hasty generalization from an unrepresentative sample: three tables on a single night cannot support "the menu is a hit" as a general verdict. The parallel needs a small, narrow sample treated as decisive. Choice (B): two drivers interviewed one morning are taken to prove a traffic pattern works — same tiny-sample leap. Choice (A) is the answers-different-question trap: professional critics are relevant experts, so their praise is legitimate evidence, not the sampling flaw. Choice (C) correctly diagnoses the stimulus's flaw instead of exhibiting it — the opposite trap (diagnosis is not parallel). Choice (D) is background trivia with no inference — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: it shares the small sample but its flaw is temporal projection (tonight → tomorrow night) rather than generalizing from an unrepresentative sample to a present verdict.`,choiceExplanations:[`Professional critics are relevant experts whose reviews are legitimate evidence — this is sound reasoning, not a sampling flaw. A non-fallacious argument is the answers-different-question trap.`,`Correct. Two drivers on one morning are treated as proof that a traffic pattern works — the same hasty generalization from a tiny, unrepresentative sample as three tables on one night.`,`This correctly points out the sample problem instead of committing it. Diagnosing a flaw does not exhibit it, so this is the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`A background fact about seasonal menus with no inference drawn. With no reasoning to compare, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`This shares the small sample but its error is projecting tonight's reaction onto tomorrow night — a temporal leap rather than a sample-to-population generalization. Close but not the same flaw: the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`Sampling-flaw parallels must generalize from a small or biased sample to a broad verdict. Expert evidence, flaw diagnoses, and future projections are different moves.`,misconceptionTags:[`hasty-generalization`,`sample-size`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How big is the evidence base — three tables, one night — compared with the sweeping conclusion?`,`Which choice also treats a tiny, narrow sample as decisive proof of a general verdict?`,`The correct choice interviews two drivers one morning and declares a whole traffic pattern a success.`],labels:{topic:`restaurant sample flaw`}},{id:`lr-f-197`,version:2,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Difficulty corrected d4->d3: surface flaw is textbook correlation-causation; the objection-vs-flaw distinction in (A)/(B) is d3 discrimination work. No other changes; single defensible answer confirmed.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:2},stimulus:`Neighborhoods with more coffee shops have higher average incomes. Therefore, opening coffee shops raises neighborhood incomes.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Coffee shops tend to open in neighborhoods where incomes are already high, which could explain the correlation.`},{text:`Higher neighborhood incomes cause more coffee shops to open, not the other way around.`},{text:`The income study surveyed only downtown neighborhoods, so its results may not generalize.`},{text:`Coffee consumption has been linked to higher workplace productivity in several studies.`},{text:`Students who own more textbooks get better grades. So buying textbooks causes better grades.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The stimulus leaps from correlation to causation: coffee shops and incomes move together, so coffee shops must cause the incomes. Choice (E) makes the identical leap — textbook ownership correlates with grades, so buying textbooks must cause them. The distractors offer rival explanations, reverse the causal arrow, attack the sample, or cite an unrelated correlation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is inferring causation from correlation alone: two things co-occur, and the argument concludes one causes the other without ruling out alternatives. The parallel must move from a mere correlation to a causal conclusion. Choice (E): textbook ownership correlates with grades, therefore buying textbooks causes better grades — the same unlicensed causal leap. Choice (A) is the causal-alternative trap in its purest form: it proposes the rival explanation (shops follow incomes) instead of committing the flaw — it is the objection, not the fallacy. Choice (B) reverses the causal arrow as an objection — again the critic's move, not the flawed move: the reverses-conditional trap. Choice (C) attacks the study's sample rather than its causal inference — a different criticism: the half-right trap, since it is a flaw-adjacent objection but not the correlation-causation leap. Choice (D) cites a different correlation without drawing the causal conclusion — the irrelevant-true trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`This proposes a rival explanation for the correlation — the objection a critic would raise — rather than committing the flaw of inferring causation. Offering the alternative is the causal-alternative trap.`,`This reverses the causal direction as a criticism of the argument. Stating the reverse-causation objection is not the same as making the original leap: the reverses-conditional trap.`,`This criticizes the study's sample, which is a different objection from the correlation-to-causation leap. A flaw-adjacent criticism is not the flaw itself: the half-right trap.`,`A different correlation cited without any causal conclusion. With no flawed inference performed, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. From the correlation between textbook ownership and grades, the argument concludes that buying textbooks causes better grades — the same causation-from-correlation leap as the coffee-shop argument.`],generalLesson:`Correlation-causation parallels must draw the causal conclusion from the correlation. Choices that propose rival causes or reverse the arrow are the critic's objections, not the flaw.`,misconceptionTags:[`correlation-causation`,`objection-vs-flaw`],trapTypes:[`causal-alternative`,`reverses-conditional`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the argument do with the correlation — does it explain it, or conclude something stronger?`,`Which choice also moves from "these go together" to "this causes that"?`,`Beware choices that sound like criticisms of the argument — objections are not parallels.`],labels:{topic:`coffee shop correlation`}},{id:`lr-f-198`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (C) performs the distort-then-attack two-step; (E) mislabels as ad hominem. Difficulty d3 honest.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:2},stimulus:`Senator Hale proposed modest limits on fertilizer runoff. Her opponent claims she wants to bankrupt every farm in the state — a plan no reasonable voter could support.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`Hale’s proposal would actually help farms in the long run by preserving soil quality for future generations.`},{text:`The opponent distorted Hale’s position, so his argument against it fails on logical grounds.`},{text:`The principal suggested a later start time; critics say he wants students to sleep through their education.`},{text:`Fertilizer runoff is a documented cause of water pollution in this state, according to environmental monitoring data.`},{text:`The opponent attacked Hale personally rather than addressing the substance of her proposal.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The opponent misrepresents Hale's modest proposal as an extreme one (bankrupting every farm), then attacks the extreme version — the straw man fallacy. Choice (C) does the same: a later start time is recast as wanting students to sleep through their education, then condemned. The distractors defend the proposal, diagnose the flaw, state facts, or misidentify it as ad hominem.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is the straw man: the opponent replaces Hale's actual position (modest runoff limits) with an exaggerated distortion (bankrupting every farm) and attacks the distortion. The parallel must distort a modest proposal into an extreme one and then reject the extreme. Choice (C): a later school start time becomes "wanting students to sleep through their education," which no parent would accept — distortion followed by rejection, the same two-step. Choice (A) is the supported-nonresponsive trap: it defends Hale's proposal on the merits, which is legitimate counterargument, not the flaw. Choice (B) correctly diagnoses the straw man instead of committing it — diagnosis is not parallel: the answers-different-question trap. Choice (D) is a factual claim about runoff with no argument — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: it mislabels the flaw as a personal attack (ad hominem), but the opponent attacked a distorted position, not Hale's character.`,choiceExplanations:[`This defends the proposal's merits (soil quality), which is legitimate counterargument — not a distortion of anyone's position. Sound reasoning cannot parallel the fallacy: the supported-nonresponsive trap.`,`This correctly identifies the distortion instead of committing it. Diagnosing a flaw is not exhibiting it: the answers-different-question trap. However reasonable it sounds, it responds to a different question than the one the stem poses.`,`Correct. A modest proposal (later start time) is recast as an extreme one (sleeping through education) and then rejected — the same distort-then-attack two-step as the fertilizer case.`,`A factual claim about runoff pollution with no argument structure. With no reasoning to compare, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`The opponent distorted the proposal, not Hale's character — this mislabels the flaw as ad hominem. Close to the action but wrong about its nature: the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`Straw man parallels need both steps: distort the position into something extreme, then attack the distortion. Diagnosing the distortion or attacking the person are different moves.`,misconceptionTags:[`straw-man`,`distortion`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`answers-different-question`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What did Hale actually propose, and what does the opponent claim she proposed? What is the gap between them?`,`Which choice also replaces a modest proposal with an extreme caricature and then rejects the caricature?`,`The correct choice turns "later start time" into "sleeping through education" before condemning it.`],labels:{topic:`fertilizer straw man`}},{id:`lr-f-199`,version:2,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Difficulty corrected d5->d4: circularity is openly announced in the stimulus, so flaw isolation is easy; only the parallel-matching (circle vs weak reason) carries load. No other changes; single defensible answer confirmed.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,abstraction:4,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3},stimulus:`This dictionary is authoritative because it was compiled by the country's leading lexicographers — and we know they are the leading lexicographers because they compiled this authoritative dictionary.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The committee’s report is unbiased: it was written by impartial experts, as proven by the fact that only impartial experts could have produced it.`},{text:`The dictionary is authoritative because language experts throughout the country rely on it for their daily work.`},{text:`No dictionary can be fully authoritative, since every dictionary contains some errors and omissions.`},{text:`Lexicography requires many years of specialized training that few people are willing to undertake.`},{text:`The dictionary’s authority rests on its impressive sales figures over the past decade, outselling every rival by a wide margin.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The stimulus begs the question: the dictionary's authority is "proved" by the compilers' eminence, which is itself "proved" by the dictionary's authority — the conclusion assumes itself. Choice (A) circles identically: the report is unbiased because impartial experts wrote it, proven by the fact that only impartial experts could produce such an unbiased report. The distractors appeal to authority, overgeneralize, state facts, or cite popularity.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is circular reasoning: the premise (leading lexicographers compiled it) depends for its support on the very conclusion it is meant to establish (the dictionary is authoritative). Each half of the argument assumes the other — remove either claim and the remaining one is unsupported. The parallel must exhibit the same mutual dependence, not merely a weak reason. Choice (A): the report is unbiased because impartial experts wrote it, and their impartiality is "proven" by the report's lack of bias — the conclusion (unbiased report) smuggles itself into the premise (impartial experts). Choice (B) is the half-right trap: it appeals to experts' reliance, which is a genuine (if defeasible) reason — an appeal to authority, not a circle. Choice (C) is the too-strong trap: it overgeneralizes from "contains errors" to "no dictionary can be fully authoritative," a different flaw. Choice (D) is a bare fact with no inference — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) cites sales figures as evidence of authority — an appeal to popularity, a different flaw: the scope-shift trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The report's lack of bias is established by the experts' impartiality, which is itself established by the report's lack of bias — each claim assumes the other, the same vicious circle as the dictionary argument.`,`This offers experts' reliance as evidence of authority — a legitimate (though defeasible) appeal to authority, not a circle. A different, weaker flaw is not the same flaw: the half-right trap.`,`This leaps from "contains some errors" to "no dictionary can be fully authoritative" — an overgeneralization, not circular reasoning. Wrong flaw type: the too-strong trap.`,`A factual claim about training with no premises-to-conclusion inference. With no reasoning to compare, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`This treats sales figures as proof of authority — an appeal to popularity, a different fallacy from begging the question. Wrong flaw type: the scope-shift trap.`],generalLesson:`Circular parallels must show mutual dependence: each claim propping up the other. A merely weak reason (popularity, authority) is linear, not circular.`,misconceptionTags:[`circular-reasoning`,`begging-the-question`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What proves the dictionary is authoritative, and what proves the lexicographers are leading? Do those two proofs depend on each other?`,`Which choice has each claim supporting the other in a circle, rather than just giving a weak reason?`,`The correct choice proves the report unbiased via the experts, and the experts impartial via the report.`],labels:{topic:`dictionary circularity`}},{id:`lr-f-200`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: single defensible answer — only (D) forces a choice between non-exhaustive options; (E) is the critic's cure. Difficulty d1 honest; clean skill-acquisition item.`}],difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:1},stimulus:`Either the town builds the new dam or it will run out of water entirely. Since running out of water is unthinkable, we must build the dam.`,stem:`Which one of the following exhibits a flaw in reasoning most similar to the flaw exhibited by the argument above?`,choices:[{text:`The town should build the dam because the municipal engineers recommend it.`},{text:`Running out of water would indeed be disastrous for the town.`},{text:`Dams can disrupt the migration patterns of local fish species.`},{text:`Either cut the arts budget or close the school; closing it is unacceptable, so cut the arts budget.`},{text:`The town could also fix its leaking pipes, which waste millions of gallons each year.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The stimulus presents a false dilemma: only two options (build the dam or run dry) when others exist (conservation, repairs, new sources), then forces the choice by making one unacceptable. Choice (D) does the same — cut arts or close the school — ignoring options like raising revenue. The distractors appeal to authority, agree with a premise, cite a side effect, or propose the missing alternative.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The flaw is the false dilemma: the argument treats two options as exhaustive when they are not — conservation, pipe repairs, and new sources are all ignored — and then eliminates one as unthinkable to force the other. The parallel must present two non-exhaustive options and force one by ruling out the other. Choice (D): cut the arts budget or close the school; closing is unacceptable; so cut arts — ignoring fundraising, tax increases, or cuts elsewhere. Choice (A) is the answers-different-question trap: appealing to engineers is a different flaw (irrelevant authority), not a forced choice between two options. Choice (B) merely agrees that running dry would be disastrous — restating a premise without any flawed inference: the restates-premise trap. Choice (C) raises a side effect of dams with no argument — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (E) is the opposite trap: it proposes the very third option the false dilemma omitted, which is the critic's correction, not the flaw.`,choiceExplanations:[`Citing the engineers' recommendation is an appeal to authority — a different flaw from forcing a choice between two non-exhaustive options. Wrong flaw type: the answers-different-question trap.`,`This merely agrees that running out of water would be disastrous — restating the premise without drawing the flawed forced choice. This is the restates-premise trap.`,`A factual claim about fish migration with no either/or forced choice. With no parallel reasoning, it is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. Two options are presented as exhaustive (cut arts or close the school) though others exist, and one is ruled out as unacceptable to force the other — the same false dilemma.`,`This proposes the missing third option (fixing leaks) — the correction a critic would offer, not the flaw itself. Supplying the omitted alternative is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`False-dilemma parallels must force a choice between two options treated as exhaustive. Proposing the omitted option is the cure, not the parallel.`,misconceptionTags:[`false-dilemma`,`exhaustiveness`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`restates-premise`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-parallel-flaw`,`f-flaws`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How many options does the argument consider — and are those really the only options?`,`Which choice also forces a pick between two options while ignoring obvious alternatives?`,`The correct choice says "cut arts or close the school," ignoring everything in between.`],labels:{topic:`dam false dilemma`}}],o=[{id:`lr-g-201`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Denominator-shift resolution (more riders, lower per-rider rate); no second defensible answer; d2 honest.`}],stimulus:`After the city painted dedicated bike lanes on its downtown avenues, the number of reported cycling accidents rose by 30 percent. The lanes were built to make cycling safer, and they meet every national safety standard.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The bike lanes were painted bright red to increase their visibility to drivers.`},{text:`The new lanes attracted many first-time cyclists, and the accident rate per cyclist fell.`},{text:`Cycling accidents also rose in neighboring cities that built no bike lanes.`},{text:`Most of the reported accidents involved experienced cyclists commuting to work.`},{text:`Bicycle sales in the city were flat in the year the lanes were painted.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`More total accidents alongside safer lanes is reconciled by a surge in new, inexperienced riders: the absolute count rose because far more people were cycling, while each cyclist's risk fell. Choice (B) explains both facts — why accidents rose and why the lanes still improved safety. The distractors are irrelevant, explain only one side, or deepen the mystery.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Pin down the two surprising facts: (1) accidents rose 30% after the lanes were painted; (2) the lanes meet safety standards and were meant to reduce accidents. A resolution must make both true at once — it must explain the rise without denying the lanes' safety. Choice (B) does this with a denominator shift: the lanes drew many first-time cyclists, so total accidents rose even as the per-cyclist rate fell. Both facts coexist: more riders overall, less risk per rider. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: lane color explains nothing about the accident count. Choice (C) is the half-right trap: it suggests a regional trend, but it does not explain why this city's lanes coincided with a rise — and a general trend would deepen, not resolve, the puzzle of safer lanes plus more accidents. Choice (D) deepens the mystery: if experienced cyclists — the least likely to crash — accounted for most accidents, the rise is even stranger. Choice (E) is the irrelevant-true trap: flat sales figures explain nothing about why accidents rose 30%.`,choiceExplanations:[`Lane color is irrelevant to the accident count — it explains neither the rise nor the lanes' safety. An unrelated true-sounding detail is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Correct. A surge of inexperienced new cyclists raises the absolute accident count while the per-cyclist rate falls — reconciling more total accidents with genuinely safer lanes.`,`A regional trend does not explain why safer lanes coincided with more accidents here; if anything, accidents rising everywhere makes the local safety improvement more puzzling. This is the half-right trap.`,`If experienced commuters — the safest riders — suffered most accidents, the rise becomes harder, not easier, to explain. A choice that deepens the mystery is the opposite trap.`,`Flat bicycle sales cannot explain a 30% rise in reported accidents — with no new bikes entering the picture, the sales figures are simply irrelevant. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`When totals and rates seem to conflict, look for a denominator shift: a growing population can raise absolute counts even as individual risk falls.`,misconceptionTags:[`rate-vs-count`,`denominator-shift`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Two facts must both stay true: accidents rose, and the lanes are safe. What could raise total accidents without making the lanes dangerous?`,`Think about who started cycling after the lanes appeared — and the difference between total accidents and accidents per cyclist.`,`The correct choice brings in new, inexperienced riders: more crashes overall, less risk per rider.`],labels:{topic:`bike lane accidents`}},{id:`lr-g-202`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Two-move resolution (use-shift + uncounted e-books); (E) is a proper half-right subset of (C); d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`Last year the public library extended its hours, opening evenings and Sundays for the first time. Yet the number of books checked out fell by 18 percent, even though foot traffic through the library increased.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The library hired additional evening staff to cover the extended hours, which substantially raised operating costs.`},{text:`A national bookstore chain announced it will open a branch near the library sometime next year.`},{text:`New-hours visitors mostly used computers and study spaces, while e-book lending — uncounted in checkout figures — rose sharply.`},{text:`The library reduced its budget for purchasing new physical books last year.`},{text:`Checkout figures count only physical books borrowed in person, not digital loans.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`More visitors but fewer checkouts is reconciled when the new visitors use the library differently: evenings and Sundays drew computer users and studiers, while borrowing shifted to e-books that the checkout statistic does not count. Choice (C) explains both the foot-traffic rise and the checkout drop. The distractors are irrelevant, deepen the mystery, or explain only one side.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The surprise has two halves: foot traffic rose (more people, more hours) but physical checkouts fell 18%. A resolution must account for both — why more visitors borrowed fewer books. Choice (C) does it in two moves: the new hours attracted a different kind of use (computers, study space — no borrowing involved), and borrowing itself migrated to e-books, which the reported figures exclude. So the library is busier and lending is healthy; only the measured number fell. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: staffing explains how the hours were covered, not the checkout decline. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: a bookstore opening next year explains nothing about last year's figures — neither the checkout drop nor the traffic rise. Choice (D) also deepens the puzzle: fewer new books plus more visitors should not produce an 18% checkout collapse on its own, and it says nothing about the traffic increase. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: it notes the figures exclude e-books, which hints at a measurement gap, but without the facts that e-book lending actually rose and new visitors came for other purposes, it does not explain the divergence.`,choiceExplanations:[`Extra evening staff explains how the library stayed open, not why borrowing fell while visits rose. An unrelated operational detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`A bookstore opening next year cannot explain last year's checkout drop or the traffic increase — a future event explains neither surprising fact. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. New-hour visitors came for computers and study space rather than borrowing, and lending shifted to uncounted e-books — so visits rose while measured checkouts fell, with no real decline in use.`,`A smaller acquisitions budget might trim checkouts, but it leaves the foot-traffic increase unexplained and makes the divergence stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap.`,`That the figures exclude e-books hints at a gap, but without evidence that e-book lending rose and new visitors used the library differently, it does not explain the divergence. A partial explanation is the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`When a metric falls while activity rises, check what the metric actually measures — and whether behavior shifted to something the metric cannot see.`,misconceptionTags:[`metric-definition`,`behavior-shift`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Foot traffic rose but checkouts fell. Could the new visitors be doing something other than borrowing books?`,`What does the checkout figure actually count — and what might it be missing?`,`The correct choice changes both what visitors do and what gets counted.`],labels:{topic:`library hours paradox`}},{id:`lr-g-203`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Clean d1 skill-acquisition item; competitor-closure resolution; distractors appropriately simple.`}],stimulus:`Marta's Diner raised its prices by 15 percent last month. Since then, the lunch line at the diner has grown noticeably longer.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The diner across the street, Marta's only nearby competitor, closed permanently.`},{text:`Marta's Diner now uses more expensive organic ingredients.`},{text:`The diner kept its number of tables unchanged.`},{text:`Customers have complained about the higher prices on social media.`},{text:`The diner extended its lunch hours by thirty minutes each day.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Higher prices should shrink demand, yet lines grew. Choice (A) resolves it: the only nearby competitor closed, so its customers had nowhere else to go — demand was pushed to Marta's regardless of price. The other choices explain the price rise, deepen the mystery, or address only one side.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The two facts: prices rose 15%, and the lunch line got longer. Normally higher prices mean fewer customers, so something must have shifted demand independently of price. Choice (A) supplies it: the sole nearby competitor closed, funneling its former customers to Marta's. Lines grow not because the price rise attracted anyone, but because the alternative disappeared. Choice (B) is the answers-different-question trap: expensive ingredients might justify the price increase, but they explain the price, not the longer lines. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap: an unchanged table count explains nothing about the longer lines. Choice (D) also deepens it: complaints suggest customers are unhappy, making longer lines stranger. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: longer hours might spread the same crowd thinner, not concentrate it into longer lines; it touches operations without explaining the demand shift.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. With the only nearby competitor gone, its customers had to eat somewhere — demand shifted to Marta's despite higher prices, reconciling the price rise with the longer lines.`,`Costlier ingredients may explain why prices rose, but they do nothing to explain why more people are lining up. Explaining the price instead of the paradox is the answers-different-question trap.`,`An unchanged table count explains nothing about why the lunch line grew after a 15% price increase — a static background fact cannot resolve the paradox. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Complaints about prices suggest customers dislike the increase, which makes their willingness to wait in longer lines stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap.`,`Longer hours spread diners over more time rather than concentrating them into longer lines. This operational detail does not explain the demand shift: the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`When price and demand move the "wrong" way together, look for an external demand shift — like a competitor's exit — that overwhelms the price effect.`,misconceptionTags:[`demand-shift`,`price-effect`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Higher prices usually mean fewer customers. What outside change could send more customers to the diner anyway?`,`Which choice changes the competitive landscape rather than the diner's costs or hours?`,`The correct choice removes the diner's only alternative — its customers have nowhere else to go.`],labels:{topic:`diner price paradox`}},{id:`lr-g-204`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Strength-becomes-weakness (drought vs flood tolerance) mechanism; d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`Farmers in the valley planted a new drought-resistant seed variety this spring. Despite receiving normal rainfall, their yields per acre fell below those of the previous five years.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The drought-resistant seeds cost considerably more than the traditional variety.`},{text:`This spring brought record flooding, and the new variety tolerates standing water poorly.`},{text:`Neighboring valleys that planted the traditional variety had normal yields.`},{text:`The new seed variety was developed over twelve years of field trials.`},{text:`Some farmers in the valley chose not to plant the new variety.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Drought-resistant seeds plus normal rain should not cut yields — unless the seeds' strength is also their weakness. Choice (B) resolves it: the spring brought record flooding, which this variety handles poorly. The other choices address cost, deepen the mystery, or leave the mechanism unexplained.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The surprise: a seed bred to protect yields coincided with the worst yields in five years, even though rainfall was normal — so drought cannot be the culprit. The resolution must supply a different threat that this variety is specifically vulnerable to. Choice (B) does: record flooding struck, and drought resistance came at the cost of flood tolerance. Both facts now fit — the seeds performed exactly as bred (against drought), but the actual enemy was water excess. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: higher seed cost affects profits, not yields per acre. Choice (C) deepens the mystery — if neighbors with traditional seeds did fine, the new variety looks even worse, with no mechanism offered. Choice (D) is irrelevant background about development time. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: some farmers opting out does not explain why those who planted the new seeds got poor yields.`,choiceExplanations:[`Seed cost affects the farmers' expenses, not the bushels harvested per acre. An irrelevant financial detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. The variety resists drought but not flooding; a record-flood spring hit its specific weakness, so yields fell despite normal rainfall totals — both facts reconciled.`,`That neighbors did fine with traditional seeds confirms the new variety underperformed but offers no mechanism — making the failure more puzzling, not less. This is the opposite trap.`,`Twelve years of field trials is background trivia that explains nothing about this spring's yields. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Some farmers opting out does not explain why the farmers who did plant the new variety harvested less. This addresses the wrong group: the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`A trait bred for one threat can create vulnerability to another. When a safeguard coincides with failure, ask what different threat the safeguard left exposed.`,misconceptionTags:[`tradeoff`,`threat-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Rainfall was normal, so drought is not the story. What other weather threat could strike — and how might drought resistance relate to it?`,`Which choice gives the new variety a specific weakness that this spring actually exploited?`,`The correct choice pairs record flooding with the variety's poor tolerance for standing water.`],labels:{topic:`drought-resistant seeds`}},{id:`lr-g-205`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`A software company switched its engineers to a four-day workweek with no pay cut. In the six months that followed, the total number of features shipped per month increased.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Engineers reported feeling happier and less burned out with the four-day schedule.`},{text:`The company's headcount remained exactly flat during the same six-month period.`},{text:`Fridays were mostly status meetings; eliminating them left four days of uninterrupted coding.`},{text:`The company's competitors also reduced their workweeks at some point last year.`},{text:`Feature quality is not measured anywhere in the company's shipping statistics.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Fewer hours should mean less output, yet output rose. Choice (C) resolves it: the eliminated day was mostly low-value meetings, so the remaining hours became far more productive — total productive time effectively grew. The distractors cite morale, flat headcount, competitors, or measurement gaps — none supplying the productivity mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The two facts: hours fell 20%, but shipped features per month rose. For both to hold, productivity per hour must have jumped enough to outweigh the lost day. Choice (C) supplies the mechanism: Fridays were largely status meetings — low-output time — and their elimination converted the other four days into deep, uninterrupted coding. The lost hours were the least productive ones, so effective coding time actually increased. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: happiness might correlate with productivity, but it does not explain how fewer hours produced more output — the mechanism is missing. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: flat headcount rules out the more-hands explanation, but it does nothing to resolve how fewer hours yielded more output. Choice (D) is the irrelevant-true trap: competitors' schedules explain nothing about this company's output. Choice (E) deepens the mystery: if quality is unmeasured, the shipping count could mask shoddier work — making the "increase" suspect rather than explained.`,choiceExplanations:[`Happier engineers might work better, but morale alone does not explain how 20% fewer hours yielded more shipped features — the productivity mechanism is missing. This is the half-right trap.`,`With headcount flat, the output gain cannot be credited to more hands — this fact rules out one alternative without explaining how fewer hours yielded more features. A non-explanatory true fact is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Correct. The cut day was mostly meetings; eliminating them turned four days into deep coding time, so productive hours effectively rose — reconciling fewer total hours with more shipped features.`,`What competitors did has no bearing on this company's output numbers, and it offers no mechanism for how fewer hours produced more features. An unrelated industry fact is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`If quality is unmeasured, the higher shipping count might reflect rushed, shoddy work — which makes the apparent gain more suspicious, not explained. This is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Not all hours are equal: when less time yields more output, look for the eliminated hours having been the least productive ones.`,misconceptionTags:[`hours-vs-output`,`productivity-composition`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Fewer hours but more output means each remaining hour did more. What could make the remaining hours so much more productive?`,`Which choice says something specific about how the eliminated day had been spent?`,`The correct choice reveals the cut day was mostly meetings — the least productive hours.`],labels:{topic:`four-day workweek`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-g-206`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,causalComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Two years after the town banned single-use plastic bags, the amount of plastic litter collected in parks and waterways has increased. The ban was intended to reduce plastic pollution, and compliance among retailers has been nearly total.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Residents buy thick reusable bags and discard them after a few uses, putting more plastic into the waste stream per bag than the thin banned bags did.`},{text:`The town also banned plastic straws in the same ordinance.`},{text:`Shoppers switched to paper bags, which tear easily and scatter in the wind, increasing visible litter even though paper is not plastic.`},{text:`Plastic litter has also increased in neighboring towns without bag bans.`},{text:`Retailers complied with the ban by switching to bags made of recycled plastic.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A fully obeyed ban on thin plastic bags coinciding with more plastic litter is reconciled by substitution with a worse offender: choice (A) — shoppers replaced the thin banned bags with thick reusable plastic bags that they discard after only a few uses, each putting more plastic into the waste stream than a banned bag did. Full compliance plus more plastic per bag yields more plastic litter. The distractors cite unrelated items, non-plastic substitutes, regional trends, or imply the ban changed nothing.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The tension: retailers nearly totally complied with the thin-bag ban, yet collected plastic litter rose. For both to hold, the plastic in the litter must come from a source the ban did not remove — or from a replacement worse than the original. Choice (A) supplies exactly that: shoppers switched to thick reusable plastic bags but discard them after only a few uses, and each such bag contains more plastic than a thin banned bag did. Full compliance is preserved (no thin bags sold), while total plastic entering the waste stream increases — so collected plastic litter rises, and both facts hold with no contradiction. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: straws are a different item and explain nothing about bag litter. Choice (C) is the opposite trap: paper bags are not plastic, so switching to paper cannot explain more plastic litter — it should reduce it. Choice (D) is the half-right trap: a regional trend widens the puzzle without explaining this town's ban-versus-litter divergence. Choice (E) is the opposite trap: recycled-plastic bags are still plastic bags, implying the ban changed nothing and contradicting near-total compliance.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The ban eliminated thin single-use bags, but shoppers replaced them with thick reusable plastic bags discarded after only a few uses — each containing more plastic than the banned bags. Total plastic entering the waste stream therefore rose even with full compliance, directly explaining why collected plastic litter increased.`,`A straw ban is a separate provision about a separate item; it explains nothing about why plastic-bag litter rose. An unrelated fact from the same ordinance is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Paper bags are not plastic, so a switch to paper cannot explain an increase in plastic litter — if anything it should reduce it. This choice deepens the mystery rather than resolving it: the opposite trap.`,`That litter also rose elsewhere does not explain why this town's fully obeyed ban coincided with more plastic litter; it merely widens the puzzle. A regional trend without a mechanism is the half-right trap.`,`Recycled-plastic bags are still plastic bags, so this would mean the ban changed nothing — contradicting the premise of near-total compliance with a meaningful ban. This is the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Bans target one product, but behavior substitutes another: when a prohibition coincides with a worsening metric, trace what replaced the banned item.`,misconceptionTags:[`substitution-effect`,`metric-scope`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`half-right`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The ban removed thin bags — but what did shoppers carry their groceries in instead?`,`Which choice describes a replacement that puts MORE plastic into the waste stream per bag?`,`The correct choice: thick reusable bags discarded after a few uses, each containing more plastic than the banned bags.`],labels:{topic:`plastic bag ban`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-g-207`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Local-compliance mechanism (brake-then-speed); (B) genuine half-right; d2 honest.`}],stimulus:`After speed cameras were installed along the highway, the number of speeding tickets issued fell by half. Yet the highway accident rate remained exactly the same.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Drivers brake for cameras then speed up, so average speeds and crash risk stay unchanged as tickets drop.`},{text:`The cameras are clearly marked with warning signs a mile in advance.`},{text:`Speeding tickets had been rising steadily for five years before the cameras were installed.`},{text:`The accident rate on nearby highways without cameras also stayed the same.`},{text:`Camera fines are significantly higher than the fines officers used to issue.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Fewer tickets but unchanged accidents is reconciled when driver behavior changed only at the cameras: drivers slow for the lens, then speed again — tickets fall, real risk does not. Choice (A) explains both facts. The distractors are irrelevant details, background trends, or deepen the mystery.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: tickets halved, accidents unchanged. Tickets measure caught speeding at camera points; accidents measure actual road risk. For both to hold, the cameras must have changed ticketed behavior without changing real driving risk. Choice (A) supplies the mechanism: drivers brake for the cameras and re-accelerate after — compliance is local and theatrical, so tickets drop while average speeds and crash risk stay flat. Choice (B) is the half-right trap: warning signs might explain fewer tickets (drivers slow in time), but not why accidents stayed the same. Choice (C) is background trivia about pre-camera trends — the irrelevant-true trap. Choice (D) is also half-right: unchanged rates elsewhere do not explain this highway's ticket-accident divergence. Choice (E) deepens the mystery: higher fines should deter speeding more, making unchanged accidents stranger — the opposite trap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Localized braking cuts tickets at camera points while post-camera speeding keeps average speeds and crash risk unchanged — reconciling fewer tickets with flat accident rates.`,`Warning signs might explain why tickets fell (drivers brake in time), but they do not explain why accidents stayed flat. Explaining only one side is the half-right trap.`,`The pre-camera trend in tickets is background that touches neither the ticket drop nor the flat accident rate. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Unchanged rates on other highways do not explain this highway's divergence between tickets and accidents. This is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`,`Higher fines should deter speeding more strongly, which makes unchanged accident rates more puzzling, not less. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`],generalLesson:`Enforcement metrics and outcome metrics can diverge when people comply only where they are watched. Ask whether the measured behavior changed everywhere or just at the sensor.`,misconceptionTags:[`measurement-point`,`local-compliance`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Tickets measure speeding at the cameras; accidents measure real risk. Could drivers behave differently at those two "locations"?`,`Which choice has drivers changing behavior only where the cameras watch?`,`The correct choice: brake for the lens, speed up after — tickets fall, risk does not.`],labels:{topic:`speed cameras`}},{id:`lr-g-208`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,abstraction:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Self-selection composition effect on the reported average; d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`When Westfield College made standardized test scores optional for applicants, the average test score among enrolled students rose the following year.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The college also increased its financial aid budget that year.`},{text:`Applications to Westfield rose sharply after the policy change, though the college admitted the same percentage of applicants as before.`},{text:`High school grade averages among enrolled students stayed the same.`},{text:`After scores became optional, only applicants with strong scores chose to submit them.`},{text:`Westfield's admissions office hired additional readers to review applications holistically.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Optional scores plus a higher average is reconciled by self-selection: when only strong scorers submit, the reported average is computed over a stronger pool. Choice (D) explains it — the average rose because weak scores simply stopped being reported. The distractors cite aid, volume, or staffing, which do not touch the average's composition.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The surprise: making scores optional raised the average score. But "average test score among enrolled students" is computed only over students who submitted scores. If low scorers withhold their scores while high scorers submit, the average of the submitted scores rises mechanically — no student needs to have scored higher. Choice (D) states exactly this self-selection: only strong scorers submitted. Both facts hold: scores became optional, and the reported average rose, because the pool being averaged changed. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: financial aid does not change which scores get submitted. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: with the acceptance rate unchanged, more applications cannot explain the higher average. Choice (C) deepens the mystery: unchanged grades make the score jump stranger. Choice (E) is irrelevant: more readers do not alter the composition of submitted scores.`,choiceExplanations:[`Financial aid affects who can afford to attend, not which test scores get submitted. An unrelated policy change is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`With the acceptance rate unchanged, a larger applicant pool cannot explain a higher average among enrolled students — selectivity did not rise. This blocks the alternative story without resolving anything: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`If grades were flat while reported scores jumped, the divergence is even stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Correct. The average is computed over submitted scores only; when weak scorers withhold theirs, the reported average rises mechanically — reconciling optional scores with a higher average.`,`Additional readers change how applications are reviewed, not which scores applicants choose to submit. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`Averages follow the reporting pool, not just the underlying population: when reporting becomes voluntary, ask who opts out before concluding anything improved.`,misconceptionTags:[`selection-bias`,`average-composition`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The "average score" is averaged over whom — all students, or only those who submitted scores?`,`If weak scorers stopped submitting, what happens to the average of the scores that remain?`,`The correct choice: only strong scorers submitted, so the reported average rose by selection.`],labels:{topic:`test-optional admissions`}},{id:`lr-g-209`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Clean d1 skill-acquisition item; product-degradation mechanism; (A) correctly half-right.`}],stimulus:`A downtown gym cut its monthly membership fee in half. In the three months that followed, average daily attendance fell by a third.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Cheaper memberships attracted many new sign-ups who rarely work out.`},{text:`To afford the fee cut, the gym canceled popular classes and reduced its hours.`},{text:`The gym advertised the lower fee heavily on social media.`},{text:`Nearby gyms kept their fees unchanged during the same period.`},{text:`The fee cut applied only to new members, not to existing ones.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Cheaper memberships should raise attendance, yet attendance fell. Choice (B) resolves it: the fee cut was funded by canceling popular classes and cutting hours — the gym became less worth visiting, so regulars stopped coming. The other choices explain sign-ups, deepen the mystery, or miss the mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: price halved, attendance fell by a third. Lower prices normally increase use, so something must have made the gym less attractive at the same time. Choice (B) supplies it: the fee cut was paid for by eliminating the most popular classes and shortening hours — the product got worse as the price fell, and worse product drove regulars away faster than the low price attracted anyone. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: new sign-ups who never come explain flat attendance, not a one-third drop — and it cannot explain why existing regulars vanished. Choice (C) deepens the mystery: heavy advertising should have raised attendance. Choice (D) is the irrelevant-true trap: competitors' unchanged fees do not explain this gym's attendance drop. Choice (E) is also half-right: limiting the cut to new members might mute the price effect, but it does not explain an actual decline.`,choiceExplanations:[`New sign-ups who never work out add little to daily attendance, but they cannot explain why attendance fell by a third — existing regulars must have left. This explains only one side: the half-right trap.`,`Correct. Funding the fee cut by canceling popular classes and reducing hours made the gym less worth visiting, so regulars stopped coming — reconciling the lower price with lower attendance.`,`Heavy advertising should increase attendance, making the drop stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Competitors' pricing does not explain why this gym's own attendance fell. An unrelated market fact is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Restricting the cut to new members might weaken the price effect, but it cannot produce an actual attendance decline. This is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`],generalLesson:`Price cuts funded by quality cuts can reduce demand: when price and quantity move the "wrong" way, check whether the product itself changed.`,misconceptionTags:[`price-vs-quality`,`product-change`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Lower prices should bring more people. What change could make the gym less appealing at the same time the price fell?`,`Which choice says the gym itself got worse — not just cheaper?`,`The correct choice funds the fee cut by cutting popular classes and hours.`],labels:{topic:`gym fee cut`}},{id:`lr-g-210`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Reported-vs-actual crime distinction with full mechanism; (C) regional trend is a designed near-miss that never engages the funding fact; d4 honest.`}],stimulus:`Last year the country doubled funding for its police forces. Over the same period, the number of reported crimes rose by 25 percent.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The additional funding was spent mostly on community outreach programs rather than on patrols, which do not directly affect whether crimes get reported to the police.`},{text:`The country's unemployment rate held steady during the same period, ruling out economic distress as a driver of the increase.`},{text:`Neighboring countries that did not increase police funding also saw reported crime rise, suggesting a regional trend in reporting that has nothing to do with policing levels.`},{text:`Most of the new funding went unspent due to bureaucratic delays, so few additional officers were actually hired.`},{text:`New officers went to neighborhoods with almost no prior police presence, where residents had stopped reporting crimes, so reporting soared while actual victimization stayed flat.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`More police plus more reported crime is reconciled when "reported" is the operative word: new officers in long-unpoliced neighborhoods caused crimes to be reported that previously went unrecorded, while actual crime stayed flat. Choice (E) explains the rise in reports without any rise in crime. The distractors cite steady unemployment, regional trends, or unspent funds — none distinguishing reported crime from actual crime.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The key distinction is reported crime versus actual crime. More police funding should reduce actual crime, but the statistic measures reports — and police presence itself increases reporting. Choice (E) builds the full mechanism: officers went to neighborhoods with almost no prior presence, where residents had given up reporting; reports soared there while actual victimization stayed flat. Both facts hold: funding doubled, reported crime rose 25%, yet policing did not fail — it illuminated dark figures of crime. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: outreach spending might explain why crime did not fall, but not why reports rose. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: steady unemployment eliminates one alternative cause, but it does not explain the 25% rise in reports — the funding-versus-reports tension is untouched. Choice (C) is the half-right trap: a regional trend does not explain this country's specific funding-crime divergence. Choice (D) deepens the mystery differently: unspent funds would predict no change, not a 25% rise in reports.`,choiceExplanations:[`Outreach spending might explain flat crime, but it does not explain why reported crimes rose by a quarter. This addresses only one side: the half-right trap.`,`Steady unemployment rules out an economic cause, but it explains nothing about why reported crimes rose 25% while police funding doubled. A true but non-explanatory fact is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`A regional trend does not explain why this country's funding increase coincided with more reported crime. Widening the puzzle is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`,`Unspent funds would predict little change, not a 25% jump in reports. This leaves the rise unexplained: the unsupported trap. With the key claim left unsupported by any evidence, it cannot do the work the correct choice does.`,`Correct. New officers in previously unpoliced neighborhoods caused long-unreported crimes to be reported; reports soared while actual victimization stayed flat — reconciling more funding with more reported crime.`],generalLesson:`Reported statistics measure reporting behavior as well as underlying reality: when enforcement rises alongside reports, ask whether the rise is in recording rather than in occurrence.`,misconceptionTags:[`reported-vs-actual`,`measurement-effect`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The statistic is reported crime, not necessarily committed crime. How could more police increase reporting without increasing crime?`,`Which choice puts new officers where crimes previously went unreported?`,`The correct choice: new presence in unpoliced neighborhoods turned silent victimization into reported crime.`],labels:{topic:`police funding paradox`}},{id:`lr-g-211`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Revenue-loss-exceeds-savings mechanism; d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`A bakery switched to a cheaper flour to cut costs. In the quarter that followed, its profits fell instead of rising.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The cheaper flour worsened the bread's taste, and the bakery lost many regular customers.`},{text:`The bakery kept the prices of its pastries unchanged during the same quarter.`},{text:`Flour prices across the industry fell during the same quarter.`},{text:`The bakery's ovens are more than twenty years old.`},{text:`The cheaper flour required slightly longer baking times.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Cheaper inputs should raise profits, yet profits fell. Choice (A) resolves it: the flour hurt the taste, regulars left, and the lost revenue outweighed the ingredient savings. The distractors deepen the mystery, cite industry trends, or offer trivial operational details.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: input costs fell, but profits fell too. For both to hold, revenue must have fallen by more than costs did — the savings were swamped by lost sales. Choice (A) supplies the mechanism: worse taste drove away regulars, so the revenue loss exceeded the flour savings. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: unchanged pastry prices explain nothing about the profit decline, leaving the core tension untouched. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap: industry flour prices do not explain this bakery's profit drop. Choice (D) is irrelevant background about oven age. Choice (E) is the half-right trap: slightly longer baking times are a minor operational wrinkle that cannot plausibly explain a profit decline — it gestures at a cost without the revenue mechanism.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The cheaper flour degraded the product, regulars left, and the resulting revenue loss exceeded the ingredient savings — reconciling lower costs with lower profits.`,`Unchanged pastry prices explain nothing about why profits fell after the flour switch — a static fact cannot account for the decline. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Industry-wide flour prices do not explain why this bakery's profits fell after its own cost cut. An unrelated market fact is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`The age of the ovens is background trivia that changed nothing this quarter and explains neither the cost cut nor the profit drop. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Slightly longer baking times are too trivial to explain a profit decline and say nothing about lost revenue. A minor detail without the revenue mechanism is the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`Cost cuts that degrade the product can destroy more revenue than they save: when lower costs coincide with lower profits, look for the demand side.`,misconceptionTags:[`cost-vs-revenue`,`quality-demand`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Lower costs should raise profits. For profits to fall anyway, what must have happened to revenue?`,`Which choice connects the cheaper flour to lost sales?`,`The correct choice: worse taste drove regulars away, and lost revenue outweighed the savings.`],labels:{topic:`bakery flour switch`}},{id:`lr-g-212`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Per-visitor revenue arithmetic; d2 honest.`}],stimulus:`An aquarium lowered its admission prices by 20 percent. Attendance rose sharply, yet total admission revenue for the season fell.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The aquarium extended its opening hours during the same season, adding two extra hours each weekend.`},{text:`Visitors spent less per person at the gift shop and café than in previous seasons, a decline unrelated to ticket pricing or attendance levels.`},{text:`The price cut applied mainly to group tickets that were already heavily discounted, so average revenue per visitor fell by more than attendance rose.`},{text:`A nearby zoo raised its admission prices during the same season, prompting complaints from local families.`},{text:`The aquarium’s most popular exhibit was closed for renovation for part of the season, reopening before the holidays.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Lower prices plus more visitors should raise revenue — unless revenue per visitor fell faster than attendance grew. Choice (C) resolves it: the discount concentrated on already-cheap group tickets, so the average take per visitor dropped more than headcount rose. The distractors address hours, non-admission spending, competitors, or exhibits without the per-visitor arithmetic.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Revenue equals attendance times revenue per visitor. Attendance rose, so for revenue to fall, revenue per visitor must have fallen proportionally more. Choice (C) gives the arithmetic: the 20% cut landed mostly on group tickets that were already deeply discounted, dragging average revenue per visitor down faster than the attendance gain. Both facts hold: more visitors, less total revenue. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: longer hours might explain higher attendance but not falling revenue. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: gift-shop and café spending is not admission revenue, so it cannot explain the admission-revenue arithmetic at all. Choice (D) is the irrelevant-true trap: the zoo's prices do not explain the aquarium's revenue arithmetic. Choice (E) deepens the mystery: a closed star exhibit should have reduced attendance, yet attendance rose.`,choiceExplanations:[`Longer hours might explain the attendance gain, but they do nothing to explain why revenue fell despite more visitors. Explaining only one side is the half-right trap.`,`Gift-shop and café spending is not admission revenue, so lower side spending cannot explain a fall in admission revenue at all. It answers about the wrong revenue stream: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Correct. Because the discount concentrated on already-cheap group tickets, average revenue per visitor fell by a larger percentage than attendance rose — so total revenue fell despite the crowds.`,`The zoo's pricing does not change the aquarium's revenue arithmetic. An unrelated competitor fact is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`A closed star exhibit should have hurt attendance, yet attendance rose — making the facts stranger, not clearer. This is the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`],generalLesson:`Revenue is price times quantity: when a price cut plus higher volume yields less revenue, check whether the cut fell unevenly and dragged the average price down disproportionately.`,misconceptionTags:[`price-volume`,`average-revenue`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Revenue is attendance multiplied by revenue per visitor. If attendance rose but revenue fell, what must have happened to revenue per visitor?`,`Which choice explains a steep drop in the average take per visitor?`,`The correct choice: the discount hit already-cheap group tickets hardest, so average revenue per visitor plunged.`],labels:{topic:`aquarium pricing`}},{id:`lr-g-213`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Net-shade-loss mechanism; (A) is a model half-right (explains no-fall, not rise); d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`The city planted 10,000 new trees last year as part of a cooling initiative. Yet average summer temperatures in the city rose slightly.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The newly planted trees were saplings that will not provide meaningful shade for at least a decade.`},{text:`The city also expanded its bus network during the same year.`},{text:`Residents were encouraged to water the new trees during dry spells.`},{text:`The planting required clearing mature shade trees, and the saplings cannot replace their cooling effect for years.`},{text:`Tree-planting initiatives are popular with voters in the region.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`More trees should cool the city, yet temperatures rose. Choice (D) resolves it: planting the saplings required clearing mature shade trees first, so the city lost immediate cooling — a net loss of shade in the short run. The distractors note the saplings' immaturity without the clearing, or cite irrelevant facts.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: 10,000 trees planted, but summer temperatures rose. New plantings cool only when they add net shade; if planting destroyed existing shade, the short-term effect reverses. Choice (D) supplies both halves: mature shade trees were cleared to make room, and saplings cannot replace their cooling for years — net shade fell, so temperatures rose, fully consistent with the planting count. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: sapling immaturity explains why temperatures did not fall, but not why they rose — something must have actively removed cooling. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: bus expansion does not explain the temperature rise. Choice (C) is irrelevant: watering does not affect city temperatures. Choice (E) is the irrelevant-true trap: voter popularity explains nothing about thermometers.`,choiceExplanations:[`Sapling immaturity explains why temperatures did not fall, but not why they rose — an active loss of cooling is still missing. Explaining only one side is the half-right trap.`,`Bus network expansion has no bearing on summer temperatures. An unrelated civic fact is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Watering new trees does not change citywide temperatures. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. Clearing mature shade trees to plant saplings produced a net loss of shade in the short run — so temperatures rose even as the tree count grew, reconciling both facts.`,`Voter popularity explains why the program exists, not why temperatures rose. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`Gross counts can mask net losses: when an addition coincides with a worsening outcome, ask what was removed to make room for it.`,misconceptionTags:[`gross-vs-net`,`short-run-effect`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Planting 10,000 trees adds shade eventually — but what had to be removed to plant them?`,`Which choice describes an immediate loss of cooling, not just a delayed gain?`,`The correct choice: mature shade trees were cleared, and saplings cannot replace them for years.`],labels:{topic:`urban tree planting`}},{id:`lr-g-214`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Demand-outruns-capacity mechanism; (C) correctly deepens the mystery; d2 honest.`}],stimulus:`The regional hospital added 40 new beds last year. Yet average emergency-room wait times increased.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The new beds are located in a newly built wing with modern equipment that improved care quality and patient outcomes.`},{text:`News of the expansion drew patients from neighboring counties with overcrowded hospitals, and demand outgrew the new capacity.`},{text:`The hospital also hired additional nurses during the same year to staff the new wing.`},{text:`Emergency-room wait times had been falling for three years before the expansion began.`},{text:`The hospital raised its parking fees last year, prompting complaints from visitors.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`More beds should shorten waits, yet waits grew. Choice (B) resolves it: publicity about the expansion attracted patients from surrounding counties, so demand outran the new supply. The distractors describe the wing, staffing, past trends, or parking — none of which explains demand outpacing capacity.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: capacity rose by 40 beds, but waits lengthened. Capacity alone does not set wait times — the balance of demand and capacity does. If demand grew faster than the added beds, waits rise despite the expansion. Choice (B) provides exactly that: news of the expansion pulled in patients from overcrowded neighboring counties, so the new beds were swamped by new demand. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: modern equipment does not explain the wait-time increase. Choice (C) deepens the mystery: more nurses should have shortened waits, making the rise stranger — the opposite trap. Choice (D) is background trivia about prior trends. Choice (E) is the irrelevant-true trap: parking fees do not drive emergency-room demand. Any genuine resolution must therefore operate on the demand side of the ledger — which is exactly what the influx of new patients from neighboring counties does.`,choiceExplanations:[`Modern equipment in the new wing does not explain why waits grew despite more beds. An unrelated facility detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. The expansion attracted patients from neighboring counties, so demand grew faster than the 40 new beds — waits rose because new demand swamped new supply.`,`Additional nurses should have reduced waits, making the increase more puzzling rather than less. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`The prior downward trend in waits is background that explains neither the expansion nor the reversal. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Parking fees do not determine emergency-room demand or wait times. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`Capacity expansions can induce their own demand: when more supply coincides with longer waits, check whether the expansion itself attracted new users.`,misconceptionTags:[`induced-demand`,`supply-demand-balance`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Wait times depend on demand versus capacity, not capacity alone. What could make demand jump when beds were added?`,`Which choice brings new patients into the picture?`,`The correct choice: publicity drew patients from neighboring counties, swamping the new beds.`],labels:{topic:`hospital bed expansion`}},{id:`lr-g-215`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:5,scopeSubtlety:4,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Last year brought record rainfall to the region. Yet the water level in the Halston Reservoir, the region's main water supply, fell to its lowest point in a decade.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Regional water consumption rose to an all-time high last year, although the Halston Reservoir supplies less than five percent of the region's water.`},{text:`The record rainfall figure was confirmed by three independent weather services.`},{text:`Climate models predict more extreme rainfall for the region in coming decades.`},{text:`The storms dropped their rain outside the reservoir's watershed, while managers released large volumes preventively to protect downstream towns from flooding.`},{text:`The reservoir's dam underwent routine maintenance last spring.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Record rain plus a falling reservoir is reconciled when the rain missed the watershed and the reservoir was deliberately drawn down: choice (D) explains both — storms hit coastal areas outside the catchment, while managers released stored water for flood control. The distractors cite consumption the reservoir barely serves, measurement verification, predictions, or maintenance without the dual mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a difficult item because two independent mechanisms must combine: the inflow side and the outflow side. Record regional rainfall should raise the reservoir — unless the rain did not fall in its watershed (inflow failed) and stored water was simultaneously released (outflow surged). Choice (D) supplies both: the storms struck coastal zones outside the reservoir's catchment, so the "record rainfall" never reached it, and managers preventively released large volumes to protect downstream towns from flooding. Both facts now coexist: regional rain records and a decade-low reservoir. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: record consumption elsewhere cannot draw down Halston when it supplies under five percent of regional water — the reservoir's own outflow side is untouched. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: verifying the rainfall record says nothing about which watershed received it or why the reservoir was drawn down. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap: future predictions explain nothing about last year. Choice (E) is the unsupported trap: routine maintenance does not imply large releases.`,choiceExplanations:[`Record regional consumption cannot explain Halston's drop when the reservoir supplies less than five percent of the region's water — the consumption happened elsewhere. This addresses the wrong water body: the half-right trap.`,`That the rainfall record was verified changes nothing about where the rain fell or where the reservoir's water went. Confirming the measurement does not explain the paradox: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Future climate predictions have no bearing on last year's reservoir level. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. The storms missed the reservoir's watershed (no inflow gain) while managers released stored water for flood control (large outflow) — the two mechanisms together reconcile record regional rain with a decade-low reservoir.`,`Routine maintenance does not imply that large volumes of water were released. Without the outflow mechanism, this is the unsupported trap. With the key claim left unsupported by any evidence, it cannot do the work the correct choice does.`],generalLesson:`Hard paradoxes often need two mechanisms — one for each side of the ledger. Check inflows and outflows (or supply and demand) separately before settling for a one-sided story.`,misconceptionTags:[`two-mechanism`,`watershed-scope`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`A reservoir's level depends on water in versus water out. Which choice addresses both sides?`,`Record regional rain means little if it fell outside the watershed — and what might managers have been releasing water for?`,`The correct choice pairs missed-watershed storms with preventive flood-control releases.`],labels:{topic:`reservoir water level`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP (good diff-5); 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-g-216`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Shelf-displacement cannibalization mechanism; d3 honest.`}],stimulus:`A beloved independent bookstore added a café to attract more foot traffic. In the year that followed, the store's book sales fell by 22 percent.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`To make room for the café, the store removed a third of its bookshelves, reducing the range of titles on display.`},{text:`The café itself was profitable in its first year, drawing steady crowds on weekends.`},{text:`Book sales fell at independent bookstores nationwide during the same year, reflecting broader trends.`},{text:`The store extended its opening hours when the café was added, staying open two hours later.`},{text:`Many customers now buy coffee and browse without purchasing books, treating the store as a café.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A café should bring more browsers who buy books, yet book sales fell. Choice (A) resolves it: the café displaced a third of the shelves, so the smaller selection sold fewer books — the traffic gain could not offset the shrunken inventory. The distractors cite café profits, national trends, hours, or browsing without the displacement mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: a café was added to boost traffic, but book sales dropped 22%. For both to hold, the café must have harmed book-selling capacity more than the extra traffic helped. Choice (A) supplies the mechanism: a third of the bookshelves were removed, so the range of titles — the store's reason for existing — shrank sharply, and fewer titles meant fewer sales. The café succeeded at drawing people but cannibalized the book business physically. Choice (B) is the half-right trap: café profits explain the café, not the book-sales decline. Choice (C) is also half-right: a national downturn might contribute, but it does not explain this store's specific divergence between the traffic strategy and the sales result. Choice (D) deepens the mystery: longer hours should have helped sales. Choice (E) is the tempting near-miss — browsers who buy only coffee — but without the shelf displacement it does not explain a 22% sales collapse; it describes a symptom, not the mechanism.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Removing a third of the shelves shrank the title selection that drives book sales — the café boosted foot traffic but physically displaced the book business, reconciling both facts.`,`Café profitability explains the café's success, not the 22% drop in book sales. Addressing only one side is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`,`A national downturn might contribute, but it does not explain why this store's traffic-boosting strategy coincided with a sales collapse. This is the half-right trap.`,`Longer opening hours should have supported sales, making the decline stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Coffee-only browsers describe a symptom, but without the shelf displacement it does not explain why sales fell 22% rather than merely shifting. This is the half-right trap.`],generalLesson:`An addition that displaces the core product can hurt overall: when a traffic strategy coincides with falling sales, check what the new attraction physically replaced.`,misconceptionTags:[`cannibalization`,`displacement`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`half-right`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The café was supposed to bring browsers who buy books. What if the café hurt the book side of the store directly?`,`Which choice says the café physically replaced bookselling capacity?`,`The correct choice: a third of the shelves were removed for the café, shrinking the selection that drives sales.`],labels:{topic:`bookstore café`}},{id:`lr-g-217`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`An airline added daily flights on its most popular route. Despite the increase in available seats, the percentage of seats filled on the route rose.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The airline also reduced the number of flight attendants per flight to cut operating costs.`},{text:`A competing airline canceled all its flights on the same route, and its former passengers shifted to the remaining carrier.`},{text:`The new flights were scheduled at off-peak times when demand is usually low, limiting their appeal to business travelers especially.`},{text:`Ticket prices on the route remained unchanged throughout the entire period.`},{text:`The airline's on-time performance was unchanged during the same period, remaining above average.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`More seats should dilute the load factor, yet it rose. Choice (B) resolves it: a competitor exited the route, so its passengers flooded onto the remaining flights — demand grew faster than the new seats. The distractors address staffing, scheduling, prices, or punctuality without the demand surge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Load factor is passengers divided by seats. Seats rose, yet the ratio rose too — so passenger numbers must have grown even faster. An external demand shock explains it. Choice (B): a rival canceled all its flights on the route, dumping its former passengers onto this airline's expanded schedule. Both facts hold: more seats, and an even larger influx of passengers. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap: staffing does not affect the load factor. Choice (C) deepens the mystery: off-peak flights should have lowered the fill rate. Choice (D) is the half-right trap: unchanged prices might sustain demand, but they do not explain a surge in passengers outpacing new seats. Choice (E) is the irrelevant-true trap: unchanged punctuality cannot explain passengers outpacing new seats.`,choiceExplanations:[`Flight-attendant staffing has no bearing on the percentage of seats filled. An unrelated operational detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. The competitor's exit shifted its entire passenger base onto the remaining flights, so passenger growth outpaced the new seats — reconciling more capacity with a higher load factor.`,`Off-peak scheduling should dilute the fill rate, making the rise stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Unchanged prices might sustain existing demand but cannot explain a passenger surge large enough to outrun added seats. This is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`,`Unchanged on-time performance explains nothing about why seat occupancy rose faster than capacity — a static fact cannot drive the demand surge. This is the irrelevant-true trap.`],generalLesson:`Ratios follow the faster-moving side: when capacity and fill rates rise together, look for an external demand shock — like a competitor's exit — outpacing the new supply.`,misconceptionTags:[`ratio-arithmetic`,`demand-shock`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Load factor is passengers divided by seats. If seats rose and the ratio still rose, what must have happened to passengers?`,`Which choice delivers a surge of new passengers from outside the airline?`,`The correct choice: a competitor left the route, and its passengers moved over.`],labels:{topic:`airline load factor`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-g-218`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: KEEP. Filled-vs-total positions distinction via chronic vacancies; d4 honest.`}],stimulus:`When the state raised its minimum wage, economists predicted employment in low-wage sectors would fall. Instead, the number of filled low-wage positions rose over the following year.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Many businesses raised their prices to cover the higher labor costs, passing the increase on to consumers throughout the region.`},{text:`The wage increase applied only to workers over the age of 21, leaving teenage employment patterns largely unaffected.`},{text:`Neighboring states that did not raise the minimum wage saw employment hold steady in low-wage sectors throughout the same period.`},{text:`Several large employers announced layoffs in the same year, citing automation rather than wage levels in their public statements.`},{text:`Chronic unfilled vacancies plagued low-wage sectors, and the higher wage drew in workers from neighboring counties, filling long-empty positions.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Higher wages should price some jobs out, yet filled positions rose. Choice (E) resolves it through vacancies: the sectors had chronic unfilled openings, and the raise attracted workers who filled them — the count of filled positions grew even if total job slots did not. The distractors cite prices, age limits, neighbors, or layoffs without the vacancy mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The subtlety is "filled positions" versus "total positions." Economists predicted fewer jobs would exist; the statistic counts jobs actually filled. If many slots stood vacant, a wage hike can increase filled positions by drawing workers into empty slots — without creating a single new job. Choice (E) builds exactly this: chronic vacancies plus an in-migration of workers from neighboring counties at the higher wage, so filled positions rose. Both facts hold: the wage rose, and more positions were filled, with no contradiction. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: higher prices might offset labor costs but do not explain more filled positions. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: an age restriction does not explain the aggregate rise. Choice (C) is the half-right trap: steady neighbors provide no mechanism for this state's increase. Choice (D) deepens the mystery: layoffs should have reduced filled positions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Higher prices might protect margins, but they do not explain why more low-wage positions got filled. This addresses costs, not the employment count: the half-right trap.`,`An age restriction on the wage law does not explain the overall rise in filled positions. An unrelated policy detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Steady employment in neighboring states provides no mechanism for this state's increase. This is the half-right trap. It captures part of the structure while missing the load-bearing element, which is exactly why it fails.`,`Large-employer layoffs should have reduced filled positions, making the rise stranger. This deepens the mystery: the opposite trap. Since it points in the opposite direction from the argument's conclusion, it cannot supply what the stem asks for.`,`Correct. Chronic vacancies meant jobs existed but stood empty; the higher wage drew workers from neighboring counties into those slots, so filled positions rose — reconciling the wage hike with higher employment counts.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish stocks from flows and filled from vacant: when a price rise coincides with higher counts, check whether the count measures filled slots against a backlog of vacancies.`,misconceptionTags:[`filled-vs-vacant`,`vacancy-backlog`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The statistic counts filled positions. Could positions have existed but stood empty before the raise?`,`Which choice describes a backlog of vacancies waiting for workers?`,`The correct choice: chronic vacancies plus higher wages drew in county workers, filling long-empty slots.`],labels:{topic:`minimum wage employment`}},{id:`lr-g-219`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:1,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The town repaved all of its main roads last summer. In the months that followed, local car repair shops reported a surge in suspension and alignment work.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Smooth new pavement encourages faster driving, which wears out tires more quickly over time.`},{text:`During repaving, detours routed traffic over rough gravel roads for weeks, damaging suspensions and alignments.`},{text:`The repaving project finished several weeks ahead of schedule.`},{text:`Car repair shops raised their prices after the repaving was completed last summer.`},{text:`The town also repaved its bicycle paths during the same summer construction season.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Smooth new roads should mean fewer repairs, yet shops got busier. Choice (B) resolves it: the damage came not from the new pavement but from the construction period — weeks of detours over rough gravel battered suspensions. The distractors blame speed, cite schedules, or raise irrelevant details.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Two facts: roads were repaved (smooth), but suspension work surged. The resolution must locate the damage somewhere other than the finished roads. Choice (B) does: during construction, detours forced cars onto rough gravel for weeks, and that is what damaged suspensions — the surge reflects the construction period, not the new pavement. Choice (A) is the half-right trap: faster driving on smooth roads might wear tires, but the surge was in suspension and alignment work specifically, and the mechanism is speculative. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap: finishing early explains nothing about repairs. Choice (D) is also irrelevant: shop pricing does not explain the volume of suspension work. Choice (E) is the irrelevant-true trap: bicycle paths do not affect car suspensions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Faster driving might wear tires, but the surge was specifically in suspension and alignment work — and this does not explain damage tied to the repaving period. This is the half-right trap.`,`Correct. The damage came from weeks of detour traffic over rough gravel during construction — not from the smooth new pavement — reconciling the repaving with the repair surge.`,`Finishing ahead of schedule is project trivia that explains nothing about suspension damage. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Shop pricing affects revenue, not the volume of suspension work reported. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Bicycle paths have no bearing on car suspensions and alignments. This is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`],generalLesson:`The transition can cause what the result prevents: when an improvement coincides with harm, check whether the disruption of getting there did the damage.`,misconceptionTags:[`transition-effect`,`construction-period`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:50,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The finished roads are smooth — so where did the damage come from? Think about the repaving period itself.`,`Which choice describes what drivers endured while the work was underway?`,`The correct choice: weeks of detours over rough gravel battered suspensions during construction.`],labels:{topic:`road repaving repairs`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-g-220`,version:2,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full-LR classification: REVISE-fixed v2. Hostile review found two independently defensible distractors: (A) budget-cut mechanism and (E) harder-test mechanism both coherently resolved the paradox. Neutralized: (A) now grant-funded with no cuts (qualifier catch), (E) now paired with rising statewide averages (opposite trap). (D) Simpson’s-paradox composition mechanism is now uniquely correct; d5 honest.`}],stimulus:`A school district launched an intensive tutoring program for struggling students. At the end of the year, the district's average test score had fallen — even though independent evaluators confirmed the tutoring genuinely helped every student who received it.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The tutoring program was expensive, but it was paid for entirely from a dedicated state grant, and no other academic programs were cut that year.`},{text:`High-performing students took the test at exactly the same rate as in previous years, so the decline in the average did not come from them.`},{text:`Teachers reported that tutored students seemed more confident and engaged in class afterward.`},{text:`The program drew in low-performing students who had never taken the test; their newly counted scores lowered the average even as tutored students improved.`},{text:`The test was redesigned that year to include more difficult questions in every subject — yet statewide average scores rose that year.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Effective tutoring plus a falling average is reconciled by a changed test-taking pool: choice (D) explains that the program brought previously untested low performers into the testing pool, so the average fell even as every tutored student improved. The distractors note steady high-performer turnout and confidence, or raise budget and redesign hypotheses that the choices themselves close off — a grant qualifier rules out program cuts, and rising statewide averages rule out the harder test.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is the hardest item because it requires holding three facts at once: the tutoring helped every recipient, yet the average fell. Both can hold only if the composition of the averaged group changed: adding low scores to the pool drags the average down even when each added student improved relative to their own baseline. Choice (D) constructs exactly this Simpson's-paradox mechanism: the program drew in the lowest performers, many previously untested, so their scores entered the average for the first time — each improved by tutoring, but from such a low base that the district average fell. Choice (A) is the irrelevant-true trap with a qualifier catch: the program was expensive, but a dedicated grant covered the cost and no other programs were cut — the budget-cut mechanism a hasty reader infers is explicitly ruled out by the choice itself. Topical and true, yet with its mechanism removed it explains nothing. Choice (B) is the irrelevant-true trap: steady participation by high performers eliminates one alternative, but explains nothing about the falling average. Choice (C) is the irrelevant-true trap: confidence is not the test average. Choice (E) is the opposite trap: a redesigned, harder test should depress scores everywhere, yet statewide averages rose that year — so the redesign cannot explain this district's falling average, and the mystery deepens instead of resolving.`,choiceExplanations:[`The program was expensive, but a dedicated grant covered the cost and no other programs were cut — the budget-cut story a hasty reader infers is explicitly ruled out by the choice itself. Topical and true, but with its mechanism removed it explains nothing: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Steady test-taking by high performers rules out one alternative story, but it explains nothing about why the average fell while every tutored student improved. Eliminating an alternative is not a resolution: the irrelevant-true trap.`,`Student confidence is not the district's average test score. An unrelated feel-good detail is the irrelevant-true trap. Its truth is beside the point, since nothing in it connects to the inference being tested.`,`Correct. Previously untested low performers entered the testing pool through the program; their newly counted scores pulled the average down even though tutoring improved each of them — reconciling genuine gains with a falling average.`,`A redesigned, harder test should depress scores everywhere — yet statewide averages rose that year, so the redesign cannot explain this district's falling average. Instead of resolving the puzzle it deepens it: the opposite trap.`],generalLesson:`Averages follow pool composition: when an intervention helps everyone it touches yet the average falls, ask who newly entered the pool — and from how low a base.`,misconceptionTags:[`simpsons-paradox`,`pool-composition`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`lr-resolve-paradox`,`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Every tutored student improved, yet the average fell. What if the group being averaged is not the same group as last year?`,`Who might the program have brought into the testing pool for the first time?`,`The correct choice: previously untested low performers entered the pool — each improved, but their low scores dragged the average down.`],labels:{topic:`tutoring program paradox`}}],s=[{id:`lr-h-221`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The town council claims the new recreation center will pay for itself within five years. That projection, however, depends on membership numbers the town has never achieved at any of its existing facilities. Moreover, the estimate ignores maintenance costs that similar centers in neighboring towns have reported. So the council's promise of a self-funding center should not be taken at face value.`,stem:`The statement that the new recreation center will pay for itself within five years plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the claim that the rest of the argument is offered to undermine.`},{text:`It is a premise that supports the argument's conclusion about the council's promise.`},{text:`It is the main conclusion of the argument.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion that the maintenance-cost evidence is used to establish.`},{text:`It is background information describing the council's past projects.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument is an attack on the council’s projection: the membership and maintenance evidence is offered to discredit it, and the final sentence delivers the verdict against it. So the projection is the target of the argument, not a premise or the conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Start by separating what the author asserts from what the author attacks. The author never endorses the five-year payback projection; it is introduced as "the town council claims," which signals that it belongs to someone else. Everything after that first sentence works against the projection: the membership numbers have never been achieved, and the maintenance costs were ignored. The final sentence — "should not be taken at face value" — is the author’s own conclusion, and it is a verdict about the projection. A statement that an argument works to undermine cannot be one of its premises, since premises are offered as support, and it cannot be the conclusion, since the conclusion is the skeptical verdict itself. Nor is it mere background: the whole argument is organized around evaluating this one claim. The correct description is therefore that it is the claim the rest of the argument is offered to undermine.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The projection is introduced as the council’s claim and every subsequent sentence supplies reasons to doubt it, culminating in the verdict that it should not be taken at face value. In argument-part terms, it is the targeted claim — the thing being argued against — which is exactly the role this choice describes.`,`Incorrect and tempting because the projection appears first, where premises often sit (trap: opposite). But premises are statements the author endorses and uses as support; the author here does the reverse, offering the membership and maintenance evidence against the projection rather than building on it.`,`Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the author’s own final judgment — that the promise should not be taken at face value. The projection is the council’s claim, and the argument’s conclusion is a skeptical verdict about that claim, not the claim itself.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Nothing in the argument tries to establish the projection; the maintenance-cost evidence is offered to discredit it, not to prove it. An intermediate conclusion must be both supported by some claims and used to support others, and the projection satisfies neither condition.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). While the sentence does supply context, calling it mere background understates its structural role: the entire argument is organized as an evaluation of this specific claim, so it is the argument’s target rather than incidental scene-setting.`],generalLesson:`When an argument opens with someone else’s claim ("critics say," "the council claims"), that claim is usually the target being evaluated, not a premise — track who endorses each statement before assigning roles.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`wrong-conclusion`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Ask yourself: does the author agree with the payback projection, or argue against it? Who does each sentence belong to?`,`Notice the phrase "the town council claims" — what does that signal about whose claim the projection is? Then check which direction the evidence points.`,`The final sentence gives the author’s verdict about the projection. If the verdict is skeptical, what role must the projection itself be playing?`],labels:{topic:`recreation center funding debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The projection is introduced as the council claim and every later sentence works against it, so premise/conclusion/background distractors fail cleanly. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-222`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The Meridian lighthouse, built in 1874, has guided ships through the strait for 150 years. Its granite tower is the oldest surviving example of its design in the region. Last year the maritime authority proposed decommissioning it in favor of an automated beacon. The lighthouse should be preserved as a working navigational aid, not merely as a museum piece. Automated beacons have repeatedly failed during the strait’s winter storms, whereas the staffed lighthouse has never missed a night.`,stem:`The statement that the lighthouse should be preserved as a working navigational aid, not merely as a museum piece, plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is evidence cited to show that the lighthouse is historically significant.`},{text:`It is the argument’s main conclusion, supported by the comparison with automated beacons.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion that supports a further claim about museum funding.`},{text:`It is background information about the maritime authority’s decommissioning proposal.`},{text:`It is a premise reporting the reliability record of automated beacons.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The preservation sentence is a recommendation — what should be done — and the beacon-failure comparison is offered as the reason to accept it. Recommendations supported by reasons are conclusions, and since nothing in the argument goes beyond it, it is the main conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the argument’s recommendation: "should be preserved as a working navigational aid" is normative language, telling us what ought to happen rather than reporting a fact. Normative claims are conclusions far more often than premises. Next, check what supports it: the final sentence contrasts automated beacons, which fail in winter storms, with the staffed lighthouse, which has never missed a night. That contrast is a reason to keep the lighthouse working — it supports the preservation recommendation and nothing else. The earlier sentences about the 1874 construction and the decommissioning proposal supply context and the occasion for the argument, but they do not themselves follow from the recommendation. Because the preservation claim is supported by other claims and supports no further claim, it sits at the top of the argument’s structure: it is the main conclusion.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The historical facts about 1874 and the granite tower are the evidence for significance; the preservation sentence is a recommendation about what to do, not a factual report offered as evidence. It confuses the conclusion with the premises that set up the issue.`,`Correct. The sentence uses "should" to state the author’s recommendation, and the final sentence’s comparison — beacons fail in storms, the staffed lighthouse never misses a night — is offered as the reason to accept that recommendation. Since no further claim is drawn from it, it is the main conclusion.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The argument contains no claim about museum funding at all, so there is no further claim for this sentence to support. An intermediate conclusion needs something above it; here the preservation claim is the top of the structure.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The decommissioning proposal is the background occasion for the argument, but the preservation sentence is the author’s response to that proposal — a verdict about what should happen — not part of the background setup.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The reliability record of automated beacons appears in the final sentence and functions as a premise; the preservation sentence is the claim that premise is offered to support. This choice swaps their roles.`],generalLesson:`"Should" language marks a recommendation, and recommendations are conclusions far more often than premises — when you see normative wording, look for the reasons offered beneath it.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which sentence tells you what ought to happen, rather than reporting a fact? What kind of statement is that usually?`,`Look at the final sentence about beacons failing in storms. Is it giving a reason for the preservation claim, or is the preservation claim giving a reason for it?`,`Check whether anything in the argument is inferred FROM the preservation sentence. If nothing follows from it, where does it sit in the structure?`],labels:{topic:`lighthouse preservation debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The should-preserve sentence is the only normative claim, supported by the beacon comparison with nothing inferred beyond it. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-223`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Restoring the Blackwater marsh will cost the county about two million dollars. A treatment-plant upgrade that would achieve the same water-quality improvement would cost three million. So the restoration is clearly the cheaper way to meet the county’s water-quality goal. Given that it is the cheaper option, the council should approve the restoration without further delay.`,stem:`The statement that the restoration is clearly the cheaper way to meet the county’s water-quality goal plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the argument’s main conclusion.`},{text:`It is a premise reporting the cost of the treatment-plant upgrade, offered as evidence for the cost comparison.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion, supported by the cost comparison and supporting the approval recommendation.`},{text:`It is background information about the county’s finances.`},{text:`It is a prediction about how marsh restoration costs will change in the future.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The cheaper-way claim is inferred from the two cost figures ("so" marks the inference) and then used as the reason the council should approve the project ("given that"). A claim that is both supported and supporting is an intermediate conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the argument’s structure in layers. The bottom layer is factual: the marsh costs two million, the plant upgrade costs three million. The word "so" then introduces an inference drawn from those figures — that restoration is the cheaper way to meet the goal. That inference is not the end of the argument, because the final sentence draws a further recommendation from it: given that it is cheaper, the council should approve it. So the cheaper-way claim has a double role: it is supported by the cost premises below it and it supports the approval recommendation above it. That double role is the definition of an intermediate conclusion (also called a subsidiary conclusion). It cannot be the main conclusion, because the argument continues past it to the "should approve" recommendation, and it cannot be a mere premise, because it is itself inferred rather than simply reported.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The argument continues past the cheaper-way claim to a further recommendation — that the council should approve the restoration. The main conclusion is the final "should" statement, not the cost comparison that leads up to it.`,`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The treatment-plant cost is one of the raw figures; the cheaper-way claim is an inference drawn from comparing the two figures, as the word "so" signals. This choice mistakes an inferred claim for a reported fact.`,`Correct. The "so" shows it is inferred from the two-million versus three-million comparison, and "given that it is the cheaper option" shows it is then used as the reason for the approval recommendation. Being both supported and supporting makes it an intermediate conclusion.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The county’s finances are the subject matter, but this sentence does argumentative work: it bridges the raw cost figures and the final recommendation. Background information would sit outside the chain of reasoning; this claim sits inside it.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The claim compares present cost estimates; it says nothing about how costs will change over time. There is no forecast here, only a comparison of two current figures.`],generalLesson:`A claim that is both inferred from premises and used to support a further claim is an intermediate conclusion — look for it sitting between the evidence and the final recommendation, often marked by "so" below and "given that" above.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`restates-premise`,`irrelevant-true`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The word "so" introduces the target sentence. What does "so" usually signal — a reported fact or an inference?`,`After the target sentence, the argument says "given that it is the cheaper option, the council should approve." What work is the target sentence doing there?`,`A claim that is inferred from evidence below it and used as a reason above it has a specific name. What is it?`],labels:{topic:`marsh restoration cost debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The so-marked cheaper-way claim is inferred from the cost figures and then feeds the approval recommendation - textbook intermediate conclusion. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-224`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`St. Aubin’s emergency department adopted a new triage protocol last spring, and average patient wait times have since fallen eighteen percent. The hospital’s director credits the protocol, and she is probably right. After all, the decline began the very month the protocol took effect, and no other operational changes were made at the hospital during that period.`,stem:`The statement that no other operational changes were made at the hospital during that period plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the argument’s main conclusion.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion about when the wait-time decline began.`},{text:`It is the evidence that establishes that wait times declined at all.`},{text:`It is a premise ruling out alternative explanations and supporting the causal conclusion.`},{text:`It is an unstated assumption that the argument requires but never asserts.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The no-other-changes claim is asserted as a fact ("after all" introduces supporting reasons), and its job is defensive: by eliminating rival causes, it shores up the conclusion that the protocol caused the decline. Stated, supportive, and defensive — that is a premise, not an assumption or a conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a causal argument: the conclusion is that the triage protocol caused the wait-time decline ("the director credits the protocol, and she is probably right"). Causal conclusions are vulnerable to alternative explanations, and the final sentence defends against them in two moves: the timing correlation ("the decline began the very month") and the elimination of rivals ("no other operational changes were made"). The target statement is introduced by "after all," which marks premises, not conclusions — the author is giving reasons, not drawing a new inference. Its specific function is to rule out competing causes, which strengthens the causal attribution without being the conclusion itself. The tempting distractor calls it an unstated assumption, but assumptions are by definition unstated, and this claim is stated outright. Nor does it establish the decline itself; the eighteen-percent figure does that. Its role is supportive and defensive: a premise that clears the field of alternative explanations.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the causal attribution — that the protocol caused the decline ("she is probably right"). The no-other-changes claim is offered as a reason for that verdict, not as the verdict itself.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The timing of the decline is established by the other half of the final sentence ("the decline began the very month"); the target statement says nothing about when anything began. It addresses what else changed, not when the decline started.`,`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The eighteen-percent figure establishes that wait times declined. The no-other-changes claim takes the decline as given and addresses a different question: whether something besides the protocol caused it.`,`Correct. Introduced by "after all" as a supporting reason, the claim is stated outright and does defensive work: eliminating rival causes so the causal conclusion — the protocol deserves the credit — stands on firmer ground. That is the role of a premise in a causal argument.`,`Incorrect and the hardest distractor (trap: unsupported). It is true that the argument needs rival causes to be absent, but this need is stated explicitly, not left unstated. An assumption, strictly speaking, is required but unasserted; a claim the author asserts outright is a premise by definition.`],generalLesson:`In causal arguments, "nothing else changed" claims are stated premises doing defensive work — don’t mistake an explicitly asserted defense against alternative explanations for an unstated assumption.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`scope-shift`,`restates-premise`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the author ultimately trying to convince you of — that something is true of the world, or that nothing else changed? Which is the conclusion?`,`The phrase "after all" introduces the final sentence. Does "after all" introduce a new conclusion or give reasons for one already stated?`,`Consider the last answer choice carefully: is the no-other-changes claim actually unstated? What does that tell you about whether it can be an assumption?`],labels:{topic:`hospital triage protocol evaluation`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The no-other-changes claim is stated outright as defensive support for the causal conclusion, so the unstated-assumption distractor fails on statedness. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-225`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Downtown merchants have complained that the new parking meters are driving customers away. They are wrong to blame the meters. Two years ago the city raised meter rates with no measurable effect on foot traffic. What has changed since then is the opening of the Riverside mall, which offers free parking and now draws shoppers who used to come downtown. The meters are not the problem; the mall is.`,stem:`The statement that two years ago the city raised meter rates with no measurable effect on foot traffic plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the argument’s main conclusion.`},{text:`It is background information about the city’s parking history.`},{text:`It is a claim the argument is offered to refute.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion supported by the claim about the Riverside mall.`},{text:`It is a premise supporting the intermediate conclusion that the merchants wrongly blame the meters.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The earlier rate hike with no effect on foot traffic is historical evidence offered to back the verdict that the merchants are wrong to blame the meters — and that verdict in turn supports the final claim that the mall is the real problem. Evidence for an intermediate verdict is a premise.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Untangle the argument’s two verdicts. The final sentence — "the meters are not the problem; the mall is" — is the ultimate conclusion, the last word on where blame lies. Before it comes an earlier verdict: "they are wrong to blame the meters." That earlier verdict is itself supported (by the rate-hike history) and then used to support the final verdict (together with the mall evidence), which makes it an intermediate conclusion. The target statement — the earlier hike with no effect on foot traffic — is a reported historical fact offered as evidence for that intermediate verdict: if higher rates didn’t hurt foot traffic before, the meters are unlikely to be hurting it now. It is not the main conclusion, it is not refuted (the argument relies on it), and it is not supported by the mall claim (the mall claim points in a different direction, toward the final verdict). It is a premise feeding the intermediate conclusion.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the final allocation of blame — "the meters are not the problem; the mall is." The rate-hike history is evidence offered early in the argument, not the verdict the argument drives toward.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). It is historical, but it is not idle context: it does direct argumentative work as evidence that meters don’t drive customers away. Background would sit outside the reasoning; this claim is a load-bearing part of it.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument endorses and relies on the rate-hike history; it is the merchants’ complaint — that meters drive customers away — that gets refuted. This choice reverses which claim is attacked and which is wielded.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The mall claim supports the final verdict that the mall is the problem; it does not support the rate-hike history, which is an independent piece of evidence. This choice miswires the argument’s structure by attaching the target to the wrong support.`,`Correct. The rate-hike fact is reported as evidence ("two years ago... with no measurable effect"), and it backs the intermediate verdict that the merchants are wrong to blame the meters — the verdict that, together with the mall evidence, yields the final conclusion. Evidence supporting an intermediate conclusion is a premise.`],generalLesson:`When an argument contains two verdicts, map which evidence supports which: a premise for the earlier (intermediate) verdict is not automatically evidence for the final one — track the support arrows separately.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How many verdicts does the argument deliver? Compare "they are wrong to blame the meters" with the final sentence — which one is the last word?`,`The target sentence reports a historical fact. Is the argument trying to prove that fact, or using that fact to prove something else?`,`Which claim does the rate-hike history most directly back: the verdict about the merchants’ blame, or the final verdict about the mall?`],labels:{topic:`downtown parking meters dispute`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The rate-hike history is evidence for the intermediate verdict that the merchants blame the wrong cause; the mall claim feeds the final verdict instead. Two-layer structure mapped. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-226`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:2,distractorProximity:1,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Requiring cyclists to wear helmets is like requiring pedestrians to wear shin guards. In both cases the rule would impose a burden on a safe activity in the name of preventing rare injuries. Just as a shin-guard mandate for pedestrians would be an overreach, so too is a helmet mandate for cyclists.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`drawing an analogy between two cases, treating what holds for one as holding for the other.`},{text:`citing statistical evidence about the frequency of cycling injuries.`},{text:`deriving a contradiction from the assumptions of supporters of the mandate.`},{text:`appealing to the authority of medical experts on head injuries.`},{text:`showing that a similar mandate has already failed in another city.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument’s entire move is comparative: helmets-for-cyclists is likened to shin-guards-for-pedestrians, the latter is judged an overreach, and that judgment is transferred to the former. That is reasoning by analogy.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Method questions ask what the argument does, not whether it does it well. Strip this argument to its skeleton: case A (helmet mandate) is said to be like case B (shin-guard mandate) in the relevant respects — a burden on a safe activity to prevent rare injuries. Case B is then evaluated: it would be an overreach. Finally, that evaluation is carried over to case A: the helmet mandate is likewise an overreach. Every step of the reasoning runs through the comparison; remove the analogy and nothing remains. The argument cites no injury statistics, derives no contradiction from anyone’s assumptions, invokes no experts, and points to no city where a mandate failed. The only argumentative device on display is the analogy and the transfer of a verdict across it, which is exactly what the correct choice describes.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The argument explicitly sets up the comparison ("is like"), identifies the shared relevant features (burden on a safe activity, rare injuries), judges the parallel case an overreach, and transfers that judgment to helmets. That sequence is the textbook structure of reasoning by analogy.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The argument mentions "rare injuries" in passing but cites no statistics, studies, or numbers of any kind. A choice describing evidence the argument never offers cannot describe its method.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Deriving a contradiction — showing an opponent’s view leads to absurdity — is a real method, but this argument never engages the mandate supporters’ assumptions at all; it simply offers its own parallel case.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No experts, doctors, or studies are mentioned anywhere in the stimulus. The argument’s authority is the reader’s own judgment that the shin-guard rule would be an overreach, not any expert testimony.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The argument is entirely hypothetical — "would be an overreach" — and never claims any mandate was tried and failed. A method choice must describe what the argument actually does, not a neighboring strategy it never uses.`],generalLesson:`To identify a method, strip the argument to its skeleton and ask what device carries the reasoning — here every step runs through the comparison, so the method is analogy regardless of whether the analogy is a good one.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:55,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the argument actually doing — is it giving you data, quoting experts, or comparing two situations?`,`Look at the first sentence. What two things are being compared, and what happens to the judgment about one of them?`,`If you deleted the shin-guard comparison, would any argument remain? What does that tell you about the method?`],labels:{topic:`bicycle helmet mandate analogy`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Every step of the reasoning runs through the helmet/shin-guard comparison; no statistics, experts, or precedent appear. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-227`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Critics say the Gazette’s use of anonymous sources makes its reporting untrustworthy. But there is a difference between granting anonymity to a whistleblower who fears retaliation and granting it to an official spinning a story. The Gazette’s recent investigation relied on the first kind, not the second. So the criticism misses its mark.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`questioning the motives of the critics rather than addressing the substance of their argument against anonymity.`},{text:`drawing a distinction between two kinds of anonymous sourcing and arguing that an objection to one does not apply to the other.`},{text:`disputing the definition of trustworthy reporting without offering a clearer definition in its place.`},{text:`presenting survey data on how readers view anonymous sources, then generalizing from that limited sample.`},{text:`arguing that anonymous sourcing is common in journalism and therefore an acceptable practice.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The argument’s pivot is the contrast between two kinds of anonymity — whistleblower protection versus official spin — followed by classifying the Gazette’s case under the innocent kind. Splitting a criticized category and exempting the case at hand is the method of distinction.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Watch where the argument turns. It opens with the critics’ blanket objection to anonymous sources, then pivots on "but there is a difference between" — the classic signal of a distinction being drawn. The distinction separates anonymity that shields vulnerable whistleblowers from anonymity that serves self-interested spin. The next sentence then classifies the Gazette’s investigation under the first, innocent heading. The conclusion follows: a criticism aimed (at best) at the second kind misses a case of the first kind. That is the method of distinction: show the objection trades on lumping together things that differ in the relevant respect, then place the case at hand on the safe side of the split. The argument never impugns the critics’ motives, never debates what "trustworthy" means, cites no surveys, and never appeals to how common the practice is.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). An ad hominem attack would target the critics’ character or motives; this argument never mentions who the critics are or why they object. It engages the objection’s content by narrowing its scope, which is the opposite of dodging it.`,`Correct. The "difference between" sentence draws the distinction, the next sentence classifies the Gazette’s investigation under the whistleblower heading, and the conclusion follows: the blanket criticism does not reach this case. Each step of the reasoning runs through the distinction.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). The argument never contests what counts as trustworthy reporting; it accepts the critics’ standard and argues the Gazette meets it in this instance. Redefining terms is a different method entirely.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No surveys, polls, or reader data appear anywhere in the stimulus. A method choice must describe the argument’s actual moves, and there is no empirical move here at all.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The argument never claims the practice is widespread or that commonness would excuse it. Even if that were true, it would be a different argument from the distinction-based defense actually given.`],generalLesson:`"There is a difference between X and Y" is the signature of the method of distinction — the argument then typically classifies the case at hand on the favorable side of the split to blunt a general objection.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`answers-different-question`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Find the sentence where the argument pivots from the critics’ claim to its own response. What words mark the pivot?`,`The argument separates anonymous sourcing into two kinds. What does it do with that separation in the next sentence?`,`Does the argument attack the critics, define "trustworthy," or cite data — or does it do something with the two kinds of anonymity?`],labels:{topic:`anonymous sources in journalism`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The pivot draws a whistleblower-vs-spin distinction and classifies the case under the innocent heading; ad-hominem and survey distractors describe moves never made. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-228`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-causation`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`After Northwind switched to four-day weeks, employee turnover dropped by a third. The drop cannot be explained by pay raises, since salaries were frozen, nor by a shrinking job market, since openings in the sector actually grew. The schedule change is the best explanation for the improved retention.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`appealing to surveys of employee satisfaction with the new schedule.`},{text:`arguing from a single employee’s experience to a general claim about the company.`},{text:`defending a causal explanation by eliminating rival explanations of the observed drop.`},{text:`attacking the credibility of critics of the four-day week.`},{text:`inferring causation from the mere correlation between the schedule change and the turnover drop.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument does more than note a correlation: it explicitly rules out the two most plausible rival causes — pay raises and labor-market shifts — before concluding the schedule change is the best explanation. That elimination of alternatives is its method.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Separate what the argument observes from what it does with the observation. The observation is a correlation: four-day weeks arrived, turnover fell. A bare correlation would make the final choice tempting, but the argument’s middle sentences do extra work: they consider and reject specific alternative explanations. Pay raises are ruled out because salaries were frozen; a shrinking job market is ruled out because sector openings grew. Only after clearing those rivals does the argument conclude that the schedule change is "the best explanation." That structure — propose a causal hypothesis, eliminate the leading competitors, then endorse the survivor — is the method of elimination (inference to the best explanation). The argument cites no surveys, generalizes from no single employee, and attacks no critics, so those choices describe moves it never makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No surveys or satisfaction data are mentioned; the evidence is turnover figures plus the absence of pay raises and market shrinkage. A method must describe moves the argument actually makes.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). The argument reasons from company-wide turnover statistics, not from any individual’s story. Generalizing from a single case is a different method from the elimination of rivals performed here.`,`Correct. The middle sentences each take a rival explanation — raises, market conditions — and knock it down with a fact, leaving the schedule change as the surviving explanation. Defending a causal claim by eliminating its competitors is exactly the argument’s procedure.`,`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument never mentions critics of the four-day week, let alone their credibility. It engages rival explanations, not rival people — an important difference.`,`Incorrect and the subtlest distractor (trap: half-right). The argument does begin from a correlation, but it does not rest on the correlation alone; the elimination sentences are precisely what lift it beyond mere "correlation implies causation." This choice describes a weaker argument than the one given.`],generalLesson:`When an argument lists rival explanations only to knock each down before endorsing its own, the method is elimination — and that extra work is what separates it from a bare correlation-to-causation leap.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the correlation the argument starts from? Then ask: does the argument stop there, or do the middle sentences do something more?`,`Each middle sentence follows the pattern "the drop cannot be explained by X, since..." What is the argument doing to X?`,`Compare the last answer choice with what the middle sentences accomplish. Does the argument rely on correlation alone?`],labels:{topic:`four-day workweek and turnover`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The argument eliminates pay raises and market shifts before endorsing the schedule change, so the mere-correlation distractor describes a weaker argument. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-229`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Either the defendant’s fingerprints got on the vault through the break-in or they were planted there afterward. If they were planted, then someone with access to the evidence room tampered with the investigation. If they got there through the break-in, then the defendant was at the scene. Either way, the defense’s claim that the defendant was never near the bank collapses.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`questioning whether the fingerprint evidence is authentic.`},{text:`relying on character evidence about the defendant’s past.`},{text:`arguing that the two explanations of the fingerprints are equally likely.`},{text:`presenting two alternatives as exhaustive, each undermining the same opposing claim.`},{text:`appealing to legal precedent about planted evidence.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument sets up a fork — break-in or planting — traces out what follows from each branch, and shows both branches refute the defense’s alibi. That fork-and-refute-both-branches structure is a dilemma (constructive dilemma).`,explanationWalkthrough:`Diagram the argument’s skeleton. Step one lays down an either/or: the prints arrived via the break-in or via planting. Note the argument treats these as covering the possibilities — it never defends the split, it just proceeds as if the two options are exhaustive. Step two follows each branch to its consequence: planting implies evidence-room tampering; break-in implies the defendant was at the scene. Step three observes that both consequences are incompatible with the defense’s claim of never being near the bank, so the defense’s claim fails "either way." The power of the move is that it never needs to decide which branch is true: each branch independently sinks the same target. That is the dilemma method. The argument never questions the prints’ authenticity (it grants them on both branches), never discusses the defendant’s character, never compares the branches’ likelihoods, and cites no precedent.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument takes the fingerprints as given on both branches — planted or genuine — and reasons from each. Questioning their authenticity would undercut the argument’s own setup rather than describe its method.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The defendant’s character, history, and past conduct are never mentioned. The argument is built entirely from the two hypotheses about the prints and their consequences.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Likelihood never enters the reasoning; the argument’s force comes from showing that the defense loses on both branches regardless of which is true. Comparing probabilities is a different move from the fork-and-refute-both structure used here.`,`Correct. The either/or is presented as exhaustive, each alternative is developed (tampering vs. presence at the scene), and each development is shown to contradict the defense’s alibi. Presenting exhaustive alternatives that converge on the same refutation is the dilemma method.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No cases, statutes, or prior rulings are cited. The argument runs on pure conditional reasoning from its two hypotheses, not on legal authority.`],generalLesson:`A dilemma works by making the opponent lose on every branch: spot the either/or setup, check that each branch is developed, and confirm both branches converge on the same target — the arguer never needs to pick a branch.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the first sentence doing? How many possibilities does it lay out, and does the argument ever choose between them?`,`Follow each possibility to its consequence. What does the planting branch imply, and what does the break-in branch imply?`,`Notice the phrase "either way." What single claim loses under both branches?`],labels:{topic:`fingerprint evidence dilemma`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Exhaustive either/or with both branches refuting the same alibi - constructive dilemma; likelihood and authenticity distractors miss the structure. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-230`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:3,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:2,viewpointComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`adversarial-reviewed`,stimulus:`The utility company insists that removing the Alder dam would devastate the local economy. Yet when the nearby Cedar dam was removed five years ago, the town of Millbrook saw its tourism revenue double within three years. A single case cannot settle every dispute, but it is enough to show that the company’s sweeping prediction is unwarranted.`,stem:`The argument proceeds by`,choices:[{text:`appealing to the findings of professional economists.`},{text:`arguing that the company has a financial motive to mislead the public.`},{text:`showing that Millbrook and the Alder dam region are identical in every relevant respect.`},{text:`relying on a statistical analysis of dam removals nationwide.`},{text:`offering a counterexample to undermine a sweeping general prediction.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The company makes an exceptionless prediction — removal devastates the economy — and the argument answers with one case where removal was followed by an economic boom. A single contrary instance refutes a "would devastate" style generalization, and the argument modestly claims only that much.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the exact target: the company’s claim is a sweeping prediction with no built-in exceptions — removing the dam "would devastate" the local economy. Sweeping claims are refuted by a single counterexample, and that is precisely the weapon deployed here. The Cedar dam removal is presented as one actual case in which removal was followed not by devastation but by doubled tourism revenue. Note the argument’s modesty, which is part of its method: it concedes that "a single case cannot settle every dispute" and claims only that the counterexample shows the prediction to be "unwarranted" — it does not pretend to prove removal is always beneficial. That restraint distinguishes the move from overclaiming. The argument cites no economists, never discusses the company’s motives, claims no identity between the two regions, and offers no nationwide statistics.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No economists, studies, or expert findings are cited; the only evidence is the Millbrook case itself. Expert appeal is a different method from the case-based refutation actually used.`,`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument never speculates about why the company makes its claim — its motives are untouched. Attacking motives would be an ad hominem move, which is not the method on display here.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument never claims the two regions are identical; it needs only the weaker point that one contrary case undercuts an exceptionless prediction. Demanding full identity would describe a stronger argument than the modest one given.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). One town’s experience is cited, not a statistical survey of removals. A nationwide analysis would be a different — and much heavier — evidentiary method.`,`Correct. The company’s prediction is framed without exceptions, so one contrary case — removal followed by an economic boom — suffices to show it unwarranted. The argument’s explicit modesty ("a single case cannot settle every dispute") confirms the move is counterexample-based refutation, not proof of a rival generalization.`],generalLesson:`An exceptionless prediction ("would devastate") can be undermined by a single counterexample — and the careful arguer claims only that the prediction is unwarranted, not that the opposite generalization is proven.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What exactly is the company predicting — a qualified trend or an exceptionless outcome? How many contrary cases would it take to dent that kind of claim?`,`What does the Millbrook case show, and how much does the argument claim it shows? Read the last sentence carefully.`,`Does the argument cite experts, discuss motives, or survey many dams — or does it do something simpler with a single case?`],labels:{topic:`dam removal economic prediction`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP-INSTRUCTIONAL (instructional use only - prose/structure too artificial for assessment; difficulty corrected d4->d3); leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-h-231`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,viewpointComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Mara: The community garden should switch to organic methods. The current pesticides are washing into the creek, and several gardeners have complained of skin irritation after spraying days.

Devon: Switching to organic methods would cut our harvest nearly in half, and the garden’s main purpose is to supply the food bank. The runoff problem can be handled with better drainage, and the irritation complaints are unproven.`,stem:`The point at issue between Mara and Devon is whether`,choices:[{text:`the garden should adopt organic growing methods.`},{text:`the garden’s harvest would decline under organic methods.`},{text:`better drainage could reduce the pesticide runoff.`},{text:`the food bank depends on the garden’s harvest.`},{text:`organic methods are more expensive than using pesticides.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Mara explicitly says the garden should switch to organic methods; Devon argues against switching (lost harvest, food-bank mission, runoff fixable another way). They directly contradict each other on what the garden should do.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A point-at-issue answer must be a proposition one speaker affirms and the other denies. Start with each speaker’s bottom line. Mara’s first sentence is a "should" claim: the garden should switch to organic methods. Devon’s response is a case against switching: it would halve the harvest, threaten the food-bank mission, and the runoff problem has another fix. Those positions are direct opposites on the same question — should the garden go organic? The distractors fail the affirm/deny test. The harvest decline is asserted only by Devon; Mara never addresses it. Better drainage is proposed only by Devon. Food-bank dependence and cost comparisons are addressed by neither speaker. Only the adoption question draws a "yes" from one speaker and a "no" from the other, which is exactly what "point at issue" requires.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Mara affirms it outright ("should switch to organic methods"), and Devon’s entire reply is a case against switching — lost harvest, endangered food-bank mission, and an alternative fix for runoff. One says yes, the other says no: that is the definition of a point at issue.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Devon addresses the harvest effect; Mara says nothing about yields. A point at issue requires both speakers to take stands, and here only one does.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Better drainage is Devon’s proposal alone; Mara never discusses drainage. With only one speaker on the record, there is no disagreement to identify.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Devon says supplying the food bank is the garden’s main purpose, but neither speaker claims the food bank depends on the garden. The proposition as worded goes beyond anything either asserts.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Cost is never mentioned by either speaker. It may sound like a natural thing to dispute, but a point-at-issue answer must come from what the speakers actually said, not from what they might have said.`],generalLesson:`For point-at-issue, test every candidate with the affirm/deny rule: one speaker must clearly say yes and the other clearly say no. If only one speaker addresses it, it cannot be the point at issue.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`answers-different-question`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is Mara’s bottom line — what does she think should happen? And what is Devon arguing for?`,`For each answer choice, ask: does Mara take a stand on it? Does Devon? You need a yes from one and a no from the other.`,`Which choice has Mara saying "should" and Devon effectively saying "should not"?`],labels:{topic:`community garden organic switch`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Clean yes/no split on the should-adopt proposition; every distractor is addressed by only one speaker or neither. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-232`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3,viewpointComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Council Member A: Cutting Sunday hours at the public library will save the city $200,000 a year. Sunday is the quietest day, and most patrons can visit on Saturdays instead.

Librarian B: Sunday is the only day many working parents can bring their children, and those families have no Saturday alternative. The projected savings ignore the cost of the security upgrades the building will need if it sits empty on Sundays.`,stem:`The point at issue between A and B is whether`,choices:[{text:`the library should cut its Sunday hours rather than its weekday hours.`},{text:`most library patrons would be able to visit on Saturdays instead of Sundays.`},{text:`the library building needs security upgrades if it sits empty on Sundays.`},{text:`Sunday is the quietest day at the library, with the fewest recorded visits of any day.`},{text:`the city needs to save money across all of its public services this year.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`A asserts most patrons can come Saturdays instead; B directly denies it, saying Sunday is the only day many working parents can bring their children. That is a clean yes/no split — the other options are addressed by only one speaker or neither.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the affirm/deny test to each candidate. The tempting first choice asks whether the library should cut Sunday hours. A clearly favors the cut, but B never takes a "should" position — B disputes A’s factual premises (patron flexibility, real savings) without ever saying whether the cut should happen. Without a "no" from B, there is no point at issue there. The second choice is the genuine split: A says "most patrons can visit on Saturdays instead" (yes), and B says Sunday is "the only day many working parents can bring their children" with "no Saturday alternative" (no). The remaining choices fail because only one speaker addresses them — security upgrades appear only in B’s remarks, Sunday quietness only in A’s — or because neither addresses them, as with the city’s need to save money.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect and the main trap (trap: unsupported). A favors the cut, but B never says whether the cut should or should not happen — B only disputes A’s supporting claims. A policy recommendation one speaker never addresses cannot be the point at issue, however natural it feels.`,`Correct. A affirms it ("most patrons can visit on Saturdays instead") and B denies it ("Sunday is the only day many working parents can bring their children, and those families have no Saturday alternative"). That is a direct yes/no contradiction on the same proposition.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only B mentions security upgrades; A says nothing about them at all. A point at issue requires both speakers to take stands on the same proposition, and a disagreement needs two sides — here only one speaker is on the record, so there is no clash to identify.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only A claims Sunday is the quietest day; B never addresses traffic levels at all. A point at issue requires both speakers to take opposing stands on the same proposition, and a claim addressed by only one speaker cannot meet that test.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Neither speaker discusses whether the city needs to save money. It is background motivation at best, not something either affirms or denies.`],generalLesson:`Disputing someone’s reasons is not the same as opposing their recommendation — a point-at-issue answer needs both speakers to take a stand on the very same proposition, so watch for "should" claims only one speaker makes.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does speaker B ever say whether the cut should happen? What does B actually dispute?`,`Find a claim that A asserts as a fact and B contradicts as a fact. Where do their factual claims collide?`,`A says most patrons can switch days. What does B say about working parents and Sundays?`],labels:{topic:`library Sunday hours cut`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-h-233`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3,viewpointComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Dr. Ito: The city’s new reflective-pavement program will lower summer street temperatures by several degrees. Laboratory tests show the coating reflects 40 percent more sunlight than asphalt.

Dr. Okafor: Laboratory tests don’t settle the question. The coating degrades within two years under heavy traffic, and the city’s streets carry far more traffic than the test roads did. The program will not deliver the promised cooling.`,stem:`The point at issue between Dr. Ito and Dr. Okafor is whether`,choices:[{text:`laboratory tests are generally unreliable.`},{text:`the coating reflects more sunlight than asphalt in laboratory tests.`},{text:`the reflective-pavement program will reduce summer street temperatures.`},{text:`heavy traffic degrades the coating.`},{text:`the city’s streets carry more traffic than the test roads did.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Ito predicts the program will lower street temperatures, while Okafor flatly denies it will deliver the promised cooling. That is a direct yes/no split on the program’s effectiveness. The remaining options are conceded, one-sided, or broader than what either speaker claims, so they cannot be the point at issue.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Locate each speaker’s verdict on the program’s real-world effect. Ito’s prediction is explicit: the program "will lower summer street temperatures by several degrees." Okafor’s verdict is equally explicit and opposite: the program "will not deliver the promised cooling." Those two claims cannot both be true, so the program’s effectiveness is genuinely at issue. The distractors dissolve under the affirm/deny test. Okafor says lab tests "don’t settle the question," which is narrower than calling them generally unreliable — and Ito never defends their general reliability. The 40-percent reflectance figure is Ito’s claim alone; Okafor never disputes the lab number, only its real-world relevance. The degradation and traffic claims appear only in Okafor’s remarks. Only the program’s cooling effect draws a yes from one speaker and a no from the other.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). Okafor says lab tests "don’t settle the question" — a narrow claim about this inference — not that lab tests are generally unreliable. This choice inflates her position beyond what she said, so it is not something she affirms.`,`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). The 40-percent figure is Ito’s uncontested premise; Okafor never disputes the lab result, only whether it settles the real-world question. An undisputed premise cannot be the point at issue.`,`Correct. Ito affirms the program will lower street temperatures by several degrees; Okafor denies it will deliver the promised cooling at all. One predicts success, the other predicts failure — a textbook point at issue.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Okafor asserts degradation under heavy traffic; Ito never addresses durability. A one-sided claim has no opponent and therefore cannot be what they disagree about.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Okafor compares the city’s traffic to the test roads’. Ito is silent on traffic levels, so there is no clash on this proposition.`],generalLesson:`When one speaker cites lab results and the other disputes their real-world relevance, the point at issue is usually the real-world prediction itself — not the lab numbers, which often go uncontested.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does each speaker predict will happen on actual city streets — not in the lab, but in the real world?`,`Okafor never disputes the 40-percent lab figure. If she concedes the lab result, what is she actually denying?`,`Find the sentence in each speech that gives a verdict on the program’s cooling effect. Can both verdicts be true?`],labels:{topic:`reflective pavement cooling dispute`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-h-234`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,viewpointComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Alvarez: Congestion pricing has cut traffic in every city that has tried it, and our downtown gridlock is worse than any of theirs was. If we want cleaner air and faster buses, we should adopt it here.

Brooks: Those other cities all paired pricing with major transit expansions, which we cannot afford. Pricing alone, without better transit options, will simply punish drivers who have no alternative.`,stem:`The point at issue between Alvarez and Brooks is whether`,choices:[{text:`congestion pricing reduced traffic in the cities that have tried it.`},{text:`the city can afford a major transit expansion.`},{text:`pricing without better transit options would harm drivers who lack alternatives.`},{text:`the city should adopt congestion pricing.`},{text:`cleaner air and faster buses are desirable goals.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Alvarez explicitly recommends adopting congestion pricing; Brooks’s reply — that pricing alone will "simply punish drivers who have no alternative" — is a case against adopting it. They split yes/no on the policy, while agreeing (or staying silent) on the distractors.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This one is tricky because the speakers seem to argue past each other, so test each candidate strictly. The first choice looks disputed but isn’t: Alvarez says pricing cut traffic elsewhere, and Brooks’s reply ("those other cities all paired pricing with transit expansions") accepts that traffic fell — Brooks disputes the cause, not the outcome. So both speakers are consistent with the claim that traffic fell; there is no yes/no split. Affordability of transit expansion is Brooks’s claim alone. Harm to drivers without alternatives is Brooks’s claim alone. Desirability of cleaner air is disputed by neither. The genuine split is the policy question: Alvarez says "we should adopt it here" (yes), while Brooks argues that adopting it without transit investment would merely punish captive drivers — a clear case against adoption (no). Implied positions count: Brooks never utters "should not," but a case that adoption would only punish drivers is unmistakably opposition.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect and the subtlest trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Brooks never denies that traffic fell in those cities — "those other cities all paired pricing with transit expansions" concedes the outcome while disputing its cause. Since both speakers are consistent with the claim, it is not at issue.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Brooks addresses affordability ("which we cannot afford"); Alvarez never discusses the transit budget or what the city can fund. A point at issue requires a yes from one speaker and a no from the other, and one-sided claims like this one cannot supply both sides.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Brooks makes the harm claim; Alvarez never addresses what pricing-without-transit would do to drivers. There is no opposing stand from Alvarez.`,`Correct. Alvarez explicitly recommends adoption ("we should adopt it here"), and Brooks’s argument that pricing alone would "simply punish drivers who have no alternative" is a direct case against adopting it. Yes from one, no from the other.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Neither speaker questions whether cleaner air and faster buses are desirable; they disagree about the means, not the ends. Undisputed goals cannot be the point at issue.`],generalLesson:`A speaker can concede an outcome while disputing its cause — "traffic fell, but because of transit, not pricing" is agreement on the fact and disagreement on the explanation, so check exactly which proposition each speaker affirms or denies.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Brooks deny that traffic fell in the other cities, or does Brooks accept that and dispute something else about it?`,`Alvarez makes a "should" claim. Does Brooks’s reply amount to opposing that claim, even without using the word "should"?`,`For each choice, write down Alvarez’s stand and Brooks’s stand. Which choice gives you a yes and a no?`],labels:{topic:`congestion pricing policy debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Brooks concedes the traffic outcome while disputing its cause, so the only genuine yes/no split is the adoption recommendation. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-235`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,viewpointComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`CEO: Our shift to remote work has boosted productivity, and the quarterly numbers prove it. Output per employee is up eleven percent since the change.

Union representative: Output is up because the remaining staff are covering for colleagues who were laid off in the same quarter. Per-employee output rose, but total output barely moved, and burnout complaints have tripled.`,stem:`The point at issue between the CEO and the union representative is whether`,choices:[{text:`output per employee has risen since the shift to remote work.`},{text:`layoffs occurred in the same quarter as the shift.`},{text:`employees prefer working remotely.`},{text:`burnout complaints have increased.`},{text:`the shift to remote work caused the rise in productivity.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The CEO credits remote work for the productivity gain; the representative credits the layoffs instead ("output is up because the remaining staff are covering"). They agree the numbers rose but disagree on the cause — and causation is what the last choice asks about.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Notice what the representative concedes: "per-employee output rose" — the representative grants the CEO’s numbers. The fight is over why they rose. The CEO’s causal claim is that the shift to remote work "boosted productivity." The representative’s rival causal claim is that the rise comes from survivors covering for laid-off colleagues. Those two explanations compete directly: if the layoffs explain the gain, remote work doesn’t get the credit, and vice versa. The distractors are all conceded or one-sided: the per-employee rise is granted by both, the layoffs and burnout figures are asserted only by the representative, and employee preferences are addressed by neither. Only the causal question — did remote work cause the productivity rise? — draws "yes" from the CEO and "no" from the representative.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). The representative explicitly concedes that "per-employee output rose." A fact both speakers accept cannot be their point of disagreement, however central it looks.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only the representative mentions layoffs; the CEO never addresses whether they occurred. Without a stand from both speakers, there is no point at issue.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Neither speaker discusses employee preferences about remote work at any point. It is a natural question a reader might wonder about, but a point-at-issue answer must come from propositions the speakers actually affirmed or denied, not from ones they never took up.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The tripling of burnout complaints is the representative’s claim alone; the CEO never mentions burnout. One-sided assertions cannot be points at issue.`,`Correct. The CEO affirms that remote work boosted productivity; the representative denies it, attributing the rise to layoff-driven workload instead ("output is up because the remaining staff are covering"). They agree on the numbers and disagree on the cause.`],generalLesson:`When both speakers accept the data but offer rival explanations, the point at issue is the causal claim — conceding the numbers is not conceding the argument.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`,`irrelevant-true`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the union representative dispute the CEO’s numbers? Read the representative’s first two sentences carefully.`,`If both sides agree output per employee rose, what is left for them to disagree about?`,`The CEO says remote work "boosted" productivity. The representative says output is up "because" of something else. What kind of claim are both making?`],labels:{topic:`remote work productivity dispute`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Both speakers grant the per-employee numbers and dispute the cause - layoff-driven workload vs remote work - making causation the point at issue. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-236`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`A clinical trial found that patients taking Verdalex reported fewer migraines than patients taking a placebo. The trial was double-blind and included 2,000 participants. Therefore Verdalex is an effective migraine treatment.`,stem:`Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Were the Verdalex and placebo groups similar in age, migraine history, and other medications?`},{text:`How much does a month’s supply of Verdalex cost for the average patient?`},{text:`Do the researchers who ran the trial personally use Verdalex themselves?`},{text:`What is the chemical formula of Verdalex’s active ingredient?`},{text:`Have other migraine drugs been tested in trials of similar size and duration?`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument assumes the migraine difference comes from the drug rather than from pre-existing differences between the groups. Learning whether the groups were comparable directly tests that assumption; cost, personal habits, chemistry, and other drugs’ trials do not.`,explanationWalkthrough:`An evaluate question asks which information would test the argument’s key assumption. Here the reasoning moves from "the Verdalex group did better than the placebo group" to "Verdalex caused the improvement." That leap is legitimate only if the two groups were alike in other relevant respects — if, say, the Verdalex group was younger or had milder migraines to begin with, the drug might deserve no credit. So the most useful information is whatever bears on group comparability: age, migraine history, and other medications are exactly the confounders that could explain the gap. Cost tells us nothing about efficacy. The researchers’ personal habits are irrelevant to a double-blind trial’s results. The chemical formula doesn’t test the causal claim. And whether other drugs had similar trials tells us nothing about whether this trial’s comparison was fair.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The argument’s causal conclusion depends on the assumption that nothing besides the drug distinguished the two groups. If the groups differed in age, baseline severity, or other medications, the migraine gap could be explained without crediting Verdalex; if they were similar, the drug explanation strengthens. This question directly probes that assumption.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Cost may matter to a patient or insurer, but it has no bearing on whether the drug caused the observed improvement. Evaluate questions reward information that tests the reasoning, not information that is merely related to the topic.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). In a double-blind trial, neither patients nor administrators know who got the drug, so the researchers’ personal habits cannot bias the outcome. This is tempting only if you confuse the researchers with the subjects.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). The chemical formula might interest a chemist, but knowing it would not help decide whether the trial’s group difference was caused by the drug or by pre-existing group differences.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). How other drugs were tested says nothing about the fairness of this trial’s comparison. It answers a question about research norms, not about this argument’s reasoning.`],generalLesson:`When an argument compares two groups and credits the treatment for the difference, the assumption to test is always group comparability — ask what else might have distinguished the groups.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument concludes the drug caused the improvement. What would have to be true about the two groups for that conclusion to follow?`,`Suppose the Verdalex group happened to be much younger with milder migraines. Would the trial still show the drug works?`,`Which answer choice asks about something that could explain the migraine gap without giving the drug any credit?`],labels:{topic:`migraine drug trial evaluation`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Only choice probing cross-group comparability of age and condition; distractors shift scope to inputs or other jurisdictions. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-237`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Since Riverton banned single-use plastic bags, plastic litter in the river has dropped 40 percent. The ban is clearly working, and neighboring towns should adopt similar bans.`,stem:`Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Do Riverton residents approve of the bag ban, and would their approval survive if the ban raised grocery prices?`},{text:`Did any other changes, such as the volunteer river cleanup begun last spring, coincide with the decline in plastic litter?`},{text:`What share of river litter was plastic before the ban took effect, and how was that share measured?`},{text:`How many towns neighbor Riverton, and do any of them share the same river system?`},{text:`Are paper bags more expensive than plastic bags for Riverton retailers to purchase in bulk?`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The argument assumes the ban caused the litter drop. If a concurrent cleanup (or anything else) could explain the decline, the causal claim weakens; if nothing else changed, it strengthens. The other options test popularity, baselines, geography, or prices — none of which bears on causation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument’s core move is causal: ban enacted, litter fell, therefore the ban worked. Every causal claim from a before-and-after comparison assumes that nothing else relevant changed in between. That assumption is exactly what the correct choice probes — a volunteer cleanup launched around the same time would be a rival explanation for cleaner water, and knowing about it would directly affect how much credit the ban deserves. The distractors each miss the causal question. Resident approval bears on the recommendation that neighbors adopt bans, but not on whether Riverton’s ban caused the drop. The pre-ban plastic share is tempting because baselines sound scientific, yet a 40-percent decline is a 40-percent decline regardless of the starting share — it doesn’t test the causal link. The number of neighboring towns and the price of paper bags are simply off-topic for the causation question.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Approval ratings might matter for the recommendation that neighbors follow suit, but they cannot tell us whether the ban caused the litter decline. This choice answers a political question, not the argument’s causal one.`,`Correct. The ban-gets-credit conclusion requires that no rival cause produced the decline. A concurrent cleanup program would be exactly such a rival; learning whether one existed directly tests the argument’s central assumption, with either answer moving our confidence in the causal claim.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Baseline data sounds relevant, but the argument already reports a percentage decline, which is interpretable without the starting share — and no baseline figure could reveal whether the ban or something else caused the drop.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The number of neighboring towns has no logical connection to whether Riverton’s ban caused its own litter decline. It is pure scene-setting trivia.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). Paper-bag prices concern shoppers’ costs, a different subject from the causal link between the plastic-bag ban and river litter. It shifts the scope from causation to consumer economics.`],generalLesson:`Before-and-after causal claims always assume "nothing else changed" — the most useful evaluative question is the one that hunts for concurrent changes that could be rival causes.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument says the ban caused the drop. What else, besides the ban, could make river litter fall during the same period?`,`Imagine you learned a big volunteer cleanup started the same month. Would you still be as confident the ban did the work?`,`Which choice asks about a possible rival cause — something that would steal credit from the ban?`],labels:{topic:`plastic bag ban effectiveness`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-h-238`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Companies whose CEOs took pay cuts during the recession recovered faster than companies whose CEOs did not. Therefore, cutting executive pay causes faster corporate recovery.`,stem:`Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?`,choices:[{text:`How large were the pay cuts that the CEOs took, and how long did those reductions remain in effect?`},{text:`Did shareholders formally approve the pay cuts, or were the reductions imposed unilaterally by the boards?`},{text:`Were the companies that cut CEO pay otherwise similar — in size, industry, and financial health — to those that did not?`},{text:`What is the average salary of a CEO at a large company, and how did that figure change during the recession?`},{text:`Did any companies raise CEO pay during the recession, and if so, how did their performance compare afterward?`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`This is a group-comparison causal claim, so it assumes the two groups of companies differed only in the pay cuts. If the pay-cut companies were already healthier or in stronger industries, that — not the cuts — could explain faster recovery.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument compares two groups — pay-cut companies versus no-cut companies — and attributes the recovery gap to the pay cuts. That attribution is valid only if the groups were otherwise comparable. But there is a classic selection worry here: perhaps only already-healthy companies could afford the symbolic gesture of a CEO pay cut, while struggling companies couldn’t. If so, prior financial health, not the cuts, explains the recovery gap, and the causal conclusion collapses. The correct choice probes exactly this comparability assumption across the obvious dimensions: size, industry, and prior health. The size of the cuts is tempting but doesn’t test whether the comparison was fair — even large cuts prove nothing if the groups differed. Shareholder approval, average CEO salaries, and whether some companies raised pay are all beside the causal question the argument actually asks.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: half-right). The magnitude of the cuts sounds pertinent, but it cannot reveal whether the two groups of companies were comparable to begin with. Even enormous cuts would not rescue the causal claim if healthier companies were the ones cutting.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Whether shareholders voted on the cuts concerns corporate governance, not whether the cuts caused faster recovery. It tests none of the argument’s assumptions.`,`Correct. The causal conclusion needs the two groups to differ only in the pay cuts. If pay-cut companies were already larger, in stronger industries, or financially healthier, those advantages — not the cuts — could explain the recovery gap. Either answer to this question moves our assessment of the causal claim.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The average CEO salary provides background context but no leverage on whether the observed recovery difference was caused by the cuts or by pre-existing differences between the companies.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Companies that raised pay are a third group the argument never discusses; learning about them would not test whether the pay-cut versus no-cut comparison was fair.`],generalLesson:`In group-comparison causal arguments, always suspect selection effects: ask whether the groups differed before the "treatment" in ways that could explain the outcome on their own.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Two groups of companies are being compared. For the pay cuts to deserve the credit, what must be true about how the groups compared before the recession?`,`Think about which companies can afford to cut CEO pay during a recession. Might those companies already be different in some important way?`,`Which choice asks whether the "treatment" group and the "control" group started on equal footing?`],labels:{topic:`CEO pay cuts and recovery`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Only choice isolating the selection worry from the magnitude-of-treatment half-right distractor. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-239`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Professor Lin’s department found that students in courses with recorded lectures scored lower on final exams than students in courses without recordings. The department concludes that access to recordings makes students study less effectively and plans to ban recordings.`,stem:`Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Do students enjoy having access to lecture recordings for exam review?`},{text:`How many courses were included in the department’s published study, and over how many semesters?`},{text:`Did the professors teaching the courses know that a study of their classes was being conducted?`},{text:`Were recorded-lecture courses comparable to unrecorded ones in subject difficulty and student strength?`},{text:`What recording software was used to capture the course lectures?`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The department blames recordings for lower scores, but that requires the recorded and unrecorded courses to be otherwise comparable. If recorded courses were harder or enrolled weaker students, the score gap needs no recording-based explanation.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The department’s reasoning has two layers: first the causal claim (recordings cause worse studying and lower scores), then the policy built on it (ban recordings). Both layers rest on the same assumption — that the score gap is attributable to the recordings rather than to pre-existing differences between the course groups. Consider the selection worry: perhaps instructors who struggle to hold students’ attention are the ones who record lectures, or perhaps recordings are offered mainly in large introductory courses with weaker average preparation. Either pattern would produce lower scores with no causal role for the recordings at all. The correct choice tests precisely this comparability across the two most threatening dimensions, subject difficulty and student strength. Enjoyment is irrelevant to the causal claim; sample size affects precision but not the confounding worry; professor awareness and recording software are red herrings with no plausible link to the score gap.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Student enjoyment might matter for a policy debate about satisfaction, but it cannot tell us whether recordings caused the lower scores. It answers a question the argument never asks.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Sample size affects how precisely the gap is measured, but a large study of incomparable courses would still be confounded. The threatening assumption is not "too few courses" but "different kinds of courses," which this question never probes.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Whether professors knew about the study has no plausible mechanism for creating a score gap between recorded and unrecorded courses. It is a methodological detail without evaluative payoff here.`,`Correct. If the recorded courses were harder or enrolled less-prepared students, the score difference is explained without any causal role for recordings, undercutting both the causal conclusion and the ban. If the courses were comparable, the recordings explanation gains real support. Either answer matters.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The brand of recording software cannot plausibly explain differences in final-exam performance between the two groups of courses. It is technical trivia dressed up as methodology, and learning it would not move our assessment of the causal claim one inch.`],generalLesson:`When an institution compares existing groups (recorded vs. unrecorded courses) rather than randomizing, the first suspicion should always be selection: were the groups already different in ways that explain the outcome?`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The department compared courses that already differed in recording status. What else might already differ between those two groups of courses?`,`Imagine recordings are mostly offered in huge intro courses with less-prepared students. Would the score gap still implicate the recordings?`,`Which choice asks whether the comparison was apples-to-apples in the first place?`],labels:{topic:`lecture recordings and exam scores`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Only choice probing comparability across subject difficulty and student strength; sample-size distractor is a genuine half-right. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-240`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-evaluate`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,causalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,inferentialDistance:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The SleepWell mattress earns a 4.8-star average across 12,000 online reviews, the highest rating of any mattress sold this year. So it is the best mattress you can buy.`,stem:`Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?`,choices:[{text:`In how many colors is the SleepWell mattress available?`},{text:`How many stars did the second-highest-rated mattress receive?`},{text:`Is the SleepWell mattress sold in physical retail stores?`},{text:`How long has the company that makes SleepWell been in business?`},{text:`Did the company offer discounts or other incentives for positive reviews?`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument treats the review average as a faithful measure of mattress quality, which assumes the reviews are genuine and representative. If positive reviews were bought with incentives, the 4.8 stars reflect marketing, not quality — and the "best mattress" conclusion falls apart.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference runs from a number — 4.8 stars across 12,000 reviews — to a verdict about quality: the best mattress you can buy. That inference is only as good as the number, and online review averages are vulnerable to a specific corruption: incentivized or fake reviews. If the company traded discounts for five-star ratings, the average measures the generosity of its promotions rather than the comfort of its mattress, and the conclusion is unsupported no matter how many reviews were counted. The correct choice probes exactly this integrity assumption. The distractors orbit the question without touching it: color options are irrelevant to quality; the runner-up’s rating tells us about the margin but nothing about whether the reviews are honest; retail availability and company age are background facts with no bearing on review integrity.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Available colors have no bearing on whether the review average reflects mattress quality. It is product trivia, not a test of the argument’s reasoning.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Knowing the runner-up’s rating would tell us how decisive the lead is, but a big lead in a corrupted metric is still corrupted. This question never probes whether the reviews themselves are trustworthy.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Where the mattress is sold does not affect whether its online reviews are genuine. Distribution channels are beside the point of the quality inference.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Company age might matter for warranty confidence, but it provides no evidence about the integrity of the 12,000 reviews the conclusion rests on.`,`Correct. The "best mattress" verdict assumes the 4.8-star average measures genuine customer satisfaction. If positive reviews were purchased with incentives, the rating is inflated and the inference collapses; if reviews were unprompted, the rating genuinely supports the conclusion. Either answer decisively affects the argument.`],generalLesson:`Arguments from aggregated ratings assume the ratings are genuine and representative — the sharpest evaluative question is always the one that probes how the number was produced, not how big it is.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-samples`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The conclusion rests entirely on the 4.8-star average. Under what circumstances would a high average NOT indicate a great mattress?`,`Twelve thousand reviews sounds impressive. But what if the company paid for the positive ones — would the number still mean anything?`,`Which choice asks about the integrity of the evidence itself, rather than details around it?`],labels:{topic:`mattress review ratings`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty overstated at d4; the credited answer is the natural first skeptical question about review integrity, with single-layer reasoning and non-competing distractors. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 4 to 3; version 2; revisedAt 2026-09-11. Single defensible answer confirmed.`}],revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-h-241`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The 1911 courthouse should be demolished. It has stood vacant for a decade, restoration would cost twice what demolition and a new building would cost, and no civic group has offered to fund its preservation.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`A historic building should be demolished when preserving it is prohibitively expensive and no one will pay for its preservation.`},{text:`Historic buildings should never be demolished, regardless of their condition or the cost of preserving them.`},{text:`Cost should never be a factor in decisions about historic preservation, no matter how tight the public budget.`},{text:`Vacant buildings should always be restored, no matter how expensive restoration would be for the community.`},{text:`Constructing a new building is always preferable to restoring an old one, whatever its historical significance.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The argument’s reasons are high cost and the absence of anyone willing to fund preservation; the correct principle takes exactly those two conditions and yields the demolition verdict. The other principles oppose the conclusion or prove far more than the argument claims.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A justifying principle must connect the argument’s actual premises to its conclusion. The premises here are threefold: the building is vacant, restoration costs twice the demolition-plus-replacement alternative, and no civic group will fund preservation. The conclusion is that it should be demolished. The correct principle mirrors that structure: when preservation is prohibitively expensive (the two-to-one cost ratio) and no one will pay for it (no civic group has offered), demolition is the warranted outcome. Plug the premises into the principle and the conclusion follows directly. The distractors fail in two ways: three of them point in the opposite direction — never demolish, ignore cost, always restore — and would justify rejecting the conclusion rather than accepting it. The last one is too strong: the argument never claims new construction is always preferable, only that demolition is warranted under these specific costly, unfunded conditions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. It takes the argument’s two load-bearing premises — prohibitive cost and no willing funder — as its triggering conditions and delivers exactly the argument’s conclusion. The principle fits the reasoning like a key fits a lock, neither opposing it nor overshooting it.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This principle would forbid the very demolition the argument recommends. A principle that contradicts the conclusion cannot justify the reasoning that reaches it.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument leans heavily on the cost comparison between restoration and demolition; a principle declaring cost irrelevant would pull the rug out from under that reasoning. Far from justifying the argument, it would undermine the very grounds the conclusion rests on.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument concludes the building should be demolished, while this principle demands restoration regardless of expense. It justifies the contrary verdict — the exact outcome the argument’s cost and funding premises are marshaled against.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument’s case is conditional — demolition is warranted given the cost and the lack of funding — not a universal preference for new construction. This principle proves far more than the argument claims and ignores the specific conditions the reasoning depends on.`],generalLesson:`A justifying principle should match the argument’s own conditions: it takes the stated premises as triggers and yields the stated conclusion, without opposing the verdict or proving much more than was argued.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`opposite`,`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What reasons does the argument give for demolition? List them before looking at the principles.`,`A good principle should start from those same reasons and end at the same verdict. Which principle does that?`,`Eliminate any principle that would argue against demolition, then check whether the survivor matches the argument’s specific conditions.`],labels:{topic:`historic courthouse demolition`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Credited principle mirrors the cost plus no-funder premises; distractors oppose the verdict or overclaim. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-242`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`My neighbor’s oak drops branches onto my driveway every storm. I have asked her twice to trim it, and she refused both times. I am going to hire someone to trim the overhanging limbs myself and send her the bill.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Neighbors should never be charged for work done without their explicit consent.`},{text:`A person asked to remedy a hazard on their property who refuses may fairly be charged for the remedy.`},{text:`Anyone may trim any tree at any time, regardless of who owns it.`},{text:`Property owners are responsible for all damage caused by storms.`},{text:`Neighborhood disputes should always be resolved through lawsuits rather than direct action.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The argument’s key facts are the repeated requests and the refusals; the correct principle makes exactly that history — asked and refused — the condition for fairly billing the neighbor. The distractors contradict the conclusion, overclaim, or change the subject.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Extract the argument’s structure. The premises: the tree creates a recurring hazard, the neighbor was asked twice to address it, and she refused both times. The conclusion: it is fair to trim the limbs and bill her. The justifying principle must therefore bridge "asked and refused" to "fair to charge." The correct choice does exactly that, with each element of the stimulus finding a home in the principle: the hazard (falling branches), the requests (asked twice), the refusals, and the billed cost. The first distractor directly contradicts the conclusion by forbidding the billing. The third is far too broad — the argument claims no general right to trim any tree, only a right arising from this specific history of refusal. The fourth shifts the subject from trimming costs to storm damage generally. The fifth prescribes lawsuits, a procedure the argument never contemplates.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument concludes that billing the neighbor is fair; this principle declares such billing impermissible. It justifies the opposite of the argument’s verdict.`,`Correct. Every element of the stimulus maps onto the principle: the hazard (branches on the driveway), the requests (asked twice), the refusals, and the resulting fairness of charging for the remedy. It connects the argument’s exact premises to its exact conclusion.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument defends billing in this specific asked-and-refused situation; it never claims a universal right to trim anyone’s tree. This principle proves far more than the reasoning needs or supports.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). Responsibility for storm damage in general is a broader and different subject from the fairness of billing for trimming after refused requests. The principle’s scope does not match the argument’s.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). The argument is about the fairness of direct action and billing, not about the proper forum for disputes. Lawsuits are simply not at issue in the reasoning.`],generalLesson:`The best justifying principle is tailor-made: each of the argument’s key facts should appear as a condition in the principle, and the principle’s verdict should be the argument’s conclusion — no more, no less.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:65,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are the two most important facts in the story — the ones the billing decision seems to rest on?`,`Which principle mentions both of those facts as its triggering conditions?`,`Cross out any principle that forbids the billing or that ignores the requests and refusals.`],labels:{topic:`neighbor tree trimming dispute`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Credited principle maps the asked-and-refused history onto fair billing; too-strong and scope-shift distractors fail. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-243`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:4,scopeSubtlety:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The paper should not publish the leaked memos. They were stolen from a private company, they contain employees’ personal data, and publishing them would serve no public interest beyond satisfying curiosity.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Newspapers should publish anything that increases readership.`},{text:`Stolen documents should always be published, since the public has a right to know everything.`},{text:`Newspapers should not publish stolen private material that harms innocent people and serves no genuine public interest.`},{text:`Curiosity about private companies is by itself a sufficient reason to publish their internal documents.`},{text:`Newspapers should never publish any leaked material under any circumstances.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The argument withholds publication on two specific grounds — harm to innocent people (personal data) and absence of genuine public interest (mere curiosity). The correct principle combines exactly those grounds; the distractors oppose the verdict or justify it for the wrong, overbroad reasons.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument’s conclusion is a refusal to publish, and its reasoning is nuanced: it does not claim all leaks are unpublishable, but rather that these memos fail two tests — they were stolen and contain personal data (harm to innocents), and publication would serve only curiosity rather than a genuine public interest. A justifying principle must therefore be conditional on those same two considerations, and the correct choice is: stolen private material plus harm to innocents plus no genuine public interest yields "should not publish." The first, second, and fourth distractors point the other way, each endorsing publication on grounds the argument rejects. The fifth distractor is the interesting one: it agrees with the verdict but for an overbroad reason — a blanket ban on all leaks. Because the argument’s reasoning turns on the specific combination of theft, personal data, and lack of public interest, the principle that best justifies the reasoning is the one that tracks those grounds, not the one that reaches the same verdict by ignoring them.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This principle would require publishing the memos to gain readers, directly contradicting the argument’s conclusion that they should be withheld. A principle that mandates the opposite action cannot justify the reasoning that rejects it.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). A universal duty to publish stolen documents collides head-on with the argument’s refusal to publish these particular stolen memos. It commands exactly what the argument’s harm and public-interest reasoning forbids.`,`Correct. It assembles the argument’s exact grounds — stolen private material, harm to innocent people via personal data, and no genuine public interest beyond curiosity — into the condition for the "should not publish" verdict the argument reaches.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument explicitly demotes curiosity below the threshold of justification; this principle elevates curiosity to sufficiency, reversing the argument’s value judgment. It would license the very publication the argument works to block.`,`Incorrect and the subtlest distractor (trap: too-strong). It agrees with the verdict but justifies it by a blanket rule the argument never invokes. The argument’s reasoning is conditional on harm and public interest, so the principle that most helps justify the reasoning is the one that mirrors those conditions rather than bypassing them.`],generalLesson:`When two principles yield the same verdict, prefer the one that mirrors the argument’s own grounds — a justifying principle should track the reasoning’s conditions, not merely coincide with its conclusion.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`opposite`,`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument doesn’t say "never publish leaks." What specific features of THESE memos drive the refusal?`,`Look for the principle whose conditions mention the same things the argument mentions: theft, personal data, and the curiosity-versus-public-interest distinction.`,`Two choices agree with the verdict. Which one reaches it through the argument’s own reasoning, and which one bypasses that reasoning with a blanket rule?`],labels:{topic:`publishing leaked memos`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Credited principle tracks the conditional grounds of theft plus personal data plus no public interest; the blanket-ban distractor agrees with the verdict for the wrong reason. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-244`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-support`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-premise-conclusion`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The district should require flu vaccination for all students. Last winter, schools with mandates had 30 percent fewer absences, and the requirement places only a trivial burden on families since the shots are free at school clinics.`,stem:`Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning above?`,choices:[{text:`Schools should require whatever reduces absences, regardless of the burden such requirements impose on families.`},{text:`Vaccination requirements should be entirely voluntary and never mandated by school districts under any circumstances.`},{text:`Only health requirements that impose literally no burden on families can ever be justified by a school district.`},{text:`A district should adopt a health requirement when evidence shows it substantially reduces illness and compliance imposes minimal burden.`},{text:`Districts should adopt whatever health policies neighboring districts have adopted, without independent evaluation.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument balances two considerations — demonstrated effectiveness (30 percent fewer absences) and low burden (free, trivial inconvenience). The correct principle makes a mandate appropriate exactly when those two conditions are met; the distractors drop one side of the balance or change the subject.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The argument’s reasoning is a two-factor balancing act. Factor one is effectiveness: the 30-percent absence reduction is evidence the mandate substantially cuts illness. Factor two is burden: free in-school shots make compliance nearly costless. The conclusion — the district should mandate — follows from the conjunction of demonstrated benefit and minimal burden. The correct principle encodes precisely that conjunction: substantial demonstrated reduction plus minimal burden warrants adoption. Each distractor breaks the balance. The first keeps effectiveness but discards the burden constraint the argument took care to establish. The second opposes mandates categorically. The third is the mirror image of the first — it keeps a burden constraint but makes it absolute ("literally no burden"), which would actually rule out even the trivial burden the argument accepts. The fifth substitutes peer imitation for the argument’s evidence-and-burden reasoning entirely.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument takes pains to show the burden is trivial, which would be unnecessary if burden never mattered. This principle discards half of the argument’s reasoning and would justify mandates the argument itself would not defend.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The argument concludes a mandate should be adopted; this principle forbids mandates altogether. It justifies the contrary position — the categorical anti-mandate stance the argument’s effectiveness and burden evidence is meant to defeat.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). "Literally no burden" is stricter than the argument’s "trivial burden" — even free shots take a few minutes. This principle would rule out the very mandate the argument defends, since the argument concedes a small burden exists.`,`Correct. It pairs the argument’s two load-bearing considerations — evidence of substantial illness reduction and minimal compliance burden — as joint conditions for adopting a health requirement, delivering exactly the argument’s conclusion from exactly its premises.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Neighboring districts are never mentioned; the argument’s case rests on effectiveness evidence and low burden, not on copying others. This principle would justify the verdict for entirely different reasons than the ones given.`],generalLesson:`When an argument balances two factors (benefit and burden), the justifying principle must preserve both — a principle that keeps only one factor misrepresents the reasoning even if it reaches the same verdict.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`too-strong`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The argument gives two kinds of reasons for the mandate. What are they?`,`Which principle requires both of those same two things before a mandate is warranted?`,`Watch out for principles that agree with the verdict but drop one of the argument’s two considerations.`],labels:{topic:`school flu vaccine mandate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Credited principle preserves the two-factor benefit/burden balance; single-factor and absolute-burden distractors break it. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-245`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A landlord must return a security deposit within 30 days unless the tenant caused damage beyond normal wear.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`Rosa’s apartment showed only normal wear, and her landlord returned her deposit after three weeks.`},{text:`Tom’s apartment showed only normal wear, but his landlord kept the entire deposit to cover repainting.`},{text:`Lee’s apartment showed only normal wear, and his landlord returned his deposit after 45 days.`},{text:`A homeowner sold her house and divided the proceeds with her sister.`},{text:`Nadia’s apartment showed only normal wear, but her landlord returned her deposit after 45 days because he had been on vacation.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The principle requires return within 30 days when there is no more-than-normal damage. Rosa’s case meets the condition (normal wear only) and the requirement (returned in three weeks). Every other case either violates the 30-day rule, withholds without qualifying damage, or isn’t a landlord-tenant deposit at all.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate the principle into a conditional: if no damage beyond normal wear, then the landlord must return the deposit within 30 days. (The "unless" clause means damage beyond normal wear lifts the duty; it does not forbid returning the deposit anyway.) Now test each scenario. Rosa: condition met (normal wear only), duty satisfied (three weeks is within 30 days) — conforms. Tom: condition met but the deposit was kept — violates the duty. Lee: condition met but returned after 45 days — violates the timing requirement. The homeowner case involves no landlord, no tenant, and no deposit — the principle simply doesn’t apply. Nadia: condition met, returned late — violates, and the vacation excuse appears nowhere in the principle, so it cannot excuse the delay. Only Rosa’s case satisfies both the condition and the requirement.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The "no damage beyond normal wear" condition is satisfied, triggering the duty to return within 30 days, and the landlord returned it after three weeks — inside the deadline. Both the condition and the requirement of the principle are met.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). With only normal wear, the principle requires return of the deposit; keeping it to cover repainting violates that duty. This scenario does the opposite of what the principle demands.`,`Incorrect (trap: half-right). It gets the return part right but misses the deadline: 45 days exceeds the 30-day requirement. Partial conformity is still nonconformity when the principle states a deadline.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). This involves a homeowner and her sister, not a landlord, tenant, or security deposit. The principle’s conditions are never triggered, so it can neither conform nor violate — it is simply outside the principle’s scope.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The vacation is true but legally irrelevant: the principle states no vacation exception to the 30-day rule. A late return is a late return, whatever the excuse.`],generalLesson:`Apply principles mechanically: first check whether the triggering condition is met, then check whether the required action was performed — excuses and partial matches not stated in the principle do not count.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`answers-different-question`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Rewrite the principle as an "if... then..." statement. What triggers the landlord’s duty?`,`For each scenario, ask two questions: was there damage beyond normal wear, and was the deposit returned within 30 days?`,`Does the principle mention any exception for vacations? If not, can a vacation excuse a late return?`],labels:{topic:`security deposit return rule`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Only the Rosa case satisfies both the no-damage condition and the 30-day requirement; deadline and vacation-excuse distractors fail. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-246`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: Employees may work from home on Fridays only if they have completed all their weekly deliverables by Thursday evening.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly violates the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`Mei finished all her deliverables by Thursday evening and worked from home on Friday.`},{text:`Jonah worked from home on Friday even though two of his deliverables were still unfinished on Thursday evening.`},{text:`Priya finished all her deliverables by Thursday evening but chose to come into the office on Friday.`},{text:`Sam finished all his deliverables by Wednesday and worked from home on both Thursday and Friday.`},{text:`Alex had no deliverables due this week and worked from home on Friday.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The principle makes Thursday completion a necessary condition for Friday remote work. Jonah worked from home Friday without meeting that condition — the one clear violation. The rest either satisfy the condition, exercise the mere permission the principle grants, or fall outside what it regulates.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle has the form "may do X only if Y" — Y (all deliverables done by Thursday evening) is necessary for X (working from home Friday). It does not say Y is sufficient to require anything, and "may" grants permission without obligation. Test each case against that logic. Mei: Y met, X done — permitted, no violation. Jonah: X done without Y — the necessary condition fails while the action is taken, which is precisely what "only if" forbids. Priya: Y met, X not done — "may" permits but never requires Friday remote work, so no violation. Sam: Friday remote work with Y met is fine; the principle says nothing about Thursdays, so Thursday remote work is unregulated by it. Alex: with no deliverables due, "all deliverables completed" is vacuously satisfied, so the condition holds. Only Jonah’s case breaks the necessary condition.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Mei satisfies the necessary condition and takes the permitted action — this is exactly what the principle allows. It describes compliance, not violation.`,`Correct. The principle requires Thursday-evening completion as a necessary condition for Friday remote work, and Jonah took the remote-work action while the condition was unmet. Doing X without the required "only if" condition is the definition of violating this principle.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It looks odd — why finish early and still come in? — but "may work from home" grants permission, not an obligation. Declining a permission never violates the rule that grants it.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The principle regulates only Friday remote work; it is silent about Thursdays. Sam’s Friday work satisfies the condition, and his Thursday work falls outside the principle’s scope entirely.`,`Incorrect (trap: keyword-match). "No deliverables due" might look like a failure to complete things, but completing all of an empty set is vacuously true — the condition is satisfied, so Friday remote work is permitted.`],generalLesson:`"Only if" sets a necessary condition, and "may" grants a permission — violating the principle means taking the action without the condition, not declining the permission or acting where the principle is silent.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`half-right`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "only if" tell you — is Thursday completion required for Friday remote work, or does it guarantee something?`,`Does "may work from home" mean employees must work from home, or just that they are allowed to?`,`Which employee took the Friday remote-work action without satisfying the Thursday condition?`],labels:{topic:`work-from-home eligibility rule`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Only-if sets the necessary condition correctly; vacuous-truth, permission-not-obligation, and scope distractors all fail. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-247`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,conditionalComplexity:3,scopeSubtlety:4,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A city may restrict a peaceful protest only to prevent a clear and specific threat to public safety, not merely because the protest’s message is unpopular.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`The city denied a parade permit because local businesses opposed the parade’s message.`},{text:`The city limited all downtown gatherings to 50 people after receiving vague rumors of possible unrest.`},{text:`The city rerouted a march away from a bridge that engineers had just declared structurally unsound.`},{text:`The city canceled a vigil because the mayor personally disagreed with its cause.`},{text:`The city banned all protests during the month of an international summit as a precaution.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The principle permits restriction only for a clear, specific safety threat. A bridge declared structurally unsound is exactly that — a concrete, identified danger — so rerouting the march conforms. Every other restriction rests on unpopularity, vague rumors, or blanket precaution, which the principle forbids.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The principle sets two bars for any restriction of a peaceful protest: the justification must be (1) a threat to public safety that is (2) clear and specific, and it explicitly excludes mere unpopularity of the message. Evaluate each restriction against both bars. Denying a permit over business opposition fails — the ground is message unpopularity, the forbidden justification. The 50-person limit fails the "clear and specific" bar: vague rumors of possible unrest are neither clear nor specific. Canceling over the mayor’s personal disagreement fails for the same reason as the first — disagreement with the message. The summit-month ban fails because blanket precaution, without any identified threat, is not a clear and specific threat. Only the rerouting passes: a structurally unsound bridge, as declared by engineers, is a concrete, identified, specific danger to the marchers themselves, and rerouting is a restriction aimed at preventing it.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The restriction is grounded in opposition to the message — precisely the justification the principle rules out. This is a violation, not conformity.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It invokes safety, which points in the right direction, but "vague rumors of possible unrest" are the opposite of a clear and specific threat. Invoking safety in general terms does not satisfy the principle’s specificity requirement.`,`Correct. Engineers declaring the bridge structurally unsound identifies a concrete, specific danger to participants, and rerouting the march prevents exactly that threat. Both of the principle’s conditions — public safety ground, clear and specific threat — are satisfied.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The mayor’s personal disagreement with the cause is message-based disapproval, the exact ground the principle forbids. Like the first choice, this scenario punishes unpopular speech rather than preventing a specific threat, so it violates the principle.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). A month-long blanket ban "as a precaution" cites no specific threat at all. Precaution without an identified, specific danger does not meet the principle’s standard.`],generalLesson:`Principles with a specificity requirement ("clear and specific") are failed by vague appeals to safety just as surely as by message-based censorship — check both the ground and its precision.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The principle allows restriction on one ground only. What are the two adjectives describing the required threat?`,`Which scenarios restrict protests because of the message? Eliminate those first.`,`Of the remaining safety-based restrictions, which one identifies a concrete, specific danger — and which rely on vagueness or blanket precaution?`],labels:{topic:`protest restriction principle`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`hostile-sample-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Independent 40-item hostile audit: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; leakage pass 2 applied.`}]},{id:`lr-h-248`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-principle-application`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Principle: A doctor should disclose a treatment’s risks unless the patient has explicitly said they do not want to hear them.`,stem:`Which one of the following most clearly violates the principle stated above?`,choices:[{text:`Dr. Chen described the risks of surgery to a patient who asked for full information.`},{text:`Dr. Osei withheld risk information at the explicit request of her patient.`},{text:`Dr. Kim disclosed the risks of a procedure to a patient who said, "Tell me everything."`},{text:`Dr. Patel did not mention the risks of a new drug to a patient who had never discussed the topic.`},{text:`Dr. Rao’s patient refused all treatment, so the risks of treatment were never discussed.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The principle requires disclosure unless the patient explicitly declines to hear the risks. Patel’s patient never said any such thing, yet the risks went undisclosed — the duty applied and was breached. The other cases either satisfy the duty, trigger the explicit-refusal exception, or involve no treatment at all.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Structure the principle: the default duty is disclosure; the "unless" clause creates exactly one exception — the patient’s explicit statement that they do not want to hear the risks. Silence, unfamiliarity, or the doctor’s own judgment do not trigger the exception; only an explicit refusal does. Now test each doctor. Chen discloses to a patient who wants full information — duty satisfied. Osei withholds, but at the patient’s explicit request — the exception applies, so no violation. Kim discloses to a patient who said "tell me everything" — duty satisfied. Patel withholds from a patient who "had never discussed the topic" — the exception’s trigger (an explicit refusal) never occurred, so the default duty stood and was breached: a violation. Rao’s patient refused all treatment, so there is no proposed treatment whose risks require disclosure — the duty never arises.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Disclosing risks to a patient who asked for full information is exactly what the default duty requires. This is compliance, described in detail to look like a test case.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Withholding risks looks like a breach, but the principle’s "unless" clause expressly permits it when the patient explicitly declines. The explicit request triggers the exception, so there is no violation.`,`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). "Tell me everything" is the opposite of declining to hear risks, so the default duty applies and Dr. Kim satisfies it. Full compliance, not violation.`,`Correct. The patient never explicitly said they did not want to hear the risks, so the principle’s sole exception was never triggered and the default duty to disclose remained — which Dr. Patel breached by saying nothing.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The duty concerns disclosing the risks of a treatment being proposed; with all treatment refused, there is no treatment on the table and the duty never arises. It shifts the scenario outside the principle’s scope.`],generalLesson:`An "unless" exception is triggered only by its exact stated condition — silence or a related refusal (of treatment, not of information) does not count as the explicit statement the principle requires.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`half-right`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the default duty, and what is the one exception? What exactly must happen for the exception to apply?`,`Dr. Patel’s patient "never discussed the topic." Is that the same as explicitly saying "I don’t want to hear the risks"?`,`Dr. Rao’s patient refused treatment itself. Does the principle impose any duty when no treatment is being proposed?`],labels:{topic:`medical risk disclosure rule`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The unless exception requires an explicit refusal - silence and refusal of treatment do not trigger it. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-249`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-flaws`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Every successful marathoner follows a strict training plan. Jamal follows a strict training plan. Therefore, Jamal will be a successful marathoner.`,stem:`The flawed reasoning in the argument above is most similar to that in which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`All genuine Rolex watches have a serial number on the case. This watch has a serial number on the case. So this watch is a genuine Rolex.`},{text:`All licensed pilots pass a medical exam. Ana passed a medical exam, but that alone does not make her a licensed pilot.`},{text:`No budget airline offers first-class seating. SwiftAir is a budget airline, so it offers no first-class seating.`},{text:`Most successful authors write every day. Lee writes every day, so Lee will probably become a successful author.`},{text:`All strict training plans require rest days. Jamal follows a strict training plan, so Jamal takes rest days.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The original moves from "all successful marathoners follow strict plans" to "Jamal follows a strict plan, so he’ll succeed" — treating a necessary trait as sufficient. The watch argument has the identical form: all genuine Rolexes have engraved serials, this watch has one, so it’s genuine.`,explanationWalkthrough:`First, name the flaw precisely. "Every successful marathoner follows a strict training plan" makes the plan a necessary condition of success (success → plan). The argument then observes the condition is met (Jamal → plan) and concludes success follows — it treats the necessary condition as sufficient. In conditional terms, it affirms the consequent: from A → B and B, it infers A. The correct parallel must commit the same fallacy with the same structure, not merely share the topic. The watch argument does: genuine → engraved serial; this watch has an engraved serial; therefore genuine. Same necessary-condition-treated-as-sufficient, same affirming-the-consequent form. The distractors differ: the pilot case states the same premises but explicitly refuses the fallacious inference (valid caution); the airline case is a valid universal instantiation (no A are B; x is A; so x is not B); the author case weakens to "most" and "probably," a different flaw of overgeneralization; the rest-days case is valid modus ponens (plan → rest days; Jamal has a plan; so rest days).`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. It replicates the flaw exactly: a necessary trait of the genuine article (engraved serial number) is treated as sufficient to establish genuineness, just as following a strict plan is treated as sufficient for marathon success. Same affirming-the-consequent structure, same necessary/sufficient confusion.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It uses parallel premises about pilots and medical exams but explicitly declines to draw the fallacious conclusion ("that alone does not make her a licensed pilot"). Matching the setup while avoiding the flaw is the opposite of parallel flawed reasoning.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). This is a valid argument: from "no budget airline offers first class" and "SwiftAir is a budget airline," the conclusion follows deductively. Parallel-flaw questions require the same flaw, and a valid argument has no flaw to share.`,`Incorrect (trap: half-right). It resembles the original in topic and in leaping from a trait to success, but its flaw is different: it generalizes from "most" with a merely probabilistic conclusion rather than confusing necessary and sufficient conditions in a deductive form.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This is valid modus ponens — all strict plans require rest days, Jamal follows one, so he takes rest days. The conclusion genuinely follows, so the reasoning exhibits no flaw at all, let alone the original’s.`],generalLesson:`To match a parallel flaw, abstract the original to its logical skeleton (here: A → B; B; therefore A) and require the answer to instantiate that same skeleton — topical similarity without structural identity is a trap.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`answers-different-question`,`half-right`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the first sentence actually guarantee — that strict plans produce success, or only that success requires a strict plan?`,`The argument observes Jamal meets a requirement and concludes he gets the reward. What is that mistake called in conditional logic?`,`Which answer choice makes the same move: treats a necessary trait as if it were enough to guarantee the status?`],labels:{topic:`marathon training plan logic`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Identical affirming-the-consequent skeleton in the credited choice; distractors are valid-caution, valid-instantiation, different-flaw, and valid modus ponens. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-250`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Dr. Vance argues that the new dam will harm fish migration. But Vance once falsified data in a grant application, so we can dismiss his warning about the dam.`,stem:`The flawed reasoning in the argument above is most similar to that in which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`The river study’s conclusions are unreliable because its lead author has no training in hydrology.`},{text:`Ruiz claims the tax bill will raise middle-class taxes, but she cheated on her exams, so her analysis must be wrong.`},{text:`The dam will harm fish migration, as demonstrated by three independent field studies.`},{text:`Vance’s warning should be rejected because dams never harm fish migration.`},{text:`We should trust the engineer’s safety report because she is a renowned expert in her field.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The original dismisses a claim about dams because of the claimant’s unrelated past misconduct — a textbook ad hominem. The Ruiz choice does the same: it rejects a tax-bill analysis because of cheating on college exams, an irrelevancy to the claim’s merits.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Name the flaw: the argument attacks Dr. Vance’s character (a past data falsification) instead of engaging his evidence about fish migration. Whether Vance once falsified a grant application has no bearing on whether this dam will harm fish — the claim should be judged on its evidence, not its author’s history. That is the ad hominem fallacy in its classic "dismiss the claim because of the claimant" form. The correct parallel must attack the person rather than the argument, using an irrelevancy of the same kind. The Ruiz choice fits perfectly: exam cheating in college is as irrelevant to tax-bill analysis as grant fraud is to fish migration, and in both cases the irrelevancy is used to dismiss the claim outright. The distractors each miss: questioning an author’s relevant training is legitimate criticism of credentials, not a personal attack; citing three studies engages evidence directly; "dams never harm fish" is a sweeping substantive claim (a different flaw); and trusting a renowned expert is an appeal to authority (a different flaw in the opposite direction).`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It looks like an attack on the author, but lack of training in hydrology is directly relevant to the reliability of conclusions about river flow. Criticizing relevant credentials engages the claim’s basis; the original’s flaw is using an irrelevant personal failing instead.`,`Correct. Like the original, it dismisses a substantive claim (about the tax bill) on the basis of the claimant’s unrelated past misconduct (cheating on college exams). In both, the personal attack is irrelevant to the claim’s merits, and in both it is used to reject the claim outright — the same ad hominem structure.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This engages the evidence directly by citing three independent studies — it is an example of good reasoning about the claim’s merits, the opposite of dismissing a claim via personal attack.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). This rejects the warning with an absolute substantive claim ("dams never harm fish") rather than with a personal attack. It is flawed, but its flaw is overgeneralization, not ad hominem.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Trusting a renowned expert is an appeal to authority — a flaw (if any) in the opposite direction from the original’s attack on the claimant. The direction of the personal reference matters: attacking versus endorsing.`],generalLesson:`Ad hominem parallels require the same irrelevancy structure: the attack must target the claimant with something beside the claim’s merits — relevant credential criticism doesn’t count.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`opposite`,`too-strong`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the argument give any reason to doubt Vance’s evidence about fish, or does it talk about something else entirely?`,`The flaw is about WHO is speaking rather than WHAT is said. Which choice rejects a claim for a reason about the speaker that has nothing to do with the claim?`,`Be careful with the hydrology choice: is lack of relevant training irrelevant to the claim, the way past fraud is?`],labels:{topic:`dam warning ad hominem`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Same ad-hominem irrelevancy structure; the relevant-credentials distractor is a genuine near-miss. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-251`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The dean promised the faculty "a voice" in the decision. The suggestion box in the lobby gives everyone a voice. Therefore, the dean has kept her promise.`,stem:`The flawed reasoning in the argument above is most similar to that in which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`The dean promised transparency but held the meeting in secret, so she broke her promise.`},{text:`The airline promised more legroom and added two inches of it, which is genuinely more legroom.`},{text:`The airline promised "room to breathe." The cabin has slightly wider aisles, which is more room. So the airline kept its promise.`},{text:`The gym promised results in 30 days, and members who followed the program lost weight, so the gym kept its promise.`},{text:`The candidate promised lower taxes and then raised municipal fees, so voters were misled.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The original equivocates on "a voice": the promise meant meaningful input, but the argument redeploys the phrase in its literal sense (a suggestion box). The airline choice does the same with "room to breathe" — comfort in the promise, literal aisle width in the fulfillment.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Identify the flaw: equivocation — a key phrase shifts meaning mid-argument. In the promise, "a voice" means genuine influence over the decision; in the fulfillment, it is reduced to its thinnest literal sense, the physical ability to drop a suggestion in a box. The conclusion ("the dean kept her promise") is true only under the shifted meaning, so the argument trades on the ambiguity. The correct parallel must shift a quoted phrase between two senses in the same promise-then-fulfillment structure. The airline choice does exactly this: "room to breathe" in the promise suggests genuine comfort and spaciousness, while the fulfillment cashes it out as marginally wider aisles — more "room" in a literal, geometric sense only. The distractors lack the meaning shift: the transparency case involves a straightforward broken promise; the legroom case uses "more legroom" consistently (two inches is genuinely more of the same thing); the gym case reports actual results; the candidate case is a broken promise about taxes versus fees with no ambiguous phrase at all.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). This describes a straightforward broken promise — secrecy contradicts transparency — with no phrase changing meaning. Equivocation requires an ambiguous term, and none shifts here.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It resembles the correct choice topically, but "more legroom" means the same thing in the promise and the fulfillment — two added inches are genuinely more legroom. Without a meaning shift, there is no equivocation.`,`Correct. It mirrors the original’s structure precisely: a promise containing an evocative phrase ("room to breathe" / "a voice"), followed by a fulfillment that redeploys the phrase in a thinner literal sense (aisle width / a suggestion box). In both, the "kept promise" verdict depends on the shifted meaning.`,`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Members actually losing weight is genuine fulfillment of "results," with the key term used consistently throughout. It is an example of a promise kept, not of reasoning that trades on ambiguity.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Lower taxes versus higher fees is a direct contradiction, not an ambiguity: "taxes" never changes meaning. The flaw here would be promise-breaking, not equivocation.`],generalLesson:`Equivocation parallels need the same two-meaning shift on the same kind of phrase — check that the key term genuinely means something different in the "fulfillment" than it did in the "promise."`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`half-right`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What did "a voice" mean in the dean’s promise? Does it mean the same thing when applied to the suggestion box?`,`The flaw is a word changing meaning halfway through. Which choice has a quoted phrase that shrinks from something grand to something literal?`,`Compare the two airline choices carefully: in which one does the key phrase mean something different at the end than at the beginning?`],labels:{topic:`equivocation on promises`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Promise-to-fulfillment meaning shift replicated exactly; the consistent-meaning airline distractor is a genuine near-miss. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-252`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-parallel-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Each of the company’s regional offices turned a profit last quarter. It follows that the company as a whole turned a profit last quarter.`,stem:`The flawed reasoning in the argument above is most similar to that in which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`Each regional office turned a profit, and headquarters costs were flat, so the company likely turned a profit.`},{text:`The company as a whole turned a profit, so each of its regional offices must have turned a profit.`},{text:`Most regional offices turned a profit, so the company as a whole probably turned a profit.`},{text:`Every player on the team is an excellent athlete, so the team must be an excellent team.`},{text:`One regional office earned an enormous profit, so the company as a whole turned a profit.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The original infers a property of the whole (company profitability) from the same property in each part (office profitability), ignoring what the parts don’t capture — headquarters costs. The team choice commits the identical part-to-whole fallacy, ignoring what individual excellence doesn’t capture: teamwork.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Name the flaw: the fallacy of composition — inferring that a whole has a property because each of its parts does. Profitable offices do not guarantee a profitable company, because the inference ignores headquarters overhead, inter-office transfers, and other whole-level factors. The correct parallel must move from parts to whole in the same unguarded way. The team choice does: excellent individual athletes do not guarantee an excellent team, because the inference ignores chemistry, coaching, and coordination — the whole-level factors. The distractors each break the parallel differently. Adding flat headquarters costs repairs the gap (no longer fallacious). Reversing the direction — whole to parts — is the fallacy of division, a different flaw. Weakening to "most" and "probably" changes the argument’s strength and structure. And inferring from one enormous office is hasty generalization from a single case, not a parts-to-whole inference.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: half-right). It looks parallel, but accounting for headquarters costs closes the very gap that makes the original fallacious. A repaired argument no longer shares the flaw — parallel-flaw requires the same mistake, not the same topic.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This runs the inference in reverse — from the whole to the parts — which is the fallacy of division, not composition. Direction matters: the original moves parts-to-whole, and only a parts-to-whole match will do.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-weak). "Most" plus "probably" makes a weaker, hedged claim with a different logical shape than the original’s "each... it follows that." The flaw profile changes when the quantifiers and modality change.`,`Correct. It reproduces the composition fallacy exactly: a property of each part (individual athletic excellence) is projected onto the whole (team excellence) while ignoring whole-level factors (teamwork, coordination) — just as the original ignores headquarters costs when projecting office profits onto the company.`,`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Inferring a whole’s property from a single exceptional part is hasty generalization, a different flaw from inferring it from every part. The quantifier — one versus each — changes the fallacy.`],generalLesson:`Composition parallels must preserve the direction (parts → whole) and the unguarded leap — watch for distractors that repair the gap, reverse the direction, or change "each" to "most" or "one."`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`opposite`,`too-weak`,`answers-different-question`],prerequisites:[`f-flaws`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the original ignore when it jumps from "each office profited" to "the company profited"? What exists at the company level but not the office level?`,`The flaw moves from parts to whole. Which choice moves in that same direction while ignoring the same kind of whole-level factor?`,`One choice fixes the flaw by adding headquarters costs — does a fixed argument still share the flaw? One choice reverses the direction — does direction matter?`],labels:{topic:`composition fallacy business`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Parts-to-whole composition ignoring whole-level factors; repaired-argument, reversed-direction, and weakened-quantifier distractors all break the parallel. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-253`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-numbers`],editorialDifficulty:2,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Last year the town doubled the size of its police force. Yet the reported crime rate rose over the same period.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The town’s population also doubled over the same period.`},{text:`Most of the newly hired officers were assigned to desk duty rather than patrol.`},{text:`Residents report feeling safer now than they did a year ago.`},{text:`Neighboring towns also saw their crime rates rise last year.`},{text:`The larger force encouraged more residents to report crimes that previously went unreported.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The puzzle is about reported crime, not necessarily actual crime. If more officers caused more reporting of previously hidden crimes, the reported rate can rise even as actual crime stays flat or falls — both facts explained at once.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A resolve-paradox answer must make both surprising facts compatible, not just restate one of them. The tension here: more police should mean less crime, yet the numbers went up. The key word is "reported" — the statistic measures crimes that get reported, not crimes that occur. The correct choice exploits that gap: a bigger, more visible force can make residents more willing to report offenses they would previously have suffered in silence. If reporting rises faster than actual crime falls, the reported rate climbs while real public safety improves — both facts explained simultaneously. The distractors fail this two-sided test. A doubled population doesn’t explain a rising rate (rates already adjust for population). Desk-duty assignments might explain why crime didn’t fall, but not why it rose. Feeling safer and neighboring towns’ trends leave the core tension untouched.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). A crime rate is already per-capita, so proportional population growth cannot explain why the rate itself rose. It may be true, but it leaves the discrepancy intact.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Desk duty could explain why the extra officers failed to reduce crime — but the paradox is that reported crime rose, not merely that it failed to fall. This explains only half the puzzle.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Residents’ feelings of safety do not explain the statistical rise in reported crime. Subjective impressions and reported rates can easily diverge, and nothing here connects them.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). A regional trend might suggest a broader cause, but it does nothing to reconcile more police with more reported crime in this town. The discrepancy remains exactly as puzzling.`,`Correct. It targets the word "reported": if the larger force increased residents’ willingness to report previously unreported crimes, the reported rate can rise even while actual crime holds steady or drops. Both surprising facts — more police, higher reported rate — are rendered compatible.`],generalLesson:`When the paradox involves a measured quantity (reported crime, diagnosed cases), check whether the measurement process itself changed — more detection can raise the numbers without raising the underlying reality.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`causal-flaw`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:60,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The stimulus says the "reported" crime rate rose. Is "reported crime" the same thing as "actual crime"?`,`What could make the number of reports go up even if the number of crimes didn’t?`,`How might doubling the police force itself change residents’ reporting behavior?`],labels:{topic:`police force and crime reports`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Reported-vs-actual crime distinction resolves both facts; distractors fail the two-sided test. Difficulty d2 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-254`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The city’s new express bus line is both faster and cheaper than driving downtown. Yet ridership on the line has fallen steadily since it launched.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`The new buses are cleaner and more comfortable than the old ones they replaced.`},{text:`The city repaved several downtown streets in the same year the new express line launched.`},{text:`A recent survey found that most residents like the new express buses.`},{text:`Gasoline is heavily subsidized in the neighboring county this year.`},{text:`The express line cut three local stops, forcing most former riders to walk over a mile to the nearest stop.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Speed and price aren’t the whole commute: if the express line removed the stops riders actually used, the total trip became far less convenient despite the faster, cheaper ride. That explains falling ridership without denying either surprising fact.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The paradox assumes faster-plus-cheaper should attract riders, so the resolution must identify a hidden cost the paradox overlooks. Total commute convenience includes access time, not just ride time and fare. The correct choice supplies that hidden cost: eliminating three local stops means most former riders now face a mile-plus walk to board. For those riders, the journey got dramatically worse even as the bus itself got faster and cheaper — so declining ridership is exactly what you’d expect. Both original facts survive: the line is faster and cheaper per ride, and ridership still falls. The distractors don’t touch the mechanism: cleaner buses and resident approval would predict rising ridership, deepening the puzzle; repaved streets are neutral background; and a neighboring county’s gas subsidies affect a different population than this city’s commuters.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: opposite). Cleaner, more comfortable buses would make the ridership decline more surprising, not less. It pushes against the needed explanation rather than supplying it.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Repaved streets may be true, but nothing connects smoother roads to bus riders abandoning a faster, cheaper line. The discrepancy is left fully intact.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). If residents like the buses, falling ridership is even more puzzling, not less. Liking the service cannot explain avoiding it, so this choice deepens the discrepancy instead of resolving it.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The subsidy applies in the neighboring county, not this city, so it cannot explain this city’s commuters’ choices. It shifts the scope to a different population.`,`Correct. It reveals the hidden cost the paradox ignored: access. A faster, cheaper ride is worth little to riders who lost their stops and now face a long walk. The line improved on two dimensions while degrading a third that mattered more — reconciling both facts.`],generalLesson:`When a "better and cheaper" product loses customers, look for the dimension of value the paradox ignored — often access, convenience, or switching costs rather than price or quality.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Faster and cheaper describe the bus ride itself. But what does a rider’s total trip include besides the ride?`,`What would have to be true for a faster, cheaper bus to still be the worse option for its former riders?`,`Look for the choice that makes the overall commute harder even though the bus ride got better.`],labels:{topic:`express bus ridership decline`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Hidden access-cost mechanism reconciles faster-plus-cheaper with falling ridership. Difficulty d3 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-255`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-resolve-paradox`,secondarySkills:[`f-structure`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,causalComplexity:2},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Farmers in the valley planted 20 percent more acres of wheat this year than last year, yet the total wheat harvest was smaller than last year’s.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,choices:[{text:`Wheat prices rose sharply this year, encouraging local farmers to plant considerably more acres.`},{text:`More farmers entered the wheat market this year than in any previous year.`},{text:`The valley’s soil is among the most fertile in the entire region.`},{text:`Last year’s harvest was the largest in the valley’s recorded history by a wide margin.`},{text:`A late frost destroyed the early-planted crop on much of the expanded acreage; replanting yielded far less per acre.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`More acres should mean more wheat — unless yield per acre collapsed. A frost wiping out the early planting on the new acreage, followed by poor-yielding replanting, explains how acreage rose while total output fell.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Total harvest equals acres planted times yield per acre, so the paradox — more acres, less wheat — requires a sharp drop in per-acre yield. The correct choice delivers exactly that mechanism in two stages: a late frost destroyed the early-planted crop across much of the expanded acreage, and the emergency replanting produced far less per acre. With yield collapsing, the arithmetic works: 20 percent more acres at much lower productivity gives a smaller total. The distractors address the wrong side of the equation. Rising prices and new entrants explain why farmers planted more (the input side) but not why the harvest shrank (the output side). Fertile soil would predict a larger harvest, deepening the puzzle. And while a record harvest last year makes "smaller than last year" less surprising, it does nothing to explain how more acres produced less wheat — the core acreage-versus-output tension remains.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Rising prices explain the decision to plant more acres, but the paradox is about the harvest shrinking despite those acres. Motives for planting cannot explain the output shortfall.`,`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). More farmers entering is just another way of saying more acres were planted — it restates one side of the paradox instead of resolving the tension between acreage and output.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). Fertile soil predicts a bumper crop, which makes a smaller harvest even more surprising. It deepens the discrepancy rather than resolving it.`,`Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). A record prior year makes "smaller than last year" easier to accept, but it leaves the real puzzle untouched: how did 20 percent more acres yield less wheat? The acreage-output tension still demands a mechanism.`,`Correct. It supplies the missing mechanism — a collapse in yield per acre. The frost destroyed the early crop on the expanded acreage and the replanted fields underperformed, so the per-acre drop outweighed the acreage gain. More acres times much lower yield equals a smaller harvest: paradox resolved.`],generalLesson:`Decompose aggregate paradoxes into their factors (here: acres × yield per acre) — the resolution usually lies in the factor the paradox didn’t mention, not the one it emphasized.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`answers-different-question`,`restates-premise`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Total harvest depends on two things multiplied together. The stimulus tells you one went up — what must have happened to the other?`,`Which choices explain why farmers planted more, versus which explain why the harvest itself was small? The paradox needs the second kind.`,`Look for the choice describing something that would slash how much wheat each acre produced.`],labels:{topic:`wheat acreage versus harvest`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Acres-times-yield decomposition; the record-prior-year distractor softens surprise without supplying a mechanism. Difficulty d4 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-256`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-main-conclusion`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,linguisticComplexity:3,distractorProximity:5,inferentialDistance:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Opponents of bail reform warn that releasing defendants before trial endangers the public. They point to a handful of sensational cases. But those cases are statistical outliers: in jurisdictions that ended cash bail, re-arrest rates for violent offenses did not rise. What did rise was the rate at which defendants appeared for trial — because courts replaced money bail with text-message reminders and transportation assistance. The real lesson is not that reform is reckless; it is that the old system jailed poor people who posed no danger simply because they could not pay. Ending cash bail, paired with court-date supports, protects both liberty and public safety.`,stem:`The main conclusion of the argument is that`,choices:[{text:`re-arrest rates for violent offenses did not rise in jurisdictions that ended cash bail.`},{text:`the old cash-bail system jailed poor people who posed no danger simply because they could not pay.`},{text:`text-message reminders and transportation assistance increase court appearance rates.`},{text:`the sensational cases cited by opponents of bail reform are statistical outliers.`},{text:`ending cash bail, paired with court-date supports, protects both liberty and public safety.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The argument builds through several intermediate verdicts — the cases are outliers, re-arrest didn’t rise, appearances did, the old system jailed the poor — all in service of the final synthesis: ending cash bail with supports protects liberty and safety. That last sentence is the claim everything else supports and nothing follows from.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Difficulty-5 main-conclusion questions bury the conclusion among strong intermediate claims, so map the support structure carefully. The re-arrest finding and the outlier finding are evidence against the opponents’ warning. The appearance-rate finding, explained by reminders and rides, is evidence for the reform package. The claim about the old system jailing poor people is presented as "the real lesson" — but notice it is itself a lesson drawn from the evidence, and the argument does not stop there. The final sentence synthesizes everything into a forward-looking verdict: ending cash bail, paired with supports, protects both liberty and public safety. Ask the structural test: which claim is supported by the others but supports nothing further? The liberty-and-safety verdict draws on the re-arrest data (safety), the jailing-the-poor lesson (liberty), and the supports evidence (the pairing) — and nothing in the argument is inferred from it. The "real lesson" sentence is the most tempting distractor precisely because it is a conclusion, but it is an intermediate one: it feeds the final synthesis rather than completing the argument.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The flat re-arrest rate is evidence offered against the opponents’ warning — it is a datum deployed in service of the larger verdict, not the verdict itself. Premises, even striking statistical ones, do not become conclusions by being interesting.`,`Incorrect and the hardest distractor (trap: half-right). This is genuinely a conclusion — "the real lesson" — but it is an intermediate one. It is drawn from the evidence and then absorbed into the final synthesis about liberty and safety. Stopping here mistakes a waypoint for the destination.`,`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The reminders-and-rides finding explains why appearance rates rose; it functions as evidence for the value of the "court-date supports" half of the final recommendation. It supports the conclusion rather than being it.`,`Incorrect (trap: restates-premise). The outlier characterization rebuts the opponents’ sensational cases — defensive evidence, not the argument’s own positive thesis. Refuting the other side is a premise-level move here.`,`Correct. It is the only claim that draws on all the strands — the safety data, the liberty lesson, and the supports evidence — while having nothing inferred from it in turn. The final position, synthesizing every intermediate verdict into one forward-looking judgment, is the main conclusion.`],generalLesson:`When several sentences each look like conclusions, apply the structural test: the main conclusion is the claim everything else supports and from which nothing further is inferred — "the real lesson" may still be a lesson on the way to the verdict.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`restates-premise`,`half-right`,`restates-premise`,`restates-premise`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Several sentences sound like verdicts. Ask of each candidate: does the argument infer anything further FROM this claim, or does the reasoning stop here?`,`The phrase "the real lesson" introduces an intermediate verdict. What does the argument do with that lesson in the final sentence?`,`Which single claim draws together the safety evidence, the liberty point, and the court-date supports — and has nothing built on top of it?`],labels:{topic:`bail reform main thesis`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The final synthesis is the only claim supported by all strands with nothing inferred from it; the real-lesson sentence is an intermediate decoy. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-257`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-must-be-true`,secondarySkills:[`f-conditional`,`f-translate`,`f-deduction`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,conditionalComplexity:4,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Every department that received the research grant hired at least one data analyst. No department that hired a data analyst failed the accreditation audit. Some departments that received the research grant also cut their travel budgets.`,stem:`If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,choices:[{text:`No department that failed the accreditation audit received the research grant.`},{text:`Every department that hired a data analyst received the research grant.`},{text:`Some departments that cut their travel budgets failed the accreditation audit.`},{text:`No department that cut its travel budget hired a data analyst.`},{text:`Every department that passed the accreditation audit hired a data analyst.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Chain the conditionals: grant → hired analyst → passed audit. Contraposing the chain, failing the audit means no analyst was hired, which means no grant was received. The other choices reverse the arrows or link the travel-budget group to things nothing connects it to.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate each statement. "Every department that received the grant hired an analyst" gives grant → analyst. "No department that hired an analyst failed the audit" gives analyst → passed audit (equivalently, failed audit → no analyst). Chaining yields grant → analyst → passed audit. The correct answer is the contrapositive of the full chain: failed audit → no analyst → no grant, i.e., no department that failed the audit received the grant. Each distractor breaks a rule. Reversing the first arrow gives "analyst → grant," the classic mistaken reversal. The travel-budget departments are only known to overlap the grant group ("some grant recipients also cut travel"); nothing links them to audit outcomes or analyst hiring, so any claim tying travel cuts to failing the audit or to not hiring analysts is unsupported. And "passed audit → hired analyst" reverses the second conditional — passing is necessary for nothing here; it is merely what follows from hiring.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. From grant → analyst → passed audit, contrapose the whole chain: failing the audit implies no analyst was hired, which implies no grant was received. This is the valid contrapositive of the chained conditionals, so it must be true.`,`Incorrect (trap: reverses-conditional). The stimulus gives grant → analyst; this choice flips it to analyst → grant. A department could have hired an analyst with other funding — the original conditional never rules that out.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Travel-budget cutters are only known to overlap the grant recipients; nothing connects that overlap to audit failure. The grant group passed the audit, but the "some" statement does not identify which grant recipients cut travel, so no audit conclusion follows for them.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Nothing in the stimulus relates travel-budget decisions to analyst hiring. The two groups are linked only through their separate relationships to grant recipients, which licenses no inference between them.`,`Incorrect (trap: reverses-conditional). The stimulus gives analyst → passed audit; this choice reverses it to passed → analyst. Departments could pass the audit without ever hiring an analyst — passing is a consequence of hiring, not a guarantee of it.`],generalLesson:`Chain conditionals before answering, then read the correct answer as the contrapositive of the full chain — and treat any choice that flips an arrow or drags in an unconnected group as guilty until proven innocent.`,misconceptionTags:[`reversal`,`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`],trapTypes:[`reverses-conditional`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`reverses-conditional`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`,`f-conditional`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Write each statement as an arrow. What does "every grant recipient hired an analyst" give you? What does "no analyst-hirer failed" give you?`,`Can you link the arrows into one chain? If so, what does the beginning of the chain guarantee about the end?`,`The safest valid inference from a chain is often its contrapositive: start from "failed the audit" and walk the arrows backward.`],labels:{topic:`grant departments audit logic`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Grant-to-analyst-to-passed chain contrapositive; reversal and unsupported travel-budget distractors fail. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-258`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-flaw`,secondarySkills:[`f-flaws`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,conditionalComplexity:4,distractorProximity:5,linguisticComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The transit authority’s plan will succeed only if ridership grows by ten percent. Ridership always grows when fares are cut. The plan includes a fare cut. Therefore, the plan will succeed.`,stem:`The reasoning in the argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,choices:[{text:`confuses a correlation between fare cuts and ridership growth with a causal relationship, ignoring other possible explanations.`},{text:`treats satisfying a condition the argument calls necessary for the plan’s success as enough to guarantee that success.`},{text:`relies on the opinions of transit experts without presenting any of their evidence or reasoning.`},{text:`attacks the motives of those who oppose the transit plan instead of addressing their objections to its cost and feasibility.`},{text:`draws a general conclusion about all transit plans from the details of a single local plan.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Growing ridership is stated as necessary for success ("only if"), and the fare cut is meant to deliver that growth — but fulfilling a necessary condition doesn’t guarantee the outcome. The argument slides from "ridership will grow" to "the plan will succeed" as if necessity were sufficiency.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Dissect the logic step by step. "Will succeed only if ridership grows" makes growth necessary for success: success → growth. It does not make growth sufficient — other unmentioned conditions (budget, politics, operations) might also be required. "Ridership always grows when fares are cut" plus "the plan includes a fare cut" validly yields "ridership will grow." So far, so good. The fallacy strikes in the final step: from "ridership will grow" the argument concludes "the plan will succeed." But growth was only ever established as necessary, and satisfying a necessary condition guarantees nothing — every successful plan has growing ridership, but not every plan with growing ridership succeeds. That is the necessary/sufficient confusion. The correlation distractor misfires because the stimulus stipulates the causal regularity ("always grows when") rather than inferring it. No experts are cited, no opponents’ motives are attacked, and no generalization across plans occurs.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect and tempting (trap: causal-alternative). It describes a real flaw, but not this argument’s: the stimulus stipulates "ridership always grows when fares are cut" as a given regularity rather than inferring causation from a correlation. The argument’s error lies downstream, in what it does with the growth, not in how the growth is explained.`,`Correct. The argument itself marks ridership growth as merely necessary ("only if"), then treats its fulfillment — via the fare cut — as settling the matter. Moving from a satisfied necessary condition to a guaranteed outcome is the necessary/sufficient confusion, stated with precision.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No experts, authorities, or cited opinions appear anywhere in the stimulus. A flaw choice must describe an error the argument actually commits.`,`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The argument never mentions opponents of the plan, let alone their motives. There is no one being attacked, so no ad hominem occurs.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The conclusion concerns this one transit plan only; no claim is made about transit plans in general. The flaw is in the conditional reasoning, not in the scope of generalization.`],generalLesson:`When you see "only if," flag the condition as necessary and watch the conclusion: if the argument later treats that condition’s fulfillment as sealing the deal, you’ve found the necessary/sufficient swap.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`reversal`],trapTypes:[`causal-alternative`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-flaws`,`f-conditional`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does "will succeed only if ridership grows" tell you about the relationship between success and growth — which one requires the other?`,`The argument establishes that ridership will grow. Does that fact, by itself, guarantee success given what "only if" means?`,`Which choice describes treating a necessary condition as if it were sufficient?`],labels:{topic:`transit plan fare cut logic`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The argument treats fulfillment of a stated necessary condition as guaranteeing success; the causal-alternative distractor describes a flaw never committed. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-259`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-weaken`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`In a six-month trial, patients who used the Cefalyne headband reported 40 percent fewer migraine days than a control group that received no treatment. The headband delivers mild electrical pulses that the manufacturer says calm overactive nerves. Researchers concluded that the pulses reduce migraine frequency.`,stem:`Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?`,choices:[{text:`The Cefalyne headband is considerably more expensive than most migraine medications on the market today.`},{text:`Some patients in the trial found the headband uncomfortable to wear for extended periods.`},{text:`Most headband users also adopted regular sleep schedules during the trial; the control group’s habits did not change.`},{text:`The manufacturer of Cefalyne has previously been sued for false advertising of other products.`},{text:`A headband made by a different company showed no effect on migraines in an unrelated clinical trial.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The researchers credit the electrical pulses, but the headband group also changed a second variable — sleep schedules — that independently affects migraines. That rival cause means the 40 percent gap may have nothing to do with the pulses, directly undercutting the causal conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The conclusion is causal and specific: the pulses reduce migraine frequency. For that to follow from the trial, the pulses must be the relevant difference between the groups — the argument assumes nothing else distinguished headband users from controls. The correct choice breaks that assumption with a confounder that travels with the treatment: enthusiasm for the device led users to regularize their sleep, and regular sleep independently reduces migraines, while the control group changed nothing. Now the 40-percent gap has two candidate explanations, and the trial cannot separate them — the evidence no longer singles out the pulses. The distractors miss the causal logic. Price and comfort don’t touch whether the pulses worked. The manufacturer’s legal history attacks the source rather than the trial data. And a different company’s device is a different intervention; its failure says nothing about Cefalyne’s pulses.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Price concerns affordability, not efficacy. An expensive treatment can still work exactly as claimed; cost gives no reason to doubt the causal conclusion about the pulses.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Discomfort might affect compliance or satisfaction, but the trial measured migraine days among users, and discomfort provides no alternative explanation for the 40-percent gap the researchers attribute to the pulses.`,`Correct. It introduces a confounder bundled with the treatment: the headband group also adopted regular sleep schedules — an independent migraine reducer — while controls did not. With two differences between the groups, the trial cannot isolate the pulses as the cause, which directly weakens the researchers’ causal conclusion.`,`Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). The manufacturer’s legal history attacks the company’s credibility, not the trial’s data. Even a disreputable manufacturer can run a valid trial; weakening requires engaging the evidence, not the source.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). A different device from a different company is a different intervention. Its failure in an unrelated trial has no logical bearing on whether Cefalyne’s pulses reduced migraines here.`],generalLesson:`To weaken a causal conclusion from a trial, find the unmeasured difference that traveled with the treatment — a confounder bundled into the treatment group breaks the claim that the treatment itself did the work.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-causation`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The researchers conclude the pulses caused the improvement. For a trial to show that, what must be true about everything besides the pulses?`,`Imagine the headband users also changed something else that fights migraines. Would the trial still prove the pulses did it?`,`Which choice gives the headband group a second, migraine-reducing difference that the control group didn’t share?`],labels:{topic:`migraine headband trial`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty overstated at d5; the confounder is the standard trial-weaken move with single-layer reasoning and non-competing distractors. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 5 to 4; version 2; revisedAt 2026-09-11. Single defensible answer confirmed.`}],revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-h-260`,version:2,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-necessary-assumption`,secondarySkills:[`f-assumption`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:4,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,causalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:3,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`The county’s plan to reintroduce beavers will restore the valley’s wetlands. Beaver dams slow stream flow, and slower streams deposit the sediment that wetland plants need. The valley’s streams currently run too fast for sediment to settle.`,stem:`Which one of the following is an assumption required by the argument?`,choices:[{text:`Beaver dams are the only way to slow the valley’s streams.`},{text:`Wetland plants will return as soon as sediment is deposited in the valley’s streams.`},{text:`The reintroduction plan is the most cost-effective way to restore the valley’s wetlands.`},{text:`The reintroduced beavers will build dams in the valley’s streams.`},{text:`No other animal in the valley builds dams.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The argument’s mechanism runs entirely through beaver dams slowing the streams — but it never establishes that reintroduced beavers will actually build dams here. Negate it (the beavers build no dams) and the restoration mechanism never starts, so the conclusion collapses: the assumption is required.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the negation test, the decisive tool for necessary assumption questions: negate each candidate and see whether the argument survives. The argument’s causal chain is beavers → dams → slower streams → sediment → wetlands. The weak link is the very first step: the stimulus asserts what beaver dams do in general but never says these reintroduced beavers will build dams in these streams. Negate the correct choice — suppose the beavers build no dams in the valley’s streams. Then stream flow never slows, sediment never settles, and the wetlands are not restored by this plan; the conclusion fails. So the assumption is required. The distractors fail the test. If beaver dams aren’t the only way to slow streams, the plan can still work — uniqueness was never needed. If plants don’t return immediately upon sediment deposit, restoration might just take longer — immediacy was never claimed. Cost-effectiveness and other dam-building animals are simply irrelevant to whether this mechanism restores the wetlands.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument needs beaver dams to work, not to be the sole possible method. Negate it — other stream-slowing methods exist — and the beaver plan still restores the wetlands. A claim whose negation leaves the argument intact cannot be required.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The argument concludes the wetlands will be restored, not restored instantly. Negate immediacy — plants return gradually — and the conclusion survives. Required assumptions must be load-bearing, and timing was never part of the load.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Cost-effectiveness is never at issue; the argument is about whether the plan will restore the wetlands, not whether it is the cheapest way. Even the priciest successful plan would satisfy the conclusion.`,`Correct. The entire mechanism — dams slowing streams, sediment settling, wetlands returning — presupposes that the reintroduced beavers actually build dams in these streams, which the stimulus never states. Negation (they build no dams here) stops the chain at its first link and the conclusion collapses, proving necessity.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Whether muskrats or other animals build dams has no bearing on whether reintroduced beavers will dam these streams and trigger the described mechanism. It is true-or-false trivia beside the argument’s point.`],generalLesson:`Necessary assumptions often hide in the argument’s first causal step — the stimulus may establish what a mechanism does in general while quietly assuming the mechanism will actually engage in this case. Negate to expose it.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`negation`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`too-strong`,`irrelevant-true`,`irrelevant-true`],prerequisites:[`f-assumption`,`f-translate`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Trace the argument’s causal chain from beavers to wetlands. Which link does the stimulus assert, and which does it merely hope for?`,`The stimulus says what beaver dams do — but does it say these beavers will build any dams in these streams?`,`Negate each choice in turn. Which negation stops the restoration mechanism before it starts?`],labels:{topic:`beaver reintroduction wetlands`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: difficulty overstated at d5; the beavers-build-dams gap sits on the argument surface and the negation test is mechanical once spotted. Downgraded editorialDifficulty 5 to 4; version 2; revisedAt 2026-09-11. Single defensible answer confirmed.`}],revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`lr-h-261`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-argument-part`,secondarySkills:[`f-indicators`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,linguisticComplexity:3,distractorProximity:4,inferentialDistance:4},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`For decades the city of Harborview has operated an overnight ferry to the mainland, a route that loses money every winter. Admittedly, a consulting firm recently reported that most overnight passengers would simply take a late-night bus if the ferry were discontinued. But the ferry is the only carrier that moves refrigerated freight after 10 p.m., and mainland hospitals rely on those overnight deliveries; thus the ferry's real value is in its cargo service, not its passenger service. Judged by what it actually contributes, then, the overnight ferry deserves continued public funding.`,stem:`The claim that the ferry's real value is in its cargo service, not its passenger service, plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It is the argument's main conclusion, which the consulting firm's report is offered to support.`},{text:`It is an intermediate conclusion, inferred from the facts about refrigerated freight and hospital deliveries, supporting the funding claim in turn.`},{text:`It is a premise that directly supports the funding claim even though no other statement in the argument supports it, standing entirely on its own.`},{text:`It is background information describing how the ferry has traditionally been used by island residents.`},{text:`It is the concession the author makes to critics of the ferry before rebutting them directly.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The cargo-service claim is introduced by "thus," so it is inferred from the freight and hospital evidence — that makes it a conclusion, not a premise or background. But it is not the main conclusion: the final sentence builds on it ("judged by what it actually contributes") to deliver the funding verdict. A claim that is both supported by other claims and used to support a further claim is an intermediate conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the argument layer by layer. The opening sentence about decades of operation and winter losses is scene-setting background. The consulting-firm sentence is a concession: introduced with "admittedly," it acknowledges a point against the ferry — that its passengers could take the bus — without the author endorsing the ferry on those grounds. Then comes the pivot: the "but" introduces the freight facts, and "thus" marks an inference drawn from them, namely that the ferry's real value lies in cargo, not passengers. Because it is inferred from other claims, it cannot be a mere premise or background. Finally, the last sentence — "judged by what it actually contributes, then, the overnight ferry deserves continued public funding" — explicitly uses that cargo-value claim as its basis ("what it actually contributes" refers back to it) to reach the funding verdict. So the cargo claim sits in the middle of the structure: supported from below by the freight evidence, supporting from above the funding conclusion. That two-sided position is exactly what defines an intermediate conclusion, and it is what the tempting near-miss choices obscure by collapsing the structure into a single layer.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the funding verdict in the final sentence — the normative "deserves continued public funding" claim everything else points toward. The consulting-firm report actually cuts against the author's case, so it cannot be offered in support of anything here.`,`Correct. The word "thus" shows the cargo-service claim is inferred from the refrigerated-freight and hospital-delivery facts, and the final sentence then leans on that very claim ("judged by what it actually contributes") to conclude the ferry deserves funding. Supported from below and supporting above, it is an intermediate conclusion.`,`Incorrect (trap: half-right). It is true that the claim supports the funding verdict, but it is false that nothing supports it: "thus" marks it as inferred from the freight and hospital evidence. A premise that is itself argued for is an intermediate conclusion, not a basic premise.`,`Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Background information sets the scene without being inferred from other claims, but this claim is explicitly derived — "thus" — from the evidence about freight and hospitals. An inferred claim is doing structural work, not scene-setting.`,`Incorrect (trap: opposite). The concession is the consulting-firm sentence about the late-night bus, which acknowledges a weakness in the ferry's case. The cargo-service claim is the rebuttal pivot that answers that concession — the opposite role, turning the argument back toward funding.`],generalLesson:`When "thus" or "so" appears mid-argument and the final sentence builds on that claim, the mid-argument claim is an intermediate conclusion — supported from below, supporting above. Keep the concession (the acknowledged weakness) and the pivot (the answer to it) in separate boxes.`,misconceptionTags:[`conclusion-vs-premise`],trapTypes:[`wrong-conclusion`,`half-right`,`irrelevant-true`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which sentence is the author's final verdict — the claim the whole argument is driving toward? Start with the last sentence.`,`The word "thus" marks an inference. What evidence is the cargo-service claim inferred from, and what does the last sentence do with that claim when it says "judged by what it actually contributes"?`,`The consulting-firm sentence acknowledges a point against the ferry — that is the concession. Is the cargo claim acknowledging a weakness, or is it the pivot that answers the concession and feeds the final verdict?`],labels:{topic:`overnight ferry funding debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The thus-marked cargo claim is supported from below and supports the funding verdict above - intermediate conclusion; concession/pivot distractors fail. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-262`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-method`,secondarySkills:[`f-analogy`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,abstraction:4,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Airlines routinely overbook flights, yet few would call the practice dishonest. The justification is straightforward: a predictable share of ticket holders will not show up, and spreading the cost of those empty seats across all passengers keeps fares lower for everyone who does fly. The same reasoning applies to restaurants that charge diners who reserve tables and then fail to appear. A predictable share of reservations go unfulfilled, and a modest no-show fee spreads that cost so that menu prices stay lower for the diners who do show up. The city's proposed ban on restaurant no-show fees should therefore be rejected: it would force responsible diners to subsidize the diners who waste tables.`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?`,choices:[{text:`It shows that a principle underlying the airline practice leads to an unacceptable consequence when applied to restaurants, and it rejects the restaurant practice on that basis.`},{text:`It cites the airlines' overbooking records as statistical evidence proving that no-show fees lower menu prices.`},{text:`It argues that because the airline and restaurant cases are alike in every relevant respect, the restaurant practice must be acceptable.`},{text:`It uses the airline case to articulate a general principle about spreading the costs of predictable no-shows, applies it to restaurant reservations, and concludes the ban should be rejected.`},{text:`It demonstrates that supporters of the ban overlooked the effect of no-shows on menu prices, and it rejects the ban solely because of that oversight.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The airline example is not offered as data, and the argument never claims the two cases match in every detail. Instead the airline case is used to state a general principle — spreading the cost of predictable no-shows keeps prices lower for everyone — which is then applied to restaurants to oppose the ban. That principle-mediated move is exactly what the correct choice describes.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Break the reasoning into three steps. First, the airline case is introduced not as evidence but as an illustration: from it the author extracts a general principle, namely that when no-shows are predictable, spreading their cost across all users keeps prices lower for everyone who shows up. The key phrase is "the same reasoning applies" — the reasoning, the principle, is what carries over, not a point-by-point similarity between airlines and restaurants. Second, that principle is applied to the new case: restaurant no-show fees do the same cost-spreading work for menu prices. Third, the principle-backed defense of the practice yields the verdict that the proposed ban should be rejected. The near-miss choice about the cases being "alike in every relevant respect" overstates the argument: the author never claims exhaustive similarity and does not need it, since the principle does the justificatory work. The other distractors misread the analogy entirely — as statistical proof, as a reductio against the restaurant practice, or as an exposé of the ban's supporters — none of which the stimulus attempts.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: opposite). This describes a reductio argument that rejects the restaurant practice, but the argument does the reverse: it endorses the restaurant practice by means of the airline case and rejects the ban. The direction of the reasoning is inverted.`,`Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). No overbooking records or statistics are ever cited; "a predictable share" is a general observation used to state a principle, not a dataset. This choice recasts an illustrative analogy as empirical proof, shifting what the airline case is doing.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). This is the tempting near-miss: the argument does move from airlines to restaurants, but it never claims the cases are alike in every relevant respect. The work is done by the stated general principle ("the same reasoning applies"), which needs no exhaustive similarity.`,`Correct. The airline case articulates the principle — predictable no-show costs, spread across users, keep prices lower for all — and that principle is then applied to restaurant reservations to defend no-show fees and reject the ban. This captures the principle-mediated structure exactly.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The argument never discusses what the ban's supporters believe, overlooked, or intended, so no claim about their oversight can be part of its method. The rejection of the ban rests on the cost-spreading principle, not on anyone's inattention.`],generalLesson:`When an argument says "the same reasoning applies," look for the general principle doing the work — the method is principle-then-application, not a claim that the two cases match in every detail. "In every relevant respect" is almost always too strong.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What job is the airline example doing — is it presented as data to prove something, or as a way of stating a general idea?`,`Find the general principle the argument states outright (something about spreading costs and lower prices). Where does the argument go on to apply it?`,`Compare "the same reasoning applies" with "the cases are alike in every relevant respect." Does the argument ever claim the second, stronger thing, or does the principle do all the work?`],labels:{topic:`restaurant no-show fee debate`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Principle-mediated analogy via the same-reasoning-applies move; the every-relevant-respect distractor is too strong. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-263`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-most-strongly-supported`,secondarySkills:[`f-quantifiers`,`f-conditional`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,conditionalComplexity:3,distractorProximity:5,inferentialDistance:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Most patients referred to the Meridian specialty clinic end up receiving a formal diagnosis. Every patient who receives a formal diagnosis is given a written treatment plan. And every written treatment plan is reviewed by the clinic's board each quarter.`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the statements above?`,choices:[{text:`Every patient referred to the clinic receives a formal diagnosis.`},{text:`Every patient referred to the clinic is given a written treatment plan.`},{text:`Every patient referred to the clinic has a treatment plan that is reviewed each quarter.`},{text:`Most patients whose treatment plans are reviewed each quarter were referred to the clinic.`},{text:`Most patients referred to the clinic have treatment plans that are reviewed each quarter.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Chain the quantifiers: most referred patients are diagnosed, and every diagnosed patient gets a plan that is reviewed each quarter — so most referred patients end up with a reviewed plan. Each wrong choice upgrades one quantifier beyond what the chain licenses: three turn a "most" into "every," and one turns a "some" into a "most."`,explanationWalkthrough:`Translate "every" as "all" and line up the chain: most referred patients are diagnosed; all diagnosed patients get treatment plans; all treatment plans are reviewed quarterly. The chain begins with "most," and "most" chained through any number of "all" statements stays "most" — the weakest link caps the conclusion. So the licensed inference is that most referred patients have plans that are reviewed each quarter. Now audit each choice's quantifier against that cap. The first three choices each take a claim the chain supports at "most" strength and upgrade it to "every": most referred are diagnosed becomes every referred is diagnosed; most referred get plans becomes every referred gets a plan; most referred have reviewed plans becomes every referred has a reviewed plan. The fourth choice is subtler: the chain does guarantee that some reviewed plans belong to referred patients, but it reverses direction and upgrades "some" to "most" — the reviewed plans could largely belong to patients diagnosed without referral. Only the final choice keeps every quantifier exactly where the chain puts it.`,choiceExplanations:[`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The first statement says most referred patients are diagnosed, and nothing upgrades that "most" to "every." This choice takes a claim the premises support at "most" strength and strengthens it by one hedge too far.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). Chaining gives most referred patients a diagnosis and every diagnosed patient a plan, which yields only that most referred patients get plans. "Every" is one quantifier stronger than the chain licenses.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). This is the full-length version of the correct inference with its quantifier upgraded: the chain supports the claim at "most" strength, not "every." The initial "most" caps everything downstream of it.`,`Incorrect (trap: too-strong). The chain does entail that some reviewed plans belong to referred patients, but this choice reverses the direction and upgrades "some" to "most." Reviewed plans could mostly belong to patients who were diagnosed without ever being referred.`,`Correct. Most referred patients are diagnosed, all diagnosed patients receive plans, and all plans are reviewed quarterly — chaining "most" through "all" statements preserves "most." This is the only choice whose quantifiers match exactly what the premises license.`],generalLesson:`In quantifier chains, the weakest link caps the conclusion: "most" chained through any number of "all" statements stays "most." When every answer looks similar, check each quantifier against the chain — the right answer is the one that upgrades nothing.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`too-strong`,`too-strong`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`f-deduction`,`f-quantifiers`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Translate "every" as "all" and line up the three statements as a chain. What kind of quantifier starts the chain?`,`If most of a group have a trait, and everyone with that trait has a second trait, what can you say about the group and the second trait — "most" or "every"?`,`Check each choice's quantifier word by word against the chain: does the chain give you "every," "most," or only "some" — and does any choice reverse the direction?`],labels:{topic:`specialty clinic referral chain`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Most chained through all-statements stays most; quantifier-upgrade and reversed-direction distractors fail. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]},{id:`lr-h-264`,version:1,sectionType:`LR`,questionType:`lr-point-at-issue`,secondarySkills:[`f-premise-conclusion`,`f-structure`],editorialDifficulty:5,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,viewpointComplexity:4,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:3},itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:`Priya: "Converting Elm Street into a pedestrian mall will revitalize downtown. The mall will draw more foot traffic, and foot traffic is what the area's struggling storefronts need most."

Marcus: "Even if the mall draws more foot traffic, that increase will not rescue the storefronts that are failing because of high rents rather than low visibility. The mall's defenders are asking the wrong question: what matters is not whether foot traffic rises, but whether the mall does anything about the operating costs that are actually driving these shops under."`,stem:`The point at issue between Priya and Marcus is whether`,choices:[{text:`an increase in foot traffic would help downtown's struggling storefronts`},{text:`converting Elm Street into a pedestrian mall would increase foot traffic downtown`},{text:`high rents are contributing to the struggles of some downtown storefronts`},{text:`the city should convert Elm Street into a pedestrian mall`},{text:`reducing operating costs would help downtown's struggling storefronts`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Marcus's "even if" concedes the foot-traffic increase rather than disputing it, so the two only appear to disagree there. But Priya claims foot traffic is what struggling storefronts need most, while Marcus claims it will not rescue them because high rents are the real problem — they genuinely take opposite sides on whether more foot traffic would help.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A point at issue requires both speakers to address the same proposition and take incompatible stands on it, so test each choice against both speakers. The tempting choice about whether the mall would increase foot traffic fails that test: Marcus's "even if" grants the increase for the sake of argument instead of denying it, so there is no genuine disagreement — they merely seem to engage on that point. The real clash is one step downstream. Priya asserts that foot traffic is what the struggling storefronts need most, which commits her to the view that more foot traffic would help them. Marcus directly denies this: the increase "will not rescue" the failing storefronts, because their problem is high rents rather than low visibility. Those are incompatible positions on the same proposition, which makes it the point at issue. The remaining choices each fail the two-speaker test: only Marcus addresses high rents, only Marcus implies anything about the mall itself (he criticizes its defenders' reasoning without taking a stand on the proposal), and only Marcus implies that lower operating costs would matter.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Priya is committed to the view that more foot traffic would help — it is what struggling storefronts "need most" — while Marcus explicitly denies it, saying the increase "will not rescue" storefronts failing because of high rents. Both address the same proposition and take opposite sides.`,`Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). This looks like a disagreement because Marcus discusses foot traffic, but his "even if" concedes the increase arguendo rather than disputing it. A proposition one speaker grants and the other asserts is not a point of genuine disagreement.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Marcus addresses high rents; Priya never mentions rents at all, so there is no clash. Her claim that foot traffic is what storefronts "need most" does not deny that rents also contribute to their struggles.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Priya supports the mall, but Marcus never takes a position on whether the city should build it — he criticizes the defenders' reasoning and redirects the question to operating costs. Without a stand from Marcus, there is no point at issue.`,`Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Marcus implies that doing something about operating costs is what matters, but Priya says nothing about operating costs either way. A point at issue needs both speakers on opposite sides of the same claim.`],generalLesson:`"Even if" is a concession flag, not a disagreement: when a speaker grants a premise arguendo and attacks what follows from it, the real dispute is downstream. A point at issue requires both speakers to address the same proposition and take incompatible stands on it.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`f-premise-conclusion`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`For a genuine point at issue, both speakers must address the same claim and take opposite sides. Which claims does each speaker actually take a stand on?`,`What does Marcus's "even if" do — does he deny that foot traffic will rise, or does he grant it for the sake of argument and attack something else?`,`Priya says foot traffic is what struggling storefronts need most. What does Marcus say about whether a foot-traffic increase will rescue them — and do those two claims conflict?`],labels:{topic:`pedestrian mall foot-traffic dispute`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`full-lr-classification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. The even-if concession removes the foot-traffic rise from dispute; the genuine clash is downstream on whether it would help. Difficulty d5 honest.`}]}],c=[{id:`rc-p01-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Judges should always decide cases as narrowly as possible, since broad rulings are invariably mistaken and democratically illegitimate in every area of law.`},{text:`Judicial minimalism — deciding narrowly on shallow grounds — is best defended as a rebuttable default: it limits error costs and respects democratic deliberation, allowing exceptions where breadth is required.`},{text:`Maximalist opinions are superior to minimalist ones because only broad rulings give citizens and lower courts the clarity they need to plan their conduct.`},{text:`The debate between minimalists and maximalists is unresolvable, so judges should ignore the theoretical debate and decide cases on purely pragmatic grounds tied to immediate outcomes.`},{text:`Minimalism is valuable chiefly because it protects the judiciary’s public legitimacy by making courts appear less political and more like neutral arbiters of disputes rather than policymakers.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage presents the minimalist case (paragraphs 1–2), gives maximalism a fair hearing (paragraphs 3–4), and concludes that minimalism works best as a rebuttable default rather than an absolute rule (paragraph 5). Choice B captures that full arc; the others overstate, invert, or shrink it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Start by mapping the passage’s structure. Paragraph 1 introduces minimalism; paragraph 2 gives three arguments for it; paragraph 3 presents the maximalist critique; paragraph 4 concedes that both sides have historical ammunition; paragraph 5 resolves the tension by recasting minimalism as a rebuttable default rather than a universal prescription. A main-point answer must reflect this whole movement, not just one stage of it. Choice A fails because the passage explicitly rejects minimalism as an absolute rule — "as a universal prescription it seems implausible." Choice C inverts the author’s sympathies, endorsing the maximalist position the passage criticizes. Choice D invents a counsel of despair the passage never offers; the author clearly thinks the debate yields a usable conclusion. Choice E is half-right — legitimacy is one of three minimalist claims — but it reduces the main point to a single supporting argument and omits the rebuttable-default conclusion. Only choice B holds together the minimalist case, the maximalist concession, and the final qualification, which is exactly what a main-point answer must do.`,choiceExplanations:[`Tempting because it echoes the passage’s sympathy for minimalism, but it fails as too-strong: "always" and "invariably" contradict the final paragraph, which explicitly rejects minimalism as a universal prescription and admits some disputes genuinely demand breadth.`,`Correct. It captures the passage’s full arc — the minimalist thesis, its rationale (limiting error costs, respecting democratic deliberation), and the crucial final qualification that minimalism is a rebuttable default rather than an absolute rule.`,`This is the opposite of the author’s position (opposite trap). The passage presents maximalist clarity arguments only to answer them; its conclusion favors minimalism as the default, not maximalism as the superior approach.`,`Tempting if you overread the concessions in paragraph 4, but it is unsupported: the author never suggests the debate is unresolvable or that judges should ignore theory. This is a scope-shift — it moves from "both sides have good points" to "reasoning about the question is pointless."`,`Half-right: protecting legitimacy is genuinely one of the three minimalist claims in paragraph 2. But it fails because it mistakes one supporting argument for the main point and omits the passage’s conclusion about minimalism as a rebuttable default.`],generalLesson:`A main-point answer must survive the whole passage: check it against the introduction, the counterargument, and especially the conclusion, where authors typically state their qualified final position.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Where does the author land at the very end? Main-point answers live or die on the conclusion.`,`Does the author defend minimalism without qualification, or with an important "but"?`,`Eliminate any choice the final paragraph contradicts — then check that what remains covers the whole passage, not just one paragraph.`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,5],supportingSentences:[`Minimalist opinions "decide no more than the case before them requires" on grounds "as shallow as possible" (paragraph 1).`,`"As a rebuttable default, however, the minimalist impulse has considerable force" (paragraph 5).`],requiredInference:`The main point is the thesis plus the final qualification: minimalism defended as a rebuttable default with error-cost and democratic-deliberation rationales, not as an absolute rule.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A too-strong ("always"/"invariably" contradicts paragraph 5’s rejection of a universal prescription); C opposite (the passage favors minimalism, not maximalism); D unsupported (the author reaches a usable conclusion); E half-right (legitimacy is one of three paragraph-2 claims, not the conclusion).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review found no second defensible answer. All passage quotes verified accurate; length ratio 1.09 and gap 17 pass leakage checks; d2 profile honest for a synthesize-the-arc main point.`}]},{id:`rc-p01-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3,viewpointComplexity:3},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`According to the passage, proponents of judicial minimalism claim that narrow rulings do all of the following EXCEPT:`,choices:[{text:`limit the damage done when judges decide cases incorrectly`},{text:`return contested questions to legislatures and citizens`},{text:`make courts appear less like a rival legislature`},{text:`guarantee that lower courts will apply the law consistently`},{text:`reduce the judiciary’s exposure to controversial questions`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 lists three minimalist claims: narrow rulings limit the damage of judicial error, respect democratic self-government by leaving questions open, and protect judicial legitimacy. Guaranteeing consistent lower-court application is actually a maximalist argument (paragraph 3), so it is the EXCEPT answer.`,explanationWalkthrough:`EXCEPT questions reward precise mapping. Paragraph 2 enumerates the minimalist case in three parts: first, judges deciding under uncertainty are prone to error, and narrow rulings limit the damage when they err — that is choice A. Second, minimalism respects democratic self-government by leaving contested questions to legislatures and citizens — choice B. Third, restraint protects the judiciary’s legitimacy by making courts appear less like a rival legislature — choices C and E both paraphrase this. Choice D, by contrast, belongs to the other side: paragraph 3 reports the maximalist complaint that minimalist rulings leave lower courts guessing, and argues that broad rulings supply the clarity lower courts need. So the claim that narrow rulings guarantee consistent application is the opposite of what minimalists argue — it is the maximalists’ selling point. That makes D the one item minimalists do not claim, and therefore the correct EXCEPT answer.`,choiceExplanations:[`Supported-nonresponsive: the passage explicitly attributes this to minimalists — paragraph 2 says narrow rulings limit the damage when judges err. It is a genuine minimalist claim, so it cannot be the exception the EXCEPT stem requires.`,`Supported-nonresponsive: paragraph 2 states that minimalism respects democratic self-government by leaving contested questions to legislatures and citizens. This is directly claimed, so it is not the answer the EXCEPT stem requires.`,`Supported-nonresponsive: paragraph 2’s third claim is that restraint protects judicial legitimacy by making courts look less like a rival legislature. Directly stated, so not the exception.`,`Correct. This claim is never made by minimalists — it is the maximalists’ argument in paragraph 3, who contend that broad rulings (not narrow ones) give lower courts usable guidance. It is the one item on the list the minimalists do not assert.`,`Supported-nonresponsive: this paraphrases the legitimacy rationale of paragraph 2 — modest, case-bound decisions keep courts from appearing political. It is a fair restatement of a claimed benefit, so it is not the exception.`],generalLesson:`On EXCEPT questions, verify each choice against the passage one by one; the answer is often a claim that belongs to the opposing viewpoint, so keep track of who argues what.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph lays out the minimalist case? List its claims before looking at the choices.`,`Four choices will match that list. Which one sounds like something the other side would say?`,`The maximalists’ big complaint is about clarity for lower courts — whose talking point is choice D?`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`"narrow rulings limit the damage when they err" (paragraph 2).`,`"by leaving contested questions open, courts return them to legislatures and citizens" (paragraph 2).`,`Maximalists complain that thin opinions leave lower courts guessing and that broad rulings give a rule citizens can plan around (paragraph 3).`],viewpointOwner:`proponents of judicial minimalism`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B, C, and E each restate a paragraph-2 minimalist claim (error-cost limitation, democratic deliberation, legitimacy/rival-legislature); D is the maximalists’ paragraph-3 clarity argument, so it is the one item minimalists do not claim.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`EXCEPT item with four genuine paragraph-2 claims and one wrong-viewpoint answer. Hostile review could not defend D as a minimalist claim — the passage attributes lower-court clarity arguments to maximalists. Leakage checks pass (ratio 1.02, gap 1); d2 honest.`}]},{id:`rc-p01-q3`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`The passage suggests that the author would most likely agree with which one of the following statements?`,choices:[{text:`A mistaken broad constitutional ruling is generally harder to correct than a mistaken narrow one.`},{text:`Judges who issue minimalist opinions are less intelligent than judges who develop comprehensive legal theories.`},{text:`Democratic legislatures invariably reach better outcomes than courts on contested moral questions.`},{text:`The Supreme Court should never issue a ruling broader than the facts of the case before it.`},{text:`Lower courts prefer narrow rulings because narrow rulings reduce their workload.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 argues that a narrow decision "can usually be revisited," while a broad one, "once woven into doctrine, resists correction." Choice A is the direct inference; the rest add claims about intelligence, legislative superiority, absolutes, or lower-court preferences that the passage never makes.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Inference questions ask what follows from the passage without being stated outright — but the inference must still be defensible, not invented. Paragraph 4 draws an explicit asymmetry: minimalist landmarks caused confusion but could be revisited, whereas maximalist, theory-laden opinions became cautionary tales precisely because broad rulings, once woven into doctrine, resist correction. Choice A restates that asymmetry as a general comparative claim, which is exactly what the paragraph supports. Choice B is a scope-shift: the passage criticizes an approach to judging, never the intelligence of judges. Choice C takes the democratic-deliberation rationale of paragraph 2 and inflates it into an "invariably better" claim the author never makes — a too-strong distortion. Choice D converts the rebuttable default of paragraph 5 into an absolute prohibition ("never"), which the author explicitly rejects. Choice E fabricates a claim about lower-court preferences; the passage says maximalists believe lower courts need clarity, not that lower courts prefer narrow rulings to lighten workloads. Only A stays within what the text actually licenses.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Paragraph 4 states the asymmetry directly: narrow decisions can usually be revisited, while broad ones, once woven into doctrine, resist correction. This choice draws the licensed inference from that comparison.`,`Tempting only if you confuse criticism of a judicial method with criticism of judges themselves — a scope-shift. The passage never discusses judicial intelligence; it debates how broadly opinions should reason.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 2 says minimalism "respects democratic self-government" by leaving questions to legislatures, but nothing in the passage claims legislatures invariably reach better outcomes. "Invariably" goes far beyond the text.`,`Too-strong and directly contradicted: the final paragraph rejects minimalism "as a universal prescription" and says some disputes "genuinely demand breadth." An absolute "never" rule is the opposite of the author’s rebuttable default.`,`Unsupported. The passage discusses what maximalists claim lower courts need (clarity), but says nothing about lower courts preferring narrow rulings to reduce workload. This invents both a preference and a motive.`],generalLesson:`A defensible inference paraphrases a real comparative or causal claim in the text; if your inference needs words like "always," "never," or "invariably" that the passage didn’t earn, it has gone too far.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Find the sentence about what happens when each kind of ruling turns out to be wrong.`,`The passage compares revisiting a narrow decision with correcting a broad one — what does that comparison imply?`,`Strike any choice containing absolutes the passage never earned, then check what remains against paragraph 4.`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`"A narrow decision can usually be revisited; a broad one, once woven into doctrine, resists correction" (paragraph 4).`],requiredInference:`The stated revisability asymmetry licenses the general comparative claim: a mistaken broad ruling is harder to correct than a mistaken narrow one.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B scope-shift (judicial intelligence never discussed); C too-strong ("invariably better" exceeds the democratic-deliberation rationale); D too-strong and contradicted (paragraph 5 rejects minimalism as a universal prescription); E unsupported (no claim about lower-court workload preferences).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Validate after revision: downgraded editorialDifficulty 3→2 (version 2). The credited inference is a near-paraphrase of paragraph 4’s explicit narrow/broad revisability comparison, and distractors fail on blatant absolutes ("invariably", "never") or invented claims — d3 rated the type’s reputation, not the item’s load. No other changes.`}],revisedAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`rc-p01-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,viewpointComplexity:2},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`The author refers to "cautionary tales about judges who mistook the moment’s certainties for timeless truths" (paragraph 4) primarily in order to`,choices:[{text:`prove that maximalist judges are motivated by arrogance rather than by a desire for clarity`},{text:`suggest that judges should avoid reasoning from moral principles in all circumstances`},{text:`argue that the history of constitutional law is too unreliable a guide to support any theory of judging`},{text:`concede that minimalism has never produced a successful constitutional precedent`},{text:`illustrate the claim that broad, theory-laden rulings carry their own distinctive risks of costly error`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The reference appears in paragraph 4, where the author concedes both sides have historical ammunition: minimalist rulings caused confusion, but maximalist ones produced cautionary tales. Its function is to show that breadth carries its own error costs — balancing the ledger before the rebuttable-default conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask why a detail is there, which means locating it in the paragraph’s argumentative job. Paragraph 4’s job is evenhanded concession: "The historical record offers ammunition to both camps, and honest partisans on each side concede the point." The first half of the paragraph gives minimalism’s embarrassments (confusion in lower courts); the "cautionary tales" sentence gives maximalism’s — confidently reasoned, theory-laden opinions that aged badly because judges mistook temporary certainties for permanent ones. Its function is therefore to illustrate that sweeping rulings have their own characteristic failure mode: confident error that is hard to undo. Choice A overreads motive — the passage says nothing about arrogance as motivation. Choice C is a scope-shift: the author uses history as evidence, not as proof that history is useless. Choice D inverts the concession — the paragraph says minimalist rulings caused confusion, not that minimalism never succeeded. Choice B is too-strong; the passage never counsels avoiding moral reasoning entirely. Only E names the detail’s actual argumentative role.`,choiceExplanations:[`Tempting because "mistook the moment’s certainties" sounds like a character judgment, but the passage attributes no motive — this is a scope-shift from a claim about the risks of broad rulings to a claim about judges’ arrogance, which the text never makes.`,`Too-strong. Nothing in the passage tells judges to avoid moral principles altogether; the debate is about breadth of rulings, and the final paragraph endorses minimalism only as a rebuttable default.`,`Unsupported scope-shift. The author treats the historical record as useful evidence for both camps ("offers ammunition to both camps"), not as so unreliable that no theory of judging can be supported.`,`Opposite of the paragraph’s point. The concession about minimalism is that its landmarks "generated years of costly confusion," not that minimalism never produced successful precedents — and the sentence in question concerns maximalist failures, not minimalist ones.`,`Correct. In paragraph 4’s evenhanded survey, the cautionary tales are maximalism’s embarrassments, balancing minimalism’s: they illustrate that broad, theory-laden opinions carry their own distinctive risk — confident, hard-to-correct error.`],generalLesson:`To answer a function question, identify the paragraph’s argumentative job first; the detail’s purpose is almost always to advance that job, not to make some freestanding point.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is paragraph 4 doing as a whole? Read its first sentence.`,`The paragraph gives each side an embarrassment — which side’s embarrassment is the "cautionary tales" sentence?`,`The right answer will describe a balancing move, not a new freestanding claim.`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`"several of the most confidently reasoned, theory-laden opinions in the canon are now taught mainly as cautionary tales about judges who mistook the moment’s certainties for timeless truths" (paragraph 4).`,`"The historical record offers ammunition to both camps" (paragraph 4).`],requiredInference:`The paragraph’s job is evenhanded concession; the "cautionary tales" sentence supplies maximalism’s embarrassment, so the detail’s function is to illustrate that broad, theory-laden rulings carry their own distinctive error costs.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A scope-shift (arrogance as motive never attributed); B too-strong (no blanket ban on moral reasoning); C scope-shift (history is treated as useful evidence, not unreliable); D opposite (the sentence concerns maximalist, not minimalist, failures).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Stem quote verified verbatim against paragraph 4. Function correctly anchored to the paragraph’s evenhanded-concession job; hostile review could not make the detail support any freestanding claim in A–D. Leakage checks pass (tied longest, gap 0); d3 honest.`}]},{id:`rc-p01-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-author-viewpoint`,secondarySkills:[`rc-attitude`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,viewpointComplexity:4},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward the proposal that minimalism should serve as a universal prescription for judges can best be described as`,choices:[{text:`enthusiastic endorsement`},{text:`hostile opposition`},{text:`skeptical, holding that it is implausible as an absolute rule though valuable as a default`},{text:`indifferent, since the author believes the debate has no practical consequences`},{text:`cautiously optimistic that it will soon be adopted by all appellate courts`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph says minimalism "as a universal prescription ... seems implausible" while defending it as a "rebuttable default." That is skepticism about the absolute version combined with qualified support — exactly choice C.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Author-viewpoint questions require distinguishing the author’s position from the positions they merely report. The author plainly sympathizes with minimalism — paragraphs 1–2 present its case without irony, and the conclusion endorses it as a default. But the final paragraph draws a sharp line: "As a universal prescription it seems implausible — some disputes ... genuinely demand breadth. As a rebuttable default, however, the minimalist impulse has considerable force." So the author’s attitude toward the universal-prescription version is skeptical, not hostile (they still favor minimalism as a default) and not enthusiastic (they explicitly call the absolute version implausible). Choice A ignores the "implausible" verdict. Choice B confuses skepticism about the absolute rule with opposition to minimalism itself. Choice D is unsupported — the author treats the debate as practically consequential throughout. Choice E invents a prediction about adoption that appears nowhere. Choice C is the only answer that holds both halves of the author’s position: doubt about the absolute rule, support for the qualified one.`,choiceExplanations:[`Half-right but ultimately wrong: the author does favor minimalism, but "enthusiastic endorsement" of the universal prescription contradicts the final paragraph’s explicit verdict that it "seems implausible." It mistakes qualified support for unqualified support.`,`Opposite. The author is skeptical of minimalism as an absolute rule, not hostile to minimalism — the conclusion endorses it as a rebuttable default. Hostility mischaracterizes a nuanced ally as an enemy.`,`Correct. It captures both halves of the author’s stated position: the universal prescription "seems implausible," while minimalism retains "considerable force" as a rebuttable default. This two-part structure is exactly what attitude questions test: the author can doubt the absolute rule while endorsing the qualified one.`,`Unsupported. The passage treats the choice between narrow and broad rulings as deeply consequential for error costs, democratic deliberation, and legitimacy; nothing suggests the author finds the debate practically indifferent.`,`Unsupported and too-strong. The passage makes no predictions about what appellate courts will adopt, let alone "all" of them "soon." This invents a forecast the text never offers.`],generalLesson:`When an author qualifies a position ("as a universal prescription ... implausible; as a rebuttable default ... considerable force"), the right attitude answer must preserve the qualification — watch for choices that drop it.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-author-viewpoint`,`rc-attitude`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Find the sentence where the author evaluates minimalism "as a universal prescription." What word does the author use?`,`Does the author reject minimalism entirely, or only its absolute version?`,`The answer must hold two ideas at once: doubt about the absolute rule, support for the qualified one.`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`"As a universal prescription it seems implausible" (paragraph 5).`,`"As a rebuttable default, however, the minimalist impulse has considerable force" (paragraph 5).`],viewpointOwner:`the passage author`,whyDistractorsFail:`A drops the "implausible" verdict (qualified, not enthusiastic, support); B mistakes skepticism about the absolute rule for hostility to minimalism itself; D unsupported (the debate is treated as practically consequential); E invents a prediction about court adoption.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Author’s two-part verdict ("implausible" as universal prescription / "considerable force" as default) is quoted verbatim. No defensible alternate reading: hostility and indifference readings both contradict explicit sentences. Leakage checks pass (ratio 1.14, gap 11); d3 honest for a qualified-attitude item.`}]},{id:`rc-p01-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p01`,stem:`Suppose a federal appeals court must decide a novel constitutional question on which no narrow ground is available, and the judges agree that lower courts will be unable to administer the law without a comprehensive standard. According to the passage’s argument, the court should`,choices:[{text:`issue a minimalist ruling anyway, since minimalism requires deciding no more than the case at hand in every instance`},{text:`decline to decide the case until the legislature clarifies the underlying constitutional provision`},{text:`rule broadly, because minimalism is defended only as a rebuttable default and this scenario rebuts the presumption`},{text:`rule broadly, because the passage argues that maximalist opinions are generally more legitimate than minimalist ones`},{text:`issue two opinions — a narrow holding for the parties and a broad advisory discussion for lower courts`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph explicitly contemplates cases that "genuinely demand breadth," such as those requiring lower courts to administer a workable standard — this scenario matches that exception. Since minimalism is a rebuttable default, the court should rule broadly here.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions test whether you can run the passage’s principle on new facts. The passage’s principle, stated in the final paragraph, is that minimalism is "best understood ... as a rebuttable default": decide narrowly unless there is good reason not to. The paragraph even names the exception that fits this scenario — "disputes, such as those requiring lower courts to administer a workable standard across thousands of cases, genuinely demand breadth." The hypothetical stipulates exactly that: no narrow ground is available and lower courts need a comprehensive standard. So the presumption is rebutted and the court should rule broadly. Choice A applies minimalism as an absolute rule, which the passage explicitly rejects. Choice B invents a dodge — declining to decide — that appears nowhere in the passage. Choice D misattributes a pro-maximalist legitimacy claim to the passage; legitimacy is a minimalist argument. Choice E invents a procedural hybrid the passage never discusses. Only C correctly applies the rebuttable-default framework to the stipulated facts.`,choiceExplanations:[`Tempting if you remember the minimalist thesis but forget the conclusion — this is the wrong-viewpoint trap in application form. The passage explicitly rejects minimalism "as a universal prescription"; applying it absolutely here ignores the rebuttable-default qualification.`,`Unsupported. The passage never suggests courts should decline to decide hard cases or wait for legislatures to clarify constitutional provisions. This invents a remedy the author does not discuss.`,`Correct. The scenario matches the final paragraph’s stated exception — cases "requiring lower courts to administer a workable standard" that "genuinely demand breadth" — so the rebuttable presumption for narrowness is overcome.`,`Wrong-viewpoint: the passage argues legitimacy favors minimalism (modest decisions make courts look less political), not maximalism. This choice flips which side the legitimacy rationale supports.`,`Unsupported. The passage never proposes split narrow/broad opinions or advisory discussions; this invents a procedural solution rather than applying the passage’s actual principle. On application questions, any choice proposing machinery the passage never discusses is automatically suspect, no matter how clever it sounds.`],generalLesson:`To apply a passage’s principle, restate the principle with its qualifications first, then check which hypothetical facts trigger the qualification — here, the "genuinely demand breadth" exception.`,misconceptionTags:[`necessary-vs-sufficient`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the passage’s final formulation of minimalism — an absolute rule or something weaker?`,`The last paragraph names a specific kind of case that "genuinely demand[s] breadth." Does the hypothetical match it?`,`If the presumption is rebutted on these facts, what follows?`],labels:{topic:`judicial minimalism`},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`"disputes, such as those requiring lower courts to administer a workable standard across thousands of cases, genuinely demand breadth" (paragraph 5).`,`Minimalism is "best understood ... as a rebuttable default" (paragraph 5).`],requiredInference:`The hypothetical stipulates exactly the paragraph-5 exception (no narrow ground available; lower courts need a comprehensive standard), so the presumption for narrowness is rebutted and a broad ruling follows.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A applies minimalism as the absolute rule paragraph 5 explicitly rejects; B invents a decline-to-decide remedy the passage never discusses; D flips the legitimacy rationale (it favors minimalism, not maximalism); E invents split-opinion machinery the passage never discusses.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Application correctly matches hypothetical facts to the paragraph-5 exception; hostile review could not defend ruling narrowly or declining to decide without contradicting "genuinely demand breadth." Leakage checks pass (credited not longest, ratio 0.98); d4 honest for principle-plus-qualification application.`}]},{id:`rc-p02-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-primary-purpose`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`"abolition is a fantasy" (paragraph 5)`,`"from whether plea bargaining should exist to how ... the law should keep it honest"`],requiredInference:`The purpose answer must synthesize all five paragraph-level moves - history, defense, critique, regulatory reframing, reform conclusion - rather than any single paragraph.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A: too-strong/opposite - the author rejects abolition ("a fantasy") and never claims unconstitutionality. B: supported-nonresponsive - the history serves an evaluative argument ending in a reform prescription. D: opposite - paragraph 3 cites exoneration studies to show innocent people DO plead guilty. E: unsupported - Brady is presented as a legitimation milestone, never attacked.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited C tracks all five paragraph moves (history, defense, critique, regulatory reframing, reform conclusion). A is too-strong/opposite (abolition rejected), B mistakes evenhandedness for neutrality, D is opposite (exoneration studies), E invents an attack on Brady. Length ratio 1.09, gap 12 - no leakage. Explanations 28-35 words. d2 honest for a full-passage synthesis item. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`,choices:[{text:`condemn plea bargaining as an unconstitutional practice that should be abolished immediately, without considering any counterarguments`},{text:`provide a neutral historical chronicle of American criminal procedure, advancing no evaluative argument about plea bargaining at all`},{text:`trace the rise of plea bargaining, present the main arguments on both sides, and argue that regulation rather than abolition is the realistic path`},{text:`prove that innocent defendants never plead guilty and that the trial penalty described by critics is therefore a myth`},{text:`argue that the Supreme Court was wrong to legitimize plea bargaining in Brady v. United States and should overturn that precedent`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The passage moves from history (paragraph 1) to the defenders’ case (paragraph 2), the critics’ case (paragraph 3), a rebuttal framing abuses as regulatory failures (paragraph 4), and a conclusion favoring reform over abolition (paragraph 5). Choice C is the only answer that captures that full purpose.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Primary-purpose answers must describe what the passage as a whole is doing, not just one paragraph of it. Here the structure is: paragraph 1 historicizes (from nineteenth-century disapproval to Brady’s legitimation), paragraph 2 gives the affirmative case, paragraph 3 the critical case, paragraph 4 reframes the dispute as a regulatory problem, and paragraph 5 concludes that abolition is a fantasy and safeguards are the live question. Choice C tracks all five moves. Choice A captures only the critics’ half and then overstates it — the author explicitly rejects abolition. Choice B mistakes evenhandedness for neutrality; the passage clearly argues for a conclusion. Choice D is the opposite of paragraph 3, which cites exoneration studies showing innocent people do plead guilty. Choice E invents an attack on Brady that the passage never makes — Brady is presented as a historical milestone, not as wrongly decided. When a passage gives both sides a full hearing before endorsing a middle path, the purpose answer must name the hearing and the verdict.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite and too-strong: the passage reports the critics’ case but its conclusion explicitly calls abolition "a fantasy." Condemnation is half the passage, not its purpose, and "unconstitutional" is a claim the text never makes.`,`Half-right in noticing the historical material, but it fails as supported-nonresponsive: paragraph 1’s history serves an evaluative argument that culminates in paragraph 5’s reform prescription. "No evaluative argument" misdescribes the passage.`,`Correct. It names each structural move — the historical rise, the two-sided debate, and the concluding turn from "whether" bargaining should exist to "how" it should be regulated.`,`Opposite: paragraph 3 cites exoneration research precisely to show that innocent defendants do plead guilty, making the trial penalty a real rather than mythical concern. This choice inverts the passage’s evidence.`,`Unsupported. Brady v. United States is presented as the culmination of plea bargaining’s legitimation, and the passage never argues the decision was wrongly decided — its argument concerns how to regulate the system Brady accepted.`],generalLesson:`For primary-purpose questions, map the passage’s paragraph-level moves first; the answer must account for the introduction, the counterargument, and the conclusion together.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`half-right`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-primary-purpose`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does each paragraph do? List the jobs: history, defense, critique, response, conclusion.`,`Does the author end up on one side, or somewhere in between?`,`Eliminate any choice that describes only one paragraph’s job.`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p02-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`"declared negotiated pleas not merely tolerable but an essential component of the administration of justice"`],whyDistractorsFail:`B: opposite - the speedy-trial promise is invoked in paragraph 2 as a reason bargaining is needed, not a right it violates. C and E: misattribution - exculpatory-evidence disclosure and capping the differential are paragraph-5 reform proposals, not Brady holdings. D: keyword-match - judges not participating in negotiations is a paragraph-4 systemic feature, not something Brady decided.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A is a near-verbatim restatement of the Brady sentence in paragraph 1. Defended each distractor: B inverts the speedy-trial point; C and E are real paragraph-5 proposals misattributed to Brady; D is a real paragraph-4 feature misattributed to Brady. Misattribution of genuine content is the live trap and each choice names its source. Length ratio 1.09. Explanations 26-45 words. d2 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`According to the passage, the Supreme Court’s decision in Brady v. United States (1970)`,choices:[{text:`declared negotiated guilty pleas an essential component of the administration of justice`},{text:`held that plea bargaining violates the constitutional right to a speedy trial`},{text:`required prosecutors to disclose all exculpatory evidence before accepting a plea`},{text:`prohibited judges from participating in plea negotiations`},{text:`limited the difference between plea offers and post-trial sentences`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states that in Brady v. United States (1970) the Court "declared negotiated pleas not merely tolerable but an essential component of the administration of justice." The other choices describe reforms the passage discusses as proposals or attributes to other sources, not to Brady.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Detail questions are won by locating the exact sentence. Paragraph 1’s final clause says Brady "declared negotiated pleas not merely tolerable but an essential component of the administration of justice" — a near-verbatim match for choice A. Each distractor borrows real passage content but misattributes it. Choice B inverts the passage: the speedy-trial promise is invoked in paragraph 2 as something bargaining protects, not as a right bargaining violates. Choice C describes a reform proposal from paragraph 5 ("requiring prosecutors to disclose exculpatory evidence before a plea"), not a Brady holding. Choice D refers to the federal rule noted in paragraph 4 that judges "may not even participate in negotiations" — a feature of the current system, not something Brady established. Choice E likewise describes a paragraph-5 reform proposal ("capping the differential"), not the decision. The trap pattern here is misattribution of genuine passage details, so the fix is to check not just whether the content appears, but where and in what role.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This restates paragraph 1’s description of Brady nearly verbatim: negotiated pleas were declared "not merely tolerable but an essential component of the administration of justice."`,`Opposite and unsupported: paragraph 2 invokes the speedy-trial promise as a reason bargaining is needed (without it, backlogs would make the promise "a dead letter"), not as a right that bargaining violates. Brady held no such thing.`,`Misattribution — a supported-nonresponsive trap. Broader pretrial disclosure of exculpatory evidence is a reform proposal in paragraph 5, not a holding of Brady. This misattribution is the passage’s signature trap: genuine reform proposals from paragraph 5 are dressed up as holdings of the paragraph-1 case.`,`Keyword-match. The passage notes in paragraph 4 that federal judges "may not even participate in negotiations," but presents this as a current systemic feature illustrating weak oversight, not as something Brady decided.`,`Scope-shift. Capping the plea/trial-sentence differential is listed among possible reforms in paragraph 5; it was not imposed by Brady. Capping the differential is one of the live reforms the author favors, but attributing it to Brady confuses a proposal with a precedent.`],generalLesson:`On detail questions, distractors often contain true passage content in the wrong role — verify not just that the words appear, but that they are attributed to the right source and claim.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`supported-nonresponsive`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Find the sentence that names Brady. What verb describes what the Court did?`,`Several choices describe real reform proposals from later paragraphs — were any of them decided in Brady?`,`Match the choice to the exact sentence, not just to familiar words.`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p02-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`"urban caseloads swelled beyond anything a trial-centered system could absorb"`,`"an essential component of the administration of justice"`],requiredInference:`From the paragraph-1 causal story (crushing caseloads forced acceptance) and Brady language of indispensability, infer that legitimation was necessity-driven rather than admiration-driven.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A: opposite - nineteenth-century courts called bargaining "a sordid practice to be discouraged." B: half-right - paragraph 3 supports the vulnerability claim but the proposed ban on pleas is invented, and paragraph 5 says defendants would not thank reformers for forcing a trial gamble. C: opposite inference - necessity, not enthusiasm. D: unsupported - the passage never explores eliminating the trial penalty entirely.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E is the licensed inference from the paragraph-1 causal story (caseload pressure) plus Brady indispensability language. A is opposite (courts discouraged bargaining); B is half-right with an invented prescription undercut by paragraph 5; C inverts necessity into enthusiasm; D invents an unexplored hypothetical. One defensible answer. Explanations 26-54 words. d3 honest for a causal-story inference. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Nineteenth-century judges approved of plea bargaining but lacked the caseload pressure to use it widely.`},{text:`Defendants who cannot afford vigorous counsel are more vulnerable to prosecutorial pressure during plea negotiations, and the passage therefore argues they should be barred from entering plea agreements.`},{text:`The Supreme Court legitimized plea bargaining primarily out of enthusiasm for bargaining as an ideal system of justice.`},{text:`Eliminating the trial penalty entirely would make plea bargaining indistinguishable from a trial.`},{text:`The Supreme Court’s legitimation of plea bargaining was driven largely by practical necessity rather than by admiration for bargaining as an ideal.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 says courts accepted bargaining only as "urban caseloads swelled beyond anything a trial-centered system could absorb," and Brady called it an "essential component" of administering justice — language of necessity, not admiration. The passage supports necessity-driven legitimation; the other choices invert or invent claims.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference must be anchored in what the passage actually says about why bargaining was legitimized. Paragraph 1 gives a causal story: nineteenth-century courts "treated plea bargaining as a sordid practice to be discouraged," and it moved "from the shadows into the open" only when "urban caseloads swelled beyond anything a trial-centered system could absorb." Brady’s language — an "essential component of the administration of justice" — speaks to systemic indispensability, not to bargaining’s intrinsic virtues. Choice E draws the licensed inference: legitimation was necessity-driven. Choice A is the opposite of paragraph 1, which says judges regarded the practice as improper. Choice B is half-right: its first half restates paragraph 3 (defendants who cannot afford counsel are "least able to call the prosecution’s bluff"), but its prescription — barring such defendants from plea agreements — is invented, and paragraph 5 undercuts it by noting few defendants would thank reformers for forcing a trial gamble. Choice C inverts the paragraph’s logic, replacing necessity with enthusiasm. Choice D is unsupported speculation — the passage never discusses what would happen if the trial penalty were eliminated entirely.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 1 says nineteenth-century courts treated plea bargaining as "a sordid practice to be discouraged," not that they approved of it. This inverts the historical claim.`,`Half-right. Paragraph 3 does say such defendants are “least able to call the prosecution’s bluff,” but the passage never argues they should be barred from plea agreements — paragraph 5 says few defendants would thank reformers for forcing them to gamble at trial. The added prescription is unsupported, so this cannot be the answer.`,`Opposite inference. Paragraph 1’s causal story is caseload pressure and systemic necessity ("beyond anything a trial-centered system could absorb"), not enthusiasm for bargaining as an ideal.`,`Unsupported. The passage never speculates about eliminating the trial penalty entirely or about what would make bargaining "indistinguishable from a trial." This invents a hypothetical the text does not explore.`,`Correct. Paragraph 1 attributes acceptance of bargaining to crushing caseloads, and Brady’s "essential component" language signals indispensability — together these support necessity, not admiration, as the driver of legitimation.`],generalLesson:`Strong inferences track the passage’s causal explanations: when a text says X happened because of practical pressure, the inference that X was necessity-driven is licensed, while motives the text never mentions are not.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Why, according to paragraph 1, did courts stop treating bargaining as sordid?`,`Does Brady’s "essential component" language sound like admiration or like necessity?`,`Cross out any choice that gives the Court a motive the passage never mentions.`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p02-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`"plea bargaining serves the interests of everyone involved"`,`"Defendants who plead guilty typically receive lighter sentences than they would risk at trial"`],requiredInference:`A wholly one-sided affirmative case placed immediately before the paragraph-3 critique functions as a steelman: its role is presentational, not conclusive.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B: too-strong/scope-shift - the paragraph argues bargaining serves interests, never "morally superior," and "prove" overstates it. C: scope-shift - the regulatory-failure concession belongs to paragraph 4. D: scope-shift - reform proposals belong to paragraphs 4 and 5. E: too-strong - "typically" does not mean "always."`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited A names the paragraph job from placement and one-sided content (steelman before critique). B upgrades to a moral-superiority verdict the passage never renders; C and D import later-paragraph material into paragraph 2; E turns "typically" into "always." No second defensible answer. Explanations 26-43 words. d3 honest for a neighbor-analysis function item. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`The second paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`present the strongest affirmative case for plea bargaining before turning to criticism`},{text:`prove that plea bargaining is morally superior to the trial system it replaced`},{text:`concede that the critics of plea bargaining are correct about the trial penalty`},{text:`describe reforms that would eliminate prosecutorial discretion over charging decisions`},{text:`argue that defendants who plead guilty are always better off than those who go to trial`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 lays out the defenders’ mutual-advantage case (lighter sentences, resource conservation, speedy resolution) immediately before paragraph 3’s critique. Its structural job is to steelman the affirmative position so the subsequent criticism engages the best version of it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function follows placement. Paragraph 2 sits between the history (paragraph 1) and the critique (paragraph 3), and its content is entirely the pro-bargaining case: defendants get lighter sentences and control over outcomes, prosecutors conserve resources, the public gets swift resolution. The paragraph does not yet evaluate these claims — evaluation arrives in paragraphs 3 and 4. Its role is therefore to present the affirmative case at full strength before subjecting it to criticism, the classic steelman-then-critique structure. Choice B overstates: the paragraph argues bargaining serves interests, not that it is "morally superior" — a comparative moral verdict the passage never renders. Choice C misplaces the concession, which belongs to paragraph 4 (abuses are regulatory failures), not paragraph 2. Choice D describes paragraph 5’s reform proposals, not paragraph 2’s content. Choice E is too-strong: the paragraph says pleading defendants "typically" receive lighter sentences, not that they are "always better off." Only A names the paragraph’s structural job.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The paragraph’s placement and content — the full mutual-advantage case, immediately preceding the critique — show its job is to steelman the affirmative position before criticism begins.`,`Too-strong and a scope-shift. Paragraph 2 argues bargaining serves various interests; it never renders a verdict of moral superiority over trials, and "prove" overstates what the paragraph attempts.`,`Scope-shift. The concession that abuses reflect regulatory failure appears in paragraph 4, not paragraph 2, which contains no critique of bargaining at all. Paragraph 2 is a pure steelman of the affirmative case; importing later paragraphs’ concessions into it misreads the passage’s structure.`,`Scope-shift. Reform proposals, including limits on prosecutorial discretion, belong to paragraphs 4 and 5; paragraph 2 describes the system’s claimed benefits, not its reform. Importing later paragraphs’ reform proposals into paragraph 2 misreads the passage’s structure.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 2 says defendants "typically receive lighter sentences," a hedged empirical claim — not that they are "always better off," which the passage never asserts.`],generalLesson:`A paragraph’s function is revealed by its neighbors: a wholly one-sided case placed just before a critique is almost always a steelman, not a final verdict.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What comes immediately before and after paragraph 2?`,`Does paragraph 2 contain any criticism, or is it entirely one-sided?`,`A one-sided case placed right before an attack is usually there to be attacked at full strength — what is that technique called?`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p02-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`"a rational surrender to coercion"`,`"innocent people do, under pressure, confess to crimes they did not commit"`],viewpointOwner:`Critics of plea bargaining (paragraph 3)`,requiredInference:`The credited reply must match the stated objection of the critics (coerced choice via the trial penalty), not an upgraded or redirected version of it.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A: unsupported - the critics never claim defendants prefer trial uncertainty. B: too-strong/wrong-viewpoint - no abolition demand is attributed to the critics, and the author calls abolition "a fantasy." C: opposite - exoneration studies show wrongly convicted defendants DID plead guilty. E: too-strong - "often underfunded" counsel is a resource claim, not "uniformly incompetent."`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited D restates the core objection of the critics from paragraph 3 (trial penalty makes the choice a "rational surrender to coercion"). A invents a preference; B upgrades the critics to abolitionists; C inverts the exoneration evidence; E turns a resource claim into a competence claim. Strict viewpoint fidelity holds. Explanations 26-35 words. d3 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`Critics of plea bargaining, as presented in the passage, would most likely respond to the claim that bargaining benefits defendants by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`defendants actually prefer the uncertainty of trial to the certainty of a negotiated sentence in the vast majority of cases`},{text:`prosecutors benefit more than defendants do, so the system should be abolished immediately rather than reformed`},{text:`empirical research has proven that no innocent defendant has ever pleaded guilty in any jurisdiction studied to date`},{text:`the gap between plea offers and threatened post-trial sentences can make the defendant’s choice effectively coerced`},{text:`defense attorneys are uniformly too incompetent to advise clients about plea offers and should be replaced by automated advisory systems`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 gives the critics’ core reply: the trial penalty can grow so large that pleading guilty becomes "a rational surrender to coercion" rather than a meaningful choice. Choice D restates that objection; the others exaggerate or invent critics’ claims.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Viewpoint questions demand strict fidelity to the attributed position. Paragraph 3’s critics make two linked points: the trial penalty can render the defendant’s choice effectively unfree ("rational surrender to coercion"), and exoneration evidence shows innocent people do plead guilty under pressure. Choice D faithfully restates the first point as a direct reply to the "benefits defendants" claim. Choice A invents a preference the critics never assert — they argue the choice is coerced, not that defendants prefer trial. Choice B goes too far: the passage’s critics are not quoted demanding abolition, and the author explicitly rejects abolition in paragraph 5. Choice C is the opposite of the cited research, which found that wrongly convicted defendants did plead guilty. Choice E takes the passage’s point about underfunded defense counsel and inflates it into "uniformly incompetent," a too-strong distortion. The discipline here is to match the choice to the critics’ actual words — coercion via the trial penalty — without upgrading their claims.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The critics argue the defendant’s choice is coerced by the trial penalty, not that defendants prefer trial’s uncertainty. This invents a preference the passage never attributes to anyone.`,`Too-strong and wrong-viewpoint. The passage never has critics demanding abolition, and the author explicitly calls abolition "a fantasy." This upgrades the critics’ regulatory critique into an abolitionist one.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 3 cites exoneration studies showing that wrongly convicted defendants — including some cleared by DNA — did plead guilty. "No innocent defendant has ever pleaded guilty" inverts the evidence.`,`Correct. It restates paragraph 3’s central objection: when the trial penalty grows large enough, the "choice" to plead guilty becomes "a rational surrender to coercion," unmasking the claimed benefit as a product of power imbalance.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 3 says defense counsel is "often underfunded," a resource claim — not that attorneys are "uniformly incompetent," a competence claim the passage never makes.`],generalLesson:`When answering for a quoted viewpoint, match the choice to that viewpoint’s actual stated objection; resist choices that upgrade, invert, or redirect it.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the critics’ single biggest objection, in the passage’s own words?`,`Do the critics say defendants prefer trials, or something about the freedom of the choice itself?`,`Watch for choices that make the critics more extreme than the passage does.`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p02-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,abstraction:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4,5],supportingSentences:[`"capping the differential between plea offers and post-trial sentences"`,`"which safeguards can preserve the efficiency of bargaining while curbing its coercive edge?"`],requiredInference:`Apply the prescriptive conclusion (regulate, do not abolish) to the new law: a cap is exactly the regulatory measure paragraph 5 lists, so the author would endorse it.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A: too-strong - a cap regulates, it does not abolish; the "fantasy" verdict attaches to abolition. C: opposite - paragraph 4 criticizes discretion as "nearly unchecked." D: scope-shift - the passage is present-tense and prescriptive, not history-only. E: too-strong - the passage lists several safeguards and proves none exclusively necessary.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISED: editorialDifficulty 4->3 (inflated: the law is named verbatim in paragraph 5 and the regulate-not-abolish conclusion is explicit, so inferential distance and distractor proximity support d3, not d4); version bumped to 2, revisedAt set. Hostile review: credited B matches the listed reform and the author prescriptive conclusion. A is too-strong (a cap is not abolition); C is opposite (unchecked discretion is criticized); D is scope-shift; E overstates exclusivity. Explanations 26-46 words. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p02`,stem:`A state enacts a law capping the difference between a prosecutor’s plea offer and the sentence the defendant would face after conviction at trial. Based on the passage, the author would most likely view this law as`,choices:[{text:`tantamount to abolishing plea bargaining, and therefore doomed to fail`},{text:`a promising instance of the passage's regulatory approach, curbing the trial penalty's coercive edge without ending bargaining`},{text:`misguided, because the passage argues that prosecutorial discretion should remain entirely unchecked`},{text:`irrelevant to the passage’s concerns, which are limited to the historical origins of plea bargaining`},{text:`insufficient on its own, because the passage proves that only judicial participation in negotiations can prevent coercion`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 explicitly lists "capping the differential between plea offers and post-trial sentences" among the live reform proposals, and the passage’s conclusion favors regulating bargaining’s coercive edge rather than abolishing it. The law is exactly the kind of measure the author endorses.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions require running the passage’s framework on new facts. The framework here: paragraph 4 reframes abuses as regulatory failures ("the plea market is among the least regulated in American law"), and paragraph 5 names "capping the differential between plea offers and post-trial sentences" as one of the proposals defining the live debate, concluding that the question is "how, given that it will [exist], the law should keep it honest." A cap on the plea/trial differential directly targets the trial penalty — paragraph 3’s central mechanism of coercion — while preserving bargaining itself. That is precisely the regulate-don’t-abolish approach the author favors. Choice A is too-strong: a cap is not abolition, and the passage’s pessimism attaches to abolition, not regulation. Choice C is the opposite of paragraph 4, which criticizes "nearly unchecked" prosecutorial discretion. Choice D is a scope-shift: the passage’s concerns are plainly present-tense and prescriptive. Choice E overstates — the passage lists judicial oversight as one option among several and "proves" nothing about exclusivity.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. A cap on the differential regulates bargaining; it does not abolish it, and the passage’s "fantasy" verdict attaches to abolition, not to measures like this one — which paragraph 5 explicitly lists as a live proposal.`,`Correct. The law matches paragraph 5’s named reform (capping the plea/trial differential) and embodies the passage’s conclusion: curb the coercive edge through regulation rather than abolish the system.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 4 criticizes prosecutorial discretion as "nearly unchecked" and presents oversight as part of the solution; the passage nowhere defends unchecked discretion. The phrase “nearly unchecked” is a criticism, not an endorsement — a common trap is mistaking a problem description for the author’s prescription.`,`Scope-shift. The passage’s historical first paragraph serves a present-tense argument culminating in reform proposals; its concerns are not "limited to" history. Limiting words like “limited to” or “only” in a choice should always be checked against the passage’s later, prescriptive paragraphs.`,`Too-strong. The passage lists several possible safeguards (discovery, judicial oversight, caps) without proving any single one exclusively necessary; "proves that only" goes well beyond the text.`],generalLesson:`To apply a passage, identify its prescriptive conclusion first, then test each new proposal against that conclusion — not against the problem description alone.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does paragraph 5 mention anything resembling this law?`,`Is the author for abolition or for regulation? Which category does a cap fall into?`,`The strongest wrong answers exaggerate — check every absolute word against the text.`],labels:{topic:`plea bargaining`}},{id:`rc-p03-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`Justifications presented (paragraph 2), moral critique aired (paragraph 3), rebutted (paragraph 4), concluding with reform-not-repeal (paragraph 5).`],requiredInference:`Synthesize the five-paragraph movement into the qualified verdict: the doctrine has real functions, modern conditions weaken some justifications, and the warranted conclusion is targeted reform, not repeal.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A states the critics’ abolitionist position as the author’s (opposite). C inflates the history into "always favored trespassers" (too-strong scope-shift). D overstates the critique to "every justification" although paragraph 4 preserves the evidentiary rationale. E limits the rationale to punishing neglect with "solely" and "illusory," contradicting paragraph 2’s substantive economic and evidentiary case.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: B alone survives the final-paragraph verdict; A opposite, C/D too-strong, E half-right. All quotes verified against passage text; length ratio 1.07, gap 11 chars; choiceExplanations >=25 words. One defensible answer, no leakage, honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Adverse possession is a morally indefensible doctrine that rewards theft and should be abolished outright.`},{text:`Adverse possession serves real economic and evidentiary functions; though modern conditions weaken some justifications, the answer is targeted reform, not repeal.`},{text:`The history of adverse possession from Roman usucapio to the American frontier shows that property law has always favored trespassers over true owners.`},{text:`Modern recording systems have eliminated every justification for adverse possession, leaving it without any defensible purpose.`},{text:`Adverse possession is justified solely because it punishes negligent owners, and its economic benefits are illusory.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage presents the doctrine’s justifications (paragraph 2), airs the moral critique (paragraph 3), rebuts it (paragraph 4), and concludes with reform-not-repeal (paragraph 5). Only choice B captures that qualified defense across the whole passage.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The defensible main point is the qualified defense: the doctrine has real economic and evidentiary functions, the moral critique has force under modern conditions, and the warranted conclusion is narrowing reform, not abolition. That is choice B. Choice A states the critics’ position as the author’s — the opposite of the conclusion. Choice C takes paragraph 1’s history and inflates it into a sweeping, cynical thesis ("always favored trespassers") the passage never asserts. Choice D overstates the paragraph-3 critique: the passage says the frontier rationale "has expired" and justifications have weakened, but paragraph 4 insists the evidentiary rationale "has not vanished." Choice E is half-right — penalizing neglect is part of the rationale — but "solely" and "illusory" contradict paragraph 2’s substantive economic and evidentiary case. A main-point answer must survive the final paragraph, and only B does.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. This is the moral critics’ position from paragraph 3, stated as if it were the author’s. The author’s conclusion explicitly favors reform over repeal, so an abolitionist main point inverts the passage.`,`Correct. It holds together the passage’s full movement: the genuine functions (paragraph 2), the weakened-but-not-dead justifications under modern conditions (paragraphs 3–4), and the reform-not-repeal verdict (paragraph 5).`,`Too-strong and a scope-shift. Paragraph 1 traces the doctrine’s lineage without claiming property law "always favored trespassers over true owners" — a cynical generalization the passage never makes.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 3 argues the frontier rationale has expired, but paragraph 4 explicitly defends the continuing evidentiary rationale (old surveys, vanished landmarks). "Every justification" and "without any defensible purpose" go beyond the text.`,`Half-right but distorted: penalizing neglect is one rationale, yet the passage also defends real economic and evidentiary functions, so "solely" and "illusory" each contradict paragraph 2.`],generalLesson:`When a passage ends with a qualified verdict (reform, not repeal), any main-point choice that drops the qualification — in either direction — is wrong.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the final paragraph actually recommend — abolition or something milder?`,`Does the author think the doctrine has any legitimate functions? Which paragraph says so?`,`Eliminate choices that sound like only the critics or only the history section.`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p03-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`the successful claimant is typically not a calculating invader but a neighbor who fenced, farmed, or built in the honest belief the land was theirs`],whyDistractorsFail:`A describes the critics’ opportunism case from paragraph 3, explicitly contrasted as not typical (opposite). B inverts paragraph 5, which notes government-owned parcels are exempted. C invents an unmentioned figure (unsupported). E contradicts the "hostile to the true owner’s rights" requirement: paying rent acknowledges the owner’s title.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: D is a near-verbatim paraphrase of the paragraph-4 sentence; every distractor fails for a nameable reason. Quoted passage sentence verified verbatim; length ratio 1.03, gap 3 chars; choiceExplanations >=25 words. One defensible answer, honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`According to the passage, the typical successful adverse possession claimant is`,choices:[{text:`a speculator who deliberately occupies unused land in hopes of eventually claiming title`},{text:`a government agency seeking to quiet title to public lands`},{text:`a corporation that purchases tax-delinquent parcels at auction`},{text:`a neighbor who fenced, farmed, or built on the land in the honest belief that it was theirs`},{text:`a tenant whose written lease has expired but who continues to pay rent`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 states that the doctrine’s requirements mean "the successful claimant is typically not a calculating invader but a neighbor who fenced, farmed, or built in the honest belief the land was theirs." Choice D restates this; the others describe figures the passage distinguishes from the typical claimant.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The sentence to find is in paragraph 4: after listing the hedging requirements (open and notorious possession, continuity, hostility, and in many jurisdictions tax payment or good-faith claim of title), the author concludes that "the successful claimant is typically not a calculating invader but a neighbor who fenced, farmed, or built in the honest belief the land was theirs." Choice D is a direct paraphrase. Choice A describes the "deliberate occupation" opportunist of paragraph 3 — the critics’ bogeyman, explicitly contrasted with the typical claimant. Choice B inverts paragraph 5, which notes that some states exempt government-owned parcels from the doctrine. Choice C invents a purchaser at a tax auction, a figure the passage never mentions. Choice E describes a holdover tenant paying rent — but rent payment acknowledges the owner’s title, contradicting the "hostile to the true owner’s rights" requirement. Detail questions reward the reader who matches the choice to the sentence rather than to a general impression.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 3 presents the deliberate, opportunistic occupier as the critics’ worrying case; paragraph 4 explicitly says the typical successful claimant is "not a calculating invader." This choice describes the exception, not the rule.`,`Unsupported and inverted: paragraph 5 notes that reforming states have exempted government-owned parcels from the doctrine’s reach, so a government agency is not the typical claimant.`,`Unsupported. The passage never mentions corporations buying tax-delinquent parcels at auction; this invents a claimant type with no textual basis. Invented specifics — auctions, corporations, dollar amounts — are classic unsupported distractors; the passage’s typical claimant is an ordinary neighbor.`,`Correct. This paraphrases paragraph 4’s conclusion nearly verbatim: the typical claimant is the neighbor who "fenced, farmed, or built in the honest belief the land was theirs."`,`Opposite. Contradicting the doctrine’s requirements. A tenant who keeps paying rent acknowledges the owner’s title, which conflicts with the "hostile to the true owner’s rights" element described in paragraph 4.`],generalLesson:`When a passage explicitly contrasts the typical case with a vivid atypical one, expect distractors built from the atypical case — anchor your answer to the word "typically."`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`keyword-match`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 4 ends with a sentence about who the successful claimant "typically" is — find it.`,`Which choices describe the critics’ scary hypothetical rather than the typical case?`,`The right answer will mention honest belief, not calculation.`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p03-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3,4],supportingSentences:[`the deliberate occupation of a neighbor’s unused strip of land in hopes of eventually claiming it`,`the evidentiary rationale, defenders insist, has not vanished`],requiredInference:`The passage’s contrast structure licenses the judgment: the moral critique bites hardest where the defense is weakest — calculating opportunism — and goes slack on good-faith mistakes and stale boundary disputes, which paragraph 4 defends.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and C name the defense’s best cases (surviving evidentiary rationale, good-faith mistakes) where the critique is weakest, not strongest. B fails the doctrine’s threshold requirements before moral questions arise. D names exempted land the doctrine does not reach, so "when applied" is a non sequitur.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: E is licensed by the paragraphs 3–4 contrast; no defensible reading supports another choice. All quoted phrases verified against passage text; length ratio 1.09, gap 6 chars; choiceExplanations >=25 words. One defensible answer, honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`The passage most strongly suggests that the moral critique of adverse possession is at its strongest when the doctrine is applied to`,choices:[{text:`long-settled boundary disputes turning on decades-old surveys`},{text:`situations in which the original owner promptly asserted their rights`},{text:`cases in which the claimant acted in good faith under color of title`},{text:`government-owned parcels that states have exempted from the doctrine`},{text:`deliberate occupation of a neighbor's unused strip in hopes of claiming it`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 locates the critique’s force in the "deliberate occupation of a neighbor’s unused strip of land in hopes of eventually claiming it," while paragraph 4 defuses the critique for good-faith boundary mistakes and stale disputes. The critique bites hardest on opportunism, not on the cases the defense covers.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This inference is licensed by the passage’s own contrast structure. Paragraph 3 builds the moral critique around a specific application: "the deliberate occupation of a neighbor’s unused strip of land in hopes of eventually claiming it," arguing that when evidentiary and economic justifications weaken, "what remains is a rule that teaches citizens that persistent boundary-pushing pays." Paragraph 4 then blunts the critique by arguing the typical case is different — good-faith mistakes hedged by strict requirements — and that the evidentiary rationale survives for genuine boundary disputes over "decades-old surveys and vanished landmarks." Put together, the passage implies the critique’s strength varies with the facts: strongest against calculating opportunism, weakest against good-faith mistakes and stale claims. Choice E names the opportunism case. Choice A names a case where paragraph 4 says the evidentiary rationale still holds — the critique is weakest there. Choice C names the good-faith case the defense covers. Choice D names exempted land, where the doctrine doesn’t apply at all. Choice B describes a situation where the doctrine’s requirements (continuous, unchallenged possession) wouldn’t even be met.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 4 defends the doctrine precisely for such disputes, where the evidentiary rationale (old surveys, vanished landmarks) still holds — this is where the moral critique is weakest, not strongest.`,`Unsupported. If the owner promptly asserted their rights, possession would not be continuous and unchallenged for the statutory period — the doctrine’s threshold requirements would fail before moral questions arise.`,`Opposite. Good-faith occupation under color of title is the sympathetic case paragraph 4 uses to defend the doctrine; the critique loses force here. This is the passage’s contrast structure at work: the critique bites on opportunism and goes slack on good-faith mistakes, so each choice must be sorted to the right side.`,`Unsupported. Exempted government parcels are ones the doctrine does not reach at all (paragraph 5), so the critique’s strength "when applied" to them is a non sequitur.`,`Correct. Paragraph 3 anchors the moral critique in exactly this scenario — deliberate occupation "in hopes of eventually claiming it" — arguing it teaches that "persistent boundary-pushing pays."`],generalLesson:`When a passage contrasts a critique’s best case with a defense’s best case, the inference "the critique is strongest where the defense is weakest" is usually licensed — map each choice to the right side of the contrast.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which specific scenario does paragraph 3 use to make the moral critique sting?`,`Which scenarios does paragraph 4 use to defend the doctrine?`,`The critique is strongest where the defense’s arguments run out — which choice is that?`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p03-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`Yet adverse possession is among the oldest principles in Western law`,`Roman jurists recognized usucapio, by which long possession ripened into ownership`],requiredInference:`Identify the rhetorical job of the historical reference in context: anchoring the antiquity claim so the doctrine reads as venerable rather than a recent aberration, before the paragraph-2 justifications arrive.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A invents a moral comparison between legal systems the passage never makes. C upgrades lineage into interpretive authority (too-strong). D misattributes the paragraph-2 economic rationale to Roman jurists. E inverts the reference’s legitimizing effect into a concession to the critics (opposite).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: B alone names the actual framing work; C, D, and E each assert claims the paragraph never makes. Quoted phrases verified against passage text; length ratio 1.19, gap 17 chars; choiceExplanations >=25 words. One defensible answer, honest d3. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`The author’s reference to Roman usucapio in the first paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`prove that Roman property law was morally superior to its modern counterparts`},{text:`establish that adverse possession is an ancient, historically rooted doctrine rather than a recent anomaly`},{text:`argue that the doctrine should be interpreted exactly as the Roman jurists interpreted it`},{text:`suggest that the economic justifications for the doctrine originated in ancient Rome`},{text:`concede that the doctrine’s critics have history on their side`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The first paragraph introduces the doctrine as "among the oldest principles in Western law" and traces it through Rome, early-modern England, and the American frontier. The usucapio reference anchors that antiquity claim, rebutting the impression that the rule is some modern aberration.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask what job a detail performs in its context. Paragraph 1’s job is to introduce a "paradoxical" doctrine and immediately complicate the reader’s likely reaction — that rewarding trespassers must be some strange modern invention — by showing its deep lineage: "Roman jurists recognized usucapio," then English limitation statutes, then American adoption. The reference therefore functions to establish antiquity and historical rootedness, which subtly legitimizes the doctrine before the justifications arrive in paragraph 2. Choice A is a scope-shift: no moral comparison of legal systems is made. Choice C is too-strong: the passage never argues for Roman-style interpretation. Choice D misattributes — the economic justifications are developed in paragraph 2 as "traditional justifications," not traced to Rome. Choice E is the opposite of the reference’s effect: antiquity supports the doctrine’s defenders, not its critics. Only B names the actual rhetorical work the historical reference performs.`,choiceExplanations:[`Scope-shift. The passage makes no moral comparison between Roman and modern property law; usucapio is invoked for lineage, not for a verdict on which system was superior.`,`Correct. The reference anchors paragraph 1’s claim that adverse possession is "among the oldest principles in Western law," countering the impression that it is a recent or anomalous invention.`,`Too-strong. The passage never argues the doctrine should be interpreted as Roman jurists interpreted it; the history establishes roots, not interpretive authority. A too-strong choice upgrades “ancient lineage” into “binding interpretive authority,” a leap the passage never makes.`,`Scope-shift. The economic and evidentiary justifications are presented in paragraph 2 as the doctrine’s traditional rationale, not as ideas originating with Roman jurists. The justifications are called “traditional” and developed in paragraph 2; nothing traces their origin to Rome specifically.`,`Opposite. The antiquity of the doctrine supports its defenders (it is venerable, not aberrant); nothing in the reference concedes ground to the critics. Antiquity here does legitimizing work for the doctrine’s defenders — it makes the rule look venerable rather than aberrant.`],generalLesson:`Historical references in introductions usually do legitimizing or framing work — ask what impression the author is trying to prevent, not just what fact is stated.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What claim does the usucapio sentence support? Read the sentence just before it.`,`Is the author trying to make the doctrine look stranger or more venerable?`,`The right answer describes framing work, not a new argument.`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p03-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-attitude`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,abstraction:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3,5],supportingSentences:[`The most plausible resolution, and the direction several states have already taken, is reform rather than repeal`,`preserve what is valuable in adverse possession`],viewpointOwner:`the author`,requiredInference:`An author who wants to preserve a doctrine’s valuable core in narrowed form is unsympathetic to abolishing it outright; the verdict is explicit, not inferred.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A inverts the verdict into abolition (opposite). B mistakes evenhanded presentation for neutrality in an evaluative passage (supported-nonresponsive). D invents expansionism, the opposite direction of the narrowing reforms. E overstates: paragraph 3 gives the moral critique a respectful hearing rather than dismissing it.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: C follows directly from the explicit final-paragraph verdict; each distractor fails for a nameable reason. Quoted phrases verified against passage text; credited choice is shorter than the longest distractor (ratio 0.98). One defensible answer, honest d2. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward proposals to abolish adverse possession entirely is best described as`,choices:[{text:`strongly supportive, since the author finds the moral critique decisive`},{text:`neutral, since the author presents only historical facts without evaluation`},{text:`unsympathetic, since the author favors preserving the doctrine in narrowed form`},{text:`enthusiastically in favor of expanding the doctrine to new categories of property`},{text:`dismissive of all criticism as motivated by misunderstanding of property law`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph endorses "reform rather than repeal" — lengthening periods, requiring good faith, exempting certain land — while "preserv[ing] what is valuable." That is opposition to abolition, not neutrality, expansionism, or blanket dismissal of critics.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Attitude questions turn on the author’s verdict. Here the verdict is explicit in paragraph 5: "The most plausible resolution ... is reform rather than repeal," with measures that "preserve what is valuable in adverse possession ... while blunting the charge that it licenses land theft." An author who wants to preserve a doctrine’s valuable core is unsympathetic to abolishing it. Choice A inverts the verdict — the author finds the critique forceful but not decisive. Choice B mistakes evenhanded presentation for neutrality; the passage plainly argues for a conclusion. Choice D invents expansionism; the author favors narrowing, the opposite direction. Choice E overstates: the author takes the moral critique seriously (paragraph 3 gets a full, respectful hearing) rather than dismissing all criticism. Choice C is the only answer consistent with "reform rather than repeal."`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The author gives the moral critique a fair hearing but concludes it warrants narrowing, not abolition — "reform rather than repeal." Support for abolition inverts the verdict.`,`Supported-nonresponsive. The passage is evaluative throughout (justifications weighed, critique answered, reform endorsed); "only historical facts" describes at most part of paragraph 1. Calling an evaluative passage “neutral” because it presents both sides is a supported-nonresponsive trap: evenhanded presentation still serves an argument.`,`Correct. The author explicitly favors preserving the doctrine’s valuable core in narrowed form, which is incompatible with abolition. “Reform rather than repeal” is the author’s explicit verdict, so any abolitionist reading directly contradicts the conclusion.`,`Opposite direction. The author favors pruning the doctrine (longer periods, good-faith requirements, exemptions), not expanding it to new categories. The reform direction is consistently narrowing — longer periods, good-faith requirements, exemptions — the opposite of expansion.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 3 presents the moral critique respectfully and concedes its force under modern conditions; the author answers the criticism rather than dismissing it as mere misunderstanding.`],generalLesson:`An author who respectfully hears criticism but concludes "reform rather than repeal" is an ally of the qualified position — don’t confuse engagement with critics for agreement with them.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-attitude`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two words in the final paragraph state the author’s verdict on abolition?`,`Does the author want more doctrine or less doctrine?`,`An author who says "preserve what is valuable" is not neutral — which direction does that point?`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p03-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-organization`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`definition plus history (paragraph 1); economic and evidentiary justifications (paragraph 2); moral critique (paragraph 3); defenders’ rebuttal (paragraph 4); reform-not-repeal conclusion (paragraph 5)`],requiredInference:`Match each clause of the abstract skeleton to the paragraphs in order; only the credited choice tracks the actual five-move sequence one-to-one.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B invents rival theories tested by empirical evidence. C invents a Supreme Court case analysis with dissenting opinions. D posits competing solutions tested against evidence; its "paradox" wording is only keyword-match from paragraph 1. E invents archival revision of a disputed chronology.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISED then validated: downgraded editorialDifficulty 4→3 (version 2, revisedAt 2026-09-11) — the difficulty was inflated on type reputation. A clean five-paragraph definition–justification–critique–rebuttal–conclusion skeleton with blatant genre distractors rates d3, not d4. Item otherwise sound: credited choice tracks the paragraphs in order; all quotes verified; length ratio 0.99. VALIDATE.`}],revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p03`,stem:`Which one of the following best describes the organization of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`A doctrine is defined and its history traced; traditional justifications are presented; a moral critique is aired and answered; a reformist conclusion is drawn.`},{text:`Two rival theories are compared; empirical evidence is shown to favor one; the losing theory is refuted; policy implications are derived.`},{text:`A legal controversy is described; the Supreme Court’s resolution is analyzed; dissenting opinions are evaluated; a prediction about future cases is offered.`},{text:`A paradox is posed; three competing solutions are tested against the evidence; the least implausible solution is endorsed with reservations.`},{text:`A historical narrative is recounted; its accuracy is challenged by new archival evidence; a revised chronology is proposed.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 defines the doctrine and sketches its history; paragraph 2 gives the economic and evidentiary justifications; paragraph 3 airs the moral critique; paragraph 4 answers it; paragraph 5 concludes with reform. Choice A tracks this sequence exactly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Organization questions require matching an abstract skeleton to the passage’s actual paragraph-level moves. Map them: paragraph 1 defines adverse possession and traces it from usucapio through English statutes to the American frontier; paragraph 2 presents the traditional justifications (economic productivity, evidentiary staleness); paragraph 3 airs the moral critique (rewards trespass, frontier rationale expired); paragraph 4 answers the critique (hedging requirements, typical good-faith claimant, surviving evidentiary rationale); paragraph 5 draws the reformist conclusion ("reform rather than repeal"). Choice A reproduces this six-step sequence. Choice B invents rival theories and empirical testing — there is no experiment here. Choice C invents a Supreme Court case analysis; no case is discussed. Choice D’s "three competing solutions tested" fits a science passage, not this one. Choice E’s "archival challenge to chronology" describes nothing in the text. The discipline is to verify each element of the skeleton against the paragraphs in order.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Each clause matches a paragraph in order: definition-plus-history (1), justifications (2), critique (3), rebuttal (4), reformist conclusion (5). On organization questions, the correct skeleton’s clauses should match the paragraphs one-to-one and in order, as this one does.`,`Unsupported. The passage contains no rival theories tested by empirical evidence and no refutation of a losing theory; its structure is justification-critique-rebuttal, not hypothesis testing.`,`Unsupported. No Supreme Court decision, dissenting opinion, or prediction about future cases appears anywhere in the passage. This skeleton describes a Supreme Court case note, a genre with no resemblance to this justification-critique-rebuttal passage.`,`Scope-shift. While paragraph 1 calls the doctrine "paradoxical," the passage does not test competing solutions against evidence; its middle is a normative debate, not a scientific one.`,`Unsupported. No archival evidence challenges any chronology here; the history in paragraph 1 is scene-setting, not a thesis under revision. Paragraph 1’s history is scene-setting background; nothing in the passage treats the chronology itself as disputed.`],generalLesson:`For organization questions, write a one-phrase job description for each paragraph first, then find the answer whose skeleton matches your list in order.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-organization`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Give each paragraph a three-word job description before looking at the choices.`,`Which choices mention things (court cases, experiments, archives) that never appear?`,`The right skeleton will have six parts in order — count them.`],labels:{topic:`adverse possession`}},{id:`rc-p04-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`"refuse to resolve into allegory"`,`"hold the descriptive and the suggestive in balance"`],requiredInference:`The main point is the author’s chastened middle position, not either camp’s thesis: synthesize the dispute (P2 vs P3), the inconclusive evidence (P4), and the qualified verdict (P5) into one claim.`,viewpointOwner:`the passage author, mediating between the moralizing and skeptical camps`,whyDistractorsFail:`A adopts the skeptical extreme the author explicitly moves beyond in P5; B overstates archival evidence that paragraph 4 says "cuts both ways"; C contradicts the passage’s debate structure by claiming unanimity; E invents a comparative judgment of Vermeer’s contemporaries that appears nowhere.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: no defensible distractor (A extreme rejected by P5, B refuted by "cuts both ways" in P4, C contradicted by debate structure, E invented). Credited choice 174 chars vs longest distractor 184 chars — no length leakage. All quoted claims verified against passage. d2 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p04`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Vermeer’s paintings are purely technical achievements with no meaningful connection to the moral culture of seventeenth-century Delft.`},{text:`The moralizing interpretation of Vermeer has been definitively refuted by archival research into Delft household inventories, closing the debate for good, though a few holdouts remain.`},{text:`Art historians unanimously agree that Vermeer’s interiors are vanitas meditations on virtue, temperance, and domestic order.`},{text:`Scholars dispute whether Vermeer’s interiors encode moral allegories; the best view credits possible moral overtones while holding description and suggestion in balance.`},{text:`Vermeer’s contemporaries were superior moralists, and his work suffers by comparison with their explicitly allegorical paintings of virtue.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The passage presents the moralizing reading (paragraph 2), the skeptical rebuttal (paragraph 3), the inconclusive evidence (paragraph 4), and a chastened middle position (paragraph 5). Only choice D captures both the dispute and the author’s qualified resolution.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A main-point answer must reflect the passage’s debate structure and its conclusion. Paragraphs 2 and 3 stage the dispute: moralizers read emblems and vanitas meanings; skeptics call the method unfalsifiable and point to shared studio props. Paragraph 4 reports evidence cutting both ways. Paragraph 5 settles on a middle position: Vermeer may have expected viewers to catch moral overtones, but the paintings "refuse to resolve into allegory," and their greatness depends on that refusal. Choice D is the only answer containing both halves — the dispute and the balanced verdict. Choice A endorses the skeptical extreme the author explicitly moves beyond. Choice B overstates: the archival research "cuts both ways," refuting nothing definitively. Choice C is the opposite of the passage, which is built around disagreement. Choice E invents a comparative judgment about contemporaries that appears nowhere.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite of the author’s conclusion. The passage’s middle position allows that Vermeer "may have expected some viewers to catch moral overtones"; "purely technical" and "no meaningful connection" adopt an extreme the author rejects.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 4 explicitly says the archival findings "cut both ways, since a symbol must be familiar to be legible" — they complicate rather than definitively refute the moralizing reading.`,`Opposite. The passage is organized around scholarly disagreement; "unanimously agree" contradicts its entire structure. Any choice claiming unanimity fails instantly against a passage built as a debate between two camps.`,`Correct. It names the dispute (moral allegory vs. resistance to allegory) and the author’s qualified resolution: possible moral overtones held in balance with descriptive fidelity.`,`Unsupported. The passage mentions Delft contemporaries only to note they used the same props; it never compares their moral seriousness to Vermeer’s or judges his work inferior.`],generalLesson:`When a passage stages a debate and then takes a middle position, the main point must contain three elements: side A, side B, and the qualification — any answer missing one is incomplete.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author fully accept either side, or land somewhere between them?`,`What does the final paragraph say the paintings’ greatness depends on?`,`Eliminate choices that describe only one side of the debate.`],labels:{topic:`Vermeer interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p04-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`"the method becomes unfalsifiable"`,`"depending on what the thesis requires"`],requiredInference:`From "unfalsifiable" to its methodological implication: a method that can supply whatever meaning the thesis requires is insulated from counterevidence and is therefore suspect — a claim about the method’s structure, not about viewers, intentions, or emblem books.`,viewpointOwner:`the passage’s skeptics of the moralizing interpretation`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is explicitly disavowed ("do not deny that seventeenth-century viewers knew their emblems"); B is stronger than the skeptics’ denial that Vermeer "necessarily deployed" emblems; C is contradicted by paragraph 4, where both camps use archival research; D questions emblem-book popularity, which the skeptics never dispute.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited E is the passage’s unfalsifiability charge restated; every distractor fails on the passage’s own terms (A disavowed, B too-strong, C contradicted by P4, D unsupported scope shift). Credited choice 128 chars vs longest distractor 113 chars — within ratio and gap limits. d3 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p04`,stem:`The skeptics’ methodological objection to the moralizing interpretation implies that`,choices:[{text:`seventeenth-century viewers were generally incapable of understanding visual symbolism in paintings of the period`},{text:`Vermeer deliberately avoided all moral content in his paintings as a consistent matter of artistic principle`},{text:`archival research into household inventories is largely irrelevant to how art history is currently practiced`},{text:`emblem books were considerably less popular in the Dutch Republic than scholars have traditionally assumed`},{text:`an interpretive method able to assign any meaning an object needs for the thesis cannot be proven wrong and is therefore suspect`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 states the objection: once every object is assumed symbolic, "the method becomes unfalsifiable" — a map or pearl can mean whatever the thesis requires. Choice E restates that unfalsifiability charge; the others attribute claims about viewers, Vermeer’s intentions, archives, or emblem books that the skeptics never make.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference must stay inside the objection’s logic. The skeptics’ point is methodological, not historical: they "do not deny that seventeenth-century viewers knew their emblems" — which eliminates choice A immediately. Their complaint is that the moralizing method is "unfalsifiable": because a map "can mean worldly ambition or honest cartography ... depending on what the thesis requires," no evidence could ever count against a moralizing reading. Choice E draws exactly that implication: unfalsifiability makes the method suspect. Choice B goes beyond the skeptics, who deny that Vermeer "necessarily deployed" emblems, not that he avoided all moral content. Choice C contradicts paragraph 4, where both sides use archival research. Choice D is unsupported — the skeptics never dispute the popularity of emblem books. The key move is recognizing that the objection targets the method’s structure (it can’t lose), not any particular factual claim.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The skeptics explicitly "do not deny that seventeenth-century viewers knew their emblems"; their objection is to the interpretive method, not to viewers’ competence. This is a wrong-viewpoint trap: it attacks the skeptics’ imagined factual claim instead of their actual methodological one.`,`Too-strong. The skeptics deny that Vermeer "necessarily deployed" emblems — a claim about what can be proven — not that he deliberately avoided all moral content, a stronger claim about intention.`,`Unsupported and contradicted: paragraph 4 shows both camps using archival inventory research, so the skeptics cannot regard it as irrelevant. Both camps’ use of inventory research in paragraph 4 directly contradicts any claim that the skeptics dismiss archives.`,`Unsupported. The skeptics never question how popular emblem books were; they question whether Vermeer used that visual vocabulary as the moralizers claim. The skeptics’ target is Vermeer’s use of the vocabulary, not the vocabulary’s popularity — a scope distinction the trap ignores.`,`Correct. This restates the unfalsifiability objection: a method that yields whatever meaning "the thesis requires" is insulated from counterevidence and therefore methodologically suspect. Recognizing unfalsifiability as a methodological flaw, rather than a factual dispute, is the key inference this question tests.`],generalLesson:`A methodological objection targets how a conclusion is reached, not the conclusion’s subject matter — keep the inference at the level of the method.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What word does the passage use to name the problem with the moralizers’ method?`,`Do the skeptics dispute that viewers knew emblems, or something else?`,`An objection that a method "becomes unfalsifiable" implies what about the method?`],labels:{topic:`Vermeer interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p04-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`"may reflect the standard props of a shared studio practice rather than a coded moral program"`],viewpointOwner:`the passage’s skeptics of the moralizing interpretation`,whyDistractorsFail:`B invents an originator claim about the emblem tradition that the passage never makes; C introduces a technical-skill comparison absent from the text; D is too-strong, since paragraph 4 credits both sides with "telling evidence"; E inverts paragraph 4, which establishes the objects were "genuinely common possessions," i.e., familiar.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: the passage explicitly draws the credited inference inside paragraph 3, so the function answer is that inference; all distractors add claims the text never makes or invert it. Credited choice 120 chars vs longest distractor 101 chars — within limits. d3 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p04`,stem:`The author mentions that many of the objects in Vermeer’s paintings also appear in the work of his less renowned Delft contemporaries primarily in order to`,choices:[{text:`support the skeptics’ suggestion that the objects may reflect shared studio props rather than a coded moral program`},{text:`prove that Vermeer’s contemporaries were the true originators of the moralizing emblem tradition`},{text:`argue that Vermeer’s technical skill was inferior to that of his Delft contemporaries`},{text:`concede that the moralizing interpretation has no evidence whatsoever in its favor`},{text:`illustrate the claim that seventeenth-century viewers were unfamiliar with everyday domestic objects`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The detail appears in paragraph 3 as the skeptics’ evidence: if the same objects appear in undistinguished contemporaries’ work that "no one proposes to read as theology," the objects likely reflect "the standard props of a shared studio practice." Choice A states that function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Locate the detail’s argumentative context: it sits inside paragraph 3, the skeptics’ case, immediately after the unfalsifiability objection. The sentence’s logic is comparative — the same objects appear in painters "of modest reputation whose pictures no one proposes to read as theology" — and the sentence draws the inference explicitly: the objects "may reflect the standard props of a shared studio practice rather than a coded moral program." Choice A restates that function. Choice B invents an originator claim about the emblem tradition that the passage never makes. Choice C introduces a technical-skill comparison absent from the text. Choice D is too-strong: the skeptics’ point is that one line of evidence is ambiguous, not that the moralizers have "no evidence whatsoever." Choice E inverts the passage’s assumption — paragraph 4 notes these were "genuinely common possessions," i.e., familiar, not unfamiliar. Function answers must match the inference the passage itself draws from the detail.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The passage draws this inference explicitly: the shared objects suggest "the standard props of a shared studio practice rather than a coded moral program," supporting the skeptical case.`,`Unsupported. The passage never claims Vermeer’s contemporaries originated the emblem tradition; they are mentioned only as users of the same pictorial props. The contemporaries appear only as a comparison class for the props; no origin story about the emblem tradition is told.`,`Unsupported scope-shift. No comparison of technical skill between Vermeer and his contemporaries appears anywhere in the passage. Technical-skill comparisons never occur in the passage, making this a pure invention distractor with no textual anchor.`,`Too-strong. The skeptics argue one kind of evidence is ambiguous, not that the moralizing reading has no evidence at all — paragraph 4 shows both sides with "telling evidence."`,`Opposite. Paragraph 4 establishes that the objects were "genuinely common possessions," hence familiar to viewers — the passage assumes familiarity, not unfamiliarity. Familiarity is actually required for the moralizers’ case, since paragraph 4 notes a symbol must be familiar to be legible.`],generalLesson:`When the passage explicitly states the inference it draws from a detail, the function answer is that inference — don’t substitute a grander or different one.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph contains this detail, and whose argument is that paragraph making?`,`Read the sentence to its end — what conclusion does it draw from the comparison?`,`The right answer stays inside the skeptics’ case; it doesn’t add new claims.`],labels:{topic:`Vermeer interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p04-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`"moving a map, altering a chair’s position"`,`"the moralizers take as proof of deliberation about meaning"`],viewpointOwner:`proponents of the moralizing interpretation`,whyDistractorsFail:`C contradicts paragraph 4’s archival finding of "genuinely common possessions" (rarity would also hurt the moralizers, since a symbol must be familiar to be legible); D contradicts paragraph 2’s premise that the culture was "saturated with emblem books"; A and E invent reputation appeals and written statements the passage never attributes to the moralizers.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: credited B is the passage’s own account of the moralizers’ counter-move (P4); the distractors are either opposite to stated findings (C, D) or invented (A, E). Credited choice 113 chars vs longest distractor 104 chars — no leakage. d3 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p04`,stem:`Proponents of the moralizing interpretation would most likely respond to the skeptics’ "shared studio props" objection by pointing to`,choices:[{text:`the fact that Vermeer’s contemporaries are now considered inferior painters`},{text:`evidence that Vermeer adjusted compositions with extraordinary care, taken as proof of deliberation about meaning`},{text:`household inventories showing that the painted objects were rare luxuries unfamiliar to ordinary viewers`},{text:`the claim that emblem books were unknown in seventeenth-century Delft`},{text:`Vermeer’s written statements explaining the moral program of each painting`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 reports how each side uses the technical evidence: the moralizers "take as proof of deliberation about meaning" the studies showing Vermeer adjusted compositions "with extraordinary care — moving a map, altering a chair’s position." That is their reply to the props objection: the objects were deliberately, meaningfully placed.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Viewpoint questions must use the attributed side’s actual counter-move. Paragraph 4 stages the evidential standoff: technical studies show Vermeer repositioned objects with "extraordinary care," and "the moralizers take as proof of deliberation about meaning, and the skeptics take as proof of deliberation about pictorial harmony." Against the objection that the objects are mere studio props, the moralizers’ natural reply is that Vermeer’s meticulous adjustments show the objects were placed with deliberation about meaning — props don’t get moved with such care unless their placement matters. Choice C is the opposite of paragraph 4’s archival finding (the objects were "genuinely common possessions"). Choice D contradicts paragraph 2’s premise that the culture was "saturated with emblem books." Choice A is unsupported — painterly reputation is never the moralizers’ argument. Choice E invents written statements; the passage never mentions any. Only B uses the passage’s own account of the moralizers’ response.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage never has the moralizers appealing to the inferior reputation of Vermeer’s contemporaries; their counter-evidence concerns Vermeer’s compositional deliberation. The moralizers’ actual counter-move concerns compositional deliberation, as paragraph 4 documents, not anyone’s reputation.`,`Correct. Paragraph 4 attributes exactly this move to the moralizers: compositional adjustments made "with extraordinary care" are taken "as proof of deliberation about meaning." This is the passage’s own account of the moralizers’ reply to the props objection, making it the viewpoint-faithful answer.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 4’s archival research shows the objects were "genuinely common possessions," not rare luxuries — and the moralizers need familiarity, since "a symbol must be familiar to be legible."`,`Opposite. The moralizing interpretation depends on emblem books being pervasive ("saturated with emblem books and proverbs"); claiming they were unknown would destroy the moralizers’ case, not support it.`,`Unsupported. The passage never mentions written statements by Vermeer about his paintings’ meanings; this invents evidence. No written statements by Vermeer are ever mentioned; the debate is conducted entirely through visual and archival evidence.`],generalLesson:`The best response a viewpoint can make is usually the one the passage already attributes to it — check the paragraph where both sides’ use of the same evidence is compared.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 4 shows both sides using the same technical evidence — how do the moralizers use it?`,`What does "deliberation about meaning" answer in the props debate?`,`Cross out any choice that contradicts paragraph 4’s findings.`],labels:{topic:`Vermeer interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p04-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`"the skeptics take as proof of deliberation about pictorial harmony"`],requiredInference:`Replay the skeptics’ inference-blocking move on the new hypothetical: grant the compositional care, but deny that care entails symbolic meaning — care evidences pictorial deliberation, which is compatible with non-symbolic motives.`,viewpointOwner:`the passage’s skeptics of the moralizing interpretation`,whyDistractorsFail:`A concedes the disputed inference the skeptics exist to block; C invents a distinguishing fact contrary to paragraph 3’s "same objects" point; D categorically rejects technical evidence the skeptics themselves use in paragraph 4; E invents a painter-confusion error with no textual basis.`},revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE then validate: editorialDifficulty downgraded 4→3 (inflated — the credited answer nearly paraphrases paragraph 4’s stated skeptical move, so the item tests one-step application, not d4 layered reasoning); version bumped 1→2 with revisedAt 2026-09-11. Hostile review: no defensible distractor; credited choice 124 chars vs longest distractor 110 chars — no leakage. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p04`,stem:`An art historian discovers that Vermeer repositioned a wall map several times between compositional stages and argues that this proves the map carries a precise moral meaning. A skeptic reasoning as the passage’s skeptics do would most likely reply that`,choices:[{text:`the repositioning proves the historian’s case, since no painter adjusts meaningless details`},{text:`the repositioning shows deliberation about pictorial harmony, which does not by itself show the map carries symbolic meaning`},{text:`wall maps never appear in the work of Vermeer’s Delft contemporaries, so the studio-props objection fails`},{text:`moral meanings can only be established by archival documents, never by technical study of paintings`},{text:`the historian has confused Vermeer with a different Delft painter who did paint moral allegories`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 gives the skeptics’ exact move: compositional care is "proof of deliberation about pictorial harmony" — not about meaning. The skeptic grants the deliberation but denies the inference from care to symbolism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions test whether you can reproduce a viewpoint’s characteristic move on new facts. The passage hands us the skeptics’ move verbatim in paragraph 4: confronted with evidence of Vermeer’s "extraordinary care" in adjusting compositions, "the skeptics take [it] as proof of deliberation about pictorial harmony." The historian’s argument runs: care → symbolic meaning. The skeptic’s reply severs that inference: care → compositional deliberation, which is compatible with purely pictorial motives. Choice B reproduces this move exactly. Choice A surrenders to the historian, the opposite of the skeptical position. Choice C invents a factual claim (maps do appear across Delft studios per paragraph 3’s "same objects" point) and misapplies it. Choice D is too-strong: the skeptics use technical studies themselves (paragraph 4), so they cannot dismiss technical evidence categorically. Choice E invents a confusion of painters with no textual basis. The discipline is to apply the viewpoint’s logic, not to re-litigate the facts.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. This concedes the historian’s inference (care proves meaning), which is exactly what the passage’s skeptics deny — they grant the care but contest what it proves.`,`Correct. It reproduces paragraph 4’s skeptical move: the repositioning evidences "deliberation about pictorial harmony," which is consistent with non-symbolic motives and therefore does not establish symbolic meaning.`,`Unsupported and contrary to the passage: paragraph 3’s studio-props point depends on the same objects appearing across Delft painters’ work. This invents a distinguishing fact.`,`Too-strong. The skeptics themselves rely on technical studies of Vermeer’s working methods (paragraph 4); they dispute the inference from those studies, not the legitimacy of technical evidence as such.`,`Unsupported. The passage never suggests confusion between painters; this invents an error to dodge the argument rather than answering it skeptically. A skeptic answers arguments about evidence, not by inventing biographical mix-ups that no one in the passage suggests.`],generalLesson:`To apply a viewpoint, isolate its characteristic inference-blocking move — here, granting the evidence while denying what it’s claimed to prove — and replay it on the new facts.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How do the passage’s skeptics handle the very same evidence (compositional adjustments) in paragraph 4?`,`Do the skeptics deny that Vermeer took care, or deny what the care proves?`,`The skeptic’s reply should grant the fact and block the inference.`],labels:{topic:`Vermeer interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p05-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`“unreliability is not a property of the narrator at all but a judgment performed by the reader”`,`“the classroom has already rendered its verdict on which version teaches better”`],requiredInference:`The main point must include the debate’s resolution/upshot — the practical pedagogical verdict of paragraph 5 — not merely one side of the theoretical dispute.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A too-strong: the passage never calls for abandonment, only for teaching the concept as an argued inference. C scope-shift: the passage deliberately leaves the governess’s status open (“may be a ghost story, a case study in self-deception, or both”). D opposite: paragraph 3 treats disagreement as informative data about where unreliability lives, not proof that criticism is futile. E opposite: paragraphs 2–3 explicitly deny unreliability is a stable textual property “like the number of chapters.”`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: no defensible distractor (A too-strong, C settles a question the passage leaves open, D/E invert passage claims). Credited choice 172 chars vs longest distractor 168 chars — no length leakage. Every quoted claim in the explanations verified verbatim against the passage. d2 honest for a main-point item requiring upshot synthesis. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Wayne Booth’s concept of the unreliable narrator has been proven incoherent and should be entirely abandoned by teachers and critics alike.`},{text:`Unreliability is a reader’s inference shaped by textual cues and assumptions; the payoff lies in teaching it as an inference to be argued, not a label to affix.`},{text:`Henry James’s The Turn of the Screw is best read as a straightforward ghost story with no ironic dimension whatsoever.`},{text:`Since readers can never fully agree about textual meaning, persistent disagreement proves that literary criticism is futile.`},{text:`The unreliable narrator is a stable textual property, like the number of chapters, that all competent readers will always identify in precisely the same way.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage introduces Booth’s concept (paragraph 1), presents the reader-centered challenge (paragraphs 2–3), reports the traditionalist defense (paragraph 4), and concludes with the classroom payoff: teaching unreliability as an argued inference rather than a label (paragraph 5). Choice B is the only answer spanning that arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the passage’s movement: paragraph 1 introduces the unreliable narrator via Booth and James’s governess; paragraphs 2–3 advance the counter-claim that unreliability is a reader’s judgment, supported by the observation that readers persistently disagree; paragraph 4 gives the traditionalist reply that unreliability names authorial design; paragraph 5 draws the pedagogical moral — the inference-based approach teaches evidence-weighing better than label-hunting. Choice B contains all four stages. Choice A is too-strong: the passage never declares the concept incoherent or calls for abandonment; it ends by endorsing a better way of teaching it. Choice C takes a side in the governess debate the passage deliberately leaves open ("may be a ghost story, a case study in self-deception, or both"). Choice D inverts paragraph 3’s use of disagreement, which is evidence about where unreliability lives, not proof that criticism is futile. Choice E is the opposite of paragraphs 2–3, which argue unreliability is not a stable textual property.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The passage criticizes the label-hunting version of the concept but ends by endorsing teaching it as an argued inference — not by calling for its abandonment.`,`Correct. It tracks the full arc: the reader-centered challenge, the traditionalist defense as authorial design, and the concluding pedagogical verdict favoring inference over labeling. Main-point answers for debate passages must include the resolution or upshot, which here is the classroom verdict on pedagogy.`,`Wrong-viewpoint and scope-shift. The passage explicitly keeps both readings of the governess open; settling the novella’s meaning was never its project. The passage deliberately leaves the governess’s status open; any choice settling it mistakes an illustration for a thesis.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 3 treats reader disagreement as informative evidence about the nature of unreliability, not as proof that criticism is futile. Disagreement is presented as informative data about where unreliability lives, not as evidence that interpretation is futile.`,`Opposite. Paragraphs 2–3 argue precisely that unreliability is not a stable textual property "like the number of chapters" — the quoted phrase is used to show what unreliability is not.`],generalLesson:`A main-point answer for a debate passage must include the debate’s resolution or upshot — here, the classroom moral — not just the opening positions.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the last paragraph say the classroom has already decided?`,`Does the author want the concept abandoned, or taught differently?`,`The right answer will mention both the theoretical debate and its teaching payoff.`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p05-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`“Booth’s prototype was the governess of Henry James’s 1898 novella The Turn of the Screw”`],whyDistractorsFail:`A keyword-match importing emblem-book vocabulary from the set’s art-history passage; B invents a falsifying historian who never appears; C misattributes the final paragraph’s criticized trick-hunting pedagogy as Booth’s prototype; D opposite — the passage’s traditionalists defend authorial design rather than dismissing intentions.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Pure verbatim retrieval: person, work, and 1898 date are stated together in paragraph 1 and match the credited choice exactly. Each distractor fails for a distinct, nameable reason. d2 earned by distractor proximity (passage-phrase recycling, cross-passage vocabulary), not by the retrieval itself. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`According to the passage, Wayne Booth’s prototype of the unreliable narrator was`,choices:[{text:`the narrator of a Dutch emblem book from the seventeenth century`},{text:`a historian who deliberately falsified archival evidence`},{text:`a teacher who trained students to hunt for tricks in fiction`},{text:`a critic who argued that authors’ intentions are irrelevant to interpretation`},{text:`the governess in Henry James’s 1898 novella The Turn of the Screw`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states it directly: "Booth’s prototype was the governess of Henry James’s 1898 novella The Turn of the Screw." The distractors borrow vocabulary from elsewhere in the passage (emblem books belong to the Vermeer passage’s world, not this one) or invent figures.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Pure retrieval: paragraph 1 names "the governess of Henry James’s 1898 novella The Turn of the Screw" as "Booth’s prototype." Choice E matches. The distractors test whether you’re tracking this passage rather than general associations. Choice A imports "emblem book" vocabulary from a different passage’s domain — a cross-contamination trap. Choice B invents a falsifying historian; the passage’s historians are narratologists debating a concept, not forgers. Choice C describes the bad pedagogy the final paragraph criticizes ("trains students to hunt for a trick"), misattributed as Booth’s prototype. Choice D invents an intentional-fallacy critic; the passage’s traditionalists actually defend the relevance of authorial design. When the answer is stated verbatim, the work is verifying the exact referent, not reasoning further. On verbatim detail questions, verify the exact referent — person, work, and date — rather than settling for familiar vocabulary.`,choiceExplanations:[`Keyword-match from the wrong domain. Emblem books belong to the passage set’s art-history context, not to Booth’s theory; the passage never connects them to the unreliable narrator.`,`Unsupported. No historian falsifying archives appears in the passage; this invents a figure. No such historian appears in the passage; this distractor invents a figure from whole cloth.`,`Keyword-match. "Hunting for a trick" describes the pedagogy the final paragraph criticizes, not Booth’s prototype narrator. The trick-hunting phrase belongs to the criticized pedagogy of the final paragraph, not to Booth’s prototype.`,`Opposite. The passage’s traditionalists defend authorial design as central to unreliability; no critic in the passage argues intentions are irrelevant. The traditionalists defend authorial design as central, so an anti-intention critic would contradict the passage’s own cast.`,`Correct. Paragraph 1 identifies the governess of James’s 1898 novella as Booth’s prototype of the unreliable narrator. Paragraph 1 names the governess, the novella, and its 1898 date together as Booth’s prototype.`],generalLesson:`On verbatim detail questions, the distractors often recycle the passage’s own phrases in wrong roles — match the exact referent, not just familiar words.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`keyword-match`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The first paragraph names the prototype explicitly — who is it?`,`Don’t let vocabulary from other passages (emblem books) sneak in.`,`Match the person, the work, and the date.`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p05-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`“If unreliability were a stable textual property, like the number of chapters, such persistent disagreement would be puzzling”`,`“it admits that the “unreliability” resides in the interaction between text and reader, not in the narrator alone”`],requiredInference:`Follow the paragraph’s stated conditional exactly: if stable-property were true, observed disagreement would be puzzling; disagreement is observed; therefore unreliability is not a stable textual property but arises in the interaction between text and reader.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B scope-shift: the passage never grades readers’ competence or prescribes deference. C unsupported: James’s intentions are never discussed. D too-strong and opposite: paragraph 1 says the concept “proved irresistibly useful.” E opposite: the final paragraph champions weighing textual evidence more rigorously.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`The credited inference is the one paragraph 3 explicitly draws — verified verbatim, including the “like the number of chapters” clause. Attempted to defend each distractor; all collapse (competence grading, authorial intent, usefulness, evidence relevance are all contradicted or absent). d3 honest for a stated-conditional inference. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`The observation that readers disagree sharply about which narrators are unreliable is offered in the passage primarily to support the conclusion that`,choices:[{text:`unreliability is not a stable property of narrators but a judgment arising from the interaction of text and reader`},{text:`most readers are incompetent and should defer to professional critics on all questions of interpretation`},{text:`Henry James deliberately wrote an incoherent novella in order to frustrate his audience`},{text:`Booth’s concept was never useful to teachers of literature and should be abandoned entirely`},{text:`textual evidence is irrelevant to determining what a story means for any reader`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 argues: if unreliability were a stable textual property "like the number of chapters," persistent disagreement "would be puzzling." The disagreement is evidence that unreliability lives in the text-reader interaction. The other choices moralize about readers, impugn James, or overstate the argument.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The question asks for the conclusion an observation supports — a short inference chain. Paragraph 3’s logic: premise — readers "disagree, often sharply, about which narrators are unreliable"; conditional — "If unreliability were a stable textual property, like the number of chapters, such persistent disagreement would be puzzling"; conclusion — unreliability must not be such a property, but rather "resides in the interaction between text and reader." Choice A states that conclusion. Choice B is a scope-shift: the passage never grades readers’ competence or prescribes deference. Choice C invents an authorial-intention claim about James that the passage never makes. Choice D is too-strong and contradicted: paragraph 1 says the concept "proved irresistibly useful" to teachers. Choice E is the opposite of the passage’s evidentiary sensibility — the final paragraph champions weighing textual evidence. The inference is licensed because the paragraph explicitly draws it.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This is paragraph 3’s stated inference: persistent disagreement would be puzzling if unreliability were a stable textual property, so it must arise in the interaction between text and reader.`,`Scope-shift. The passage never evaluates readers’ competence or recommends deference to critics; disagreement is treated as data, not as failure. Treating disagreement as reader failure is a scope-shift; the passage treats it as theoretical data.`,`Unsupported. The passage never claims James wrote incoherently or discusses his intentions regarding the novella’s construction. James’s intentions regarding the novella’s construction are never discussed anywhere in the passage.`,`Opposite and too-strong. Paragraph 1 explicitly says the concept "proved irresistibly useful" and gave teachers "a vocabulary for discussing narrative technique." Paragraph 1’s verdict that the concept proved irresistibly useful directly contradicts any claim of uselessness.`,`Opposite. The passage’s final paragraph argues for weighing textual evidence more rigorously, not for declaring it irrelevant. The final paragraph champions weighing textual evidence more rigorously, the opposite of declaring it irrelevant.`],generalLesson:`When a passage says "if X were true, Y would be puzzling," the licensed inference is "X is probably not true" — follow the conditional exactly where it points.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 3 say would be "puzzling" if unreliability were a stable textual property?`,`The observation is data — what hypothesis does the data argue against?`,`The conclusion is about where unreliability lives, not about readers’ competence.`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p05-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`“Where the debate lands matters beyond the seminar room, because the concept shapes how fiction is taught”`,`“the classroom has already rendered its verdict on which version teaches better”`],requiredInference:`The framing sentence announces an application move: the paragraph’s function is to extract the debate’s practical pedagogical stakes (inference-arguing trains evidence-weighing; label-hunting trains trick-hunting), not to add theory or settle the theoretical dispute.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A unsupported: no empirical learning research is cited; the paragraph offers a normative argument. B too-strong: the verdict is practical (“teaches better”), not a logical refutation of traditionalism. D opposite: the paragraph opens by claiming the debate matters beyond the seminar room. E unsupported: no plot summary appears anywhere in the passage.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Function follows the paragraph’s announced job verbatim; attempted to defend each distractor and each fails on the paragraph’s own first sentence. The B/C contrast (practical vs logical verdict) is a fair near-miss but loses on the exact wording “which version teaches better.” d3 honest. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`The final paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`introduce new empirical research on how students actually learn narrative technique in literature classrooms`},{text:`prove that the traditionalist defense of Booth’s concept is logically incoherent and unworthy of serious scholarly attention`},{text:`draw out the debate's pedagogical stakes: teaching unreliability as argued inference builds better reasoning than teaching it as a label`},{text:`concede that the entire debate has no implications beyond academic journals and need not concern teachers of literature`},{text:`summarize the plot of The Turn of the Screw for readers who are unfamiliar with James’s novella`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The last paragraph opens "Where the debate lands matters beyond the seminar room, because the concept shapes how fiction is taught," then contrasts label-hunting (trains students "to hunt for a trick") with inference-arguing (trains "the weighing of evidence"). Its job is to extract the teaching payoff from the theory debate.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function follows the paragraph’s framing sentence: "Where the debate lands matters beyond the seminar room" signals an application move — taking the theoretical dispute and showing its practical stakes. The paragraph then contrasts two pedagogies and their cognitive products: the label approach yields trick-hunting; the inference approach yields "the weighing of evidence, the articulation of reasons, and the humility of recognizing that even careful readers can disagree." Choice C captures this. Choice A invents empirical research; the paragraph offers an argument, not data. Choice B overstates — the paragraph doesn’t refute traditionalism as incoherent; it says the classroom has "rendered its verdict" on which version teaches better, a practical rather than logical judgment. Choice D is the opposite of the paragraph’s opening claim ("matters beyond the seminar room"). Choice E misplaces content: no plot summary appears anywhere.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The paragraph contains no empirical research on learning; it advances a normative argument about which pedagogy cultivates better reasoning. The paragraph offers a normative pedagogical argument, and no empirical learning research is cited anywhere.`,`Too-strong. The paragraph does not attempt a logical refutation of traditionalism; it renders a practical verdict about teaching efficacy ("which version teaches better"). A practical verdict about teaching efficacy is weaker than a logical refutation, and the paragraph claims only the former.`,`Correct. The paragraph’s announced job is to show why the debate "matters beyond the seminar room": the inference-based pedagogy teaches evidence-weighing, while label-hunting teaches trick-hunting.`,`Opposite. The opening sentence explicitly claims the debate matters beyond academic settings ("beyond the seminar room"), so a concession of irrelevance inverts its function. Function answers must match the paragraph’s announced job, which here is showing why the debate matters beyond the seminar room.`,`Unsupported. The passage never summarizes the novella’s plot; the governess is discussed only as an illustration of the theoretical issue. Plot summary appears nowhere; the governess serves solely as an illustration of the theoretical dispute.`],generalLesson:`Final paragraphs that begin "where this matters" or "the payoff" are application moves — their function is to convert theory into stakes, not to add new theory.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the paragraph’s first sentence say the debate’s landing affects?`,`Does the paragraph add a new theory, or use the existing debate for something?`,`Contrast the two pedagogies: what does each one train students to do?`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p05-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-attitude`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,viewpointComplexity:4,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4,5],supportingSentences:[`“Traditionalists have not surrendered the concept, and their best defense draws a distinction the critics are said to blur”`,`“whatever the narratologists finally decide about where unreliability lives, the classroom has already rendered its verdict on which version teaches better”`],requiredInference:`Separate tone of presentation from direction of verdict: paragraph 4 steelmans the defense (respectful), while paragraph 5 issues a practical — not theoretical — verdict favoring the inference-based approach.`,viewpointOwner:`The author: attitude is inferred from the author’s own framing (“their best defense”, “whatever the narratologists finally decide”), not from the traditionalists’ self-description.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite: nothing suggests contempt or an accusation of dishonesty. B unsupported: the author visibly cares which pedagogy prevails. C too-strong: paragraph 5 explicitly withholds theoretical settlement. E opposite: authorial design is reported neutrally, never attacked.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Attitude requires the two-part structure (respectful steelman + practical-only verdict), and the credited choice is the only one holding both halves. Defended C as a near-miss: it collapses on the explicit withholding sentence in paragraph 5. d3 honest; viewpointComplexity 4 for the tone-vs-verdict separation. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward the traditionalist defense of Booth’s concept (paragraph 4) is best described as`,choices:[{text:`contemptuous dismissal of a defense the author considers dishonest`},{text:`indifference, since the author regards both sides as equally confused`},{text:`complete agreement that settles the theoretical debate in the traditionalists’ favor`},{text:`respectful presentation followed by a practical verdict favoring the inference-based approach`},{text:`hostility toward any role for authorial intention in literary interpretation`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 gives the traditionalist defense its strongest form ("their best defense draws a distinction"), and paragraph 5 renders a practical — not theoretical — verdict: the classroom favors the inference approach because it "teaches better." That is respectful presentation plus a practical, not contemptuous or total, judgment.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Attitude questions require separating how the author treats a position from which position the author finally prefers. Paragraph 4 is notably fair: "Traditionalists have not surrendered the concept, and their best defense draws a distinction the critics are said to blur" — the author steelmans the authorial-design argument and even grants that reader disagreement may be "a feature rather than a bug." That rules out contempt (A) and hostility (E). But paragraph 5 does not hand traditionalists the theoretical victory either: it says "whatever the narratologists finally decide about where unreliability lives, the classroom has already rendered its verdict on which version teaches better." The verdict is practical and pedagogical, not a theoretical settlement — ruling out complete agreement (C). Indifference (B) fails because the author clearly cares which pedagogy wins. Choice D holds both halves: respectful presentation, practical verdict for the inference approach.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 4 presents "their best defense" fairly and grants it a genuine distinction; nothing suggests contempt or an accusation of dishonesty. Paragraph 4’s respectful steelman of the traditionalist distinction rules out any reading of contempt or bad faith.`,`Unsupported. The author is invested throughout in which approach prevails pedagogically; "equally confused" and "indifference" misdescribe an engaged, discriminating stance. An author who cares visibly about which pedagogy prevails cannot be described as indifferent to the dispute.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 5 explicitly withholds theoretical settlement ("whatever the narratologists finally decide"); the verdict concerns teaching efficacy, not the truth of traditionalism. Withholding theoretical settlement while issuing a practical verdict is the author’s characteristic move in paragraph 5.`,`Correct. The defense gets a respectful steelman in paragraph 4, and paragraph 5 issues a practical verdict — the inference-based version "teaches better" — without claiming a final theoretical settlement.`,`Opposite. The author reports the authorial-design defense without hostility and never attacks the role of intention in interpretation. The authorial-design defense is reported neutrally; no hostility toward intention-based interpretation appears.`],generalLesson:`An author can present a position respectfully while still ruling against it on practical grounds — distinguish the tone of presentation from the direction of the verdict.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-attitude`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How does paragraph 4 introduce the traditionalist defense — with scorn or with care?`,`Does paragraph 5 settle the theory debate, or something narrower?`,`The verdict is about teaching — what kind of verdict is that?`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p05-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,linguisticComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,4],supportingSentences:[`readers infer unreliability when a narrator’s claims clash with “contradictions with what other characters report” or “violations of the reader’s background expectations”`,`the governess can be “both a victim of suggestion and a teller of truths, depending on which cues the reader weights most heavily”`],requiredInference:`Translate the new facts into the framework’s own terms: the students weight different cues (cross-character contradiction vs. sincerity/background-expectation signals), and cue-weighting is the passage’s stated mechanism for persistent disagreement.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite: disagreement is treated as informative data, not proof of misreading. B unsupported: blaming the author is no one’s move in the passage. D unsupported and contrary: the passage trades on the sincerity/reliability gap (the governess “might not know they are lying”). E opposite: the final paragraph celebrates argued disagreement as teaching “the weighing of evidence.”`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Application is a genuine two-step map (new facts → cue signals in paragraph 2 → cue-weighting mechanism in paragraph 4). Attempted to defend A and D; A contradicts the passage’s treatment of disagreement, D contradicts the governess example’s premise. d4 honest for the abstraction load, not inflation. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p05`,stem:`Two students disagree about whether a novel’s narrator is unreliable. One cites contradictions between the narrator’s account and other characters’ reports; the other cites details suggesting the narrator is sincere but mistaken about key events. According to the passage’s analysis, this disagreement best illustrates the claim that`,choices:[{text:`one of the students has misread the novel, since textual evidence determines unreliability objectively`},{text:`the author of the novel failed to signal the narrator’s status clearly enough`},{text:`judgments of unreliability depend on which textual cues and background assumptions a reader weights most heavily`},{text:`sincerity and reliability are identical, so the second student’s distinction is meaningless`},{text:`classroom debate about literature is a waste of time that the passage condemns`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraphs 2–4 locate unreliability in the reader’s inference from cues: contradictions with other reports and violations of background expectations are exactly the signals paragraph 2 lists, and paragraph 4 notes disagreement reflects "which cues the reader weights most heavily." The students exemplify that mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the passage’s framework to the new case. Paragraph 2 defines the reader-centered mechanism: readers infer unreliability from "inconsistencies in the account, contradictions with what other characters report, or violations of the reader’s background expectations." The first student uses exactly the second signal; the second student invokes background expectations about sincerity versus mistake. Paragraph 4 then explains persistent disagreement: readers weight cues differently, and the governess can be "both a victim of suggestion and a teller of truths, depending on which cues the reader weights most heavily." Choice C states that explanation. Choice A contradicts the passage, which treats disagreement as informative rather than as proof of misreading. Choice B blames the author, a move the passage never makes. Choice D invents an identity claim (sincerity = reliability) the passage never asserts — indeed the governess example trades on their difference. Choice E is the opposite of the final paragraph, which celebrates such debate as teaching "the weighing of evidence."`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The passage treats persistent disagreement as evidence about where unreliability lives, not as proof that one party misread; nothing here is objectively settled by the text alone.`,`Unsupported. The passage never blames authors for failing to signal clearly; the traditionalist defense says authors engineer cues, and disagreement persists even so. Blaming the author is no one’s move in the passage; the traditionalists say authors engineer cues, and disagreement persists regardless.`,`Correct. The students are weighting different cues (contradictions vs. sincerity signals) — precisely paragraph 4’s account of why competent readers disagree about unreliability. Cue-weighting is paragraph 4’s explicit mechanism for persistent disagreement among competent readers.`,`Unsupported and contrary to the passage’s logic: the governess example depends on the gap between sincerity and reliability (she may be sincere yet self-deceived). The passage never equates them.`,`Opposite. The final paragraph celebrates exactly this kind of argued disagreement as teaching "the weighing of evidence" — it is the model pedagogy, not a waste of time.`],generalLesson:`To apply a theoretical framework, translate the new facts into the framework’s own terms (here: cues, weighting, background assumptions) and check which conclusion those terms generate.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What signals does paragraph 2 say readers use to infer unreliability? Which ones do the students use?`,`How does paragraph 4 explain why readers disagree?`,`The disagreement itself is the phenomenon the passage explains — which choice states that explanation?`],labels:{topic:`unreliable narration`}},{id:`rc-p06-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`Each answers a different version of the explanation question`,`match the scale to the question being asked`],requiredInference:`Synthesize the three surveyed approaches with the ecumenical conclusion -- the thesis is pluralism, not a winner.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A inverts the conclusion; C mistakes an illustration for the thesis; D is too-strong and inverts paragraph 4; E elevates one example to exclusivity.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Narrative history has been decisively refuted by the Annales school and should no longer be practiced by serious historians.`},{text:`Narrative, structural, and microhistorical approaches each answer a different explanation question; the historian’s task is matching the scale of explanation to the question asked.`},{text:`The fall of Rome was caused exclusively by weak emperors and invading armies, as narrative historians have always maintained without serious challenge.`},{text:`Microhistory has resolved the debate between narrative and structural history by proving that structures never constrain human action under any circumstances whatsoever.`},{text:`Fernand Braudel’s study of the Mediterranean is the only historical work that has ever successfully explained change.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage surveys narrative history (paragraph 1), the Annales structural challenge (paragraph 2), the critique of structuralism (paragraph 3), the microhistorical synthesis (paragraph 4), and an ecumenical conclusion (paragraph 5). Only choice B captures the survey plus the "match the scale to the question" verdict.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage’s structure is a three-position survey with a synthesizing conclusion. Paragraph 1: narrative explanation as artful arrangement of events. Paragraph 2: the Annales structuralist challenge (Braudel, deep structures). Paragraph 3: the critique (structures explain continuity better than change; selection of structures is itself interpretive). Paragraph 4: microhistory as a third way (the miller, structures constraining without determining). Paragraph 5: the ecumenical verdict — each approach answers a different version of the question, so match scale to question. Choice B is the only answer with all three approaches and the concluding prescription. Choice A is the opposite of the conclusion, which rehabilitates narrative history. Choice C mistakes an illustrative example for a thesis. Choice D is too-strong and inverts paragraph 4, which says structures "constrain without determining" — not that they never constrain. Choice E absurdly elevates one example into exclusivity the passage never suggests.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The conclusion explicitly rehabilitates narrative history as answering "how we got from one event to the next"; nothing is "decisively refuted" or abandoned. The ecumenical conclusion rehabilitates rather than refutes narrative history, so any refutation narrative inverts the passage.`,`Correct. It names all three approaches and the final paragraph’s prescription: each answers a different version of the explanation question, so match the scale to the question.`,`Scope-shift. The fall of Rome is a passing illustration of narrative history’s style, not the passage’s thesis; "exclusively" is also far stronger than anything asserted.`,`Too-strong and inverted. Paragraph 4 says structures "constrain without determining" — they do constrain. "Never constrain" flips the microhistorical claim. Constrain-without-determining is the microhistorical slogan; dropping either half distorts it into something the passage never claims.`,`Unsupported. Braudel’s study is presented as the most famous example of structural history, not as the sole successful explanation of change. Braudel’s study exemplifies structural history; elevating one example into exclusivity is an unsupported leap.`],generalLesson:`Survey passages that end ecumenically ("each answers a different question") punish answers that crown one side the winner — the main point is the pluralism, not the victor.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author declare a winner among the three approaches?`,`What does the final paragraph say each approach is good for?`,`The verdict is a prescription about matching — which choice states it?`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`relegated kings and battles to the book’s final section`,`devoting the bulk of his analysis to mountains, seas, and grain prices`],whyDistractorsFail:`A, C, E invert Braudel’s role; B swaps in the microhistory exemplar.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`According to the passage, Fernand Braudel’s study of the Mediterranean in the age of Philip II`,choices:[{text:`focused exclusively on the decisions of Philip II himself and the diplomacy of his court`},{text:`was written as a microhistory of a single Pyrenean village and its parish records`},{text:`argued that narrative history is the only valid form of explanation for major events`},{text:`relegated kings and battles to the book’s final section, devoting most of the analysis to mountains, seas, and grain prices`},{text:`proved that geography has no influence on historical change, which proceeds entirely from individual will and decisive action`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 states that Braudel "famously relegated kings and battles to the book’s final section, devoting the bulk of his analysis to mountains, seas, and grain prices." Choice D restates this; the others invert or invent the study’s character.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The detail is stated nearly verbatim in paragraph 2, where Braudel’s work exemplifies the Annales elevation of deep structures over events. Choice D matches. Choice A is the opposite: the study is famous precisely for demoting kings. Choice B confuses Braudel’s macro-structural study with the microhistory examples of paragraph 4 (the Pyrenean village belongs to a different historian’s work). Choice C inverts Braudel’s position — he is the leading anti-narrative figure in the passage. Choice E inverts the study’s content: mountains and seas are central to it, so it cannot have proved geography irrelevant. Detail success here is a matter of keeping the three approaches’ exemplars straight. In survey passages, keep each school’s flagship example labeled, since distractors routinely swap exemplars between positions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Braudel’s study is famous for demoting kings, not centering them; "exclusively on Philip II" inverts paragraph 2’s description. Demoting kings is the study’s famous feature, so any choice centering Philip II inverts paragraph 2’s description.`,`Keyword-match. The Pyrenean village heresy trial is paragraph 4’s microhistory example, not Braudel’s Mediterranean study. The village trial belongs to paragraph 4’s microhistory; swapping exemplars between schools is this set’s signature trap.`,`Opposite. Braudel is presented as the foremost challenger of narrative history, not its defender. Braudel leads the anti-narrative challenge, so casting him as narrative history’s defender reverses his role.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 2: kings and battles go last; mountains, seas, and grain prices get the bulk of the analysis. The sentence’s structure — relegated versus bulk — is the detail to match, not any general impression of the book.`,`Opposite. The study’s emphasis on mountains and seas shows geography’s centrality to it; it could not have proved geography irrelevant. Mountains and seas are the study’s center of gravity, making any geography-is-irrelevant reading self-contradictory.`],generalLesson:`In survey passages, keep each school’s flagship example labeled — distractors routinely swap exemplars between positions.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which school does Braudel exemplify, and what does that school de-emphasize?`,`Find the sentence with "relegated" — what was relegated, and what took its place?`,`Don’t confuse Braudel’s big-structures book with the small-scale examples of paragraph 4.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`smuggled its own narrative in through the back door`,`selecting which structures matter is itself an interpretive, story-shaping act`],requiredInference:`The objection targets the method’s self-understanding: if choosing structures is story-shaping, structuralism has not escaped narrative.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A too-strong; C attributes views the critics never expressed; D inverts the comparison’s point; E converts criticism into a prohibition.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`The passage suggests that the critics of structural history would most likely agree with which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`Geography and climate have no effect whatsoever on human societies, a claim no structural historian would actually endorse`},{text:`Choosing which structures to emphasize is itself an interpretive, story-shaping act that undercuts structural historians’ claim to have escaped narrative.`},{text:`Microhistory is merely narrative history under a different name and offers nothing genuinely new to historical understanding`},{text:`The Mediterranean world of 1600 was identical to that of 1400 in all important respects, including its economic structures, trade patterns, and demographic pressures`},{text:`Historians should never study demographic or economic structures, since such structures cannot be known with any precision`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3’s second objection is that structural history "smuggled its own narrative in through the back door, since selecting which structures matter is itself an interpretive, story-shaping act." Choice B restates it; the others deny obvious facts, misstate the critics’ view of microhistory, or overstate into prohibitions.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 3 gives the critics two objections; the question targets the second, subtler one. After arguing that structures explain continuity better than change (illustrated by the 1400 vs. 1600 Mediterranean comparison), the critics add that structural history "smuggled its own narrative in through the back door" because "selecting which structures matter is itself an interpretive, story-shaping act." Choice B is a direct paraphrase. Choice A is too-strong: the critics dispute structures’ sufficiency for explaining change, not their having "no effect whatsoever." Choice C is unsupported — the critics of paragraph 3 never discuss microhistory (it arrives in paragraph 4 as a separate development). Choice D inverts the 1400/1600 comparison, which is used to show dramatic difference, not identity. Choice E converts a methodological criticism into a prohibition ("never study") the critics never issue.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The critics argue structures are insufficient to explain change, not that geography and climate have "no effect whatsoever." Insufficiency for explaining change is the critics’ claim; upgrading it to zero effect is a classic too-strong distortion.`,`Correct. This paraphrases paragraph 3’s "back door" objection: choosing which structures matter is an interpretive, story-shaping act, so structural history does not escape narrative after all.`,`Unsupported. Paragraph 3’s critics never address microhistory; the passage introduces it separately in paragraph 4 as a third approach. Paragraph 3’s critics predate the microhistory discussion, so attributing views about it to them is anachronistic.`,`Opposite. The 1400-vs.-1600 comparison is used to show dramatic change despite unmoved mountains — difference, not identity, is the point. The comparison’s point is change amid continuity of landscape, so identity readings invert its logic.`,`Too-strong. The critics challenge structuralism’s explanatory ambitions, not the legitimacy of studying structures at all; "never" goes beyond the text. Methodological criticism challenges explanatory ambitions; converting it into a research ban adds a prohibition never issued.`],generalLesson:`A criticism that a method "smuggles in" what it claims to avoid is a reflexivity objection — the licensed inference stays at the level of the method’s self-understanding, not a ban on its subject matter.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 3 makes two objections — what is the second one, about the "back door"?`,`Does that objection ban studying structures, or say something about the historian choosing them?`,`The right answer is about the act of selecting, not about structures themselves.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`The method’s wager is that intensive scrutiny of the small scale can reveal how structures were actually experienced`,`evidence that structures constrain without determining`],requiredInference:`The example instantiates the paragraph’s thesis: small-scale lensing of large structures.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B opposite; C unsupported; D too-strong; E misfires on geography and refutation.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`The discussion of the sixteenth-century miller in the fourth paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`illustrate how microhistory uses intensive small-scale study to show structures as experienced and negotiated: constraining without determining`},{text:`prove that peasant culture was entirely independent of elite ideas, with no meaningful contact or exchange between the two spheres at all`},{text:`demonstrate that the Inquisition’s records are too unreliable for any serious historical use by modern scholars`},{text:`argue that microhistory has fully replaced both narrative and structural history as the dominant historical method`},{text:`provide a counterexample that decisively refutes the Annales school’s claims about Mediterranean geography`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 introduces microhistory’s "wager" and uses the miller — whose cosmology was "a creative recombination of elite and popular ideas" — as evidence that structures "constrain without determining." The example’s job is to instantiate the method’s payoff.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The miller appears as paragraph 4’s worked example of microhistory’s method: take an "apparently insignificant episode" and use it "as a lens onto the larger structures." The paragraph draws an explicit moral from the case: the miller’s worldview was "neither a mere reflection of peasant culture nor an inexplicable personal invention but a creative recombination of elite and popular ideas — evidence that structures constrain without determining." Choice A restates that function. Choice B is the opposite: the "creative recombination" point explicitly denies independence from elite ideas. Choice C is unsupported — the passage never questions the Inquisition records’ reliability. Choice D is too-strong: microhistory is presented as a third approach alongside the others, and the conclusion keeps all three. Choice E misfires: the miller example concerns culture and ideas, not geography, and it qualifies rather than refutes structural claims.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The miller instantiates microhistory’s wager: small-scale scrutiny revealing how structures were "experienced, negotiated, and sometimes subverted" — constraining without determining. The example’s job is to show the method working — small scale lensing large structures — not to score points against rivals.`,`Opposite. The paragraph stresses the miller’s "creative recombination of elite and popular ideas," explicitly denying that peasant culture was independent of elite influence. Creative recombination of elite and popular ideas is the paragraph’s explicit phrase, and it denies independence, not dependence.`,`Unsupported. The passage never assesses the reliability of Inquisition records; they are simply the source from which the miller’s worldview is reconstructed. Sources are used, not evaluated, here; the passage never pauses to assess the Inquisition records’ reliability.`,`Too-strong. Microhistory is introduced as a fertile third approach, and the conclusion retains all three; nothing claims it "replaced" the others. A third approach alongside the others is not a replacement; the conclusion’s pluralism depends on keeping all three.`,`Scope-shift. The example concerns cultural/ideational structures, not geography, and its moral ("constrain without determining") qualifies structuralism rather than refuting it. Qualify is not refute: the miller’s case softens structural determinism without denying that structures constrain.`],generalLesson:`Worked examples in survey passages instantiate the abstract claim of their paragraph — the function answer is the paragraph’s thesis applied to the example.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 4 say is microhistory’s "wager"?`,`Read the sentence beginning "The miller’s cosmology turns out to be neither..." — what conclusion does it draw?`,`The example’s job is to show the method working, not to refute anyone outright.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`structures set the stage but human agency writes the play`,`history becomes a machine without operators`],viewpointOwner:`narrative historians (as presented in paragraph 3)`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B, D invent objections; C is anachronistic.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`Narrative historians, as presented in the passage, would most likely object to the Annales approach on the grounds that`,choices:[{text:`geography and demography are too difficult to research rigorously, so historians should avoid them`},{text:`the Annales school was wrong to found its journal in 1929 rather than earlier, betraying its own principles`},{text:`microhistory has already rendered the entire debate obsolete and settled every remaining question`},{text:`Braudel’s prose style is less artful than that of the traditional narrative historians`},{text:`structures explain continuity better than change, and history without human decisions becomes a machine without operators`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 attributes exactly this objection to narrative historians: structures explain continuity better than change, and "without decisions, contingencies, and the occasional blunder, history becomes a machine without operators." The other choices invent aesthetic, chronological, or difficulty-based objections.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Strict viewpoint fidelity is required. Paragraph 3 states the narrative historians’ objection in two parts: the explanatory asymmetry ("structures explain continuity far better than they explain change," illustrated by the unmoved mountains between 1400 and 1600) and the agency point ("structures set the stage but human agency writes the play — that without decisions, contingencies, and the occasional blunder, history becomes a machine without operators"). The last choice combines both parts of the objection. Choice A invents a research-difficulty complaint the passage never voices. Choice B is nonsense — no one objects to a founding date. Choice C anachronistically puts microhistory (paragraph 4’s later development) in the narrative historians’ mouths as a debate-ender. Choice D invents an aesthetic judgment about prose style. The correct answer is essentially a quotation of the paragraph’s own words.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage never has narrative historians complaining that structures are too hard to research; their objection concerns explanatory power, not research difficulty. Explanatory power, not research difficulty, is the currency of paragraph 3’s objections throughout.`,`Unsupported. The founding date is a neutral fact in paragraph 2; no one in the passage objects to it. This is a nonsense distractor. Neutral facts like founding dates make effective nonsense distractors precisely because they are true but irrelevant.`,`Anachronistic and unsupported. Microhistory arrives in paragraph 4 as a separate third approach; paragraph 3’s narrative historians never invoke it. Putting later developments into earlier speakers’ mouths is an anachronism trap to watch for in survey passages.`,`Unsupported. No aesthetic comparison of prose styles appears in the passage; the dispute is about explanation, not artfulness. Aesthetics never enter the dispute; every objection in paragraph 3 concerns what explains historical change.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 3’s two-part objection: the continuity/change asymmetry and the "machine without operators" agency point. Near-quotation of the paragraph’s own language is usually the mark of the viewpoint-faithful answer.`],generalLesson:`When a passage states a viewpoint’s objection in memorable language, the correct answer is usually the closest paraphrase of that language — don’t upgrade it into a different kind of complaint.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two-part objection does paragraph 3 attribute to narrative historians?`,`Look for the "machine" metaphor — what is it a metaphor for?`,`The answer should sound like paragraph 3, not like a new complaint.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`use it as a lens onto the larger structures`],requiredInference:`Classify by method: small-scale reconstruction lensing structural pressures is microhistory’s signature move.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A classifies by topic; B is half-right; D inverts the method; E invents an era restriction.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`A historian writes a book centered on a single nineteenth-century factory town, reconstructing through workers’ letters how one extended family navigated wage labor, religious revivalism, and chain migration, and using that reconstruction to illuminate industrialization’s broader social pressures. According to the passage’s taxonomy, this project most closely exemplifies`,choices:[{text:`Annales-style structural history, because it addresses industrialization`},{text:`traditional narrative history, because it tells a story about a family in chronological order`},{text:`microhistory, because it uses intensive small-scale scrutiny as a lens onto larger structures`},{text:`a refutation of microhistory, because it generalizes beyond a single episode`},{text:`none of the passage’s approaches, because the passage discusses only pre-modern history`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 defines microhistory’s method: take an apparently small-scale subject and use it "as a lens onto the larger structures." The hypothetical — one family’s letters illuminating industrialization’s pressures — matches that method exactly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the passage’s taxonomy by matching the project’s method to each approach’s definition. Annales structuralism (paragraph 2) works at the scale of deep, slow-moving structures — geography, demography, centuries — not a single town’s letters. Traditional narrative (paragraph 1) explains by arranging events into a plot centered on decisions; the hypothetical’s distinctive move is not plotting events but using one family’s experience as a lens onto structural pressures. Microhistory (paragraph 4) is defined precisely by this move: "intensive scrutiny of the small scale" revealing "how structures were actually experienced, negotiated, and sometimes subverted." The family’s navigation of wage labor, revivalism, and migration under industrialization’s pressures is the modern analogue of the miller’s cosmology under early-modern structures. Choice D misreads microhistory, which explicitly aims to illuminate larger structures through the small scale. Choice E is false — the passage’s approaches are not era-restricted.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong-viewpoint. Addressing industrialization does not make a work Annales-style; the Annales approach is defined by its large scale and deep structures (paragraph 2), not by its topic. Method, not subject matter, determines the category.`,`Half-right but incomplete. A family chronicle has narrative elements, but the project’s defining ambition — illuminating structural pressures through small-scale experience — is what makes it microhistorical rather than merely narrative.`,`Correct. The project performs microhistory’s signature move: intensive small-scale reconstruction used "as a lens onto the larger structures" (paragraph 4). Signature moves, not topics, determine classification: lensing large structures through a small case is microhistory’s alone.`,`Opposite. Generalizing to larger structures through a small-scale case is microhistory’s stated method ("The method’s wager"), not its refutation. The method’s wager explicitly aims at larger structures, so treating generalization as refutation inverts the method.`,`Unsupported. Nothing in the passage restricts its three approaches to pre-modern history; the taxonomy concerns scale and method, not era. Era never defines these approaches; scale and method do, so era-based exclusions are always suspect.`],generalLesson:`Classify by method, not topic: ask what the work does with its subject (plots events? maps deep structures? lenses structures through a small case?) rather than what the subject is.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`,`opposite`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is each approach’s defining method, in one phrase?`,`The project’s key move is using one family to illuminate big pressures — which approach is defined by that move?`,`Don’t classify by topic (industrialization); classify by how the historian works.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`}},{id:`rc-p06-q7`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:4,viewpointComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`explanation consisted in the artful arrangement of events`,`causes rendered as episodes in a plot, with human decisions at the center`],whyDistractorsFail:`A states the Annales definition of explanation (P2); B states microhistory’s definition (P4); D states the passage author’s ecumenical verdict (P5); E is a too-strong determinism no school in the passage endorses.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-set-size-diversification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`New item for 6→7 set-size diversification (2026-09-11): fills the set’s untested P1 content (narrative history’s positive definition of explanation). Hostile review: credited D is near-verbatim P1; the A/B/C/E distractors each state a different passage-named position (Annales, microhistory, absolute determinism, author’s verdict) and fail the stem’s "narrative historian" restriction. Credited choice 108 chars vs longest distractor 104 chars — no length leakage. d3 honest: the reasoning is one-step, but the distractors are the other positions’ genuine definitions, demanding exact school-labeling. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p06`,stem:`According to the passage, a narrative historian would regard which one of the following as the best explanation of a historical event?`,choices:[{text:`An account identifying the deep structural constraints that narrowed the range of possible outcomes`},{text:`An intensive reconstruction of how one individual experienced and negotiated larger impersonal forces`},{text:`A statistical demonstration that the event was determined entirely by geography and climate`},{text:`An account arranging the event’s causes as episodes in a plot centered on human decisions`},{text:`An account matching the scale of explanation to the particular question being asked`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 defines narrative explanation directly: "explanation consisted in the artful arrangement of events — causes rendered as episodes in a plot, with human decisions at the center." Choice D restates that definition; every other choice states a rival position’s definition.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage gives each school a one-phrase definition of explanation, and the question asks specifically for the narrative historian’s. Paragraph 1: narrative explanation is "the artful arrangement of events — causes rendered as episodes in a plot, with human decisions at the center." Choice D matches. Choice A is the Annales definition from paragraph 2 (structural constraints within which events "merely played out their assigned roles"). Choice B is microhistory’s definition from paragraph 4 (small-scale scrutiny revealing "how structures were actually experienced, negotiated, and sometimes subverted"). Choice E is too-strong: no school in the passage, not even the Annalistes, claims events are "determined entirely" by geography. Choice C is the passage author’s ecumenical verdict from paragraph 5 ("match the scale to the question being asked"), not the narrative historian’s position. The discipline is school-labeling: answer from the requested school’s definition, not from the author’s synthesis. In survey passages, keep each school’s flagship definition labeled, since distractors routinely swap definitions between positions.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong-viewpoint. This is the Annales definition of explanation: identifying structural constraints (paragraph 2). A narrative historian explains by plotting events around decisions, not by mapping constraints.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. This is microhistory’s definition: small-scale reconstruction revealing how structures were experienced (paragraph 4). The narrative historian’s unit of explanation is the plotted event, not the individual’s negotiation of structures.`,`Too-strong. No position in the passage claims events are "determined entirely" by geography and climate — even the Annalistes allow events and individuals their "assigned roles." Determinism this absolute appears nowhere in the passage.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 1’s definition: narrative explanation is the artful arrangement of events, with causes as episodes in a plot centered on human decisions.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. "Match the scale to the question" is the passage author’s concluding prescription (paragraph 5), not the narrative historian’s definition of explanation. The author’s synthesis must not be mistaken for any one school’s position.`],generalLesson:`In survey passages, each school gets a one-phrase definition — label them at first reading, because distractors swap definitions between positions.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph defines narrative history’s idea of explanation, in its own words?`,`Each school has its own definition — which definition belongs to the narrative historians specifically?`,`Don’t answer with the author’s final verdict; the stem asks for one school’s view.`],labels:{topic:`historiography`},createdAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`rc-p07-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-primary-purpose`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`The debate’s current frontier concerns not whether nudges work but when their use is legitimate`],requiredInference:`Cover all five stages; the purpose is mapping the dispute to its live edge.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B, C opposite; D scope-shift; E too-strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`,choices:[{text:`describe the nudge agenda, the leading objections, and the defenders’ replies, identifying legitimate nudging under ethical guardrails as the debate’s frontier`},{text:`prove that nudges never work, that behavioral economics is a pseudoscience, and that its findings can never legitimately inform public policy`},{text:`argue that governments should replace all regulation with nudges immediately, since mandates and penalties never influence behavior effectively under any circumstances`},{text:`provide a technical manual for designing cafeteria layouts and retirement forms that choice architects could implement without further study`},{text:`demonstrate that autonomy objections to nudging have been definitively refuted and that choice architecture raises no serious ethical concerns for policymakers to consider`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 introduces libertarian paternalism, paragraph 2 its early successes, paragraph 3 the autonomy and empirical objections, paragraph 4 the defenders’ replies, and paragraph 5 the current frontier — when nudging is legitimate under guardrails. Choice A tracks that whole sequence.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Primary purpose must cover the passage’s full movement. The structure here is: exposition (paragraph 1: the nudge idea), encouraging evidence (paragraph 2: nudge units, trials), objections (paragraph 3: autonomy bypass + empirical shrinkage), replies (paragraph 4: architecture is unavoidable; research programs mature), and reframing (paragraph 5: the live question is legitimacy under guardrails). Choice A names each stage. Choice B is the opposite of paragraphs 2 and 4, which report real effects. Choice C inverts the passage’s caution — the author stresses limits and guardrails, not wholesale replacement. Choice D mistakes examples (cafeterias, forms) for the subject; the passage is about the debate, not a how-to manual. Choice E is too-strong: paragraph 5 says the strongest critics remain unsatisfied, so the autonomy objection is contained, not "definitively refuted."`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. It follows the passage’s five-stage movement: exposition, evidence, objections, replies, and the reframed frontier question about legitimacy under guardrails. Five-stage debate passages resolve into reframed frontiers, and the purpose answer must name that reframing.`,`Opposite. Paragraphs 2 and 4 report genuine (if modest) nudge effects and defend the research program; "never work" and "pseudoscience" contradict the passage. Genuine-but-modest effects are the passage’s repeated finding; absolutizing them into zero effect inverts paragraphs 2 and 4.`,`Opposite. The passage emphasizes nudges’ limits, context-dependence, and need for guardrails — the reverse of demanding immediate wholesale replacement of regulation. Limits, context-dependence, and guardrails are the passage’s refrain — the opposite of wholesale replacement advocacy.`,`Scope-shift. Cafeteria layouts and retirement forms are illustrative examples in paragraph 1, not the passage’s subject; no design instructions are given. Illustrative examples are not the subject; mistaking them for it is a scope-shift from instance to topic.`,`Too-strong. Paragraph 5 explicitly notes the guardrails "would not satisfy the strongest critics," so the autonomy objection is managed, not definitively refuted. Managed is not refuted: paragraph 5’s guardrails concede the critics’ persistence rather than claiming victory.`],generalLesson:`Debate passages that end by reframing the question ("the current frontier concerns not whether but when") signal their purpose: mapping the dispute to its live edge, not settling it absolutely.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-primary-purpose`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`List what each paragraph does — does any choice cover all five?`,`Does the author call for replacing all regulation with nudges, or for guardrails?`,`The last paragraph reframes the debate — the purpose answer should reflect that reframing.`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p07-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`Default enrollment works reliably`,`many subtler interventions -- social-norm messages, framing tweaks -- show effects that shrink on replication`],whyDistractorsFail:`A, B name the fragile category; D invented; E contradicts the nudge definition.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`According to the passage, which one of the following is cited as an example of a nudge whose effects have proven relatively reliable?`,choices:[{text:`Social-norm messages about tax compliance`},{text:`Framing tweaks in public-health messaging`},{text:`Automatic enrollment in retirement plans with an opt-out`},{text:`Subliminal advertising in government communications`},{text:`Mandatory savings requirements with no opt-out provision`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 contrasts interventions: "Default enrollment works reliably; many subtler interventions — social-norm messages, framing tweaks — show effects that shrink on replication." Automatic enrollment (a default) is the reliable example; the others are the fragile ones or inventions.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage draws an explicit contrast in paragraph 3 between robust and fragile findings. On the robust side: "Default enrollment works reliably." Automatic enrollment in retirement plans with an opt-out is the paragraph-1 example of a default, so choice C is the cited reliable case. Choices A and B name the fragile side — "social-norm messages, framing tweaks — show effects that shrink on replication or vary unpredictably." Choice D invents subliminal advertising, which the passage never mentions (and which would violate the "preserve freedom of choice" criterion). Choice E contradicts the nudge definition: a mandate with no opt-out is coercion, not a nudge, and paragraph 1 stresses that nudging "leav[es] the individual free to opt out." The contrast structure is the key: reliable = defaults, reminders, simplification (paragraph 4); fragile = subtler psychological tweaks.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 3 places social-norm messages among the "subtler interventions" whose effects "shrink on replication" — the unreliable category, not the reliable one. Sorting each intervention into the reliable or fragile column is the skill this question tests; social-norm messages belong to the fragile one.`,`Opposite. Framing tweaks are listed alongside social-norm messages as effects that "vary unpredictably across settings" — fragile, not reliable. Fragile means shrink-on-replication, and framing tweaks are paragraph 3’s named example of that category.`,`Correct. Default enrollment is explicitly said to "work reliably," and automatic enrollment with an opt-out is the passage’s flagship example of a default. Defaults are the passage’s reliably-effective category, and automatic enrollment is its flagship default example.`,`Unsupported. Subliminal advertising appears nowhere in the passage and would contradict the nudge criterion of preserving freedom of choice. Subliminal influence would violate the opt-out criterion that defines nudging in paragraph 1.`,`Opposite. A mandate with no opt-out is coercion, not a nudge; paragraph 1 defines nudging as preserving the individual’s freedom to opt out. Removing opt-out converts a nudge into coercion, contradicting the definition the whole passage presupposes.`],generalLesson:`When a passage contrasts robust vs. fragile findings, expect EXCEPT-style and example questions to test which side each item belongs to — sort before you select.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 3 draws a contrast — what goes on the "works reliably" side?`,`Which choices name the "subtler interventions" side instead?`,`A nudge must preserve opt-out — which choice violates that definition?`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p07-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`choice architecture is unavoidable`,`pretending otherwise is not neutrality but abdication`],requiredInference:`Unavoidability of steering implies the autonomy objection overreaches -- it proves too much.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B, C invert the defenders’ stance; E is too-strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`The defenders’ reply to the autonomy objection implies that`,choices:[{text:`citizens are incapable of rational deliberation and must be managed by experts`},{text:`any influence on choice is morally equivalent to coercion`},{text:`private actors design choice environments more ethically than governments do`},{text:`the autonomy objection proves too much, because some choice architecture is unavoidable`},{text:`because influence is inevitable, any form of steering is automatically legitimate`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4’s reply: "choice architecture is unavoidable: the retirement form must have some default, the cafeteria some layout, and pretending otherwise is not neutrality but abdication." The implication is that an objection to all steering proves too much — it condemns the unavoidable.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The defenders’ autonomy reply has a specific logical form: the critics object to steering that bypasses deliberation, and the defenders respond that steering is unavoidable — every form has a default, every cafeteria a layout — so the critics’ principle would condemn even the status quo. That is a "proves too much" objection: if the principle rules out the unavoidable, the principle (not the practice) has to give. Choice D states this implication. Choice A inverts the defenders’ stance: they argue for transparent steering in citizens’ interests, not for managing the incapable — and paragraph 5’s guardrails presuppose citizens as equals. Choice B is the opposite of the reply, which distinguishes inevitable influence from coercion. Choice C misreads the "private actors" remark: defenders say influence shouldn’t be "left to chance or to private actors with no such scruples" — i.e., private actors are less scrupulous, not more ethical. Choice E is too-strong: the defenders argue influence should be "wielded transparently and in citizens’ interests" under guardrails (paragraph 5), not that inevitability makes "any" steering automatically legitimate.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The defenders’ position — transparent steering in citizens’ interests, with public disclosure (paragraph 5) — treats citizens as equals to be served, not as incompetents to be managed.`,`Opposite. The reply depends on distinguishing unavoidable influence from coercion; equating them would concede the critics’ case rather than answer it. Collapsing the influence-coercion distinction would hand the critics their conclusion instead of answering it.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 4 says influence should not be "left to chance or to private actors with no such scruples" — private actors are presented as less scrupulous, not more ethical.`,`Correct. The unavoidability argument ("not neutrality but abdication") implies the autonomy objection overreaches: it would condemn choice architecture that cannot be avoided, so the objection proves too much.`,`Unsupported and too-strong. The defenders favor constrained, transparent steering toward ends citizens would endorse (paragraph 5’s guardrails); "any form of steering" and "automatically legitimate" abandon those constraints.`],generalLesson:`"X is unavoidable, so objecting to X proves too much" is a distinctive defensive move — the licensed inference is about the objection’s overreach, not a blank check for the practice.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the defenders’ key premise in replying to the autonomy objection? (One word: unavoidable.)`,`If some steering is unavoidable, what does that imply about an objection to all steering?`,`Watch for choices that turn a limited reply into a blank check.`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p07-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`early enthusiasm outran the evidence`,`the normal lifecycle of a research program, not its refutation`],requiredInference:`Concede the fact, reframe its significance: maturation, not refutation.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B, C opposite; D too-strong and scope-shift.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`The reference to "the normal lifecycle of a research program" in the fourth paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`suggest that policymakers should ignore empirical evidence when designing nudges, relying on theory alone instead`},{text:`argue that behavioral economics should be replaced by a newer research program with fundamentally different methods`},{text:`concede that the empirical objection to nudging is unanswerable and that the entire program should be abandoned`},{text:`prove that all early scientific findings are eventually shown to be false, without exception, in every field`},{text:`characterize the shrinking of some nudge effects on replication as an expected maturation rather than a refutation of nudging`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4’s defenders "concede that early enthusiasm outran the evidence but note that this is the normal lifecycle of a research program, not its refutation." The phrase reframes replication shrinkage as maturation — expected, not fatal.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The phrase appears inside the defenders’ reply to the empirical objection. The critics’ charge: celebrated effects shrink on replication. The defenders’ move has two parts: concede the fact ("early enthusiasm outran the evidence") but reframe its significance ("the normal lifecycle of a research program, not its refutation"). The phrase’s function is that reframing — to normalize the pattern so it doesn’t count as a death blow. Choice E states this. Choice B inverts the move: the point is to defend the program, not replace it. Choice C is the opposite: the reply is designed precisely to show the objection is answerable. Choice D is too-strong and a scope-shift — the claim is about this research program’s maturation, not a universal law that all findings prove false. Choice A is the opposite of the paragraph’s counsel, which is to "follow the strongest findings" and test interventions "rigorously."`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 4 counsels following "the strongest findings" and rigorous pre-deployment testing — evidence-guided policy, not evidence-ignoring policy. Evidence-guided policy — follow the strongest findings, test rigorously — is the paragraph’s counsel, not evidence avoidance.`,`Opposite. The move defends continuing the research program (following the strongest findings), not replacing it with a newer one. The lifecycle remark defends continuing the program, so replacement readings invert its function.`,`Opposite. The defenders introduce the phrase precisely to show the empirical objection is answerable; conceding unanswerability would surrender the argument. Answerable is the point of the reply; reading it as surrender mistakes a defense for a concession.`,`Too-strong and a scope-shift. The phrase concerns this research program’s maturation, not a universal claim that all early findings are eventually falsified. Universalizing a research-program observation into a law of science is the scope-shift this trap relies on.`,`Correct. The phrase reframes replication shrinkage as expected maturation ("not its refutation"), blunting the empirical objection without denying the underlying fact. Concede-the-fact, reframe-its-significance is the defenders’ characteristic move, and this phrase performs the reframing.`],generalLesson:`"Concede the fact, reframe its significance" is a standard defensive move — the function answer names the reframing, not the concession alone.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Whose reply does the phrase belong to, and what objection are they answering?`,`Do the defenders deny that effects shrank, or deny what the shrinkage means?`,`The phrase contrasts "lifecycle" with something — what is the something?`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p07-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-author-viewpoint`,secondarySkills:[`rc-attitude`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`the psychology underlying nudging is real`,`its policy effects are genuine but limited`],requiredInference:`Real psychology + limited effects + guardrails = measured stance.`,viewpointOwner:`the author`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B opposite; D confuses evenhandedness with neutrality; E invented.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward the nudge agenda as a whole is best described as`,choices:[{text:`uncritically enthusiastic, endorsing every nudge intervention ever proposed without any qualification or concern`},{text:`wholly hostile, regarding all nudging as illegitimate manipulation that governments must never employ`},{text:`measured: accepting the underlying psychology and some interventions, but insisting effects are limited and guardrails are needed`},{text:`indifferent, presenting the debate without any evaluative judgment of its own`},{text:`nostalgic for an era before behavioral economics existed as a field`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5’s synthesis — "the psychology underlying nudging is real, ... its policy effects are genuine but limited" — plus the guardrail proposals and the acknowledgment that strong critics remain unsatisfied, shows a measured stance: neither cheerleading nor hostile, and certainly not neutral.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The author’s verdict is paragraph 5’s "maturing recognition on both sides": the psychology is real, effects are "genuine but limited," and legitimacy requires guardrails (disclosure, reflectively endorsed ends, rigorous testing). That is a qualified, discriminating endorsement — measured, not enthusiastic (A) or hostile (B). It is also not neutral (D): the author takes positions throughout, e.g., that the "free lunch ... was smaller than advertised" and that the frontier question is legitimacy under oversight. Choice E invents nostalgia absent from the text. The key discriminator is paragraph 5’s balanced triad — real psychology, limited effects, need for oversight — which only choice C reproduces. An author who grants both sides something and then prescribes constraints is measured — neither cheerleader nor debunker nor neutral.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The author stresses that effects are "genuine but limited," that flashier interventions deserve "appropriate skepticism," and that guardrails are needed — the reverse of uncritical enthusiasm.`,`Opposite. The author accepts the psychology as real and defends constrained nudging; "wholly hostile" and "illegitimate manipulation" describe the strongest critics, not the author. Attributing the critics’ language to the author is a wrong-viewpoint trap; the author’s own synthesis is the measured paragraph-5 triad.`,`Correct. It reproduces paragraph 5’s synthesis: real psychology, genuine-but-limited effects, and ethically constrained practice. Real psychology, limited effects, ethical constraints: the triad is the author’s measured verdict in miniature.`,`Unsupported. The passage is evaluative throughout ("smaller than advertised," "genuine but limited," guardrail prescriptions); presenting both sides is not the same as judging neither. Evenhanded presentation in service of an argument is not neutrality, and the passage’s evaluations are explicit throughout.`,`Unsupported. The passage contains no nostalgia for a pre-behavioral-economics era; its stance is forward-looking (guardrails, better testing). Forward-looking prescriptions about guardrails and testing leave no room for nostalgic readings.`],generalLesson:`An author who grants both sides something ("maturing recognition on both sides") and then prescribes constraints is measured — don’t confuse evenhandedness with either enthusiasm or neutrality.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-author-viewpoint`,`rc-attitude`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What three-part assessment does paragraph 5 offer ("the psychology... the effects... the question")?`,`Does the author sound like a cheerleader, a debunker, or something in between?`,`Which choice preserves all three parts of that assessment?`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p07-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,3,5],supportingSentences:[`Default enrollment works reliably`,`that nudges be publicly disclosed`,`tested rigorously before deployment at scale`],requiredInference:`Checklist the proposal: default mechanism + opt-out + disclosure + published results = model legitimate nudge.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B voices the critics; C opposite; D inverts disclosure; E scope-shift.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p07`,stem:`A city proposes to increase organ-donor registration by changing the driver’s license form from opt-in to opt-out, publicly announcing the change and its rationale, and committing to publish registration results annually. According to the passage’s framework, the author would most likely regard this proposal as`,choices:[{text:`an especially defensible nudge: it uses a reliably effective mechanism (a default), preserves opt-out, and satisfies the guardrails of disclosure and testing`},{text:`illegitimate manipulation, because it bypasses rational deliberation about a matter as serious as organ donation`},{text:`ineffective, because the passage proves that defaults never influence behavior in any domain`},{text:`unacceptable, because the passage argues nudges should never be publicly disclosed to those they affect`},{text:`irrelevant to the passage’s analysis, which concerns only retirement savings, cafeteria layouts, and workplace wellness programs rather than medical decisions`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`The proposal hits everything the passage endorses: defaults "work reliably" (paragraph 3), opt-out preserves freedom of choice (paragraph 1), and public announcement plus published results satisfy paragraph 5’s guardrails (disclosure, rigorous follow-up). It is the model legitimate nudge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Run the proposal through the passage’s criteria. Mechanism: it’s a default, and paragraph 3 says "default enrollment works reliably" — the strongest-evidence category, which paragraph 4 says policy should follow. Freedom: opt-out is preserved, satisfying paragraph 1’s definitional criterion. Guardrails (paragraph 5): publicly announced rationale = disclosure; annual published results = rigorous testing and transparency. Ends: organ-donor registration is plausibly an end citizens would endorse on reflection. Every element the passage’s synthesis requires is present, so the author would regard it as especially defensible. Choice B voices the strongest critics’ position, which paragraph 5 says the guardrails "would not satisfy" — but the author is not among those critics. Choice C is the opposite of paragraph 3. Choice D inverts paragraph 5, which proposes that nudges "be publicly disclosed." Choice E is a scope-shift: retirement and cafeterias are examples, not the analysis’s limits.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. The proposal combines the reliably effective mechanism (defaults), preserved opt-out, and the passage’s guardrails — public disclosure of the intervention and ongoing publication of results.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. This is the strongest critics’ objection, which the author acknowledges but does not adopt; the author’s framework of guardrails is designed to legitimate nudges like this one.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 3 explicitly states that default enrollment "works reliably"; the passage never claims defaults are ineffective. Paragraph 3’s explicit reliability ranking makes any ineffectiveness claim about defaults directly contradictory.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 5 proposes that nudges "be publicly disclosed" — disclosure is a guardrail, not a prohibition. Disclosure is proposed as a guardrail, so treating it as prohibited inverts paragraph 5’s prescription.`,`Scope-shift. Retirement plans and cafeterias are illustrative examples from paragraph 1; the framework (defaults, guardrails, reflective endorsement) applies generally. Examples illustrate a general framework; confining the framework to its examples is a scope-shift.`],generalLesson:`To evaluate a new case under a passage’s framework, checklist it against each criterion the passage establishes — mechanism strength, freedom preserved, guardrails met — rather than reacting to the topic.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:115,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the passage say is the most reliably effective nudge mechanism?`,`Does the proposal preserve opt-out? What does paragraph 5 require beyond that?`,`Check each element — mechanism, freedom, disclosure, follow-up — against the text.`],labels:{topic:`nudges`}},{id:`rc-p08-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`not resources but the quality of institutions that existed before the windfall arrived`,`the probabilistic claim survives`],requiredInference:`Refinement, not refutation: keep the probabilistic core plus the institutional mediator.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A states the crude claim paragraph 4 rejects; B total debunking; D fabrication; E the retired prescription.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Natural resources invariably doom developing countries to slow growth, corruption, and civil war, regardless of their political institutions, policy choices, or historical circumstances.`},{text:`The resource curse has been completely debunked: natural resources never harm economic development, and the entire literature rests on statistical artifacts.`},{text:`The resource curse captures real patterns, but institutional quality mediates resource effects, so the prescription shifts from fatalism to building transparent revenue institutions before windfalls arrive.`},{text:`Norway and Botswana prove that the resource curse never existed, that economists fabricated the supporting data, and that resource wealth always accelerates development in every case.`},{text:`The only solution to the resource curse is for poor countries to leave all natural resources permanently in the ground, forgoing any extraction revenue whatsoever.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The passage presents the curse thesis (paragraphs 1–2), the institutionalist critique with its exceptions (paragraph 3), the defenders’ probabilistic refinement (paragraph 4), and the prescriptive synthesis favoring institutional preparation (paragraph 5). Only choice C holds the thesis, the revision, and the shifted prescription together.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage’s arc: paragraphs 1–2 establish the resource curse and its mechanisms; paragraph 3 introduces the revisionist challenge (Norway, Botswana — institutions, not resources, are the real variable); paragraph 4 has defenders retreat to a probabilistic claim while conceding the crude version is false; paragraph 5 synthesizes into a prescription — transparent revenue management built before pumping begins. Choice C is the only answer containing the original pattern, the institutionalist revision, and the new prescription. Choice A states the "crude claim" paragraph 4 explicitly says is false. Choice B overstates the critique into total debunking, which neither side endorses. Choice D invents data fabrication, a charge the passage never makes. Choice E states the "early curse literature" counsel that paragraph 5 says has been retired in favor of institutional preparation.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong and contradicted: paragraph 4 explicitly calls the crude "resources always harm" claim false, citing Norway and Botswana as counterexamples. The crude universal claim is the one paragraph 4 explicitly declares false, so it cannot anchor the main point.`,`Too-strong in the other direction. The critics argue institutions mediate the effect, and defenders retain a probabilistic claim; nobody in the passage says resources "never harm."`,`Correct. It captures the curse pattern, the institutionalist revision (prior institutional quality as the real variable), and the prescriptive shift from fatalism to preparation. Refinement rather than refutation is the passage’s pattern: keep the probabilistic core while adding the institutional mediator.`,`Too-strong and unsupported. The exceptions qualify the thesis; the passage never suggests the data were fabricated or that the curse "never existed." Qualification is not fabrication; the exceptions discipline the thesis without impugning the data behind it.`,`Opposite of the conclusion. Paragraph 5 says the leave-it-in-the-ground counsel has been retired; the synthesis favors institutional preparation, not permanent non-extraction. Retired prescriptions make effective opposite-traps: the passage moves past leave-it-in-the-ground, so reviving it inverts the conclusion.`],generalLesson:`When a passage refines a thesis rather than killing it ("the probabilistic claim survives"), the main point must preserve the refinement — answers that keep the crude original or declare total victory both fail.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the passage end by telling countries to leave resources in the ground, or something else?`,`What is the "real variable" according to the revisionists?`,`The main point must include both the original pattern and what the debate did to it.`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p08-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`Windfall revenues inflate the currency and draw investment away from manufacturing, hollowing out the tradable sector`],whyDistractorsFail:`B names the conflict channel; C distorts the taxation channel; D invented; E the overall correlation.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`According to the passage, "Dutch disease" refers to the phenomenon in which`,choices:[{text:`resource windfalls inflate the currency and draw investment away from manufacturing, hollowing out the tradable sector`},{text:`civil wars erupt over control of diamond mines, diverting resource revenues from public investment`},{text:`governments become dependent on foreign aid rather than taxation`},{text:`sovereign wealth funds lose value during commodity price collapses`},{text:`resource-poor countries grow faster than resource-rich ones for unrelated reasons`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 defines Dutch disease: "Windfall revenues inflate the currency and draw investment away from manufacturing, hollowing out the tradable sector." Choice A paraphrases it; the other choices describe different curse channels or unrelated phenomena.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage enumerates the curse’s "several reinforcing channels" in paragraph 1, and Dutch disease is the first: currency inflation plus investment flight from manufacturing, hollowing out tradables. Choice A matches. Choice B describes the conflict channel ("armed conflict over control of the prize"), a different mechanism. Choice C distorts the taxation channel: the passage says easy money relieves governments of the need to tax citizens (weakening accountability), not that governments become dependent on foreign aid. Choice D invents a claim about sovereign wealth funds losing value — Norway’s fund is cited as a success, with no mention of such losses. Choice E restates the overall correlation (the paradox of plenty) rather than the specific Dutch disease mechanism. Mechanism-label questions reward matching the label to its exact channel.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This paraphrases paragraph 1’s definition: windfall revenues inflate the currency and pull investment from manufacturing, hollowing out the tradable sector. Currency, manufacturing, tradables: the three-element definition is the precise content the label questions test.`,`Scope-shift. Armed conflict over lootable wealth is a separate mechanism in paragraph 1 ("inviting corruption, patronage, and armed conflict"), not Dutch disease. Mechanism labels are not interchangeable; armed conflict is a distinct channel with its own paragraph-1 description.`,`Unsupported. The passage’s taxation channel concerns governments relieved of taxing their own citizens, weakening accountability — not dependence on foreign aid, which is never mentioned.`,`Unsupported. Sovereign wealth funds appear only as Norway’s success story; the passage never discusses such funds losing value. Norway’s fund appears only as a success illustration; no performance claims about such funds are made.`,`Scope-shift. This restates the overall resource-curse correlation, not the specific economic mechanism labeled Dutch disease. The overall correlation and the specific mechanism are different levels of the argument; this choice confuses them.`],generalLesson:`When a passage lists several mechanisms under one thesis, learn each label’s exact content — distractors will swap mechanisms between labels.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`unsupported`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 1 lists several channels — which one gets the "Dutch disease" name?`,`Dutch disease is the economic channel, not the conflict or taxation channel.`,`Match the label to currency, manufacturing, and the tradable sector.`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p08-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`these cases reveal the real variable: not resources but the quality of institutions`,`resources do not curse countries -- they amplify whatever institutional trajectory a country is already on`],requiredInference:`Exceptions support a "the real variable is X" inference.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A nonsense; B too-strong; C denies the accepted cases; E invented.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`The passage implies that the critics of the resource curse thesis invoke Norway and Botswana primarily in order to show that`,choices:[{text:`oil and diamonds are not actually valuable commodities in the modern global economy`},{text:`statistical studies of economic growth are inherently unreliable and should be disregarded entirely by policymakers`},{text:`no country has ever suffered from the alleged resource curse at any point in history`},{text:`the quality of pre-existing institutions, not resource wealth itself, determines whether resources become a blessing or a curse`},{text:`the resource curse applies only to oil, not to diamonds or copper or other extractive commodities studied`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3: the exceptions "reveal the real variable: not resources but the quality of institutions that existed before the windfall arrived." Norway and Botswana are evidence for the institutionalist revision — resources "amplify whatever institutional trajectory a country is already on."`,explanationWalkthrough:`The critics’ use of the exceptions is explicitly glossed in paragraph 3: "Critics contend that these cases reveal the real variable: not resources but the quality of institutions that existed before the windfall arrived." Norway (oil fund, trusted institutions) and Botswana (diamonds, stable democracy) are invoked not to deny that Nigeria or Venezuela suffered, but to show that suffering isn’t determined by geology — it’s mediated by prior institutions. Choice D states that implication. Choice A is nonsense economics the passage never suggests. Choice B is too-strong: the critics dispute an interpretation, not the reliability of all statistical studies. Choice C overstates — the critics don’t deny the stark cases; they reinterpret what the cases prove. Choice E invents a commodity-specific limitation absent from the text.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage never questions the value of oil or diamonds; the debate concerns what resource wealth does to institutions, not commodity prices. Commodity values are never at issue; the debate is about what wealth does to institutions, not what wealth is worth.`,`Too-strong. The critics challenge the curse interpretation of the data, not the reliability of statistical studies as such. Challenging an interpretation is not challenging statistics as such; the too-strong upgrade invents a broader skepticism.`,`Too-strong. The critics accept the stark cases (Nigeria, Venezuela); they argue the exceptions reveal the real variable, not that no country ever suffered. Reinterpretation, not denial: the critics accept the suffering cases and dispute what the exceptions prove.`,`Correct. Paragraph 3 explicitly draws this moral from the exceptions: pre-existing institutional quality is "the real variable," and resources "amplify whatever institutional trajectory a country is already on."`,`Unsupported. The passage never limits the curse thesis to particular commodities; oil, diamonds, and copper are treated together throughout. Oil, diamonds, and copper travel together throughout the passage; commodity-specific limits are never drawn.`],generalLesson:`Exceptions in an argument usually support a "the real variable is X" inference — identify what the exceptions have in common that the failures lack.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What do Norway and Botswana have that Nigeria and Venezuela lacked, according to paragraph 3?`,`The paragraph names "the real variable" explicitly — what is it?`,`The exceptions don’t deny the suffering; they reinterpret its cause.`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p08-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`The thesis gained influence because the examples seemed so stark`],requiredInference:`Stage-setting: make the thesis formidable so the critique has something to bite on.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, E misplace paragraphs; C opposite; D too-strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`The second paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`refute the resource curse thesis by presenting its most famous counterexamples in full detail`},{text:`establish the thesis’s empirical plausibility with stark examples and statistical studies before the revisionist critique arrives`},{text:`argue that Sierra Leone’s civil war had nothing to do with diamonds or the illicit trade that funded the fighting`},{text:`prove that cross-country statistical studies are methodologically flawless and immune to any criticism whatsoever`},{text:`introduce the institutionalist revision that the rest of the passage develops at length`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 ("The thesis gained influence because the examples seemed so stark") supplies Nigeria, Venezuela, Sierra Leone, and the 1990s cross-country studies — building the case’s credibility just before paragraph 3’s critics attack it. Its job is to make the thesis worth taking seriously.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function follows placement and framing. The paragraph opens by explaining why "the thesis gained influence": stark cases plus statistical confirmation. Nigeria, Venezuela, Sierra Leone, and the 1990s studies are marshaled to show the pattern is real and well-evidenced — which is exactly what makes the subsequent revisionist critique interesting rather than trivial. If the thesis were obviously baseless, the critics’ refinements wouldn’t matter. Choice B captures this stage-setting role. Choice A misplaces the counterexamples, which belong to paragraph 3. Choice C is the opposite of the paragraph’s claim (diamonds "funded a brutal civil war"). Choice D overstates: the studies are said to "appear to confirm," not to be flawless. Choice E misplaces the institutionalist revision, which is paragraph 3’s job.`,choiceExplanations:[`Scope-shift. The famous counterexamples (Norway, Botswana) appear in paragraph 3 as the critics’ evidence; paragraph 2 builds the thesis up rather than refuting it. Paragraph 2 builds the thesis up; the teardown begins only in paragraph 3, so placement determines function.`,`Correct. The paragraph’s framing ("gained influence because the examples seemed so stark") and its stark-cases-plus-statistics content establish the thesis’s credibility before the critique. Formidable targets make interesting critiques: the paragraph’s credibility-building is what gives paragraph 3 something to bite on.`,`Opposite. The paragraph states that Sierra Leone’s diamonds "funded a brutal civil war" — asserting the connection, not denying it. Funded is the paragraph’s verb for the diamond-war link; denying the connection reverses the stated claim.`,`Too-strong. The studies "appeared to confirm the pattern"; the passage claims apparent confirmation, not methodological flawlessness. Appeared to confirm is hedged deliberately; upgrading hedge to flawlessness is a strength-mismatch trap.`,`Scope-shift. The institutionalist revision is introduced in paragraph 3; paragraph 2’s job is to establish what is being revised. What is being revised must first be established; paragraph 2’s job is the establishing, not the revising.`],generalLesson:`In thesis-critique-synthesis passages, the paragraph that piles up supporting evidence right before the critique is doing stage-setting: making the thesis formidable so the critique has something to bite on.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What comes right after paragraph 2? How does paragraph 2 set up that turn?`,`The opening clause explains why the thesis "gained influence" — what work does that do?`,`A critique is only interesting if its target is formidable — which paragraph makes it formidable?`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p08-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`the probabilistic claim survives`,`prove demanding rather than comforting`],viewpointOwner:`defenders of the resource curse thesis`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, B deny facts; C opposite; D inverts the exceptions into disguised decay.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`Defenders of the resource curse thesis, as presented in the passage, would most likely respond to the Norway and Botswana counterexamples by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`Norway and Botswana are not actually wealthy in natural resources`},{text:`the counterexamples are fabricated and the underlying data should be disregarded`},{text:`institutions are irrelevant to economic development`},{text:`Norway and Botswana actually confirm the thesis, because both countries suffered the institutional decay the thesis predicts and their resource wealth merely concealed it from outside observers`},{text:`the probabilistic claim survives, and the exceptions required deliberate institutional engineering that is difficult for most developing countries to replicate under boom pressures`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4: defenders "concede that the crude claim — resources always harm — is false, but argue that the probabilistic claim survives," citing post-discovery governance declines, and add that the exceptions "prove demanding rather than comforting" because they required deliberate engineering "hard to replicate under the political pressures a boom creates."`,explanationWalkthrough:`Strict fidelity to paragraph 4’s two-part reply is required. First, the concession-plus-retreat: the crude universal claim is abandoned ("concede that the crude claim ... is false") while the probabilistic claim is retained ("resource wealth raises the odds of institutional decay"), supported by evidence that windfalls actively degrade governance. Second, the deflation of the exceptions: Norway and Botswana succeeded through "deliberate, difficult institutional engineering — transparency rules, stabilization funds, parliamentary oversight — that most developing countries find hard to replicate under the political pressures a boom creates." Choice E contains both parts. Choice D is an inversion trap: it turns the genuine exceptions into disguised confirmations of decay, the opposite of paragraph 4’s treatment of them as real successes. Choice A denies plain facts. Choice B invents fabrication charges. Choice C contradicts the defenders, who accept institutions matter but argue windfalls degrade them.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage treats Norway’s oil and Botswana’s diamonds as plain facts; no one disputes the resource wealth itself. Resource wealth itself is common ground; the fight is entirely over what it does to institutions.`,`Unsupported. No one in the passage accuses anyone of fabricating data; the dispute concerns interpretation, not data integrity. Interpretation disputes presuppose shared data; fabrication charges would be a different argument entirely.`,`Opposite. The defenders accept that institutions matter — their claim is that windfalls actively degrade governance over time, which presupposes institutions’ relevance. Active degradation of governance presupposes that governance matters, so irrelevance readings contradict the defenders’ logic.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 4 treats Norway and Botswana as genuine successes — exceptions that “prove demanding rather than comforting” — not as disguised confirmations of decay. The defenders concede the crude universal claim and reinterpret what the exceptions prove; they never claim the successes were illusory, so this inversion directly contradicts their stated reply.`,`Correct. It reproduces both parts of paragraph 4’s reply: the retreat to the probabilistic claim (with active-degradation evidence) and the deflation of the exceptions via hard-to-replicate institutional engineering under boom pressures.`],generalLesson:`When two choices both sound right, check which one contains the complete mechanism from the text — near-duplicate distractors usually drop a crucial qualifier.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 4 gives the defenders a two-part reply — what are the two parts?`,`Do the defenders deny the counterexamples, or reinterpret what they prove?`,`The complete answer includes why the exceptions are "demanding rather than comforting."`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p08-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`transparent revenue management, independent oversight bodies, and rules that smooth spending across commodity cycles`,`ideally constructed before the first barrel is pumped`],requiredInference:`Port the prescriptive conclusion: fragile institutions + new windfall = preparation first.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A retired counsel; B opposite; D too-strong; E the core corrosive mechanism.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p08`,stem:`A developing country with weak courts and limited bureaucratic capacity has just discovered large offshore oil reserves. An advisor whose recommendations follow the passage’s concluding synthesis would most likely urge the country to`,choices:[{text:`leave the oil permanently in the ground, since extraction can only end in institutional decay`},{text:`extract as quickly as possible before institutions have a chance to interfere with development`},{text:`establish transparent revenue management, stabilization funds, and independent oversight before extraction begins`},{text:`do nothing, since the passage proves that the resource curse is a myth`},{text:`rely on the oil windfall to substitute for building tax-collection capacity`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5’s synthesis prescribes "institutional preparation: transparent revenue management, independent oversight bodies, and rules that smooth spending across commodity cycles — ideally constructed before the first barrel is pumped." Choice C applies that prescription to the new case.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the concluding prescription to the stipulated facts. Paragraph 5 retires the fatalist counsel ("leave resources in the ground") and replaces it with "institutional preparation ... ideally constructed before the first barrel is pumped": transparent revenue management, independent oversight, and spending-smoothing rules. The hypothetical country has exactly the fragile institutions that make preparation urgent. Choice C is a direct application. Choice A states the retired early-literature counsel the synthesis explicitly moves beyond. Choice B inverts the prescription — racing to extract before oversight exists is the path to the pathologies described. Choice D is too-strong: the passage never "proves" the curse a myth; the probabilistic claim survives. Choice E recommends the very mechanism paragraph 1 identifies as corrosive — windfalls relieving governments of the need to tax, weakening accountability.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite of the synthesis. Paragraph 5 explicitly retires the leave-it-in-the-ground counsel in favor of institutional preparation; this choice revives the retired prescription. The synthesis explicitly retires the fatalist counsel, so choices reviving it are opposite-traps by design.`,`Opposite. Extracting before oversight exists accelerates the pathologies (patronage, weakened accountability) the passage describes; the synthesis demands preparation first. Speed before oversight is the pathology’s accelerant, not its cure; the prescription demands preparation first.`,`Correct. This applies paragraph 5’s prescription — transparent revenue management, stabilization across commodity cycles, independent oversight, built "before the first barrel is pumped" — to the fragile-state case.`,`Too-strong. The passage never proves the curse a myth; paragraph 4 retains the probabilistic claim that windfalls raise the odds of institutional decay. Surviving probabilistic claims are not myths; paragraph 4 retains raised odds of institutional decay.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 1 identifies substituting windfalls for taxation as a core curse mechanism (it severs the taxation-accountability bargain); the synthesis would never recommend it. Severing the taxation-accountability bargain is paragraph 1’s core mechanism, so recommending it inverts the analysis.`],generalLesson:`Prescriptive conclusions ("ideally constructed before...") are directly portable: match the new case’s timing and conditions to the prescription’s stated prerequisites.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 5 prescribe, and when should it be built?`,`Which choices state the retired fatalist counsel rather than the new synthesis?`,`The country’s weak institutions make the prescription more urgent, not less.`],labels:{topic:`resource curse`}},{id:`rc-p09-q1`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`quietly detonated the standard story of human progress`,`converted Sahlins’s suggestion into an indictment`,`the skeletal evidence became a research program`,`turned Diamond’s moral provocation into a structural explanation`,`changing shape with each generation of evidence`],requiredInference:`Main point must name the historical arc: Sahlins -> Diamond -> refinements -> trade-off synthesis.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; C Diamond-only half-right; D ancient-DNA overclaim; E Sahlins misattribution.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Agriculture was an unambiguous triumph for human welfare, and the skeletal evidence cited by its critics has been thoroughly discredited by later bioarchaeological research.`},{text:`The agriculture-was-a-mistake thesis developed in stages — Sahlins, Diamond’s skeletal indictment, later refinements — and now centers on specifying trade-offs rather than a verdict.`},{text:`Jared Diamond’s 1987 essay settled the question of agriculture’s costs, and subsequent decades of research have added nothing of substance to his original indictment.`},{text:`Ancient-DNA studies have proven that farming spread exclusively through migration, fully vindicating the demographic-trap explanation of agriculture’s triumph.`},{text:`Sahlins and Diamond agreed that foraging life should be romanticized and that modern societies ought to abandon farming and return to hunting and gathering.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage traces an idea’s history: Sahlins’s 1960s–70s affluent-forager claim (paragraph 1), Diamond’s 1987 “worst mistake” indictment built on skeletal evidence (paragraph 2), the 1980s–90s research program and its transitional-penalty qualification (paragraph 3), the Boserup-inspired demographic-trap refinement (paragraph 4), and the current move to specifying trade-offs (paragraph 5). Only choice B names that whole arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage is organized as intellectual history, and the main point must capture the trajectory rather than any single station. Paragraph 1 establishes the starting point: Sahlins’s argument that foragers were affluent in the sense of modest wants easily met, which removed the premise that farming triumphed by rescuing humanity from misery — but Sahlins did not himself indict agriculture. Paragraph 2 marks the escalation: Diamond’s 1987 essay converted the suggestion into the “worst mistake” indictment, grounding it in bioarchaeological evidence of shorter, sicker, overworked early farmers. Paragraph 3 shows the indictment maturing into a research program (Cohen and Armelagos, 1984) and simultaneously generating its first major qualification — the transitional-penalty response. Paragraph 4 supplies the second refinement, the demographic trap answering why farming spread despite its costs. Paragraph 5 describes the current synthesis: neither the strong mistake claim nor the old progress narrative survives, and attention has moved to trade-offs, with ancient DNA adding migration to the story. Choice B is the only answer that spans all five movements. Choice A is the opposite of the passage’s evidence. Choice C is half-right — Diamond matters, but the passage insists later research transformed the debate. Choice D overstates the ancient-DNA finding, which the passage presents as a complication, not a proof, and “exclusively” contradicts the text. Choice E misattributes to both authors a romantic prescription neither makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The passage treats the skeletal evidence as the empirical core of Diamond’s case and of the research program it spawned; nothing in the text discredits it, and the transitional-penalty critics qualify rather than dismiss it. An answer that declares the evidence discredited contradicts the passage’s central presupposition.`,`Correct. It names each stage of the idea’s development in order — Sahlins’s thesis, Diamond’s indictment, the two refinements — and captures the endpoint the final paragraph states explicitly: the move from verdict to trade-offs. The historical arc is the passage’s subject, and this choice mirrors its five-paragraph structure exactly.`,`Half-right. Diamond’s essay is genuinely pivotal in the passage’s history, but “settled” and “nothing of substance” contradict paragraphs 3 through 5, which detail the research program, the transitional-penalty qualification, the demographic trap, and the ancient-DNA complication. A main point that erases three of the passage’s five stages is incomplete.`,`Too-strong. The passage says ancient-DNA studies show farming spread through migration “as well as” conversion — a complication, not an exclusive mechanism — and it never claims the demographic trap is vindicated “in full.” “Exclusively” and “proven” are absolutes the passage’s careful hedging does not support.`,`Unsupported. The passage explicitly says Sahlins “was not attacking agriculture directly,” and neither author prescribes a return to foraging. This choice invents both a shared romanticism and a policy prescription the text never attributes to either figure.`],generalLesson:`When a passage traces an idea’s history, the main point must name the trajectory — each stage and the endpoint — not just the most dramatic station.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the passage’s subject — agriculture itself, or the debate about agriculture?`,`Does the final paragraph render a verdict or describe a shift in the questions asked?`,`The main point must span Sahlins, Diamond, and both refinements.`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p09-q2`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`they satisfied their material wants with only a few hours of work a day`,`it could mean modest wants easily met`,`Sahlins was not attacking agriculture directly`],whyDistractorsFail:`A invents prescription; B is Diamond; C opposite; D is Boserup tradition.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`According to the passage, Sahlins’s “original affluent society” thesis holds that`,choices:[{text:`foraging peoples were morally superior to farmers, and modern societies should abandon agriculture and return to hunting and gathering.`},{text:`agriculture was the worst mistake in the history of the human race, as proven by the skeletal evidence of early farmers.`},{text:`hunter-gatherers met their material wants with relatively little labor, so affluence can mean modest wants easily satisfied rather than endless production.`},{text:`skeletal remains demonstrate that early farming populations were taller and healthier than the foragers who preceded them.`},{text:`population growth drove agricultural intensification, trapping societies in a demographic cycle they could not escape.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states Sahlins’s claim directly: foragers satisfied their wants with a few hours of work daily, so “affluence” could mean modest wants easily met rather than endless production. The same paragraph adds that Sahlins was not attacking agriculture directly. Only choice C reproduces both the definition and its limits.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a straightforward detail question, but the distractors are built from the passage’s other stages, so it tests whether the reader keeps the chronology straight. Paragraph 1 gives Sahlins’s thesis in two parts: the empirical observation (foragers meeting material wants with a few hours of daily work, varied diets, abundant leisure) and the conceptual move (redefining affluence as modest wants easily met). It then explicitly limits the thesis: Sahlins “was not attacking agriculture directly” — his target was the premise that farming triumphed by rescuing humanity from misery. Choice C captures the definition precisely. Choice A invents both a moral-superiority claim and a return-to-foraging prescription that the passage explicitly denies. Choice B belongs to Diamond (paragraph 2), not Sahlins — the classic wrong-viewpoint trap in a multi-figure passage. Choice D states the opposite of the skeletal evidence. Choice E describes the Boserup-inspired demographic mechanism of paragraph 4, a full stage later than Sahlins. Readers who blur the passage’s stages will drift toward B or E; the question rewards tracking who said what, when.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage explicitly states that Sahlins “was not attacking agriculture directly,” which rules out both the moral-superiority claim and the return-to-foraging prescription. This choice converts an analytical redefinition of affluence into a romantic policy program the text never attributes to Sahlins.`,`Wrong viewpoint. “Worst mistake in the history of the human race” is Diamond’s 1987 indictment from paragraph 2, not Sahlins’s thesis. In a passage that tracks several thinkers across decades, answers must be matched to the right figure; this one borrows Diamond’s signature phrase.`,`Correct. It reproduces paragraph 1’s two elements: the empirical claim (material wants met with a few hours of daily labor) and the conceptual redefinition (affluence as modest wants easily met). The phrasing stays within the modesty of the original — “can mean” rather than “must mean” — matching the passage’s hedging.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 2 reports that early farmers’ skeletons were shorter, more malnourished, and more disease-ravaged than foragers’. This choice reverses the skeletal evidence that the passage presents as the factual core of Diamond’s case.`,`Wrong viewpoint. The population-driven intensification mechanism belongs to the Boserup tradition discussed in paragraph 4 — two stages after Sahlins. Attributing the demographic argument to Sahlins collapses the passage’s chronology.`],generalLesson:`In multi-figure passages, pin each claim to its author and its stage; distractors routinely transplant later ideas onto earlier figures.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph introduces Sahlins, and what does it say he was — and was not — claiming?`,`Whose phrase is “worst mistake”: Sahlins’s or Diamond’s?`,`The demographic mechanism arrives in paragraph 4, not paragraph 1.`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p09-q3`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`if farming made people worse off, why did it conquer the globe`,`Societies were not persuaded by agriculture’s benefits`,`they were captured by its irreversibility`],requiredInference:`The trap explains the spread of farming despite its costs — the puzzle Diamond’s indictment creates.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is the transitional-penalty question; B is the ancient-DNA complication; C unasked; D asks about Diamond’s motives, not the trap’s target.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`The passage implies that the “demographic trap” argument was developed primarily to answer which one of the following questions?`,choices:[{text:`How many centuries did the transitional penalty in early farmer health actually last?`},{text:`Did farming spread mainly through the migration of farming peoples or the conversion of foragers?`},{text:`Were Sahlins’s observations of contemporary foraging societies methodologically sound?`},{text:`What motivated Diamond to call agriculture the worst mistake in human history?`},{text:`If farming made its early adopters worse off, why did it spread to encompass nearly the entire globe?`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 opens by naming the exact puzzle the trap answers: “if farming made people worse off, why did it conquer the globe?” The mechanism — yield per acre, population expansion, irreversibility — explains spread without appealing to desirability. Only choice E restates that question.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 4 is explicit about the dialectical role of the demographic trap: it “answered the question Diamond’s indictment left hanging,” and the passage states that question verbatim — “if farming made people worse off, why did it conquer the globe?” The trap’s content then matches that question exactly: farming’s advantage was yield per acre rather than welfare, so farming populations grew, displaced or absorbed foragers, and became too numerous to return to foraging. The answer is structural (irreversibility), not evaluative (betterness). Choice E is the direct restatement. The distractors each belong to a different stage of the debate. Choice A asks about the duration of the transitional penalty — the critics’ qualification in paragraph 3, a question about the evidence’s scope, not about farming’s spread. Choice B is the ancient-DNA complication of paragraph 5, which “reframes” the trap rather than motivating it. Choice C raises a methodological worry about Sahlins that the passage never entertains. Choice D asks about Diamond’s personal motives, which the passage never discusses; the trap responds to the logical gap in his argument, not to his biography. The inference is modest — the passage nearly states it — but it requires mapping each question to its proper stage.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong stage. The duration of the transitional penalty is the critics’ qualification in paragraph 3 — a question about how to read the skeletal evidence. The demographic trap of paragraph 4 addresses a different puzzle: not how bad or how long the costs were, but why a costly practice spread globally.`,`Wrong stage. Migration versus conversion is the ancient-DNA complication introduced in paragraph 5, which the passage says “reframes” the demographic trap. It is a later refinement of the trap’s story, not the question the trap was originally developed to answer.`,`Unsupported. The passage never questions Sahlins’s methodology; it presents his observations as the accepted starting point of the debate’s history. This choice invents a controversy the text does not contain.`,`Unasked. The passage discusses Diamond’s argument, not his motivations, and the demographic trap responds to the logical gap his indictment leaves — the unexplained global spread — not to anything about Diamond personally.`,`Correct. The passage states this question nearly verbatim as the one Diamond’s indictment “left hanging,” and the trap’s mechanism — superior yield per acre driving irreversible population growth — is purpose-built to answer it without claiming farming was better for those who adopted it.`],generalLesson:`Refinements in an intellectual history answer the specific puzzle the previous stage created; match each answer to the question its stage was asking.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 4 opens by naming the question it answers — what is it?`,`Which puzzle does Diamond’s indictment leave hanging?`,`The trap explains spread, not health timelines or motives.`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p09-q4`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,viewpointComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`the skeletal evidence became a research program`,`the deeper evidence base also produced the first major qualification of Diamond’s case`,`The debate’s center of gravity shifted`],requiredInference:`P3 both consolidates Diamond’s case (research program) and qualifies it (transitional penalty) — a dual function.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; B belongs to P4; C unsupported; D wrong figure.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`The discussion of the Cohen and Armelagos volume (paragraph 3) functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`refute Diamond’s claim by showing that the skeletal evidence for early farmer ill-health was weaker than he had supposed.`},{text:`introduce the demographic mechanism that explains why farming spread globally despite its heavy costs to individuals.`},{text:`argue that bioarchaeological evidence is too unreliable to support any conclusions about early farmer health.`},{text:`demonstrate that Sahlins’s observations of contemporary foraging peoples were fundamentally mistaken.`},{text:`show how Diamond’s provocation matured into a research program whose success generated the first major qualification of his case.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 does two things at once: the 1984 volume assembles worldwide studies confirming the skeletal pattern — turning Diamond’s provocation into a research program — and that deeper evidence base then produces the transitional-penalty qualification. Only choice E captures this dual function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask what a paragraph does in the passage’s architecture, and paragraph 3 has a deliberately double-edged role in this intellectual history. On one edge, the Cohen and Armelagos volume consolidates Diamond’s position: studies “from every continent” show the same pattern of shrinking stature, dental disease, and anemia at the agricultural transition, which is what the passage means by saying “the skeletal evidence became a research program.” Diamond’s essay is no longer a lone provocation; it has an empirical literature behind it. On the other edge, the passage explicitly says “the deeper evidence base also produced the first major qualification” — the transitional-penalty argument that later farming societies recovered and surpassed forager health. The paragraph thus advances and complicates the indictment in the same movement, shifting the debate’s center of gravity from whether early farming hurt to how long the hurt lasted. Choice E names both edges. Choice A gets the paragraph backwards: the volume confirms, not refutes, the pattern. Choice B belongs to paragraph 4’s Boserup-inspired demographic mechanism. Choice C invents a skepticism about bioarchaeology the passage never expresses. Choice D misattributes the paragraph’s target — Sahlins is not under discussion here.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The volume is presented as confirming the skeletal pattern across continents, which strengthens rather than weakens Diamond’s empirical base. The paragraph’s qualification concerns the evidence’s interpretation (transitional penalty), not its reliability.`,`Wrong paragraph. The demographic mechanism — yield per acre, expansion, irreversibility — is the work of paragraph 4’s Boserup tradition. Paragraph 3 is about the skeletal evidence and its first qualification, not about why farming spread.`,`Unsupported. The passage never questions the reliability of bioarchaeological evidence; it treats the skeletal findings as the debate’s shared factual ground. This choice invents a methodological skepticism the text does not contain.`,`Wrong figure. Sahlins’s observations of living foragers are not revisited or challenged in paragraph 3, which concerns the skeletal evidence and Diamond’s case. The paragraph’s dialectical partner is Diamond, not Sahlins.`,`Correct. It captures the paragraph’s double movement: consolidation (the 1984 volume turning scattered findings into a worldwide research program) and self-generated qualification (the deeper evidence producing the transitional-penalty response). Both halves are stated explicitly in the text.`],generalLesson:`In intellectual-history passages, a single stage often both consolidates and qualifies the previous one; function answers must capture both movements.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does paragraph 3 strengthen or weaken Diamond’s empirical base?`,`What “first major qualification” does the deeper evidence produce?`,`The paragraph does two things — which choice names both?`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p09-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`Few researchers now defend the strong claim that agriculture was simply a mistake`,`Attention has moved to specifying trade-offs`,`showing that farming often spread through the migration of farming peoples as well as the conversion of foragers`],requiredInference:`Current state = neither verdict survives; debate now about trade-off specification, with DNA adding migration.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; B opposite; C contradicts DNA mention; D too-strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`It can most reasonably be inferred from the passage that the author regards the current state of the debate as`,choices:[{text:`a vindication of Diamond’s original indictment, now confirmed by ancient-DNA evidence.`},{text:`a return to the pre-Sahlins view of agriculture as uncomplicated human progress.`},{text:`stalled, since no significant new evidence has emerged in recent decades.`},{text:`settled entirely by the demographic-trap explanation, which answered every outstanding question.`},{text:`a shift from asking whether agriculture was a mistake to specifying the trade-offs it imposed.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 states the current position directly: few defend the strong mistake claim or the old progress narrative; “attention has moved to specifying trade-offs,” and ancient DNA complicates rather than settles the story. Only choice E matches.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The final paragraph is the passage’s verdict on the debate’s present state, and it is carefully balanced. Two negations open it: “few researchers now defend the strong claim that agriculture was simply a mistake, and few defend the old progress narrative either.” That rules out both a Diamond vindication and a return to the pre-Sahlins story. What replaces the verdict-seeking is specified positively: “attention has moved to specifying trade-offs” — individual welfare against collective capability, health against numbers. Ancient DNA is then introduced not as a clincher but as “a further complication,” showing migration as well as conversion. Choice E restates this exactly. Choice A fails twice: the strong claim is explicitly not defended, and DNA is a complication, not a confirmation. Choice B contradicts the second negation. Choice C contradicts the DNA sentence — new evidence has emerged. Choice D overstates the trap, which the passage presents as one refinement among several, subsequently reframed by the DNA findings. The inference is close to the text, but it requires holding both negations and the positive characterization together.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The paragraph explicitly says few researchers now defend the strong mistake claim, and it presents ancient-DNA findings as a complication to the existing story, not as confirmation of Diamond’s indictment.`,`Opposite. The passage states that “few defend the old progress narrative either.” The current state is defined precisely by the rejection of both the original indictment and the original celebration.`,`Contradicted. The paragraph cites ancient-DNA studies as recent evidence that reframes the demographic trap as partly a story of displacement. The debate is described as active and evolving, not stalled.`,`Too-strong. The demographic trap is presented as one important refinement, but the passage goes on to describe the trade-off synthesis and the ancient-DNA complication — developments the trap alone does not explain. “Settled entirely” and “every outstanding question” are absolutes the text rejects.`,`Correct. It reproduces the paragraph’s positive characterization verbatim in substance: the debate’s center has moved from verdict (“was it a mistake?”) to specification (“trade-offs: individual welfare against collective capability, health against numbers”).`],generalLesson:`Final-paragraph syntheses often define the present by double negation (neither old position survives) plus a positive reframing; the inference must hold all three together.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What two positions does paragraph 5 say few researchers now defend?`,`Is ancient DNA presented as settling the debate or complicating it?`,`The paragraph names what attention has “moved to” — what is it?`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p09-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`Diamond called agriculture “the worst mistake in the history of the human race.”`,`the bones documented a transitional penalty, not a permanent verdict`,`like judging industrialization by the slums of the 1840s`],requiredInference:`Map: catastrophe-indictment + later-recovery reply = Diamond + transitional-penalty stage.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A mismatches figures; B is the spread mechanism; C is the DNA complication; D is the current synthesis, not the indictment-reply pair.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p09 to historical-development archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p09`,stem:`Suppose a historian argues that industrialization was “a catastrophe for the workers who lived through it,” citing falling life expectancy in early factory towns, and critics reply that later industrial societies surpassed pre-industrial health. This exchange most closely parallels which stage of the debate described in the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Sahlins’s affluent-forager thesis and Diamond’s popularization of that thesis two decades later.`},{text:`The Boserup-inspired demographic explanation for why farming spread despite its costs to individuals.`},{text:`The ancient-DNA finding that farming spread partly through migration, reframing the demographic trap.`},{text:`The current convergence on specifying trade-offs between individual welfare and collective capability.`},{text:`Diamond’s skeletal indictment of agriculture and the “transitional penalty” qualification raised against it.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The hypothetical has the exact shape of paragraphs 2–3: a catastrophe indictment grounded in bodily evidence (skeletons / life expectancy), met by the reply that the damage was transitional and later societies recovered. Choice E names that pair; the other stages concern different questions.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions test whether the reader has abstracted the debate’s structure, not just memorized its content. The hypothetical is constructed to mirror paragraphs 2 and 3 point for point: an indictment (“catastrophe for the workers”) supported by bodily evidence of early suffering (falling life expectancy, like the skeletal evidence of shorter, sicker farmers), answered by critics who invoke later recovery (later industrial societies surpassing pre-industrial health, like the transitional-penalty claim that later agricultural societies surpassed forager outcomes). The passage even supplies the bridge: the transitional-penalty critics compare judging agriculture by its first millennia to “judging industrialization by the slums of the 1840s” — the analogy is nearly the passage’s own. Choice E is the match. Choice A misdescribes the Sahlins–Diamond relationship (Diamond did not popularize Sahlins’s thesis; he converted it into an indictment). Choice B addresses a different question — why farming spread — which the hypothetical never raises. Choice C is the DNA complication, about migration versus conversion, with no analogue in the hypothetical. Choice D is tempting because the hypothetical involves a trade-off-like weighing, but the passage’s current stage is about specifying trade-offs as the debate’s endpoint, whereas the hypothetical replays the earlier indictment-and-reply exchange specifically.`,choiceExplanations:[`Mismatch. Diamond did not popularize Sahlins’s thesis; the passage says he “converted Sahlins’s suggestion into an indictment” — a sharper, different claim. The hypothetical’s structure (indictment plus transitional-penalty reply) does not map onto the Sahlins-to-Diamond succession.`,`Mismatch. The demographic explanation answers why a costly practice spread globally; the hypothetical exchange is about whether the costs were permanent or transitional. Different question, different stage.`,`Mismatch. The ancient-DNA finding concerns migration versus conversion in farming’s spread. The hypothetical contains no analogue — no new evidence reframing the mechanism, only an indictment and a recovery reply.`,`Near-miss. The current stage does involve weighing costs against capabilities, but the hypothetical’s specific shape — catastrophe claim from early bodily evidence, answered by later-recovery evidence — is the paragraph 2–3 exchange, not the passage’s endpoint synthesis. The analogy must match the exchange’s structure, not just its theme of costs and benefits.`,`Correct. The mapping is exact: bodily evidence of early suffering (skeletons / life expectancy) supporting a catastrophe indictment (Diamond / the historian), met by the transitional-penalty reply (later agricultural / industrial societies recovered and surpassed the baseline). The passage itself invites this parallel with its 1840s-slums comparison.`],generalLesson:`To apply a debate’s structure, match the dialectical shape — claim type plus reply type — not just the topic; each stage of an intellectual history has a distinctive fingerprint.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:120,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What kind of evidence does each side use in the hypothetical?`,`Which two paragraphs of the passage have that same claim-and-reply shape?`,`The passage itself compares the transitional penalty to industrialization — where?`],labels:{topic:`origins of agriculture`}},{id:`rc-p10-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,5],supportingSentences:[`the strong version ... was largely abandoned by linguists decades ago`,`a shared universal foundation -- overlaid with habitual tunings supplied by language and culture`],requiredInference:`Contain the rejection and the qualified replacement together.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A the abandoned thesis; B opposite; D scale error; E the skeptics’ extreme.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Language determines the boundaries of thought, so that speakers of different languages inhabit entirely different cognitive worlds with no shared perceptual foundation beneath linguistic differences.`},{text:`Linguistic research has proven that language has no effect whatsoever on cognition, and every reported Whorfian finding is a methodological artifact.`},{text:`The strong determinist thesis is abandoned, but color, space, and time research supports a modest neo-Whorfian claim: language shapes perception below conscious deliberation, atop a shared cognitive foundation.`},{text:`Russian speakers are cognitively superior to English speakers because their language has more color terms, giving them measurably better reasoning abilities across every tested domain.`},{text:`The only scientifically respectable position is that grammar is merely a vehicle for expressing pre-existing thought, with no influence on cognition at all.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 abandons strong determinism and states the modest thesis; paragraphs 2–4 test it (evidence, skeptical objections, narrowed defense); paragraph 5 synthesizes: universal foundation plus habitual linguistic tuning. Only choice C contains the rejection, the evidence-based modest claim, and the synthesis.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage’s arc: paragraph 1 distinguishes the abandoned strong thesis (language determines thought) from the revived weak one (language "habitually nudges" it); paragraph 2 gives the color/space/time evidence; paragraph 3 gives the skeptics’ methodological objections; paragraph 4 narrows the neo-Whorfian claim to habitual, below-deliberation influence; paragraph 5 synthesizes — "shared universal foundation ... overlaid with habitual tunings." Choice C is the only answer with all three elements. Choice A states the abandoned strong thesis — the opposite of the passage’s position. Choice B is the opposite of paragraphs 2 and 4, which defend real (if small) effects. Choice D invents cognitive superiority; the passage reports millisecond discrimination advantages, not superiority. Choice E states the skeptics’ extreme, which paragraph 4’s narrowed defense and paragraph 5’s synthesis both move beyond.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 1 explicitly says the strong version "was largely abandoned by linguists decades ago as both untestable and implausible." Abandoned-as-untestable is paragraph 1’s verdict on the strong thesis, making its endorsement impossible.`,`Opposite. Paragraphs 2 and 4 defend genuine (if modest) linguistic effects on cognition; "no effect whatsoever" contradicts them. Modest-but-genuine effects are defended in paragraphs 2 and 4; zero-effect claims contradict both.`,`Correct. It captures the abandonment of determinism, the modest habitual-influence claim supported by the color/space/time research, and the final universal-foundation-plus-tuning synthesis. Three elements — rejection, modest replacement, universal-plus-tuning synthesis — make this the complete main point.`,`Unsupported and too-strong. The passage reports faster discrimination of certain shades under specific conditions — milliseconds in lab tasks — not general cognitive superiority. Milliseconds in lab tasks are the passage’s unit of effect; inflating them into general superiority is a scale error.`,`Opposite of the synthesis. Paragraph 5 concludes grammar is "not just tools we use but environments we inhabit" — more than a mere vehicle for pre-existing thought.`],generalLesson:`Passages that replace a strong thesis with a modest one punish both extremes: the main point must contain the rejection and the qualified replacement together.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What happens to the strong version of the thesis in paragraph 1?`,`What weaker claim replaces it, and what does the final paragraph add?`,`Eliminate any choice stating an extreme the passage explicitly abandons.`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p10-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`only when they could silently name the colors to themselves`,`under verbal interference, the advantage vanished`],whyDistractorsFail:`A, C, E invented; B confuses the two boundary conditions.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`According to the passage, the Russian speakers’ advantage in discriminating certain shades of blue disappeared when`,choices:[{text:`the researchers stopped offering financial incentives for fast responses`},{text:`the shades being discriminated did not straddle the Russian light-blue/dark-blue boundary`},{text:`the experiment was conducted in English rather than in Russian`},{text:`subjects were prevented from silently naming the colors to themselves through verbal interference`},{text:`the researchers used a larger sample of English speakers`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 states the advantage held "only when they could silently name the colors to themselves; under verbal interference, the advantage vanished." Choice D restates the condition; the others invent methodological details.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The detail is conditional and precise: Russian speakers were faster at discriminating shades straddling their language’s light-blue/dark-blue boundary — "but only when they could silently name the colors to themselves; under verbal interference, the advantage vanished." Choice D matches. Choice A invents financial incentives, never mentioned. Choice C invents a language-of-instruction manipulation; the interference manipulation concerned subvocal naming, not the experiment’s language. Choice B describes a different boundary condition (the advantage is defined for straddling shades) rather than the interference finding. Choice E invents sample-size details. Note paragraph 4’s use of this same fact: proponents say the disappearance under interference "demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it" — so tracking this detail precisely matters for the argument, not just the question.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. Financial incentives are never mentioned anywhere in the passage; this invents an experimental detail. Invented experimental details like incentives are classic unsupported distractors with no textual anchor.`,`Scope-shift. The advantage is defined for shades straddling the boundary; the vanishing-under-interference finding is a separate, additional result. Two separate boundary conditions should not be confused: straddling defines the effect, interference tests its mechanism.`,`Unsupported. The passage describes no manipulation of the experiment’s conducted language; the interference targeted subvocal naming, not instruction language. The manipulation targeted subvocal naming specifically, not the language in which instructions were given.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 2’s condition: the advantage held only with silent self-naming and "under verbal interference, the advantage vanished." Both the condition and its removal matter: silent naming enables the effect, interference removes it.`,`Unsupported. Sample sizes and their adjustments are never discussed in the passage. Sample-size specifics are never discussed; the passage reports the conditional pattern, not its statistics.`],generalLesson:`Conditional findings ("only when X; under Y, the effect vanished") are high-yield details — record both the condition and its removal, since the argument later depends on them.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The advantage came with a "but only when" qualifier — what was it?`,`What manipulation made the advantage vanish?`,`Don’t confuse the boundary-straddling condition with the interference condition.`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p10-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`exactly what a language-mediated effect should do`,`it demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it`],requiredInference:`A theory predicting disappearance under condition C is confirmed by disappearance under C.`,viewpointOwner:`neo-Whorfians (proponents)`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; B invented; C contradicts; D invented.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`Neo-Whorfians, as presented in the passage, would most likely regard the disappearance of the Russian blue advantage under verbal interference as`,choices:[{text:`a fatal refutation of their theoretical position`},{text:`evidence that the original experiments were fraudulently conducted and their data fabricated`},{text:`irrelevant to the debate, because their claims concern only spatial reasoning rather than color perception`},{text:`proof that English speakers are cognitively superior to others under cognitive load`},{text:`confirmation of the language-mediated mechanism: a labeling-dependent effect should disappear when labeling is blocked`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 states the proponents’ reading explicitly: the disappearance "is exactly what a language-mediated effect should do — it demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it." Choice E restates that logic.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is an inference the passage essentially draws for us, making it a test of viewpoint fidelity. Paragraph 4: "The Russian blue effect disappears under verbal interference, proponents note, which is exactly what a language-mediated effect should do — it demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it." The logic: if the effect works through silent linguistic labeling, then blocking labeling (verbal interference) should remove the effect; its removal therefore confirms, rather than undermines, the proposed mechanism. Choice E states this. Choice A is the opposite — it adopts the skeptics’ hoped-for reading, which the proponents explicitly reject. Choice B invents fraud allegations absent from the text. Choice C contradicts paragraph 2, where color is "the most studied test case" for the neo-Whorfians. Choice D invents a superiority claim no one makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. Paragraph 4 explicitly says the disappearance "demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it" — the proponents’ reading is confirmatory, not fatal. Confirmatory readings of apparent refutations are the proponents’ signature move in paragraph 4.`,`Unsupported. No one in the passage alleges fraud; the dispute concerns interpretation of a replicated pattern, not research integrity. Integrity is never at issue; the entire dispute is about interpreting an accepted pattern.`,`Opposite. Color is described as "the most studied test case" for the neo-Whorfian position; it is central, not irrelevant, to their claims. Most-studied test case is paragraph 2’s description of color, making irrelevance readings contradictory.`,`Unsupported. The passage never compares the groups’ general cognitive standing, let alone under load; this invents a ranking. Group rankings are never constructed; the passage compares conditions, not peoples’ cognitive worth.`,`Correct. This reproduces paragraph 4’s logic: a language-mediated effect should vanish when linguistic labeling is blocked, so the vanishing confirms the mechanism. Mechanism checks that behave as predicted confirm rather than debunk — the logic this question rewards.`],generalLesson:`A result that looks like a refutation can be a mechanism check: if a theory predicts its effect should disappear under condition C, disappearance under C confirms the theory.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 4 say the disappearance "demonstrates"?`,`If an effect works through silent labeling, what should blocking labeling do?`,`The proponents’ reading turns an apparent weakness into evidence — how?`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p10-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`small, fragile, and difficult to replicate`,`with language merely along for the ride`,`a far cry from the grand claim that language shapes worldviews`],requiredInference:`Inventory all three objections, not just the first.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B too-strong; C misplaces; D universalizes; E overstates.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`The third paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`present the skeptics’ methodological objections: fragile effects, uncertain causal direction, and the gap between lab findings and grand claims`},{text:`concede that the neo-Whorfian research program has been definitively refuted by the replication failures described in the paragraph`},{text:`introduce the Russian color experiments for the first time, before the passage has established the neo-Whorfian thesis`},{text:`argue that publication bias is the sole cause of all scientific error, dismissing every other methodological concern raised`},{text:`prove that environment, not language, explains every observed cognitive difference between linguistic communities`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 is the skeptics’ case, with three distinct objections: effects are "small, fragile, and difficult to replicate" (publication bias); the causal direction is uncertain (habitual attention vs. language); and lab findings are "a far cry from the grand claim that language shapes worldviews." Choice A enumerates all three.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 3’s job in the passage’s debate structure is to give the skeptical challenge its full strength before paragraph 4’s neo-Whorfian reply. It advances three objections, not one: (1) methodological fragility — "small, fragile, and difficult to replicate," the expected profile "from publication bias in an exciting research area"; (2) causal direction — the blue advantage might reflect habitual attention or shared environment, "with language merely along for the ride"; (3) scope — even the positive findings show "milliseconds of advantage in laboratory tasks," far from grand worldview claims. Choice A is the only answer containing all three. Choice B is too-strong: the paragraph raises objections, and paragraph 4 answers them — no definitive refutation occurs. Choice C misplaces the experiments, introduced in paragraph 2. Choice D universalizes one objection’s mechanism into "the sole cause of all scientific error." Choice E overstates: the skeptics offer environment as an alternative explanation, not a proof covering "every observed cognitive difference."`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. It enumerates paragraph 3’s three skeptical objections: replicability/publication bias, causal direction, and the lab-to-worldview scope gap. Three objections inventoried together mark the complete function answer; partial lists are half-right traps.`,`Too-strong. The paragraph voices objections that paragraph 4 then answers; "definitively refuted" overstates a challenge into a verdict. Challenges awaiting replies are not verdicts; paragraph 4’s existence proves the debate continues.`,`Scope-shift. The Russian color experiments are introduced in paragraph 2 as the neo-Whorfians’ evidence; paragraph 3 criticizes such findings. Paragraph 2 introduces, paragraph 3 criticizes: misplacing content between them breaks the debate’s structure.`,`Too-strong and scope-shift. Publication bias is invoked as one concern about this literature, not as the sole cause of all scientific error. One concern about one literature universalized into all science is the scope-shift to reject.`,`Too-strong. The skeptics propose environmental/habitual-attention alternatives for the observed effects; "prove" and "every observed cognitive difference" exceed their claims. Alternatives proposed are not proofs delivered; prove and every are the overstatements to catch.`],generalLesson:`Objection paragraphs often contain multiple distinct criticisms — the function answer must inventory all of them, not just the first or most vivid.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`supported-nonresponsive`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How many separate objections does paragraph 3 raise? List them.`,`Does the paragraph declare the program refuted, or leave room for reply?`,`The right answer will have three parts — count them.`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p10-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`not linguistic causation but shared environment`,`with language merely along for the ride`],requiredInference:`Replay the causal-direction objection’s logical form on the new finding.`,viewpointOwner:`the skeptics`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; C, D, E invented.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`Skeptics, as presented in the passage, would most likely respond to the finding that speakers of absolute-direction languages show superior dead-reckoning abilities by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`the finding conclusively proves linguistic determinism and refutes all alternative explanations`},{text:`the superior dead-reckoning more likely reflects habitual attention or shared environmental demands than language causing a cognitive difference`},{text:`dead-reckoning ability is entirely genetic and unaffected by any environmental factor, including training, practice, or cultural habit`},{text:`the researchers must have fabricated their data, since no genuine effect could produce such a result`},{text:`absolute-direction languages are inherently superior to relative-direction languages in every cognitive respect`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3’s causal-direction objection: observed advantages might reflect "not linguistic causation but shared environment or the simple fact that people who habitually attend to a distinction get better at perceiving it, with language merely along for the ride." Choice B applies that objection to the dead-reckoning finding.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply the skeptics’ characteristic move — the causal-direction challenge — to a new instance of the evidence. Paragraph 3 grants the phenomena arguendo and disputes their interpretation: "That Russian speakers discriminate certain blues faster might reflect not linguistic causation but shared environment or the simple fact that people who habitually attend to a distinction get better at perceiving it, with language merely along for the ride." The dead-reckoning finding (paragraph 2) is structurally identical: a cognitive advantage correlated with a linguistic feature. The skeptic’s consistent reply is that the correlation may run through habitual attention or environmental demands rather than linguistic causation. Choice B states this. Choice A is the opposite — it embraces the strongest claim the skeptics exist to deny. Choice C invents a genetic determinism the passage never mentions. Choice D invents fraud allegations. Choice E invents a language-superiority ranking absent from the text.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. "Conclusively proves linguistic determinism" is the strong thesis paragraph 1 says was abandoned; the skeptics are its leading opponents. The abandoned strong thesis cannot be proved by findings its leading opponents cite.`,`Correct. This replays paragraph 3’s causal-direction objection: habitual attention or shared environment, rather than linguistic causation, may explain the correlated advantage. Portable objections keep their logical form across findings; this replays the paragraph-3 template faithfully.`,`Unsupported. Genetic determinism of dead reckoning is never mentioned; the skeptics’ alternatives are environmental and attentional, not genetic. Environmental and attentional alternatives exhaust the skeptics’ repertoire; genetics is never added.`,`Unsupported. The passage contains no allegations of data fabrication; the dispute is interpretive. Fabrication allegations appear nowhere; the dispute is interpretive from start to finish.`,`Unsupported. The passage never ranks languages as superior or inferior; the issue is whether linguistic features cause cognitive differences. Causal questions about features are not rankings of languages; superiority readings miss the issue entirely.`],generalLesson:`A viewpoint’s objection is portable: learn its logical form (here: correlation ≠ linguistic causation; third variables may explain it) and replay it on each new finding.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the skeptics’ standard move against correlated advantages? (Paragraph 3 names two alternatives.)`,`Does that move deny the finding, or dispute its interpretation?`,`Apply the same two alternatives to dead reckoning.`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p10-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`might reflect not linguistic causation but shared environment`],requiredInference:`Vanishing-under-controls fulfills the causal-direction objection’s prediction.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; C inverts; D reversed; E confuses confounding with publication bias.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`RC hostile validation 2026-09-11: KEEP; 0 two-defensible findings; passage claims verified against text; leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p10`,stem:`A new study finds that speakers of a language with obligatory future-tense marking save less for retirement than speakers of a language without it — but the savings gap disappears entirely once national savings institutions and economic conditions are statistically controlled. According to the passage, the skeptics would most likely cite this result as illustrating`,choices:[{text:`a decisive confirmation of strong linguistic determinism, vindicating the neo-Whorfian program against its skeptics`},{text:`the causal-direction problem: the language effect was actually driven by shared institutional factors, with language along for the ride`},{text:`proof that statistical controls are an illegitimate research method that can never support causal conclusions`},{text:`evidence that future-tense marking directly causes national savings institutions to weaken over successive generations`},{text:`the publication-bias problem, since the study was never published in a peer-reviewed journal`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3’s causal-direction objection is exactly this pattern: an apparent linguistic effect that "might reflect not linguistic causation but shared environment," with "language merely along for the ride." When controls for institutions erase the gap, the skeptics’ alternative explanation is vindicated on their terms.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map the new result onto the skeptics’ objection template. The observed pattern: language feature correlates with behavior (savings). The critical test: controlling for third variables (institutions, economic conditions) eliminates the correlation. The skeptics’ paragraph-3 move predicts precisely this: apparent language effects may reflect "shared environment" rather than "linguistic causation." The vanishing gap under controls is therefore, in the skeptics’ framework, an illustration of the causal-direction problem — the confound, not the language, was doing the work. Choice B states this. Choice A is the opposite: the result weakens the linguistic claim. Choice C inverts the skeptics’ methodology — they would welcome controls as clarifying, not condemn them. Choice D reverses the causal arrow into a claim no one makes. Choice E misfires: the study’s publication status is unstated, and the issue illustrated is confounding, not publication bias.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. A gap that vanishes under institutional controls weakens the linguistic-causation claim; it cannot "decisively confirm" determinism. Vanishing-under-controls weakens causal claims; it cannot confirm the thesis it undermines.`,`Correct. This is paragraph 3’s causal-direction objection instantiated: the language effect disappears once shared institutional/environmental factors are accounted for — language was "along for the ride."`,`Opposite. Skeptics rely on precisely such controls to separate causation from correlation; they would endorse the method, not condemn it. Controls are the skeptics’ favored tool for separating causation from correlation, not a method they oppose.`,`Unsupported and causally reversed. No one in the passage claims grammar causes institutions to weaken; the skeptics’ point runs the other way. Reversed causal arrows — grammar weakening institutions — are claimed by no one in the passage.`,`Scope-shift. The result illustrates confounding (the causal-direction problem), not publication bias; the study’s publication status is never stated. Confounding and publication bias are distinct objections; the vanishing gap illustrates the former, not the latter.`],generalLesson:`When a correlation vanishes under controls for third variables, the licensed skeptical inference is confounding — match the new result to the objection whose prediction it fulfills.`,misconceptionTags:[`causal-flaw`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:115,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What happens to the language effect when institutions are controlled for?`,`Which paragraph-3 objection predicts exactly this pattern?`,`The skeptics’ slogan for this situation: language was "along for the ride."`],labels:{topic:`linguistic relativity`}},{id:`rc-p11-q1`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`more than one of them appears to be right — in different cases`,`treat these four as a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds`,`which fits a given case`],requiredInference:`Main point = survey of four accounts + toolkit framing.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; C overstates equivalence claim; D opposite; E wrong-viewpoint.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Kin selection has been decisively refuted by documented cases of cooperation among genetically unrelated individuals in nature.`},{text:`Four accounts of altruism — kin selection, reciprocal altruism, group selection, costly signaling — are increasingly treated as complementary tools for different cases rather than rival doctrines.`},{text:`The four accounts of altruism are mathematically equivalent descriptions, so biologists may use their theoretical vocabularies interchangeably without any loss of explanatory meaning in practice.`},{text:`Altruism remains fundamentally unexplained because each proposed account fails against the evidence from its own flagship cases.`},{text:`Costly signaling theory has superseded the other three accounts as the only explanation consistent with every observed case of animal altruism in nature.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage’s structure is a survey: paragraph 1 poses the puzzle and previews that “more than one” explanation is right “in different cases”; paragraphs 2–5 present kin selection, reciprocal altruism, group selection, and costly signaling with their flagship evidence; paragraph 6 reframes them as a toolkit. Only choice B captures both the survey and the toolkit conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Because the passage is a survey rather than a debate, the main point must do two things: enumerate the landscape and report the field’s current attitude toward it. Paragraph 1 establishes the puzzle (ants, squirrels, bats) and the thesis that multiple explanations are each right in different cases. Paragraphs 2 through 5 then deliver the survey proper: kin selection with Hamilton’s rule and the sterile castes; reciprocal altruism with Trivers and the score-keeping vampire bats; group selection with Wilson’s 2010 revival and its critics’ equivalence objection; costly signaling with Zahavi and the audience effect in babblers. Paragraph 6 converts the survey into a conclusion: researchers treat the four as “a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds,” asking which fits a given case. Choice B is the only answer containing both elements. Choice A is the opposite of the passage’s treatment — kin selection remains “the default for aid among relatives.” Choice C overstates a claim the passage attributes only to group selection’s critics, and only about group versus kin formalisms. Choice D contradicts the flagship-case successes the passage documents. Choice E elevates one account to exclusivity the passage never grants.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The passage presents kin selection as “the most influential account” and “the default for aid among relatives,” with its flagship cases intact. Cooperation among non-relatives is handled by the other accounts, not treated as refuting kin selection.`,`Correct. It enumerates all four accounts in the passage’s order and captures the concluding reframing: complementary tools for different cases rather than rival doctrines competing for a single truth. Both the survey structure and the toolkit thesis are stated in the text.`,`Too-strong. Mathematical equivalence is claimed only by critics of group selection, and only as between group-selection and kin-selection formalisms — not across all four accounts, and not endorsed by the passage. “Interchangeably without loss” goes well beyond the text.`,`Opposite. Each account is presented with flagship evidence that succeeds: sterile castes for kin selection, score-keeping bats for reciprocal altruism, and so on. The passage’s puzzle is abundance of explanations, not absence.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Costly signaling is presented as the fourth account, suited to cases “where generosity tracks an audience” — one tool among four. The passage never ranks it above the others or grants it exclusive explanatory power.`],generalLesson:`For survey passages, the main point must enumerate the surveyed positions and report the field’s stance toward them — here, complementarity rather than rivalry.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the passage stage a two-sided debate or map several positions?`,`What does the final paragraph say researchers now do with the four accounts?`,`An answer naming only one account cannot be the main point of a survey.`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p11-q2`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`provided the benefit to relatives, discounted by relatedness, exceeds the cost`,`rB > C`,`The account’s showcase is the sterile castes`],whyDistractorsFail:`A invented; B group selection; C reciprocal altruism; D costly signaling.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`According to the passage, Hamilton’s rule states that altruism toward kin evolves when`,choices:[{text:`the number of offspring an individual produces exceeds the number of relatives it assists.`},{text:`groups containing many altruists outcompete groups composed mainly of selfish individuals.`},{text:`cheaters can be reliably detected and punished across repeated encounters.`},{text:`the behavior is performed before an audience of potential mates or allies.`},{text:`the benefit to relatives, discounted by their degree of relatedness, exceeds the cost to the actor.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 states Hamilton’s rule explicitly: altruism toward kin evolves when rB > C — the benefit to relatives, discounted by relatedness, exceeds the cost to the actor. Each distractor describes a different account’s mechanism. Only choice E reproduces the rule.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 2 presents kin selection as the passage’s first and most influential account, and it states Hamilton’s rule twice — once in prose (“the benefit to relatives, discounted by relatedness, exceeds the cost”) and once in symbols (rB > C). The rule’s three terms are each defined: r is relatedness, B the benefit to the recipient, C the cost to the actor. Choice E restates the prose version exactly. The distractors are constructed as viewpoint traps, each importing the mechanism of one of the passage’s other three accounts — a natural hazard in a survey passage. Choice B is group selection’s between-group competition (paragraph 4). Choice C is reciprocal altruism’s cheater-detection mechanism (paragraph 3). Choice D is costly signaling’s audience requirement (paragraph 5). Choice A invents a comparison between offspring counts and relatives assisted that appears nowhere in the text. The question therefore tests both recall of the rule and the ability to keep the four accounts’ mechanisms distinct.`,choiceExplanations:[`Invented. The passage defines Hamilton’s rule in terms of benefit, relatedness, and cost (rB > C); it never compares an individual’s offspring count to the number of relatives assisted. This choice assembles technical-sounding quantities the text does not contain.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Between-group competition — groups of altruists outcompeting groups of egoists — is the mechanism of multilevel or group selection from paragraph 4, not of Hamilton’s kin-selection rule.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Detecting and punishing cheaters across repeated encounters is the mechanism of reciprocal altruism from paragraph 3 (Trivers; the score-keeping bats), not Hamilton’s rule.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Performing generosity before an audience of potential mates or allies is the mechanism of costly signaling from paragraph 5 (Zahavi), not Hamilton’s rule.`,`Correct. It reproduces the passage’s prose statement of the rule exactly: benefit to relatives, discounted by the degree of relatedness, exceeding the cost to the actor — rB > C with each term as defined in paragraph 2.`],generalLesson:`In survey passages, detail questions often trap by transplanting one position’s mechanism onto another; verify the mechanism belongs to the named account.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph presents Hamilton’s rule, and what are its three terms?`,`Each distractor describes a real mechanism — but from which account?`,`rB > C: what do r, B, and C stand for?`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p11-q3`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,viewpointComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`donors keep score`,`individuals that accept blood without ever donating are eventually refused`,`only memory, repeated interaction, and the capacity to withhold future help`],requiredInference:`Reciprocal altruism explanation of bats = score-keeping exchange, not kinship or display.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A signaling; B kin selection; C group selection; D contradicts score-keeping.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`A proponent of reciprocal altruism, as described in the passage, would most likely explain blood-sharing among vampire bats as`,choices:[{text:`a conspicuous display of physical vigor intended to attract mates and impress potential allies.`},{text:`aid directed preferentially toward close genetic relatives sharing the same roost.`},{text:`behavior favored because bat colonies with many sharers outcompete rival colonies of non-sharers.`},{text:`an instinctive response triggered by hunger cues, unaffected by any bat’s past behavior.`},{text:`an exchange sustained by memory and withheld future help: bats that take without ever giving are eventually refused.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 makes the vampire bat the textbook case of reciprocal altruism: no kinship required, only memory, repeated interaction, and the capacity to withhold help — “donors keep score” and perpetual takers “are eventually refused.” Only choice E describes that mechanism.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Viewpoint questions in a survey passage require inhabiting one position while the distractors speak in the voices of the others. Paragraph 3 defines reciprocal altruism’s requirements precisely: repeated encounters, memory, cheater detection, and punishment by withholding future help — with kinship explicitly not required. The vampire bat case is then described in exactly those terms: donors “keep score,” and bats that accept blood without donating are “eventually refused.” Choice E restates the mechanism completely. Choice A ventriloquizes costly signaling (paragraph 5): display, vigor, attracting mates — the audience logic, which the bat case does not involve. Choice B ventriloquizes kin selection: the passage explicitly says “no kinship is required” for reciprocal altruism. Choice C ventriloquizes group selection: between-colony competition, which paragraph 3 never invokes for the bats. Choice D contradicts the paragraph’s central detail — the bats’ behavior is the opposite of past-insensitive instinct; score-keeping is the whole point. The item earns its difficulty from the viewpoint discipline: four of the five choices are true descriptions of something in the passage, and only one belongs to the asked-about account.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong viewpoint. Display of vigor to attract mates and allies is costly signaling’s logic from paragraph 5 (Zahavi; the babblers). The bat case in paragraph 3 involves score-keeping and refusal, not audience-directed display.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Preferential aid to close relatives is kin selection’s mechanism from paragraph 2. Paragraph 3 explicitly states that reciprocal altruism requires “no kinship” — only memory, repeated interaction, and punishable cheating.`,`Wrong viewpoint. Colonies of sharers outcompeting other colonies is group selection’s between-group logic from paragraph 4. The bat explanation in paragraph 3 operates entirely within the roost, through dyadic score-keeping.`,`Contradicted. Paragraph 3’s signature detail is that donors “keep score” and refuse perpetual takers — behavior exquisitely sensitive to past conduct. An instinct unaffected by past behavior is the opposite of the described mechanism.`,`Correct. It names all three of paragraph 3’s requirements — memory, repeated interaction, withholding future help as punishment — and the bat-specific outcome: takers who never give are eventually refused. This is reciprocal altruism’s fingerprint.`],generalLesson:`To answer viewpoint questions in survey passages, list each position’s proprietary mechanism first, then match; surface plausibility is not enough.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What three things does paragraph 3 say reciprocal altruism requires?`,`Is kinship among them?`,`Each distractor is true of some account — which one is true of this account?`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p11-q4`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`remove the onlookers, and the generosity should fade`,`not sacrificing for kin or banking future reciprocation`,`demonstrating vigor to an audience`],requiredInference:`The audience effect is unique to signaling: the other accounts predict no audience dependence.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A kin selection; B reciprocal altruism; C group-selection critics; D invented.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`The passage implies that the prediction that distinguishes costly signaling from the other three accounts is that`,choices:[{text:`the altruistic behavior should occur only among closely related individuals.`},{text:`individuals that fail to reciprocate should be excluded from future exchanges.`},{text:`the mathematics of the account should prove formally equivalent to kin selection.`},{text:`the behavior should become more frequent as group size decreases.`},{text:`the altruistic behavior should diminish when there is no audience to observe it.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 defines costly signaling’s unique prediction as the audience effect: generosity functions as a display of vigor to onlookers, so “remove the onlookers, and the generosity should fade.” None of the other accounts predicts audience dependence. Only choice E states it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This inference requires comparing the four accounts’ predictions and isolating what is proprietary to one. Paragraph 5 presents costly signaling’s logic — conspicuous generosity as honest advertisement of quality, attracting mates and allies — and then explicitly marks its distinguishing prediction: “The prediction that distinguishes this account is its audience effect: remove the onlookers, and the generosity should fade.” The inference is that the other three accounts carry no such prediction: kin selection depends on relatedness, not observers; reciprocal altruism depends on the partner’s memory, not third parties; group selection depends on group composition, not audiences. Choice E states the audience effect directly. Choice A is kin selection’s domain condition, not a prediction of signaling. Choice B is reciprocal altruism’s cheater-punishment mechanism. Choice C is the critics’ equivalence objection to group selection from paragraph 4. Choice D invents a group-size prediction the passage never makes. The difficulty is honest: the passage nearly states the answer, but reaching it requires holding all four accounts’ logics in mind simultaneously and checking each against the candidate prediction.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong account. Occurrence only among close relatives is kin selection’s condition from paragraph 2, where Hamilton’s rule governs aid to kin. Costly signaling explicitly operates beyond kin — the babblers’ audience includes potential mates and allies, not just relatives — so a relatives-only prediction belongs to a different account entirely.`,`Wrong account. Excluding non-reciprocators from future exchanges is reciprocal altruism’s punishment mechanism from paragraph 3, where donors keep score and refuse perpetual takers. Costly signaling’s payoff is reputational — mates and allies attracted by display — not the restoration of an exchange relationship.`,`Wrong account. Formal equivalence to kin selection is the critics’ objection to group selection in paragraph 4, not a prediction of costly signaling. This choice transplants another paragraph’s controversy.`,`Invented. The passage never discusses group size as a variable for any of the four accounts, let alone as costly signaling’s distinguishing prediction. A trend in which generosity frequency varies with group size appears nowhere in the text and follows from none of the accounts’ stated logics.`,`Correct. It restates paragraph 5’s explicitly marked distinguishing prediction: because the generosity is a display to an audience, removing the onlookers should make it fade — a consequence none of the other three accounts predicts.`],generalLesson:`A survey’s “distinguishing prediction” for one position is found by checking which consequence follows from its logic alone and from no other position’s.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 5 say “distinguishes this account”?`,`Do kin selection, reciprocal altruism, or group selection predict any audience effect?`,`The answer is the consequence that follows from signaling logic alone.`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p11-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[6],supportingSentences:[`treat these four as a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds`,`The productive question has shifted from which account is true to which fits a given case`],requiredInference:`Final paragraph reframes: complementarity + new question.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A opposite; B unsupported; C misstates; D too narrow.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`The final paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`argue that the accumulated evidence has now resolved the long debate decisively in favor of kin selection.`},{text:`introduce a fifth explanatory account of altruism that supersedes the previous four.`},{text:`concede that none of the four accounts can adequately explain the evolution of human cooperation.`},{text:`summarize the empirical evidence supporting multilevel selection over its rivals.`},{text:`reframe the four accounts as complementary tools and name the researchers’ new question: which account fits a given case.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph explicitly reframes the four as “a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds” and states the field’s new question: not which account is true, but “which fits a given case.” Only choice E captures both the reframing and the new question.`,explanationWalkthrough:`In a survey passage, the final paragraph typically delivers the author’s verdict on the surveyed landscape, and here that verdict is a reframing. The paragraph opens with the toolkit metaphor — “treat these four as a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds” — which recasts the preceding four paragraphs from a contest into a repertoire. It then assigns each tool its domain: kin selection for aid among relatives, reciprocal altruism for stable non-kin exchange, group selection where group structure does causal work, costly signaling where generosity tracks an audience. The paragraph closes by naming the field’s productive question going forward: “which fits a given case,” with human cooperation as the hard case implicating all four. Choice E contains both halves of this function. Choice A is the opposite: kin selection is one tool among four, not the victor. Choice B invents a fifth account the paragraph never introduces. Choice C misreads the human-cooperation sentence, which says the hardest cases “may implicate all four at once” — the opposite of a concession of failure. Choice D is too narrow: group selection gets one clause in a paragraph about all four.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The paragraph demotes kin selection from sole champion to one tool among four — “the default for aid among relatives,” not the debate’s winner. A toolkit has no victor.`,`Unsupported. No fifth account is introduced; the paragraph works entirely with the four already surveyed, assigning each its domain. This choice invents content the text does not contain.`,`Misstates. The paragraph says hard cases like human cooperation “may implicate all four at once” — an embarrassment of explanatory riches, not a concession that none applies.`,`Too narrow. Group selection receives a single clause (“for cases where group structure does visible causal work”) within a paragraph whose subject is all four accounts and the field’s new question. This choice mistakes a part for the whole.`,`Correct. It captures the paragraph’s two moves: the reframing (toolkit, not rival creeds, with each account’s domain specified) and the forward-looking question it enables (which account fits a given case).`],generalLesson:`Survey closers typically reframe rather than crown: the function answer must name the reframing and the new question it opens.`,misconceptionTags:[`half-right`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the final paragraph declare a winner among the four?`,`What metaphor does it use for the four accounts?`,`The paragraph ends by naming the field’s new question — what is it?`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p11-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3,5],supportingSentences:[`donors keep score`,`individuals that accept blood without ever donating are eventually refused`,`only memory, repeated interaction, and the capacity to withhold future help`],requiredInference:`Non-kin + repeated exchange + punishment of skippers = reciprocal altruism’s fingerprint.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A wrong (non-kin); B near-miss (no audience/reputation payoff); C no between-group evidence; D false.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p11 to survey archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p11`,stem:`Researchers observe a bird species in which unrelated neighbors take turns standing guard while others feed, and individuals that skip their turns are later denied access to shared feeding sites. This pattern would most strongly support which account described in the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Kin selection, because guard duty increases the reproductive success of neighboring birds.`},{text:`Costly signaling, because standing guard is conspicuous behavior performed in full view of the other birds.`},{text:`Group selection, because neighborhoods with reliable guards will outcompete neighborhoods without them.`},{text:`None of the accounts, because the birds described are genetically unrelated to one another.`},{text:`Reciprocal altruism: non-relatives engage in repeated turn-taking, and non-contributors are punished by exclusion.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The case matches reciprocal altruism’s fingerprint from paragraph 3 exactly: non-relatives (no kinship required), repeated exchange (turn-taking), and cheater punishment (skippers denied feeding sites) — the bat case’s “donors keep score” logic. Only choice E names all three elements.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application items test whether the reader can diagnose a new case using the passage’s categories, and this one is built so that each distractor invokes a genuine account while missing the case’s diagnostic features. The observed pattern has three signature elements: the birds are unrelated (ruling out kin selection’s requirement), they alternate roles over time (repeated exchange), and skippers are punished by exclusion (cheater detection). That is paragraph 3’s reciprocal altruism point for point — Trivers’s requirements of memory, repeated interaction, and withheld future help, illustrated by the score-keeping bats. Choice E names all three. Choice A fails on relatedness: kin selection needs beneficiaries to carry shared genes, and the birds are explicitly unrelated. Choice B is the designed near-miss: guard duty is indeed conspicuous, but costly signaling requires the payoff to be reputational — attracting mates or allies through display — with the audience effect as its signature prediction; here the mechanism is score-keeping and exclusion, not display, and no audience-dependent payoff is described. Choice C invokes between-group competition for which the observation provides no evidence — no neighborhoods are compared. Choice D is false on the passage’s terms: reciprocal altruism was developed precisely for non-kin.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong account. Kin selection requires the beneficiaries to share genes with the actor; the birds are explicitly described as unrelated. “Benefits the neighbors’ reproductive success” is not enough — Hamilton’s rule needs relatedness, which is absent.`,`Near-miss. Conspicuousness alone does not make a behavior costly signaling: the account requires the payoff to be reputational (mates or allies attracted by the display) and predicts the audience effect. The observed mechanism — turn-taking with exclusion of skippers — is score-keeping and punishment, reciprocal altruism’s signature, not display.`,`Unsupported. Group selection requires evidence of between-group competition — neighborhoods with guards outcompeting those without. The observation describes punishment within a neighborhood, not differential success between neighborhoods.`,`False. The passage presents reciprocal altruism precisely as the account developed for cooperation among non-relatives, with the vampire bat as its textbook case. Unrelatedness rules out kin selection’s mechanism, not explanation altogether — it is the condition that points toward reciprocity.`,`Correct. It identifies all three diagnostic features — non-kin participants, repeated turn-taking exchange, and punishment of non-contributors by exclusion — which together constitute reciprocal altruism’s fingerprint as defined in paragraph 3.`],generalLesson:`Diagnose new cases by checking each account’s necessary conditions (relatedness? repetition? audience? between-group competition?) rather than by surface resemblance.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:120,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which of the four accounts is built for non-relatives?`,`What happens to cheaters in the bat case — and in the bird case?`,`Conspicuous is not the same as reputational: what payoff does signaling require?`],labels:{topic:`evolution of altruism`}},{id:`rc-p12-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-primary-purpose`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,5],supportingSentences:[`the early, triumphalist version of the Yellowstone story oversold what was known`,`what counts as sufficient evidence for action under uncertainty`],requiredInference:`The purpose must span the narrative (P1), the complications (P2-3), the defense (P4), and the epistemological framing (P5).`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is too-strong (confounded, not nonexistent); B mistakes debate for manual; C takes the skeptics’ side as the author’s verdict; D is the opposite of P5’s oversold verdict.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`,choices:[{text:`prove that wolf reintroduction had no ecological effects whatsoever in Yellowstone, contrary to the popular narrative`},{text:`provide a field guide to identifying trophic cascades in terrestrial ecosystems for use by field researchers`},{text:`argue that rewilding projects should be halted until laboratory-grade evidence is available, given the risks of acting on incomplete data`},{text:`celebrate the return of wolves to Yellowstone as an unqualified scientific triumph and a model for future interventions`},{text:`recount the Yellowstone wolf narrative and the scientific skepticism about cascade claims, framing their dispute as one about sufficient evidence for action`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 tells the popular Yellowstone story, paragraph 2 the scientific complications, paragraph 3 the "novel ecosystem" critique, paragraph 4 the defenders’ reply, and paragraph 5 frames the disagreement as epistemological — laboratory standards vs. convergent imperfect evidence weighed against inaction. Only choice E spans that arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage’s structure: paragraph 1 recounts the famous narrative (wolves → elk → willows → beavers → rivers); paragraph 2 complicates it (confounding factors: drought, winters, hunting, bison; unreplicated "ecology of fear" findings); paragraph 3 adds the critics’ deeper point (rewilding yields novel ecosystems, not restored pasts); paragraph 4 gives the defenders’ reply (convergent evidence, costs of waiting, cultural value); paragraph 5 abstracts the dispute to epistemology — "what counts as sufficient evidence for action under uncertainty." Choice E is the only answer containing the narrative, the skepticism, and the framing move. Choice A is too-strong: the passage says the wolf’s contribution is hard to disentangle, not nonexistent. Choice B mistakes the subject for a manual; no identification guidance is given. Choice C takes the skeptics’ side as the author’s verdict, which paragraph 5 withholds. Choice D is the opposite: the passage says the early story "oversold what was known."`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The passage argues the wolf’s contribution is confounded and hard to isolate, not that reintroduction had "no ecological effects whatsoever." Confounded and hard to isolate is not nonexistent; the passage’s caution is evidential, not ontological.`,`Scope-shift. The passage debates cascade claims; it never instructs readers in identifying cascades in the field. Debates are not manuals; no identification guidance is offered anywhere in the passage.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. Halting projects pending perfect evidence is the skeptics’ position; paragraph 5 presents it as one side of an unresolved epistemological dispute, not the author’s verdict.`,`Opposite. The passage explicitly states that the early triumphalist story "oversold what was known" — the reverse of unqualified celebration. Oversold is the passage’s verdict on the early story, the direct opposite of unqualified celebration.`,`Correct. It tracks the full movement: the narrative (1), the scientific complications (2–3), the defense (4), and the final framing as a dispute over evidentiary standards under uncertainty (5).`],generalLesson:`When a passage ends by abstracting a concrete dispute to a methodological principle, the purpose answer must include that abstraction — it’s the payoff of the whole piece.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-primary-purpose`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the last paragraph say the debate "ultimately turns on"?`,`Does the author declare the wolves had no effect, or something more careful?`,`The purpose answer must reach the final paragraph’s level of abstraction.`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p12-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`The concept’s strongest evidence comes from relatively simple systems`,`sea otters, urchins, and kelp forests`],whyDistractorsFail:`A, D, E invent unmentioned evidence types; C inverts the ranking (Yellowstone is the famous but messier case).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`According to the passage, the strongest scientific evidence for trophic cascades comes from`,choices:[{text:`computer models simulating Pleistocene ecosystems and predator-prey dynamics`},{text:`relatively simple systems such as those involving sea otters, urchins, and kelp forests`},{text:`the reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone National Park in the 1990s`},{text:`laboratory experiments on captive wolf packs in controlled settings`},{text:`historical accounts of predator extinctions across early modern Europe`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2: "The concept’s strongest evidence comes from relatively simple systems — sea otters, urchins, and kelp forests, for example — where removing the predator produces dramatic, well-documented transformations." Yellowstone is presented as the messier, contested case.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage explicitly ranks the evidence: the "strongest evidence" comes from "relatively simple systems," with sea otters/urchins/kelp as the example, while "large terrestrial mammals present a far messier picture." Choice B matches. Choice A invents computer models of the Pleistocene. Choice C inverts the ranking: Yellowstone is the popular story but the scientifically contested case, confounded by drought, winters, hunting, and bison. Choice D invents captive-wolf lab experiments. Choice E invents European historical accounts. The trap is the vividness of the Yellowstone narrative — the passage’s point is precisely that the most famous case is not the strongest evidence. The trap is vividness: the most famous case is not the strongest evidence, and the passage explicitly ranks simple systems above messy Yellowstone.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. Computer models of Pleistocene ecosystems are never mentioned in the passage. Pleistocene models are never mentioned; the strong evidence comes from living simple systems.`,`Correct. Paragraph 2 identifies "relatively simple systems — sea otters, urchins, and kelp forests" as the source of the concept’s strongest evidence. Otters, urchins, kelp: the named trio is the evidential gold standard against which Yellowstone is measured.`,`Opposite ranking. Yellowstone is the famous but scientifically "messier" case, confounded by multiple factors — the passage contrasts it with the stronger simple-system evidence. Famous is not strongest: the passage’s explicit ranking puts messy Yellowstone below the simple systems.`,`Unsupported. No laboratory experiments on captive wolves appear in the passage. Captive-wolf experiments are never mentioned; the evidence discussed is field-observational. The case the passage discusses is a wild reintroduction tracked in the field, not a controlled laboratory manipulation.`,`Unsupported. Historical European predator extinctions are never discussed. European predator extinctions are never discussed anywhere in the passage, giving this distractor no textual anchor whatsoever.`],generalLesson:`Passages often contrast the most famous example with the strongest evidence — don’t let vividness substitute for the passage’s explicit evidential ranking.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`opposite`,`keyword-match`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Find the phrase "strongest evidence" — what system does it point to?`,`Is Yellowstone presented as the strongest evidence or as the messier case?`,`Fame and evidential strength are different things in this passage.`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p12-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`cannot recreate the ecosystem of 1900`,`not a restored past but a novel ecosystem`],requiredInference:`Changed background conditions (warmer, drier, more visited) make restoration impossible and novelty inevitable.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B and D are opposites of P3’s conclusion; C misattributes the defenders’ P4 argument to the critics; E invents a natural-return claim.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`The passage implies that, on the critics’ view, rewilding efforts`,choices:[{text:`produce novel ecosystems rather than restored historical ones, since the background conditions of the historical baseline no longer exist`},{text:`are always ecologically beneficial regardless of local conditions or historical context`},{text:`should be evaluated solely on their cultural and ethical value rather than their ecological outcomes`},{text:`have been proven to recreate the exact ecosystems of 1900 in every documented case`},{text:`are unnecessary because predators naturally return to all degraded ecosystems without any human intervention whatsoever`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3: reintroducing wolves to a "warmer, drier, and more visited" twenty-first-century Yellowstone "cannot recreate the ecosystem of 1900, because that ecosystem’s other conditions no longer exist. What rewilding produces ... is not a restored past but a novel ecosystem."`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference is licensed by paragraph 3’s explicit reasoning. Premise: predators don’t consult historical baselines, and their effects depend on current landscape, climate, and species assemblage. Application: the modern Yellowstone is "warmer, drier, and more visited than the Yellowstone of 1900." Conclusion: reintroduction "cannot recreate the ecosystem of 1900" and yields "a novel ecosystem, one whose desirability must be argued for on its own terms." Choice A states this. Choice B is the opposite of the critics’ caution. Choice C misattributes: cultural/ethical value is the defenders’ additional argument (paragraph 4), not the critics’. Choice D is the opposite of paragraph 3’s conclusion. Choice E invents a natural-return claim the passage never makes. Cannot-go-home-again arguments turn on background conditions: when they lapse, outcomes are novel and need independent justification.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. This restates paragraph 3’s reasoning: changed background conditions mean rewilding yields "a novel ecosystem," not a restored past. Background conditions do the work: changed climate and visitation make restoration impossible and novelty inevitable.`,`Opposite. The critics stress that predator effects "depend on landscape, climate, and the particular assemblage of species" — the reverse of guaranteed benefit. Dependence on local conditions is the critics’ point; guaranteed benefit is its direct opposite.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. Cultural and ethical value is the defenders’ supplementary argument in paragraph 4, not the critics’ position. Supplementary arguments belong to their own camp; this one is the defenders’, from paragraph 4.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 3 explicitly denies this: reintroduction "cannot recreate the ecosystem of 1900." Cannot recreate 1900 is paragraph 3’s explicit conclusion, stated without qualification. A restoration claim this direct directly contradicts the paragraph’s thesis, making it an easy opposite-trap to eliminate.`,`Unsupported. The passage never claims predators naturally return to all degraded ecosystems; rewilding is presented as active intervention. Active intervention is the premise of rewilding; natural-return claims appear nowhere in the passage.`],generalLesson:`A "you can’t go home again" argument has the form: the target state depended on background conditions that no longer hold — so the outcome is novel, and must be justified on its own terms.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Why, according to paragraph 3, can’t rewilding recreate the 1900 ecosystem?`,`What does the passage say rewilding produces instead of "a restored past"?`,`Whose view is this — the critics’ or the defenders’?`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p12-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`the claim that elk avoided risky areas because of wolves`,`later, more careful studies failed to replicate robustly`],requiredInference:`The example’s function is evidential: a celebrated mechanism with a fragile observational basis.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is too-strong (fragility, not proof of no effect); B is the opposite (contested, not uncontested); C overgeneralizes one replication failure; E shifts from evidence to policy verdict.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`The discussion of the "ecology of fear" in the second paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`prove that elk behavior is entirely unaffected by the presence of wolves in the Yellowstone ecosystem`},{text:`provide an uncontested example of a trophic cascade operating cleanly in the Yellowstone wolf system`},{text:`concede that all behavioral ecology research is methodologically worthless and should be disregarded by policymakers`},{text:`illustrate the claim that elk avoided risky areas because of wolves, a claim later and more careful studies called into question`},{text:`argue that wolves should never have been reintroduced to Yellowstone, regardless of the ecological evidence presented`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 introduces the "ecology of fear" as "the claim that elk avoided risky areas because of wolves," then notes it "rests on behavioral observations that later, more careful studies failed to replicate robustly." Its function is to exemplify a cascade claim with shaky foundations.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The "ecology of fear" appears as paragraph 2’s culminating example of why the Yellowstone case is messy. The paragraph first lists confounds (drought, winters, hunting, bison), then turns to a behavioral claim central to the popular narrative — elk avoiding exposed riverbanks from fear of wolves — and reports that "later, more careful studies failed to replicate" it "robustly." The function is illustrative: a concrete instance of a celebrated cascade mechanism whose evidence proved fragile. Choice D states this. Choice A is too-strong: "failed to replicate robustly" is not proof of no effect. Choice B is the opposite: the point is precisely that this example is contested, not uncontested. Choice C universalizes one replication failure into a dismissal of all behavioral ecology. Choice E overreaches: the paragraph questions evidential claims, not the reintroduction decision itself.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. "Failed to replicate robustly" falls short of proving no effect; the passage claims evidential fragility, not behavioral immunity. Fragility is an evidential grade, not an ontological verdict; the passage claims the former only.`,`Opposite. The example is introduced to show contestability — its basis was called into question — not to provide an uncontested case. Contestability is the example’s function; uncontested illustrations would serve the opposite purpose.`,`Too-strong and scope-shift. One replication failure does not license dismissing all behavioral ecology, and the passage never does so. Single replication failures license caution about a claim, not dismissal of an entire field.`,`Correct. The "ecology of fear" exemplifies a specific, celebrated cascade mechanism (elk avoiding risky riverbanks) whose observational basis later studies failed to replicate robustly. Named mechanisms with shaky bases are the paragraph’s exhibit A for why the Yellowstone case is messy.`,`Scope-shift. The paragraph evaluates evidential claims about cascade mechanisms; it never argues the reintroduction itself was wrong. Evidential evaluation and policy verdicts are different questions; the paragraph addresses only the first.`],generalLesson:`"Failed to replicate robustly" is a claim about evidential strength, not about the underlying phenomenon — keep the function at the level of evidence, not verdict.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What claim does the "ecology of fear" name, and what happened to its evidence?`,`Does the paragraph use it to prove something about elk, or to show something about the evidence?`,`"Failed to replicate robustly" is a careful phrase — what does it not claim?`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p12-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-attitude`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,5],supportingSentences:[`the most enthusiastic telling`,`both sides agree that the early, triumphalist version of the Yellowstone story oversold what was known`],requiredInference:`Distancing language plus reported consensus on overselling equals skepticism without outrage or hostility.`,viewpointOwner:`The author.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A ignores the distancing; B invents fraud/outrage; D contradicts the account’s structural role; E confuses narrative skepticism with policy hostility.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward the popular account of wolves transforming Yellowstone’s rivers is best described as`,choices:[{text:`fully credulous, repeating the account as established scientific fact`},{text:`outraged, condemning the account as a deliberate fraud`},{text:`skeptical, noting both sides in the debate agree the early story oversold what was known`},{text:`indifferent, since the author considers popular accounts irrelevant to science`},{text:`hostile to wolf reintroduction as a conservation policy`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 presents the rivers claim as "the most enthusiastic telling," paragraph 2 dismantles its evidential basis, and paragraph 5 notes "both sides agree that the early, triumphalist version of the Yellowstone story oversold what was known." The stance is skeptical but not outraged or hostile to rewilding itself.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Track the author’s handling of the rivers claim. Paragraph 1 attributes it to "the most enthusiastic telling" — distancing language, not endorsement. Paragraph 2 subjects the cascade narrative to confounds and replication failures. Paragraph 5 delivers the verdict both camps share: the "triumphalist version ... oversold what was known." That is skepticism grounded in a consensus the author reports. Choice C captures it. Choice A ignores the distancing and the debunking. Choice B invents outrage and fraud allegations; the author treats the overselling as enthusiasm, and paragraph 4 even defends continued rewilding experimentation. Choice D is wrong because the author engages the popular account as the debate’s starting point, not as irrelevant. Choice E confuses skepticism about the story with hostility to the policy — paragraph 4’s defenders (whose reply the author presents fairly) favor continued rewilding.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. "The most enthusiastic telling" is distancing language, and paragraph 2 dismantles the claim’s basis; nothing is repeated as established fact. Distancing language plus detailed debunking equals skepticism, not credulity, toward the popular account.`,`Too-strong. The author describes overselling, not fraud, and shows no outrage; the tone is analytical throughout. Analytical tone throughout excludes outrage; overselling is criticized, fraud never alleged.`,`Correct. The author distances from the claim ("most enthusiastic telling"), details its evidential problems, and reports both sides’ agreement that it "oversold what was known."`,`Unsupported. The popular account opens the passage and motivates the entire discussion; it is treated as relevant, not irrelevant. Openers that motivate whole passages cannot be irrelevant; the account is the debate’s starting point.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. Skepticism about the narrative is not hostility to reintroduction — paragraph 4’s pro-rewilding reply is presented fairly, and the author takes no anti-policy stance.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish skepticism about a story from hostility to a policy: an author can debunk the Yellowstone narrative while remaining neutral on rewilding itself.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-attitude`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What phrase introduces the rivers claim in paragraph 1 — does it sound endorsing?`,`What do "both sides agree" about the early story (paragraph 5)?`,`Is the author attacking wolves, or attacking the story about wolves?`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p12-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:2,abstraction:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`The skeptics demand the standards of the laboratory`,`convergent but imperfect evidence, weighed against the costs of inaction`],requiredInference:`The lynx case shares Yellowstone’s evidence profile, so P5’s epistemological divide reproduces.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and C invent criteria never at issue; D invents a historical dispute; E is too-strong (higher standards, not a ban).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p12`,stem:`A critic and an advocate debate a proposed lynx reintroduction for which the only evidence is correlational before-and-after data from a similar project elsewhere. According to the passage’s analysis, their disagreement is fundamentally about`,choices:[{text:`whether lynx are capable of hunting the region’s prey species, given the differences in terrain and available prey density`},{text:`what counts as sufficient evidence for action under uncertainty: laboratory-grade replication versus imperfect evidence weighed against the costs of inaction`},{text:`whether the public finds lynx charismatic enough to support the project, since public enthusiasm is often said to drive conservation funding`},{text:`the exact historical date on which lynx disappeared from the region, a background detail the passage never presents as contested`},{text:`whether correlational data should be permanently banned from ecology rather than merely subjected to higher standards of proof`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 abstracts the debate: "The skeptics demand the standards of the laboratory — isolated variables, replicated results — before endorsing interventions... The advocates accept a humbler epistemology: convergent but imperfect evidence, weighed against the costs of inaction." The lynx debate replays that epistemological divide.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Apply paragraph 5’s framing to the new case. The passage’s final move is to redescribe the Yellowstone dispute as epistemological: skeptics require "the standards of the laboratory — isolated variables, replicated results"; advocates accept "convergent but imperfect evidence, weighed against the costs of inaction." Correlational before-and-after data from elsewhere is exactly the kind of imperfect, confounded evidence that triggers this divide: the critic will find it insufficient (no isolated variables, no replication), while the advocate will weigh it against the costs of waiting. Choice B states the fundamental disagreement. Choice A invents a prey-capability question never at issue. Choice C invents a charisma criterion. Choice D invents a historical-date dispute. Choice E is too-strong: the skeptics demand higher standards, not a permanent ban on correlational data.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. Prey capability is never the issue in the passage’s debate; the dispute concerns evidentiary standards, not predator competence. Evidentiary standards, not predator competence, are what the passage’s final paragraph says is at stake.`,`Correct. This reproduces paragraph 5’s framing: laboratory-grade attribution versus convergent imperfect evidence weighed against inaction’s costs — the epistemological core of the dispute. Epistemological cores travel: the same evidence profile reproduces the same laboratory-versus-convergence divide.`,`Unsupported. Public charisma is never mentioned as a criterion in the passage’s analysis of the debate. Charisma criteria are never mentioned; the analysis is strictly about evidentiary sufficiency.`,`Unsupported. Historical disappearance dates play no role in the passage’s account of what the sides disagree about. Disappearance dates are absent from the passage’s account of the disagreement’s fundamentals.`,`Too-strong. The skeptics demand isolated variables and replication before acting; a "permanent ban" on correlational data goes beyond their stated position. Higher standards are not bans; the skeptics demand replication before action, not prohibition of data types.`],generalLesson:`When a passage abstracts its dispute to a principle ("what counts as sufficient evidence"), new cases are resolved by matching them to that principle, not to the original case’s surface details.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:115,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the final paragraph say the debate "ultimately turns on"?`,`Which kind of evidence does each side accept as sufficient?`,`The lynx case has the same evidence profile as Yellowstone — so the same divide applies.`],labels:{topic:`rewilding`}},{id:`rc-p13-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,5],supportingSentences:[`That the early Earth was instead wet and clement is the faint young Sun paradox`,`The paradox endures`,`scientifically productive`],requiredInference:`The main point needs three parts: the paradox, the troubled solutions, and the productivity payoff.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B is the opposite (P3 challenges the CO2 solution); C contradicts P1’s undoubted stellar physics; D invents fabrication; E inverts P5’s exoplanet payoff.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`Which one of the following best expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`The faint young Sun paradox — a dimmer early Sun versus geological evidence of liquid water — has no fully satisfactory solution yet, but its persistence has been scientifically productive.`},{text:`The faint young Sun paradox has been definitively solved by the discovery of high carbon dioxide levels in ancient atmospheres, ending all scientific debate about early Earth temperatures.`},{text:`Stellar physics is fundamentally mistaken, and the young Sun was actually brighter than the present Sun, invalidating all standard solar models and their predictions.`},{text:`Geologists have fabricated the evidence for liquid water on the early Earth in order to sustain an otherwise untenable research program.`},{text:`The search for habitable exoplanets is a waste of resources motivated by an unsolvable paradox that planetary scientists should abandon.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states the paradox, paragraphs 2–4 survey solutions and their difficulties (greenhouse vs. paleosols, supplementary mechanisms, slushball Earth), and paragraph 5 concludes the paradox "endures" but has been "scientifically productive." Only choice A contains the paradox, the unsatisfactory solutions, and the productivity claim.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage’s arc: paragraph 1 poses the contradiction (30% dimmer Sun vs. riverbeds and temperate isotopic signatures); paragraph 2 offers the favored greenhouse solution (CO2, methane); paragraph 3 raises the paleosol objection and surveys supplementary proposals, each creating "new ones" of problems; paragraph 4 considers the radical slushball alternative and its escape-from-ice difficulty; paragraph 5 concludes the paradox "endures" because stellar physics and rock chemistry "refuse to be satisfied simultaneously without strain" — yet this persistence "has sharpened techniques," improved models, and informed exoplanet research. Choice A is the only answer with all three elements. Choice B is the opposite: the paleosol evidence challenges the CO2 solution. Choice C contradicts paragraph 1, which says stellar physics "leaves no room for doubt." Choice D invents fabrication never alleged. Choice E inverts paragraph 5, which presents exoplanet relevance as a benefit, not a waste.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. It captures the paradox, the failure of each proposed solution to fully satisfy both constraints, and the concluding claim that the paradox’s persistence has been scientifically productive.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 3’s paleosol evidence suggests CO2 levels "far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require" — the CO2 solution is challenged, not confirmed. Challenged, not confirmed: the paleosol objection is paragraph 3’s central move against the CO2 solution.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 1 explicitly states stellar physics "leaves no room for doubt" that the young Sun was dimmer; the passage never questions stellar physics. Undoubted stellar physics is a fixed premise; questioning it would contradict paragraph 1’s explicit statement.`,`Unsupported. No fabrication of geological evidence is alleged anywhere; the rock record is treated as a genuine constraint throughout. Genuine constraints are the passage’s premise; fabrication allegations would collapse its whole structure.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 5 cites the faint-star problem’s recurrence around red dwarfs as one of the paradox’s productive payoffs, not as a waste. Productive payoffs, not waste, are paragraph 5’s claim about exoplanet research’s connection to the paradox.`],generalLesson:`Science passages that end with "productive persistence" want a main point containing both the unsolved problem and its fertility — answers declaring victory or defeat alone both fail.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the paradox solved by the end of the passage, or does it "endure"?`,`What does the final paragraph say the paradox’s persistence produced?`,`The main point needs three parts: the paradox, the troubled solutions, the payoff.`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}},{id:`rc-p13-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`appear to record atmospheric CO2 levels far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require`,`carbon dioxide alone cannot close the gap`],whyDistractorsFail:`A overstates (constraints, not proof of temperateness); B is the opposite of P2’s outgassing assumption; C invents a methanogen claim; E misattributes P1’s stellar-physics figure to paleosols.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`According to the passage, the paleosol evidence is significant because it`,choices:[{text:`proves that the early Earth was continuously and uniformly temperate throughout its history`},{text:`demonstrates that volcanic outgassing did not occur anywhere on the early Earth`},{text:`shows that methanogenic microbes could not possibly have existed four billion years ago`},{text:`appears to record carbon dioxide far below strong-greenhouse requirements, challenging the greenhouse solution`},{text:`confirms that the young Sun was thirty percent dimmer than today`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3: "Certain ancient minerals — most notably the iron-rich formations known as paleosols, fossil soils — appear to record atmospheric CO2 levels far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require. If those readings are correct, carbon dioxide alone cannot close the gap."`,explanationWalkthrough:`The paleosol finding is paragraph 3’s "stubborn geochemical objection" to the paragraph-2 greenhouse solution. Its significance is comparative: the recorded CO2 levels fall "far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require," so "carbon dioxide alone cannot close the gap." Choice D restates this. Choice A overstates: paleosols constrain CO2, they don’t prove continuous uniform temperateness (paragraph 4 entertains glaciations). Choice B is the opposite: paragraph 2 assumes volcanic outgassing "proceeding apace" as a CO2 source. Choice C invents a claim about methanogens’ existence; methane’s role is affirmed in paragraph 2. Choice E misattributes: the 30%-dimmer figure comes from stellar physics in paragraph 1, not from paleosols. Objection-evidence matters for what it rules out: state the ruled-out claim precisely, here the CO2 levels the models require.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. Paleosols constrain atmospheric CO2; they do not prove continuous uniform temperateness — paragraph 4 explicitly entertains long glaciations. Constraint evidence limits what solutions can claim; it does not by itself prove climatic histories.`,`Opposite. Paragraph 2’s greenhouse account assumes volcanic outgassing "proceeding apace"; the paleosol objection concerns CO2 levels, not outgassing’s existence. Proceeding apace is paragraph 2’s assumption about outgassing; the objection targets levels, not existence.`,`Unsupported. Methanogenic microbes are presented in paragraph 2 as likely methane sources; the passage never questions their existence. Likely methane sources are affirmed, not questioned; the passage never doubts the microbes’ existence.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 3: paleosols appear to record CO2 "far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require," so CO2 alone "cannot close the gap." Mismatches between recorded and required levels are the objection’s precise content.`,`Scope-shift. The thirty-percent figure comes from stellar physics (paragraph 1), not from paleosol geochemistry. Stellar-physics numbers and geochemical numbers must not be confused; each constrains the paradox differently.`],generalLesson:`Objection-evidence in science passages is significant for what it rules out — state the ruled-out claim precisely (here: CO2 levels sufficient for the strong-greenhouse models).`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which solution does the paleosol evidence object to?`,`What exactly do the paleosols record — and what do the models require?`,`The significance is a mismatch between two numbers.`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}},{id:`rc-p13-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`trades one difficulty for another`,`a fully frozen Earth reflects so much sunlight that escaping glaciation becomes extremely difficult`],requiredInference:`The answer restates the named trade-off: warm-interval sampling traded for the deglaciation problem.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is too-strong (a possibility, not proven); C misattributes to the author; D is the opposite (the proposal preserves stellar physics); E contradicts the warm-interval accommodation.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`The passage implies that the "slushball Earth" proposal`,choices:[{text:`has been proven correct by isotopic signatures in ancient minerals that rule out every competing proposal`},{text:`trades one difficulty for another, since it must explain repeated escape from glaciation despite ice’s high reflectivity`},{text:`was originally proposed by the author of the passage in an earlier, now discredited paper`},{text:`requires abandoning all of stellar physics, including the well-confirmed theory of stellar evolution`},{text:`is incompatible with the existence of liquid water at any point in Earth’s history, contradicting the geological record`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4: "This solution trades one difficulty for another: it must explain what repeatedly rescued the planet from the ice, since a fully frozen Earth reflects so much sunlight that escaping glaciation becomes extremely difficult." Choice B restates the trade-off.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The inference is drawn explicitly in paragraph 4’s final sentence, which names the structure ("trades one difficulty for another") and both difficulties: the proposal handles the liquid-water evidence by sampling warm intervals, but then "must explain what repeatedly rescued the planet from the ice" — hard because high reflectivity makes "escaping glaciation ... extremely difficult." Choice B reproduces this. Choice A is too-strong: the proposal is presented as a possibility ("some researchers argue"), not a proven conclusion. Choice C is unsupported: the passage attributes it to "some researchers," not to the author. Choice D is the opposite: the slushball proposal is offered precisely to preserve stellar physics while reinterpreting the geological record. Choice E contradicts the proposal, which explicitly accommodates liquid water during warm intervals.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The proposal is attributed to "some researchers" as a possibility; the passage never claims it has been proven correct. Attributed possibilities are not proven conclusions; some researchers argue is the passage’s hedged framing.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 4’s explicit trade-off: the proposal must explain repeated deglaciation, which ice’s reflectivity makes "extremely difficult." Named trade-offs should be restated whole: the traded-away ease and the newly-incurred difficulty together.`,`Unsupported. The passage attributes the proposal to "some researchers," never to its author. Some researchers is the attribution; the author never claims the proposal as their own.`,`Opposite. The slushball idea works within stellar physics (a dim Sun); it reinterprets the geological record rather than abandoning stellar physics. Dim-Sun-preserving reinterpretations are the proposal’s point; abandoning physics would defeat its purpose.`,`Opposite. The proposal explicitly accommodates liquid water during warm intervals; the rock record’s water signatures are said to "sample the warm intervals." Warm-interval sampling is the proposal’s accommodation of water evidence, stated explicitly in paragraph 4.`],generalLesson:`When a passage says a solution "trades one difficulty for another," both difficulties are usually named — the inference answer restates the trade, not just one side.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What phrase does paragraph 4 use to describe the slushball proposal’s cost?`,`Why is escaping a frozen Earth hard, according to the passage?`,`The answer names both the traded-away difficulty and the new one.`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}},{id:`rc-p13-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`nevertheless scientifically productive`,`keeps an entire field honest about what it does not yet know`],requiredInference:`The final paragraph’s two-part job is concession (the paradox endures) plus payoff (productivity).`,whyDistractorsFail:`A misreads exoplanets as a new solution; B and C are opposites; D invents a history-of-physics summary.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`The final paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`introduce a novel solution to the paradox based on the latest exoplanet observations`},{text:`concede that the paradox is scientifically uninteresting and should be entirely abandoned by working researchers as a dead end`},{text:`prove that the greenhouse solution is correct after all`},{text:`summarize the history of stellar physics from the nineteenth century to the present in broad strokes`},{text:`argue that the paradox’s persistence has been scientifically productive, sharpening methods and models while keeping the field honest`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 opens "The paradox endures because..." and then pivots: "Its persistence is nevertheless scientifically productive: the effort to resolve it has sharpened techniques for reading ancient atmospheres, improved models of planetary climate, and informed the search for habitable exoplanets." It closes by valorizing persistent paradoxes for keeping "an entire field honest about what it does not yet know."`,explanationWalkthrough:`The final paragraph has a two-part job: acknowledge endurance ("The paradox endures because every proposed solution is constrained by two independent bodies of evidence ... that refuse to be satisfied simultaneously without strain") and then reframe endurance as fertility ("nevertheless scientifically productive," with three named payoffs: atmospheric-reading techniques, climate models, exoplanet habitability). It closes with the general moral: "A paradox that will not die ... can be more valuable than a problem neatly solved — it keeps an entire field honest." Choice E contains both parts. Choice A misreads: exoplanets illustrate the payoff, not a new solution. Choice B is the opposite: the paragraph argues the paradox is valuable, not uninteresting. Choice C contradicts paragraph 3’s paleosol objection, which the paragraph does not retract. Choice D invents a history-of-physics summary absent from the text.`,choiceExplanations:[`Scope-shift. Exoplanets appear as a beneficiary of paradox-driven research (the faint-star problem "recurs around ... red dwarfs"), not as the source of a new solution.`,`Opposite. The paragraph argues the enduring paradox is "more valuable than a problem neatly solved," the reverse of calling it uninteresting. More valuable than neatly solved is the paragraph’s comparative claim, the reverse of uninteresting.`,`Opposite. The paragraph does not revisit or vindicate the greenhouse solution; the paleosol objection from paragraph 3 stands unretracted. Unretracted objections stand; the final paragraph assesses productivity, not greenhouse vindication.`,`Unsupported. No history of stellar physics is summarized; the paragraph assesses the paradox’s scientific productivity. Productivity assessments are not physics histories; the paragraph’s subject is the paradox’s fertility.`,`Correct. It captures both moves: the paradox endures under dual constraints, and that endurance has been productive (techniques, models, exoplanets) while enforcing intellectual honesty. Concession plus payoff is the paragraph’s two-part job, and this choice is the only one containing both.`],generalLesson:`Closing "nevertheless productive" paragraphs convert an unsolved problem into a methodological moral — the function answer must include both the concession (unsolved) and the payoff.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`supported-nonresponsive`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the final paragraph solve the paradox or accept its endurance?`,`What three payoffs does it name?`,`The last sentence states a general moral — what is it?`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}},{id:`rc-p13-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`Proposed supplements abound`,`each proposal solves part of the puzzle while creating new ones`],requiredInference:`The defensive repertoire pairs auxiliary hypotheses with candor about their costs.`,viewpointOwner:`Proponents of the strong-greenhouse solution (as reported in P3).`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and D are opposites of shared premises; B is the opposite (supplements save, not abandon, the approach); E is too-strong (conditional doubt, not universal unreliability).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`Proponents of the strong-greenhouse solution, as presented in the passage, would most likely respond to the paleosol objection by`,choices:[{text:`denying outright that the Sun was ever dimmer than it is today`},{text:`abandoning the greenhouse approach entirely in favor of the slushball hypothesis of a globally frozen early Earth`},{text:`supplementing the greenhouse account with other warming mechanisms, while conceding each must fit the geological evidence`},{text:`arguing that geological evidence is simply irrelevant to scientific questions about the ancient climate`},{text:`claiming that paleosol evidence has been proven unreliable in every geological context studied`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3: "Proposed supplements abound: other greenhouse gases such as ammonia or carbonyl sulfide; a lower planetary albedo from fewer reflective clouds; even a somewhat more massive young Sun ... Each proposal solves part of the puzzle while creating new ones, since the supplementary mechanisms must themselves be reconciled with the geological evidence."`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 3 attributes a specific defensive repertoire to the greenhouse camp after the paleosol objection: supplements including "other greenhouse gases such as ammonia or carbonyl sulfide," "a lower planetary albedo," and "a somewhat more massive young Sun" — coupled with the candid acknowledgment that "each proposal solves part of the puzzle while creating new ones, since the supplementary mechanisms must themselves be reconciled with the geological evidence." Choice C reproduces both the supplements and the acknowledgment. Choice A contradicts paragraph 1’s shared premise (stellar physics undoubted). Choice B is the opposite: the supplements are offered to save the greenhouse approach, not abandon it. Choice D contradicts the passage’s treatment of geology as an indispensable constraint ("two independent bodies of evidence"). Choice E is too-strong: the passage says the readings hold "if those readings are correct," entertaining doubt without claiming universal unreliability of paleosols.`,choiceExplanations:[`Opposite. The dim young Sun is a shared premise ("stellar physics leaves no room for doubt"); greenhouse proponents accept it — their solution is designed around it.`,`Opposite. The supplements are proposed to preserve the greenhouse approach, not to abandon it for the slushball alternative. Save-the-approach supplements are the opposite of abandonment; their function is preservation, not surrender.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 3’s attributed reply: supplementary mechanisms (other gases, lower albedo, more massive young Sun), each requiring reconciliation with the geological evidence. Auxiliaries plus candor about their costs is the defensive repertoire the passage attributes to the greenhouse camp.`,`Opposite. The geological record is treated throughout as a genuine, indispensable constraint; no one in the passage dismisses it as irrelevant. Indispensable constraints cannot be dismissed by the very researchers whose proposals must satisfy them.`,`Unsupported and too-strong. The passage entertains the readings conditionally ("if those readings are correct"); it never claims paleosols are unreliable "in all contexts." Conditional entertaining of readings is not a universal reliability verdict; if-correct is the passage’s hedge.`],generalLesson:`Defensive repertoires in science passages usually pair new auxiliary hypotheses with candor about their costs — the faithful answer includes both the auxiliaries and the acknowledgment.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`After the paleosol objection, what does paragraph 3 say "abound"?`,`List the three supplements named.`,`What price does the paragraph say each supplement pays?`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}},{id:`rc-p13-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-organization`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:4,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`paradox stated (P1)`,`leading solution (P2)`,`objection plus supplements (P3)`,`radical alternative (P4)`,`productivity assessment (P5)`],requiredInference:`Each clause of the skeleton must correspond to exactly one paragraph’s job, in order.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B describes a formalism-equivalence debate; C, D, E invent elements (archives, experiments, ridicule) absent from the text.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p13`,stem:`Which one of the following best describes the organization of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`A paradox is stated, the leading solution is presented, a geochemical objection is raised, and supplementary proposals are surveyed.`},{text:`Two rival theories are shown to make identical predictions; their mathematical equivalence is demonstrated; the debate is declared meaningless.`},{text:`A historical narrative is recounted; new archival evidence overturns it; a revised chronology is proposed.`},{text:`An experiment is described; its results are reported; the implications for public policy are derived.`},{text:`A consensus view is presented; dissenting scientists are ridiculed; the consensus is reaffirmed without qualification.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states the paradox (dim Sun vs. liquid water); paragraph 2 presents the strong-greenhouse solution; paragraph 3 raises the paleosol objection and surveys supplements; paragraph 4 considers the slushball alternative; paragraph 5 assesses the enduring paradox’s productivity. Choice A tracks this five-step sequence.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Map each paragraph’s job: (1) paradox stated — faint Sun vs. geological liquid water; (2) leading solution — enhanced greenhouse (CO2, methane); (3) objection + supplements — paleosols record too little CO2, so ammonia/carbonyl sulfide, lower albedo, or a more massive young Sun are proposed, each with costs; (4) radical alternative — slushball Earth with its deglaciation difficulty; (5) assessment — the paradox endures but productively. Choice A reproduces this sequence exactly. Choice B describes a different passage’s structure (mathematical equivalence debates). Choice C invents archival revision. Choice D invents an experiment and policy implications. Choice E invents ridicule of dissenters and unqualified consensus — the passage’s consensus is precisely what’s missing. Verify skeletons paragraph by paragraph in order, since each clause should correspond to exactly one paragraph’s job.`,choiceExplanations:[`Correct. Each clause matches a paragraph in order: paradox (1), leading solution (2), objection plus supplements (3), radical alternative (4), productive-persistence assessment (5). Five clauses in order, one per paragraph, is the verification standard for organization answers.`,`Scope-shift. Mathematical equivalence of rival theories belongs to a different debate; nothing here turns on formalism translation. Formalism-translation debates belong elsewhere; this passage turns on evidence, not mathematical equivalence.`,`Unsupported. No archival evidence or revised chronology appears; the passage’s structure is problem-solutions-assessment, not historical revision. Problem-solutions-assessment is the passage’s genre; archival revision belongs to a different kind of text.`,`Unsupported. No experiment is described and no policy implications are derived; the passage is a theoretical survey. Theoretical surveys propose and assess; they describe no experiments and derive no policies.`,`Unsupported. There is no ridicule of dissenters and no unqualified consensus — the passage stresses the absence of a satisfactory resolution. Absent resolutions and present disagreements define this passage; consensus-affirming skeletons contradict it.`],generalLesson:`For organization questions, verify the skeleton paragraph by paragraph in order — each clause should correspond to exactly one paragraph’s job.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-organization`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Give each paragraph a one-phrase job description.`,`Which choice has five parts in the right order?`,`Eliminate skeletons mentioning things (experiments, archives, ridicule) that never appear.`],labels:{topic:`faint young Sun paradox`}}],l=[{id:`rc-p14-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4,6],supportingSentences:[`the debate is less absolute than the rhetoric suggests`,`often converge on the same outcome`,`an aspiration rather than a formula that decides cases by itself`],requiredInference:`The main point must hold both halves: practical convergence and the ineliminable role of judgment.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and E adopt one camp’s extreme as the author’s view; B upgrades qualified convergence to an absolute; D upgrades the technology illustration into obsolescence.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Textualism is the superior approach to statutory interpretation because it constrains judicial discretion and gives citizens fair notice of the law.`},{text:`The debate between textualists and purposivists is largely illusory, since judges in practice always follow the plain meaning of the text.`},{text:`Although textualists and purposivists present their methods as opposites, the two often converge, and neither can fully separate interpretation from judgment.`},{text:`New technologies such as smartphones have rendered both textualism and purposivism obsolete as methods of statutory interpretation.`},{text:`The plain-meaning rule should be abandoned because statutory language is inherently too imprecise to be applied as written.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The author devotes the passage to both sides but concludes (paragraphs 4 and 6) that the debate is less absolute than its rhetoric: the approaches converge in practice, and no method fully separates interpretation from judgment. Choice C captures both halves of that conclusion.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The passage opens by laying out the two camps: textualists who favor the plain-meaning rule and purposivists who emphasize legislative purpose, illustrated by the murderous-heir example. A main-point answer must reflect what the author does with this debate, not merely repeat one side's slogan. The turning point comes in the fourth paragraph, where the author states that "the debate is less absolute than the rhetoric suggests" and that the two approaches "often converge on the same outcome." The final paragraph deepens the point: even textualist tools like canons involve judicial choice, so the plain-meaning rule is "an aspiration... rather than a formula that decides cases by itself." Choice C is the only option that captures both elements — practical convergence and the ineliminable role of judgment. The distractors each take one side's argument (A, E) or exaggerate a subsidiary point (B, D) and mistake it for the author's thesis.`,choiceExplanations:[`This choice is tempting because the passage does report these textualist arguments in the third paragraph, and they sound conclusive. But it fails because it mistakes one side's position for the author's conclusion — a classic wrong-viewpoint trap. The author presents these claims as the textualists' reply, then undercuts the stark opposition in paragraphs 4 and 6.`,`This choice is tempting because the author does say the approaches "often converge," which can sound like the debate is fake. It fails because "always follow the plain meaning" is far too strong and directly contradicts the passage: purposivists depart from text, and the author says the methods differ in emphasis. It converts a qualified convergence claim into an absolute one.`,`This is correct. It captures the passage's two central moves: first, that the textualist/purposivist opposition is overstated because the methods converge in practice (paragraph 4), and second, that no interpretive method fully removes judgment from interpretation (paragraph 6). It performs exactly the task the stem requests — stating the author's overall thesis rather than a subsidiary point.`,`This choice is tempting because the fifth paragraph does discuss smartphones and wiretap statutes. It fails because the passage says new technology "reopens the quarrel" — it intensifies the debate rather than ending it. "Rendered obsolete" is unsupported and far stronger than anything the author claims.`,`This choice is tempting because purposivists in the passage do argue that language is imprecise. It fails for the same reason as A: it adopts one camp's extreme position as the author's view. The author never calls for abandoning the plain-meaning rule; the conclusion treats it as a valuable aspiration.`],generalLesson:`For main-point questions, the correct answer must match the author's thesis, not merely restate one side's argument in a debate passage. Watch for paragraphs where the author steps back to characterize the debate itself.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author spend the passage arguing for one side, or doing something else with the debate? Check the fourth paragraph for a shift.`,`The author says the debate is "less absolute than the rhetoric suggests." Which choice reflects that the two methods are not as opposed as they claim?`,`The final paragraph adds a second claim: no method fully separates interpretation from judgment. Which choice includes both the convergence point and this deeper point?`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-primary-purpose`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`the debate is less absolute than the rhetoric suggests`],requiredInference:`Purpose is the author’s job across all paragraphs: exposition followed by qualification, never partisanship.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and D assign partisan purposes the author never pursues; C mistakes P5’s illustration for the whole; E invents a historical survey.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`,choices:[{text:`argue that courts should abandon textualism in favor of a purpose-driven approach to statutes`},{text:`describe a longstanding debate in legal interpretation and show its opposing positions are less stark than they appear`},{text:`explain how the plain-meaning rule is applied by courts in cases involving new technologies`},{text:`defend the claim that legislative purpose is a discoverable fact that judges can reliably identify`},{text:`survey the history of statutory interpretation from early common law to the present day`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage presents both textualism and purposivism, gives each side's best arguments, then argues the opposition is overstated: the methods converge and neither eliminates judgment. Choice B describes that purpose exactly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Primary-purpose questions ask what the author is doing, not just what the topic is. The passage's structure is diagnostic: paragraph 1 introduces the plain-meaning rule, paragraph 2 presents the purposivist challenge, paragraph 3 gives the textualist reply, paragraph 4 steps back to observe convergence, paragraph 5 applies the debate to new technology, and paragraph 6 draws a broader conclusion. The author never endorses one side outright — choices A and D fail because they assign the author a partisan goal the passage never states. Choice C mistakes the fifth paragraph's illustration for the whole passage's purpose. Choice E invents a historical survey the passage never conducts. Only B captures the full arc: describe the debate, then qualify it. Note how the distractors fail the coverage test: each captures at most one paragraph's content, while the correct answer accounts for the author's two-phase movement across all six paragraphs.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the purposivist arguments (paragraph 2) are vivid and the murderous-heir example is memorable. It fails because the author never advocates abandoning textualism; paragraph 4 explicitly notes that even purposivists begin with the text. It attributes a partisan purpose the author does not pursue.`,`This is correct. The passage's dominant activity is exposition of the textualism/purposivism debate followed by the author's qualifying thesis — that the opposition is "less absolute than the rhetoric suggests." Every paragraph serves that two-part purpose.`,`This is tempting because paragraph 5 discusses wiretap statutes applied to smartphones. It fails because that discussion is an illustration of the debate's persistence, not the passage's purpose. A primary-purpose answer must cover the whole passage, not one paragraph.`,`This is tempting because purposivists rely on legislative purpose. It fails twice: the author never defends this claim, and the textualists' reply in paragraph 3 directly attacks it ("purpose is not a fact waiting to be discovered") without authorial rebuttal. The passage does not take this side.`,`This is tempting because the passage discusses an interpretive tradition. It fails because there is no historical survey here — no eras, no development over time. The passage is analytical, not historical.`],generalLesson:`Primary purpose is about the author's job, not the topic. If the passage presents two sides and then qualifies the debate, the purpose is almost certainly descriptive and qualifying — not partisan.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-primary-purpose`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Ask what the author is doing in each paragraph. Is the author arguing for a side, or presenting and then commenting on a debate?`,`Eliminate any choice that makes the author a partisan for textualism or purposivism — does the passage ever endorse one side outright?`,`The fourth paragraph is the hinge: the author steps back and characterizes the debate itself. Which purpose choice matches that move?`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`Purpose is not a fact waiting to be discovered`,`a single statute typically serves several purposes, sometimes in tension`],whyDistractorsFail:`A invents legislator deception; B is too-strong (textualists use context, P4); C is the author’s P6 point against textualism, a viewpoint reversal; E contradicts P4 (purposivists begin with the words).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`According to the passage, textualists argue that relying on legislative purpose is problematic because`,choices:[{text:`legislators deliberately conceal their true purposes when drafting statutes`},{text:`the plain meaning of statutory language is always sufficiently clear to decide every case`},{text:`dictionaries and canons of construction are themselves products of judicial choice`},{text:`a statute typically serves several purposes that may conflict, and selecting among them requires discretion`},{text:`purposivists refuse to consider the text of a statute at all`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 states the textualist objection directly: "Purpose... is not a fact waiting to be discovered; a single statute typically serves several purposes, sometimes in tension, and choosing among them is itself an act of discretion." Choice D restates this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Detail questions reward locating the exact claim. The textualist reply occupies paragraph 3, and its core objection to purpose-based interpretation is epistemic and practical: purpose is not a single discoverable fact but a multiplicity of possibly conflicting aims, so invoking "purpose" merely relocates judicial discretion rather than eliminating it. Choice D paraphrases this precisely. Choice A invents legislator deception, which the passage never mentions. Choice B overstates the textualist position — paragraph 4 notes even textualists use context and canons, so "always sufficiently clear" is too strong. Choice C is actually the author's criticism of textualism in paragraph 6, a viewpoint reversal. Choice E misdescribes purposivists, who "begin with the words" per paragraph 4. The question rewards locating the exact objection in paragraph 3 rather than reconstructing it from general knowledge: the passage's textualists object to purpose's multiplicity, not to its secrecy or to purposivists' good faith.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because distrust of hidden motives feels like a natural objection. It fails because the passage never says legislators conceal purposes; the textualist objection is that purposes are multiple and conflicting, not secret. It answers a question the passage never raises.`,`This is tempting because textualists emphasize clear text. It fails as too strong: the passage shows textualists acknowledging hard cases (the murderous heir) and using context and canons (paragraph 4). "Always sufficiently clear" goes beyond their stated position.`,`This is tempting because it is a genuine claim from the passage — but it is the author's point against textualism in the final paragraph, not the textualists' point against purposivism. It is a precise wrong-viewpoint reversal. Keeping a mental ledger of who claims what — textualists object to purpose, purposivists invoke it, the author qualifies the debate — is the single most reliable habit for detail questions in debate passages like this one.`,`This is correct. It directly restates paragraph 3: purpose is not a discoverable fact, statutes serve several sometimes-conflicting purposes, and choosing among them is itself discretionary — which is exactly why textualists say purpose-based interpretation licenses judicial policymaking.`,`This is tempting as a caricature of the debate. It fails because paragraph 4 explicitly says even committed purposivists "begin with the words." The passage never attributes text-ignoring to purposivists.`],generalLesson:`On detail questions, match the choice to the specific paragraph and the specific speaker. When a passage stages a debate, always check whose claim each choice reports.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph gives the textualists' reply to purposivism? Re-read it and find their specific objection to "purpose."`,`The key sentence says purpose "is not a fact waiting to be discovered." What reason does the passage give for that claim?`,`Watch out for the choice that states a true claim from the passage but attributes it to the wrong side.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`,`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,4,6],supportingSentences:[`an aspiration`,`rather than a formula that decides cases by itself`],requiredInference:`The author’s qualified verdict preserves both the commitment’s value and its mechanical insufficiency.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A upgrades a reported argument to a uniquely-endorsed method; C invents a design history; D contradicts P5’s ongoing debate; E contradicts P4’s context-consulting textualists.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`It can most reasonably be inferred from the passage that the author believes the plain-meaning rule`,choices:[{text:`is the only interpretive method consistent with democratic lawmaking`},{text:`expresses a valuable commitment though it cannot mechanically decide cases on its own`},{text:`was originally designed to handle statutes involving new technologies`},{text:`has been rejected by most contemporary judges in favor of purposivism`},{text:`requires judges to ignore the broader context of neighboring provisions`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph calls the plain-meaning rule "an aspiration — a commitment to decide cases on the basis of enacted words — rather than a formula that decides cases by itself." Choice B restates this qualified endorsement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Inference questions require a conclusion the passage supports without stating verbatim. The author's final assessment is carefully balanced: the plain-meaning rule is not dismissed (the author takes its democratic-legitimacy rationale seriously in paragraph 1 and notes the convergence in paragraph 4), but it is demoted from a decision procedure to "an aspiration." Choice B captures both halves — valuable commitment plus mechanical insufficiency. Choice A takes the textualists' strongest claim (paragraph 3) and upgrades "the author notes this argument" to "the author believes it is the only method," which the convergence discussion contradicts. Choice C is unsupported; new technology is presented as a challenge, not a design context. Choice D contradicts paragraph 5, where the debate is described as ongoing. Choice E contradicts paragraph 4, where textualists are said to consult neighboring provisions and context.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because paragraph 1 links the text to democratic lawmaking. It fails because "the only interpretive method" is far stronger than the author's position — the author explicitly describes convergence between methods and never ranks one as uniquely legitimate. It converts a reported argument into an authorial endorsement.`,`This is correct. The final paragraph's "aspiration... rather than a formula" language supports exactly this inference: the rule embodies a genuine commitment (deciding on enacted words) while failing as a mechanical decision procedure (canons and dictionaries involve choice). Both halves are textually grounded.`,`This is tempting because paragraph 5 applies old statutes to new technology. It fails because the passage says nothing about the rule's origins or design purpose; the technology discussion illustrates the debate's persistence, not the rule's history.`,`This is tempting if one misreads the author's evenhandedness as favoring purposivism. It fails because paragraph 5 says "neither side has surrendered," and nothing suggests most judges have abandoned textualism. It is directly contradicted.`,`This is tempting because textualists emphasize text over purpose. It fails because paragraph 4 explicitly says textualists consult "neighboring provisions, established legal usage, and the canons of construction." The passage refutes this characterization.`],generalLesson:`Inference answers must stay inside the passage's degree of commitment. When the author's verdict is qualified ("an aspiration rather than a formula"), the right answer preserves the qualification.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The author's verdict on the plain-meaning rule comes in the final paragraph. What two things does the author say it is — and is not?`,`"An aspiration... rather than a formula that decides cases by itself" — which choice preserves both the positive and the limiting halves?`,`Eliminate choices that make the author sound like a partisan textualist or purposivist; the author's tone throughout is qualifying, not endorsing.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:1},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`For purposivists, such cases prove that meaning depends on the mischief the law was meant to cure`],requiredInference:`The example’s function follows its owner (the purposivists) and the moral the surrounding sentence draws.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A inflates an extreme illustration into a statistical claim; B and C invent conclusions about legislatures and history; D misattributes ownership of the example.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`The author discusses the grandson who murders his grandfather primarily in order to`,choices:[{text:`prove that the plain-meaning rule produces unjust results in the majority of cases`},{text:`demonstrate that legislatures are incapable of drafting unambiguous statutes`},{text:`provide a historical example of the first case in which purposivism was applied`},{text:`show that textualists secretly rely on legislative purpose when deciding difficult cases`},{text:`illustrate the purposivist claim that a statute's meaning depends on the problem it was meant to solve`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The example appears in paragraph 2 as the purposivists' exhibit A: courts deny the murderer the inheritance because "the legislature could not have meant to reward murder" — i.e., meaning follows the law's purpose, not just its words. Choice E states that function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask why the author includes a detail, and the answer lies in the surrounding paragraph's argumentative job. The grandson example sits inside paragraph 2, which presents the purposivist position: "a statute is best understood as a solution to a problem." The example is introduced with "Consider" and followed by the moral the purposivists draw — "For purposivists, such cases prove that meaning depends on the mischief the law was meant to cure." Choice E restates that illustrative role. Choice A overstates: the passage presents it as an extreme case, not a typical one, and textualists explicitly call it that in paragraph 3. Choice B is unsupported; no claim about legislative competence appears. Choice C invents a historical first that the passage never asserts. Choice D misattributes: the example is offered by purposivists, not as exposure of textualist hypocrisy.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the example does show a harsh literal result. It fails on "majority of cases": paragraph 3 explicitly frames it as an extreme example, and the author never generalizes from it to most cases. It inflates an illustration into a statistical claim.`,`This is tempting because the example involves a statute producing an unintended result. It fails because the passage draws no conclusion about legislatures' drafting abilities; the point is about how judges should read statutes, not about how legislators write them.`,`This is tempting because the example is concrete and case-like. It fails because the passage never identifies it as a historical first or names any actual case; it is a hypothetical introduced with "Consider." The historical claim is fabricated.`,`This is tempting because the debate is adversarial and "gotcha" readings feel natural. It fails because the example is deployed by purposivists to support their own view, not by the author to expose textualists. The surrounding sentences make the ownership explicit.`,`This is correct. The example's job in paragraph 2 is to make the purposivist argument vivid: courts look past literal words to the "mischief the law was meant to cure." The choice names both the example's owner (the purposivist claim) and its illustrative function.`],generalLesson:`For function questions, read the sentences immediately around the detail — especially the sentence that follows it, which usually states the moral the author (or the quoted side) draws.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`In which paragraph does the example appear, and whose argument is that paragraph presenting?`,`Look at the sentence right after the example: "For purposivists, such cases prove..." What do they prove, in the author's telling?`,`The correct answer will mention the purposivist claim and the example's role in illustrating it — not a broader conclusion about most cases.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-organization`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`debate presented (P1-3)`,`the opposition qualified (P4-6)`],requiredInference:`The two-phase structure (exposition, then qualification) is the skeleton; any missing phase disqualifies a choice.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A invents a historical narrative; C invents a proposed reform; D invents a third theory; E invents analyzed court decisions.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`Which one of the following best describes the organization of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`A historical narrative traces the development of an interpretive doctrine from its origins to its current crisis.`},{text:`A scholarly debate is presented, each side's arguments are examined, and the opposition is shown to be less stark than claimed.`},{text:`A proposed legal reform is introduced, objections to it are rebutted one by one, and its adoption is urged.`},{text:`Two competing theories are shown to be logically incompatible, and a third theory is proposed to reconcile them.`},{text:`A series of court decisions is analyzed to demonstrate a gradual shift in judicial philosophy over time.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraphs 1–3 present the textualist/purposivist debate with each side's arguments; paragraphs 4–6 step back to show the opposition is overstated (convergence, technology reopens the quarrel, judgment is ineliminable). Choice B maps this structure exactly.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Organization questions require matching an abstract description to the passage's actual movement. The passage has a clear two-phase structure: exposition of a debate (paragraphs 1–3: rule, challenge, reply) followed by authorial qualification (paragraph 4: convergence; paragraph 5: application to technology; paragraph 6: deeper unresolved question). Choice B captures both phases. Choice A fails because there is no historical narrative or timeline. Choice C fails because no reform is proposed or urged. Choice D fails because no third theory is proposed — the author qualifies the debate rather than reconciling it with a new method. Choice E fails because no series of decisions is analyzed; the only case-like material is a hypothetical. The decisive test is the final paragraph: any organization answer that omits the author's deeper unresolved question about judgment cannot describe this passage, which builds toward that question throughout.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the passage discusses a longstanding doctrine. It fails because nothing is narrated historically — there are no eras, origins, or chronological development. The passage's movement is analytical (debate, then qualification), not temporal.`,`This is correct. It tracks the passage paragraph by paragraph: debate presented (1–3), arguments examined on both sides, then the opposition qualified as "less absolute than the rhetoric suggests" (4–6). No other choice describes this two-phase movement.`,`This is tempting because the passage evaluates interpretive methods. It fails because the author proposes no reform and urges no adoption; the conclusion is diagnostic ("the deeper question remains"), not prescriptive.`,`This is tempting because convergence might sound like reconciliation. It fails because the author proposes no third theory — the point is that the existing two approaches already overlap in practice, not that a new method synthesizes them.`,`This is tempting because legal passages often proceed case by case. It fails because the passage analyzes no actual decisions; the murderous-heir scenario is a hypothetical, and the technology discussion names no cases.`],generalLesson:`For organization questions, sketch the passage's movement in your own words first (here: "debate, then qualification"), then find the choice that matches — and reject any choice containing an element the passage lacks.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-organization`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Summarize the passage's movement in one sentence before looking at the choices. What happens in paragraphs 1–3 versus paragraphs 4–6?`,`Does the author propose anything new — a reform, a third theory? If not, eliminate choices that say so.`,`Look for the choice that includes both phases: presenting the debate AND then qualifying how stark the opposition really is.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`}},{id:`rc-p14-q7`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,viewpointComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`textualists ask what the words covered at enactment`,`purposivists ask what problem the legislature was trying to solve and how that problem appears today`],requiredInference:`Replay the textualist method from paragraph 5 on the new-technology hypothetical: the question is coverage-at-enactment, not the legislature’s problem.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A performs the purposivist move paragraph 5 assigns to the other side; B has textualists freely departing from text, contrary to paragraphs 1 and 4; D states the passage author’s deeper question, not a textualist method; E is stronger than anything the textualists claim.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-set-size-diversification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`New item for 6→8 set-size diversification (2026-09-11): covers paragraph 5 (new technology), the only passage paragraph with no dedicated question. Hostile review: credited B replays paragraph 5’s textualist move verbatim; A is paragraph 5’s purposivist move; B’s "freely depart" contradicts P1/P4; D is the author’s P6 question, not a textualist answer; E too-strong. Credited choice 112 chars vs longest distractor 108 chars — no length leakage. d3 honest: one-step application, but the rival schools’ moves are genuinely confusable. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`A 1986 statute prohibits the use of "mechanical listening devices" to intercept private conversations. A court must decide whether the statute covers a smartphone application that records nearby conversations. A textualist reasoning as the passage’s textualists do would most likely`,choices:[{text:`ask what problem the legislature was trying to solve in 1986 and how that problem appears in the age of smartphones`},{text:`ask what the statutory words covered at the time of enactment, rather than what problem the legislature was trying to solve`},{text:`consult the statute’s underlying purpose and depart freely from the text whenever technology has changed since enactment`},{text:`conclude that no interpretive method can decide the case, since interpretation cannot be fully separated from judgment`},{text:`refuse to apply the statute at all, since its words cannot possibly cover any technology invented after 1986`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 gives the two schools’ technology moves directly: "textualists ask what the words covered at enactment, while purposivists ask what problem the legislature was trying to solve and how that problem appears today." Choice B replays the textualist move; choice A replays the purposivist one.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions test whether you can reproduce a school’s characteristic move on new facts. Paragraph 5 stages exactly this scenario — a decades-old wiretapping-era statute applied to smartphone location data — and assigns each school its question: "textualists ask what the words covered at enactment, while purposivists ask what problem the legislature was trying to solve and how that problem appears today." The stem asks for the textualist’s move, so the answer is the coverage-at-enactment question (choice B). Choice A is the passage’s purposivist move, not the textualist’s — a wrong-viewpoint trap built from the same sentence. Choice C has textualists "depart freely from the text," which contradicts paragraph 1’s rule (apply the text "as written, even when the result seems unwise") and paragraph 4’s point that textualists consult context, not purpose. Choice D states the author’s deeper question from paragraph 6, not any textualist method. Choice E is too-strong: asking what words covered at enactment is not the same as refusing to apply the statute categorically. The discipline is school-labeling: keep each side’s paragraph-5 question attached to the right side.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong-viewpoint. This is paragraph 5’s purposivist move — "what problem the legislature was trying to solve and how that problem appears today." The stem asks for the textualist’s reasoning, so the rival school’s genuine move is the classic trap.`,`Correct. It replays paragraph 5’s textualist move verbatim: the question is what the statutory words covered at enactment, not the legislature’s underlying problem.`,`Opposite. Textualists do not "depart freely from the text"; paragraph 1’s rule applies the text "as written, even when the result seems unwise," and paragraph 4 limits textualist context to neighboring provisions, legal usage, and canons — not purpose.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. "Interpretation cannot be fully separated from judgment" is the passage author’s deeper question in paragraph 6, not a textualist method for deciding cases. The author’s diagnostic conclusion must not be mistaken for either school’s approach.`,`Too-strong. Asking what words covered at enactment leaves open that the 1986 words might cover the app; categorical refusal to apply the statute goes far beyond anything the textualists claim.`],generalLesson:`When a passage assigns each school its characteristic question, application items are won by school-labeling: replay the requested side’s question, not the rival’s.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Paragraph 5 stages this exact scenario — what question does each school ask about new technology?`,`The stem asks for the textualist. Which paragraph-5 question belongs to the textualist?`,`Don’t let the purposivist’s question (the problem the legislature was solving) answer for the textualist.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`},createdAt:`2026-09-11`},{id:`rc-p14-q8`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:4,viewpointComplexity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,4],supportingSentences:[`meaning depends on the mischief the law was meant to cure, not merely on dictionary definitions`,`even the most committed among them begin with the words`],whyDistractorsFail:`A states the textualist rule from paragraph 1; B is contradicted by paragraph 4’s "begin with the words"; D states the author’s paragraph-6 question; E asserts a single-determinate-purpose claim the purposivists never make.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-set-size-diversification-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`New item for 6→8 set-size diversification (2026-09-11): covers the purposivists’ positive definition (P2), previously tested only via the grandson example’s function (q5). Hostile review: credited C is near-verbatim P2; A is P1’s textualist rule; B contradicted by P4 ("begin with the words"); D is the author’s P6 question; E invents a determinacy claim no one makes. Credited choice 109 chars vs longest distractor 105 chars — no length leakage. d3 honest for viewpoint discrimination among four passage-named positions. VALIDATE.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p14`,stem:`Purposivists, as presented in the passage, would most likely maintain that`,choices:[{text:`clear statutory language settles a case even when the resulting outcome seems unwise`},{text:`judges should ignore statutory text entirely and decide cases on the basis of fairness alone`},{text:`a statute’s meaning depends on the mischief it was meant to cure, even when its language appears clear`},{text:`no interpretive method can fully separate the act of interpretation from the exercise of judgment`},{text:`legislative purpose is a discoverable fact that always yields a single determinate answer in every case`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 defines the purposivist position: a statute is "a solution to a problem," and "meaning depends on the mischief the law was meant to cure, not merely on dictionary definitions." Choice C restates that definition; the murderous-heir example exists to prove it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The question asks for the purposivists’ positive claim, stated in paragraph 2 before the grandson example illustrates it. The definition has two halves: statutes are solutions to problems, and meaning follows the mischief-to-be-cured rather than the dictionary. Choice C contains both. Choice A is the textualist rule from paragraph 1 ("apply it as written, even when the result seems unwise") — the rival school’s position. Choice B is too-strong and contradicted: paragraph 4 says "few purposivists ignore the text: even the most committed among them begin with the words." Choice D is the passage author’s deeper question from paragraph 6, not the purposivists’ doctrine. Choice E invents a determinacy claim — the passage never has purposivists claiming purpose always yields one answer; indeed paragraph 3’s textualist objection is precisely that purpose is hard to pin down. The safeguard is to answer from the requested school’s paragraph-2 definition and to reject any choice the passage attributes to a different voice.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong-viewpoint. This is the textualist plain-meaning rule from paragraph 1, the position the purposivists are defined against. The rival school’s genuine rule is the standard wrong-viewpoint trap.`,`Too-strong and contradicted. Paragraph 4 explicitly limits this: "few purposivists ignore the text: even the most committed among them begin with the words." Purposivists depart from text only when it would defeat the law’s evident design — they do not ignore text entirely.`,`Correct. This restates paragraph 2’s definition: statutes are solutions to problems, and meaning follows "the mischief the law was meant to cure, not merely dictionary definitions." The grandson example exists to prove exactly this claim.`,`Wrong-viewpoint. This is the passage author’s concluding diagnostic question (paragraph 6), not the purposivists’ position. The author’s voice must not be confused with either school’s doctrine.`,`Unsupported. The passage never attributes to purposivists the claim that purpose always yields a single determinate answer; paragraph 3 shows the textualists attacking purpose precisely as indeterminate. A claim no one in the passage makes cannot be any school’s position.`],generalLesson:`For "as presented" viewpoint questions, anchor on the school’s definitional paragraph and reject choices voiced by the rival school or by the author.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph defines the purposivist position, before any example is given?`,`The definition has two halves: what a statute IS, and what meaning depends on. Find both.`,`Cross out any choice that states the textualist rule or the author’s final question.`],labels:{topic:`statutory interpretation`},createdAt:`2026-09-11`}],u=[{id:`rc-p15-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`Every act of art restoration is also an act of interpretation`,`Until that question is answered, every cleaned canvas will remain, in part, a portrait of its restorer`],requiredInference:`The main point must survive the final paragraph’s open question: tension plus unresolved authority.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A takes the critics’ extreme as the thesis; B overstates the Sistine debate’s outcome; C contradicts P5 (digital still involves interpretation); E contradicts P3 (reversibility called a fiction).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately states the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Art restoration is inherently unethical because every intervention permanently destroys historical evidence.`},{text:`The cleaning of the Sistine Chapel ceiling was a mistake that art historians have now definitively proven.`},{text:`Digital reconstruction has resolved the ethical dilemmas of physical art restoration.`},{text:`Restoration is an interpretive act torn between preserving and altering objects, and the question of whom it serves remains open.`},{text:`Conservators and art historians agree that the principle of reversibility provides a reliable ethical standard.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The passage opens with restoration-as-interpretation, stages the conservator/critic debate, concedes both sides accept some intervention, notes digital restoration still involves judgment, and closes on the unresolved question of authority. Choice D is the only option spanning that arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The main point must account for the passage's full movement, not just its most dramatic episode. Paragraph 1 frames restoration as interpretation; paragraphs 2–3 stage the debate between conservators (reversibility, Sistine Chapel defense) and critics (patina, irreversibility); paragraph 4 notes the shared concession that some intervention is unavoidable; paragraph 5 shows even digital alternatives involve interpretation; paragraph 6 ends on the unresolved question of authority — maker, public, or future. Choice D captures the central tension (preserve vs. alter) and the open ending. Choice A takes the critics' extreme as the author's view. Choice B overstates the Sistine debate, which the passage leaves contested. Choice C contradicts paragraph 5, which says digital restoration "involves interpretation" too. Choice E contradicts paragraph 3, where critics call reversibility "a comforting fiction."`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the critics' objections are forceful and memorable. It fails because it adopts one side's extreme as the author's thesis; the author explicitly notes in paragraph 4 that both sides accept some intervention is unavoidable. It is a wrong-viewpoint trap.`,`This is tempting because the Sistine controversy gets extended treatment. It fails twice: the passage never declares the cleaning a proven mistake (the debate is presented as ongoing), and one episode cannot be the main point of a passage about the broader ethics of restoration.`,`This is tempting because paragraph 5 presents digital imaging as a promising "third path." It fails because the same paragraph undercuts it: digital restoration "involves interpretation" and embeds "the same judgments the physical restorer makes." The dilemma is not resolved.`,`This is correct. It reflects the passage's thesis (restoration as interpretation, paragraph 1), its central tension (preserve vs. alter, paragraphs 2–4), and its conclusion (the unanswered question of authority, paragraph 6) — the complete arc rather than one episode.`,`This is tempting because reversibility is presented as the profession's "central principle." It fails because paragraph 3 shows critics rejecting it as "a comforting fiction." Agreement is precisely what the passage denies.`],generalLesson:`Main-point answers must survive the passage's final paragraph. If the author ends on an unresolved question, any choice claiming the issue is settled is wrong.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does the first paragraph claim restoration is? And what question does the last paragraph leave open?`,`Be suspicious of any choice that declares one side the winner — does the author ever do that?`,`The correct choice should cover both the central tension of the debate and the unresolved ending.`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}},{id:`rc-p15-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`the patina of age`,`possibly Michelangelo’s own final glazes, which the restorers mistook for dirt`],whyDistractorsFail:`A scrambles the conservators’ defense into a critics’ charge; C invents a tourism motive; D contradicts P2’s documented interventions; E misreads the charge as violation rather than impossibility.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`According to the passage, critics of the Sistine Chapel cleaning argued that the restoration`,choices:[{text:`was performed with materials that could not be removed by future conservators`},{text:`destroyed authentic features, including the patina of age and possibly Michelangelo's own final glazes`},{text:`was motivated primarily by a desire to attract more tourists to the Vatican`},{text:`failed to document the interventions with sufficient care for future generations`},{text:`ignored the principle of reversibility that the conservators themselves claimed to follow`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 lists the critics' charges: the cleaning destroyed "the patina of age, the subtle tonal harmonies," and possibly "Michelangelo's own final glazes, which the restorers mistook for dirt." Choice B restates this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a straightforward detail question anchored in paragraph 3. The critics' argument has two layers: the philosophical claim (reversibility is a fiction) and the factual charge about what was lost — patina, tonal harmonies experienced by centuries of viewers, and possibly the artist's own glazes. Choice B combines the key losses. Choice A reverses the issue: removable materials are the conservators' defense, not the critics' charge. Choice C invents a tourism motive the passage never mentions. Choice D contradicts the passage: meticulous documentation is part of the conservators' framework (paragraph 2), and critics do not dispute the documentation. Choice E misattributes: critics attack reversibility as a fiction, not as a principle the restorers violated. The broader lesson is about reading critics precisely: the passage's critics grant documentation and sincerity while denying that reversibility holds in practice, so any choice crediting them with a different objection misreads the dispute.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because reversibility and removable materials are discussed nearby. It fails because removable materials are the conservators' defense (paragraph 2), not something critics accused them of neglecting. It scrambles who claimed what.`,`This is correct. Paragraph 3 explicitly lists these losses: the patina of age, the tonal harmonies centuries of viewers experienced, and possibly Michelangelo's own final glazes mistaken for dirt. It is a direct restatement of the critics' factual charge.`,`This is tempting because tourism is a common motive in cultural controversies. It fails because the passage never mentions tourism or visitor numbers as a motive for the cleaning. It is entirely unsupported.`,`This is tempting because documentation is emphasized in paragraph 2. It fails because documentation is presented as something the conservators did do; the critics' objection is that no record can undo the alteration, not that records were poorly kept.`,`This is tempting because reversibility is the central principle under dispute. It fails because the critics' charge is deeper: not that restorers violated reversibility, but that reversibility itself is "a comforting fiction" — unachievable in principle.`],generalLesson:`Detail questions often test whether you keep the two sides' claims straight. When choices mix true passage statements with wrong attributions, verify who said what.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The critics' specific charges are listed in the third paragraph. What exactly do they say was lost?`,`Distinguish the critics' factual charge (what was destroyed) from their philosophical charge (about reversibility).`,`Eliminate any choice describing the conservators' defense rather than the critics' attack.`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}},{id:`rc-p15-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`even this compromise involves interpretation`,`the same judgments the physical restorer makes`],requiredInference:`When the author presents a compromise and undercuts it, the inference is that the underlying problem survives the compromise.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A prescribes what the author never prescribes; B mischaracterizes an ethical dispute as a chemistry one; D speculates beyond the text; E contradicts the burden-of-proof and authenticity disagreements.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Museums should exhibit only unrestored works to avoid misrepresenting the artist's intentions.`},{text:`The dispute over restoration ultimately turns on incompatible factual claims about chemistry and materials science.`},{text:`Even approaches presented as compromises in the restoration debate require the same judgment that makes restoration controversial.`},{text:`Future generations will likely reverse most twentieth-century restorations once better techniques emerge.`},{text:`Art historians and conservators fundamentally agree on the goals of restoration but disagree only about methods.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 presents digital restoration as a "third path" and "compromise," then undercuts it: it "involves interpretation" and embeds "the same judgments the physical restorer makes." Choice C restates this inference.`,explanationWalkthrough:`"Most strongly supported" signals an inference that stays close to the text. Paragraph 5 is the key: digital imaging is introduced as a compromise — exhibit the damaged original alongside its virtual reconstruction — but the author immediately notes that "even this compromise involves interpretation: someone must decide which pigments to simulate and which losses to fill." The supported inference is that compromise solutions do not escape the judgment problem; they relocate it. Choice C states exactly this. Choice A is a prescription the author never makes (paragraph 4 concedes some intervention is unavoidable). Choice B mischaracterizes the dispute, which the passage frames as ethical and interpretive, not as a chemistry disagreement. Choice D speculates about the future beyond anything stated. Choice E contradicts the passage: the sides disagree about the burden of proof and about what counts as authentic — goals, not just methods.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the critics value authenticity highly. It fails because the author never prescribes exhibiting only unrestored works; paragraph 4 concedes intervention is sometimes unavoidable, and the author's stance is diagnostic, not prescriptive.`,`This is tempting because conservation involves technical science. It fails because the passage frames the dispute as ethical and interpretive — about authenticity, authority, and the burden of proof — not as a factual disagreement about chemistry.`,`This is correct. Paragraph 5 sets up digital restoration as a compromise and then shows it requires the same interpretive decisions (which pigments, which losses to fill) that make physical restoration controversial. The inference follows directly.`,`This is tempting because reversibility is meant to protect future generations' options. It fails because the passage never predicts what future generations will actually do; it only notes the principle aims to preserve their ability to decide.`,`This is tempting as a "reasonable people disagree" reading. It fails because the passage shows disagreement about ends: critics value the accumulated history of the object while conservators prioritize recovering the artist's hand — a conflict over what restoration is for, not merely how to do it.`],generalLesson:`Strong inferences stay close to the author's explicit moves. When the author presents a compromise and then undercuts it, the inference is that the underlying problem survives the compromise.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`too-strong`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Focus on the fifth paragraph: what does the author say about digital restoration, the apparent compromise?`,`The key sentence is "even this compromise involves interpretation." What follows from that about compromise solutions generally?`,`Eliminate choices that prescribe actions or predict the future — the author does neither.`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}},{id:`rc-p15-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`Both sides, notably, accept that some intervention is unavoidable`,`The real dispute is about the burden of proof`],requiredInference:`A both-sides-accept opening is a narrowing move: the payoff is the precisely defined remaining disagreement.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A invents a new objection; C invents a structural/aesthetic distinction; D mistakes a shared premise for proved evidence; E mistakes a concession for a conclusion.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`The fourth paragraph functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`introduce a new objection to restoration that neither side had previously considered`},{text:`establish shared ground between the opposing sides in order to narrow the real disagreement`},{text:`argue that structural repairs are ethically distinct from aesthetic restorations`},{text:`provide scientific evidence that paintings inevitably disintegrate without intervention`},{text:`conclude the debate by showing that the critics' position is ultimately untenable`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 opens "Both sides, notably, accept that some intervention is unavoidable" and then reframes the dispute as being about "the burden of proof." Its function is to locate common ground so the remaining disagreement is precisely defined. Choice B states this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask what a paragraph does in the argument's architecture. Paragraph 4 begins with an explicit concession both sides share — intervention is sometimes unavoidable — and then uses that shared premise to sharpen what is actually contested: where the burden of proof lies. This is a classic narrowing move: agree on the easy point to isolate the hard one. Choice B describes exactly this. Choice A fails because no new objection appears; the paragraph synthesizes. Choice C fails because the paragraph never distinguishes structural from aesthetic repair. Choice D fails because no scientific evidence is offered — the claim about deterioration is a shared premise, not a proven finding. Choice E fails because the paragraph does not conclude anything against the critics; the debate continues into paragraphs 5 and 6.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the paragraph adds nuance. It fails because it introduces no new objection — the burden-of-proof framing refines the existing dispute rather than adding a fresh charge either side had missed.`,`This is correct. The paragraph's "both sides, notably, accept" opening establishes common ground (some intervention is unavoidable), and the burden-of-proof contrast then pinpoints the actual remaining disagreement. That is its structural job.`,`This is tempting because structural decay (flaking, corrosion) is mentioned. It fails because the paragraph draws no ethical distinction between structural and aesthetic work; both are covered by the same burden-of-proof analysis.`,`This is tempting because deterioration is stated as fact. It fails because the paragraph offers no scientific evidence — the inevitability of decay is treated as a shared background assumption, not as a finding being proved.`,`This is tempting if one misreads concessions as conclusions. It fails because the paragraph leaves the critics' core objection intact and the passage continues debating for two more paragraphs; nothing is concluded here.`],generalLesson:`Concession paragraphs ("both sides accept...") usually function to narrow the dispute, not end it. Ask what disagreement remains after the concession — that is the paragraph's payoff.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are the first five words of the paragraph? What work does that opening do?`,`After establishing what both sides accept, what does the paragraph say they still disagree about?`,`The correct answer describes a narrowing move, not a concluding or rebutting one.`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}},{id:`rc-p15-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-author-viewpoint`,secondarySkills:[`rc-attitude`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3,6],supportingSentences:[`Its central principle is reversibility`,`a comforting fiction`],requiredInference:`Strong evaluative language stays with its quoted owner unless the author adopts it; the open ending confirms neutrality.`,viewpointOwner:`The author (reporting both sides evenhandedly; “comforting fiction” belongs to the critics).`,whyDistractorsFail:`A ignores the unrebutted objections; B upgrades philosophical criticism to a self-serving accusation; D overweights the digital paragraph; E invents a hypocrisy charge.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`The author's attitude toward the principle of reversibility is best described as`,choices:[{text:`wholehearted endorsement of it as the solution to restoration's ethical problems`},{text:`dismissal of it as a self-serving invention of the conservation profession`},{text:`neutral reporting of it as one side's position, complicated by serious objections`},{text:`indifference, since the passage is concerned only with digital restoration`},{text:`hostility toward conservators for failing to live up to it`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The author presents reversibility as the conservators' "central principle" (paragraph 2) and then reports the critics' charge that it is "a comforting fiction" (paragraph 3) without taking a side — the passage ends on an open question. Choice C matches this evenhanded treatment.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Attitude questions require tracking the author's own stance as distinct from the reported views. The author introduces reversibility descriptively as the profession's ethical framework, gives its rationale (protecting future generations' options), then gives critics full voice to attack it, and never resolves the dispute — the final paragraph leaves the deeper question of authority open. That is neutral, thorough reporting rather than endorsement or dismissal. Choice C captures this. Choice A fails because the author relays devastating objections without rebuttal. Choice B fails because "self-serving invention" is far harsher than anything the author says; the criticism reported is philosophical, not accusatory. Choice D fails because most of the passage concerns physical restoration. Choice E fails because the author never accuses conservators of violating the principle.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because reversibility is presented sympathetically as a thoughtful framework. It fails because the author gives the critics' "comforting fiction" objection equal weight and never endorses the principle as a solution — the passage ends with the underlying question unanswered.`,`This is tempting because the critics' attack is sharp. It fails because the sharp words ("comforting fiction") belong to the critics, not the author, and the author never suggests the principle is self-serving — only that its achievability is disputed.`,`This is correct. The author reports reversibility as the conservators' central principle, reports serious objections to it, and takes no side — consistent with the passage's overall evenhanded, open-ended stance.`,`This is tempting only if one overweights the digital paragraph. It fails because four of six paragraphs concern physical restoration, and reversibility is central to the ethical framework under discussion throughout.`,`This is tempting because the debate is adversarial. It fails because the author never charges conservators with hypocrisy or failure; the dispute presented is about whether reversibility is possible, not whether conservators honor it.`],generalLesson:`Separate the author's attitude from the attitudes reported. Strong evaluative language ("comforting fiction") belongs to a quoted side unless the author adopts it — check whether the author ever endorses it.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-author-viewpoint`,`rc-attitude`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Who calls reversibility "a comforting fiction" — the author, or someone the author is reporting on?`,`Does the author ever say which side is right about reversibility? Check the final paragraph for a verdict.`,`The author's stance matches the passage's overall posture toward the whole debate. How would you describe that posture?`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}},{id:`rc-p15-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`reversibility is a comforting fiction`,`every restoration gambles the irreplaceable on the taste of the present`],requiredInference:`Extract the critics’ operative principles (cleaning destroys authenticity; safeguards are insufficient) and test each proposal against them.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A, C, D, E describe non-intervention or pure documentation, which the critics favor; only B triggers both objections.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: one defensible answer; explanation claims verified against passage text; leakage discipline confirmed (credited <=1.25x, gap <=20).`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p15`,stem:`Based on the passage, a critic of restoration as described in the third paragraph would most likely object to which one of the following proposals?`,choices:[{text:`A museum's decision to leave a flaking medieval altarpiece untreated until better conservation methods are developed`},{text:`A proposal to clean a landscape painting to remove yellowed varnish, with every step photographed and all materials fully removable`},{text:`A plan to build a climate-controlled storage facility to slow the deterioration of textile collections`},{text:`A project to create a digital archive of photographs documenting a fresco's current condition`},{text:`A conservator's refusal to reconstruct missing sections of a damaged mosaic`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The paragraph-3 critics hold that cleaning destroys authentic features (patina, possibly original glazes) and that reversibility is a fiction — so a cleaning, however carefully documented, is exactly what they oppose. The other options involve non-intervention or pure documentation, which the critics favor.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions ask you to extend a stated position to a new case. The paragraph-3 critics make two claims: (1) cleaning destroys authentic historical features, and (2) no intervention is truly reversible. A proposal to clean a painting — even with documentation and removable materials — triggers both objections: the varnish removal may strip original glazes, and the documentation does not undo the alteration. Choice B is therefore the natural target. The distractors all describe things the critics would welcome or at least not oppose: leaving the altarpiece untreated (A) is the non-interference they favor; climate-controlled storage (C) prevents deterioration without altering the object; photographic documentation (D) is pure recording; refusing to reconstruct (E) aligns with their minimal-intervention stance. Note the trap in B's details — "photographed" and "fully removable" are the conservators' defenses, which the critics explicitly reject as insufficient.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because it involves a decision about a damaged work. It fails because non-intervention is what the critics favor — they place the burden of proof on those who would intervene. A critic would applaud, not object to, waiting.`,`This is correct. The critics' two objections apply directly: the cleaning may destroy authentic features (patina, original glazes), and the safeguards mentioned — photography, removable materials — are precisely the reversibility framework the critics call "a comforting fiction."`,`This is tempting because it is a conservation action. It fails because climate control prevents decay without altering the object's surface; nothing is cleaned, filled, or overpainted, so neither of the critics' objections is triggered.`,`This is tempting because it involves a fresco and technology. It fails because pure photographic documentation alters nothing; the critics object to physical intervention, not to recording the work's condition.`,`This is tempting because it concerns a damaged object. It fails because refusing to reconstruct is restraint, which is the critics' preferred posture — they argue the burden of proof lies on interveners.`],generalLesson:`To apply a viewpoint, extract its operative principles first (here: cleaning destroys authenticity; reversibility is fictional), then test each new case against those principles — not against your own instincts about the case.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`half-right`,`opposite`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are the paragraph-3 critics' two main objections? State them as general principles.`,`Now test each proposal against those principles: which one involves cleaning or altering the object's surface?`,`Notice the reassuring details in one choice — photographed steps, removable materials. How do the critics regard those safeguards?`],labels:{topic:`art restoration ethics`}}],d=[{id:`rc-p16-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`pilots have clarified what adopting one would entail`,`The evidence can discipline the debate, but it cannot end it`],requiredInference:`Synthesize the author's middle position across all six paragraphs: real findings plus principled limits plus design value plus unsettleable value questions.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Each takes one side's extreme (A advocates, C critics, B) or shrinks the passage to a single paragraph's finding (E).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Main-point middle-position item; every distractor is a one-sided extreme or a single-paragraph shrink; no leakage.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Basic income pilots have conclusively proven that a universal basic income would succeed if adopted nationally, in any economy`},{text:`The debate over basic income is purely ideological, and empirical evidence has no role to play in it.`},{text:`Critics have shown that basic income pilots are methodologically worthless and should be discontinued.`},{text:`Pilot programs provide genuine but limited evidence: useful for policy design yet unable to settle whether societies should adopt a basic income.`},{text:`The primary value of basic income pilots is demonstrating that recipients spend cash payments responsibly.`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The passage presents both the real findings (paragraph 2) and the critics' limits (paragraphs 3–4), then concludes pilots are valuable design tools (paragraph 5) but cannot resolve the deeper value disagreements (paragraph 6). Choice D captures that balanced thesis.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The author's thesis is a middle position between the advocates' vindication narrative and the skeptics' dismissal. Paragraph 2 reports real, favorable findings; paragraphs 3–4 report serious limits on what pilots can prove (temporariness, outside funding, scale effects); paragraph 5 finds common ground in pilots as design tools; paragraph 6 concludes the deepest disagreements are about values no trial can settle. Choice D is the only option holding both halves — genuine evidence plus principled limits. Choice A takes the advocates' overclaim as fact, which the author explicitly rejects ("overclaimed"). Choice B goes too far: the author says evidence can "discipline the debate," so it has a role. Choice C takes the critics' extreme, but the author credits pilots with real design value. Choice E shrinks the passage to one finding from paragraph 2, ignoring the entire critical apparatus.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because paragraph 2 reports genuinely favorable findings. It fails because the author explicitly says the evidence is "overclaimed" and devotes two paragraphs to limits on what pilots prove. "Conclusively proven" contradicts the passage's central caution.`,`This is tempting because the final paragraph emphasizes value disagreements. It fails because the author says evidence can "discipline the debate" — a real role — and credits pilots with clarifying design questions. "No role" is too strong.`,`This is tempting because the critics' objections (temporariness, funding, scale) are formidable. It fails because the author explicitly rejects dismissal: paragraph 5 says even skeptics acknowledge pilots answered practical design questions. The author's verdict is "limited," not "worthless."`,`This is correct. It mirrors the passage's structure exactly: real findings (paragraph 2), principled limits (paragraphs 3–4), design value (paragraph 5), and the unsettleable remainder (paragraph 6). It is the author's thesis, not either side's slogan.`,`This is tempting because responsible spending is indeed reported in paragraph 2. It fails as too narrow: it captures one finding while ignoring the passage's main business, which is evaluating what the evidence as a whole can and cannot establish.`],generalLesson:`When a passage stages a debate and then stakes out a middle position, the main point is the middle position — not the strongest argument of either side.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`half-right`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author fully agree with the advocates? With the critics? Check paragraphs 2 and 5 for signs of a middle position.`,`The final paragraph says the evidence can "discipline the debate, but it cannot end it." Which choice reflects that two-sided verdict?`,`Eliminate choices that declare the pilots conclusive, worthless, or irrelevant — the author rejects all three extremes.`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}},{id:`rc-p16-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`concede the point but reverse its moral`,`a reason to run larger experiments, not a reason to dismiss the idea`],viewpointOwner:`supporters of a basic income`,whyDistractorsFail:`A states the critics' position; C, D, E contradict passage facts (paragraphs 5-6).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Viewpoint attribution is exact; choice A is a clean viewpoint-reversal trap.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`Supporters of a basic income, as described in the passage, would most likely agree with which one of the following statements?`,choices:[{text:`Pilot programs are too small to provide any information relevant to basic income policy.`},{text:`The absence of large-scale evidence for basic income justifies larger experiments rather than dismissing the idea.`},{text:`Taxes required to fund a permanent basic income would have no effect on labor markets.`},{text:`Monthly payments and lump-sum payments produce identical outcomes for recipients.`},{text:`Questions about work, security, and the state's obligations can be settled by randomized trials.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 attributes this exact move to supporters: they "concede the point but reverse its moral," arguing the lack of large-scale evidence is "a reason to run larger experiments, not a reason to dismiss the idea." Choice B restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Other-viewpoint questions require matching a choice to the specified speaker's stated position. The supporters' distinctive argumentative move appears in paragraph 4: confronted with the scale objection, they concede it and reverse its implication — absence of evidence warrants bigger tests, not rejection. Choice B is nearly verbatim. Choice A states the critics' position, a viewpoint reversal. Choice C goes beyond anything supporters claim; they do not deny tax effects. Choice D contradicts paragraph 5, where monthly payments are said to work better than lump sums. Choice E contradicts paragraph 6, which says those value questions are precisely what "no randomized trial can answer" — a point the author makes about the debate generally, and supporters are not shown claiming otherwise.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because scale limitations are discussed at length. It fails as a viewpoint reversal: the "too small to matter" reasoning belongs to the critics; supporters explicitly argue the opposite — that limited evidence calls for larger experiments.`,`This is correct. Paragraph 4 gives supporters this exact response to the scale objection: concede the evidentiary gap, but treat it as a reason to expand experimentation rather than abandon the policy. It is their characteristic move.`,`This is tempting because supporters minimize the critics' worries. It fails because the passage never attributes this claim to supporters; the tax question is raised by critics as an unanswered problem, and supporters are not shown denying it.`,`This is tempting as a detail from the design discussion. It fails because paragraph 5 says the opposite: monthly payments "appear to work better than lump sums." The choice misstates a passage fact.`,`This is tempting because supporters are enthusiastic about evidence. It fails because paragraph 6 — describing the debate as a whole — says these value questions are what "no randomized trial can answer," and supporters are never shown disputing that boundary.`],generalLesson:`For viewpoint questions, anchor each choice to the paragraph where that speaker's position is stated, and be alert to choices that belong to the other side.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Where does the passage describe how supporters respond to objections? Focus on the fourth paragraph.`,`Supporters "concede the point but reverse its moral" — what does that reversal look like?`,`Eliminate any choice that sounds like something the critics would say instead.`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}},{id:`rc-p16-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`usually funded by outside grants`,`reveal nothing about the taxes that would be needed`],whyDistractorsFail:`A, B, D, E are invented or directly contradicted by paragraph 2's reported findings.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Direct paragraph-3 retrieval; distractors are invented or contradicted.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`According to the passage, critics argue that basic income pilots are limited in what they can prove because the pilots`,choices:[{text:`enroll only participants who are already employed full-time`},{text:`measure outcomes using inconsistent methods across different countries`},{text:`are typically funded by outside grants rather than by the taxes a permanent program would require`},{text:`give participants payments that are too small to affect their behavior`},{text:`fail to collect data on health and educational outcomes, focusing only on employment statistics`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 makes the funding point explicitly: pilots are "usually funded by outside grants, so they reveal nothing about the taxes that would be needed to pay for a genuine universal program." Choice C restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 3 lists three distinct limits: temporariness (participants know payments end), funding (outside grants, not program taxes), and by extension the scale point developed in paragraph 4. Choice C matches the funding limit verbatim. Choice A is invented; the passage says employment effects concentrate among students, parents, and job-switchers, implying varied enrollment. Choice B is invented; no claim about inconsistent methods appears. Choice D contradicts the passage, which reports measurable behavioral and well-being effects. Choice E is directly contradicted: paragraph 2 reports health and schooling findings as headline results. The correct answer comes from the middle of the critics' enumerated limits in paragraph 3, where funding sits alongside temporariness; together these explain why behavior under a grant-funded pilot may not predict behavior under a tax-funded permanent program.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because employment effects are discussed. It fails because the passage never restricts enrollment to full-time workers; it describes effects "concentrated among students, new parents, and people between jobs," implying the opposite.`,`This is tempting because cross-country pilots are mentioned. It fails because the passage never criticizes measurement methods or consistency; the critics' objections are about temporariness, funding, and scale — not methodology.`,`This is correct. Paragraph 3 states it directly: outside-grant funding means pilots "reveal nothing about the taxes that would be needed," severing the link between pilot behavior and the fiscal reality of a permanent program.`,`This is tempting because payment size could plausibly matter. It fails because the passage reports real effects on stress, health, and hours worked — evidence that payments did affect behavior. The critics' point is about duration and funding, not size.`,`This is tempting as a generic research weakness. It fails because it is factually wrong about the passage: health improvements and longer school enrollment are among the headline findings reported in paragraph 2.`],generalLesson:`Detail questions test precise recall of a specific paragraph's list. When a paragraph gives multiple reasons, match the choice to one of them exactly — do not accept plausible-sounding additions.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`opposite`,`keyword-match`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The critics' specific objections are enumerated in the third paragraph. List them.`,`One objection concerns how the pilots are paid for. What follows from outside-grant funding?`,`Eliminate choices that contradict findings the passage reports as established.`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}},{id:`rc-p16-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`,`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5,6],supportingSentences:[`have clarified what adopting one would entail`,`the experiments' most important product`],requiredInference:`Map the phrase "most important product" (paragraph 6) back to the clarification defined in paragraph 5.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A overstates "modest" findings; C is unsupported; D contradicts paragraph 4; E contradicts the evenhanded treatment.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Near-verbatim ranking sentence; distractors each contradict a specific paragraph.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`It can most reasonably be inferred that the author believes the most important product of basic income pilots is`,choices:[{text:`proof that unconditional cash payments do not reduce work effort`},{text:`clarification of the practical questions involved in designing a basic income program`},{text:`evidence that basic income would be politically popular with voters`},{text:`demonstration that small-scale experiments can fully predict economy-wide effects`},{text:`confirmation that the critics' objections to basic income are unfounded`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5 concludes that pilots "have not settled whether societies should adopt a basic income, but they have clarified what adopting one would entail," and paragraph 6 calls that clarification "the experiments' most important product." Choice B restates this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The phrase "most important product" is nearly quoted from paragraph 6: "That clarification may be the experiments' most important product." The clarification in question is defined in paragraph 5 — answers to practical design questions (payment frequency, conditionality, administrative simplicity) — coupled with the explicit denial that pilots settle the adoption question. Choice B captures this precisely. Choice A overstates: employment effects were "modest," not proof of zero reduction. Choice C is unsupported; political popularity is never discussed. Choice D contradicts paragraph 4's scale argument. Choice E contradicts the author's evenhanded treatment — the critics' objections are presented as serious and largely conceded. The author's ranking is explicit rather than inferred: paragraph 6 names clarification as "the experiments' most important product," so the strongly supported answer is the one that restates that ranking without upgrading it into a verdict on adoption.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because paragraph 2 reports modest employment effects. It fails because "proof" and "do not reduce" overstate the finding — the passage reports small reductions in hours, and the author never claims the work-effort question is settled.`,`This is correct. Paragraphs 5–6 define the pilots' chief value as clarification: they answered design questions (timing, conditionality, simplicity) even though they cannot settle the adoption question. The author names this "the experiments' most important product."`,`This is tempting because policy debates involve politics. It fails because voter popularity is never mentioned anywhere in the passage; the discussion is about evidence and values, not electoral prospects.`,`This is tempting as an optimistic reading of experiments. It fails because it directly contradicts paragraph 4, where the author endorses the critics' point that small pilots "cannot capture economy-wide effects."`,`This is tempting if one reads the author as pro-basic-income. It fails because the author treats the critics' objections as legitimate limits, conceding temporariness, funding, and scale problems rather than refuting them.`],generalLesson:`When the author explicitly ranks something ("most important product"), the inference answer is usually a careful restatement of that ranking — check the paragraphs just before the ranking sentence.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The final paragraph names "the experiments' most important product." What is "that clarification" referring back to?`,`Look at the fifth paragraph: what did the pilots actually settle, according to the author?`,`The correct answer pairs a positive (what pilots clarified) with the limit (what they did not settle).`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}},{id:`rc-p16-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`,`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`Testing a lifeboat, the critics say, tells you little about building an ark`],requiredInference:`Identify the abstract claim the analogy illustrates: partial, temporary, grant-funded pilots cannot validate a permanent universal program.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A literalizes the analogy; C reverses its point; D invents a historical claim; E misreads the evenhanded tone.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Classic analogy-function item; correct answer tracks paragraph 3's thesis.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`The author's reference to testing "a lifeboat" as opposed to "building an ark" serves primarily to`,choices:[{text:`suggest that basic income pilots are as dangerous as untested seacraft`},{text:`illustrate the critics' point that a small, temporary experiment cannot validate a permanent, society-wide program`},{text:`argue that policymakers should prefer small pilot programs to large-scale reforms`},{text:`provide historical evidence that small experiments have often preceded major social programs, from vaccines to microfinance`},{text:`mock the supporters of basic income for their unrealistic expectations`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The analogy closes paragraph 3, the critics' paragraph: a lifeboat test (the pilot) tells you little about building an ark (a permanent universal program) because the scale, duration, and funding differ fundamentally. Choice B states that illustrative function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Analogies in argumentative prose illustrate an abstract point vividly; the function question asks which point this one illustrates. It appears at the end of paragraph 3, which argues pilots are "partial and temporary" and funded by grants rather than program taxes. The lifeboat/ark contrast dramatizes exactly that gap: success at small, temporary scale does not validate the large, permanent enterprise. Choice B states this. Choice A literalizes the analogy into a danger claim the passage never makes. Choice C reverses the point — the analogy criticizes reliance on pilots, not endorses them. Choice D invents a historical claim; the analogy is illustrative, not evidentiary. Choice E misreads tone: the passage is evenhanded, and the analogy is presented as the critics' serious argument, not mockery.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because analogies can carry warnings. It fails because nothing in the passage suggests pilots are dangerous; the point is about what they can prove, not about risk. It literalizes a figurative comparison.`,`This is correct. The analogy caps the critics' paragraph: just as testing a lifeboat does not validate building an ark, a partial, temporary, grant-funded pilot cannot validate a permanent, universal, tax-funded program. It makes the scale objection memorable.`,`This is tempting because pilots are small programs. It fails because the analogy's point runs the other way — it warns against treating the small test as validation of the large program, rather than recommending small tests over reforms.`,`This is tempting because the analogy sounds historical. It fails because no historical claim is made; the lifeboat and ark are hypothetical illustrations, not evidence from past social programs.`,`This is tempting because debates breed ridicule. It fails on tone: the author presents the critics' argument respectfully as part of an evenhanded analysis, and the analogy is offered as their reasoning, not as mockery of supporters.`],generalLesson:`Analogies illustrate; to find their function, identify the abstract claim in the surrounding sentences and match the analogy to it. Never literalize the comparison.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph contains the analogy, and whose argument is that paragraph making?`,`What abstract point do the sentences just before the analogy establish about pilots?`,`The analogy contrasts something small and temporary with something large and permanent — what does each correspond to?`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}},{id:`rc-p16-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-attitude`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,5,6],supportingSentences:[`evidence can discipline the debate, but it cannot end it`],viewpointOwner:`the author`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is the advocates' position; B is the critics' extreme; D ignores the empirical engagement; E confuses limits on inference with distrust of findings.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Attitude requires both halves; distractors capture only one or misattribute.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p16`,stem:`The author's attitude toward basic income pilot programs is best described as`,choices:[{text:`enthusiastic advocacy for their immediate expansion into permanent policy`},{text:`outright rejection of them as a waste of research resources`},{text:`qualified appreciation: valuable for design questions, but incapable of settling the fundamental debate`},{text:`indifference, since the passage focuses on theoretical rather than empirical questions`},{text:`skepticism that any empirical findings about basic income are trustworthy`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The author credits pilots with real findings (paragraph 2) and genuine design value (paragraph 5) while insisting they cannot prove what advocates claim (paragraphs 3–4) or resolve value disagreements (paragraph 6). Choice C captures this qualified stance.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Attitude questions require synthesizing the author's evaluations across the passage. The author is consistently two-handed: paragraph 2 reports favorable findings without debunking them; paragraphs 3–4 present the critics' limits as serious; paragraph 5 finds common ground in design value; paragraph 6 limits what evidence can do. That is neither advocacy nor rejection but qualified appreciation. Choice C states both halves. Choice A fails because the author stresses the evidence is "overclaimed" and cannot justify immediate adoption. Choice B fails because paragraph 5 explicitly credits pilots with answering practical questions. Choice D fails because the passage is substantially about empirical findings. Choice E fails because the author accepts the measurements ("Critics do not dispute the measurements") and disputes only the inferences drawn from them.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the reported findings are favorable. It fails because the author's own verdict is cautionary: the evidence is "overclaimed," and pilots "have not settled whether societies should adopt a basic income." Enthusiasm for immediate adoption is the advocates' position, not the author's.`,`This is tempting because the critics' objections occupy two full paragraphs. It fails because the author explicitly salvages the pilots in paragraph 5, noting even skeptics acknowledge their design value. Rejection is the extreme the author avoids.`,`This is correct. It holds together the passage's two consistent judgments: pilots genuinely advanced knowledge of program design (paragraphs 2, 5) yet cannot validate a permanent program or resolve the underlying value disputes (paragraphs 3, 4, 6).`,`This is tempting only as a misreading of the theoretical closing. It fails because most of the passage analyzes empirical findings in detail; the author is engaged with the evidence, not indifferent to it.`,`This is tempting because limits on inference are emphasized. It fails by confusing limits on what findings prove with distrust of the findings themselves — the author accepts the measurements and questions only the leap from pilot to policy.`],generalLesson:`Authorial attitude in a balanced passage is usually itself balanced. Look for the author's verdict sentences (often near the end) and check that your choice preserves both the positive and negative halves.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-attitude`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author think the pilot findings are real? Does the author think they settle the question? The attitude must accommodate both answers.`,`Paragraph 5 ("common ground") and paragraph 6 ("cannot end it") together define the author's stance. What do they say?`,`Eliminate any choice that makes the author sound like an advocate or a debunker — the author is neither.`],labels:{topic:`universal basic income pilots`}}],f=[{id:`rc-p17-q1`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`coral conservation meant one thing: protection`,`broke that assumption`,`The institutional expression of that shift`,`The funding shift also exposed a governance gap`,`The result is a hybrid institutional regime`],requiredInference:`Main point = institutional transformation protection -> intervention + governance gap.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A too-strong; B opposite; C unsupported; D opposite prescription.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Marine parks have proven useless against ocean warming and should be abolished entirely, with their waters and budgets transferred to large-scale genetic engineering of reefs.`},{text:`Coral conservation shifted from marine-park protection to a hybrid regime where public programs engineer reefs for a hotter ocean, raising governance questions the old laws never anticipated.`},{text:`The 1998 mass-bleaching event demonstrated that coral reefs are doomed regardless of conservation policy, so further spending is unjustified.`},{text:`The Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program has already completed the full restoration of the Great Barrier Reef’s northern sector.`},{text:`Conservation agencies should abandon all intervention programs and return to the preservation model that prevailed in the 1970s.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage traces institutions, not just ideas: the marine-park protection era (paragraph 1), the 1998 bleaching shock and the U.S. legislative response (paragraph 2), Australia’s Reef 2050 and the 2016–17 bleaching (paragraph 3), the 2020 interventionist turn with the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program (paragraph 4), the governance gap it exposed (paragraph 5), and the resulting hybrid regime (paragraph 6). Only choice B spans that institutional arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Because the passage is institutional history, its main point must name the institutions and their transformation, not merely the underlying science. Paragraph 1 establishes the baseline: the marine park as the institutional model, with the operating assumption that reefs left alone would recover. Paragraph 2 marks the first rupture — the 1998 bleaching event — and the first institutional response, the U.S. Coral Reef Conservation Act of 2000 and its “resilience” reframing. Paragraph 3 follows Australia’s parallel arc through Reef 2050 (2015) to the 2016–17 bleaching that shifted funding “from keeping threats out to building tolerance in.” Paragraph 4 gives the transformation its name and budget: the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program (2020, roughly 150 million Australian dollars), premised on deliberately engineering reefs. Paragraph 5 shows the transformation outrunning the law — permits, liability, cross-boundary coordination unanticipated by statute. Paragraph 6 synthesizes: a hybrid regime, protection as base layer, intervention as growing overlay, with political decisions still pending. Choice B is the only answer containing the starting point, the transformation, and the governance consequence. Choice A is too strong — the passage says protection “remains the base layer.” Choice C contradicts the passage’s premise that intervention is worth attempting. Choice D invents a completed restoration the passage never claims. Choice E prescribes the reverse of the described trajectory.`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The passage’s final paragraph explicitly states that protection “remains the base layer” and that “no serious proposal abandons water quality or fishing limits.” Abolishing marine parks goes far beyond anything the text supports.`,`Correct. It tracks the passage’s institutional chronology — marine-park protection, the bleaching shocks, the legislative and programmatic responses, the interventionist turn — and includes the governance consequence of paragraph 5 and the hybrid-regime synthesis of paragraph 6. Nothing in it exceeds the text.`,`Opposite. The passage treats reefs as worth active, expensive intervention; nothing suggests doom or recommends ending spending. This choice contradicts the entire institutional trajectory the text describes.`,`Unsupported. The program is described as conducting research and field trials — breeding, outplanting experiments — not as having completed restoration of any sector. “Already completed” invents an outcome the passage never claims.`,`Opposite prescription. The passage describes a one-directional institutional shift from protection toward intervention; it never advocates reversing course. This choice prescribes what the text presents as the abandoned past.`],generalLesson:`For institutional-history passages, the main point must name the institutions, the sequence of change, and its consequences — not just the topic area.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`half-right`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the passage describe the science of corals or the history of conservation institutions?`,`What does the final paragraph say remains the “base layer”?`,`The main point must include the governance gap, not just the programs.`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}},{id:`rc-p17-q2`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,linguisticComplexity:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`the marine park — set boundaries, restrict fishing and development, and let the reef heal itself`,`reefs were resilient if left alone`,`Intervention in the reef’s biology was not debated; it was simply outside the institutional imagination`],whyDistractorsFail:`A intervention era; B misstates; C unsupported; D opposite era.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`According to the passage, the operating assumption of the marine-park era was that`,choices:[{text:`corals could be selectively bred in laboratories and then deployed onto degraded reefs.`},{text:`establishing marine reserves would reverse the warming of the ocean itself over time.`},{text:`mass-bleaching events were caused primarily by overfishing inside the parks’ boundaries.`},{text:`governments should finance the genetic engineering of wild reef species directly.`},{text:`undisturbed reefs would recover on their own once local stresses like fishing and development were removed.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states the assumption directly: reefs were “resilient if left alone,” so removing local stresses would let “the ecosystem’s own recuperative powers” work — and biological intervention was “outside the institutional imagination.” Only choice E reproduces it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 1 defines the marine-park era by its institutional logic, and the question asks for the assumption that logic rested on. The text gives it in two formulations: positively, as the belief that “reefs were resilient if left alone” and that removing local stresses would allow “the ecosystem’s own recuperative powers” to operate; negatively, as the fact that “intervention in the reef’s biology was not debated; it was simply outside the institutional imagination.” Choice E combines both halves: self-recovery upon removal of local stresses. The distractors each belong to the later intervention era or misstate the history. Choice A describes the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program’s portfolio from paragraph 4 — the antithesis of the marine-park assumption. Choice B misattributes to the era a claim about reversing ocean warming that the passage never makes; parks addressed local stresses, and warming is what later broke the paradigm. Choice C invents a causal claim about bleaching and overfishing absent from the text. Choice D is the intervention era’s premise stated as the protection era’s — exactly backwards.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong era. Selective breeding and deployment of lab-reared corals are the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program’s methods from paragraph 4 — the interventionist turn that the marine-park era, by the passage’s account, could not even imagine.`,`Misstates. The passage never claims marine parks were expected to reverse ocean warming; they addressed local stresses like fishing and development. Warming is presented as the force that later overwhelmed the paradigm, not something parks were designed to fix.`,`Unsupported. The passage does not attribute bleaching to overfishing or discuss bleaching’s causes within park boundaries at all in paragraph 1. This choice manufactures a causal claim the text never makes.`,`Opposite era. Public financing of genetic intervention is the 2020-era premise described in paragraph 4. Attributing it to the 1970s marine-park era reverses the passage’s chronology.`,`Correct. It restates paragraph 1’s assumption in both its positive form (self-recovery once local stresses are removed) and its negative form (biological intervention unthinkable), matching the text’s “resilient if left alone” formulation.`],generalLesson:`Era-based detail questions test chronology discipline: each assumption must be matched to the institutional period that held it.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What did the marine-park model assume reefs would do if left alone?`,`Which paragraph describes breeding heat-tolerant corals — and which era is that?`,`“Outside the institutional imagination” refers to what?`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}},{id:`rc-p17-q3`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:3,viewpointComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`The program’s premise would have been unthinkable to the marine-park generation`,`warned that intervention spending could siphon support from emissions reduction and water quality`,`the unglamorous work on which everything else depends`],requiredInference:`Controversy = paradigm break + fear of displacing foundational work.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A unsupported; B invented; C contradicted; D too-strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`The passage implies that the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program was controversial within conservation agencies primarily because`,choices:[{text:`its heat-tolerant corals had already proven invasive and ecologically disruptive in early field trials.`},{text:`it declined to coordinate its research agenda with the United States’ 2000 coral conservation legislation or share its breeding protocols and field-trial data internationally.`},{text:`agency scientists disputed whether the widely reported 2016–2017 bleaching events had actually occurred at all.`},{text:`it proposed eliminating all existing water-quality targets in favor of purely genetic research programs.`},{text:`it broke with the protection paradigm to engineer reef populations, raising fears that intervention spending would displace emissions and water-quality work.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 presents the program’s premise as “unthinkable to the marine-park generation” — a deliberate break with protection — and reports the internal warning that intervention spending could “siphon support” from emissions reduction and water quality. Only choice E combines the paradigm break with the displacement fear.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The question asks for the source of internal controversy, and paragraph 4 supplies two linked reasons. First, the program represented a categorical break: its premise — deliberately engineering reefs for a hotter ocean — “would have been unthinkable to the marine-park generation,” whose institutional identity was protection. Second, the objection from “traditional conservationists within the agencies” was specifically about displacement: intervention spending could “siphon support” from emissions reduction and water quality, described as “the unglamorous work on which everything else depends.” The controversy is thus not about whether the science works but about institutional priorities — a paradigm break plus a budgetary fear. Choice E captures both. Choice A invents an invasiveness finding the passage never reports. Choice B fabricates a U.S.–Australia coordination dispute absent from the text. Choice C contradicts the passage, which treats the 2016–17 bleaching as the accepted catalyst for the funding shift. Choice D overstates the warning: “siphon support from” is a fear of displacement, not a proposal to eliminate targets.`,choiceExplanations:[`Unsupported. The passage reports field trials and risk assessments for outplanting, but never claims any introduced strain proved invasive. This choice converts a governance worry from paragraph 5 into a false empirical finding.`,`Invented. The passage never discusses coordination — or non-coordination — between the Australian program and U.S. legislation. The two countries’ policies are presented as parallel arcs, not as parties to a dispute.`,`Contradicted. The 2016–2017 bleaching events are presented as the accepted, catalyst fact that “convinced policymakers that protection alone could not hold the line.” No one in the passage disputes their occurrence.`,`Too-strong. The internal warning is that intervention spending could “siphon support from” emissions reduction and water quality — a fear of budgetary displacement, not a proposal to eliminate targets. “Eliminating all” converts a caution into a policy the text never attributes to anyone.`,`Correct. It names both halves of paragraph 4’s controversy: the categorical break with the protection paradigm (“unthinkable to the marine-park generation”) and the specific internal fear that intervention spending would displace the foundational emissions and water-quality work.`],generalLesson:`Institutional controversies usually have two layers — the paradigm challenge and the resource threat; inference answers must capture both.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`unsupported`,`too-strong`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 4 say the program’s premise would have been to the marine-park generation?`,`Who objected within the agencies, and what exactly did they fear?`,`“Siphon support from” is weaker than “eliminate” — which choice respects that?`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}},{id:`rc-p17-q4`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-organization`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`For most of the twentieth century`,`The 1998 global mass-bleaching event broke that assumption`,`The institutional expression of that shift`,`also exposed a governance gap`,`The result is a hybrid institutional regime`],requiredInference:`Organization = chronological institutional change, not debate or survey.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A old archetype; B survey archetype; C paradox; D prescriptive.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`Which one of the following best describes the organization of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Two rival scientific theories are presented in turn, the evidence for each is carefully weighed, and the better-supported theory is endorsed.`},{text:`Four competing explanations of a single phenomenon are surveyed in turn and treated as complementary analytical tools.`},{text:`A puzzling paradox is introduced and then resolved by drawing a sharp new conceptual distinction.`},{text:`A specific policy proposal is advanced at length and a series of objections to it are systematically rebutted.`},{text:`An institutional shift is traced chronologically: the protection paradigm, the shocks undermining it, the new programs, and the resulting governance problems.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`The passage moves in chronological order through institutional change: marine-park protection (paragraph 1), the 1998 rupture and 2000 U.S. response (paragraph 2), Australia’s Reef 2050 and 2016–17 bleaching (paragraph 3), the 2020 interventionist program (paragraph 4), the governance gap (paragraph 5), and the hybrid-regime synthesis (paragraph 6). Only choice E describes that organization.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Organization questions reward readers who track what the passage does rather than what it is about, and this passage’s doing is chronological institutional narration. Each paragraph advances the timeline: paragraph 1 establishes the twentieth-century baseline (marine parks, 1975); paragraph 2 introduces the 1998 rupture and the 2000 U.S. legislative response; paragraph 3 follows Australia through Reef 2050 (2015) to the 2016–17 bleaching; paragraph 4 presents the 2020 interventionist turn and its budget; paragraph 5 examines the governance gap the turn exposed; paragraph 6 synthesizes the resulting hybrid regime. Choice E names each phase in order. The distractors describe the passage archetypes this rewrite was designed to escape: choice A is the side-A/side-B debate structure, choice B the survey structure, choice C a paradox-resolution structure, choice D a prescriptive-advocacy structure. None matches a passage that narrates institutional change over time without endorsing a theory or prescribing a policy.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong structure. No rival scientific theories are weighed and none is endorsed; the passage’s actors are agencies and programs, and its movement is chronological. This describes the debate archetype the rewrite deliberately left behind.`,`Wrong structure. Four competing explanations surveyed as complementary tools describes the rc-p11 survey archetype, not this passage. Here there is one institutional trajectory, not a repertoire of explanations.`,`Wrong structure. The passage introduces no paradox and draws no resolving distinction; its engine is a sequence of events — shocks, legislation, programs — not a conceptual puzzle.`,`Wrong structure. The passage advances no policy proposal and rebuts no objections; it narrates how institutions changed and what problems the change created. Advocacy is absent.`,`Correct. It identifies the chronological institutional narration — baseline paradigm, rupturing shocks, new programs, resulting governance problems — matching the passage’s paragraph-by-paragraph movement through time.`],generalLesson:`Organization answers must describe what the passage does (narrate, survey, debate, prescribe), and each paragraph should be placeable in the chosen description.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-organization`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the passage endorse a theory, survey explanations, or narrate change over time?`,`Can you place each paragraph on a timeline?`,`Which choice’s stages match the paragraphs in order?`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}},{id:`rc-p17-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`raised questions no statute had anticipated`,`who bears liability if an introduced strain proves invasive or disease-prone`,`the mismatch between twentieth-century conservation law and twenty-first-century intervention science remains unresolved`],requiredInference:`P5 function = show institutional change outran legal framework.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A too-strong; B opposite; C wrong paragraph; D opposite.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`The discussion of permits, liability, and cross-boundary coordination (paragraph 5) functions primarily to`,choices:[{text:`argue that all active restoration programs should be suspended until new conservation laws are enacted.`},{text:`demonstrate that marine parks had never possessed any clear legal authority over the areas they administered.`},{text:`explain the underlying oceanographic causes of the 1998 mass-bleaching event in detail.`},{text:`praise regulators for having fully and finally resolved the legal challenges of reef intervention.`},{text:`show that active restoration created governance problems the existing legal framework was not designed to handle.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5’s role in the institutional history is to show the transformation outrunning the law: restoration raised questions “no statute had anticipated” — authorization, liability, cross-boundary coordination — and the mismatch “remains unresolved.” Only choice E states that function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`In the passage’s chronological arc, paragraph 5 occupies a specific position: after the interventionist turn (paragraph 4) and before the synthesis (paragraph 6). Its function is to register a consequence of that turn — that the institutions changed faster than the law governing them. The paragraph makes this concrete through three unanticipated questions: who authorizes releasing lab-bred corals, who bears liability if an introduced strain proves harmful, and how jurisdictions coordinate when larvae drift across boundaries. It then generalizes: regulators “improvised,” and “the mismatch between twentieth-century conservation law and twenty-first-century intervention science remains unresolved.” Choice E captures exactly this consequence-registering function. Choice A converts the paragraph’s diagnosis into a prescription — suspension — that the passage never makes. Choice B is the opposite of the text: marine parks “had clear legal authority” over their boundaries; the gap concerns the new restoration activities. Choice C belongs to paragraph 2’s bleaching discussion, not paragraph 5. Choice D contradicts the paragraph’s explicit “remains unresolved.”`,choiceExplanations:[`Too-strong. The paragraph diagnoses a governance gap and notes improvisation, but it never prescribes suspending restoration programs. Diagnosis is not prescription; this choice converts one into the other.`,`Opposite. The paragraph explicitly contrasts the new gap with the old clarity: marine parks “had clear legal authority” regulating activity inside boundaries. The unanticipated questions concern restoration, not parks.`,`Wrong paragraph. The oceanographic causes of bleaching belong to paragraph 2’s discussion of the 1998 event. Paragraph 5 is about permits, liability, and jurisdiction — legal, not oceanographic, questions.`,`Opposite. The paragraph ends by stating the mismatch “remains unresolved” and describes regulators as merely improvising with research permits and case-by-case approvals. “Fully resolved” directly contradicts the text’s closing assessment of the governance gap.`,`Correct. It names the paragraph’s precise function in the arc: showing that the institutional shift to active restoration generated governance problems — authorization, liability, coordination — that the existing twentieth-century legal framework had not anticipated.`],generalLesson:`Consequence paragraphs in institutional histories show the change outrunning its framework; function answers should name the consequence, not convert it into advocacy.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What three questions does paragraph 5 say no statute anticipated?`,`Does the paragraph prescribe halting restoration or diagnose a gap?`,`“Remains unresolved” points toward which choice?`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}},{id:`rc-p17-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[5],supportingSentences:[`who authorizes the release of lab-bred corals into the wild`,`who bears liability if an introduced strain proves invasive or disease-prone`,`how neighboring jurisdictions coordinate when larvae drift across boundaries`],requiredInference:`Analogous question = authority/liability for releasing bred organisms.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A technical; B protection-paradigm; C funding popularity; D irrelevant chronology.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-archetype-rewrite-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Full passage+question rewrite 2026-09-11: converted rc-p17 to institutional-history archetype; hostile-reviewed (no second defensible answer); leakage pass applied.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p17`,stem:`A fisheries agency debating whether to release hatchery-bred fish to rebuild a collapsed stock would face a question most analogous to the one raised in paragraph 5 if it asked`,choices:[{text:`whether hatchery-bred fish grow to maturity significantly faster than their wild counterparts in the same waters.`},{text:`whether existing fishing limits inside established marine reserves should be further tightened next season.`},{text:`whether the public supports increased government spending on fisheries management and stock assessments.`},{text:`whether hatchery breeding techniques were first developed before or after the year 2000.`},{text:`who may legally authorize the release and who bears liability if the introduced fish cause unforeseen harm.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 5’s questions are about governance of deliberate biological intervention: who authorizes releasing bred organisms, who bears liability for unforeseen harm, how jurisdictions coordinate. The fisheries analogue must ask the same governance questions about the same kind of act. Only choice E does.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Application questions require mapping the passage’s structure onto a new domain, and paragraph 5’s structure is governance-of-intervention: a deliberate release of bred organisms raises questions of authorization, liability, and coordination that existing law did not anticipate. The fisheries hypothetical must therefore ask about authority and liability for the release — not about the biology of hatchery fish, not about protection-era tools, not about funding popularity. Choice E is the direct analogue: legal authority to approve the release and liability for unforeseen harm mirror the paragraph’s first two questions exactly. Choice A asks a technical-biological question (growth rates) that belongs to the science of intervention, not its governance. Choice B asks a protection-paradigm question — tightening limits inside reserves — which is the old institutional logic paragraph 5 has moved beyond. Choice C asks about public support for spending, a political-popularity question the paragraph never raises. Choice D asks an irrelevant chronological question with no governance content. The item earns d4 because the distractors are all sensible questions an agency might ask, and only one replays paragraph 5’s specific governance logic.`,choiceExplanations:[`Wrong domain. Growth-rate comparisons are technical questions about the intervention’s biology. Paragraph 5’s questions are about governance — authority, liability, coordination — not about how the organisms perform.`,`Wrong paradigm. Tightening fishing limits inside reserves is the protection-era tool from paragraph 1. Paragraph 5’s questions arise precisely because active restoration goes beyond what the protection framework was designed to govern.`,`Wrong question-type. Public support for spending is a political question the paragraph never raises. Its three questions concern legal authority, liability, and inter-jurisdictional coordination — institutional, not popularity, questions.`,`Irrelevant. When hatchery techniques were developed has no bearing on paragraph 5’s logic, which concerns who may authorize a release and who answers for its consequences under law that never anticipated the act.`,`Correct. It replays paragraph 5’s governance questions in the new domain: legal authority to approve the deliberate release of bred organisms and liability for unforeseen harm — the same two questions the paragraph lists first for lab-bred corals.`],generalLesson:`Analogies to governance passages must match the question-type (authority, liability, jurisdiction), not just the topic of intervention.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:115,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are paragraph 5’s three questions about — biology, popularity, or governance?`,`Which choice asks about authority and liability?`,`Tightening reserve limits belongs to which era’s toolkit?`],labels:{topic:`coral conservation policy`}}],p=[{id:`rc-p18-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`did what Manson had not`,`the next generation of wrongful convictions`],requiredInference:`Cover the trigger, the response, and the qualification: the full institutional-history arc.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A proposes abolition the author never seeks; C endorses the discredited confidence factor; D claims solved what paragraph 4 says is not; E absolutizes an estimator variable.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. New eyewitness passage set; main point spans the institutional arc; distractors are abolition, absolution, absolutes.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`Eyewitness testimony is so unreliable that courts should bar it from criminal trials entirely, relying instead only on physical evidence.`},{text:`DNA exonerations and memory research exposed eyewitness fallibility, prompting science-informed reforms led by New Jersey—though the new framework remains incomplete and contested.`},{text:`The Supreme Court was correct in Manson to treat a witness’s confidence as the strongest indicator of an identification’s reliability.`},{text:`Police departments nationwide have now adopted double-blind sequential lineups, and researchers agree these procedures have effectively eliminated mistaken identifications.`},{text:`Psychological research proves that stressful conditions invariably make accurate eyewitness identification impossible in every case.`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The passage traces an institutional history: the old trust in eyewitness confidence under Manson (paragraph 1), the DNA exonerations and memory science that undermined it (paragraph 2), the Henderson reforms built on that science (paragraph 3), and the resistance and limits that keep the reform incomplete (paragraphs 4–5). Choice B captures that whole arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The author’s thesis is historical and qualified, not polemical. Paragraph 1 establishes the old regime: Manson’s totality test and its treatment of witness certainty as evidence of accuracy. Paragraph 2 delivers the twin blows — DNA exonerations in which mistaken identification played a central role, and research showing memory is reconstructive, with estimator variables beyond the system’s control and system variables within it. Paragraph 3 describes the institutional response: Henderson’s science-based hearings and jury instructions, plus double-blind sequential lineups and related reforms. Paragraphs 4 and 5 qualify the story: police resistance, most states still on Manson, estimator variables as a hard ceiling — and an open question about how far the new model will travel. Choice B is the only answer that holds all of this: the causal story (science exposed fallibility), the institutional response (Henderson-led reform), and the qualification (incomplete, contested). Choice A demands abolition, which the author never proposes — the reforms aim to improve identification evidence, not eliminate it. Choice C endorses Manson’s confidence factor, which paragraph 2 explicitly demolishes as measuring the procedure as much as the memory. Choice D claims nationwide adoption and elimination of error, contradicting paragraph 4 (most states still apply Manson) and paragraph 3 (reform reallocates error). Choice E overstates the science into impossibility; the passage says stress is an estimator variable that degrades accuracy, not that it makes accuracy impossible.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the passage is sharply critical of eyewitness error. It fails because the author never proposes barring the evidence — the entire reform project (Henderson hearings, better lineups, jury instructions) presupposes that identification evidence continues to be used, just more carefully.`,`This is correct. It tracks the passage’s institutional-history arc: the trigger (DNA exonerations plus memory science), the response (Henderson and science-informed procedures), and the honest qualification (resistance, limits, most states unchanged). No other choice covers all three movements.`,`This is tempting because Manson is described at length. It fails because paragraph 2 directly undermines the confidence factor: confirming feedback inflates certainty, so certainty measures the procedure as much as the memory. The passage treats Manson as the outdated regime, not the correct one.`,`This is tempting because reforms are described enthusiastically. It fails twice: paragraph 4 says most states still apply Manson’s test unchanged, and paragraph 3 explicitly warns that sequential lineups trade fewer false picks for fewer correct ones — error is reallocated, not eliminated.`,`This is tempting because stress is listed as an estimator variable. It fails on the absolute: the passage says such variables degrade accuracy and set a ceiling on reform, not that they make accuracy impossible. “Invariably” and “in every case” go beyond anything stated.`],generalLesson:`A main-point answer for an institutional-history passage must cover the trigger, the response, and the qualification — not just one movement of the story.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does the author call for abolishing eyewitness testimony, or for reforming how it is handled? Check what the Henderson reforms actually do.`,`The passage ends with an open question about how far reform will travel. Which choice reflects that incompleteness?`,`Eliminate choices that endorse Manson’s confidence factor or claim the problem is already solved.`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}},{id:`rc-p18-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-primary-purpose`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3,4,5],supportingSentences:[`rebuilt the framework around it`],requiredInference:`Identify the author's project as tracing institutional change rather than advocacy or comparison.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A mistakes reform for abolition; B reverses the stance on Manson; D invents foreign courts; E mistakes the trigger for the purpose.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Purpose matches the chronological causal spine.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`,choices:[{text:`argue that all eyewitness identifications should be excluded from criminal trials as a matter of constitutional law`},{text:`defend the Supreme Court’s Manson framework against recent scientific criticism of its reliability factors`},{text:`trace how scientific findings about memory have reshaped the legal treatment of eyewitness identification`},{text:`compare the American approach to eyewitness regulation with the approaches taken by courts in other countries`},{text:`explain the laboratory techniques by which DNA testing exposes wrongful convictions in criminal cases`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The passage is organized as an institutional history: Manson’s trust-based regime, the DNA and memory-science challenge, the Henderson response, and the qualified aftermath. Its purpose is to trace that reshaping, not to advocate exclusion, defend Manson, compare countries, or explain DNA lab work.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Purpose questions ask what the author is doing overall. The passage’s spine is chronological and causal: an old legal framework built on trust in eyewitness confidence (Manson, 1977); a scientific challenge from two directions (DNA exonerations and reconstructive-memory research with its estimator/system variable distinction); an institutional response (Henderson’s hearings, revised jury instructions, double-blind sequential lineups); and a sober assessment of resistance and limits. Choice C names exactly that project. Choice A mistakes the reform project for abolition — the author wants better procedures, not exclusion as constitutional law. Choice B reverses the passage’s stance: Manson is the outdated regime the science undermined, not a framework being defended. Choice D invents a comparative project the passage never undertakes — no foreign courts appear. Choice E mistakes a supporting detail (DNA exonerations as the trigger) for the purpose; the passage never explains lab techniques.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the passage is critical of eyewitness error. It fails because the author’s project is reform through science-informed procedure — hearings, instructions, better lineups — not constitutional exclusion of the evidence category.`,`This is tempting because Manson receives extended treatment. It fails on direction: the passage presents Manson as the regime the science discredited, devoting paragraph 2 to demolishing its confidence factor. Nothing defends it.`,`This is correct. The passage moves from the old legal treatment (Manson’s totality test) through the scientific findings (DNA exonerations, reconstructive memory, estimator vs. system variables) to the reshaped treatment (Henderson, double-blind lineups, new jury instructions) and its limits.`,`This is tempting as a generic “compare approaches” purpose. It fails because the passage never leaves the United States — no foreign court or system is mentioned. A purpose answer cannot introduce content the passage lacks.`,`This is tempting because DNA exonerations open paragraph 2. It fails as scope shift: DNA is the trigger for the story, mentioned in two sentences, while the passage’s subject is the legal treatment of eyewitness evidence. Lab techniques are never explained.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish the passage’s project (tracing institutional change) from its trigger (DNA exonerations) and from stronger claims it never makes (abolition, constitutional argument).`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-primary-purpose`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Is the author telling a story about change over time, or making a legal argument for exclusion? Check the verbs in each paragraph.`,`Which choice accounts for all five paragraphs — Manson, the science, Henderson, resistance, and the open future?`,`Eliminate any purpose that requires content (foreign courts, lab techniques, constitutional claims) the passage never contains.`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}},{id:`rc-p18-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`measuring the procedure as much as the memory`],requiredInference:`Combine "feedback inflates certainty" with "certainty was treated as reliability evidence" to conclude post-feedback confidence overstates evidentiary strength.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B contradicts the paragraph-3 trade-off; C inverts Henderson; D contradicts the hard-ceiling passage; E contradicts "most states still apply Manson unchanged."`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Every distractor is contradicted by a specific sentence: the strongest item in the new set.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the passage?`,choices:[{text:`A witness who reports high confidence only after receiving confirming feedback provides weaker evidence than the reported confidence level suggests.`},{text:`Double-blind sequential lineups have been proven to eliminate mistaken identifications without reducing the number of correct ones.`},{text:`The New Jersey Supreme Court in Henderson abolished the use of eyewitness testimony in New Jersey criminal trials.`},{text:`Estimator variables such as stress and brief exposure can be fully corrected through improved lineup procedures.`},{text:`Most states have now replaced the Manson totality test with Henderson-style pretrial hearings on suggestiveness.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 establishes that confirming feedback (“good, you picked our suspect”) swells witness certainty, so that the Manson certainty factor measures the procedure as much as the memory. It follows that confidence produced by feedback overstates the evidence’s strength — exactly what choice A says.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Inference answers must follow from the text without adding absolutes. Paragraph 2’s feedback finding is explicit: certainty is malleable and inflated by confirming feedback, which is why treating certainty as reliability evidence is flawed. Choice A draws the direct consequence: post-feedback confidence is weaker evidence than its face value. The other choices each contradict the text. Choice B collides with paragraph 3’s explicit trade-off: sequential lineups reduce false picks at the cost of somewhat fewer correct ones — error is reallocated, not eliminated. Choice C inverts Henderson: the decision rebuilt the framework around hearings and jury instructions; it presupposes eyewitness evidence continues to be used. Choice D contradicts paragraph 4’s “hard ceiling”: estimator variables are beyond procedural control and cannot be corrected by better lineups. Choice E contradicts paragraph 4 directly: most states still apply Manson’s totality test unchanged.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It combines two stated premises — feedback inflates certainty, and certainty therefore measures the procedure as much as the memory — to infer that post-feedback confidence overstates evidentiary strength. Nothing is added beyond that consequence.`,`This is tempting because double-blind sequential lineups are praised. It fails on paragraph 3’s explicit caution: they trade fewer false picks for somewhat fewer correct ones. “Eliminate” and “without reducing” both contradict the stated trade-off.`,`This is tempting because Henderson is the passage’s turning point. It fails because Henderson reformed the framework (hearings, instructions) rather than abolishing the evidence — the reforms only make sense if eyewitness testimony continues to be admitted.`,`This is tempting because reforms are the passage’s subject. It fails on the estimator/system distinction itself: estimator variables are defined as beyond the system’s control, and paragraph 4 calls them a hard ceiling no lineup protocol can fix.`,`This is tempting because Henderson “led” and other states followed. It fails on the quantifier: paragraph 4 states plainly that most states still apply Manson unchanged. “Most” reverses the passage’s explicit claim.`],generalLesson:`A valid inference combines stated premises into their direct consequence. Watch for answers that upgrade a trade-off into a cure or a leading example into a majority.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 2 say confirming feedback does to a witness’s certainty? What does that imply about the certainty’s value as evidence?`,`For each wrong choice, find the sentence that contradicts it — especially the trade-off sentence in paragraph 3 and the “most states” sentence in paragraph 4.`,`The credited inference adds nothing beyond the logical consequence of the feedback finding. Prefer the answer that stays closest to the text.`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}},{id:`rc-p18-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-map`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,3],supportingSentences:[`the witness's own certainty`,`treated as evidence of accuracy`],whyDistractorsFail:`A reverses Manson's permissiveness; B and E describe Henderson; C swaps the two courts.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. "Differed in that" item; three distractors accurately describe Henderson, testing framework separation.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`According to the passage, the Manson framework differed from the Henderson framework in that Manson`,choices:[{text:`prohibited the use of any identification procedure that could be described as suggestive`},{text:`required pretrial hearings examining both estimator variables and system variables`},{text:`was established by a state supreme court rather than the United States Supreme Court`},{text:`counted the witness’s own certainty as a factor supporting the identification’s reliability`},{text:`required that all lineups be administered on a double-blind and sequential basis`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 states that under Manson, courts weighed the witness’s own certainty among the circumstances supporting reliability — treating confidence as evidence of accuracy. Paragraph 3 shows Henderson replacing that approach with science-based hearings and jury instructions. Choice D is the stated difference.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Detail questions reward exact retrieval. Paragraph 1’s account of Manson is explicit: suggestive identifications could still be admitted if “reliable” under a totality of the circumstances, and “among the circumstances courts were told to weigh was the witness’s own certainty.” Paragraph 3’s Henderson does the opposite — it treats certainty as suspect (paragraph 2’s feedback finding) and substitutes hearings plus jury instructions. Choice D restates the Manson side of that contrast. Choice A reverses Manson: it permitted suggestive identifications deemed reliable rather than prohibiting them. Choice B describes Henderson’s pretrial hearings, not Manson’s. Choice C reverses the courts: Manson was the United States Supreme Court (1977); Henderson was the New Jersey Supreme Court (2011). Choice E describes Henderson-era reforms (double-blind, sequential), not Manson’s 1977 framework.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because suggestiveness is central to both frameworks. It fails by reversal: Manson allowed suggestive identifications that seemed reliable overall — the passage never says it prohibited them.`,`This is tempting because estimator and system variables are prominent. It fails by misattribution: those hearings are Henderson’s innovation in paragraph 3, not Manson’s 1977 approach.`,`This is tempting because two courts are named with dates. It fails by reversal: Manson was decided by the United States Supreme Court; Henderson by New Jersey’s. The choice swaps them.`,`This is correct. Paragraph 1 explicitly lists the witness’s certainty among Manson’s reliability circumstances, glossed as treating “confidence as evidence of accuracy” — the very factor Henderson’s science-based framework demotes.`,`This is tempting because these procedures dominate paragraph 3. It fails by misattribution: double-blind sequential administration is the Henderson-era reform package, three decades after Manson.`],generalLesson:`For “differed in that” questions, verify each choice against BOTH sides of the contrast — many distractors describe the other framework accurately.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What five circumstances did Manson tell courts to weigh? Which one does the passage single out?`,`Three distractors describe Henderson accurately — but the question asks about Manson. Keep the frameworks separate.`,`Check the court names against the dates: which decision belongs to which court?`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}},{id:`rc-p18-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`,`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,4],supportingSentences:[`beyond the justice system's control`,`can only police the system's own contributions to error`],requiredInference:`Track the estimator/system distinction to its payoff in paragraph 4's hard ceiling.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A invents an exclusion rule; C reverses the variable definitions; D reverses the passage's stance on science; E absolutizes one example.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Function is architectural: the distinction returns as the hard ceiling.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`The author discusses estimator variables (paragraph 2) primarily in order to`,choices:[{text:`argue that identifications affected by stress or brief exposure should be automatically excluded from trial as unreliable`},{text:`distinguish sources of eyewitness error the legal system can control from those it cannot, thereby defining the limits of procedural reform`},{text:`demonstrate that system variables such as lineup construction have no measurable effect on identification accuracy`},{text:`suggest that psychological research on memory has little relevance to how courts should handle eyewitness evidence`},{text:`prove that cross-racial identifications are always mistaken and therefore should be disregarded by juries`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 2 introduces estimator variables (stress, exposure, weapon focus, cross-race difficulty) precisely to contrast them with system variables — the lineup procedures the justice system controls. That distinction does argumentative work later: paragraph 4 invokes estimator variables as the “hard ceiling” on reform. Choice B captures that structural role.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Function questions ask what a detail does for the argument, not what it says. Paragraph 2’s estimator/system distinction is load-bearing: it sorts error into what procedure can fix (system variables — lineup construction, instructions, feedback) and what it cannot (estimator variables — stress, exposure, cross-race effects). The payoff arrives in paragraph 4, where estimator variables return as the “hard ceiling” limiting Henderson-style reform, and in paragraph 3’s honest caveat that reform reallocates error. Choice B names that function. Choice A upgrades a diagnostic distinction into an exclusionary rule the author never proposes — the passage’s project is better procedure, not automatic exclusion. Choice C reverses the distinction: system variables are precisely the ones the system’s choices affect. Choice D reverses the paragraph’s stance: the research is the engine of the entire reform story. Choice E absolutizes one example (cross-race difficulty) into “always mistaken,” which the passage never claims — it is listed as a risk factor, not a universal.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because estimator variables sound damning. It fails because the author never proposes exclusion rules — the passage’s answer to error is improved procedure (Henderson hearings, better lineups), and estimator variables return in paragraph 4 as a limit on reform, not a trigger for exclusion.`,`This is correct. The estimator/system split sorts error by controllability, which is exactly what lets paragraph 4 argue that reform “can only police the system’s own contributions to error.’ The detail’s function is architectural, not decorative.`,`This is tempting if the two variable types blur together. It fails by reversal: system variables are defined as the system’s own doing — lineup construction, administrator influence, feedback — the very things procedure can change.`,`This is tempting as a skeptical reading. It fails because the passage’s entire narrative — from DNA exonerations through Henderson — treats memory science as the authority that discredited Manson and rebuilt the framework.`,`This is tempting because the cross-race effect is vivid. It fails on the absolute: the passage lists it among variables that degrade accuracy, never claiming such identifications are always wrong. “Always” and “prove” exceed the text.`],generalLesson:`Ask what work a distinction does later in the passage. If a concept returns in a later paragraph (here, the “hard ceiling”), its function is to set up that return.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is the paragraph contrasting estimator variables WITH? What does that contrast let the author say later?`,`Where does the concept of estimator variables reappear? Its function is tied to that reappearance.`,`Eliminate any choice containing absolutes (“always,” “prove,” “no effect”) the passage never states.`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}},{id:`rc-p18-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-attitude`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3,4],supportingSentences:[`did what Manson had not`],viewpointOwner:`the author`,whyDistractorsFail:`A ignores the stated limits; B denies the scientific basis; D ignores evaluative language; E mistakes reported resistance for endorsed resistance.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Both approval and qualification are textually grounded.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p18`,stem:`The author’s attitude toward the Henderson reforms is best described as`,choices:[{text:`uncritical enthusiasm, treating the reforms as a complete and final solution to the problem of mistaken identification`},{text:`skeptical dismissal of the reforms as expensive symbolism lacking any real scientific basis or practical value`},{text:`cautious support, welcoming their scientific grounding while acknowledging their limits and the resistance they face`},{text:`detached neutrality, presenting the reforms without indicating any evaluation of them`},{text:`hostility toward judicial interference with established police practices`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The author presents Henderson as a genuine advance — extensive scientific hearings, a rebuilt framework — but devotes paragraph 4 to resistance, costs, most states’ non-adoption, and estimator variables as a hard ceiling, and paragraph 3 notes reform reallocates rather than abolishes error. That is support with eyes open: choice C.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Attitude must be read from evaluative language and structural choices. The author’s approval is real: Henderson “did what Manson had not,” holding “extensive hearings on the science” and rebuilding the framework “around it” — language of respect. But enthusiasm is checked at every turn: paragraph 3 itself cautions that sequential lineups trade fewer false picks for fewer correct ones; paragraph 4 catalogs police resistance, training costs, most states’ continued use of Manson, divided courts on expert testimony, and the hard ceiling of estimator variables; paragraph 5 leaves the future an open question. Choice C holds both halves. Choice A fails on “uncritical” and “complete solution” — the passage stresses incompleteness throughout. Choice B fails on “no scientific basis” — the science is the reforms’ foundation and the author treats it as authoritative. Choice D fails because the evaluative language (“did what Manson had not,” “cruelest finding”) is anything but neutral. Choice E fails because the author sides with the reforming court against police resistance, not the reverse.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the Henderson section is admiring. It fails on “uncritical” and “complete solution”: paragraph 3 admits the false-pick/correct-pick trade-off, paragraph 4 lists resistance and limits, and paragraph 5 ends on an open question. Nothing here is uncritical.`,`This is tempting because costs and resistance get a full paragraph. It fails because the resistance is reported, not endorsed — and “no scientific basis” directly contradicts paragraph 3’s account of extensive scientific hearings as the reforms’ foundation.`,`This is correct. “Did what Manson had not” and the detailed, respectful account of the science show support; the trade-off caveat, the resistance paragraph, and the hard ceiling on estimator variables show the caution. Both halves are textually grounded.`,`This is tempting because the author reports opposing views fairly. It fails because evaluative language — “the cruelest finding,” “did what Manson had not” — reveals a clear stance favoring the science-based approach. Fairness to both sides is not neutrality.`,`This is tempting if one reads the resistance paragraph as the author’s view. It fails because the author presents police objections as obstacles to a welcome reform, siding with the Henderson court’s intervention rather than against it.`],generalLesson:`Attitude answers must survive both the praise and the criticism in the passage. Check the author’s own evaluative adjectives — they reveal stance even in balanced presentations.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-attitude`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What evaluative phrases does the author use about Henderson itself (“did what Manson had not”)? What does that rule out?`,`Does paragraph 4’s discussion of resistance read as endorsement of the resistance, or as an honest accounting of obstacles?`,`The correct choice must contain both the approval and the qualification — reject any choice with only one half.`],labels:{topic:`eyewitness identification`}}],m=[{id:`rc-p19-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,4,5,6],supportingSentences:[`at bottom it is a dispute about identity`,`the debate continues`],requiredInference:`Combine the paragraph-1 reframing with the paragraph-6 open ending.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A/B take opposite extremes; D contradicts the paragraph-1 demotion of law; E overstates the digital-archive compromise.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Reframing-plus-open-ending main point.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`,choices:[{text:`All cultural artifacts held in Western museums should be immediately returned to their countries of origin without exception.`},{text:`Universal museums have decisively refuted the case for repatriation by demonstrating their superior conservation resources and expertise.`},{text:`The repatriation debate is about cultural identity and moral obligation; while practice has found compromises, the criterion for return remains unresolved.`},{text:`The repatriation debate is primarily a legal dispute that courts are well equipped to resolve through established property law.`},{text:`Digital archives have made the physical location of cultural artifacts irrelevant to the repatriation debate, which can now proceed entirely online.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 reframes the debate as about identity rather than law; paragraphs 2–3 stage the two cases; paragraph 4 complicates both; paragraph 5 notes emerging compromises; paragraph 6 ends on the unresolved criterion. Choice C spans that arc.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The author's thesis has three components: the debate's true subject (identity and moral obligation, not just law — paragraph 1), the state of play (both positions complicated by experience, compromises emerging — paragraphs 4–5), and the open ending (no agreed criterion — paragraph 6). Choice C contains all three. Choice A takes the advocates' extreme as the author's view, ignoring the universalist case the author treats seriously. Choice B takes the universalists' extreme and is contradicted by paragraph 4, where returns are "celebrated" and receiving institutions undercut the preservation claim. Choice D contradicts paragraph 1 explicitly: the debate "is often framed as a legal dispute" but "at bottom" is about identity. Choice E overstates paragraph 5: digital archives are one element of compromise, not a resolution making location irrelevant.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the case for return is passionately argued. It fails because the author gives the universal museums' position serious weight and ends on an unresolved criterion — immediate total return is the advocates' extreme, not the author's thesis.`,`This is tempting because conservation resources are a real universalist argument. It fails because paragraph 4 directly undercuts it: returns have been celebrated and receiving institutions have built impressive museums. "Decisively refuted" contradicts the passage.`,`This is correct. It captures the reframing (identity, not just law), the qualified state of the debate (compromises in practice), and the open ending (no agreed criterion) — the passage's full movement.`,`This is tempting because ownership sounds legal. It fails because paragraph 1 explicitly demotes the legal framing: the dispute is "at bottom" about identity and "less a legal question than a moral one" (paragraph 6).`,`This is tempting because digital archives are mentioned as part of compromise. It fails as too strong: they "make collections visible everywhere," but the passage never claims location has become irrelevant — the physical returns themselves are celebrated.`],generalLesson:`When a passage reframes a debate in paragraph 1 ("not just X, but Y") and ends on an unresolved question, the main point usually includes both the reframing and the open ending.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does paragraph 1 say the debate is "at bottom" about? And what does the final paragraph say remains unresolved?`,`Does the author declare either side the winner? What would that imply for choices A and B?`,`The correct choice should include the reframing, the emerging compromises, and the unresolved criterion.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}},{id:`rc-p19-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-other-viewpoints`,secondarySkills:[`rc-detail`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`custodians of a shared human heritage`,`encounter the art of every civilization under one roof`],viewpointOwner:`universal museum advocates`,whyDistractorsFail:`B is the other side's view; C contradicts paragraph 3 (though paragraph 4 complicates it); D is the author's framing; E is unsupported.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Tests attribution, not truth: D is the classic true-but-misattributed trap.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`Advocates of the "universal museum" position, as described in the passage, would most likely agree with which one of the following?`,choices:[{text:`Museums holding contested artifacts serve humanity best by keeping collections together, preserving them, and enabling cross-cultural encounters.`},{text:`Any artifact acquired under colonial rule should be returned immediately regardless of its condition or available conservation`},{text:`Source nations are generally better equipped than Western museums to conserve fragile artifacts.`},{text:`The repatriation debate is fundamentally a moral question rather than a legal one.`},{text:`Long-term loans are an unacceptable compromise because they leave ownership unresolved.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 3 gives the universalist case: shared human heritage under one roof, superior preservation resources, cross-cultural encounters, and the slippery-slope warning. Choice A combines the positive elements of that case.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The universal museum position is developed in paragraph 3 with three pillars: the encyclopedic mission (encountering every civilization under one roof), the preservation advantage (resources source institutions may lack), and the slippery-slope warning. Choice A restates the first two pillars positively. Choice B states the advocates-of-return position — a viewpoint reversal. Choice C contradicts paragraph 3, which claims the preservation advantage runs the other way (though paragraph 4 complicates it). Choice D is the author's framing (paragraphs 1 and 6), not the universalists' — they frame their case in terms of stewardship and access. Choice E is unsupported: the passage never says universalists reject loans; paragraph 5 presents loans as an emerging middle path without attributing opposition. The recurring trap in viewpoint questions is the true-but-misattributed choice: D states the author's framing and C states a complication of the universalist claim, while only A reports the universalists' own position.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It restates paragraph 3's universalist case: custodianship of shared heritage, superior preservation resources, and the cross-cultural encounters that intact collections make possible.`,`This is tempting because colonial-era acquisitions are central to the debate. It fails as a viewpoint reversal: immediate return regardless of condition is the repatriation advocates' position, the opposite of the universal museum stance.`,`This is tempting because paragraph 4 notes receiving institutions building good museums. It fails because that observation complicates the universalist claim rather than expressing it — paragraph 3 asserts the preservation advantage lies with the universal museums.`,`This is tempting because it is true in the passage. It fails on attribution: the moral-not-legal framing is the author's (paragraphs 1, 6), not something the universalists are shown asserting.`,`This is tempting because ownership is contested. It fails because the passage never attributes this rejection to universalists; loans are presented as an emerging compromise without specifying who opposes them.`],generalLesson:`Viewpoint questions often include choices that are true in the passage but belong to the wrong speaker. Always verify attribution, not just truth.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The universal museum case is made in the third paragraph. What are its main pillars?`,`Two of the choices state things that are true in the passage but said by someone else. Who says them?`,`The correct choice combines the "shared heritage" mission with the preservation and access arguments.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}},{id:`rc-p19-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`generally been celebrated rather than mourned`,`built impressive new museums to house returned works`],whyDistractorsFail:`A states the opposite of the paragraph's finding; B contradicts the premise that returns occurred; C/D are unsupported inventions.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Straight paragraph-4 retrieval; the undercutting inference is the author's own.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`According to the passage, the actual returns of contested artifacts that have occurred show that`,choices:[{text:`receiving institutions have generally been unable to care for returned works`},{text:`Western museums have refused to participate in any repatriation efforts`},{text:`returns have been limited to objects looted within living memory`},{text:`legal disputes over ownership have increased as a result of the returns`},{text:`returns were generally celebrated, and some receiving institutions built impressive new museums`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 4 states this directly: returns "have generally been celebrated rather than mourned, and several receiving institutions have built impressive new museums to house returned works, undercutting the claim that they cannot care for them." Choice E restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Paragraph 4 is the "experience has complicated both positions" paragraph, and its first complication favors the repatriation side: actual returns went well. The passage gives two facts — celebration rather than mourning, and impressive new museums built by recipients — and one inference the author draws: this "undercuts" the universalist preservation claim. Choice E reports both facts. Choice A states the opposite of the paragraph's point. Choice B contradicts the premise: returns occurred, so museums participated. Choice C is unsupported; the passage gives examples (Berlin bronzes, ancestral remains) without limiting returns to recent lootings. Choice D is invented; legal disputes increasing is never mentioned. The inference is deliberately modest: it asks only what the reported outcomes imply about the preservation objection, not what they prove about every return. Choices that broaden the claim to all lootings, or invent legal trends, exceed the paragraph's stated evidence.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because preservation capacity is debated. It fails because it states the opposite of the paragraph's finding: recipients built "impressive new museums," which is presented as evidence they can care for the works.`,`This is tempting as a cynical assumption. It fails because the paragraph's premise is that returns have occurred — a Berlin museum's restitution is named — so refusal is contradicted.`,`This is tempting because the final paragraph mentions living-memory lootings as the easy case. It fails because the passage never limits actual returns to that category; the examples given are not characterized that way.`,`This is tempting because ownership disputes sound litigious. It fails because the passage never mentions increased legal disputes; the reported outcome of returns is celebration, not litigation. Paragraph 4's structure is "experience complicated both positions," and this question tests the first complication: the facts about returns favoring the repatriation side must be read as reported, not as either side wishes they were.`,`This is correct. It reports paragraph 4's two findings — returns "generally celebrated rather than mourned" and recipients building impressive new museums — which the author says undercut the claim that source institutions cannot care for returned works.`],generalLesson:`"Experience has complicated both positions" paragraphs deliver empirical verdicts. Read them for what actually happened, and resist choices stating the opposite of the reported outcome.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The fourth paragraph begins "Experience has complicated both positions." What does experience show about the returns that happened?`,`What two facts does the author cite, and what do they "undercut"?`,`Eliminate any choice contradicting the reported celebration and the new museums.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}},{id:`rc-p19-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[6],supportingSentences:[`between those poles lies the vast majority of contested holdings`],requiredInference:`Restate the poles-and-middle structure without adding predictions.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A proposes a legal solution the paragraph rejects; C predicts a consensus never forecast; D contradicts the poles' different treatment; E contradicts paragraph 5's emerging compromises.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Inference stays strictly inside the paragraph's logic.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`Based on the final paragraph, it can most reasonably be inferred that the author believes`,choices:[{text:`a clear legal rule could resolve most repatriation disputes if courts applied it consistently`},{text:`agreement exists only at the extremes, while most contested holdings fall in a disputed middle ground`},{text:`most contested artifacts will eventually be returned as moral consensus emerges`},{text:`the distinction between recent lootings and ancient transfers is morally irrelevant`},{text:`museums and source nations will never reach any compromise on contested objects`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph says everyone agrees on the extremes (recent thefts return; ancient transfers stay) but "between those poles lies the vast majority of contested holdings, and there the debate continues." Choice B restates this structure.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The final paragraph has a precise logical structure: agreement at both poles, disagreement in the middle, with the middle containing "the vast majority" of cases. Choice B maps this exactly. Choice A contradicts the paragraph's framing — the question is "less a legal question than a moral one," so a legal rule is not the expected resolution. Choice C predicts an eventual consensus the author never forecasts; the debate "continues" is the closing note. Choice D contradicts the paragraph, which treats the poles differently (recent thefts clearly return; ancient transfers clearly stay), implying the distinction matters. Choice E contradicts paragraph 5, where compromises are already emerging in practice. Application questions test whether the rule or distinction can be carried to new cases. The consensus pole's criteria are stated as observable facts — timing, identifiable agents — so the correct application matches those facts rather than importing sympathies about older or morally weightier claims.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because ownership sounds like law. It fails because the paragraph explicitly says the issue is "less a legal question than a moral one" — a legal rule is presented as the wrong kind of solution.`,`This is correct. The paragraph's structure is poles-versus-middle: consensus that recent thefts return and ancient transfers stay, with "the vast majority of contested holdings" lying between, where "the debate continues." The inference stays strictly inside the paragraph's logic: poles of consensus plus a large contested middle, with no forecast about which way the middle will resolve and no promotion of legal over moral reasoning.`,`This is tempting as an optimistic trajectory. It fails because the author forecasts nothing about eventual returns; the final word is that "the debate continues." Prediction beyond the text is unsupported.`,`This is tempting because both poles are mentioned together. It fails because the paragraph treats the poles as opposite consensus outcomes — recent thefts go back, ancient transfers do not — which only makes sense if the distinction matters morally.`,`This is tempting as a pessimistic reading of "the debate continues." It fails because paragraph 5 documents compromises already emerging (loans, shared stewardship, joint exhibitions); "never" contradicts that progress.`],generalLesson:`Closing paragraphs with a "poles and middle" structure are making a precise logical point: consensus at extremes plus a large contested middle. The inference answer restates that structure without adding predictions.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-passage-map`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The final paragraph describes two poles of agreement. What are they?`,`Where does "the vast majority of contested holdings" lie, according to the paragraph?`,`The correct choice restates the poles-and-middle structure without predicting the future.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}},{id:`rc-p19-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-function`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[4],supportingSentences:[`undercutting the claim that they cannot care for them`],requiredInference:`Connect the Berlin example to paragraph 4's thesis about experience complicating both positions.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A overgeneralizes from one example; B contradicts "celebrated"; C confuses paragraphs 4 and 5; E invents motives.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Example serves the paragraph's thesis.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`The author mentions the Berlin museum's restitution of bronze plaques to Nigeria primarily in order to`,choices:[{text:`prove that all colonial-era artifacts will soon be returned to their countries of origin`},{text:`provide an example of a return that was widely condemned by art historians`},{text:`illustrate how digital archives can substitute for physical repatriation`},{text:`support the claim that actual returns have gone well and undercut the universalists' preservation argument`},{text:`show that European museums initiated the repatriation movement without external pressure`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`The example appears in paragraph 4, whose thesis is that experience "complicated both positions": returns were "celebrated rather than mourned" and recipients built good museums, "undercutting the claim that they cannot care for them." The Berlin case exemplifies this. Choice D states the function.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The example's function is determined by its paragraph's job. Paragraph 4 argues that real-world returns have complicated both sides' positions, and the Berlin restitution is offered as evidence for the first complication: returns went well. It specifically undercuts the universalist claim (paragraph 3) that source institutions lack preservation resources. Choice D captures this evidentiary role. Choice A overstates: one example cannot prove "all... will soon be returned." Choice B contradicts the paragraph: returns were "celebrated rather than mourned." Choice C confuses paragraphs: digital archives belong to paragraph 5's compromise discussion. Choice E is unsupported: the passage says nothing about what pressure prompted the restitution. Function answers must name the paragraph's argumentative job, not just its topic. Paragraph 4 exists to show lived experience complicating both camps' positions; the Berlin case is the half of that complication that favors returns, offered as reported outcomes rather than as proof of either side's theory.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the example is a real return. It fails on "all" and "will soon": a single example cannot support a universal prediction, and the passage ends with the criterion unresolved, not with total return imminent.`,`This is tempting because controversies surround restitutions. It fails because it directly contradicts the paragraph: returns "have generally been celebrated rather than mourned." Condemnation is the opposite of what is reported.`,`This is tempting because both topics appear in the passage. It fails because digital archives are discussed in paragraph 5 as a separate compromise element; the Berlin example illustrates successful physical return, not digital substitution.`,`This is correct. The Berlin restitution exemplifies paragraph 4's thesis: actual returns have gone well ("celebrated rather than mourned"), and capable receiving institutions undercut the universalists' preservation-resources objection.`,`This is tempting as backstory. It fails because the passage says nothing about the motives or pressures behind the Berlin decision; the example is used as evidence of outcomes, not of institutional initiative. Function questions are answered by the paragraph's thesis: paragraph 4 argues experience complicated both positions, and the Berlin case is the evidence for the complication favoring returns — successful restitutions undercutting the preservation objection.`],generalLesson:`Examples serve their paragraph's thesis. Before choosing a function answer, state the paragraph's main claim in your own words, then pick the choice that connects the example to that claim.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-function`,`rc-paragraph-roles`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which paragraph contains the Berlin example, and what is that paragraph's opening thesis?`,`The paragraph says returns "have generally been celebrated" and that this undercuts a specific claim. Which claim?`,`The correct answer links the example to the paragraph's thesis about how actual returns went.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}},{id:`rc-p19-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-application`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4,abstraction:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[6],supportingSentences:[`objects looted in living memory by identifiable thieves should go back`],requiredInference:`Match each new case against the consensus pole's exact features: recent, looted, identifiable agent.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A nears the opposite pole; C, D, E fall in the disputed middle with no stated consensus.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Mechanical pole-matching; only B instantiates the stated consensus. Difficulty 4 earned by the five-way case sorting.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-p19`,stem:`Based on the passage, advocates of return and defenders of universal museums would most likely agree that which one of the following objects should be returned?`,choices:[{text:`A sculpture purchased legally by a museum from a willing seller two centuries ago`},{text:`A ceremonial mask stolen by an identifiable thief twenty years ago and later sold to a museum`},{text:`A manuscript whose country of origin cannot be determined from any available records`},{text:`A set of bronze vessels excavated jointly by archaeologists from the museum's and the source nation's universities`},{text:`A painting that has been on continuous public display in the museum for over a hundred years`}],correctIndex:1,explanationQuick:`The final paragraph establishes consensus at one pole: "objects looted in living memory by identifiable thieves should go back." A mask stolen by an identifiable thief twenty years ago fits that pole exactly, so both sides would agree. The other options fall in the disputed middle or the opposite pole.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This applies the final paragraph's poles-and-middle framework. The author states explicit consensus: "Everyone agrees that objects looted in living memory by identifiable thieves should go back." Choice B instantiates that pole precisely — recent theft, identifiable thief. The distractors are engineered to fall elsewhere: A (legal purchase centuries ago) approaches the opposite pole ("every antiquity that crossed a border centuries ago" need not return); C (unknown origin) and D (joint excavation) and E (century of display) all sit in the disputed middle where, per the passage, "the debate continues." Only B triggers stated consensus. The application strategy is mechanical: extract the consensus pole's exact features from the final paragraph — looted in living memory by identifiable thieves — then test each object against those features rather than against general sympathies about return.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because colonial-era acquisitions are contested. It fails because a legal purchase two centuries ago falls near the opposite pole — the passage says "almost no one thinks every antiquity that crossed a border centuries ago must" return. No consensus for return.`,`This is correct. It matches the consensus pole exactly: "objects looted in living memory by identifiable thieves should go back" — a twenty-year-old theft by an identifiable thief is precisely that case, commanding agreement from both sides.`,`This is tempting because unknown provenance seems to favor return. It fails because the passage establishes no consensus for this category; with origin undeterminable, it falls in the disputed middle where "the debate continues."`,`This is tempting because the source nation was involved. It fails because joint excavation implies lawful, cooperative acquisition — nothing like the looting pole — placing it in the contested middle, not under the consensus.`,`This is tempting because long display might suggest entitlement either way. It fails because the passage gives no rule tying display duration to return; a century of display resembles the "centuries ago" pole more than the looting pole.`],generalLesson:`To apply a "consensus at the poles" framework, match each new case to a pole before judging it. Only cases instantiating a stated consensus pole command agreement; everything else is contested.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`half-right`,`unsupported`,`wrong-viewpoint`],prerequisites:[`rc-application`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`The final paragraph states one case on which "everyone agrees." What are its exact features?`,`Now test each object against those features: recent looting by an identifiable thief. Which one matches?`,`The other objects fall in the disputed middle or near the opposite pole — only one instantiates the consensus.`],labels:{topic:`cultural repatriation`}}],h=[{id:`rc-c01-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3],supportingSentences:[`(Passage A) demonstrably discriminatory`,`(Passage B) efficient laundries for old biases`],requiredInference:`Test each choice for a yes/no split: only the bias-reduction question divides them.`,viewpointOwner:`both authors`,whyDistractorsFail:`A is too abstract (B's objection is practical); B is common ground; C is never discussed; D is agreed by both.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Central-disagreement item; agreement points are the distractors.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`The two passages disagree most centrally over whether`,choices:[{text:`auditing a hiring process is theoretically possible under any circumstances`},{text:`automated hiring systems are currently widely used by large employers`},{text:`employers should be permitted to consider educational background when hiring`},{text:`human hiring managers are ever influenced by unconscious bias`},{text:`algorithmic screening reduces hiring bias or merely reproduces it in harder-to-challenge form`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`Passage A claims algorithms can be audited and corrected, making hiring fairer than the "demonstrably discriminatory" human system; Passage B claims algorithms launder old biases into mathematics that is harder to recognize and challenge. Choice E states that disagreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The central disagreement must be the point on which the passages take directly opposing positions. Passage A's thesis: automation replaces subjective bias with improvable, auditable fairness. Passage B's thesis: automation encodes historical bias as mathematics, making discrimination harder to detect and contest. These are direct contraries on the same question — does algorithmic hiring reduce bias or entrench it? Choice E states exactly that. Choice D fails because both passages accept human bias (A calls human hiring "demonstrably discriminatory"; B discusses managers who discriminate). Choice B is a background fact neither disputes. Choice A is too abstract: B disputes that audits happen in practice (trade secrecy), not that auditing is theoretically possible. Choice C is never discussed by either passage.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because auditability is debated. It fails because B's objection is practical (proprietary black boxes, trade secrecy), not theoretical — B never claims auditing is impossible in principle, only that it is "largely theoretical" in current practice.`,`This is tempting as background. It fails because neither passage disputes that these systems are being deployed — A cites "several large employers" and B discusses "the systems being deployed today." Usage is common ground, not contention.`,`This is tempting because universities appear as an example of biased criteria. It fails because neither passage takes a position on whether considering education is permissible; the dispute is about how criteria operate, not this specific one.`,`This is tempting because bias is the topic. It fails because there is no disagreement here: Passage A calls the human system "demonstrably discriminatory" and Passage B describes managers who discriminate. Both sides accept human bias exists.`,`This is correct. It is the precise point of opposition: A says algorithms reduce bias and are correctable; B says they reproduce bias as mathematics that is harder to recognize and defend against. Every paragraph of each passage serves this dispute.`],generalLesson:`The central disagreement is the question both passages answer in opposite ways. Test each choice: does Passage A say yes while Passage B says no (or vice versa)? Points of agreement are distractors.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`State each passage's thesis in one sentence. What single question do those theses answer oppositely?`,`Check each choice: do the passages actually take opposite sides on it, or do they agree?`,`The disagreement is about what algorithms DO to bias — reduce it or reproduce it.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}},{id:`rc-c01-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3],supportingSentences:[`(Passage B) efficient laundries for old biases`,`(Passage B) largely theoretical`],requiredInference:`Map each of B's paragraphs onto A's claims: point-by-point rebuttal, not concession.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B concedes nothing (rejects concession); C claims factual agreement that does not exist; D invents test results; E ignores the shared topic.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Relationship is systematic challenge; mapping B-to-A paragraphs confirms.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`Which one of the following best describes the relationship between the two passages?`,choices:[{text:`Passage B challenges Passage A's claims about auditability and bias reduction as illusory in practice.`},{text:`Passage B concedes Passage A's main argument and merely adds a minor qualification about implementation.`},{text:`Passage A and Passage B agree on the facts but draw different policy recommendations from them.`},{text:`Passage A proposes a new hiring technology, and Passage B reports the results of testing that technology.`},{text:`The two passages address unrelated topics that share only a superficial vocabulary.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A's pillars are auditability ("every decision is logged... can be detected and corrected") and bias reduction; B attacks both — audits are "largely theoretical" behind trade secrecy, and the systems are "efficient laundries for old biases." Choice A describes this direct challenge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Relationship questions ask how the passages engage each other. Passage B is structured as a rebuttal: its first paragraph answers A's bias-reduction claim (bias is laundered, not removed), its second answers A's auditability claim (black boxes, trade secrecy, narrow audits), and its third answers A's "better than humans" comparison (the accountability gap). This is point-by-point challenge, not concession or topic-shift. Choice A captures it. Choice D fails because B reports no test results. Choice E fails because both address algorithmic hiring directly. Choice C fails because they dispute the facts (whether audits occur, whether bias is reduced), not just the policy. Choice B fails because B's challenge is fundamental, not a minor qualification. Relationship questions in comparative sets are answered by mapping each passage's thesis onto the other's claims point by point. Here every element of A's defense — bias reduction, auditability, the human comparison — meets a direct rebuttal in B, which is the signature of systematic challenge rather than supplementation.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. B's three paragraphs map onto A's three claims: bias reduction becomes bias laundering, auditability becomes theoretical behind trade secrecy, and "better than humans" becomes an accountability gap. It is a direct, systematic challenge. Mapping B's paragraphs onto A's claims is the reliable method here: bias reduction becomes bias laundering, auditability becomes theoretical opacity, and the human comparison becomes an accountability gap — rebuttal at every point.`,`This is tempting because B's tone is measured. It fails because B concedes nothing of A's main argument — it calls the promised objectivity a description of "a future" that current systems do not inhabit, which is rejection, not qualification.`,`This is tempting because both discuss the same systems. It fails because they dispute the facts themselves: A says systems are auditable and reduce bias; B says audits are blocked and bias is entrenched. The disagreement is factual, not merely prescriptive.`,`This is tempting because A is optimistic and B is critical. It fails because Passage B reports no test results — its argument is analytical (about how training data and opacity work), not empirical reporting on A's proposal.`,`This is tempting only if read carelessly. It fails because both passages are plainly about the same subject — automated hiring systems — engaging the same claims from opposite sides.`],generalLesson:`To characterize a comparative relationship, map the second passage's paragraphs onto the first's claims. Point-by-point rebuttal looks different from concession-plus-qualification.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What are Passage A's main claims? Now check: does Passage B accept any of them, or attack each one?`,`B calls the systems "efficient laundries for old biases" and auditability "largely theoretical." Is that concession or challenge?`,`The correct choice will describe a direct challenge to A's central claims, not agreement or a side topic.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}},{id:`rc-c01-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`(Passage A) it can retrain a model, adjust its thresholds, and publish the results`,`(Passage B) proprietary black boxes`],requiredInference:`Extend A's improvability thesis to the black-box objection: correctability matters more than current opacity.`,viewpointOwner:`author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`A understates to mere equivalence; B reverses A's stance; C adds "every industry"; D defends secrecy A never defends.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Hardest comparative item; credited answer is A's principle extended, not invented. Difficulty 4 honest.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`The author of Passage A would most likely respond to Passage B's claim about proprietary "black boxes" by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`human hiring managers are equally secretive about their own decision-making processes`},{text:`regulators should ban all automated hiring tools until perfect transparency can be guaranteed by independent third-party auditors`},{text:`black-box systems have been proven to outperform human managers in every industry studied so far`},{text:`trade secrecy is necessary to protect innovation in hiring technology firms`},{text:`the ability to log, test, and retrain algorithmic decisions makes them more accountable than human judgment even when vendors resist scrutiny`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A's core commitments are that algorithmic decisions are logged and explicit, that disparate outcomes "can be detected and corrected," and that models can be retrained — i.e., the technology is inherently improvable in ways human prejudice is not. Choice E extends that position to answer B's black-box objection.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This asks for A's likely reply, which must be grounded in A's stated commitments. A's central comparative claim is about improvability: "A company cannot easily rewire the intuitions of ten thousand hiring managers, but it can retrain a model, adjust its thresholds, and publish the results." Applied to B's black-box point, A would concede current opacity problems but insist the technology's loggable, testable nature makes accountability achievable — unlike human bias, which cannot be rewired at all. Choice E states this qualified defense. Choice D is unsupported: A never defends trade secrecy; A wants results published. Choice A is close but weaker — A's point is not mere equivalence of secrecy but the asymmetry of correctability. Choice C overstates with "every industry" and "proven." Choice B contradicts A's pro-automation stance; A would never urge a ban.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because A does compare algorithms favorably to humans. It fails because it understates A's position: A's claim is not that both are equally opaque but that algorithms are structurally more correctable ("it can retrain a model... and publish the results").`,`This is tempting because transparency is at issue. It fails because it reverses A's stance: A advocates adopting and improving these systems, not banning them pending perfection.`,`This is tempting as a strong pro-A statement. It fails on "every industry" and "proven" — A claims algorithms are better than the flawed human system they replace, not universally proven superior across all industries.`,`This is tempting because trade secrecy is B's target. It fails because Passage A never defends trade secrecy — A's vision requires that results be "published" and models retrained openly. Defending secrecy contradicts A's commitments.`,`This is correct. It applies A's improvability thesis to B's objection: even granting current vendor resistance, the technology's logged, explicit, retrainable nature makes accountability possible in a way human intuition never allows.`],generalLesson:`For "how would X respond" questions, extract X's core principle first, then apply it to the objection. The answer is the principle's natural extension — not a stronger or weaker claim than X actually makes.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`half-right`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is Passage A's fundamental claim about algorithms versus humans? Find the sentence about retraining a model versus rewiring intuitions.`,`How would that claim answer the black-box objection — by denying opacity, or by arguing correctability matters more?`,`Eliminate choices that defend trade secrecy or propose bans — neither matches A's pro-improvement stance.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}},{id:`rc-c01-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2,3],supportingSentences:[`(Passage A) demonstrably discriminatory`,`(Passage B) exactly what that company has always done`],requiredInference:`Find what both sides presuppose while disputing the solution: human hiring is bias-prone.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B is contradicted by B; C invents a legal obligation; D is explicitly denied by A; E is far too strong.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Shared-assumption item; the common enemy, not the disputed solution.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`Both passages are committed to which one of the following assumptions?`,choices:[{text:`Human hiring processes are susceptible to bias and discrimination.`},{text:`Independent audits of hiring algorithms are currently routine and effective.`},{text:`Employers have a legal obligation to explain every hiring rejection.`},{text:`Algorithmic hiring systems are inherently neutral and objective.`},{text:`Increasing workforce diversity is the only valid goal of hiring reform.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A calls the human system "demonstrably discriminatory" and describes interview bias in detail; B discusses managers who discriminate and never disputes that human hiring is biased — its argument is that algorithms reproduce rather than remove that bias. Both assume human hiring is bias-prone. Choice A states the shared assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A shared assumption must be something both passages take for granted, even as they disagree about algorithms. Passage A's entire case presupposes human hiring is biased — it opens with subjectivity, name-based discrimination, and mood-driven interviews, and its closing comparison calls the human system "demonstrably discriminatory." Passage B likewise presupposes it: its argument is that algorithms learn "exactly what that company has always done" (i.e., discriminate), and its accountability paragraph contrasts algorithmic opacity with a manager who discriminates. Neither passage defends human hiring. Choice A is the common ground. Choice D is A's opponents' caricature — A explicitly says "None of this means algorithms are neutral by nature." Choice B is contradicted by B and unclaimed by A. Choice C is invented; B mentions a moral "right to be judged by reasons," not a legal obligation. Choice E is far too strong and stated by neither.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. Both passages treat human bias as the baseline problem: A's case for automation presupposes it, and B's "laundering" argument presupposes there is historical discrimination for algorithms to learn. It is the shared starting point.`,`This is tempting because A praises auditability. It fails because B says audits are "largely theoretical" and A claims only that systems can be audited, not that audits are routine. Neither assumes current effectiveness.`,`This is tempting because B discusses the right to explanations. It fails because B frames it as a moral point about fair treatment ("the right to be judged by reasons"), not as a legal obligation — and A never assumes any such obligation.`,`This is tempting because A is pro-algorithm. It fails because A explicitly denies it: "None of this means algorithms are neutral by nature." Neither passage assumes inherent neutrality.`,`This is tempting because diversity appears in A. It fails on "only valid goal": A also values efficiency and fairness generally, and B values accountability and explainability. Neither reduces hiring reform to diversity alone.`],generalLesson:`Shared assumptions are found in what both sides take for granted while arguing about something else. Look for the problem both sides agree exists — here, human bias — rather than their competing solutions.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What problem does Passage A say algorithms solve? Does Passage B dispute that the problem exists, or argue the solution fails?`,`B says algorithms learn "exactly what that company has always done." What must B be assuming about what companies have done?`,`The shared assumption is the common enemy, not the disputed solution.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}},{id:`rc-c01-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`(Passage A) every decision is logged, every criterion is explicit, and disparate outcomes can be detected and corrected`],whyDistractorsFail:`A is explicitly denied by A ("not neutral by nature"); B reverses the charm point; D/E are unsupported (elimination, cost).`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Direct paragraph-2 paraphrase.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`According to Passage A, which one of the following is an advantage of algorithmic screening over human hiring managers?`,choices:[{text:`Algorithms are naturally free of all bias because they are based on mathematics.`},{text:`Algorithms can evaluate candidates' charm and interpersonal skills more accurately than interviewers can.`},{text:`Algorithmic decisions are logged and explicit, so disparate outcomes can be detected and corrected.`},{text:`Algorithms eliminate the need for any human involvement in the hiring process.`},{text:`Algorithms are less expensive to operate than employing human hiring managers.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`Passage A states this directly: "every decision is logged, every criterion is explicit, and disparate outcomes can be detected and corrected." Choice C restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Passage A's second paragraph lists the auditability advantage in nearly these words. Choice C is a direct paraphrase. Choice A contradicts A's explicit denial ("None of this means algorithms are neutral by nature"). Choice B reverses A's point: interviews rewarding "charm over competence" is presented as a human flaw, not something algorithms replicate. Choice D is unsupported; A never claims humans can be eliminated entirely. Choice E is invented; cost is never discussed in either passage. The tested detail is A's auditability triad from paragraph 2 — logged decisions, explicit criteria, correctable outcomes. The right answer paraphrases that triad; the distractors either contradict A's explicit denial that algorithms are neutral by nature or import considerations like cost and charm that the passage assigns elsewhere or nowhere.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a pro-algorithm slogan. It fails because Passage A explicitly rejects it: "None of this means algorithms are neutral by nature." A's claim is improvability, not inherent neutrality.`,`This is tempting because interviews are discussed. It fails because A presents charm-rewarding interviews as the problem ("interviews reward charm over competence"), not as a capacity algorithms should or do replicate.`,`This is correct. It paraphrases paragraph 2: logged decisions, explicit criteria, and the resulting ability to detect and correct disparate outcomes — the core of A's auditability argument.`,`This is tempting as an automation extreme. It fails because A never claims human involvement can or should be eliminated; the argument is about improving screening, not removing humans entirely.`,`This is tempting because automation is often about cost. It fails because cost is never mentioned in either passage; A's advantages are fairness, auditability, and improvability.`],generalLesson:`When a passage states an advantage in a list ("every decision is logged, every criterion is explicit"), the detail answer is usually a clean paraphrase of that list — watch for choices that upgrade it to an absolute.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage A's second paragraph lists what makes algorithms auditable. What three things does it say?`,`Eliminate any choice Passage A explicitly denies — check the sentence beginning "None of this means."`,`The correct choice concerns logging, explicit criteria, and correctability.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}},{id:`rc-c01-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`(Passage B) is not removing bias from hiring; it is automating a workplace culture`],requiredInference:`Restate the caregiver example's moral without adding intent or absolutes.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B adds deliberate intent B never alleges; C advocates discretion B never seeks; D contradicts B's critique of narrow parity; E claims hopelessness B never states.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile audit: KEEP. Thesis-in-miniature inference; distractors add intent or absolutes.`}],stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c01`,stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly suggested by Passage B?`,choices:[{text:`Rejecting caregivers because past top performers worked uninterrupted hours perpetuates bias rather than removing it.`},{text:`Vendors of hiring software deliberately design their systems to discriminate against protected groups.`},{text:`Human hiring managers should be given even greater discretion to compensate for algorithmic flaws.`},{text:`Statistical parity is the only meaningful measure of fairness in hiring.`},{text:`No hiring system, human or automated, can ever produce fair outcomes.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B's second paragraph gives exactly this example: an algorithm screening out caregivers because past "high performers" worked uninterrupted eighty-hour weeks "is not removing bias from hiring; it is automating a workplace culture." Choice A restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Passage B's caregiver example is its concrete illustration of bias laundering: the criterion looks neutral (work history patterns) but encodes a discriminatory workplace culture. Choice A restates the example and its moral. Choice E is too strong: B criticizes current automated systems and defends human accountability, never claiming fairness is impossible. Choice B adds deliberate intent ("deliberately design... to discriminate") that B never alleges — B's claim is about training data and opacity, not malice. Choice C is unsupported: B wants explainability and accountability, not greater managerial discretion. Choice D contradicts B: audits measuring "only narrow statistical parity" are criticized as missing the deeper question of whether criteria are fair. Inference questions in comparative sets often turn on a single loaded example. B's hiring-criterion case carries the passage's thesis in miniature: a neutral-looking rule encoding a discriminatory culture. The supported inference restates that moral without adding new claims about what B would endorse elsewhere.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It restates B's caregiver example and the conclusion B draws from it: the system "is not removing bias from hiring; it is automating a workplace culture that many consider part of the problem."`,`This is tempting because B is harshly critical of vendors. It fails by adding intent: B accuses vendors of resisting scrutiny via trade secrecy, not of deliberately designing discrimination. The bias mechanism is training data, not malice.`,`This is tempting because B contrasts algorithms unfavorably with managers. It fails because B's point is about accountability and explainability, not about expanding managerial discretion — B never advocates giving managers more power.`,`This is tempting because audits are discussed. It fails because B criticizes audits that measure "only narrow statistical parity" as inadequate; statistical parity is presented as insufficient, not as the sole meaningful measure. B's example does the argumentative work of the whole passage in miniature: a neutral-looking criterion encoding a discriminatory culture, which is why restating the example's moral is the strongly supported inference.`,`This is tempting as a pessimistic summary. It fails because B defends the value of human accountability (appeals to conscience, policy, law) and never claims fairness is unattainable — only that current automated systems fail at it.`],generalLesson:`Concrete examples in a passage usually illustrate the author's thesis in miniature. An inference that restates the example's point is strongly supported; one that adds intent or absolutes is not.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage B gives a specific example about caregivers and eighty-hour weeks. What conclusion does B draw from it?`,`The correct choice restates that example and its moral without adding claims about intent.`,`Eliminate choices that make B sound hopeless about all fairness or that add motives B never alleges.`],labels:{topic:`algorithmic hiring`}}],g=[{id:`rc-c02-q1`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`A P2: "it is between managed recovery and managed extinction"`,`B P3: "a site-specific management decision ... rather than as a moral crusade to undo the past"`],requiredInference:`A generalizes from the condor to endorse broad reintroduction; B insists celebrated cases do not license broad programs.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A and B are undisputed background facts; C is agreed-upon (both acknowledge costs); E is an undisputed historical event.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The authors of the two passages would most likely disagree over whether`,choices:[{text:`the California condor was brought back from 22 birds to more than 500 through captive breeding`},{text:`lead poisoning from spent ammunition was the main driver of the condor’s decline`},{text:`conservation programs should openly acknowledge the costs they impose rather than pretending coexistence is free`},{text:`successful recoveries like the condor’s show that reintroduction should be pursued broadly rather than decided case by case`},{text:`wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in the mid-1990s, a historical fact neither passage disputes`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A draws a general lesson from the condor — intensive intervention can reverse even brink-of-extinction declines — and treats that as validation for pursuing reintroduction broadly. B argues celebrated narratives “outrun their evidence” and that reintroduction must be a “site-specific management decision,” not a broad program. Choice D states that policy-level disagreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The disagreement must be a point where the passages take opposing stances on the same question. A generalizes from the condor: “extinction is not always inevitable,” and determined intervention is “proof” that recovery programs work — a brief for reintroduction as a general strategy. B resists exactly that move from celebrated cases to broad programs: the Yellowstone story "was overstated," the wolf’s role is “contingent,” and reintroduction should be “weighed against local conditions” rather than pursued as “a moral crusade to undo the past.” Choice D captures this dispute over what reintroduction successes license. Choice A is a background fact from A that B never addresses, so there is no disagreement. Choice B is A’s causal claim, undisputed by B. Choice C is common ground: A admits the program was “not cheap or simple” and faced “political resistance,” while B centers costs — they agree costs must be acknowledged and dispute how to weigh them. Choice E is shared history. The method is to test each choice for a yes/no split across the policy question: does A affirm broad reintroduction while B denies it? Only the licensing question — what successes justify — splits them.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a factual anchor. It fails because B never addresses the condor at all — the recovery is background from A, not a point of contention. There is no disagreement about the event itself.`,`This is tempting because lead poisoning is central to A’s story. It fails because B does not dispute the condor’s cause of decline; B’s skepticism targets the Yellowstone narrative and the policy generalization, not condor toxicology.`,`This is tempting because costs divide the passages’ tone. It fails because both acknowledge costs openly: A calls the program costly and politically resisted, B details rancher burdens. The dispute is about how to weigh acknowledged costs, not whether they exist.`,`This is correct. A treats the condor as validation for broad reintroduction (“managed recovery” over “managed extinction”); B argues celebrated stories overstate their evidence and that reintroduction must be site-specific rather than crusading. That is the central fault line.`,`This is tempting as a shared-facts trap. It fails because neither passage disputes the Yellowstone reintroduction as a historical event — A no longer discusses it, B critiques the narrative built on it. The event itself is common ground.`],generalLesson:`Distinguish disputed interpretations from shared facts. Passages in disagreement often agree on what happened while disputing what it proves — the disagreement question targets the "what it proves" level.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What general lesson does Passage A draw from the condor? What does Passage B say about generalizing from celebrated cases?`,`A calls the condor “proof that determined intervention works”; B calls reintroduction “a moral crusade to undo the past.” What policy difference follows?`,`The disagreement is about what reintroduction successes justify, not about the basic facts.`],labels:{topic:`species reintroduction`}},{id:`rc-c02-q2`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,3],supportingSentences:[`B P1: "the celebrated trophic cascade was overstated"`,`B P3: "site-specific management decision ... moral crusade to undo the past"`],requiredInference:`B engages on two levels: disputing the evidence and reframing the moral question.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B never claims preservation loses money, the passages are adversarial not complementary, B engages ecology head-on, and B grants no general license from the condor case.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following best describes the relationship between the two passages?`,choices:[{text:`Passage B disputes the evidence behind celebrated reintroduction narratives and urges site-specific decisions, while Passage A treats the condor recovery as validating broad reintroduction.`},{text:`Passage A and Passage B present complementary accounts that together give a complete and balanced picture of condor biology and of the modern policy debates surrounding species reintroduction.`},{text:`Passage A directly refutes Passage B’s claims about rancher compensation programs by showing that payments now fully cover livestock losses.`},{text:`Passage B ignores every ecological question entirely and focuses solely on the tourism revenue that wolves generate for gateway towns each year.`},{text:`Passage B fully accepts Passage A’s condor narrative but argues that the economic costs of reintroduction ultimately outweigh its ecological benefits.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B disputes the move from celebrated cases to broad programs: the Yellowstone narrative "was overstated," costs are inequitably shared, and reintroduction should be a “site-specific management decision.” A moves the opposite way, drawing a general lesson from the condor for reintroduction broadly. Choice A captures both halves of the engagement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B engages the pro-reintroduction position on two levels. Evidential: the most celebrated case — Yellowstone — is “overstated,” with elk already declining, climate confounding the data, and recoveries in areas wolves rarely visit. Framing: A presents recovery as correcting past error and asks whether we manage for price tags or preservation; B rejects this moralized framing, calling broad reintroduction “a moral crusade to undo the past” and insisting on negotiation with affected communities. Choice A names both the evidential caution and the reframing. Choice E fails because B does not accept A’s narrative as a general license — B’s whole point is that celebrated stories prove less than claimed. Choice B fails because the passages are adversarial, not complementary. Choice D fails because B engages ecological claims head-on in paragraph 1 and mentions tourism only as an aside about who benefits. Choice C reverses the direction: neither passage refutes the other’s compensation claims; A presents mitigation as working while B calls programs “chronically underfunded.”`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It identifies B’s two moves: warning that celebrated reintroduction stories outrun their evidence and that costs fall on specific communities, then urging site-specific, negotiated decisions — set against A’s use of the condor as validation for reintroduction as a broad strategy.`,`This is tempting because both discuss reintroduction seriously. It fails because the passages are in tension, not complementary: B’s “overstated” directly undercuts A’s “proof that determined intervention works,” and their policy conclusions oppose each other.`,`This is tempting because compensation is discussed. It fails twice: the compensation debate belongs to B’s wolf discussion, which A never addresses, and A refutes nothing — A’s mitigation claims concern condor monitoring and ammunition, not rancher payments.`,`This is tempting because tourism revenue is mentioned. It fails twice: B engages ecological claims head-on in paragraph 1, and tourism appears only as an aside about who benefits — not as B’s focus.`,`This is tempting because B emphasizes costs. It fails because B does not grant A’s general lesson: B’s first paragraph argues celebrated narratives prove less than claimed. The challenge is evidential and structural, not merely a weighing of accepted ecology against economics.`],generalLesson:`Relationship answers must capture all of the second passage's engagement. If B both disputes evidence and reframes the moral question, the answer needs both halves.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage B accept that celebrated reintroductions license broad programs? Check B’s verdict on the Yellowstone evidence.`,`Beyond evidence, how does B characterize the pro-reintroduction framing? Look at B’s final paragraph.`,`The correct choice covers both the evidential caution and the clash over broad versus site-specific policy.`],labels:{topic:`species reintroduction`}},{id:`rc-c02-q3`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`A P3: "None of this was cheap or simple"`,`A P3: "the alternative was a sky permanently without condors"`],requiredInference:`Apply A's two-part cost reply (admit, then re-weigh against irreversible loss) to B's rancher-burden objection.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`B denies no costs (A itemizes them); C invents cost-free zones; D adopts B's premises; E overstates tourism into full compensation.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The author of Passage A would most likely respond to Passage B's concern about the burdens on ranching communities by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`those burdens are real but manageable and shared, and they are outweighed by preventing irreversible loss`},{text:`ranchers’ losses are entirely imaginary, no mitigation is necessary, and compensation programs should be abolished`},{text:`reintroduction should occur only in places where no one bears any cost whatsoever, regardless of the species’ plight`},{text:`the evidence is so uncertain that reintroduction should halt entirely until every affected group gives explicit approval`},{text:`tourism revenue from reintroduced species fully compensates every affected rancher for all economic harm suffered`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A’s third paragraph directly addresses cost objections: the program was “not cheap or simple,” yet “the alternative was a sky permanently without condors,” and the question is whether conservation is judged “by its price tag or by what it preserves.” Choice A restates that reply: acknowledge and manage the burdens, weigh them against irreversible loss.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A anticipates the cost objection in paragraph 3 and answers it two ways: forthright admission (tens of millions, perpetual monitoring, political resistance over ammunition) and re-weighing (“the alternative was a sky permanently without condors” — judge “by what it preserves”). Choice A combines both elements. Choice B contradicts A, which never denies costs — it itemizes them. Choice C is unsupported: A argues for determined intervention as a general matter, not for cost-free zones it never proposes. Choice D adopts B’s premises (uncertainty, universal approval); A is confident the intervention worked and does not condition it on consensus. Choice E overstates: A’s dividends are the preserved species itself, not a claim that tourism dollars make each affected party financially whole. A’s reply is the passage’s own anticipation of B’s objection, so the hypothetical response is found, not invented: admit the costs, manage them, and weigh them against permanent loss.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It reproduces A’s two-part reply: the burdens are admitted and can be managed (shared funding, monitoring, ammunition cooperation), and they must be weighed against the irreversible alternative — a sky permanently without condors.`,`This is tempting as a dismissive reply. It fails because A explicitly itemizes the costs — tens of millions, perpetual monitoring, political resistance — rather than denying them. Denial contradicts A’s text.`,`This is tempting as a compromise. It fails because A defends determined intervention generally (“proof that determined intervention works”), not reintroduction limited to cost-free locations — a restriction A never proposes.`,`This is tempting because B stresses uncertainty. It fails as A’s reply because it adopts B’s premises: A is confident the condor program worked and never conditions intervention on unanimous approval.`,`This is tempting because tourism revenue appears in the debate. It fails because A’s “dividends” are the preserved species and the principle vindicated, not a financial claim that tourism makes each rancher whole.`],generalLesson:`When a passage anticipates an objection, its reply is usually the best source for "how would X respond." Quote the reply's structure: mitigation plus re-weighing.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:105,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage A address cost objections anywhere? Look at its final paragraph.`,`A’s reply has two parts: an admission about the program’s costs, and a re-weighing against the alternative. Find both.`,`Eliminate choices that deny the costs or adopt B’s skepticism — neither matches A.`],labels:{topic:`species reintroduction`}},{id:`rc-c02-q4`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{inferentialDistance:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`A P1: "over 300 soaring wild"`,`B P1: "The wolf's ecological role is real but contingent"`],requiredInference:`"Overstated" and "contingent" qualify a genuine reestablishment — disagreement about degree presupposes agreement about possibility.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Each distractor is contradicted by one passage: B disputes unqualified success, A stresses perpetual monitoring, B documents resistance, A notes political resistance.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Both passages assume that`,choices:[{text:`every reintroduction effort is an unqualified scientific success`},{text:`the condor recovery required no ongoing management after the first releases`},{text:`local communities uniformly welcome reintroduction programs`},{text:`regulating lead ammunition faced no meaningful political opposition`},{text:`reintroduced species can genuinely reestablish themselves in the wild`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A’s entire narrative presupposes that released condors can survive and breed in the wild — over 300 now fly free. B’s critique is that the wolf’s influence was “overstated” and is “contingent” — qualifications of a real reestablishment, not denials. Both take for granted that reintroduction can biologically succeed; they dispute the policy implications. Choice E states the shared assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The shared assumption must survive both passages’ disagreement. A’s condor story — captive-bred birds released, now breeding wild — presupposes reintroduction can work biologically. B disputes the Yellowstone narrative’s strength and generality, calling the wolf’s role “real but contingent”; “contingent” and “overstated” qualify a genuine reestablishment rather than denying it. Choice E captures this precisely. Choice A fails because B explicitly disputes any “unqualified success” narrative. Choice B fails because A stresses “perpetual monitoring of released birds.” Choice C fails because B’s whole second paragraph documents community resistance. Choice D fails because A notes the ammunition ask “met sustained political resistance.” This is the general principle for shared-assumption questions in adversarial pairs: look inside the disagreement’s presuppositions, where qualifiers like “overstated” and “contingent” reveal a contested degree built on an uncontested possibility.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because A celebrates the condor. It fails as shared: B’s whole first paragraph argues celebrated reintroduction narratives overstate their success. “Unqualified” is precisely what B denies.`,`This is tempting as background detail. It fails because A explicitly says the program “demands perpetual monitoring of released birds” — ongoing management is central to A’s account, not absent.`,`This is tempting as background goodwill. It fails because B documents the opposite: burdens falling on rural communities that did not choose the experiment, with compensation chronically underfunded.`,`This is tempting as background policy. It fails because A states the non-lead ammunition ask “has met sustained political resistance” — opposition is acknowledged, not absent.`,`This is correct. A’s wild-breeding condors and B’s “real but contingent” wolf role both presuppose that reintroduced species can genuinely reestablish; the disagreement is over how much that success proves and what policy follows — not whether reestablishment is possible.`],generalLesson:`Qualifiers like "overstated" and "contingent" signal disagreement about degree, which presupposes agreement about existence. Shared assumptions often hide inside the disagreement's presuppositions.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`B says the Yellowstone story was “overstated” and the wolf’s role “contingent.” Do those words deny that reintroduction can work, or qualify it?`,`What must both authors believe for their disagreement about policy implications to make sense?`,`Eliminate choices that either passage explicitly contradicts — B disputes unqualified success, A stresses perpetual monitoring and political resistance.`],labels:{topic:`species reintroduction`}},{id:`rc-c02-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:1,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`A P1: lead poisoning -> last 22 captured -> captive breeding -> releases -> 500+ birds`],whyDistractorsFail:`B invents a hunting ban and diet switch; C reverses the outcome; D imports Passage B's wolves; E reverses both the breeding outcome and the mechanism.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`According to Passage A, the recovery of the California condor involved which one of the following sequences?`,choices:[{text:`Lead poisoning drove the decline; the last 22 wild birds were taken into captivity for breeding; releases began and the population grew past 500.`},{text:`Hunting was banned outright across all condor habitat, which caused condors to abandon scavenging and hunt live prey instead.`},{text:`Condors were moved permanently into zoos after wildlife agencies concluded that any wild release was impossible.`},{text:`The population rebounded on its own once wolf packs reduced elk herds and restored streamside nesting habitat.`},{text:`Captive breeding failed completely, but the remaining wild condors gradually developed resistance to lead poisoning.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`Paragraph 1 traces the sequence: lead poisoning from spent ammunition drove the decline; in 1987 the last 22 wild condors were captured for captive breeding; releases began four years later; today more than 500 exist with over 300 flying wild. Choice A follows that sequence.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This is a detail question testing recall of A’s causal chain. The passage gives the sequence explicitly: lead fragments in scavenged carcasses → population collapse to 22 → capture and captive breeding → releases beginning four years later → 500+ birds, 300+ wild. Choice A reproduces those links accurately. Choice B invents an outright hunting ban and a dietary switch the passage never mentions. Choice C reverses the outcome: release succeeded rather than being abandoned — the passage celebrates wild condors, not permanent captivity. Choice D imports the other passage’s wolves-and-elk story, which has nothing to do with condors. Choice E reverses both the breeding outcome (it worked) and the mechanism (lead was removed through regulation and ammunition switching, not adapted to). Detail questions about historical sequences are defeated by writing the chain out as stated and checking each link before choosing.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It follows paragraph 1’s chain: lead poisoning → capture of the last 22 wild birds → captive breeding → releases four years later → more than 500 condors, over 300 wild.`,`This is tempting because hunting and ammunition are discussed. It fails twice: no outright hunting ban is mentioned, and condors remain scavengers — the passage never claims a switch to live prey.`,`This is tempting because captivity is central. It fails by reversing the outcome: the passage’s triumph is precisely that captive-bred birds were released and now fly wild — over 300 of them.`,`This is tempting because wolves and elk appear in the pair. It fails because that causal story belongs to Passage B’s Yellowstone discussion, which concerns a different species in a different ecosystem — a cross-passage contaminant.`,`This is tempting as a natural-selection story. It fails twice: captive breeding succeeded rather than failed, and the threat was addressed by removing lead (regulation, ammunition switching), not by the birds evolving resistance.`],generalLesson:`Causal-chain details are tested by reversing links or outcomes. Trace the chain as stated and check each link's direction before choosing.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`keyword-match`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage A’s first paragraph lists the recovery step by step. What caused the decline, and what happened to the last 22 birds?`,`Follow the chain: lead poisoning → capture → breeding → release → 500+ birds. Which choice preserves every link?`,`Eliminate any choice that reverses an outcome (failure versus success) or imports the other passage’s species.`],labels:{topic:`species reintroduction`}},{id:`rc-c02-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c02`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "weighed against local conditions, honest about uncertainties, and negotiated with the people who will live alongside the consequences"`],requiredInference:`The credited choice paraphrases B's explicit closing prescription.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage B`,whyDistractorsFail:`B alleges overstatement not fraud; B treats predation losses as real and wants better compensation; B says predators have a place; B demands urgent mitigation, not impossibility.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly suggested by Passage B?`,choices:[{text:`Wolf reintroduction decisions should be made locally, acknowledging uncertainties and including affected communities.`},{text:`The Yellowstone reintroduction was based on fabricated data and deliberate deception.`},{text:`Compensation programs for ranchers are unnecessary because wolf predation causes no real economic harm.`},{text:`Wolves have no ecological value and should be removed from all protected areas.`},{text:`Ecological restoration is impossible in any landscape where humans and predators coexist.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B's final paragraph prescribes exactly this: reintroduction as "a site-specific management decision — weighed against local conditions, honest about uncertainties, and negotiated with the people who will live alongside the consequences." Choice A restates it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B's conclusion is prescriptive, and choice A paraphrases it nearly verbatim: site-specific, honest about uncertainties, negotiated with affected people. The other choices all contradict B's explicit concessions: Choice D contradicts "the wolf's ecological role is real" and "predators have no place" being denied ("None of this means predators have no place"). Choice C contradicts "chronically underfunded" programs paying "a fraction of actual losses." Choice B adds fabrication and deception, far beyond B's claim of overstated narrative and confounded data. Choice E is far too strong; B endorses coexistence managed locally, not impossibility. Viewpoint questions reward attending to the author's stated concessions, which bound how far the position extends. B concedes the wolf's real ecological role and endorses negotiated local coexistence; any choice pushing B toward blanket rejection or toward A's enthusiasm contradicts those explicit boundaries.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It restates B's closing prescription: site-specific decisions, honesty about uncertainties, and negotiation with the communities living alongside the consequences — rather than crusading reintroduction.`,`This is tempting because B criticizes the evidence. It fails by escalating criticism into fraud: B says the narrative "was overstated" and data were confounded — methodological overreach, not fabrication or deception.`,`This is tempting because costs are debated. It fails because B treats predation losses as real and serious ("constant vigilance," "a fraction of actual losses" compensated). B wants better compensation, not none.`,`This is tempting as an anti-wolf extreme. It fails because B explicitly says "None of this means predators have no place in modern landscapes" and calls the wolf's role "real." B argues for restraint, not removal.`,`This is tempting as a pessimistic reading. It fails as far too strong: B's entire prescription assumes coexistence is possible with proper local management. "Impossible" contradicts the passage's conclusion.`],generalLesson:`A passage's prescription is usually stated plainly in its final paragraph. The strongly-supported inference restates it; distractors escalate it into extremes the author explicitly disavowed. B's closing prescription is unusually explicit — site-specific, honest about uncertainties, negotiated with affected communities — so the answer is essentially a paraphrase, while the wrong choices contradict B's stated concessions about the wolf's real role.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does Passage B recommend instead of crusading reintroduction? Read its final paragraph.`,`The prescription has three parts: site-specific, honest about uncertainties, and negotiated with someone. With whom?`,`Eliminate choices that contradict B's explicit concessions (the wolf's role is real; predators have a place).`],labels:{topic:`wolf reintroduction`}}],_=[{id:`rc-c03-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:1,itemPurpose:`skill-acquisition`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,3],supportingSentences:[`A P1: "a productive asset"`,`B P3: "we should save old streets first because they embody who we are"`],requiredInference:`Strip each passage's rationale; the shared conclusion is that preservation is worthwhile.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A bans nothing (it "channels" growth); B welcomes prosperity as consequence; A's whole thesis is economic; A celebrates heritage tourism.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The authors of the two passages agree that`,choices:[{text:`new construction should be banned in all historic areas`},{text:`preservationists should never consider economic consequences`},{text:`historic preservation can never be economically justified`},{text:`historic districts are worth preserving`},{text:`tourism is harmful to the character of historic neighborhoods`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A argues preservation is a "productive asset" worth pursuing; B argues old streets "embody who we are" and should be saved. Both conclude preservation is worthwhile — they differ only on why. Choice D states the agreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Agreement questions ask for the shared conclusion beneath different reasonings. A's conclusion: preserve historic districts because they pay off economically. B's conclusion: save old streets because they embody collective memory. The shared conclusion is that preservation is worthwhile. Choice D states it plainly. Choice C contradicts A, which makes the economic case. Choice A is too strong: A explicitly says preservation "does not prohibit growth" but "channels it." Choice E contradicts both: A celebrates heritage tourism, and B never criticizes tourists. Choice B contradicts B, which says "no one mourns" the prosperity that follows preservation — B welcomes economic benefits as a consequence. Agreement questions in same-verdict pairs are answered by stripping away each author's rationale and keeping only the shared conclusion. A preserves districts for economic reasons, B for memory and recognition; the agreement is the verdict both reach, not the reasons either gives.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a strong preservationist stance. It fails because A explicitly rejects it: thoughtful preservation "does not prohibit growth; it channels it." Neither author calls for banning new construction.`,`This is tempting because B prioritizes memory over money. It fails on "never": B says prosperity following preservation is a "fortunate consequence" no one mourns — economic consequences may be welcomed, just not made the justification. Agreement questions in agree-but-for-different-reasons pairs are answered by the shared verdict stripped of rationale: both passages conclude old districts should be preserved, and only that neutral verdict survives both authors' scrutiny.`,`This is tempting because B subordinates economics. It fails because A's entire passage argues preservation is economically justified — the passages cannot agree on something A's thesis contradicts.`,`This is correct. It is the common conclusion both passages reach by different routes: A via economic productivity, B via cultural memory. Everything else they dispute is about reasons and priorities, not the verdict.`,`This is tempting because tourism changes neighborhoods. It fails because A treats heritage tourism as a core benefit (travelers "stay longer — and spend more"), and B never criticizes tourism.`],generalLesson:`When two passages agree on a conclusion but differ on reasons, the agreement answer is the shared verdict stated neutrally — without either side's distinctive rationale.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does each passage conclude should be done about historic districts?`,`The passages differ on WHY. What do they share — the verdict or the reasoning?`,`Eliminate any choice that contradicts either passage's explicit statements.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}},{id:`rc-c03-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,3],supportingSentences:[`A P1: "its strongest justification may be economic"`,`B P3: "The point is one of priority"`],requiredInference:`The significant difference is justificatory, not factual: productivity versus memory.`,whyDistractorsFail:`No revenue or cost facts are disputed; neither questions districts' existence; beauty grounds neither argument.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The two passages differ most significantly in`,choices:[{text:`their estimates of how much tourism revenue historic districts generate`},{text:`their views on whether historic buildings can be rehabilitated at reasonable cost`},{text:`whether they believe historic districts exist in most cities`},{text:`the primary justification each offers for preserving historic districts`},{text:`their opinions about the architectural beauty of old buildings`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A justifies preservation economically (property values, tourism, virtuous cycles); B justifies it culturally (collective memory, recognition) and explicitly argues the economic justification should not come first. The difference is the primary rationale. Choice D states this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Both passages favor preservation, so the significant difference lies in their supporting reasons. A's rationale is economic throughout: rising property values, tourism spending, business clustering, rehabilitation cheaper than new construction. B's rationale is memorial and identitarian: autobiography of a community, recognition for displaced peoples, "we should save old streets first because they embody who we are." B even addresses A's rationale directly, arguing preservation "must" not "justify itself on a balance sheet." Choice D captures this. Choices A and B fail because neither passage disputes the other's factual claims about revenue or costs. Choice C fails because the existence of historic districts is never questioned. Choice E fails because beauty is not the ground of either argument — A argues utility, B argues meaning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because tourism revenue is discussed. It fails because B never disputes A's revenue claims — B's objection is about priority, not about the numbers. There is no factual disagreement here.`,`This is tempting because rehabilitation costs appear in A. It fails because B never addresses rehabilitation costs at all; cost-effectiveness is simply not contested between the passages.`,`This is tempting as a background fact. It fails because neither passage questions whether historic districts exist; both take their existence as given and argue about why to preserve them.`,`This is correct. The passages' significant difference is justificatory: economic productivity (A) versus cultural memory and identity (B), with B explicitly arguing the economic rationale must not be primary.`,`This is tempting because old buildings are described attractively. It fails because neither passage rests its case on beauty — A's case is economic, B's is memorial. Beauty is not the contested ground.`],generalLesson:`When passages agree on a conclusion, comparative questions target the reasoning. Distinguish factual disagreements (rare here) from justificatory ones (the real difference).`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`half-right`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Both passages want preservation. So what do they give different answers to — the "what" or the "why"?`,`How does Passage B respond to the economic argument? Look at its final paragraph.`,`The correct choice names the difference in primary justification.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}},{id:`rc-c03-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`,`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "None of this denies that preservation has practical benefits ... The point is one of priority"`],requiredInference:`B's move is concession plus reordering: accept the economics, demote them from justification to consequence.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B never claims preservation loses money; the arguments are structurally different; B advocates saving streets; A concedes nothing.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following best describes the relationship between the two passages?`,choices:[{text:`Passage B accepts Passage A's economic claims but argues they should be subordinate to a cultural justification for preservation.`},{text:`Passage B refutes Passage A's economic claims by showing that preservation consistently loses money.`},{text:`Passage A and Passage B make identical arguments using different examples.`},{text:`Passage B argues that historic districts should be demolished to make way for affordable housing.`},{text:`Passage A concedes that economic arguments for preservation are invalid and defers to Passage B's cultural case.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B says "None of this denies that preservation has practical benefits" and "no one mourns" the prosperity — accepting A's economics — but insists "the point is one of priority": memory first, prosperity as "fortunate consequence." Choice A describes this accept-and-subordinate relationship.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B's engagement with A is concessive but reordering. The key sentences are in B's final paragraph: "None of this denies that preservation has practical benefits; thriving historic districts do attract investment, and no one mourns that. The point is one of priority." B does not dispute A's facts; B disputes their place in the argument — economics as consequence rather than justification, warning that balance-sheet preservation "has already lost the deeper argument." Choice A captures this precisely. Choice B fails because B never claims preservation loses money. Choice C fails because the arguments differ fundamentally (utility vs. memory). Choice D fails because B advocates saving old streets, not demolishing them. Choice E reverses the direction: A never concedes anything to B.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It tracks B's exact move: conceding the economic benefits ("none of this denies... no one mourns that") while demoting them from justification to "fortunate consequence" — a dispute about priority, not facts.`,`This is tempting because B criticizes economic justification. It fails because B never disputes the economic facts — B explicitly grants that thriving districts attract investment. The criticism targets the rationale's priority, not its truth.`,`This is tempting because both favor preservation. It fails because the arguments are structurally different: A's is instrumental (preservation pays), B's is intrinsic (preservation honors memory). Same verdict, different arguments.`,`This is tempting because A mentions housing-supply critics. It fails because B never advocates demolition — B's thesis is that old streets should be saved for what they embody. Demolition is the opposite of B's position.`,`This is tempting as a symmetric concession. It fails because Passage A never appears to concede anything — A's passage is a self-contained economic case with no acknowledgment of B's cultural argument.`],generalLesson:`"Accept the facts, dispute the priority" is a distinctive comparative relationship. Look for concession language ("none of this denies") followed by a reordering claim ("the point is one of priority").`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`half-right`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage B say A's economic claims are false? Find the sentence beginning "None of this denies."`,`If B accepts the economics, what is B's objection? Look for the sentence about "priority."`,`The correct choice describes accepting claims while demoting their role in the argument.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}},{id:`rc-c03-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{inferentialDistance:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3,2],supportingSentences:[`A P3: "becoming more interchangeable"`,`B P2: "irreplaceable vessels of collective memory"`],requiredInference:`Both arguments presuppose distinctive, non-generic character — A's premiums and B's meanings both require it.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B assumes no profitability claim; A channels nearby growth; neither says districts were built for tourists; B denies primarily-economic valuation.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Both passages assume that historic districts`,choices:[{text:`are always more profitable than new commercial developments`},{text:`should be preserved without any new construction nearby`},{text:`were originally built to attract tourists`},{text:`have distinctive character setting them apart from generic new development`},{text:`are valued by their residents primarily for economic reasons`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A contrasts distinctive old districts with "generic development," warning against becoming "more interchangeable"; B describes districts as a community's "autobiography" carrying irreplaceable meanings. Both presuppose distinctive, non-generic character. Choice D states the shared assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The shared assumption must underlie both arguments. A's economic case depends on distinctiveness: travelers "seek out historic character," businesses thrive on "character... that new construction cannot replicate cheaply," and demolishing it makes cities "more interchangeable." B's memory case likewise depends on it: districts carry meanings "no plaque can fully capture" as vessels of "collective memory." Both assume historic districts have a distinctive character worth the argument. Choice D states this. Choice A is too strong and B never assumes it. Choice C is invented by neither. Choice B contradicts A ("channels" growth, not bans it). Choice E contradicts B, which says value "cannot be measured in tourist dollars." Shared-assumption questions look beneath the disagreement to what both arguments presuppose. Neither passage's case functions without distinctive character: A's price premiums need character buyers pay for, and B's recognition claims need places carrying irreplaceable meaning.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because A is bullish on economics. It fails on "always": A claims districts outperform "comparable neighborhoods," not that they beat every new development — and B assumes no such profitability claim.`,`This is tempting as a preservationist assumption. It fails because A explicitly favors channeling density to appropriate sites near protected cores — new construction nearby is accepted, even welcomed.`,`This is tempting because tourism is discussed. It fails because neither passage suggests districts were built for tourists; A says travelers seek out existing character, B roots meaning in lived community history.`,`This is correct. A's case requires that old districts offer character new construction "cannot replicate cheaply" (else no premium), and B's requires they carry irreplaceable meanings. Distinctive character is the shared presupposition. Neither passage could get started without it: A's price premiums require character buyers will pay for, and B's memory claims require places that carry irreplaceable meanings — the same distinctiveness doing different argumentative work.`,`This is tempting because economics matter in A. It fails because B explicitly denies it: the value B describes "cannot be measured in tourist dollars," and B's communities value recognition and memory, not primarily economics.`],generalLesson:`Shared assumptions often concern the subject's nature rather than the argument's conclusion. Ask what both sides must believe about historic districts for either argument to get off the ground.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:100,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`A warns against becoming "more interchangeable." What must A be assuming about historic districts for that warning to make sense?`,`B calls districts a community's "autobiography." What does that presuppose about their character?`,`The shared assumption is about what historic districts are like, not about whether to preserve them.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}},{id:`rc-c03-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`A P1: "its strongest justification may be economic"`,`A P1: "a productive asset"`],requiredInference:`Isolate Passage A: its thesis is the economic case, announced in its first paragraph.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`Beauty/memory is B's ground; the housing objection is the critics' view A rebuts; "only tourists" narrows A; museums are never mentioned.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of Passage A?`,choices:[{text:`Historic districts should be preserved because they are beautiful reminders of the past.`},{text:`Preservation restricts housing supply and therefore harms the cities it claims to help.`},{text:`The only reason to preserve historic districts is to attract wealthy tourists.`},{text:`Museums should replace historic districts as the primary custodians of cultural memory.`},{text:`Historic preservation pays economically: higher property values, tourism, and thriving local businesses.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A's thesis is stated in paragraph 1: "its strongest justification may be economic," followed by property values, tourism spending, and business clustering, plus the virtuous-cycle mechanism. Choice E summarizes this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Passage A's main point is its economic justification, announced in the first paragraph ("a productive asset," "strongest justification may be economic") and developed through property values, tourism, small-business clustering, and rehabilitation cost advantages, with a reply to the housing-supply objection. Choice E compresses this accurately. Choice A substitutes beauty/memory — B's ground, not A's. Choice B states the critics' objection that A rebuts, not A's point. Choice C narrows the economics to "only... wealthy tourists," contradicting A's broader claims (property values, local trades, businesses). Choice D is invented; museums appear nowhere in A. Isolating one passage within a comparative set is the essential discipline for single-passage questions. A's thesis is announced in its first paragraph as the economic case for preservation; importing B's memory rationale, the critics' housing objection, or invented institutions confuses whose argument is being summarized.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because preservation is often defended this way. It fails because beauty and memory are Passage B's grounds; A explicitly says the strongest justification "may be economic," not aesthetic.`,`This is tempting because the objection is discussed. It fails because it is the critics' position, which A rebuts: thoughtful preservation "channels" growth rather than freezing the city. It is the view A argues against.`,`This is tempting as a cynical summary of the economics. It fails on "only" and "wealthy tourists": A's benefits include property values, local businesses, and skilled trades — far broader than tourism alone.`,`This is tempting because memory institutions are adjacent topics. It fails because museums are never mentioned in Passage A; the choice imports an entirely foreign proposal.`,`This is correct. It restates A's thesis: preservation as "productive asset" — property values, tourism spending, business clustering — with the economic justification presented as the strongest one. Isolating one passage within a comparative set is the key discipline: A's thesis is announced in its first paragraph as the economic case, and importing B's memory rationale or the critics' housing objection confuses whose argument is being summarized.`],generalLesson:`For a single passage's main point within a comparative set, focus only on that passage. Do not let the other passage's rationale bleed into your answer.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`],estimatedSeconds:80,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does Passage A say is preservation's "strongest justification"?`,`List the economic benefits A names. Which choice covers them without adding B's ideas?`,`Eliminate choices stating the critics' objection or B's rationale as if they were A's.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}},{id:`rc-c03-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c03`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`B P2: "an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered"`],whyDistractorsFail:`Written-record reliability is never discussed; no statute is mentioned; property values are A's argument; investment is B's demoted consequence.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`According to Passage B, for neighborhoods whose histories include displacement or discrimination, preserving physical traces of that history serves as`,choices:[{text:`a substitute for written historical records, which are usually unreliable`},{text:`a legal requirement imposed by federal preservation statutes`},{text:`a way to increase property values for current residents`},{text:`an act of recognition that those lives counted and the place mattered`},{text:`a means of attracting investment from outside developers`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`B's second paragraph states this directly: preserving such traces "is an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered." Choice D quotes the passage's language.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This detail is stated nearly verbatim in Passage B's second paragraph. The passage argues that for communities with histories of struggle, preservation's value is recognitional rather than economic. Choice D reproduces the passage's own phrasing. Choice C imports A's economic rationale into B. Choice E similarly imports investment logic B subordinates. Choice A invents a claim about written records' reliability, never discussed. Choice B invents a legal requirement; B's argument is moral and cultural, not legal. Detail answers are strongest when they track the passage's own distinctive phrasing. B states the point as "an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered" — language no distractor reproduces, because each distractor belongs to a different argumentative register: A's economics, legal procedure, or claims about written records the passage never makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because traces versus records sounds plausible. It fails because the passage never discusses the reliability of written records or presents buildings as substitutes for them.`,`This is tempting because preservation involves regulation. It fails because B's argument here is moral ("an act of recognition"), not legal — no statute is mentioned anywhere in the passage. Because B states the point in such distinctive language — "an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered" — the correct choice is recognizable as a near-quotation, while every distractor belongs to a different argumentative register.`,`This is tempting because property values are discussed in the pair. It fails as an attribution error: rising property values are Passage A's argument, while this sentence belongs to B's non-economic case for recognition.`,`This is correct. It restates B's second paragraph verbatim in substance: for such neighborhoods, preservation "is an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered."`,`This is tempting because investment follows preservation. It fails because B explicitly demotes investment to a "fortunate consequence," not the purpose — and this sentence is about recognition, not economics.`],generalLesson:`In comparative sets, detail questions test whether you keep each passage's distinctive claims separate. Match the detail to its passage before matching it to a choice.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Which passage discusses neighborhoods with histories of displacement? Find that sentence.`,`What does the passage say preservation "is" for those communities? Look for the colon.`,`The correct choice uses the passage's own language about recognition.`],labels:{topic:`historic preservation`}}],v=[{id:`rc-c04-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`A P3: "we will need both"`,`B P3: "Mitigation first, at full speed, with removal developed as a backstop rather than a centerpiece"`],requiredInference:`Feasibility and desirability are shared; the dispute is sequence and emphasis.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Neither doubts capture is possible; both favor emissions cuts; the solar aside is undisputed; models' predictive power is never challenged.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The two passages disagree most centrally over`,choices:[{text:`whether carbon dioxide can be removed from the atmosphere by any means`},{text:`whether emissions reductions are desirable at all`},{text:`what priority direct air capture should receive relative to cutting emissions`},{text:`whether solar panels were expensive forty years ago`},{text:`whether climate models are capable of predicting future temperatures`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`A argues DAC must be developed aggressively now as an essential complement ("we will need both, and the longer we delay the second, the more expensive the first becomes"); B argues for "mitigation first, at full speed, with removal developed as a backstop rather than a centerpiece." The dispute is priority and sequence. Choice C states it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Both passages accept that DAC works technically and that some removal will eventually be needed, so the disagreement is not about feasibility or desirability in the abstract — it is about emphasis, sequence, and scale. A wants parallel aggressive development; B wants mitigation first with removal as backstop, warning that DAC-centered strategy creates moral hazard. Choice C captures this priority dispute. Choice A fails because neither disputes that CO2 can be captured (B calls it "an engineering marvel"). Choice B fails because both favor emissions cuts — B most emphatically. Choice D is an undisputed aside in A. Choice E is never discussed. The priority dispute is explicit in the passages' closing lines — A's "we will need both" with urgent parallel development against B's "mitigation first, at full speed" — making relative emphasis, not feasibility, the central fault line.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the technology is debated. It fails because feasibility is common ground: A describes operating machines, B calls DAC "an engineering marvel." Neither doubts capture is possible.`,`This is tempting as a climate-policy fault line. It fails because both passages favor cutting emissions — A says "every ton not emitted is cheaper," B demands "mitigation first, at full speed." Desirability is shared.`,`This is correct. The passages' dispute is explicitly about sequence and emphasis: A's "we will need both" with urgent parallel development versus B's "mitigation first... with removal developed as a backstop rather than a centerpiece."`,`This is tempting because A mentions it. It fails because it is an undisputed historical aside used as an analogy for cost declines — B never contests it, and it is not the subject of disagreement.`,`This is tempting because models are mentioned. It fails because neither passage disputes models' predictive capability; A cites models' reliance on removal, and B never challenges modeling.`],generalLesson:`When passages agree on ends but dispute means, the central disagreement is usually about priority or sequence — not about whether the goal matters.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Do the passages disagree about whether DAC works, or about something else? Check what B concedes.`,`A says "we will need both"; B says "mitigation first." What kind of disagreement is that?`,`The correct choice is about relative priority, not about feasibility.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}},{id:`rc-c04-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "this is not an argument that carbon removal research should stop"`,`B P3: "Mitigation first, at full speed"`],requiredInference:`B's structure is concession plus reordering: dispute over "sequence and scale," not existence.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B explicitly concedes eventual need; both prioritize cuts; B calls DAC "an engineering marvel"; both passages address DAC directly.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following best describes the relationship between the two passages?`,choices:[{text:`Passage B denies that carbon removal will ever be necessary and calls for abandoning all research into it.`},{text:`Passage A and Passage B agree that emissions reductions are unnecessary because DAC will solve the problem.`},{text:`Passage B is a point-by-point refutation proving that direct air capture violates the laws of physics.`},{text:`Passage A proposes an unrelated technology that has no bearing on Passage B's concerns.`},{text:`Passage B concedes some carbon removal will eventually be needed but disputes the priority and scale Passage A gives DAC.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`B's third paragraph explicitly concedes eventual need ("some removal capacity will eventually be needed") while arguing DAC should be "a backstop rather than a centerpiece" — disputing A's urgency and emphasis, not the technology's eventual role. Choice E describes this concessive challenge.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B's structure is concession plus reordering: paragraph 1 raises cost/energy objections, paragraph 2 raises the moral-hazard objection, and paragraph 3 concedes "this is not an argument that carbon removal research should stop" before insisting on "sequence and scale" — mitigation first, removal as backstop. This is not denial but demotion. Choice E captures it. Choice A contradicts B's explicit concession. Choice B contradicts both passages, which prioritize emissions cuts. Choice C is absurd: B calls DAC "an engineering marvel," never disputing physics. Choice D fails because both passages address DAC directly. The priority dispute is explicit in the passages' closing lines — A's "we will need both" with urgent parallel development against B's "mitigation first, at full speed." Framing the disagreement as relative emphasis rather than feasibility keeps every distractor that absolutizes either side into denial or endorsement from gaining traction.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because B is sharply critical. It fails because B explicitly says "this is not an argument that carbon removal research should stop" and grants eventual need — B disputes priority, not existence.`,`This is tempting as a techno-optimist reading. It fails because both passages insist emissions cuts come first — A calls un-emitted tons cheaper, B demands mitigation "at full speed." Neither treats DAC as a substitute.`,`This is tempting because B's critique is forceful. It fails because B calls DAC "an engineering marvel," accepting its physics; the objections are economic (cost, energy) and strategic (moral hazard), not physical.`,`This is tempting only if skimmed. It fails because both passages are about direct air capture and its role in climate strategy — the same technology, opposite emphases.`,`This is correct. It reflects B's actual structure: granting that "some removal capacity will eventually be needed" while contesting A's call for urgent, large-scale DAC development — a dispute over "sequence and scale." B's third paragraph is the linchpin: "this is not an argument that carbon removal research should stop" concedes the technology's eventual role, so the relationship is demotion rather than denial — a challenge to A's urgency and emphasis, not to DAC's possibility.`],generalLesson:`Concessive rebuttals ("not an argument that X should stop") define the relationship as dispute-over-emphasis. The right answer preserves the concession.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage B say DAC research should stop? Check its third paragraph carefully.`,`B's objection is framed as being about "sequence and scale." What does that tell you about the relationship?`,`The correct choice includes both B's concession and B's remaining objection.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}},{id:`rc-c04-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`A P3: "mitigation and removal are complements, not competitors"`,`A P3: hospital analogy`],requiredInference:`A denies the trade-off B's moral-hazard worry assumes: developing removal does not mean abandoning prevention.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`A concedes DAC "is expensive today"; A answers the worry rather than dismissing the concept; A keeps mitigation first on cost; A never discusses corporate behavior.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The author of Passage A would most likely respond to Passage B's moral-hazard argument by claiming that`,choices:[{text:`direct air capture is already cheaper than all emissions-reduction measures`},{text:`moral hazard is a concept from economics that has no application to climate policy`},{text:`climate strategy should abandon emissions reductions and rely entirely on carbon removal`},{text:`carbon removal and emissions cuts are complementary, and delaying removal only makes mitigation more expensive`},{text:`fossil fuel companies have never attempted to use carbon removal as a justification for continued emissions`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A's third paragraph preempts this: "mitigation and removal are complements, not competitors," and "refusing to develop carbon removal until emissions reach zero is like refusing to fund hospitals until disease is eradicated." A would deny the trade-off B assumes. Choice D states that reply.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's position contains a direct answer to the moral-hazard worry. A denies that removal and mitigation compete for the same dollar of effort: they are "complements, not competitors," and delaying removal raises mitigation's cost ("the longer we delay the second, the more expensive the first becomes"). The hospital analogy makes the point vivid: preparing the cleanup does not require abandoning prevention. Choice D combines these elements. Choice E is an empirical claim A never makes — A does not discuss fossil companies' behavior. Choice B contradicts A's engagement: A answers the substance of the worry rather than dismissing the concept. Choice A contradicts A, which admits DAC "is expensive today." Choice C reverses A: A says "every ton not emitted is cheaper than a ton captured later" — mitigation first in cost-effectiveness, removal as complement.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a pro-DAC claim. It fails because A explicitly concedes the opposite: DAC "is expensive today," and un-emitted tons are cheaper. Cost parity is not A's claim.`,`This is tempting as a dismissive move. It fails because A takes the underlying worry seriously enough to answer it substantively; A never quibbles with the concept of moral hazard itself.`,`This is tempting as an extreme pro-removal stance. It fails because it reverses A's stated view: A insists cutting emissions comes first on cost grounds and calls the two efforts complements — not substitutes.`,`This is correct. It restates A's third paragraph: removal and mitigation are complements, and the hospital analogy shows that developing the backstop does not mean abandoning prevention — delay only raises costs.`,`This is tempting because B mentions fossil companies embracing DAC. It fails because A never discusses corporate behavior or motives; A's reply is structural (complements, not competitors), not a denial about what companies have done.`],generalLesson:`"Complements, not competitors" is a standard reply to moral-hazard objections. The response denies the assumed trade-off rather than denying the objector's facts.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage A anticipate the worry that DAC distracts from mitigation? Look at its final paragraph.`,`A calls the two efforts "complements, not competitors" and uses a hospital analogy. What point does the analogy make?`,`The correct choice denies the trade-off B assumes, using A's own framing.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}},{id:`rc-c04-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{inferentialDistance:2,abstraction:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`A P2: net-zero models "rely on large-scale carbon removal in virtually every scenario"`,`B P3: "some removal capacity will eventually be needed"`],requiredInference:`B's explicit concession plus A's model reliance make eventual necessity common ground beneath the priority dispute.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Both say DAC is expensive now; B portrays fossil firms as seeking cover; A says most nations are "not on track"; both demand mitigation first.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Both passages assume that`,choices:[{text:`some carbon removal capacity will eventually be necessary`},{text:`direct air capture is currently cheaper than planting trees`},{text:`fossil fuel companies are acting in good faith on climate policy`},{text:`emissions pledges made by nations are being fully met`},{text:`renewable energy deployment should be halted until DAC is perfected`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`A says the models behind net-zero "rely on large-scale carbon removal in virtually every scenario"; B concedes "some removal capacity will eventually be needed" for "truly unavoidable emissions." Eventual necessity is common ground. Choice A states it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The shared assumption must be affirmed by both. A's case presupposes removal's eventual necessity (net-zero models rely on it); B explicitly grants it ("some removal capacity will eventually be needed"). They differ only on timing, scale, and priority. Choice A is the common ground. Choice B contradicts both: A says DAC "is expensive today," B cites "hundreds of dollars per ton." Choice C contradicts B, which portrays fossil companies as embracing DAC as "an excuse." Choice D contradicts A: "most are not on track." Choice E contradicts both passages' mitigation-first commitments. Agreement questions are answered by the common ground beneath the dispute. B's concession — "some removal capacity will eventually be needed" — is granted even by the critic, and A's net-zero models presuppose it; eventual necessity is shared while timing, scale, and sequencing are contested.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. A treats large-scale removal as required by "virtually every" net-zero scenario, and B grants that "some removal capacity will eventually be needed." Eventual necessity is the shared premise beneath their priority dispute.`,`This is tempting because costs are compared. It fails because both passages say the opposite: A admits DAC "is expensive today," and B prices it at "hundreds of dollars per ton" — neither claims it beats trees on cost now.`,`This is tempting because B discusses fossil companies. It fails because B suggests the opposite — that they embrace DAC as cover for continued emissions ("an excuse"). Neither passage assumes corporate good faith.`,`This is tempting as background. It fails because A explicitly says most nations "are not on track" to meet pledges. Neither passage assumes pledges are being met.`,`This is tempting as an extreme sequencing claim. It fails because both passages demand the reverse: A says un-emitted tons are cheaper, B demands renewables "at full speed." Halting renewables is no one's position. B's concession does the work here: "some removal capacity will eventually be needed" is granted even by the critic, and A's net-zero models presuppose it, so eventual necessity is the common ground beneath their dispute over timing and scale.`],generalLesson:`Explicit concessions ("some removal capacity will eventually be needed") are the clearest markers of shared assumptions. When one side grants the other's premise while disputing its implications, that premise is common ground.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`too-strong`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does Passage B explicitly concede in its final paragraph, even while criticizing DAC's priority?`,`Does Passage A also treat eventual removal as necessary? Check its second paragraph on net-zero models.`,`The shared assumption is the premise B grants while disputing A's conclusions.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}},{id:`rc-c04-q5`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-paragraph-roles`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`A P2: "Planting trees helps, but forests burn, and there is simply not enough land"`,`A P2: storage "can be measured and verified"`],whyDistractorsFail:`A says "planting trees helps"; A concedes DAC "is expensive today"; D contradicts "planting trees helps"; A never claims zero energy.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`According to Passage A, direct air capture is preferable to relying on tree planting for carbon removal because`,choices:[{text:`trees cannot absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere at all, a claim Passage A never comes close to making and explicitly contradicts`},{text:`DAC facilities are already cheaper to operate than forest conservation programs in every region studied`},{text:`forests can burn and there is too little land for trees to absorb residual emissions, while DAC storage can be measured and verified`},{text:`tree planting has been proven to increase atmospheric carbon dioxide, reversing its intended effect entirely`},{text:`DAC requires no energy input whatsoever, making its operating costs effectively zero`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`A's second paragraph gives three reasons: "forests burn," "there is simply not enough land," and DAC storage "can be measured and verified." Choice C combines them.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's comparison with tree planting is explicit in paragraph 2: "Planting trees helps, but forests burn, and there is simply not enough land to absorb the residual emissions of a modern economy," followed by DAC's advantages — little land, measurable and verifiable storage. Choice C restates all three points. Choice A contradicts A ("planting trees helps"). Choice B contradicts A's admission that DAC "is expensive today." Choice D is invented and contradicts "planting trees helps." Choice E contradicts B's (undisputed) point that DAC consumes "enormous amounts of energy" — A never claims zero energy input. The tested detail is A's enumerated comparison from the second paragraph: forests burn, available land is insufficient, and DAC storage is measurable and verifiable. The correct choice collects that enumeration; the distractors absolutize it into claims about inability, cost superiority, or zero energy that neither passage actually makes.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a pro-DAC extreme. It fails because A explicitly says "planting trees helps" — trees do absorb CO2; A's point is about limits (fire, land), not inability.`,`This is tempting because costs are debated. It fails because A concedes DAC "is expensive today" and never claims it undercuts forest conservation on cost. The comparison A makes is about land, permanence, and verifiability.`,`This is correct. It combines A's three stated points: forests burn (impermanence), insufficient land for the scale needed, and DAC's advantage of measurable, verifiable underground storage.`,`This is tempting as an anti-tree extreme. It fails because it contradicts A's explicit "planting trees helps" and is scientifically absurd — the passage never suggests trees add CO2.`,`This is tempting because energy is discussed. It fails because A never claims zero energy use; indeed B's point about "enormous amounts of energy" goes uncontested by A. The choice invents a claim. A's comparison is an enumerated one — forests burn, land is insufficient, DAC storage is measurable and verifiable — and the detail answer simply collects that enumeration, while the distractors absolutize it into claims about inability, cost superiority, or zero energy that neither passage makes.`],generalLesson:`Comparative advantages in a passage are usually enumerated ("forests burn... not enough land... measured and verified"). The detail answer collects the enumeration; distractors absolutize it.`,misconceptionTags:[`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage A compares DAC to tree planting in its second paragraph. What three points does it make?`,`Note the concession "planting trees helps" — what limitations follow it?`,`The correct choice combines the limitations of trees with DAC's verifiability advantage.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}},{id:`rc-c04-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c04`,difficultyProfile:{inferentialDistance:3,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "confused the cleanup crew with the fire department"`,`B P3: "Prevention first, cleanup second"`],requiredInference:`Fire department = urgent emissions cuts; cleanup crew = later-stage removal; confusing them reverses proper priority.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage B`,whyDistractorsFail:`B grants eventual need; literalizing the analogy misses the point; the hierarchy runs urgent-primary first; B demands mitigation "at full speed".`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Passage B's comparison of a DAC-centered strategy to confusing "the cleanup crew with the fire department" most strongly suggests that B believes`,choices:[{text:`carbon removal technologies will never work under any circumstances`},{text:`fire departments should be responsible for climate policy`},{text:`leading with carbon removal mistakes a secondary later task for the urgent primary one of stopping emissions`},{text:`cleanup crews are generally more effective than fire departments at handling emergencies`},{text:`climate change is no longer an emergency requiring urgent action`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`The analogy caps B's sequence argument: mitigation (stopping emissions) is the fire department — the urgent primary response — while DAC is the cleanup crew, a secondary later task. Leading with DAC reverses the proper order. Choice C states this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The analogy's structure maps fire department → stopping the fire (cutting emissions, the urgent primary task) and cleanup crew → dealing with aftermath (carbon removal, secondary and later). To "confuse" them is to give the secondary task the primary's priority. Choice C decodes this. Choice A contradicts B's concession that some removal will eventually be needed. Choice B literalizes the analogy absurdly. Choice D reverses the analogy's hierarchy — the fire department is the urgent responder, not the less effective one. Choice E contradicts B's demand for mitigation "at full speed," which presupposes urgency. Decoding the analogy means mapping its roles onto the argument: the fire department is urgent emissions cuts, the cleanup crew is later-stage removal, and "confusing" their order is B's charge that a DAC-led strategy reverses the proper sequence. Choices that deny removal any role misread the analogy's second half.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because B is critical of DAC. It fails because B explicitly grants eventual need for removal ("some removal capacity will eventually be needed") — the objection is to priority, not to the technology's possibility.`,`This is tempting only if the analogy is literalized. It fails because the fire department is a figure for urgent emissions cuts, not a policy proposal about actual fire departments.`,`This is correct. The analogy encodes B's sequence thesis: stopping emissions is the urgent primary task (fire department); carbon removal is the secondary aftermath task (cleanup crew); a DAC-led strategy reverses that order.`,`This is tempting as a reading of the comparison. It fails because it inverts the analogy's evaluative direction — the fire department represents the more urgent, primary response, not the less effective one.`,`This is tempting as a complacent reading. It fails because B's entire passage demands urgent mitigation ("at full speed") — the analogy presupposes an ongoing emergency, it does not deny one. Decoding the analogy means mapping its roles: the fire department is urgent emissions cuts, the cleanup crew is later-stage removal, and "confusing" them is B's charge that a DAC-led strategy reverses the proper order of operations.`],generalLesson:`Analogies map roles: identify what each figure stands for, then read the evaluative point. Never literalize the figures themselves.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`too-strong`,`scope-shift`,`opposite`,`unsupported`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`In the analogy, what does the fire department correspond to, and what does the cleanup crew correspond to?`,`Which one handles the urgent emergency, and which handles the aftermath?`,`The correct choice describes a reversal of proper priority.`],labels:{topic:`direct air capture`}}],y=[{id:`rc-c05-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "The disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals"`],requiredInference:`Trust B's explicit characterization and verify the choice matches it.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Learnability is common ground; both aim at academic proficiency; A never takes a position on transfer; teacher nativeness is never mentioned.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The two passages differ most centrally in their views on`,choices:[{text:`whether children are capable of learning a second language at school`},{text:`whether bilingual education should aim at academic proficiency or conversational fluency`},{text:`whether literacy skills developed in one language can transfer to another`},{text:`how to sequence instruction: maximum target-language exposure first versus home-language literacy first`},{text:`whether teachers should be native speakers of the target language`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A prescribes immersion — "taught entirely in the target language from the first day" — while B prescribes building "academic proficiency in the home language while systematically adding the second." B explicitly frames it: "The disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals." Choice D states this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B itself names the disagreement: "The disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals." A's method is maximum early exposure (the "linguistic deep end"); B's is foundation-first (literacy transfers across languages). Choice D captures the sequencing dispute. Choice A fails because both assume children can learn — that is common ground. Choice B fails because both aim at academic proficiency (A cites subject-matter parity; B cites academic skills). Choice C fails because only B discusses transfer; A never denies it — A's claim is about exposure, and the transfer question is B's argument, not a shared debate topic. Choice E is never discussed by either passage. B's explicit characterization controls the answer: "the disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals" rules out the goal-based and capacity-based distractors, leaving the contrast between immersion from day one and foundation-first as the central difference.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a foundational question. It fails because learnability is common ground: A's immersion and B's developmental programs both presuppose children can become bilingual. Neither passage questions the capacity itself.`,`This is tempting because goals matter. It fails because both passages aim at academic proficiency — A stresses students match peers in subject-matter learning, B stresses academic skills that transfer. The goal is shared.`,`This is tempting because transfer is B's key mechanism. It fails as the central difference because A never takes a position on transfer; A's argument is about exposure intensity. The passages differ on sequencing, within which transfer is B's supporting reason.`,`This is correct. It restates B's own characterization: "The disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals" — immersion from day one (A) versus home-language foundation first (B).`,`This is tempting as a practical concern. It fails because teacher nativeness is never mentioned in either passage; the debate is about instructional sequencing, not staffing.`],generalLesson:`When a passage explicitly names the disagreement ("about sequencing, not about goals"), trust it — but verify the choice matches that characterization rather than a nearby subsidiary dispute.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage B contains a sentence explicitly stating what the disagreement is about. Find it.`,`Does either passage doubt that children can learn, or disagree about the ultimate goal? If not, eliminate those.`,`The correct choice contrasts "maximum exposure from the start" with "home-language foundation first."`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}},{id:`rc-c05-q2`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2,3],supportingSentences:[`A P2: students "match or exceed their peers ... while acquiring dramatically stronger second-language skills"`,`B P3: "emphatically bilingual, not monolingual"`],requiredInference:`Each passage's success description converges on genuine two-language proficiency.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A says the lag is "fully closed"; A disparages 45-minute classes; B champions home-language literacy; B calls immersion "impatience masquerading as pedagogy".`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The authors of the two passages would agree that`,choices:[{text:`the early lag immersion students show in their first language is permanent and irreversible, persisting even after years of continued immersion`},{text:`forty-five-minute daily language classes are sufficient for full bilingualism in most students, making longer programs unnecessary`},{text:`home-language instruction is harmful to second-language acquisition in all contexts, without exception`},{text:`effective bilingual education should develop genuine proficiency in two languages, not monolingual instruction in disguise`},{text:`immersion programs should be adopted in all schools immediately without further study or pilot programs`}],correctIndex:3,explanationQuick:`A aims at students "match[ing] or exceed[ing] their peers in subject-matter learning while acquiring dramatically stronger second-language skills"; B insists its programs are "emphatically bilingual, not monolingual" and criticizes outcomes that are "semi-proficient in one and shaky in the other." Both demand real two-language proficiency. Choice D states the agreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`Agreement must be a proposition both endorse. A's goal is full bilingualism (strong second language plus grade-level first language); B's goal is students "literate in two languages" and explicitly rejects "semi-proficient in one and shaky in the other." Both therefore demand genuine two-language proficiency. Choice D captures this. Choice E is A's enthusiasm, not B's — B criticizes immersion's sequencing. Choice C contradicts B, which champions home-language literacy. Choice B contradicts A, which says such classes would take "nearly a decade" to match immersion exposure. Choice A contradicts A, which says the lag is "fully closed" by late elementary (B disputes this, so it cannot be agreement). The shared goal is visible in how each passage describes success: A's students reaching grade level with strong second-language skills, B's students "literate in two languages." Both demand genuine two-language proficiency — the sequencing of how to get there is precisely what they dispute.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the lag is debated. It fails because A asserts the lag is temporary ("fully closed"), so permanence cannot be common ground — it is precisely what they dispute. The shared goal is visible in how each passage describes success: A's students with strong second-language skills at grade level, B's students "literate in two languages" — both demanding genuine two-language proficiency rather than the semi-proficiency both reject.`,`This is tempting because class length is mentioned. It fails because A explicitly disparages the forty-five-minute class as inadequate (a decade to match immersion exposure), and B never endorses it either.`,`This is tempting as an immersion-adjacent claim. It fails because B's entire argument is that home-language literacy accelerates second-language acquisition — the opposite of this claim.`,`This is correct. Both passages aim at genuine bilingual proficiency: A wants strong second-language skills alongside grade-level first-language performance; B wants literacy in two languages and explicitly rejects semi-proficiency outcomes.`,`This is tempting because A is enthusiastic about immersion. It fails as agreement: B argues immersion's sequencing is wrong ("impatience masquerading as pedagogy"), so B would not endorse universal immediate adoption.`],generalLesson:`Agreement answers often describe the shared goal both methods serve. When passages dispute methods, check what goal both methods are presented as serving.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What goal does each passage set for bilingual education? Compare A's outcome description with B's "literate in two languages."`,`B says its programs are "emphatically bilingual, not monolingual." What does that rule out as a shared aim?`,`Eliminate choices that state only one passage's position.`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}},{id:`rc-c05-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`B P2: "acquire second-language academic skills faster, not slower"`,`B P2: the early lag is "not temporary at all" for many`],requiredInference:`B reframes home-language time as investment: transfer makes it pay off in faster second-language growth.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage B`,whyDistractorsFail:`B values "rich second-language exposure"; B never denies immersion students learn; teacher quality is never discussed; D states A's position.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The author of Passage B would most likely respond to Passage A's "time on task" argument by claiming that`,choices:[{text:`exposure time is irrelevant to language learning under all circumstances, a claim no researcher in the debate accepts`},{text:`immersion students never achieve strong second-language skills, regardless of program quality or duration`},{text:`teachers in immersion programs are generally unqualified, which explains the mixed results better than any theory`},{text:`maximum exposure is the only factor that determines language-learning success, rendering all other variables moot`},{text:`strong first-language literacy accelerates second-language learning, so early home-language support is an investment, not a delay`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`B's counter is the transfer research: "Students with strong first-language literacy acquire second-language academic skills faster, not slower," and the early lag is "not temporary at all" for many — so foundation-building speeds rather than slows acquisition. Choice E states this reply.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's "time on task" argument says more target-language hours equal faster learning, making home-language time look like lost time. B's reply reframes that time as investment: because literacy transfers, hours spent building first-language literacy pay off in faster second-language academic growth — "faster, not slower." B adds that what A calls a temporary lag is, for many students pulled from support too early, a permanent detour into remedial tracks. Choice E captures the investment reframing. Choice A is too strong: B says programs should be "emphatically bilingual" with "rich second-language exposure" — exposure matters, sequencing is the issue. Choice B contradicts B's acknowledgment that immersion students do acquire language; B disputes the cost, not the outcome. Choice C is unsupported; teacher quality is never discussed. Choice D states A's position, not B's reply.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a strong counter to "time on task." It fails because B explicitly values "rich second-language exposure" — B disputes the sequencing of exposure, not its relevance. "Irrelevant under all circumstances" goes beyond B.`,`This is tempting because B criticizes immersion. It fails because B never denies immersion students learn the second language; B's critique is that many pay too high a price (remedial tracks, shaky literacy), not that acquisition never happens.`,`This is tempting as an institutional critique. It fails because teacher qualifications are never mentioned in either passage; B's argument is about sequencing and transfer, not staffing.`,`This is tempting because it sounds like the exposure argument. It fails as B's reply because it is A's position — B's whole point is that transferable literacy, not raw exposure hours, drives academic second-language success.`,`This is correct. It restates B's transfer-based reply: strong first-language literacy makes second-language acquisition "faster, not slower," so home-language support is an investment that pays off rather than time stolen from exposure.`],generalLesson:`Replies that reframe ("investment rather than delay") accept the opponent's value (learning speed) while reclassifying the disputed activity. Match the reply to the reframing, not to a denial.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is B's key mechanism — the finding about students with strong first-language literacy? What does it imply about time spent on the home language?`,`B says such students acquire second-language skills "faster, not slower." How does that answer the "time on task" point?`,`The correct choice reframes home-language time as an investment, not a delay.`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}},{id:`rc-c05-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "impatience masquerading as pedagogy"`,`B P3: "emphatically bilingual, not monolingual"`],requiredInference:`B is adversarial on method, convergent on ends: dispute sequencing, share the bilingual goal.`,whyDistractorsFail:`The alignment is shared goals/disputed methods, not reversed; no age-group difference exists; A never addresses transfer research; B champions bilingual programs.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following best describes the relationship between the two passages?`,choices:[{text:`Passage B disputes Passage A's sequencing while sharing its goal of genuine bilingual proficiency.`},{text:`Passage B agrees with Passage A's methods but disputes its stated goals.`},{text:`The two passages discuss entirely different age groups and are therefore incomparable.`},{text:`Passage A refutes Passage B's claims about literacy transfer with contradictory research.`},{text:`Passage B argues that bilingual education itself is unattainable and should be abandoned.`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B states the disagreement is "about sequencing, not about goals": it rejects immersion-first ordering (calling it "impatience masquerading as pedagogy") while insisting its own programs are "emphatically bilingual." Choice A describes this challenge-within-shared-goals relationship.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B's relationship to A is adversarial on method, convergent on ends. B attacks A's sequencing directly — the deep-end approach, the dismissal of early lag, the "half-measures" doctrine — while explicitly sharing the bilingual goal ("emphatically bilingual, not monolingual"; "literate in two languages"). Choice A captures both halves. Choice B reverses it: they dispute methods, not goals. Choice C is invented; both discuss schoolchildren generally. Choice D reverses the direction: A never addresses transfer research — A's argument is about exposure, and no refutation of B occurs. Choice E contradicts B's advocacy of developmental bilingual programs. B's self-description again controls the answer: the closing lines frame the dispute as sequencing versus goals, with "impatience masquerading as pedagogy" rejecting A's ordering while "emphatically bilingual" affirms the shared destination. Any choice denying the shared goal misreads B's explicit framing.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It matches B's self-description: the fight is over sequencing (immersion-first vs. foundation-first) while both passages aim at genuine proficiency in two languages.`,`This is tempting as a symmetric disagreement. It fails because it reverses the actual alignment: the passages share goals (bilingual proficiency) and dispute methods (sequencing) — not the reverse.`,`This is tempting as a way to dissolve the comparison. It fails because both passages address school-age children learning a second language at school; no age-group difference is stated or implied.`,`This is tempting because research is cited on both sides. It fails on direction: Passage A never mentions, let alone refutes, transfer research — A's case is built on exposure studies, and the passages do not engage each other's citations.`,`This is tempting as an extreme anti-bilingual reading. It fails because B champions developmental bilingual programs as the better path to bilingualism — B disputes A's method, not the enterprise. B's self-description is again decisive: the closing lines frame the dispute as sequencing versus goals, with "impatience masquerading as pedagogy" rejecting A's ordering while "emphatically bilingual" affirms the shared destination.`],generalLesson:`Method disputes within shared goals are a common comparative structure. The relationship answer must name both the shared goal and the disputed method.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-passage-structure`],estimatedSeconds:95,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does B share A's goal? Check B's "emphatically bilingual, not monolingual."`,`Does B accept A's method? Check B's closing line about "impatience masquerading as pedagogy."`,`The correct choice pairs shared goals with disputed sequencing.`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}},{id:`rc-c05-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:1,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`A P2: "follow-up studies consistently show immersion students performing at grade level in their native language by late elementary school"`],whyDistractorsFail:`The passage reports catch-up, not fade-out; A's main finding is stronger second-language skills; no math/science split is drawn; the lag is "fully closed," not permanent.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: walkthrough cited "paragraph 4" but Passage A has 3 paragraphs; corrected to the second paragraph. No answer/prose changes.`}],stem:`According to Passage A, follow-up studies of immersion students show that`,choices:[{text:`the benefits of immersion disappear entirely after elementary school`},{text:`immersion has no measurable effect on second-language skills`},{text:`immersion students permanently lose proficiency in their native language`},{text:`immersion students consistently outperform their peers in mathematics but not in science`},{text:`immersion students perform at grade level in their native language by late elementary school`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A's second paragraph states: "follow-up studies consistently show immersion students performing at grade level in their native language by late elementary school, with the temporary lag of the early years fully closed." Choice E restates this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`This addresses A's reply to the first-language-harm objection. The passage is explicit: early lag, then full catch-up by late elementary. Choice E paraphrases it. Choice C states the opposite of "fully closed." Choice B contradicts A's "dramatically stronger second-language skills." Choice D invents a math/science split never mentioned. Choice A contradicts the catch-up finding — benefits persisting is the point of "fully closed" lag. The question tests A's objection-and-reply structure in the second paragraph: the first-language-harm concern is raised only to be answered by follow-up studies showing the lag is temporary and fully closed by late elementary. The correct choice paraphrases that reply. The distractors state the objection's opposite, invent a subject-matter split the passage never mentions, or project a fade-out of benefits that contradicts the catch-up finding. Reading the full reply before choosing is the essential habit, since the objection stated alone points toward the wrong answer.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a fade-out worry. It fails because the passage reports catch-up, not fade-out; nothing suggests benefits disappear after elementary school. The question tests A's objection-and-reply structure: the first-language-harm concern is raised only to be answered by follow-up studies showing temporary lag fully closed, so the correct choice paraphrases that reply while the distractors state its opposite, invent subject splits, or project a fade-out the passage never mentions. Reading the full reply before choosing is the essential habit, since the objection alone points toward the wrong answer. The passage's trajectory runs from temporary lag to full closure — the reverse of disappearing benefits.`,`This is tempting as a skeptical counter. It fails because A's central finding is "dramatically stronger second-language skills" — the effect on the target language is the program's main documented success.`,`This is tempting because early lag is acknowledged. It fails on "permanently": A explicitly says the lag is "temporary" and "fully closed" by late elementary. Permanence is the opposite of A's claim.`,`This is tempting because subject-matter learning is discussed. It fails because A says students "match or exceed their peers in subject-matter learning" generally — no math/science split is ever drawn.`,`This is correct. It restates A's second paragraph: follow-up studies show grade-level native-language performance by late elementary, with the early lag fully closed. This directly answers the objection that immersion harms first-language development, which A says has not survived scrutiny.`],generalLesson:`Objection-and-reply structures ("concerns... have not survived scrutiny") contain the detail answer in the reply. Read the full reply before choosing.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`],estimatedSeconds:70,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage A addresses the worry that immersion harms first-language development. What do the follow-up studies show?`,`Look for the words "temporary lag" — what happens to it, and by when?`,`Eliminate any choice claiming the harm is permanent or the benefits vanish.`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}},{id:`rc-c05-q6`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-inference`,secondarySkills:[`rc-author-viewpoint`],editorialDifficulty:3,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c05`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:3,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`A P3: diluting the model yields "correspondingly diluted results"`,`A P3: "half-measures produce half-speakers"`],requiredInference:`Extend A's dose-response logic: weaker immersion -> weaker outcomes.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`A says design intensity determines outcomes; class size is never mentioned; A reports immersion students match peers in subjects; E states B's transfer thesis.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Which one of the following is most strongly suggested by Passage A?`,choices:[{text:`All bilingual programs, regardless of design, produce identical outcomes.`},{text:`Parents should choose schools based solely on class size.`},{text:`Weaker bilingual outcomes when immersion programs dilute intensive target-language exposure.`},{text:`Children cannot learn academic subjects in a language they are still acquiring.`},{text:`Home-language literacy is the primary driver of second-language success.`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`A's third paragraph insists programs that "dilute the model" see "correspondingly diluted results" and that "half-measures produce half-speakers." The inference that diluted immersion weakens outcomes follows directly. Choice C states it.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's "commitment" paragraph makes intensity a dose-response claim: diluting the model (native-language blocks, translating difficult material) yields "correspondingly diluted results." Choice C is a direct inference from that dose-response logic. Choice A contradicts the dose-response claim — design matters enormously to A. Choice B is irrelevant; class size is never mentioned. Choice D contradicts A: immersion students are taught math and science in the target language "from the first day" and match peers. Choice E states B's thesis, not A's — a viewpoint reversal. A's third paragraph makes intensity dose-dependent: diluting the immersion model yields "correspondingly diluted results," so the inference extends that logic to weakened programs generally. The viewpoint-reversal distractor is the chief trap — transfer-driven success is B's mechanism, and assigning it to A inverts the pair's entire structure.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a neutral summary. It fails because A's whole third paragraph argues design intensity determines outcomes — "half-measures produce half-speakers" directly contradicts identical outcomes.`,`This is tempting as practical advice. It fails because class size is never mentioned in either passage; the choice imports an entirely foreign consideration. A choice addressing an unmentioned factor cannot be supported, however sensible it sounds outside the passage.`,`This is correct. It follows from A's dose-response claim: diluting intensive exposure produces "correspondingly diluted results" — weaker bilingual outcomes from weaker immersion. This is the natural extension of A's insistence that only full commitment produces full bilingualism.`,`This is tempting because learning subjects in a new language sounds hard. It fails because A reports the opposite as fact: immersion students "match or exceed their peers in subject-matter learning" while being taught in the target language.`,`This is tempting because it is true in the pair. It fails on attribution: home-language literacy as the primary driver is Passage B's thesis (transfer), not A's — A's driver is exposure intensity. A's third paragraph makes intensity dose-dependent — diluting the model yields "correspondingly diluted results" — so the inference extends that logic to weakened programs generally. The viewpoint-reversal distractor is the chief trap: transfer-driven success is B's mechanism, and assigning it to A inverts the pair's entire structure.`],generalLesson:`Dose-response claims ("dilute X, get diluted Y") support inferences about what happens when the dose changes. Keep each passage's causal mechanism straight when the pair offers rival mechanisms.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-inference`,`rc-author-viewpoint`],estimatedSeconds:90,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What does Passage A say happens to programs that "dilute the model"?`,`"Half-measures produce half-speakers" — what does that imply about weakened immersion?`,`Eliminate choices stating B's thesis or contradicting A's reported results.`],labels:{topic:`bilingual education`}}],b=[{id:`rc-c06-q1`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{inferentialDistance:2,abstraction:3,distractorProximity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "Both sides of this dispute, notably, accept the same starting premise: that copyright law ... is the proper framework"`],requiredInference:`B names the shared premise explicitly; verify A likewise operates within fair-use doctrine.`,whyDistractorsFail:`Lawfulness is the disputed conclusion; both affirm some author rights; B explicitly rejects inventing a new regime; per-work compensation is contested.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`Both passages assume that`,choices:[{text:`AI training on copyrighted works is clearly lawful under current doctrine`},{text:`authors should have no rights whatsoever over works used in AI training`},{text:`a new legal regime unrelated to copyright must be invented for AI`},{text:`licensing every work in a training corpus would meaningfully compensate individual authors`},{text:`copyright law, with its machinery of rights, exceptions, and remedies, is the proper framework for the dispute`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`B states this explicitly: "Both sides of this dispute, notably, accept the same starting premise: that copyright law — with its familiar machinery of exclusive rights, exceptions, and remedies — is the proper framework." A likewise argues entirely within fair-use doctrine. Choice E states the shared assumption.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The shared assumption is stated outright in B's final paragraph, which notes that "no one here proposes scrapping copyright for AI or inventing an entirely new regime." A's whole argument operates within fair-use doctrine (transformativeness, market substitution), and B's within licensing and permission — both inside copyright's framework. Choice E captures this. Choice A is A's conclusion, which B disputes — not shared. Choice B contradicts both: A preserves infringement remedies for outputs, B demands compensation. Choice C is explicitly denied by B ("no one here proposes... inventing an entirely new regime"). Choice D is A's disputed claim ("per-work value... approaches zero"), which B contests via the music-royalty analogy. Shared-assumption questions in adversarial pairs reward finding the common framework beneath opposed conclusions. B's explicit statement — both sides work "within" copyright law — does half the work; verifying A's transformativeness and market-substitution arguments as pure fair-use doctrine completes it.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because A argues it forcefully. It fails as shared: B's entire passage argues training requires permission — lawfulness is the disputed conclusion, not a shared premise.`,`This is tempting as an extreme pro-AI position. It fails because neither passage holds it: A preserves output-infringement liability, and B demands creator compensation. Both affirm author rights of some kind.`,`This is tempting because AI is novel. It fails because B explicitly says "no one here proposes scrapping copyright for AI or inventing an entirely new regime" — both sides work within existing copyright.`,`This is tempting because compensation is debated. It fails because it is contested, not shared: A says per-work value "approaches zero," while B argues aggregation makes payments meaningful (the music analogy).`,`This is correct. B names it as the shared "starting premise," and A's fair-use argument likewise operates entirely within copyright doctrine — neither side steps outside the framework. B's explicit identification of the shared premise does half the work; the other half is verifying A's compliance, which is straightforward since A's transformativeness and market-substitution arguments are pure fair-use doctrine operating inside copyright's existing machinery.`],generalLesson:`When a passage explicitly identifies a shared premise ("both sides... accept the same starting premise"), the assumption question is answered — but verify the other passage genuinely operates within it.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`opposite`,`unsupported`,`half-right`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Passage B's final paragraph explicitly names something both sides accept. What is it?`,`Does Passage A also work within that framework? Check whether A ever appeals outside copyright doctrine.`,`Eliminate choices stating conclusions the passages dispute rather than premises they share.`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}},{id:`rc-c06-q2`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-main-point`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[3],supportingSentences:[`B P3: "whether mass ingestion for model training is the kind of transformative learning fair use was meant to shelter, or the kind of uncompensated commercial exploitation it was meant to prevent"`],requiredInference:`Use B's own summary of "the fight" as the guide to the central disagreement.`,whyDistractorsFail:`A concedes outputs can infringe; both accept human learning; both assume copyright continues; training volume is shared background.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: choice explanation carried wrong letter labels "(D) versus (E)"; removed the labels. No answer/prose changes.`}],stem:`The two passages disagree most centrally over whether`,choices:[{text:`an AI model's outputs can ever infringe copyright`},{text:`human authors learn from the works they read`},{text:`copyright law should continue to exist in any form`},{text:`AI models were trained on large quantities of copyrighted text and images`},{text:`training AI models on copyrighted works without permission is fair use or needs licensing`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A argues training is "a textbook case of fair use" (transformative, non-substitutive); B argues it is "uncompensated commercial exploitation" requiring permission/licensing. B itself frames the fight: "whether mass ingestion for model training is the kind of transformative learning fair use was meant to shelter, or the kind of uncompensated commercial exploitation it was meant to prevent." Choice E states the disagreement.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The central disagreement is the legal characterization of training ingestion. A's answer: transformative fair use, like a student learning. B's answer: commercial exploitation requiring licenses, like any industrial use of creative labor. Choice E states this precisely, echoing B's own framing. Choice D is shared background ("every major model was trained on vast quantities"). Choice C fails because both accept copyright's framework (q1). Choice A fails because A explicitly concedes outputs reproducing works "can infringe" — no dispute. Choice B is an undisputed analogy premise, not a point of contention. The central disagreement must be the point of direct opposition, and B's own summary of "the fight" is the most reliable guide: transformative learning sheltered by fair use versus uncompensated commercial exploitation requiring permission. Choices describing shared background, conceded points, or undisputed premises fail because agreement cannot be the disagreement.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a legal subtlety. It fails because A concedes it: outputs reproducing "substantial portions of a specific work... can infringe just as a human-authored copy would." Agreement, not dispute.`,`This is tempting because the library analogy is central. It fails because both sides accept that humans learn from reading — they dispute whether machine ingestion is relevantly similar, not whether human learning occurs. B's own summary of "the fight" is the most reliable guide: transformative learning sheltered by fair use versus uncompensated commercial exploitation requiring permission. Choices describing shared background or conceded points fail because agreement cannot be the central disagreement.`,`This is tempting because copyright's future is at stake. It fails because both passages assume copyright continues — they dispute its application, not its existence.`,`This is tempting as background. It fails because both passages accept it as fact — A opens with it, B's argument presupposes it. Shared facts cannot be the central disagreement.`,`This is correct. It restates B's own summary of "the fight": transformative learning sheltered by fair use versus uncompensated commercial exploitation requiring permission — the precise legal characterization on which the two passages take opposite sides.`],generalLesson:`The central disagreement is the question the passages answer oppositely. Concessions and shared background (training happened, outputs can infringe) are distractors — eliminate them first.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`half-right`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`,`keyword-match`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`How does Passage B itself summarize "the fight" in its final paragraph? Use that framing.`,`Eliminate anything both passages explicitly agree on — training occurred, copyright continues, outputs can infringe.`,`The disagreement is the legal characterization of the training ingestion itself.`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}},{id:`rc-c06-q3`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`,`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1],supportingSentences:[`B P1: "a student reading a book does not produce a commercial product that competes with the book's author"`,`B P1: "the scale and purpose of the use are fundamentally different"`],requiredInference:`B's rebuttal targets the analogy's mapping (scale, purpose), not its vehicle.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage B`,whyDistractorsFail:`B never proposes licensing readers; the objection is to the comparison's logic; D concedes A's framing; B works within fair-use doctrine.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The author of Passage B would most likely respond to Passage A's "student with a library card" analogy by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`the analogy fails because commercial model training differs in scale and purpose from personal study`},{text:`students should also be required to obtain licenses before reading library books`},{text:`library cards are obsolete and no longer provide a useful comparison for anything`},{text:`AI models are actually more transformative than human students are`},{text:`fair use has never protected any form of learning from copyrighted works`}],correctIndex:0,explanationQuick:`B's first paragraph directly attacks the analogy: "a student reading a book does not produce a commercial product that competes with the book's author," and "the scale and purpose of the use are fundamentally different." Choice A restates that rebuttal.`,explanationWalkthrough:`B anticipates and rebuts A's analogy explicitly, calling it "a sleight of hand." The rebuttal has two prongs: scale (millions of works ingested) and purpose (building "a commercial product that competes with the book's author" versus personal reading). Choice A captures both prongs. Choice B is absurd and unsupported — B never suggests licensing readers. Choice C is irrelevant; the analogy's vehicle (library cards) is not the issue, the mapping is. Choice D states A's position, not B's reply. Choice E contradicts copyright basics both passages assume — fair use has long sheltered study, quotation, and commentary; B disputes this application, not the doctrine's existence. B's rebuttal targets the analogy's mapping rather than its vehicle: scale (millions of works ingested) and purpose (building a competing commercial product) are the alleged disanalogies. The correct choice names those differences instead of proposing student licensing or ranking transformativeness, which the passage never does.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is correct. It restates B's two-pronged rebuttal: the student produces no competing commercial product, and "the scale and purpose of the use are fundamentally different" — the analogy is a "sleight of hand."`,`This is tempting as a reductio of B's licensing demand. It fails because B never proposes licensing readers; B's licensing regime targets commercial model training, and the passage contains no such suggestion about students.`,`This is tempting as a dismissal of the analogy. It fails because B's objection is to the comparison's logic (scale and purpose), not to library cards as such — attacking the vehicle misses B's actual argument.`,`This is tempting because transformativeness is debated. It fails as B's reply because it concedes A's framing and merely outbids it; B's actual move is to reject the comparison as categorically inapt, not to rank transformativeness.`,`This is tempting as a strong anti-fair-use stance. It fails because B works within fair-use doctrine (arguing this use falls outside it), not against the doctrine — B never claims fair use never protects learning. B's rebuttal targets the analogy's mapping rather than its vehicle: scale (millions of works) and purpose (a competing commercial product) are the disanalogies alleged, which is why the correct choice names those differences instead of proposing student licensing or ranking transformativeness.`],generalLesson:`Rebuttals of analogies attack the mapping (scale, purpose), not the vehicle. The right answer names the disanalogy the author actually alleges.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`scope-shift`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage B address the library-card analogy? Look at its opening sentences.`,`B calls the analogy a "sleight of hand." What two differences does B cite — think scale and purpose?`,`The correct choice names the disanalogy without adding proposals B never makes.`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}},{id:`rc-c06-q4`,version:1,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-comparative`,secondarySkills:[`rc-inference`,`rc-other-viewpoints`],editorialDifficulty:4,itemPurpose:`mixed-discrimination`,validationStatus:`validated`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:3,inferentialDistance:4,distractorProximity:4},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`A P2: licensing "would not compensate authors meaningfully"`,`A P2: it "would entrench the largest technology companies"`],requiredInference:`A's reply has two prongs: futility (per-work value ~zero) and perversity (entrenchment).`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`A states B's music analogy, not its own; A preserves output liability; A defends fair use within copyright; A's concern is market structure, not affordability.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`Hostile review: one defensible answer confirmed against the passage text; distractors fail for nameable reasons; difficulty honest; no leakage cues.`}],stem:`The author of Passage A would most likely respond to Passage B's proposal for collective licensing of training data by arguing that`,choices:[{text:`collective licensing works well for music and therefore must work for AI training data`},{text:`authors whose works are used in training deserve no compensation under any circumstances`},{text:`licensing billions of works would yield negligible per-work payments, entrenching only the largest companies`},{text:`copyright law should be abolished so that no licensing is ever required`},{text:`AI companies are too poor to afford any licensing fees`}],correctIndex:2,explanationQuick:`A's second paragraph preempts this: licensing billions of works "would not compensate authors meaningfully — the per-work value of training data approaches zero — but it would entrench the largest technology companies, the only ones able to negotiate such licenses at scale." Choice C restates that reply.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's second paragraph is a direct answer to the licensing proposal, with two prongs: futility (per-work value "approaches zero," so payments would not be meaningful) and perversity (only the largest companies could negotiate at scale, so licensing entrenches incumbents). Choice C combines both. Choice A states B's music analogy, not A's reply. Choice B is too strong: A preserves output-infringement liability and never says authors deserve nothing "under any circumstances." Choice D contradicts A, which works within copyright and defends fair use as part of it. Choice E is invented and contrary to A's framing — A's concern is market structure (entrenchment), not corporate poverty. A's second paragraph is a preemptive rebuttal containing both prongs the correct answer needs: negligible per-work value making payments meaningless, and billion-work scale entrenching only the largest firms. Distractors that escalate to abolishing copyright or deny all compensation exceed what A actually claims.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting because the music analogy is prominent. It fails as A's reply because it is B's argument — A's whole point is that the music model does not transfer, since per-work training value approaches zero.`,`This is tempting as a hardline pro-AI stance. It fails on "under any circumstances": A explicitly preserves creators' protection against infringing outputs. A's claim is about training ingestion specifically, not all compensation.`,`This is correct. It reproduces A's two-pronged reply: negligible per-work value (so licensing would not meaningfully compensate) plus entrenchment of the largest firms (the only ones able to negotiate at billion-work scale).`,`This is tempting as an extreme conclusion. It fails because A defends fair use within copyright — "what fair use has always permitted" — not copyright's abolition. A's argument presupposes copyright continues.`,`This is tempting as a sympathy argument. It fails because A never claims AI companies cannot afford fees; A's objections are about meaningfulness of payments and competitive entrenchment, not affordability. A's second paragraph is a preemptive rebuttal containing both prongs the correct answer needs: negligible per-work value making payments meaningless, and billion-work scale entrenching only the largest firms. Distractors that escalate to abolition or deny all compensation exceed what A actually claims.`],generalLesson:`Preemptive rebuttals ("the alternative is unworkable") are the best source for hypothetical responses. Extract each prong of the rebuttal and check the answer contains them.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`opposite`],prerequisites:[`rc-comparative`,`rc-inference`],estimatedSeconds:110,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`Does Passage A anticipate the licensing proposal? Look at its second paragraph beginning "The alternative is unworkable."`,`A gives two reasons licensing fails. What are they — think payment size and market structure?`,`The correct choice contains both prongs without escalating to abolition or denying all compensation.`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}},{id:`rc-c06-q5`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-main-point`,secondarySkills:[`rc-passage-structure`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,distractorProximity:3},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[1,2],supportingSentences:[`A P1: "a textbook case of fair use"`,`A P2: "The alternative is unworkable"`],requiredInference:`Join A's positive thesis (fair use) with its negative thesis (licensing unworkable), bounded by the output-infringement concession.`,viewpointOwner:`Author of Passage A`,whyDistractorsFail:`A preserves output liability; A says per-work value "approaches zero"; copyright's purpose is progress, not veto; the analogy runs machines-get-what-students-get.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: walkthrough had a missing closing quote and a tacked-on summary fragment; repaired punctuation and clarified the final sentence. No answer/prose changes.`}],stem:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of Passage A?`,choices:[{text:`AI companies should be exempt from all copyright liability, including for infringing outputs.`},{text:`The per-work value of training data is so high that authors are owed substantial royalties.`},{text:`Copyright was originally designed to give authors veto power over new technologies.`},{text:`Human students should be subject to the same copyright rules as AI training systems.`},{text:`AI training on copyrighted works is transformative fair use; licensing would be unworkable and counterproductive.`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`A's thesis spans its three paragraphs: training is "a textbook case of fair use" (transformative, non-substitutive), licensing is "unworkable" (negligible per-work value, entrenches big tech), and output infringement remains actionable. Choice E summarizes this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`A's main point has two components: the positive claim (training is fair use — transformative learning producing no substitute for any particular work) and the negative claim (the licensing alternative is unworkable and would entrench incumbents, contrary to copyright's purpose of promoting progress). The third paragraph's concession on outputs bounds the claim. Choice E contains the positive and negative components. Choice A contradicts the third paragraph, which preserves output liability. Choice B reverses A's "approaches zero" claim. Choice D reverses the analogy's direction: A says machines should get what students get, not vice versa. Choice C reverses A's purpose claim: copyright was "designed to promote the progress of knowledge, not to give every author a veto." The main point joins A's positive and negative theses — training as transformative fair use, licensing as unworkable and entrenching — bounded by the third paragraph's concession that infringing outputs remain actionable, which the overbroad distractor ignores."`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a maximal pro-AI position. It fails because A's third paragraph explicitly preserves liability: infringing outputs "can infringe just as a human-authored copy would." The exemption claimed here is broader than A's.`,`This is tempting because compensation is discussed. It fails by reversing A's claim: A says per-work training value "approaches zero," which is why licensing "would not compensate authors meaningfully."`,`This is tempting because copyright's purpose is discussed. It fails by inverting A's claim: copyright was designed to promote progress, "not to give every author a veto over new technologies."`,`This is tempting because the student analogy is central. It fails by reversing its direction: A argues machines should enjoy what students enjoy (fair-use shelter for learning), not that students should face machines' restrictions.`,`This is correct. It joins A's positive thesis (transformative fair use) with its negative thesis (licensing unworkable: negligible per-work value, entrenches the largest companies) — the passage's full argument.`],generalLesson:`Main-point answers for argumentative passages should include both the positive thesis and the key negative argument (why the alternative fails), bounded by any concessions.`,misconceptionTags:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`strength-mismatch`],trapTypes:[`wrong-viewpoint`,`too-strong`,`opposite`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-main-point`],estimatedSeconds:85,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What is A's positive claim about training? And what does A say about the licensing alternative?`,`Check the third paragraph for the boundary A places on its own claim — which choices ignore that boundary?`,`The correct choice pairs "fair use" with "licensing unworkable."`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}},{id:`rc-c06-q6`,version:2,sectionType:`RC`,questionType:`rc-detail`,secondarySkills:[`rc-analogy`],editorialDifficulty:2,itemPurpose:`independent-blocked`,validationStatus:`validated`,revisedAt:`2026-09-11`,stimulus:``,passageId:`rc-c06`,difficultyProfile:{structuralComplexity:2,inferentialDistance:2,linguisticComplexity:2},evidenceMap:{supportingParagraphs:[2],supportingSentences:[`B P2: collective licensors "already manage rights for millions of musical works"`,`B P2: "small payments across billions of uses built the entire recorded-music economy"`],requiredInference:`The music example is an existence proof against A's "unworkable" objection: feasibility plus aggregation.`,whyDistractorsFail:`B's example depends on copyright applying to music; B presents the music system as a success; B argues feasibility by analogy, not legal identity; "only" over-narrows the generalization.`},reviewHistory:[{date:`2026-09-11`,reviewer:`rc-validation-2026-09-11`,verdict:`validate`,notes:`REVISE-fixed: walkthrough ended with a pasted paragraph about the main point (from q5); removed it so the walkthrough covers only the music-example detail. No answer/prose changes.`}],stem:`In Passage B, the reference to collective licensing organizations for musical works is used to support the claim that`,choices:[{text:`copyright law should not apply to musical works at all, since music belongs to everyone`},{text:`the music industry has been destroyed by collective licensing and cannot recover`},{text:`AI training is legally identical to playing a song on the radio, so the same royalties apply`},{text:`songwriters are the only creators who deserve compensation for the use of their work`},{text:`licensing training data at scale is administratively feasible and can direct real money to creators`}],correctIndex:4,explanationQuick:`B's second paragraph: collective licensing bodies "already manage rights for millions of musical works, distributing royalties to songwriters"; therefore "a similar regime for training data is administratively feasible and would direct real money to creators." Choice E restates this.`,explanationWalkthrough:`The music example is B's existence proof against A's "unworkable" objection. Its function is to show large-scale licensing already works (millions of works, royalties distributed) and that aggregation makes small per-use payments meaningful ("small payments across billions of uses built the entire recorded-music economy"). Choice E captures both halves. Choice B contradicts the example's use — the music economy is presented as built, not destroyed. Choice D is absurdly narrow; the point is generalizability. Choice C overstates: B claims feasibility by analogy, not legal identity. Choice A contradicts B, which relies on copyright applying to music as the model.`,choiceExplanations:[`This is tempting as a radical reading. It fails because B's argument depends on copyright applying to music — the licensing organizations operate under copyright. Exempting music would destroy B's own example.`,`This is tempting as a cynical take on licensing. It fails because B presents the music system as a success — "small payments across billions of uses built the entire recorded-music economy." Destruction is the opposite of B's point.`,`This is tempting because the analogy is legal. It fails on "legally identical": B argues feasibility by analogy, not identity of legal treatment — the claim is that a similar regime could work, not that the uses are the same.`,`This is tempting because songwriters are the example's beneficiaries. It fails on "only": the example generalizes to all creators whose works train models — songwriters merely illustrate the mechanism.`,`This is correct. The music example is B's proof that billion-scale licensing is "administratively feasible" and that aggregation turns tiny per-use values into "real money" for creators.`],generalLesson:`Existence-proof examples ("already manage rights for millions") rebut infeasibility objections. The detail answer states what the example proves: feasibility plus the aggregation mechanism.`,misconceptionTags:[`scope-shift`],trapTypes:[`opposite`,`too-strong`,`unsupported`,`scope-shift`],prerequisites:[`rc-detail`,`rc-analogy`],estimatedSeconds:75,sourceType:`original`,hints:[`What objection from Passage A is B answering with the music example? (Hint: "unworkable.")`,`What two things does the example show — about administration and about money reaching creators?`,`The correct choice covers both feasibility and the aggregation point.`],labels:{topic:`AI training and copyright`}}],x=[...l,...u,...d,...f,...p,...m,...h,...g,..._,...v,...y,...b];export{a,n as c,o as i,t as l,c as n,i as o,s as r,r as s,x as t,e as u};